const CARDS=[{"id":"qrh-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Emergency Descent (737 MAX QRH)","a":"**Condition:** One or more of these occur: Cabin altitude cannot be controlled; A rapid descent is needed.\n\n**Immediate action items:**\n1. Announce the emergency descent. The pilot flying will advise the cabin crew, on the PA system, of impending rapid descent. The pilot monitoring will advise ATC and obtain the area altimeter setting.\n2. Passenger signs ... ON\n3. Without delay, descend to the lowest safe altitude or 10,000 feet, whichever is higher.\n4. ENGINE START switches (both) ... CONT\n5. Thrust levers (both) ... Reduce thrust to minimum or as needed for anti-ice\n6. SPEED BRAKE ... FLIGHT DETENT\n7. If structural integrity is in doubt, limit speed as much as possible and avoid high maneuvering loads. Set target speed to Mmo/Vmo.\n\n(Reference items follow the dashed line; PF calls for the checklist when the flight path is under control.)","keys":[{"k":"Announce the emergency descent","m":["announce the emergency descent","announce emergency descent","announce"]},{"k":"Passenger signs: ON","m":["passenger signs","pax signs","seat belt sign","seatbelt sign"]},{"k":"Without delay, descend to the lowest...","m":["10000","ten thousand","lowest safe altitude"]},{"k":"ENGINE START switches (both): CONT","m":["cont","continuous","engine start switch"]},{"k":"Thrust levers (both): Reduce thrust to...","m":["thrust levers","reduce thrust","idle","thrust to minimum"]},{"k":"SPEED BRAKE: FLIGHT DETENT","m":["speed brake","speedbrake","flight detent"]},{"k":"If structural integrity is in doubt,...","m":["mmo","vmo"]}],"src":"QRH 0.1","ref":"Emergency Descent","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-1","type":"order","q":"Put the immediate action items for Emergency Descent in order.","items":["Announce the emergency descent. The pilot flying will advise the cabin crew, on the PA system, of impending rapid...","Passenger signs: ON","Without delay, descend to the lowest safe altitude or 10,000 feet, whichever is higher.","ENGINE START switches (both): CONT","Thrust levers (both): Reduce thrust to minimum or as needed for anti-ice","SPEED BRAKE: FLIGHT DETENT","If structural integrity is in doubt, limit speed as much as possible and avoid high maneuvering loads. Set target..."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH 0.1).","src":"QRH 0.1","ref":"Emergency Descent","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"Which condition statement applies to Emergency Descent?","o":["One or more of these occur: Cabin altitude cannot be controlled; A rapid descent is needed.","One or more of these occur: A cabin altitude exceedance; In flight, the intermittent cabin altitude/configuration warning horn sounds or a CABIN ALTITUDE light illuminates.","One or more of these occur: Engine fire warning; Airframe vibrations with abnormal engine indications; Engine separation; Engine failure with smoke or fumes in the flight deck or cabin.","On the ground, the intermittent cabin altitude/configuration warning horn sounds or a TAKEOFF CONFIG light illuminates when advancing the thrust levers to takeoff thrust."],"a":0,"why":"QRH 0.1: One or more of these occur: Cabin altitude cannot be controlled; A rapid descent is needed.","src":"QRH 0.1","ref":"Emergency Descent","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING or Rapid Depressurization (737 MAX QRH)","a":"**Condition:** One or more of these occur: A cabin altitude exceedance; In flight, the intermittent cabin altitude/configuration warning horn sounds or a CABIN ALTITUDE light illuminates.\n\n**Immediate action items:**\n1. Don oxygen masks and set regulators to 100%.\n2. Establish crew communications.\n3. Pressurization mode selector ... MAN\n4. Outflow VALVE switch ... Hold in CLOSE until the outflow VALVE indication shows fully closed\n5. If cabin altitude is uncontrollable: Passenger signs ... ON; PASS OXYGEN switch ... ON; Go to the Emergency Descent checklist on page 0.1\n\n(Reference items follow the dashed line; PF calls for the checklist when the flight path is under control.)","keys":[{"k":"Don oxygen masks and set regulators to...","m":["oxygen mask","o2 mask","100"]},{"k":"Establish crew communications.","m":["crew communication","communications","crew comm"]},{"k":"Pressurization mode selector: MAN","m":["mode selector","pressurization mode","pressurisation mode","manual","man"]},{"k":"Outflow VALVE switch: Hold in CLOSE...","m":["outflow valve","outflow","hold in close","fully closed"]},{"k":"If cabin altitude is uncontrollable:...","m":["pass oxygen","passenger oxygen","pax oxygen","emergency descent"]}],"src":"QRH 2.1","ref":"CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING or Rapid Depressurization","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-2","type":"order","q":"Put the immediate action items for CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING or Rapid Depressurization in order.","items":["Don oxygen masks and set regulators to 100%.","Establish crew communications.","Pressurization mode selector: MAN","Outflow VALVE switch: Hold in CLOSE until the outflow VALVE indication shows fully closed","If cabin altitude is uncontrollable: Passenger signs: ON; PASS OXYGEN switch: ON; Go to the Emergency Descent..."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH 2.1).","src":"QRH 2.1","ref":"CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING or Rapid Depressurization","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"Which condition statement applies to CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING or Rapid Depressurization?","o":["Both of these occur: Both engines have a loss of thrust; Both ENG FAIL alerts show.","One or more of these occur: A cabin altitude exceedance; In flight, the intermittent cabin altitude/configuration warning horn sounds or a CABIN ALTITUDE light illuminates.","Airspeed or Mach indications are suspected to be unreliable. (Items which might indicate unreliable airspeed are listed in the Additional Information section.)","Windshear warning (two-tone siren followed by \"WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR\"), windshear encountered in flight, or unacceptable flight path deviations below 1000 feet AGL. (Predictive \"GO-AROUND, WINDSHEAR AHEAD\" on approach: escape maneuver or, at pilot's discretion, a normal go-around.)"],"a":1,"why":"QRH 2.1: One or more of these occur: A cabin altitude exceedance; In flight, the intermittent cabin altitude/configuration warning horn sounds or a CABIN ALTITUDE light illuminates.","src":"QRH 2.1","ref":"CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING or Rapid Depressurization","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Aborted Engine Start (737 MAX QRH)","a":"**Condition:** On the ground, an aborted engine start is needed.\n\n**Immediate action items:**\n1. Engine start lever (affected engine) ... CUTOFF\n\n(Reference items follow the dashed line; PF calls for the checklist when the flight path is under control.)","keys":[{"k":"Engine start lever (affected engine):...","m":["cutoff","cut off","start lever"]}],"src":"QRH 7.1","ref":"Aborted Engine Start","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"Which condition statement applies to Aborted Engine Start?","o":["An overheat is detected in the engine.","One of these occurs: In flight, at an airplane flight altitude above 10,000 feet MSL, the intermittent warning horn sounds or a CABIN ALTITUDE light illuminates; On the ground, the intermittent warning horn sounds or a TAKEOFF CONFIG light illuminates when advancing the thrust levers to takeoff thrust.","On the ground, an aborted engine start is needed.","Windshear warning (two-tone siren followed by \"WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR\"), windshear encountered in flight, or unacceptable flight path deviations below 1000 feet AGL. (Predictive \"GO-AROUND, WINDSHEAR AHEAD\" on approach: escape maneuver or, at pilot's discretion, a normal go-around.)"],"a":2,"why":"QRH 7.1: On the ground, an aborted engine start is needed.","src":"QRH 7.1","ref":"Aborted Engine Start","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Engine Limit or Surge or Stall (737 MAX QRH)","a":"**Condition:** One or more of these occur: Engine indications are abnormal; Engine indications are rapidly approaching or exceeding limits; Abnormal engine noises are heard, possibly with airframe vibration; There is no response to thrust lever movement or the response is abnormal; Flames in the engine inlet or exhaust are reported.\n\n**Immediate action items:**\n1. Autothrottle (if engaged) ... Disengage\n2. Thrust lever (affected engine) ... Confirm ... Retard until engine indications stay within limits or the thrust lever is closed\n\n(Reference items follow the dashed line; PF calls for the checklist when the flight path is under control.)","keys":[{"k":"Autothrottle (if engaged): Disengage","m":["autothrottle","auto throttle","a/t","at disengage"]},{"k":"Thrust lever (affected engine):...","m":["retard","within limits","thrust lever"]}],"src":"QRH 7.2","ref":"Engine Limit or Surge or Stall","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"Which condition statement applies to Engine Limit or Surge or Stall?","o":["In flight, the steady warning horn sounds.","Captain's decision to reject, made in time to start the maneuver by V1. Before 80 knots: master caution activation, system failure(s), unusual noise or vibration, tire failure, abnormally slow acceleration, takeoff configuration warning, fire or fire warning, engine failure, predictive windshear warning, side window opens, airplane unsafe or unable to fly. Above 80 knots and before V1: fire or fire warning, engine failure, predictive windshear warning, airplane unsafe or unable to fly.","On the ground, an aborted engine start is needed.","One or more of these occur: Engine indications are abnormal; Engine indications are rapidly approaching or exceeding limits; Abnormal engine noises are heard, possibly with airframe vibration; There is no response to thrust lever movement or the response is abnormal; Flames in the engine inlet or exhaust are reported."],"a":3,"why":"QRH 7.2: One or more of these occur: Engine indications are abnormal; Engine indications are rapidly approaching or exceeding limits; Abnormal engine noises are heard, possibly with airframe vibration; There is no response to thrust lever movement or the response is abnormal; Flames in the engine inlet or exhaust are reported.","src":"QRH 7.2","ref":"Engine Limit or Surge or Stall","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines (737 MAX QRH)","a":"**Condition:** Both of these occur: Both engines have a loss of thrust; Both ENG FAIL alerts show.\n\n**Immediate action items:**\n1. ENGINE START switches (both) ... FLT\n2. Engine start levers (both) ... CUTOFF\n3. When EGT decreases: Engine start levers (both) ... IDLE detent\n4. If EGT reaches a redline or there is no increase in EGT within 30 seconds: Engine start lever (affected engine) ... Confirm ... CUTOFF, then IDLE detent. If EGT again reaches a redline or there is no increase in EGT within 30 seconds, repeat as needed.\n\n(Reference items follow the dashed line; PF calls for the checklist when the flight path is under control.)","keys":[{"k":"ENGINE START switches (both): FLT","m":["flt","flight"]},{"k":"Engine start levers (both): CUTOFF","m":["cutoff","cut off"]},{"k":"When EGT decreases: Engine start levers...","m":["idle detent","idle","egt decreases"]},{"k":"If EGT reaches a redline or there is no...","m":["30 seconds","30 sec","30s","redline","red line"]}],"src":"QRH 7.7","ref":"Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-3","type":"order","q":"Put the immediate action items for Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines in order.","items":["ENGINE START switches (both): FLT","Engine start levers (both): CUTOFF","When EGT decreases: Engine start levers (both): IDLE detent","If EGT reaches a redline or there is no increase in EGT within 30 seconds: Engine start lever (affected engine):..."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH 7.7).","src":"QRH 7.7","ref":"Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"Which condition statement applies to Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines?","o":["Both of these occur: Both engines have a loss of thrust; Both ENG FAIL alerts show.","Immediately do the following at the first indication of stall (buffet or stick shaker). Do all recoveries from approach to stall as if an actual stall has occurred.","One or more of these occur: Cabin altitude cannot be controlled; A rapid descent is needed.","One or more of these occur: Engine fire warning; Airframe vibrations with abnormal engine indications; Engine separation; Engine failure with smoke or fumes in the flight deck or cabin."],"a":0,"why":"QRH 7.7: Both of these occur: Both engines have a loss of thrust; Both ENG FAIL alerts show.","src":"QRH 7.7","ref":"Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: APU FIRE (737 MAX QRH)","a":"**Condition:** Fire is detected in the APU.\n\n**Immediate action items:**\n1. APU fire switch ... Confirm ... Pull, rotate to the stop, and hold for 1 second\n2. APU switch ... OFF\n\n(Reference items follow the dashed line; PF calls for the checklist when the flight path is under control.)","keys":[{"k":"APU fire switch: Confirm: Pull, rotate...","m":["apu fire switch","fire switch","pull","1 second","one second"]},{"k":"APU switch: OFF","m":["apu switch","apu off"]}],"src":"QRH 8.1","ref":"APU FIRE","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"Which condition statement applies to APU FIRE?","o":["A TCAS resolution advisory (RA) occurs (immediately accomplish by recall). Comply with the RA if there is a conflict between the RA and air traffic control.","Fire is detected in the APU.","One or more of these occur: Engine indications are abnormal; Engine indications are rapidly approaching or exceeding limits; Abnormal engine noises are heard, possibly with airframe vibration; There is no response to thrust lever movement or the response is abnormal; Flames in the engine inlet or exhaust are reported.","Airspeed or Mach indications are suspected to be unreliable. (Items which might indicate unreliable airspeed are listed in the Additional Information section.)"],"a":1,"why":"QRH 8.1: Fire is detected in the APU.","src":"QRH 8.1","ref":"APU FIRE","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: ENGINE FIRE or Engine Severe Damage or Separation (737 MAX QRH)","a":"**Condition:** One or more of these occur: Engine fire warning; Airframe vibrations with abnormal engine indications; Engine separation; Engine failure with smoke or fumes in the flight deck or cabin.\n\n**Immediate action items:**\n1. Autothrottle (if engaged) ... Disengage\n2. Thrust lever (affected engine) ... Confirm ... Close\n3. Engine start lever (affected engine) ... Confirm ... CUTOFF\n4. Engine fire switch (affected engine) ... Confirm ... Pull. To manually unlock the engine fire switch, press the override and pull.\n5. If the engine fire switch or ENG OVERHEAT light is illuminated: Engine fire switch (affected engine) ... Rotate to the stop and hold for 1 second\n\n(Reference items follow the dashed line; PF calls for the checklist when the flight path is under control.)","keys":[{"k":"Autothrottle (if engaged): Disengage","m":["autothrottle","auto throttle","a/t"]},{"k":"Thrust lever (affected engine):...","m":["thrust lever","close"]},{"k":"Engine start lever (affected engine):...","m":["start lever","cutoff","cut off"]},{"k":"Engine fire switch (affected engine):...","m":["fire switch","fire handle","pull"]},{"k":"If the engine fire switch or ENG...","m":["rotate","1 second","one second","discharge"]}],"src":"QRH 8.2","ref":"ENGINE FIRE or Engine Severe Damage or Separation","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-4","type":"order","q":"Put the immediate action items for ENGINE FIRE or Engine Severe Damage or Separation in order.","items":["Autothrottle (if engaged): Disengage","Thrust lever (affected engine): Confirm: Close","Engine start lever (affected engine): Confirm: CUTOFF","Engine fire switch (affected engine): Confirm: Pull. To manually unlock the engine fire switch, press the override...","If the engine fire switch or ENG OVERHEAT light is illuminated: Engine fire switch (affected engine): Rotate to the..."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH 8.2).","src":"QRH 8.2","ref":"ENGINE FIRE or Engine Severe Damage or Separation","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"Which condition statement applies to ENGINE FIRE or Engine Severe Damage or Separation?","o":["One or more of these occur: A cabin altitude exceedance; In flight, the intermittent cabin altitude/configuration warning horn sounds or a CABIN ALTITUDE light illuminates.","An overheat is detected in the engine.","One or more of these occur: Engine fire warning; Airframe vibrations with abnormal engine indications; Engine separation; Engine failure with smoke or fumes in the flight deck or cabin.","One of these occurs: In flight, at an airplane flight altitude above 10,000 feet MSL, the intermittent warning horn sounds or a CABIN ALTITUDE light illuminates; On the ground, the intermittent warning horn sounds or a TAKEOFF CONFIG light illuminates when advancing the thrust levers to takeoff thrust."],"a":2,"why":"QRH 8.2: One or more of these occur: Engine fire warning; Airframe vibrations with abnormal engine indications; Engine separation; Engine failure with smoke or fumes in the flight deck or cabin.","src":"QRH 8.2","ref":"ENGINE FIRE or Engine Severe Damage or Separation","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: ENGINE OVERHEAT (737 MAX QRH)","a":"**Condition:** An overheat is detected in the engine.\n\n**Immediate action items:**\n1. Autothrottle (if engaged) ... Disengage\n2. Thrust lever (affected engine) ... Confirm ... Close\n3. If the ENG OVERHEAT light stays illuminated: Go to the ENGINE FIRE or Engine Severe Damage or Separation checklist on page 8.2\n\n(Reference items follow the dashed line; PF calls for the checklist when the flight path is under control.)","keys":[{"k":"Autothrottle (if engaged): Disengage","m":["autothrottle","auto throttle","a/t"]},{"k":"Thrust lever (affected engine):...","m":["thrust lever","close"]},{"k":"If the ENG OVERHEAT light stays...","m":["engine fire","severe damage","stays illuminated","stays on"]}],"src":"QRH 8.8","ref":"ENGINE OVERHEAT","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-5","type":"order","q":"Put the immediate action items for ENGINE OVERHEAT in order.","items":["Autothrottle (if engaged): Disengage","Thrust lever (affected engine): Confirm: Close","If the ENG OVERHEAT light stays illuminated: Go to the ENGINE FIRE or Engine Severe Damage or Separation checklist..."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH 8.8).","src":"QRH 8.8","ref":"ENGINE OVERHEAT","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"Which condition statement applies to ENGINE OVERHEAT?","o":["Both of these occur: Both engines have a loss of thrust; Both ENG FAIL alerts show.","In flight, the steady warning horn sounds.","Captain's decision to reject, made in time to start the maneuver by V1. Before 80 knots: master caution activation, system failure(s), unusual noise or vibration, tire failure, abnormally slow acceleration, takeoff configuration warning, fire or fire warning, engine failure, predictive windshear warning, side window opens, airplane unsafe or unable to fly. Above 80 knots and before V1: fire or fire warning, engine failure, predictive windshear warning, airplane unsafe or unable to fly.","An overheat is detected in the engine."],"a":3,"why":"QRH 8.8: An overheat is detected in the engine.","src":"QRH 8.8","ref":"ENGINE OVERHEAT","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Runaway Stabilizer (737 MAX QRH)","a":"**Condition:** Uncommanded stabilizer trim movement occurs continuously or in a manner not appropriate for flight conditions.\n\n**Immediate action items:**\n1. Control column ... Hold firmly\n2. Autopilot (if engaged) ... Disengage\n3. Autothrottle (if engaged) ... Disengage\n4. Control column and thrust levers ... Control airplane pitch attitude and airspeed\n5. Main Electric Stabilizer trim ... Reduce control column forces\n6. If the runaway stops after the autopilot is disengaged: Do not re-engage the autopilot or autothrottle.\n7. If the runaway continues after the autopilot is disengaged: STAB TRIM cutout switches (both) ... CUTOUT. If the runaway continues: Stabilizer trim wheel ... Grasp and hold\n\n(Reference items follow the dashed line; PF calls for the checklist when the flight path is under control.)","keys":[{"k":"Control column: Hold firmly","m":["hold firmly","control column","column firmly"]},{"k":"Autopilot (if engaged): Disengage","m":["autopilot","auto pilot","a/p"]},{"k":"Autothrottle (if engaged): Disengage","m":["autothrottle","auto throttle","a/t"]},{"k":"Control column and thrust levers:...","m":["pitch attitude and airspeed","control airplane","control aircraft","pitch and airspeed","attitude and airspeed"]},{"k":"Main Electric Stabilizer trim: Reduce...","m":["electric trim","electric stabilizer trim","main electric","reduce control column forces","column forces"]},{"k":"If the runaway stops after the...","m":["do not re-engage","do not reengage","not re engage","dont reengage","dont re engage"]},{"k":"If the runaway continues after the...","m":["cutout","cut out","grasp and hold","trim wheel"]}],"src":"QRH 9.1","ref":"Runaway Stabilizer","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-6","type":"order","q":"Put the immediate action items for Runaway Stabilizer in order.","items":["Control column: Hold firmly","Autopilot (if engaged): Disengage","Autothrottle (if engaged): Disengage","Control column and thrust levers: Control airplane pitch attitude and airspeed","Main Electric Stabilizer trim: Reduce control column forces","If the runaway stops after the autopilot is disengaged: Do not re-engage the autopilot or autothrottle.","If the runaway continues after the autopilot is disengaged: STAB TRIM cutout switches (both): CUTOUT. If the runaway..."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH 9.1).","src":"QRH 9.1","ref":"Runaway Stabilizer","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Which condition statement applies to Runaway Stabilizer?","o":["Uncommanded stabilizer trim movement occurs continuously or in a manner not appropriate for flight conditions.","An overheat is detected in the engine.","Immediately do the following at the first indication of stall (buffet or stick shaker). Do all recoveries from approach to stall as if an actual stall has occurred.","One or more of these occur: Cabin altitude cannot be controlled; A rapid descent is needed."],"a":0,"why":"QRH 9.1: Uncommanded stabilizer trim movement occurs continuously or in a manner not appropriate for flight conditions.","src":"QRH 9.1","ref":"Runaway Stabilizer","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Airspeed Unreliable (Stick Shaker Deactivation by Pulling Circuit Breaker) (737 MAX QRH)","a":"**Condition:** Airspeed or Mach indications are suspected to be unreliable. (Items which might indicate unreliable airspeed are listed in the Additional Information section.)\n\n**Immediate action items:**\n1. Autopilot (if engaged) ... Disengage\n2. Autothrottle (if engaged) ... Disengage\n3. F/D switches (both) ... OFF\n4. Set the following gear up pitch attitude and thrust: Flaps extended ... 10° and 80% N1; Flaps up ... 4° and 75% N1\n\n(Reference items follow the dashed line; PF calls for the checklist when the flight path is under control.)","keys":[{"k":"Autopilot (if engaged): Disengage","m":["autopilot","auto pilot","a/p"]},{"k":"Autothrottle (if engaged): Disengage","m":["autothrottle","auto throttle","a/t"]},{"k":"F/D switches (both): OFF","m":["f/d","fd switches","flight director","fd off"]},{"k":"Set the following gear up pitch...","m":["10 degrees and 80","10° and 80","10 and 80","80% n1","80 percent","80 n1"]}],"src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"Airspeed Unreliable (Stick Shaker Deactivation by Pulling Circuit Breaker)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-7","type":"order","q":"Put the immediate action items for Airspeed Unreliable (Stick Shaker Deactivation by Pulling Circuit Breaker) in order.","items":["Autopilot (if engaged): Disengage","Autothrottle (if engaged): Disengage","F/D switches (both): OFF","Set the following gear up pitch attitude and thrust: Flaps extended: 10° and 80% N1; Flaps up: 4° and 75% N1"],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH 10.1).","src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"Airspeed Unreliable (Stick Shaker Deactivation by Pulling Circuit Breaker)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"Which condition statement applies to Airspeed Unreliable (Stick Shaker Deactivation by Pulling Circuit Breaker)?","o":["On the ground, the intermittent cabin altitude/configuration warning horn sounds or a TAKEOFF CONFIG light illuminates when advancing the thrust levers to takeoff thrust.","Airspeed or Mach indications are suspected to be unreliable. (Items which might indicate unreliable airspeed are listed in the Additional Information section.)","A TCAS resolution advisory (RA) occurs (immediately accomplish by recall). Comply with the RA if there is a conflict between the RA and air traffic control.","One or more of these occur: Engine indications are abnormal; Engine indications are rapidly approaching or exceeding limits; Abnormal engine noises are heard, possibly with airframe vibration; There is no response to thrust lever movement or the response is abnormal; Flames in the engine inlet or exhaust are reported."],"a":1,"why":"QRH 10.1: Airspeed or Mach indications are suspected to be unreliable. (Items which might indicate unreliable airspeed are listed in the Additional Information section.)","src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"Airspeed Unreliable (Stick Shaker Deactivation by Pulling Circuit Breaker)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: LANDING CONFIGURATION (737 MAX QRH)","a":"**Condition:** In flight, the steady warning horn sounds.\n\n**Immediate action items:**\n1. Assure correct airplane landing configuration.\n\n(Reference items follow the dashed line; PF calls for the checklist when the flight path is under control.)","keys":[{"k":"Assure correct airplane landing...","m":["landing configuration","landing config"]}],"src":"QRH 15.1","ref":"LANDING CONFIGURATION","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"Which condition statement applies to LANDING CONFIGURATION?","o":["One or more of these occur: Engine indications are abnormal; Engine indications are rapidly approaching or exceeding limits; Abnormal engine noises are heard, possibly with airframe vibration; There is no response to thrust lever movement or the response is abnormal; Flames in the engine inlet or exhaust are reported.","Uncommanded stabilizer trim movement occurs continuously or in a manner not appropriate for flight conditions.","In flight, the steady warning horn sounds.","A TCAS resolution advisory (RA) occurs (immediately accomplish by recall). Comply with the RA if there is a conflict between the RA and air traffic control."],"a":2,"why":"QRH 15.1: In flight, the steady warning horn sounds.","src":"QRH 15.1","ref":"LANDING CONFIGURATION","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: TAKEOFF CONFIGURATION (737 MAX QRH)","a":"**Condition:** On the ground, the intermittent cabin altitude/configuration warning horn sounds or a TAKEOFF CONFIG light illuminates when advancing the thrust levers to takeoff thrust.\n\n**Immediate action items:**\n1. Assure correct airplane takeoff configuration.\n\n(Reference items follow the dashed line; PF calls for the checklist when the flight path is under control.)","keys":[{"k":"Assure correct airplane takeoff...","m":["takeoff configuration","takeoff config","take off config"]}],"src":"QRH 15.1","ref":"TAKEOFF CONFIGURATION","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"Which condition statement applies to TAKEOFF CONFIGURATION?","o":["One or more of these occur: Engine fire warning; Airframe vibrations with abnormal engine indications; Engine separation; Engine failure with smoke or fumes in the flight deck or cabin.","Activation of \"PULL UP\" or \"TERRAIN TERRAIN PULL UP\" warning; activation of the \"PULL UP\" or \"OBSTACLE OBSTACLE PULL UP\" warning; other situations resulting in unacceptable flight toward terrain.","On the ground, an aborted engine start is needed.","On the ground, the intermittent cabin altitude/configuration warning horn sounds or a TAKEOFF CONFIG light illuminates when advancing the thrust levers to takeoff thrust."],"a":3,"why":"QRH 15.1: On the ground, the intermittent cabin altitude/configuration warning horn sounds or a TAKEOFF CONFIG light illuminates when advancing the thrust levers to takeoff thrust.","src":"QRH 15.1","ref":"TAKEOFF CONFIGURATION","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: WARNING HORN (INTERMITTENT) or WARNING LIGHT - CABIN ALTITUDE OR TAKEOFF CONFIGURATION (737 MAX QRH)","a":"**Condition:** One of these occurs: In flight, at an airplane flight altitude above 10,000 feet MSL, the intermittent warning horn sounds or a CABIN ALTITUDE light illuminates; On the ground, the intermittent warning horn sounds or a TAKEOFF CONFIG light illuminates when advancing the thrust levers to takeoff thrust.\n\n**Immediate action items:**\n1. If the intermittent warning horn sounds or a CABIN ALTITUDE light illuminates in flight at an airplane flight altitude above 10,000 feet MSL: Don the oxygen masks and set the regulators to 100%. Establish crew communications. Go to the CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING or Rapid Depressurization checklist on page 2.1\n2. If the intermittent warning horn sounds or a TAKEOFF CONFIG light illuminates on the ground when advancing the thrust levers to takeoff thrust: Assure correct airplane takeoff configuration.\n\n(Reference items follow the dashed line; PF calls for the checklist when the flight path is under control.)","keys":[{"k":"If the intermittent warning horn sounds...","m":["oxygen mask","cabin altitude warning","rapid depressurization","rapid depressurisation"]},{"k":"If the intermittent warning horn sounds...","m":["takeoff configuration","takeoff config","take off config"]}],"src":"QRH 15.2","ref":"WARNING HORN (INTERMITTENT) or WARNING LIGHT - CABIN ALTITUDE OR TAKEOFF CONFIGURATION","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"Which condition statement applies to WARNING HORN (INTERMITTENT) or WARNING LIGHT - CABIN ALTITUDE OR TAKEOFF CONFIGURATION?","o":["One of these occurs: In flight, at an airplane flight altitude above 10,000 feet MSL, the intermittent warning horn sounds or a CABIN ALTITUDE light illuminates; On the ground, the intermittent warning horn sounds or a TAKEOFF CONFIG light illuminates when advancing the thrust levers to takeoff thrust.","Airspeed or Mach indications are suspected to be unreliable. (Items which might indicate unreliable airspeed are listed in the Additional Information section.)","Windshear warning (two-tone siren followed by \"WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR\"), windshear encountered in flight, or unacceptable flight path deviations below 1000 feet AGL. (Predictive \"GO-AROUND, WINDSHEAR AHEAD\" on approach: escape maneuver or, at pilot's discretion, a normal go-around.)","Fire is detected in the APU."],"a":0,"why":"QRH 15.2: One of these occurs: In flight, at an airplane flight altitude above 10,000 feet MSL, the intermittent warning horn sounds or a CABIN ALTITUDE light illuminates; On the ground, the intermittent warning horn sounds or a TAKEOFF CONFIG light illuminates when advancing the thrust levers to takeoff thrust.","src":"QRH 15.2","ref":"WARNING HORN (INTERMITTENT) or WARNING LIGHT - CABIN ALTITUDE OR TAKEOFF CONFIGURATION","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Approach to Stall or Stall Recovery (737 MAX non-normal maneuver)","a":"**When:** Immediately do the following at the first indication of stall (buffet or stick shaker). Do all recoveries from approach to stall as if an actual stall has occurred.\n\n**Pilot Flying:**\n1. Initiate the recovery: Hold the control column firmly.\n2. Disengage autopilot and autothrottle.\n3. Smoothly apply nose down elevator to reduce the angle of attack until buffet or stick shaker stops. Nose down stabilizer trim can be needed.\n4. Continue the recovery: Roll in the shortest direction to wings level if needed.\n5. Advance thrust levers as needed. Retract the speedbrakes.\n6. Do not change gear or flap configuration, except: During liftoff, if flaps are up, call for flaps 1.\n7. Complete the recovery: Check airspeed and adjust thrust as needed. Establish pitch attitude. Return to the desired flight path. Re-engage the autopilot and autothrottle if desired.\n\n**Pilot Monitoring:** Monitor altitude and airspeed. Verify all needed actions have been done and call out any omissions. Call out any trend toward terrain contact. Set the FLAP lever as directed.\n\n**Notes:** Do not use flight director commands during the recovery.","keys":[{"k":"Initiate the recovery: Hold the control...","m":["hold the control column","control column firmly","hold firmly"]},{"k":"Disengage autopilot and autothrottle.","m":["autopilot","auto pilot","a/p"]},{"k":"Smoothly apply nose down elevator to...","m":["nose down","angle of attack","reduce aoa","reduce the aoa"]},{"k":"Continue the recovery: Roll in the...","m":["wings level","shortest direction"]},{"k":"Advance thrust levers as needed","m":["advance thrust","thrust levers","retract the speedbrake","retract speedbrake","speedbrakes","speed brakes"]},{"k":"Do not change gear or flap...","m":["do not change gear","dont change gear","gear or flap","flaps 1","flap 1"]},{"k":"Complete the recovery: Check airspeed...","m":["desired flight path","establish pitch","check airspeed","flight path"]}],"src":"QRH MAN.1.1","ref":"Approach to Stall or Stall Recovery","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-8","type":"order","q":"Put the Pilot Flying actions for Approach to Stall or Stall Recovery in order.","items":["Initiate the recovery: Hold the control column firmly.","Disengage autopilot and autothrottle.","Smoothly apply nose down elevator to reduce the angle of attack until buffet or stick shaker stops. Nose down...","Continue the recovery: Roll in the shortest direction to wings level if needed.","Advance thrust levers as needed. Retract the speedbrakes.","Do not change gear or flap configuration, except: During liftoff, if flaps are up, call for flaps 1.","Complete the recovery: Check airspeed and adjust thrust as needed. Establish pitch attitude. Return to the desired..."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH MAN.1.1).","src":"QRH MAN.1.1","ref":"Approach to Stall or Stall Recovery","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"Which trigger applies to Approach to Stall or Stall Recovery?","o":["Fire is detected in the APU.","Immediately do the following at the first indication of stall (buffet or stick shaker). Do all recoveries from approach to stall as if an actual stall has occurred.","In flight, the steady warning horn sounds.","A TCAS resolution advisory (RA) occurs (immediately accomplish by recall). Comply with the RA if there is a conflict between the RA and air traffic control."],"a":1,"why":"QRH MAN.1.1: Immediately do the following at the first indication of stall (buffet or stick shaker). Do all recoveries from approach to stall as if an actual stall has occurred.","src":"QRH MAN.1.1","ref":"Approach to Stall or Stall Recovery","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: GPWS Warning (737 MAX non-normal maneuver)","a":"**When:** Activation of \"PULL UP\" or \"TERRAIN TERRAIN PULL UP\" warning; activation of the \"PULL UP\" or \"OBSTACLE OBSTACLE PULL UP\" warning; other situations resulting in unacceptable flight toward terrain.\n\n**Pilot Flying:**\n1. Disengage autopilot. Disengage autothrottle.\n2. Aggressively apply maximum thrust.\n3. Simultaneously roll wings level and rotate to an initial pitch attitude of 20°.\n4. Retract speedbrakes.\n5. If terrain remains a threat, continue rotation up to the pitch limit indicator (if available) or stick shaker or initial buffet.\n6. Do not change gear or flap configuration until terrain separation is assured.\n7. Monitor radio altimeter for sustained or increasing terrain separation. When clear of terrain, slowly decrease pitch attitude and accelerate.\n\n**Pilot Monitoring:** Assure maximum thrust. Verify all needed actions have been completed and call out any omissions. Monitor vertical speed and altitude (radio altitude for terrain clearance and barometric altitude for a minimum safe altitude). Call out any trend toward terrain contact.\n\n**Notes:** Do not use flight director commands. Maximum thrust can be obtained by advancing the thrust levers full forward if the EECs are in the normal mode. If terrain contact is imminent, advance thrust levers full forward.","keys":[{"k":"Disengage autopilot","m":["autopilot","auto pilot","a/p"]},{"k":"Aggressively apply maximum thrust.","m":["maximum thrust","max thrust","full thrust","firewall"]},{"k":"Simultaneously roll wings level and...","m":["20 degrees","20°","20 deg","twenty degrees","20 nose up"]},{"k":"Retract speedbrakes.","m":["speedbrake","speed brake"]},{"k":"If terrain remains a threat, continue...","m":["pitch limit","stick shaker","initial buffet","pli"]},{"k":"Do not change gear or flap...","m":["do not change gear","dont change gear","gear or flap","configuration"]},{"k":"Monitor radio altimeter for sustained...","m":["radio alt","clear of terrain","slowly decrease"]}],"src":"QRH MAN.1.3","ref":"GPWS Warning","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-9","type":"order","q":"Put the Pilot Flying actions for GPWS Warning in order.","items":["Disengage autopilot. Disengage autothrottle.","Aggressively apply maximum thrust.","Simultaneously roll wings level and rotate to an initial pitch attitude of 20°.","Retract speedbrakes.","If terrain remains a threat, continue rotation up to the pitch limit indicator (if available) or stick shaker or...","Do not change gear or flap configuration until terrain separation is assured.","Monitor radio altimeter for sustained or increasing terrain separation. When clear of terrain, slowly decrease pitch..."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH MAN.1.3).","src":"QRH MAN.1.3","ref":"GPWS Warning","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"Which trigger applies to GPWS Warning?","o":["Uncommanded stabilizer trim movement occurs continuously or in a manner not appropriate for flight conditions.","Immediately do the following at the first indication of stall (buffet or stick shaker). Do all recoveries from approach to stall as if an actual stall has occurred.","Activation of \"PULL UP\" or \"TERRAIN TERRAIN PULL UP\" warning; activation of the \"PULL UP\" or \"OBSTACLE OBSTACLE PULL UP\" warning; other situations resulting in unacceptable flight toward terrain.","One or more of these occur: A cabin altitude exceedance; In flight, the intermittent cabin altitude/configuration warning horn sounds or a CABIN ALTITUDE light illuminates."],"a":2,"why":"QRH MAN.1.3: Activation of \"PULL UP\" or \"TERRAIN TERRAIN PULL UP\" warning; activation of the \"PULL UP\" or \"OBSTACLE OBSTACLE PULL UP\" warning; other situations resulting in unacceptable flight toward terrain.","src":"QRH MAN.1.3","ref":"GPWS Warning","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Rejected Takeoff (737 MAX non-normal maneuver)","a":"**When:** Captain's decision to reject, made in time to start the maneuver by V1. Before 80 knots: master caution activation, system failure(s), unusual noise or vibration, tire failure, abnormally slow acceleration, takeoff configuration warning, fire or fire warning, engine failure, predictive windshear warning, side window opens, airplane unsafe or unable to fly. Above 80 knots and before V1: fire or fire warning, engine failure, predictive windshear warning, airplane unsafe or unable to fly.\n\n**Pilot Flying:**\n1. Without delay: Simultaneously close the thrust levers, disengage the autothrottles and apply maximum manual wheel brakes or verify operation of RTO autobrake.\n2. If RTO autobrake is selected, monitor system performance and apply manual wheel brakes if the AUTO BRAKE DISARM light illuminates or deceleration is not adequate.\n3. Raise SPEED BRAKE lever.\n4. Apply reverse thrust up to the maximum amount consistent with conditions.\n5. Continue maximum braking until certain the airplane can stop on the runway.\n6. When stopping is assured: Start movement of the reverse thrust levers to reach the reverse idle detent before taxi speed. After the engines are at reverse idle, move the reverse thrust levers to full down.\n\n**Pilot Monitoring:** (First Officer) Verify: thrust levers closed; autothrottles disengaged; maximum brakes applied. Verify SPEED BRAKE lever UP and call \"SPEEDBRAKES UP\" (or \"SPEEDBRAKES NOT UP\"). Reverse thrust applied: when both REV indications are green, call \"REVERSERS NORMAL\" (or \"NO REVERSER ENGINE NUMBER 1\", \"NO REVERSER ENGINE NUMBER 2\", \"NO REVERSERS\"). Call out omitted action items. Call out 60 knots. Communicate the reject decision to the control tower and cabin as soon as practical.\n\n**Notes:** The captain has the sole responsibility for the decision to reject the takeoff. If the first officer is making the takeoff, the first officer maintains control of the airplane until the captain makes a positive input to the controls.","keys":[{"k":"Without delay: Simultaneously close the...","m":["close the thrust levers","close thrust levers","thrust levers closed","thrust levers"]},{"k":"If RTO autobrake is selected, monitor...","m":["auto brake disarm","autobrake disarm","maximum manual","max manual","rto autobrake","max braking","maximum brakes"]},{"k":"Raise SPEED BRAKE lever.","m":["speed brake","speedbrake"]},{"k":"Apply reverse thrust up to the maximum...","m":["reverse thrust","reverser","reverse"]},{"k":"Continue maximum braking until certain...","m":["continue maximum braking","continue max braking","stop on the runway","can stop"]},{"k":"When stopping is assured: Start...","m":["reverse idle","taxi speed","full down"]}],"src":"QRH MAN.1.5 to MAN.1.8","ref":"Rejected Takeoff","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-10","type":"order","q":"Put the Pilot Flying actions for Rejected Takeoff in order.","items":["Without delay: Simultaneously close the thrust levers, disengage the autothrottles and apply maximum manual wheel...","If RTO autobrake is selected, monitor system performance and apply manual wheel brakes if the AUTO BRAKE DISARM...","Raise SPEED BRAKE lever.","Apply reverse thrust up to the maximum amount consistent with conditions.","Continue maximum braking until certain the airplane can stop on the runway.","When stopping is assured: Start movement of the reverse thrust levers to reach the reverse idle detent before taxi..."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH MAN.1.5 to MAN.1.8).","src":"QRH MAN.1.5 to MAN.1.8","ref":"Rejected Takeoff","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-16","type":"mcq","q":"Which trigger applies to Rejected Takeoff?","o":["On the ground, the intermittent cabin altitude/configuration warning horn sounds or a TAKEOFF CONFIG light illuminates when advancing the thrust levers to takeoff thrust.","Windshear warning (two-tone siren followed by \"WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR\"), windshear encountered in flight, or unacceptable flight path deviations below 1000 feet AGL. (Predictive \"GO-AROUND, WINDSHEAR AHEAD\" on approach: escape maneuver or, at pilot's discretion, a normal go-around.)","Both of these occur: Both engines have a loss of thrust; Both ENG FAIL alerts show.","Captain's decision to reject, made in time to start the maneuver by V1. Before 80 knots: master caution activation, system failure(s), unusual noise or vibration, tire failure, abnormally slow acceleration, takeoff configuration warning, fire or fire warning, engine failure, predictive windshear warning, side window opens, airplane unsafe or unable to fly. Above 80 knots and before V1: fire or fire warning, engine failure, predictive windshear warning, airplane unsafe or unable to fly."],"a":3,"why":"QRH MAN.1.5 to MAN.1.8: Captain's decision to reject, made in time to start the maneuver by V1. Before 80 knots: master caution activation, system failure(s), unusual noise or vibration, tire failure, abnormally slow acceleration, takeoff configuration warning, fire or fire warning, engine failure, predictive windshear warning, side window opens, airplane unsafe or unable to fly. Above 80 knots and before V1: fire or fire warning, engine failure, predictive windshear warning, airplane unsafe or unable to fly.","src":"QRH MAN.1.5 to MAN.1.8","ref":"Rejected Takeoff","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Traffic Avoidance (RA, except a climb in landing configuration) (737 MAX non-normal maneuver)","a":"**When:** A TCAS resolution advisory (RA) occurs (immediately accomplish by recall). Comply with the RA if there is a conflict between the RA and air traffic control.\n\n**Pilot Flying:**\n1. If maneuvering is needed, disengage the autopilot and autothrottle.\n2. Smoothly adjust pitch and thrust to satisfy the RA command.\n3. Follow the planned lateral flight path unless visual contact with the conflicting traffic requires other action.\n4. Attempt to establish visual contact.\n\n**Pilot Monitoring:** Call out any conflicting traffic.\n\n**Notes:** Do not follow a DESCEND (fly down) RA issued below 1000 feet AGL. Do not use flight director pitch commands until clear of conflict. If stick shaker or initial buffet occurs, immediately accomplish the APPROACH TO STALL RECOVERY procedure. For a TA: look for traffic using traffic display as a guide; if traffic is sighted, maneuver if needed (maneuvers based solely on a TA are not recommended).","keys":[{"k":"If maneuvering is needed, disengage the...","m":["autopilot","auto pilot","a/p"]},{"k":"Smoothly adjust pitch and thrust to...","m":["satisfy the ra","adjust pitch","pitch and thrust","follow the ra","green arc","green band"]},{"k":"Follow the planned lateral flight path...","m":["lateral","planned lateral"]},{"k":"Attempt to establish visual contact.","m":["visual contact","look out","lookout","visual"]}],"src":"QRH MAN.1.9","ref":"Traffic Avoidance (RA, except a climb in landing configuration)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-11","type":"order","q":"Put the Pilot Flying actions for Traffic Avoidance (RA, except a climb in landing configuration) in order.","items":["If maneuvering is needed, disengage the autopilot and autothrottle.","Smoothly adjust pitch and thrust to satisfy the RA command.","Follow the planned lateral flight path unless visual contact with the conflicting traffic requires other action.","Attempt to establish visual contact."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH MAN.1.9).","src":"QRH MAN.1.9","ref":"Traffic Avoidance (RA, except a climb in landing configuration)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-17","type":"mcq","q":"Which trigger applies to Traffic Avoidance (RA, except a climb in landing configuration)?","o":["A TCAS resolution advisory (RA) occurs (immediately accomplish by recall). Comply with the RA if there is a conflict between the RA and air traffic control.","Activation of \"PULL UP\" or \"TERRAIN TERRAIN PULL UP\" warning; activation of the \"PULL UP\" or \"OBSTACLE OBSTACLE PULL UP\" warning; other situations resulting in unacceptable flight toward terrain.","On the ground, an aborted engine start is needed.","An overheat is detected in the engine."],"a":0,"why":"QRH MAN.1.9: A TCAS resolution advisory (RA) occurs (immediately accomplish by recall). Comply with the RA if there is a conflict between the RA and air traffic control.","src":"QRH MAN.1.9","ref":"Traffic Avoidance (RA, except a climb in landing configuration)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Traffic Avoidance (climb RA in landing configuration) (737 MAX non-normal maneuver)","a":"**When:** A TCAS climb RA occurs in landing configuration.\n\n**Pilot Flying:**\n1. Disengage the autopilot and autothrottle.\n2. Advance thrust levers forward to ensure maximum thrust is attained and call for FLAPS 15.\n3. Smoothly adjust pitch to satisfy the RA command.\n4. Follow the planned lateral flight path unless visual contact with the conflicting traffic requires other action.\n5. Verify a positive rate of climb on the altimeter and call \"GEAR UP.\"\n6. Attempt to establish visual contact.\n\n**Pilot Monitoring:** Verify maximum thrust set. Position flap lever to 15 detent. Verify a positive rate of climb on the altimeter and call \"POSITIVE RATE.\" Set the landing gear lever to UP. Call out any conflicting traffic.","keys":[{"k":"Disengage the autopilot and...","m":["autopilot","auto pilot","a/p"]},{"k":"Advance thrust levers forward to ensure...","m":["flaps 15","flap 15","maximum thrust","max thrust"]},{"k":"Smoothly adjust pitch to satisfy the RA...","m":["satisfy the ra","adjust pitch","follow the ra"]},{"k":"Follow the planned lateral flight path...","m":["lateral","planned lateral"]},{"k":"Verify a positive rate of climb on the...","m":["gear up","positive rate"]},{"k":"Attempt to establish visual contact.","m":["visual contact","look out","lookout","visual"]}],"src":"QRH MAN.1.10","ref":"Traffic Avoidance (climb RA in landing configuration)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-12","type":"order","q":"Put the Pilot Flying actions for Traffic Avoidance (climb RA in landing configuration) in order.","items":["Disengage the autopilot and autothrottle.","Advance thrust levers forward to ensure maximum thrust is attained and call for FLAPS 15.","Smoothly adjust pitch to satisfy the RA command.","Follow the planned lateral flight path unless visual contact with the conflicting traffic requires other action.","Verify a positive rate of climb on the altimeter and call \"GEAR UP.\"","Attempt to establish visual contact."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH MAN.1.10).","src":"QRH MAN.1.10","ref":"Traffic Avoidance (climb RA in landing configuration)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Windshear Escape Maneuver (manual flight) (737 MAX non-normal maneuver)","a":"**When:** Windshear warning (two-tone siren followed by \"WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR\"), windshear encountered in flight, or unacceptable flight path deviations below 1000 feet AGL. (Predictive \"GO-AROUND, WINDSHEAR AHEAD\" on approach: escape maneuver or, at pilot's discretion, a normal go-around.)\n\n**Pilot Flying:**\n1. Disengage autopilot.\n2. Push either TO/GA switch.\n3. Aggressively apply maximum thrust.\n4. Disengage autothrottle.\n5. Simultaneously roll wings level and rotate toward an initial pitch attitude of 15°.\n6. Retract speedbrakes.\n7. Follow flight director TO/GA guidance (if available). Do not exceed the Pitch Limit Indication.\n\n**Pilot Monitoring:** Verify maximum thrust. Verify all needed actions have been completed and call out any omissions.\n\n**Notes:** Manual or automatic flight: do not change flap or gear configuration until windshear is no longer a factor; maintain wings level to maximize climb gradient, unless a turn is required for obstacle clearance; monitor vertical speed and altitude; do not attempt to regain lost airspeed until windshear is no longer a factor.","keys":[{"k":"Disengage autopilot.","m":["autopilot","auto pilot","a/p"]},{"k":"Push either TO/GA switch.","m":["toga","to/ga","to ga","takeoff go around"]},{"k":"Aggressively apply maximum thrust.","m":["maximum thrust","max thrust","full thrust"]},{"k":"Disengage autothrottle.","m":["autothrottle","auto throttle","a/t"]},{"k":"Simultaneously roll wings level and...","m":["15 degrees","15°","15 deg","fifteen degrees"]},{"k":"Retract speedbrakes.","m":["speedbrake","speed brake"]},{"k":"Follow flight director TO/GA guidance...","m":["flight director","fd guidance","pitch limit"]}],"src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"Windshear Escape Maneuver (manual flight)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-13","type":"order","q":"Put the Pilot Flying actions for Windshear Escape Maneuver (manual flight) in order.","items":["Disengage autopilot.","Push either TO/GA switch.","Aggressively apply maximum thrust.","Disengage autothrottle.","Simultaneously roll wings level and rotate toward an initial pitch attitude of 15°.","Retract speedbrakes.","Follow flight director TO/GA guidance (if available). Do not exceed the Pitch Limit Indication."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH MAN.1.14).","src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"Windshear Escape Maneuver (manual flight)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-18","type":"mcq","q":"Which trigger applies to Windshear Escape Maneuver (manual flight)?","o":["One or more of these occur: Engine indications are abnormal; Engine indications are rapidly approaching or exceeding limits; Abnormal engine noises are heard, possibly with airframe vibration; There is no response to thrust lever movement or the response is abnormal; Flames in the engine inlet or exhaust are reported.","Uncommanded stabilizer trim movement occurs continuously or in a manner not appropriate for flight conditions.","Windshear warning (two-tone siren followed by \"WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR\"), windshear encountered in flight, or unacceptable flight path deviations below 1000 feet AGL. (Predictive \"GO-AROUND, WINDSHEAR AHEAD\" on approach: escape maneuver or, at pilot's discretion, a normal go-around.)","Immediately do the following at the first indication of stall (buffet or stick shaker). Do all recoveries from approach to stall as if an actual stall has occurred."],"a":2,"why":"QRH MAN.1.14: Windshear warning (two-tone siren followed by \"WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR\"), windshear encountered in flight, or unacceptable flight path deviations below 1000 feet AGL. (Predictive \"GO-AROUND, WINDSHEAR AHEAD\" on approach: escape maneuver or, at pilot's discretion, a normal go-around.)","src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"Windshear Escape Maneuver (manual flight)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Windshear Escape Maneuver (automatic flight) (737 MAX non-normal maneuver)","a":"**When:** Windshear escape needed with the autopilot engaged.\n\n**Pilot Flying:**\n1. Push either TO/GA switch. (If TO/GA is not available, disengage autopilot and autothrottle and fly manually.)\n2. Verify TO/GA mode annunciation.\n3. Verify GA thrust.\n4. Retract speedbrakes.\n5. Monitor system performance. (Severe windshear can exceed the performance of the AFDS. Be prepared to disengage the autopilot and autothrottle and fly manually.)\n\n**Pilot Monitoring:** Verify GA thrust. Verify all needed actions have been completed and call out any omissions.","keys":[{"k":"Push either TO/GA switch","m":["toga","to/ga","to ga"]},{"k":"Verify TO/GA mode annunciation.","m":["mode annunciation","fma","annunciation"]},{"k":"Verify GA thrust.","m":["ga thrust","go around thrust"]},{"k":"Retract speedbrakes.","m":["speedbrake","speed brake"]},{"k":"Monitor system performance","m":["system performance","monitor"]}],"src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"Windshear Escape Maneuver (automatic flight)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-14","type":"order","q":"Put the Pilot Flying actions for Windshear Escape Maneuver (automatic flight) in order.","items":["Push either TO/GA switch. (If TO/GA is not available, disengage autopilot and autothrottle and fly manually.)","Verify TO/GA mode annunciation.","Verify GA thrust.","Retract speedbrakes.","Monitor system performance. (Severe windshear can exceed the performance of the AFDS. Be prepared to disengage the..."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH MAN.1.14).","src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"Windshear Escape Maneuver (automatic flight)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-21","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Upset Recovery: Nose High (737 MAX non-normal maneuver)","a":"**When:** Nose high upset (airplane diverting from the intended airplane state), airplane not stalled.\n\n**Pilot Flying:**\n1. Recognize and confirm the developing situation.\n2. Disengage autopilot. Disengage autothrottle.\n3. Recover: Apply nose-down elevator. Apply as much elevator as needed to obtain a nose down pitch rate.\n4. Apply appropriate nose down stabilizer trim.\n5. Reduce thrust.\n6. Roll (adjust bank angle) to obtain a nose down pitch rate.\n7. Complete the recovery: When approaching the horizon, roll to wings level. Check airspeed and adjust thrust. Establish pitch attitude.\n\n**Pilot Monitoring:** Call out attitude, airspeed and altitude throughout the recovery. Verify all needed actions have been done and call out any continued deviation.\n\n**Notes:** If needed, use minimal pitch trim during initial recovery. Careful use of rudder to aid roll control should be considered only if roll control is ineffective and the airplane is not stalled.","keys":[{"k":"Recognize and confirm the developing...","m":["recognize","recognise","confirm the developing"]},{"k":"Disengage autopilot","m":["autopilot","auto pilot","a/p"]},{"k":"Recover: Apply nose-down elevator","m":["nose down elevator","nose-down elevator","nose down pitch rate","push"]},{"k":"Apply appropriate nose down stabilizer...","m":["nose down stabilizer trim","nose down trim","stabilizer trim"]},{"k":"Reduce thrust.","m":["reduce thrust"]},{"k":"Roll (adjust bank angle) to obtain a...","m":["roll","bank angle"]},{"k":"Complete the recovery: When approaching...","m":["horizon","wings level"]}],"src":"QRH MAN.1.11","ref":"Upset Recovery: Nose High","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-15","type":"order","q":"Put the Pilot Flying actions for Upset Recovery: Nose High in order.","items":["Recognize and confirm the developing situation.","Disengage autopilot. Disengage autothrottle.","Recover: Apply nose-down elevator. Apply as much elevator as needed to obtain a nose down pitch rate.","Apply appropriate nose down stabilizer trim.","Reduce thrust.","Roll (adjust bank angle) to obtain a nose down pitch rate.","Complete the recovery: When approaching the horizon, roll to wings level. Check airspeed and adjust thrust...."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH MAN.1.11).","src":"QRH MAN.1.11","ref":"Upset Recovery: Nose High","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-22","type":"recall","q":"Memory items: Upset Recovery: Nose Low (737 MAX non-normal maneuver)","a":"**When:** Nose low upset (airplane diverting from the intended airplane state).\n\n**Pilot Flying:**\n1. Recognize and confirm the developing situation.\n2. Disengage autopilot. Disengage autothrottle.\n3. Recover: Recover from stall, if needed.\n4. Roll in the shortest direction to wings level. If bank angle is more than 90 degrees, unload and roll.\n5. Complete the Recovery: Apply nose up elevator.\n6. Apply nose up trim, if needed.\n7. Adjust thrust and drag, if needed.\n\n**Pilot Monitoring:** Call out attitude, airspeed and altitude throughout the recovery. Verify all needed actions have been done and call out any continued deviation.","keys":[{"k":"Recognize and confirm the developing...","m":["recognize","recognise","confirm the developing"]},{"k":"Disengage autopilot","m":["autopilot","auto pilot","a/p"]},{"k":"Recover: Recover from stall, if needed.","m":["recover from stall","stall"]},{"k":"Roll in the shortest direction to wings...","m":["wings level","shortest direction","unload"]},{"k":"Complete the Recovery: Apply nose up...","m":["nose up elevator"]},{"k":"Apply nose up trim, if needed.","m":["nose up trim"]},{"k":"Adjust thrust and drag, if needed.","m":["thrust and drag","drag"]}],"src":"QRH MAN.1.12","ref":"Upset Recovery: Nose Low","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-order-16","type":"order","q":"Put the Pilot Flying actions for Upset Recovery: Nose Low in order.","items":["Recognize and confirm the developing situation.","Disengage autopilot. Disengage autothrottle.","Recover: Recover from stall, if needed.","Roll in the shortest direction to wings level. If bank angle is more than 90 degrees, unload and roll.","Complete the Recovery: Apply nose up elevator.","Apply nose up trim, if needed.","Adjust thrust and drag, if needed."],"why":"Sequence as printed in the QRH (QRH MAN.1.12).","src":"QRH MAN.1.12","ref":"Upset Recovery: Nose Low","prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-19","type":"mcq","q":"Airspeed Unreliable, flaps extended: what gear up pitch attitude and thrust are set as immediate action items?","o":["10° and 80% N1","4° and 75% N1","15° and 85% N1","10° and 75% N1"],"a":0,"why":"Step 4: Flaps extended 10° and 80% N1; Flaps up 4° and 75% N1.","src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"Airspeed Unreliable (Stick Shaker Deactivation by Pulling Circuit Breaker)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-20","type":"mcq","q":"Airspeed Unreliable, flaps up: what gear up pitch attitude and thrust are set as immediate action items?","o":["10° and 80% N1","4° and 75% N1","4° and 80% N1","2.5° and 70% N1"],"a":1,"why":"Step 4: Flaps up 4° and 75% N1; Flaps extended 10° and 80% N1.","src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"Airspeed Unreliable (Stick Shaker Deactivation by Pulling Circuit Breaker)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-num-1","type":"num","q":"Airspeed Unreliable, flaps extended: gear up pitch attitude to set (degrees)?","ans":10,"unit":"°","why":"Flaps extended: 10° and 80% N1.","src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"Airspeed Unreliable (Stick Shaker Deactivation by Pulling Circuit Breaker)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-num-2","type":"num","q":"Airspeed Unreliable, flaps up: N1 to set (%)?","ans":75,"unit":"%","why":"Flaps up: 4° and 75% N1.","src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"Airspeed Unreliable (Stick Shaker Deactivation by Pulling Circuit Breaker)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-23","type":"recall","q":"Airspeed Unreliable: after the immediate action items, which indications does the QRH say are reliable?","a":"Step 6 (reference item): the following indications are reliable:\n- Attitude\n- N1\n- Ground speed\n- Radio altitude\n\nNote: Stick shaker, overspeed warning and AIRSPEED LOW alerts may sound erroneously or simultaneously. Step 5 before it: PROBE HEAT switches, Check ON.","keys":[{"k":"Attitude","m":["attitude"]},{"k":"N1","m":["n1"]},{"k":"Ground speed","m":["ground speed","groundspeed"]},{"k":"Radio altitude","m":["radio alt","rad alt"]}],"src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"Airspeed Unreliable (Stick Shaker Deactivation by Pulling Circuit Breaker)","asked":true,"prio":2,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-num-3","type":"num","q":"Emergency Descent: descend without delay to the lowest safe altitude or how many feet, whichever is higher?","ans":10000,"unit":"ft","why":"Step 3: Without delay, descend to the lowest safe altitude or 10,000 feet, whichever is higher.","src":"QRH 0.1","ref":"Emergency Descent","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-21","type":"mcq","q":"Emergency Descent: what target speed is set (structural integrity not in doubt)?","o":["Mmo/Vmo","250 knots","280 knots/M0.76","VREF 40 + 70"],"a":0,"why":"Step 7: Set target speed to Mmo/Vmo. If structural integrity is in doubt, limit speed as much as possible and avoid high maneuvering loads.","src":"QRH 0.1","ref":"Emergency Descent","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-22","type":"mcq","q":"Emergency Descent: which item marks the first reference item (after the dashed line)?","o":["When approaching the level off altitude: smoothly lower the SPEED BRAKE lever to DOWN and level off","SPEED BRAKE ... FLIGHT DETENT","Set target speed to Mmo/Vmo","ENGINE START switches (both) ... CONT"],"a":0,"why":"Steps 1 to 7 are immediate action items; step 8 (level off) is the first reference item.","src":"QRH 0.1","ref":"Emergency Descent","asked":true,"prio":2,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-23","type":"mcq","q":"CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING: when may oxygen masks be removed (cabin altitude controllable)?","o":["When the cabin altitude is at or below 10,000 feet","When the cabin altitude is at or below 14,000 feet","When the airplane is at or below FL100","When the cabin altitude is at or below 8,000 feet"],"a":0,"why":"Step 6 (reference item): When the cabin altitude is at or below 10,000 feet: Oxygen masks may be removed.","src":"QRH 2.2","ref":"CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING or Rapid Depressurization","asked":true,"prio":2,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-24","type":"mcq","q":"Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines: after the immediate items, what airspeed does the QRH set at or above FL270?","o":["275 knots","300 knots","250 knots","Best L/D (flaps up manoeuvre speed)"],"a":0,"why":"Step 5 (first reference item, after the dashed line): At or above FL270, set airspeed to 275 knots. Below FL270, set airspeed to 300 knots.","src":"QRH 7.7","ref":"Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines","asked":true,"prio":2,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-25","type":"mcq","q":"Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines: what airspeed is set below FL270?","o":["300 knots","275 knots","250 knots","220 knots"],"a":0,"why":"Step 5: Below FL270, 300 knots; at or above FL270, 275 knots.","src":"QRH 7.7","ref":"Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines","asked":true,"prio":2,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-num-4","type":"num","q":"Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines: within how many seconds must EGT increase before the start lever is cycled to CUTOFF then IDLE detent?","ans":30,"unit":"s","why":"Step 4: If EGT reaches a redline or there is no increase in EGT within 30 seconds: start lever CUTOFF, then IDLE detent.","src":"QRH 7.7","ref":"Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-26","type":"mcq","q":"Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines: what does the QRH say about starting the APU?","o":["Do not wait for a successful engine start before starting the APU","Start the APU only after both relights fail","Start the APU only below FL250","Do not start the APU until below 10,000 feet"],"a":0,"why":"Step 7: Do not wait for a successful engine start before starting the APU.","src":"QRH 7.7","ref":"Loss Of Thrust On Both Engines","asked":true,"prio":2,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-num-5","type":"num","q":"ENGINE FIRE: in flight, after how many seconds with the fire switch or ENG OVERHEAT light still illuminated is the fire switch rotated to the other stop?","ans":30,"unit":"s","why":"Step 7 (reference item): If after 30 seconds the engine fire switch or ENG OVERHEAT light stays illuminated: rotate to the other stop and hold for 1 second.","src":"QRH 8.3","ref":"ENGINE FIRE or Engine Severe Damage or Separation","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-num-6","type":"num","q":"Engine or APU fire switch: how long is it held after rotating to the stop?","ans":1,"unit":"s","why":"Rotate to the stop and hold for 1 second (ENGINE FIRE step 5, APU FIRE step 1).","src":"QRH 8.2","ref":"ENGINE FIRE or Engine Severe Damage or Separation","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-27","type":"mcq","q":"Aborted Engine Start: after CUTOFF, with the ENGINE START switch in GRD, what is done?","o":["Motor the engine for 60 seconds, then ENGINE START switch OFF","Motor the engine for 30 seconds, then OFF","Switch OFF immediately, then wait for N2 below 20%","Motor until EGT is below 150°C"],"a":0,"why":"Step 2: ENGINE START switch in GRD: Motor the engine for 60 seconds; ENGINE START switch (affected engine) OFF. If in OFF: after N2 decreases below 20%, GRD, motor 60 seconds, OFF.","src":"QRH 7.1","ref":"Aborted Engine Start","prio":2,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-28","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement about the CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING checklist vs the WARNING HORN (INTERMITTENT) checklist is correct?","o":["The WARNING HORN checklist sends the crew to CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING only in flight above 10,000 feet MSL","The intermittent horn in flight always means a takeoff configuration fault","The steady horn in flight is the cabin altitude warning","The WARNING HORN checklist applies only on the ground"],"a":0,"why":"QRH 15.2: in flight above 10,000 feet MSL, intermittent horn or CABIN ALTITUDE light: oxygen masks 100%, crew communications, go to CABIN ALTITUDE WARNING (2.1). On the ground with thrust levers advanced: takeoff configuration. A steady horn in flight is LANDING CONFIGURATION (15.1).","src":"QRH 15.2","ref":"WARNING HORN (INTERMITTENT) or WARNING LIGHT - CABIN ALTITUDE OR TAKEOFF CONFIGURATION","prio":2,"trap":true,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-num-7","type":"num","q":"GPWS Warning: initial pitch attitude to rotate to (degrees)?","ans":20,"unit":"°","why":"Simultaneously roll wings level and rotate to an initial pitch attitude of 20°.","src":"QRH MAN.1.3","ref":"GPWS Warning","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-num-8","type":"num","q":"Windshear Escape (manual): initial pitch attitude to rotate toward (degrees)?","ans":15,"unit":"°","why":"Simultaneously roll wings level and rotate toward an initial pitch attitude of 15°.","src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"Windshear Escape Maneuver (manual flight)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-29","type":"mcq","q":"GPWS pull up vs windshear escape: which pair of initial pitch targets is correct?","o":["GPWS 20°, windshear 15°","GPWS 15°, windshear 20°","Both 15°","Both 20°"],"a":0,"why":"GPWS Warning: initial pitch attitude of 20°. Windshear Escape: toward an initial pitch attitude of 15°.","src":"QRH MAN.1.3; MAN.1.14","ref":"GPWS Warning","asked":true,"prio":1,"trap":true,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-num-9","type":"num","q":"Rejected Takeoff: below what speed is the takeoff rejected for master caution, system failures, tire failure etc.?","ans":80,"unit":"kt","why":"Before 80 knots, reject for any of the listed items; above 80 knots and before V1 only for fire or fire warning, engine failure, predictive windshear warning, or airplane unsafe or unable to fly.","src":"QRH MAN.1.6","ref":"Rejected Takeoff","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-24","type":"recall","q":"Rejected Takeoff: above 80 knots and before V1, for what do you reject?","a":"Above 80 knots and before V1, reject the takeoff for any of the following:\n- fire or fire warning\n- engine failure\n- predictive windshear warning\n- if the airplane is unsafe or unable to fly.\n\nBefore 80 knots the list also includes master caution activation, system failure(s), unusual noise or vibration, tire failure, abnormally slow acceleration, takeoff configuration warning and a side window opening.","keys":[{"k":"Fire or fire warning","m":["fire"]},{"k":"Engine failure","m":["engine failure","engine fail"]},{"k":"Predictive windshear warning","m":["windshear","wind shear"]},{"k":"Unsafe or unable to fly","m":["unsafe","unable to fly"]}],"src":"QRH MAN.1.6","ref":"Rejected Takeoff","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-num-10","type":"num","q":"Rejected Takeoff: which speed does the First Officer call out during the RTO?","ans":60,"unit":"kt","why":"First Officer: Call out 60 knots.","src":"QRH MAN.1.8","ref":"Rejected Takeoff","asked":true,"prio":2,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-num-11","type":"num","q":"TCAS: below what height AGL must a DESCEND (fly down) RA not be followed?","ans":1000,"unit":"ft","why":"WARNING: Do not follow a DESCEND (fly down) RA issued below 1000 feet AGL.","src":"QRH MAN.1.9","ref":"Traffic Avoidance (RA, except a climb in landing configuration)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-30","type":"mcq","q":"TCAS climb RA in landing configuration: what flap setting is called?","o":["FLAPS 15","FLAPS 5","FLAPS 1","Flaps unchanged"],"a":0,"why":"PF advances thrust levers to ensure maximum thrust and calls for FLAPS 15; PM positions flap lever to 15 detent.","src":"QRH MAN.1.10","ref":"Traffic Avoidance (climb RA in landing configuration)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-31","type":"mcq","q":"TCAS: if the RA conflicts with an ATC instruction, what do you do?","o":["Comply with the RA","Comply with ATC","Follow the RA only if traffic is in sight","Comply with whichever gives more separation"],"a":0,"why":"WARNING: Comply with the RA if there is a conflict between the RA and air traffic control.","src":"QRH MAN.1.8","ref":"Traffic Avoidance (RA, except a climb in landing configuration)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-25","type":"recall","q":"Windshear: what are unacceptable flight path deviations (below 1000 feet AGL)?","a":"Uncontrolled changes from normal steady state flight conditions below 1000 feet AGL, in excess of any of the following:\n- 15 knots indicated airspeed\n- 500 fpm vertical speed\n- 5° pitch attitude\n- 1 dot displacement from the glideslope\n- unusual thrust lever position for a significant period of time.","keys":[{"k":"15 kt IAS","m":["15 knots","15 kt","15kt","15 kts"]},{"k":"500 fpm","m":["500"]},{"k":"5° pitch","m":["5 degrees","5°","5 deg","five degrees"]},{"k":"1 dot glideslope","m":["1 dot","one dot"]},{"k":"Unusual thrust lever position","m":["thrust lever position","unusual thrust"]}],"src":"QRH MAN.1.13","ref":"Windshear Escape Maneuver (manual flight)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-32","type":"mcq","q":"Predictive windshear warning during the takeoff roll: what is the action?","o":["Before V1 reject; after V1 perform the Windshear Escape Maneuver","Always continue and perform the escape maneuver","Reject at any speed","Before 80 knots reject; above 80 knots continue"],"a":0,"why":"\"WINDSHEAR AHEAD, WINDSHEAR AHEAD\" during takeoff roll: before V1, reject takeoff; after V1, perform the Windshear Escape Maneuver.","src":"QRH MAN.1.12","ref":"Windshear Escape Maneuver (manual flight)","asked":true,"prio":1,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-num-12","type":"num","q":"Windshear near rotation with insufficient runway to stop: initiate a normal rotation at least how many feet before the end of the runway?","ans":2000,"unit":"ft","why":"Initiate a normal rotation at least 2,000 feet before the end of the runway, even if airspeed is low.","src":"QRH MAN.1.13","ref":"Windshear Escape Maneuver (manual flight)","asked":true,"prio":2,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-recall-26","type":"recall","q":"Upset Recovery: how was an upset historically defined?","a":"Unintentionally exceeding one or more of:\n- Pitch attitude greater than 25 degrees nose up\n- Pitch attitude greater than 10 degrees nose down\n- Bank angle greater than 45 degrees\n- Less than the above but flying at an airspeed inappropriate for the conditions.\n\nNow an upset is any time the airplane is diverting from the intended airplane state.","keys":[{"k":"25° nose up","m":["25"]},{"k":"10° nose down","m":["10 degrees nose down","10° nose down","10 nose down","10 deg"]},{"k":"45° bank","m":["45"]},{"k":"Inappropriate airspeed","m":["inappropriate","airspeed"]}],"src":"QRH MAN.1.10","ref":"Upset Recovery: Nose High","prio":2,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-33","type":"mcq","q":"Nose Low Recovery: if bank angle is more than 90 degrees, what does the QRH say?","o":["Unload and roll","Roll first, then unload","Pull to the horizon, then roll","Apply rudder to roll"],"a":0,"why":"Roll in the shortest direction to wings level. If bank angle is more than 90 degrees, unload and roll.","src":"QRH MAN.1.12","ref":"Upset Recovery: Nose Low","prio":2,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"qrh-mcq-34","type":"mcq","q":"Under the current QRH paradigm, how are immediate action items separated from reference items?","o":["The last immediate action item is followed by a dashed horizontal line","Immediate action items are boxed","Immediate action items are printed in bold red","They are listed on a separate Quick Action Index page"],"a":0,"why":"CI.2.2: Immediate action items must be done before completing the rest of the checklist, either by memory or from an approved, immediately accessible reference such as a QRC. The last immediate action item is followed by a dashed horizontal line. (Revision: \"Memory Items\" renamed \"Immediate Action Items\".)","src":"QRH CI.2.2","ref":"Immediate Action and Reference Items","prio":2,"trap":true,"topic":20,"deck":"qrh"},{"id":"t1-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"Explain EDTO in your own words.","src":"OM-A 4.1.1, 4.1.2.2","ref":"EDTO brief 1","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"EDTO is any operation by a turbine aeroplane with two or more engines where the diversion time to an en-route alternate exceeds the threshold time set by the State.\n- For twins DGCA sets 60 min at the approved one-engine-inoperative speed, ISA, still air.\n- Beyond that we need DGCA approval, shown in our OpsSpecs.\n- Aircraft EDTO type-certified and maintained to the CMP standard.\n- EDTO alternates meeting dispatch minima through their validity windows; block fuel the greater of normal or EDTO critical fuel.\n- Our B737-8 MAX: 120 min at 0.79M/330 KIAS = 816 NM; most routes planned EDTO 90.","keys":[{"k":"60 min threshold","m":["60 min","60min","60 minutes","sixty minutes","one hour","1 hour"]},{"k":"OEI speed","m":["one engine inoperative","one-engine","oei","single engine","engine out"]},{"k":"Threshold set by the State","m":["state","dgca"]},{"k":"Approval in OpsSpecs","m":["opsspecs","ops specs","operations specifications","approval"]},{"k":"Critical fuel","m":["critical fuel"]},{"k":"120 min / 816 NM","m":["816","120 min","120 minutes"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"Why does EDTO exist? How does it manage the risk?","src":"OM-A 4.1.1, 4.1.1.2","ref":"EDTO brief 1","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"A twin has no spare engine after a failure. The longer it is from a runway, the longer it is exposed to a second failure on the remaining engine or a system that depends on it. EDTO manages that three ways:\n- **Reliable aircraft:** type design, CMP standard, engine and APU monitoring, IFSD rate limits.\n- **Nearby runways:** en-route alternates within the approved MDT, weather checked for the arrival time.\n- **Enough fuel:** critical fuel covers the worst diversion from the worst point.\nThe design leaves enough electrical, hydraulic and pneumatic power after one engine or primary system failure for safe flight and landing at an alternate.","keys":[{"k":"Exposure to second failure","m":["second failure","remaining engine","no spare engine"]},{"k":"Reliable aircraft / CMP","m":["reliab","cmp","type design"]},{"k":"Alternates within range","m":["alternate","runway"]},{"k":"Critical fuel","m":["critical fuel","enough fuel","fuel"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between ETOPS and EDTO? Why was the name changed?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.1, 4.1.2.2","ref":"EDTO brief 1","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **ETOPS:** Extended Twin Operations, twins more than 1 h from an alternate at OEI speed [OM-A 4.1.2.1].\n- **EDTO:** Extended Diversion Time Operations, aeroplanes with two or more turbine engines beyond the State's threshold time [OM-A 4.1.2.2].\n- ETOPS applied only to twins. ICAO introduced EDTO in Annex 6 to cover two or more engines under one framework, focusing on diversion time rather than engine count.\n- For three or more engines the trigger is 2 h at all-engine cruise speed.\n- The OM-A notes EDTO may be referred to as ETOPS in some documents. EDTO applies over water and remote land.","keys":[{"k":"ETOPS twins only","m":["twin","two engine"]},{"k":"EDTO two or more engines","m":["two or more","more than two","all aeroplanes","three or more"]},{"k":"ICAO Annex 6","m":["annex 6","icao"]},{"k":"Diversion time focus","m":["diversion time"]},{"k":"2 h for 3+ engines","m":["2 hours","two hours","120 min"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"What was the 60-minute rule?","src":"Standard theory; OM-A 4.1.2.23","ref":"EDTO brief 1","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"The older rule under which twins had to stay within 60 min of an adequate aerodrome. ETOPS and then EDTO replaced it by allowing twins to fly beyond 60 min with approval, provided extra reliability, maintenance, planning, fuel and crew requirements are met. Today the 60 min survives as the DGCA threshold time for twins.","keys":[{"k":"Twins within 60 min","m":["60 min","60 minutes","sixty"]},{"k":"Adequate aerodrome","m":["adequate","aerodrome","airport"]},{"k":"Replaced by ETOPS/EDTO with approval","m":["etops","edto","approval"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"Which regulations and company documents govern EDTO?","src":"OM-A 4.1.1.1, 4.1.2.11, 4.1.1.3, 4.4","ref":"EDTO brief 1","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- CAR Section 8, Series S, Part I (EDTO).\n- CAR Section 8, Series O, Part II (operations, fuel, critical fuel scenarios).\n- CAR Section 8, Series C, Part I (all-weather operations, take-off alternate).\n- ICAO Annex 6 Part I, Attachment C (critical fuel scenarios).\n- Company: OM-A Chapter 4, OM-D (training), the EDTO MEL, the CAME and the OpsSpecs.","keys":[{"k":"CAR Sec 8 Series S Part I","m":["series s"]},{"k":"Series O Part II","m":["series o"]},{"k":"Annex 6 Attachment C","m":["annex 6","attachment c"]},{"k":"OM-A Chapter 4","m":["om-a","oma","om a","chapter 4"]},{"k":"MEL / CAME / OpsSpecs","m":["mel","came","opsspecs","ops specs"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"Which CAR section and series covers EDTO?","src":"OM-A 4.1.1.1","ref":"EDTO brief 1","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["CAR Section 8, Series S, Part I","CAR Section 8, Series O, Part III","CAR Section 2, Series C, Part I","CAR Section 8, Series C, Part II"],"a":0,"why":"EDTO is CAR Sec 8 Series S Part I. Series O Part II covers operations and fuel; Series C Part I covers all-weather operations; Sec 2 Series C Part I is reliability reporting.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"Define threshold time. Who sets it? What is it for DGCA?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.23, 4.1.2.2","ref":"EDTO brief 2","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Threshold time is the range, in time, set by the State of the Operator to an en-route alternate, beyond which EDTO approval is needed.\n- DGCA: 60 min for twins, at the approved OEI speed, ISA, still air.\n- For three or more engines: 2 h at all-engine cruise speed.\n- Each State sets its own, so it is not the same everywhere.\n- For our MAX that is 420 NM at 0.79M/330 KIAS.","keys":[{"k":"Range in time to en-route alternate","m":["en-route alternate","en route alternate","alternate"]},{"k":"Set by State of the Operator","m":["state"]},{"k":"Beyond it EDTO approval needed","m":["approval"]},{"k":"60 min threshold","m":["60 min","60min","60 minutes","sixty minutes","one hour","1 hour"]},{"k":"2 h for 3+ engines","m":["2 hours","two hours"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"Why is a threshold time set at all? Is it the same in every State?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.23","ref":"EDTO brief 11","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"It defines the point beyond which a twin is far enough from a runway that the extra EDTO risk controls (reliability, alternates, critical fuel, procedures) must be applied under State approval. It is set by the State of the Operator, so it is not the same everywhere: ours is DGCA's 60 min for twins at OEI speed, ISA and still air.","keys":[{"k":"Beyond it, EDTO approval needed","m":["approval","edto"]},{"k":"Set by each State","m":["state","each state"]},{"k":"60 min threshold","m":["60 min","60min","60 minutes","sixty minutes","one hour","1 hour"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"Define maximum diversion time. What is it based on, and is it an operational limit?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.19, 4.2.4.2 (3)","ref":"EDTO brief 2","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Maximum diversion time is the maximum range, in time, from a point on the route to an en-route alternate.\n- DGCA grants it in the OpsSpecs (OM-A lists 75, 90, 120 or 180 min).\n- It must not exceed the aircraft's EDTO type-design capability, or the most limiting time-limited system in the AFM minus 15 min.\n- It is used to size the area of operation, not as an operational limit on an actual diversion.\n- Ours: 120 min.","keys":[{"k":"Max range in time to en-route alternate","m":["range in time","maximum time","from a point on the route"]},{"k":"Granted by DGCA in OpsSpecs","m":["dgca","opsspecs","ops specs"]},{"k":"Time-limited system minus 15 min","m":["minus 15","15 min","15 minutes","time limited","time-limited"]},{"k":"Sizes area, not limit on diversion","m":["area of operation","not an operational limit","not a limit"]},{"k":"120 min","m":["120 min","120min","120 minutes","two hours","2 hours"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"Define maximum diversion distance and the EDTO area of operation.","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.20, 4.1.2.7","ref":"EDTO brief 2","prio":2,"a":"- **Maximum diversion distance:** distance covered in ISA, still air, within the MDT at the selected OEI speed, including the descent to diversion altitude. Used to size the area of operation.\n- **EDTO area of operation:** the area beyond 60 min from an en-route alternate and within the MDT. Drawn as circles centred on the alternates, radius equal to the maximum diversion distance.\n- For the MAX: 816 NM at 120 min.","keys":[{"k":"ISA, still air","m":["still air","isa"]},{"k":"Within MDT at OEI speed","m":["maximum diversion time","mdt","oei"]},{"k":"Includes descent","m":["descent"]},{"k":"Circles on alternates","m":["circle","radius","ring"]},{"k":"Beyond 60 min, within MDT","m":["beyond 60","60 min","within the mdt"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"Define EEP, EDTO segment, ETP, CP and EXP.","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.5, 4.1.2.6, 4.1.2.8, 4.1.2.9, 4.1.2.10","ref":"EDTO brief 2","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **EEP:** first point on the route more than the threshold (60 min) from an en-route alternate at OEI speed, ISA, still air. Starts the EDTO segment.\n- **EDTO segment:** from the EEP until the route is back, and stays, within 60 min of an alternate. A route may have several.\n- **ETP:** point where flight time, with wind, to two selected alternates is equal.\n- **CP:** the ETP where EDTO diversion fuel is closest to, or exceeds, planned fuel on board. Usually the last ETP.\n- **EXP:** last point beyond the threshold time from an en-route alternate.","keys":[{"k":"EEP first point beyond 60 min","m":["first point","entry"]},{"k":"ETP equal time with wind","m":["equal time","equal flight time","equitime","equal"]},{"k":"CP fuel closest to / exceeds FOB","m":["closest","exceeds","fuel"]},{"k":"EXP last point beyond threshold","m":["last point","exit"]},{"k":"Segment EEP to back within 60","m":["segment"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match each EDTO point to its definition.","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.5, 4.1.2.8, 4.1.2.9, 4.1.2.10, 4.1.2.22","ref":"EDTO brief 2","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["EEP","First point more than 60 min from an en-route alternate (OEI, ISA, still air)"],["ETP","Equal flight time, with wind, to two selected alternates"],["CP","ETP where diversion fuel is closest to, or exceeds, planned FOB"],["EXP","Last point beyond the threshold time from an en-route alternate"],["PNR","Last point from which both destination and an available en-route alternate can be reached"]],"why":"OM-A 4.1.2 definitions. The ETP is about time, the CP about fuel.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between the CP and the PNR?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.9, 4.1.2.22","ref":"EDTO brief 2","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **CP (Critical Point):** an ETP, the one where EDTO diversion fuel is closest to, or exceeds, the planned fuel on board. It is a fuel concept, usually the last ETP, and it is not a reclearance point.\n- **PNR (Point of No Return):** the last geographic point from which the aircraft can reach both the destination and an available en-route alternate.\nSo the CP tells you where the critical fuel scenario is sized; the PNR tells you the last point at which both options remain open.","keys":[{"k":"CP is an ETP","m":["etp","equitime"]},{"k":"CP: diversion fuel closest to FOB","m":["closest","exceeds","critical fuel","fuel"]},{"k":"PNR last point","m":["last point","last geographic"]},{"k":"PNR reach destination and alternate","m":["both the destination","destination and","both"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"What is the one-engine-out diversion speed, and what is it used for?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.21, 4.3.4.2, 4.3.4.3, 4.3.6.1","ref":"EDTO brief 2, 7","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"It is a Mach/IAS combination: the Mach at the start of the diversion descent, then IAS once the transition point is reached. For the MAX it is 0.79M/330 KIAS. It is used for:\n1. establishing the area of operation;\n2. the single-engine critical fuel scenario;\n3. the net level-off altitude that clears en-route obstacles by the required margin.\nIt can be any speed from flaps-up (minimum manoeuvring) speed to MMO/VMO, with the live engine at MCT or less.","keys":[{"k":"Mach then IAS","m":["mach"]},{"k":"0.79M / 330 KIAS","m":["330","079","0.79"]},{"k":"Area of operation","m":["area of operation"]},{"k":"Critical fuel scenario","m":["fuel"]},{"k":"Obstacle clearance / net level-off","m":["obstacle","level-off","level off"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"Define en-route alternate, EDTO alternate, take-off alternate and isolated aerodrome.","src":"OM-A 4.3.2, 4.3.2.1, 4.3.2.2, 4.1.2.13, 4.1.2.16, 12.1.6","ref":"EDTO brief 2","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **En-route alternate:** an alternate you could land at if a diversion becomes necessary en route. Take-off and destination aerodromes may also count for EDTO.\n- **EDTO alternate:** an en-route alternate designated for an EDTO diversion that meets dispatch minima. A planning definition only: it does not limit the PIC in flight.\n- **Take-off alternate:** within 1 h at OEI speed (ISA, still air, actual TOM). For EDTO, if none exists, the first available within the MDT.\n- **Isolated aerodrome:** a destination with no suitable destination alternate. AIX does not operate to them.","keys":[{"k":"Departure/destination can count","m":["departure","destination","take-off and destination"]},{"k":"EDTO alternate meets dispatch minima","m":["dispatch minima","planning minima"]},{"k":"Planning only, not a PIC limit","m":["planning","does not limit"]},{"k":"Take-off alternate within 1 h OEI","m":["1 hour","one hour","60 min"]},{"k":"Isolated: no destination alternate","m":["no suitable destination alternate","no destination alternate","isolated"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"Adequate versus suitable aerodrome: what is the difference?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.13, 4.3.2.4","ref":"NR38","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Adequate:** meets landing performance and has the facilities, available at the expected time of use: ATC, lighting, communications, weather reporting, navaids (at least one let-down aid) and RFFS.\n- **Suitable:** an adequate aerodrome where forecast weather and field conditions are at or above the required minima for the expected time of use. For dispatch that means EDTO planning minima through the validity window.\n- OM-A terms: \"alternate aerodrome\" with the 4.3.2.4 checklist means adequate; \"EDTO alternate\" means suitable.\n- Every suitable airport is adequate, but not every adequate airport is suitable.","keys":[{"k":"Adequate: performance + facilities","m":["performance","facilit"]},{"k":"Available at expected time","m":["available","time of use"]},{"k":"Suitable: adequate plus weather","m":["weather"]},{"k":"At or above minima","m":["minima"]},{"k":"OM-A EDTO alternate = suitable","m":["edto alternate"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"An examiner asks what a \"sufficient\" aerodrome is. How do you answer?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.13; Pending: CAR Sec 8 Series S Part I definitions","ref":"EDTO brief 2","trap":true,"prio":3,"a":"\"Sufficient\" is not a defined term in our OM-A. I would say I know adequate (performance, facilities, available at the time) and suitable (adequate plus weather and field at or above minima), and that the OM-A itself uses \"alternate aerodrome\" and \"EDTO alternate\" instead of the older ETOPS terms.","keys":[{"k":"Not a defined term","m":["not defined","not a defined","no definition"]},{"k":"Adequate","m":["adequate"]},{"k":"Suitable","m":["suitable"]},{"k":"OM-A uses EDTO alternate","m":["edto alternate"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"Define EDTO critical fuel, EDTO dispatch planning minima and validity period.","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.11, 4.1.2.14, 4.1.2.15","ref":"EDTO brief 2","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **EDTO critical fuel:** fuel to fly to an en-route alternate from the most critical point, with the most limiting system failure (ICAO Annex 6 Part I, Attachment C).\n- **Dispatch planning minima:** additives to published minima (or fixed values) so planning is more conservative than the actual approach minima.\n- **Validity period (time window):** earliest to latest expected arrival time at each EDTO alternate, based on diversions from its first and last ETPs.","keys":[{"k":"From most critical point","m":["most critical point","critical point"]},{"k":"Most limiting failure","m":["most limiting","failure"]},{"k":"Additives to published minima","m":["additive","added","more conservative"]},{"k":"Earliest to latest ETA","m":["earliest","latest"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"What is an EDTO significant system? What is the CMP standard? What is an IFSD?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.12, 4.1.2.17, 4.1.2.18, 4.2.4.2","ref":"EDTO brief 2","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **EDTO significant system:** a system whose failure could affect safety particular to EDTO, or whose working is important for safe flight and landing during an EDTO diversion. The propulsion system is one.\n- **CMP:** Configuration, Maintenance and Procedures standard: minimum configuration, special inspections, life limits, MMEL constraints and practices that make the airframe-engine combination fit for EDTO.\n- **IFSD:** in-flight shutdown for any cause: flameout, failure, crew action, FOD, icing, or inability to control thrust.","keys":[{"k":"Significant: safety particular to EDTO","m":["particular to edto","safe flight and landing","diversion"]},{"k":"Configuration Maintenance Procedures","m":["configuration","maintenance and procedures"]},{"k":"Airframe-engine combination","m":["airframe","engine combination"]},{"k":"IFSD in-flight shutdown any cause","m":["in-flight shutdown","in flight shutdown","any cause"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-1","type":"num","q":"B737-8 MAX: threshold distance (60 min) at the approved OEI speed?","src":"OM-A 4.3.5.4","ref":"EDTO brief 3","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":420,"unit":"NM","why":"60 min at 0.79M/330 KIAS = 420 NM (B737-800: 431 NM).","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-2","type":"num","q":"B737-8 MAX: maximum diversion distance for 120 min?","src":"OM-A 4.3.5.4, 4.6.4","ref":"EDTO brief 3","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":816,"unit":"NM","why":"120 min at 0.79M/330 KIAS = 816 NM (B737-800: 842 NM).","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-3","type":"num","q":"B737-8 MAX: diversion distance for 90 min (EDTO 90)?","src":"OM-A 4.3.5.4","ref":"EDTO brief 3","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":618,"unit":"NM","why":"90 min = 618 NM at 0.79M/330 KIAS.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-4","type":"num","q":"AIX B737-8 MAX approved maximum diversion time?","src":"OM-A 4.3.5.4","ref":"EDTO brief 3","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":120,"unit":"min","why":"DGCA approval in the OpsSpecs: 120 min (816 NM).","topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"What is the approved one-engine-inoperative diversion speed for the AIX B737-8 MAX?","src":"OM-A 4.3.5.4, 4.6.5.3","ref":"EDTO brief 3","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["0.79M / 330 KIAS","0.78M / 290 KIAS","0.79M / 310 KIAS","0.82M / 340 KIAS"],"a":0,"why":"0.79M/330 KIAS is used for all flight-plan EDTO calculations. 290 and 310 are fixed-speed diversion options.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-5","type":"num","q":"B737-8 MAX EDTO reference weight?","src":"OM-A 4.3.5.2","ref":"EDTO brief 3","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":75000,"unit":"kg","why":"75,000 kg for the MAX (70,000 kg for the B737-800). Area of operation is computed once at this weight.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-6","type":"num","q":"Class C cargo fire suppression time on the B737 MAX?","src":"OM-A 4.6.5.2","ref":"EDTO brief 3","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":195,"unit":"min","why":"195 min, the most time-limited system. The MDT must not exceed it minus 15 min.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"Aeroplane RFFS category of the B737-8 MAX, and the minimum RFFS needed at an EDTO alternate?","src":"OM-A 17.1.2.3, 4.5.1 (10)","ref":"EDTO brief 3","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Cat 7; Cat 6 at EDTO alternates","Cat 7; Cat 4 at EDTO alternates","Cat 8; Cat 7 at EDTO alternates","Cat 6; Cat 5 at EDTO alternates"],"a":0,"why":"MAX is Cat 7. EDTO alternates need one category below the aeroplane (Cat 6), never below Cat 4.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-7","type":"num","q":"Dep-to-dest distance below which a MAX may continue with the APU running when an alternate is lost before the EEP?","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.4.3","ref":"EDTO brief 3","prio":2,"ans":1632,"unit":"NM","why":"Less than 1632 NM (exactly 2 x 816 NM), so every point is within 120 min of departure or destination. B737-800: 1684 NM.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-8","type":"num","q":"Planned diversion cruise level in the OFP EDTO table (over water, no obstacle limit)?","src":"OM-A 4.7.3","ref":"EDTO brief 3","prio":2,"ans":100,"unit":"FL","why":"FL100. Diversion legs are planned at FL100.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"What is the EDTO reference weight and how is it derived?","src":"OM-A 4.3.5.1, 4.3.5.2","ref":"EDTO brief 3","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"The reference weight is the highest estimated gross weight at the critical points of the routes in the area, taking off at MTOW with a standard speed schedule, in ISA and still air. DGCA approves it, and the area of operation is computed once at that weight; it is not recomputed per flight. For the MAX it is 75,000 kg (70,000 kg for the -800).","keys":[{"k":"Highest weight at critical points","m":["highest","critical point"]},{"k":"From MTOW","m":["mtow","maximum take-off","maximum takeoff"]},{"k":"ISA, still air","m":["still air","isa"]},{"k":"Computed once, not per flight","m":["once","not recomputed","not per flight"]},{"k":"75,000 kg","m":["75000","75 000","75 tonnes","75 tons"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"How is the 420 NM threshold distance derived, and why is it more than a level cruise at FL100 would give?","src":"OM-A 4.3.5.3","ref":"EDTO brief 3","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Distance is the OEI TAS at the optimum diversion level for the reference weight, multiplied by the time. The rules let you count the faster descent segment too, which is why 420 NM in 60 min is more than a level cruise at FL100 would give. As mental arithmetic, 420 NM per 60 min is 7 NM per minute, about 420 kt average including the descent.","keys":[{"k":"OEI TAS x time","m":["tas","true airspeed"]},{"k":"Reference weight / optimum level","m":["reference weight","optimum"]},{"k":"Descent segment counted","m":["descent"]},{"k":"7 NM per minute","m":["7 nm","7nm","seven nm","seven nautical","420 kt","420 knots"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"Why is our maximum diversion time 120 min? What limits it?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.19, 4.2.4.2 (3), 4.6.5.2","ref":"EDTO brief 3","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"120 min is the DGCA approval in our OpsSpecs. The MDT must not exceed the aircraft's EDTO type-design capability, or the most limiting time-limited system in the AFM minus 15 min. With 195 min Class C cargo fire suppression the system limit alone would allow 180 min. So 120 min reflects what AIX applied for and what the route network needs, not a system limit.","keys":[{"k":"DGCA approval / OpsSpecs","m":["dgca","opsspecs","ops specs","approval"]},{"k":"Time-limited system minus 15","m":["minus 15","15 min","time limited","time-limited"]},{"k":"Cargo fire 195 min","m":["195","cargo fire"]},{"k":"Not a system limit / network","m":["network","applied for","not a system limit"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-21","type":"recall","q":"What is the most time-limited system for EDTO, and why is it not oxygen?","src":"OM-A 4.6.5.2","ref":"EDTO brief 3","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"Class C cargo fire suppression, 195 min. Oxygen only has to cover the emergency descent to FL100 (or up to FL150 with supplemental oxygen); after that you cruise unpressurised at a breathable level. A cargo fire keeps burning until you land, so the extinguishing agent must last the whole diversion.","keys":[{"k":"Cargo fire suppression","m":["cargo fire","cargo"]},{"k":"195 min","m":["195"]},{"k":"Oxygen only for descent","m":["descent","fl100","fl 100"]},{"k":"Agent must last whole diversion","m":["until you land","whole diversion","until landing"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["oxygen is the most","oxygen system is"],"why":"Oxygen only covers the descent; cargo fire suppression (195 min) is the time-limited system."}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"Why is the 1632 NM figure significant for a MAX?","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.4.3","ref":"EDTO brief 3","prio":2,"o":["It is 2 x 816 NM, so every point is within 120 min of departure or destination","It is the maximum range of the MAX with reserves","It is 4 x 420 NM, the threshold ring limit","It is the distance at which the APU must be started"],"a":0,"why":"If departure and destination are closer than 1632 NM, every point on the route is within 120 min of one of them.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-22","type":"recall","q":"What four approvals or requirements does EDTO rest on?","src":"OM-A 4.2.1, 4.2.2, 4.2.4.1, 4.4","ref":"EDTO brief 4","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"1. **Aircraft type design:** Boeing's EDTO type-design approval for the B737-8 MAX, standard in the AFM, aircraft to CMP standard.\n2. **Operator approval:** DGCA, endorsed on the AOC in the OpsSpecs.\n3. **Maintenance programme (CAME):** PDSC, dual maintenance limits, verification, reliability reporting.\n4. **Crew and dispatch training** per OM-D.\nRemove any one and the flight is not EDTO-capable.","keys":[{"k":"Type design / AFM","m":["type design","afm","boeing"]},{"k":"DGCA operator approval / OpsSpecs","m":["dgca","opsspecs","ops specs","operator approval"]},{"k":"Maintenance / CAME","m":["maintenance","came"]},{"k":"Training / OM-D","m":["training","om-d","omd","om d"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-23","type":"recall","q":"What must the AFM show for EDTO type design?","src":"OM-A 4.2.7","ref":"EDTO brief 4","prio":3,"a":"- The maximum flight time with one engine inoperative for which system reliability is approved.\n- The additional equipment fitted for EDTO.\n- EDTO performance data and procedures.\n- A statement that meeting these criteria does not by itself approve EDTO.","keys":[{"k":"Max OEI flight time approved","m":["maximum flight time","one engine"]},{"k":"Additional equipment","m":["equipment"]},{"k":"Performance data and procedures","m":["performance","procedure"]},{"k":"Not by itself an approval","m":["does not by itself","not by itself","not an approval","not approve"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-24","type":"recall","q":"Where do you find the company threshold time and MDT? What do the OpsSpecs show for EDTO?","src":"OM-A 4.1.1.3, 4.1.3, 4.7.2.1 (c)","ref":"EDTO brief 4","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"In the Operations Specifications endorsed on the AOC, and in the OM-A 4.1.3 table. The OpsSpecs cover:\n- the airframe-engine combination;\n- the authorised area of operation;\n- the maximum diversion time at the approved OEI speed;\n- the threshold time (may also be given in NM).\nAt the aircraft, check that the OpsSpecs for that registration show EDTO approval.","keys":[{"k":"OpsSpecs on the AOC","m":["opsspecs","ops specs","operations specifications","aoc"]},{"k":"Airframe-engine combination","m":["airframe","engine combination"]},{"k":"Area of operation","m":["area of operation"]},{"k":"MDT at OEI speed","m":["maximum diversion time","mdt"]},{"k":"Threshold time","m":["threshold"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-25","type":"recall","q":"What did DGCA consider when granting AIX its EDTO approval?","src":"OM-A 4.2.2","ref":"EDTO brief 4","prio":3,"a":"DGCA looked at the area of operation, the en-route alternates, the critical fuel scenario, AIX training and maintenance programmes, and previous EDTO experience. The approval is endorsed on the AOC in the Operations Specifications, which list the airframe-engine combination, the authorised area, the MDT at the approved OEI speed and the threshold time.","keys":[{"k":"Area of operation","m":["area"]},{"k":"En-route alternates","m":["alternate"]},{"k":"Critical fuel","m":["fuel"]},{"k":"Training and maintenance","m":["training","maintenance"]},{"k":"Previous experience","m":["experience"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-26","type":"recall","q":"What is the PDSC?","src":"OM-A 4.2.4.1","ref":"EDTO brief 4","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"The EDTO pre-departure service check, done immediately before each EDTO flight and certified by an authorised PDSC signatory. It covers:\n- the condition of all EDTO significant systems;\n- a review of maintenance records;\n- an interior and exterior inspection, including engine and APU oil levels and consumption.","keys":[{"k":"Pre-departure service check","m":["pre-departure","pre departure","service check"]},{"k":"Before each EDTO flight","m":["before each","immediately before"]},{"k":"EDTO significant systems","m":["significant system"]},{"k":"Oil levels / records","m":["oil","records"]},{"k":"Authorised signatory","m":["signatory","certif"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-27","type":"recall","q":"What are the dual maintenance limits and the verification programme?","src":"OM-A 4.2.4.1","ref":"EDTO brief 4","prio":2,"a":"- **Dual maintenance:** the same or a similar EDTO significant system on both sides must not be worked on in the same visit. If unavoidable, use different AMEs (or one AME under direct supervision), followed by a verification test.\n- **Verification programme:** after maintenance on an EDTO significant system, a ground check and, if required, a verification flight. It can be done on a revenue EDTO flight if signed off as complete before the EEP.","keys":[{"k":"Not both sides same visit","m":["both sides","same visit","same or similar"]},{"k":"Different AMEs","m":["different ame","different engineer","supervision"]},{"k":"Verification flight","m":["verification flight","verification"]},{"k":"Signed off before EEP","m":["before the eep","before eep"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-28","type":"recall","q":"What is EDTO reliability reporting? What must be reported, and within what time?","src":"OM-A 4.2.4.1 (7)","ref":"EDTO brief 4","prio":2,"a":"Report within 72 h to the DGCA regional office:\n- IFSDs;\n- diversions and turnbacks for technical reasons;\n- uncommanded thrust changes or surges;\n- inability to control an engine;\n- fuel loss or imbalance that can't be corrected;\n- EDTO significant system failures;\n- anything that jeopardises safe flight and landing.","keys":[{"k":"72 h","m":["72 h","72h","72 hours","seventy two"]},{"k":"DGCA regional office","m":["dgca","regional"]},{"k":"IFSDs","m":["ifsd","shutdown"]},{"k":"Diversions / turnbacks","m":["diversion","turnback"]},{"k":"Uncommanded thrust / surge","m":["thrust","surge"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-match-2","type":"match","q":"IFSD rate triggers (12-month rolling, whole EDTO fleet): match the approval band to the rate per 1,000 engine hours.","src":"OM-A 4.2.4.1 (8)","ref":"EDTO brief 4","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Up to 120 min","0.05"],["120 to 180 min","0.03"],["Beyond 180 min","0.01"]],"why":"Exceeding the rate triggers a full review.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-29","type":"recall","q":"Besides the PDSC and reliability reporting, what other EDTO maintenance programmes exist?","src":"OM-A 4.2.4.1","ref":"EDTO brief 4","prio":3,"a":"- Engine condition monitoring, keeping engine-limit margins for a long single-engine diversion.\n- Oil consumption monitoring, including the APU if it is required.\n- APU in-flight start (cold soak) programme.\n- EDTO parts control programme.\n- Centralised maintenance control.\n- EDTO maintenance training.","keys":[{"k":"Engine condition monitoring","m":["condition monitoring","engine condition"]},{"k":"Oil consumption","m":["oil"]},{"k":"APU in-flight start","m":["apu","cold soak"]},{"k":"Parts control","m":["parts"]},{"k":"Maintenance training","m":["training"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-30","type":"recall","q":"How does the EDTO MEL differ from the normal MEL, and what does the MMEL consider?","src":"OM-A 4.3.8, 4.3.8.2","ref":"EDTO brief 4","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"The MEL is built from the EDTO MMEL and can only be more restrictive. It adds AIX network factors: diversion times, alternate equipment, navigation and communication means, and typical weather. The MMEL considers longer diversion times, what still works in emergency electrical configuration, crew workload and cabin environment after a further failure, and icing being more likely at low diversion levels.","keys":[{"k":"More restrictive","m":["more restrictive","restrictive"]},{"k":"Built from EDTO MMEL","m":["mmel"]},{"k":"Network factors","m":["diversion time","network","weather"]},{"k":"Emergency electrical config","m":["emergency electrical","electrical"]},{"k":"Icing at low levels","m":["icing","ice"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-31","type":"recall","q":"Which systems have a fundamental influence on EDTO safety (the EDTO MEL system groups)? What equipment is mandatory for EDTO?","src":"OM-A 4.3.8.4","ref":"EDTO brief 4","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"OM-A 4.3.8.4 lists 14 groups:\n- electrical including battery; hydraulic; pneumatic;\n- flight instruments; fuel; flight controls; ice protection;\n- engine start and ignition; propulsion instruments;\n- navigation and communication; APU;\n- air conditioning and pressurisation; cargo fire suppression; emergency equipment.","keys":[{"k":"Electrical incl. battery","m":["electrical","battery"]},{"k":"Hydraulic","m":["hydraulic"]},{"k":"Fuel","m":["fuel"]},{"k":"APU","m":["apu"]},{"k":"Cargo fire suppression","m":["cargo fire"]},{"k":"Nav and comm","m":["navigation","communication"]},{"k":"Pressurisation / air con","m":["pressuri","air condition"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-32","type":"recall","q":"What are the PIC's EDTO duties when planning or flying EDTO?","src":"OM-A 4.2.4.2 (4)","ref":"EDTO brief 4","prio":2,"a":"- Consider the MEL, comms, navaids, fuel and oil, alternates and performance.\n- After an engine shutdown, land at the nearest (least flying time) en-route alternate where a safe landing can be made.\n- After any EDTO significant system failure, do the same, unless no substantial loss of safety results from continuing.","keys":[{"k":"MEL, comms, navaids, fuel, alternates","m":["mel","navaid","comms","alternate"]},{"k":"Engine shutdown: nearest by time","m":["least flying time","nearest","flying time"]},{"k":"Significant system failure: same","m":["significant system"]},{"k":"Unless no substantial loss of safety","m":["substantial"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-33","type":"recall","q":"What is the EDTO communications requirement?","src":"OM-A 4.2.6, 4.5.1","ref":"EDTO brief 4","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"At least one voice system wherever voice is available, and reliable two-way voice communication with ATC along the route at the normal OEI cruise altitude, in normal propagation conditions. Dispatch also requires two-way communication with the operations centre, non-visual navaids for the route and approach aids at the alternates. In flight, Flight Dispatch and IOCC provide flight watch by HF, VHF or ACARS.","keys":[{"k":"Two-way voice with ATC","m":["two-way","two way","voice"]},{"k":"At OEI cruise altitude","m":["oei","one engine","engine out"]},{"k":"Normal propagation","m":["propagation"]},{"k":"Operations centre","m":["operations centre","operations center","company"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-34","type":"recall","q":"When can an EDTO flight be dispatched? What if a condition fails?","src":"OM-A 4.5.3.2","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Only when four conditions hold:\n- the aircraft is EDTO-capable;\n- every EDTO alternate meets dispatch minima for its whole validity window;\n- block fuel is the greater of normal fuel and EDTO critical fuel;\n- the route stays inside the area of operation.\nIf any fails, the flight is re-planned to stay within 60 min of an aerodrome.","keys":[{"k":"Aircraft EDTO-capable","m":["capable","aircraft"]},{"k":"Alternates meet dispatch minima through window","m":["dispatch minima","window","planning minima"]},{"k":"Greater of normal and critical fuel","m":["greater","critical fuel"]},{"k":"Inside area of operation","m":["area of operation"]},{"k":"Else re-plan within 60 min","m":["60 min","within 60","non-edto","non edto"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-35","type":"recall","q":"How do you choose an EDTO alternate? What must it have at the expected time of use?","src":"OM-A 4.3.2.4, 4.5.1 (10), 7.5","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Must have at the expected time of use:\n- the airport available, with overflight and landing permission;\n- ATC, MET, AIS and lighting;\n- navaids (ILS, VOR, NDB);\n- RFFS at least one category below the aeroplane (Cat 6 for the MAX), never below Cat 4; outside India, 30 min notice;\n- weather at or above dispatch minima.\nAlso considered: technical support, ground comms, medical facilities, handling and fuel, passenger accommodation.","keys":[{"k":"Available + permissions","m":["permission","available"]},{"k":"ATC / MET / lighting","m":["atc","met","lighting"]},{"k":"Navaids","m":["navaid","ils","vor"]},{"k":"RFFS Cat 6","m":["rffs","fire","cat 6","category 6"]},{"k":"Weather at dispatch minima","m":["dispatch minima","weather","minima"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-36","type":"recall","q":"What RFFS is required at an EDTO alternate?","src":"OM-A 4.5.1 (10), 17.1.2.3","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"At least one category below the aeroplane's RFFS category, never below Cat 4. The B737-8 MAX is Cat 7, so EDTO alternates need Cat 6. Outside India, 30 min notice to the aerodrome operator. Note the general en-route rule (Cat 4 with 30 min notice, OM-A 17.1.2.3) is different: quote the EDTO rule for EDTO questions.","keys":[{"k":"One category below","m":["one category below","one below","1 below"]},{"k":"Never below Cat 4","m":["cat 4","category 4"]},{"k":"Cat 6 for MAX","m":["cat 6","category 6"]},{"k":"30 min notice","m":["30 min","30 minutes"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["cat 4 is enough","category 4 is enough"],"why":"Cat 4 with notice is the general en-route rule; EDTO alternates need one below the aeroplane (Cat 6)."}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-9","type":"num","q":"Notice required to the aerodrome operator for RFFS at an EDTO alternate outside India?","src":"OM-A 4.5.1 (10)","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":30,"unit":"min","why":"30 min notice outside India.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-37","type":"recall","q":"State the EDTO dispatch (planning) minima for alternates.","src":"OM-A 7.5","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **One approach:** DA/MDA + 400 ft and minimum + 1500 m.\n- **Two approaches to different suitable runways (separate aids):** DA/MDA + 200 ft (on the higher of the two) and minimum + 800 m.\n- **Two approaches, one CAT II:** 300 ft and RVR 1200 m.\n- **Two approaches, one CAT III:** 200 ft and RVR 550 m.\nPROB40 and TEMPO below planning minima must be considered; wind including gusts within limits; MEL effects applied.","keys":[{"k":"+400 ft / +1500 m","m":["400"]},{"k":"+1500 m","m":["1500"]},{"k":"+200 ft / +800 m","m":["800"]},{"k":"CAT II 300 ft / 1200 m","m":["1200"]},{"k":"CAT III 200 ft / 550 m","m":["550"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-match-3","type":"match","q":"Match the alternate's facilities to the EDTO planning minima.","src":"OM-A 7.5","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["One approach","DA/MDA + 400 ft, minimum + 1500 m"],["Two approaches, different runways, separate aids","DA/MDA + 200 ft, minimum + 800 m"],["Two approaches, one CAT II","300 ft, RVR 1200 m"],["Two approaches, one CAT III","200 ft, RVR 550 m"]],"why":"OM-A 7.5 planning minima.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-10","type":"num","q":"EDTO planning minima: alternate with only an ILS CAT I, DA 200 ft. Required forecast ceiling?","src":"OM-A 7.5","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":600,"unit":"ft","why":"One approach: DA + 400 ft = 600 ft.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-11","type":"num","q":"EDTO planning minima: alternate with only an ILS CAT I, RVR 550 m. Required forecast visibility?","src":"OM-A 7.5","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":2050,"unit":"m","why":"One approach: minimum + 1500 m = 2050 m.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-38","type":"recall","q":"Why are EDTO planning minima higher than landing minima? Which minima apply in an actual diversion?","src":"OM-A 4.3.3.1, 4.3.3.1 Note, 4.6.2.4.3","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Weather varies over the hours between planning and a possible diversion, so the margin makes sure an approach will still be possible if you need it. The dispatch minima are planning-only. In an actual diversion, normal company landing minima decide. Airborne before the EEP, the test is the forecast at or above normal landing minima.","keys":[{"k":"Weather varies over time","m":["weather var","varies","hours","change"]},{"k":"Planning only","m":["planning"]},{"k":"Normal landing minima in flight","m":["landing minima","normal minima"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-39","type":"recall","q":"How is the validity window of an EDTO alternate calculated?","src":"OM-A 4.3.3.2","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Earliest ETA:** ETD + normal flight time to the ETP with the previous alternate + diversion time from that ETP at normal cruise speed and level.\n- **Latest ETA:** ETD + normal flight time to the ETP with the next alternate + diversion time from that ETP at LRC at FL100 (or MORA).\n- Departure airport as alternate: window starts at ETD.\n- Destination as alternate: window ends at the later of the normal ETA and the diversion from the last ETP at LRC, FL100 or MORA.\nWeather must be at or above planning minima for the whole window.","keys":[{"k":"Earliest via previous ETP","m":["previous etp","first etp","earliest"]},{"k":"Normal cruise for earliest","m":["normal cruise"]},{"k":"Latest via next ETP","m":["next etp","last etp","latest"]},{"k":"LRC at FL100 for latest","m":["lrc","long range","fl100","fl 100"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-12","type":"num","q":"Validity window: ETD 1000Z, ETP A/B reached at 1100Z, diversion to B at normal cruise 70 min. Earliest ETA at B (answer as HHMM, e.g. 1210)?","src":"OM-A 4.3.3.2","ref":"EDTO brief 5","prio":2,"ans":1210,"unit":"Z","why":"1100Z + 70 min = 1210Z.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-13","type":"num","q":"Validity window: ETP B/C reached at 1200Z, diversion to B at LRC and FL100 takes 95 min. Latest ETA at B (HHMM)?","src":"OM-A 4.3.3.2","ref":"EDTO brief 5","prio":2,"ans":1335,"unit":"Z","why":"1200Z + 95 min = 1335Z. B's TAF must be at or above planning minima 1210Z to 1335Z.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-40","type":"recall","q":"Do you add a buffer (e.g. 1 h) either side of the EDTO validity window?","src":"OM-A 4.3.3.2; Standard theory","ref":"EDTO brief 5","trap":true,"prio":3,"a":"The OM-A defines only the earliest-to-latest ETA window, so that is what I quote. Some operators add a buffer either side, commonly 1 h, but that is standard theory, not in our OM-A; any company buffer would be in the Flight Dispatch procedures.","keys":[{"k":"OM-A: earliest to latest only","m":["earliest","latest","only"]},{"k":"1 h buffer is other operators","m":["1 hour","one hour","other operators","some operators"]},{"k":"Not in OM-A","m":["not in","om-a","oma"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-41","type":"recall","q":"What does the dispatcher check, and what must the release show, for an EDTO flight?","src":"OM-A 4.5.1, 4.5.2.1, 4.5.1 (11)","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Aircraft status:** EDTO configuration, MEL, CDL and defects from the latest MCC status message; re-route if needed.\n- **Comms and nav:** two-way voice at OEI altitude, two-way with the operations centre, non-visual navaids for the route, approach aids at the alternates.\n- **Fuel and performance:** fuel to the most critical point and a diversion from it, alternate performance data, driftdown and cruise altitude coverage.\n- **Release:** must list the EDTO alternates and the authorised EDTO diversion time.","keys":[{"k":"Aircraft status / MEL","m":["mel","status","mcc"]},{"k":"Comms at OEI altitude","m":["two-way","two way","voice"]},{"k":"Fuel to critical point","m":["critical point","fuel"]},{"k":"Release lists EDTO alternates","m":["alternates"]},{"k":"Authorised diversion time","m":["diversion time"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-42","type":"recall","q":"What EDTO documents does the crew receive?","src":"OM-A 4.5.5","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **CFP (OFP)** on forecast winds and temperatures, showing EEP, EXP and ETPs, the validity window for each alternate, and EDTO fuel from each ETP to both alternates for each scenario.\n- **Plotting chart** with circles on the alternates, non-authorised areas hatched, route, EEP, EXP, ETPs and CP marked.\n- **Weather:** TAFs covering each window, SIGWX (turbulence, icing), wind and temperature at FL100, typical OEI level and normal cruise levels.\n- Also Tech Log EDTO status and OpsSpecs.","keys":[{"k":"OFP/CFP with EEP/EXP/ETPs","m":["ofp","cfp","flight plan"]},{"k":"Plotting chart with circles","m":["plotting","chart","circle"]},{"k":"TAFs for windows","m":["taf"]},{"k":"SIGWX","m":["sigwx","significant weather"]},{"k":"Winds at FL100","m":["fl100","fl 100","wind"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-43","type":"recall","q":"Walk me through the EDTO table on the OFP.","src":"OM-A Annex A","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **AT POSN:** ETP lat/long. **ETOPS 60/90 TAS:** OEI TAS setting threshold and MDT radius.\n- **ETP / GO XXXX:** ETP and the alternates either side. **ALTN (TIME):** each alternate with its weather window.\n- **TRK, DIST, FL, TIME:** diversion leg planned at FL100. **SHT:** highest terrain within 10 NM + 2000 ft.\n- **MINF:** trip to alternate + 15 min hold at 1,500 ft + missed approach.\n- **REQ CRIT:** block needed to reach the ETP plus the largest MINF; shortfall uplifted as ADD.E, repeated as CRIT.\n- **RF / SRP:** fuel remaining and surplus. **RMK:** what drove MINF. **EDTO 90:** the MDT used.","keys":[{"k":"ETPs and alternates with windows","m":["window","etp"]},{"k":"SHT 10 NM + 2000 ft","m":["2000","10 nm","10nm"]},{"k":"MINF","m":["minf","minimum fuel"]},{"k":"REQ CRIT / ADD.E","m":["req crit","adde","add e","add.e","crit"]},{"k":"EDTO 90 header","m":["edto 90","90 min","90 minutes"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-match-4","type":"match","q":"Match the OFP EDTO table column to its meaning.","src":"OM-A Annex A","ref":"EDTO brief 5","prio":2,"pairs":[["SHT","Highest terrain within 10 NM of the leg + 2000 ft"],["MINF","Trip to alternate + 15 min hold at 1,500 ft + missed approach"],["REQ CRIT","Block fuel to reach the ETP plus the largest MINF"],["RMK","What drove MINF (engine inop, decompression, anti-ice, MEL)"],["ALTN (TIME)","Alternate with its weather validity window"],["EDTO 90 (header)","Route needs at most 90 min from an ETP to an alternate"]],"why":"OM-A Annex A.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-44","type":"recall","q":"What does \"EDTO 90\" on the OFP mean? What is the EDTO category?","src":"OM-A Annex A, 4.6.5.3","ref":"EDTO brief 11","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"It is the MDT used for that flight plan: the route needs at most 90 min (618 NM for the MAX) from an ETP to an alternate. The category is the MDT band the route needs (EDTO 75, 90, 105, 120), worked out from the longest ETP-to-alternate time. Most AIX routes are EDTO 90, although our approval is 120 min.","keys":[{"k":"At most 90 min ETP to alternate","m":["90 min","90min","90 minutes","ninety"]},{"k":"618 NM","m":["618"]},{"k":"Longest ETP-to-alternate time","m":["longest"]},{"k":"Approval still 120","m":["120"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-45","type":"recall","q":"What is an unbalanced EDTO, and where is the limiting critical fuel then?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.3","ref":"EDTO brief 5","prio":3,"a":"An unbalanced EDTO is where the ETP lies between an EDTO alternate and an airport that doesn't meet EDTO minima, so only one side of the ETP has a valid EDTO alternate. The limiting critical fuel is then usually not at an ETP but from the EEP or EXP to the one valid alternate, because that is the longest diversion to an alternate that meets the minima.","keys":[{"k":"ETP between valid and invalid alternate","m":["doesn't meet","does not meet","not meet","one valid"]},{"k":"From EEP or EXP","m":["eep","exp"]},{"k":"To the one valid alternate","m":["valid alternate","one alternate"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-46","type":"recall","q":"What do you check for EDTO in your pre-flight review?","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.1, 4.6.2.2","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- NOTAMs for departure, destination, destination alternate and EDTO alternates.\n- Critical fuel against FOB; area of operation on the chart.\n- EDTO alternates at or above planning minima through each window.\n- ATC FPL Item 18 RALT/ with the alternates.\n- Gross-error checks on the CFP.\n- Aircraft not downgraded (MEL/DDG), PDSC done, EDTO status in the Tech Log.\n- FMC: EEP, ETPs, EXP as waypoints, winds on all pages, cross-checked.\n- Crossfeed valve operational check.\n- If two CFPs, label one MASTER.","keys":[{"k":"NOTAMs incl. EDTO alternates","m":["notam"]},{"k":"Critical fuel vs FOB","m":["critical fuel","fob"]},{"k":"Alternates through window","m":["window","planning minima"]},{"k":"PDSC / Tech Log","m":["pdsc","tech log","techlog"]},{"k":"FMC waypoints","m":["waypoint","fmc"]},{"k":"Crossfeed check","m":["crossfeed","cross feed","cross-feed"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"How are EDTO en-route alternates shown in the ICAO flight plan?","src":"Doc 4444 App 2, Item 18","ref":"EDTO brief 5","prio":2,"o":["Item 18: RALT/ followed by the ICAO four-letter indicators","Item 16: second alternate box","Item 18: EET/ with the alternates","Item 19: ALTN/ followed by names"],"a":0,"why":"Item 18 RALT/ with the ICAO indicators (or names if no indicator is allocated).","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-47","type":"recall","q":"How do you set up the FMC for an EDTO flight?","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.2, 4.6.2.4","ref":"EDTO brief 5","prio":2,"a":"- EEP, ETPs and EXP stored as waypoints, not in LEGS, shown with WPT on the EFIS panel.\n- Winds entered on all pages.\n- EDTO alternates on the FIX page, updated as you go, with BRG/DIST and FUEL/TIME to the next alternate.\n- All entries cross-checked by both pilots.","keys":[{"k":"Stored waypoints, not in LEGS","m":["not in legs","waypoint","stored"]},{"k":"WPT on EFIS","m":["wpt","efis"]},{"k":"Winds on all pages","m":["wind"]},{"k":"FIX page alternates","m":["fix page","fix"]},{"k":"Cross-checked","m":["cross-check","cross check","crosscheck","both pilots"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-48","type":"recall","q":"What is EDTO critical fuel and what are the three critical fuel scenarios?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7, 4.3.7.1, 4.3.7.3","ref":"EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Fuel to divert from the critical point to an en-route alternate after the worst of three failures, then hold, fly two approaches and land.\n- **(a) Depressurisation, both engines:** emergency descent to FL100 (or MORA), cruise at LRC.\n- **(b) Depressurisation + engine failure:** emergency descent to FL100, cruise at OEI speed 0.79M/330 KIAS.\n- **(c) Engine failure only:** driftdown at OEI speed, cruise at driftdown level.\nBlock fuel is the greater of this and normal fuel.","keys":[{"k":"Depressurisation","m":["depressuri","decompression","pressuri"]},{"k":"Depress + engine failure","m":["both","plus engine","and engine","engine failure and"]},{"k":"Engine failure only","m":["engine failure"]},{"k":"From critical point","m":["critical point"]},{"k":"Greater of normal fuel","m":["greater","higher"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-49","type":"recall","q":"Which critical fuel scenario is most limiting, and why? Where is consumption highest?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.3, 4.3.7.3 Note","ref":"EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"Usually scenario (b), depressurisation plus engine failure. You are at FL100 in dense air, flying fast (0.79M/330 KIAS) on one engine at MCT: the highest fuel flow per NM of all the cases. Engine failure alone (c) is always least limiting because it cruises high at the driftdown level, so AIX line analysis computes only (a) and (b).","keys":[{"k":"Depress + engine failure (b)","m":["depressurisation and engine","depressurization and engine","both","plus engine","and engine failure","scenario b"]},{"k":"Low level FL100","m":["fl100","fl 100","low level","low altitude"]},{"k":"One engine at MCT","m":["mct","one engine","single engine"]},{"k":"High speed","m":["high speed","fast","330"]},{"k":"Engine failure only least","m":["least"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["engine failure alone is most","engine failure only is most"],"why":"Engine failure only cruises high at driftdown level and is always least limiting."}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-match-5","type":"match","q":"Match each critical fuel scenario to its diversion cruise and speed.","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.1, 4.3.7.3","ref":"EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["(a) Depressurisation, both engines","FL100 at LRC"],["(b) Depressurisation + engine failure","FL100 at 0.79M/330 KIAS, one engine at MCT"],["(c) Engine failure only","Driftdown level at the approved OEI speed"]],"why":"Scenario (b) usually limits; (c) always least limiting.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"Which critical fuel scenarios does AIX line analysis actually compute?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.3 Note","ref":"EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["(a) and (b) only","(b) and (c) only","(a), (b) and (c)","(b) only"],"a":0,"why":"Because (c) engine failure only always burns least, AIX computes only (a) and (b).","topic":1},{"id":"t1-order-1","type":"order","q":"Order the diversion profile used for every critical fuel scenario.","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.3 (2)","ref":"EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["Descent at the planned speed strategy to the diversion level","Cruise at the planned speed","Normal descent to 1,500 ft above the alternate","Hold 15 min at 1,500 ft","First (IFR) approach and go-around","Second approach (IFR or VFR) and landing"],"why":"OM-A 4.3.7.3 (2) diversion profile.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-50","type":"recall","q":"What allowances are added on top of the critical fuel diversion burn?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.1, 4.3.7.2, 4.3.7.3 (3)","ref":"EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Contingency:** 5% of the burn.\n- **Wind:** 5% wind factor (headwind increased, tailwind decreased); if not from an approved model such as WAFS, 5% extra fuel.\n- **Performance:** 5% fuel mileage penalty or the demonstrated performance factor.\n- **Icing:** the greater of airframe icing for 10% of forecast icing time, or engine and wing anti-ice for the whole icing time.\n- **MEL/CDL** effects; **APU** burn if required; **driftdown** at OEI speed included.","keys":[{"k":"15 min hold + two approaches","m":["15 min","hold","two approaches","second approach"]},{"k":"5% contingency","m":["contingency"]},{"k":"5% wind factor","m":["wind"]},{"k":"Icing 10% or anti-ice whole time","m":["10 percent","icing","anti-ice","anti ice"]},{"k":"Performance factor","m":["performance","deterioration"]},{"k":"APU / MEL","m":["apu","mel"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-14","type":"num","q":"Critical fuel: holding time included at the alternate?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.3 (2)","ref":"EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":15,"unit":"min","why":"15 min at 1,500 ft above the alternate at flaps-up speed.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-15","type":"num","q":"Critical fuel: holding height above the alternate?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.3 (2)","ref":"EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":1500,"unit":"ft","why":"Hold 15 min at 1,500 ft.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-16","type":"num","q":"Critical fuel wind factor applied to the forecast wind?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.1","ref":"EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":5,"unit":"%","why":"5% (headwind increased, tailwind decreased).","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-17","type":"num","q":"Critical fuel icing allowance: airframe icing for what percentage of the forecast icing time?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.1","ref":"EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":10,"unit":"%","why":"10% of forecast icing time, or anti-ice for the whole time, whichever is greater.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-51","type":"recall","q":"When is icing assumed for critical fuel without a reliable forecast?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.1","ref":"EDTO brief 6","prio":3,"a":"Without a reliable icing forecast, icing is assumed when TAT is below +10°C at the OEI speed, or when OAT is between 0°C and minus 20°C with relative humidity of 55% or more. The allowance is then the greater of airframe icing for 10% of the icing time or engine and wing anti-ice for the whole time.","keys":[{"k":"TAT below +10°C","m":["10°c","10 c","10 degrees","plus 10"]},{"k":"OAT 0 to minus 20","m":["20°c","20 c","20 degrees","minus 20"]},{"k":"RH 55%","m":["55 percent","55 per cent","humidity"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-52","type":"recall","q":"How is block fuel decided on an EDTO flight? What are ADD.E and CRIT fuel?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.3 (3)(c)","ref":"EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **EDTO critical fuel planning:** normal fuel from departure to the CP + the worst scenario from the CP to the alternate, with all the additions.\n- **Standard fuel planning:** departure to destination to alternate per the normal fuel policy.\n- **Block fuel = the higher of the two.** When EDTO is higher, the difference shows on the OFP as ADD.E and again as CRIT fuel.\n- Non-mandatory AIX additions: performance factor on all calculations and 2 to 3% contingency from departure to the CP.","keys":[{"k":"Normal fuel to CP + worst scenario","m":["to the cp","critical point","worst"]},{"k":"Standard fuel plan","m":["normal fuel","standard"]},{"k":"Higher of the two","m":["higher","greater"]},{"k":"ADD.E / CRIT","m":["adde","add e","add.e","crit"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-53","type":"recall","q":"Why do we carry extra fuel on EDTO?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.3 (3)(c), 4.7.2.5.1, Annex A","ref":"EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"At the CP a diversion at FL100, possibly on one engine, can need more fuel than continuing to the destination with normal reserves. The EDTO requirement tops that up: the difference is uplifted as ADD.E and shown as CRIT fuel. In flight that extra fuel is treated as part of contingency and not managed separately.","keys":[{"k":"Worst-case diversion at CP","m":["critical point","worst"]},{"k":"FL100 / one engine","m":["fl100","fl 100","one engine","low level"]},{"k":"More than normal plan","m":["more than","normal"]},{"k":"ADD.E / CRIT","m":["adde","add e","add.e","crit"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-54","type":"recall","q":"Is the CP a reclearance point? Must FOB at the CP equal critical fuel?","src":"OM-A 4.6.3","ref":"EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"No. There is no rule that FOB at the CP must equal the critical fuel requirement, and the CP is not a reclearance point. If estimated FOB at the CP will be below critical fuel, a diversion is not required, provided the estimated fuel at destination is above the minimum needed to divert to the destination alternate. Normal fuel rules apply. If CPs are regularly overflown short, dispatch corrects the planning (higher performance factor or more route reserve).","keys":[{"k":"Not a reclearance point","m":["not a reclearance","not reclearance","isn't a reclearance"]},{"k":"No diversion required","m":["not required","no diversion","no requirement"]},{"k":"Destination alternate fuel protected","m":["destination alternate"]},{"k":"Dispatch corrects planning","m":["dispatch","performance factor","route reserve"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["must divert","mandatory diversion","reclearance point is"],"why":"OM-A 4.6.3: CP shortfall does not force a diversion if destination-alternate fuel is protected."}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-55","type":"recall","q":"Why is a later ETP usually the CP?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.9, Annex A","ref":"EDTO brief 6","prio":2,"a":"Although the aircraft is heavier early in the flight, on most AIX routes the later ETP has less fuel remaining for a diversion of similar length, so the fuel margin is smallest there. It is usually, but not always, the last ETP in the segment.","keys":[{"k":"Less fuel remaining later","m":["less fuel","fuel remaining"]},{"k":"Similar diversion length","m":["similar"]},{"k":"Smallest margin","m":["margin","smallest"]},{"k":"Usually not always","m":["usually","not always"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-56","type":"recall","q":"Can the PIC deviate from the planned OEI speed in an actual diversion?","src":"OM-A 4.3.4.4, 4.6.5.2; FCTM 4.16","ref":"EDTO brief 7","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Yes. The planned OEI speed (0.79M/330 KIAS) sizes the area of operation and the fuel. In an actual diversion the crew may fly whatever strategy is safest after assessing the situation, but should fly at maximum speed when the situation is time-critical. Boeing's FCTM also says the captain may modify the speed.","keys":[{"k":"Yes, after assessment","m":["yes","assess"]},{"k":"Planned speed sizes area and fuel","m":["area","fuel","planning"]},{"k":"Max speed when time-critical","m":["time-critical","time critical","maximum speed"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-57","type":"recall","q":"Explain the trade-off in choosing the OEI diversion speed.","src":"OM-A 4.3.6.2","ref":"EDTO brief 7","prio":2,"a":"- **Faster:** bigger circles, so a larger area of operation and more routing freedom, but a lower diversion level and more fuel.\n- **Slower:** smaller circles and a higher diversion level, less fuel.\n- The minimum speed is the one at which adjacent alternates' circles just join. Operators pick something faster to get overlap, which gives room for weather avoidance and more direct routes; the critical fuel must then be carried for that speed.","keys":[{"k":"Faster: bigger area","m":["bigger","larger area","larger circle"]},{"k":"Faster: more fuel, lower level","m":["more fuel","lower"]},{"k":"Slower: less fuel","m":["less fuel"]},{"k":"Circles just join = minimum speed","m":["join","minimum speed"]},{"k":"Overlap for weather/routing","m":["overlap","weather","direct"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-58","type":"recall","q":"What diversion strategies are in the B737 table? What is the fixed speed strategy?","src":"OM-A 4.6.5.3 Table 1, 4.3.6.1 (3)","ref":"EDTO brief 7","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Standard:** 0.79M/290 KIAS at MCT to the LRC ceiling, then LRC. Best fuel, longest time.\n- **Obstacle:** driftdown at FMC engine-out speed at MCT, hold minimum manoeuvring speed until the obstacle is cleared, then standard.\n- **Fixed speed 0.79/290:** fuel-critical. **0.79/310:** normal EDTO 90 diversion. **0.79/330:** time-critical, the planned EDTO speed.\n\"Fixed\" means the same speed schedule for descent and cruise, minimum time at the cost of fuel. Not used after a pressure loss (emergency descent is at MMO/VMO).","keys":[{"k":"Standard: best fuel, LRC","m":["standard","lrc"]},{"k":"Obstacle: driftdown","m":["obstacle","driftdown","drift down"]},{"k":"Fixed: same descent and cruise","m":["same","fixed"]},{"k":"290 / 310 / 330","m":["310"]},{"k":"330 time-critical","m":["330","time critical","time-critical"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-match-6","type":"match","q":"Match the B737 fixed-speed diversion schedule to when you use it.","src":"OM-A 4.6.5.3 Table 1","ref":"EDTO brief 7","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["0.79 / 290","Fuel-critical EDTO diversion"],["0.79 / 310","Normal EDTO 90 diversion"],["0.79 / 330","Time-critical: minimum time at the expense of fuel"],["Standard (LRC)","Fuel matters most: best fuel, longest time"],["Obstacle","Terrain under the diversion path"]],"why":"OM-A 4.6.5.3 Table 1.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-59","type":"recall","q":"What is the AIX guidance for flying an EDTO diversion speed?","src":"OM-A 4.6.5.3","ref":"EDTO brief 7","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"1. All flight-plan calculations use 0.79M/330 KIAS.\n2. Fixed-speed diversion: higher speed if time is critical, slower if fuel is critical.\n3. Most routes are EDTO 90, so 0.79/290 or 0.79/310 is recommended.\n4. Enter level and speed in the FMC cruise page, update OFP winds, and make sure the ETA overhead the alternate is within 120 min; if not, select 330 kt.\n5. If the OFP is EDTO 120 with departure and destination as alternates, only then might you need 330 KIAS.","keys":[{"k":"Plans use 0.79/330","m":["330"]},{"k":"290 or 310 recommended","m":["290","310"]},{"k":"ETA within 120 min","m":["120"]},{"k":"Else select 330","m":["select 330","330 kt","330 knots","330"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-60","type":"recall","q":"Engine failure in cruise on a 737: describe the driftdown technique.","src":"FCTM 4.12 to 4.16","ref":"EDTO brief 7","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Disconnect the A/T and set CON thrust manually; select ENG OUT on the FMC CRZ page; set the engine-out MAX altitude and target speed on the MCP; let the speed bleed back to the engine-out speed, then engage LVL CHG. After level-off keep MCT and accelerate to single-engine LRC. That gives the highest level on one engine, which matters over terrain. On EDTO, the approved procedure typically uses higher speeds and a lower level to reach the alternate within the approved time.","keys":[{"k":"CON / MCT thrust","m":["con","mct","maximum continuous"]},{"k":"ENG OUT on CRZ page","m":["eng out","engine out"]},{"k":"Speed bleeds to E/O speed","m":["bleed","slow"]},{"k":"LVL CHG","m":["lvl chg","level change"]},{"k":"Highest level / terrain","m":["highest","terrain"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-order-2","type":"order","q":"Order the actions for an engine failure in cruise in the EDTO segment.","src":"OM-A 4.2.4.2 (4b), 4.6.5.3; Doc 4444 15.2.2","ref":"EDTO brief 7","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["Memory items and NNC (QRH)","Set MCT and choose the strategy","Turn towards the alternate, offset and descend per Doc 4444 contingency","MAYDAY, squawk 7700, coordinate with ATC and company","Land at the nearest en-route alternate by flying time"],"why":"OM-A EDTO sequence: aviate, navigate, communicate, then land at the nearest alternate in time.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-61","type":"recall","q":"What happens at the EEP? What is the go/no-go review?","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.4, 4.6.2.4.3, 4.6.2.4.1 Note 4","ref":"EDTO brief 8","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"The EEP is the gate. Before it, alternate weather is tested against landing minima and a system or APU problem can still stop you entering. The review before the EEP covers alternates' forecast and NOTAMs at or above normal landing minima for the ETA (dispatch minima no longer apply airborne), aircraft status, fuel remaining, and field conditions and services. After the EEP, degraded alternate weather does not by itself require a diversion; standard diversion philosophy applies.","keys":[{"k":"Landing minima before EEP","m":["landing minima"]},{"k":"Aircraft status","m":["status","system"]},{"k":"Fuel","m":["fuel"]},{"k":"After EEP no forced diversion for weather","m":["no requirement","not require","standard diversion","does not"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-62","type":"recall","q":"Is the alternate weather check \"20 minutes before the EEP\" a B737 requirement?","src":"OM-A 4.7.2.4.1, 4.6.2.4.1","ref":"EDTO brief 8, 12","trap":true,"prio":3,"a":"Not as written. The 20 min timing is in the A320 section of the OM-A (4.7.2.4.1). The B737 text says only \"before the EEP\" for the alternate review, and \"APU about 10 minutes before\" the EEP. Quote each as written.","keys":[{"k":"20 min is A320 section","m":["a320"]},{"k":"B737 says before the EEP","m":["before the eep","before eep"]},{"k":"APU 10 min before EEP","m":["10 min","10 minutes","ten minutes"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-63","type":"recall","q":"What is the EDTO APU policy on the B737?","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.4.1, Notes 1, 2","ref":"EDTO brief 8","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Start the APU about 10 min before the EEP and leave it running until the EXP. If it fails to start, two further attempts are allowed (QRH). With several segments, start it before the first EEP and shut it down after the last EXP. Aircraft certified for APU on demand need not start it.","keys":[{"k":"About 10 min before EEP","m":["10 min","10 minutes","ten minutes"]},{"k":"Run to EXP","m":["exp","exit"]},{"k":"Two further attempts","m":["two further","two more","2 more","three attempts","3 attempts"]},{"k":"APU on demand exception","m":["on demand","on-demand"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-18","type":"num","q":"How many minutes before the EEP is the APU started on the B737?","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.4.1","ref":"EDTO brief 8","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":10,"unit":"min","why":"About 10 min before the EEP, running to the EXP.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-64","type":"recall","q":"What is the minimum number of electrical sources to enter the EDTO segment?","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.4, 4.6.6.1","ref":"EDTO brief 8","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Three: two IDGs plus the APU generator. If an engine generator has failed, or the APU won't start, do not enter. After the EEP, losing one source does not necessarily require a diversion; losing both IDGs is a mandatory diversion. Aircraft certified for APU on demand need not run the APU.","keys":[{"k":"Three sources","m":["three","3 sources","3 independent"]},{"k":"Two IDGs","m":["idg","two engine generators","engine driven"]},{"k":"APU generator","m":["apu"]},{"k":"Do not enter if one lost","m":["do not enter","don't enter","not enter"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-order-3","type":"order","q":"Order the EDTO in-flight phases.","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.3, 4.6.2.4, 4.6.2.4.2, 4.6.9","ref":"EDTO brief 8","prio":2,"items":["After door close: any new failure, NNC then check MEL/DDG","Climb and cruise: alternates on FIX page, monitor fuel and status","Before the EEP: go/no-go review of alternates, status, fuel","About 10 min before EEP: start APU","In the segment: weather updates, flight watch, normal fuel checks","Passing the EXP: APU off, crossfeed check in last hour","After landing: Tech Log entries"],"why":"OM-A 4.6.2.3 to 4.6.9 phase sequence.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-65","type":"recall","q":"What do you do in the EDTO segment, and at the EXP?","src":"OM-A 4.5.7, 4.6.3, 4.6.4, 4.6.2.4.2, 4.6.9","ref":"EDTO brief 8","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- Keep updating weather for the alternates, destination and destination alternate.\n- Flight watch from Flight Dispatch (Mumbai) and IOCC (Delhi) by HF, VHF or ACARS: alternate closures, SIGMETs, NOTAMs.\n- Fuel checks per normal policy (TOC, TOD and every 30 min); no special CP check.\n- Accept no clearance beyond 120 min or 816 NM from an alternate.\n- At the EXP: shut down the APU; crossfeed valve check in the last hour of cruise.\n- After landing: EDTO-relevant failures in the Tech Log, never verbal only.","keys":[{"k":"Weather updates","m":["weather"]},{"k":"Flight watch","m":["flight watch","dispatch","iocc"]},{"k":"Normal fuel checks, every 30 min","m":["30 min","30 minutes","normal fuel"]},{"k":"Nothing beyond 120 min / 816 NM","m":["816","120"]},{"k":"APU off at EXP","m":["apu"]},{"k":"Crossfeed in last hour","m":["crossfeed","cross feed","cross-feed"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-66","type":"recall","q":"How are SIGMETs handled on an EDTO flight?","src":"OM-A 4.5.7, 4.5.7.3","ref":"EDTO brief 11","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Flight watch (Flight Dispatch Mumbai and IOCC Delhi) monitors them and tells the crew, by HF, VHF or ACARS, along with alternate closures and NOTAMs. The crew checks SIGMETs pre-flight and in flight for the route and the diversion paths.","keys":[{"k":"Flight watch monitors","m":["flight watch","dispatch","iocc"]},{"k":"Pre-flight and in flight","m":["pre-flight","preflight","in flight"]},{"k":"Route and diversion paths","m":["diversion"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"Fuel checks within the EDTO segment are done:","src":"OM-A 4.6.3","ref":"EDTO brief 8","prio":2,"o":["Per normal policy: TOC, TOD and every 30 min; no special CP check","At every ETP and the CP","Every 15 min and at the CP","Only at the CP"],"a":0,"why":"Normal fuel policy applies; the CP is not a reclearance point.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-67","type":"recall","q":"What is the maximum clearance you can accept in EDTO?","src":"OM-A 4.6.4","ref":"EDTO brief 8; E23","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Nothing that takes the aircraft beyond 120 min or 816 NM from an en-route alternate. That applies to ATC re-routes and weather deviations too: deviate within the area of operation or pick a route that keeps an alternate in range, and tell ATC and the company.","keys":[{"k":"120 min","m":["120"]},{"k":"816 NM","m":["816"]},{"k":"From an en-route alternate","m":["alternate"]},{"k":"Do not accept","m":["not accept","do not","refuse","nothing beyond"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-68","type":"recall","q":"What does the APU reliability (in-flight start) programme require on APU-on-demand aircraft?","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.5","ref":"EDTO brief 8","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"On the 1st of each month, or when Maintenance asks, on an inbound sector to India: start the APU about 20 min before descent and run it for 5 min. The flight needs at least 1 h 30 min of cruise to cold-soak the APU. Two more attempts if the first fails. Tech Log entry either way; if unsuccessful include flight details and SAT.","keys":[{"k":"1st of each month","m":["1st","first of","monthly","each month"]},{"k":"20 min before descent","m":["20 min","20 minutes"]},{"k":"Run 5 min","m":["5 min","5 minutes","five minutes"]},{"k":"1 h 30 min cruise","m":["1 h 30","1h 30","90 min","90 minutes","one and a half","1 hour 30"]},{"k":"Tech Log entry","m":["tech log","techlog"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-69","type":"recall","q":"What is cold soak in the EDTO context?","src":"OM-A 4.2.4.1 (11), 4.6.2.5; FCOM L.10.11","ref":"EDTO brief 11; E18","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"A long cruise chills the APU and the fuel. A cold-soaked APU may not start at altitude, hence the APU in-flight start reliability programme (at least 1 h 30 min of cruise before the monthly start). Fuel also cools, hence the fuel temperature limits: minimum minus 43°C or 3°C above freezing point, whichever is higher.","keys":[{"k":"Long cruise chills APU and fuel","m":["chill","cold","long cruise"]},{"k":"APU may not start","m":["apu"]},{"k":"In-flight start programme","m":["programme","program","reliability"]},{"k":"Fuel temperature limits","m":["fuel temperature","fuel temp","minus 43","43°c","43 degrees"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-70","type":"recall","q":"What is ETOPS? What extensions are available?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.1, 4.1.2.2, 4.1.2.19","ref":"NR23","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"ETOPS/EDTO: a twin flying a route with a point more than the threshold time (DGCA 60 min) from an en-route alternate at OEI speed, ISA, still air, with DGCA approval.\n- Our OM-A lists MDT approvals of 75, 90, 120 or 180 min.\n- Internationally (FAA/EASA, not in our OM-A): 75, 90, 120, 138, 180, 207, 240 min and beyond 240 (about 330 to 370 min for 777, 787, A350).\n- AIX B737-8 MAX: 120 min = 816 NM at 0.79M/330 KIAS; most routes EDTO 90.","keys":[{"k":"60 min threshold","m":["60 min","60min","60 minutes","sixty minutes","one hour","1 hour"]},{"k":"75 / 90 / 120 / 180","m":["180"]},{"k":"138 / 207 / 240 international","m":["138","207","240"]},{"k":"MAX 120 min / 816 NM","m":["816","120"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"Which set of maximum diversion times does the AIX OM-A list?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.19","ref":"NR23","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["75, 90, 120 or 180 min","75, 90, 120, 138, 180, 207, 240 min","60, 90, 120 or 180 min","90, 120, 180 or 240 min"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 4.1.2.19 lists 75, 90, 120, 180. The 138, 207, 240 steps are FAA/EASA levels not in the OM-A. Annex A also mentions EDTO 105 as an OFP category.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-71","type":"recall","q":"Which failures force a diversion in the EDTO segment?","src":"OM-A 4.6.6.1","ref":"EDTO brief 9","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Mandatory diversion to the nearest en-route alternate:\n1. Engine fire. 2. APU fire. 3. Cargo fire.\n4. Single-engine operation (failure, shutdown or engine at idle).\n5. Fuel burn or loss beyond available reserves.\n6. Loss of cabin pressurisation.\n7. Both IDGs failed.\n8. Air-conditioning smoke. 9. Electrical smoke.\n10. Flight control failures.\n11. Loss of two hydraulic systems.","keys":[{"k":"Fires: engine, APU, cargo","m":["fire"]},{"k":"Single-engine operation","m":["single engine","single-engine","engine failure","one engine"]},{"k":"Loss of pressurisation","m":["pressuri"]},{"k":"Both IDGs","m":["both idg","two idg","both generators","both engine driven"]},{"k":"Smoke","m":["smoke"]},{"k":"Flight controls / two hydraulics","m":["flight control","hydraulic"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Which of these is NOT on the OM-A EDTO mandatory-diversion list?","src":"OM-A 4.6.6, 4.6.6.1","ref":"EDTO brief 9","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Loss of one hydraulic system","Electrical smoke","Loss of cabin pressurisation","Both IDGs failed"],"a":0,"why":"Loss of TWO hydraulic systems is mandatory; one system lost is a PIC assessment.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"Which of these is NOT on the OM-A EDTO mandatory-diversion list?","src":"OM-A 4.6.6.1","ref":"EDTO brief 9; E21","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Radio communication failure","APU fire","Flight control failure","Air-conditioning smoke"],"a":0,"why":"RCF alone is not a mandatory diversion.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-72","type":"recall","q":"Apart from the mandatory list, what are reasons to re-route or divert? How do you choose the diversion aerodrome in flight?","src":"OM-A 4.6.5.1, 4.7.2.5.2","ref":"EDTO brief 9","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Other reasons: loss of RVSM capability before the area, alternate below landing minima or unusable before the EEP with no replacement, crew below minimum complement, medical emergency (and fuel consumption).\nChoosing the aerodrome: performance with the failure (landing distance, climb, fuel penalty), wind and weather en route and at the diversion level, minimum altitudes, fuel, the aerodrome itself (terrain, weather, runways and condition, approach aids, lighting, RFFS), passenger and medical facilities, curfew, political factors.","keys":[{"k":"RVSM loss","m":["rvsm"]},{"k":"Medical / incapacitation","m":["medical","incapacita","complement"]},{"k":"Performance with failure","m":["performance","landing distance"]},{"k":"Weather / runway / aids","m":["weather","runway","approach aid"]},{"k":"RFFS / facilities","m":["rffs","facilit","fire"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-order-4","type":"order","q":"Order the EDTO diversion checklist (OM-A quick reference).","src":"OM-A 4.7","ref":"EDTO brief 9","prio":3,"items":["Check aircraft status","Check alternate status (weather, NOTAMs)","Check fuel","Set up the diversion in the FMC","Review contingency procedures"],"why":"OM-A 4.7 quick reference.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-73","type":"recall","q":"E1. Engine failure in the EDTO segment over water. Talk me through it.","src":"OM-A 4.2.4.2 (4b), 4.6.5.3, 4.6.6.1, 4.6.7; Doc 4444 15.2.2.3","ref":"E1","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Aviate:** memory items and QRH, MCT, strategy (obstacle if terrain matters, otherwise standard or fixed speed).\n- **Mandatory diversion** to the nearest en-route alternate by flying time, not distance.\n- **Navigate:** Doc 4444 contingency: turn towards the alternate away from traffic, offset, minimise descent rate, 500 ft off normal levels, lights, TCAS, 121.5.\n- **Communicate:** MAYDAY, 7700, ATC and company.\n- **Speed:** 0.79/290 or 0.79/310 on EDTO 90; ETA within 120 min, else 330.\n- **After:** verification flight and 72 h report.","keys":[{"k":"QRH / MCT","m":["mct","qrh","memory"]},{"k":"Nearest by flying time","m":["flying time","time not distance","nearest"]},{"k":"Doc 4444 offset","m":["offset","4444"]},{"k":"MAYDAY / 7700","m":["mayday","7700"]},{"k":"0.79/290 or 310","m":["290","310"]},{"k":"Verification flight","m":["verification"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-74","type":"recall","q":"E1 detail. Doc 4444 in-flight contingency after an engine failure over water: what do you fly?","src":"Doc 4444 15.2.2.3, 15.2.2.4","ref":"E1","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"As printed in our 16th edition copy (Doc 4444 15.2.2.3):\n- turn at least 45° towards the alternate, away from adjacent traffic;\n- acquire a 15 NM offset track;\n- minimise the rate of descent and settle 500 ft off the normal levels;\n- lights and transponder on, watch TCAS, broadcast on 121.5 (backup 123.45);\n- tell ATC you are a twin with an engine shutdown, ask for expeditious handling.\nLater ICAO amendments use 30° and 5 NM: quote the version in your Jeppesen ATC pages.","keys":[{"k":"Turn 45°","m":["45°","45 degrees","45 deg","fortyfive"]},{"k":"15 NM offset","m":["15 nm","15nm","15 nautical"]},{"k":"500 ft off levels","m":["500 ft","500ft","500 feet"]},{"k":"121.5 broadcast","m":["1215","121.5","121 5"]},{"k":"Later: 30° and 5 NM","m":["30°","30 degrees","30 deg","5 nm","5nm"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["only 30 degrees","30 degrees and 5"],"why":"Your 16th edition copy says 45° and 15 NM; 30°/5 NM is a later amendment. Quote the version in your Jeppesen pages and know both."}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-75","type":"recall","q":"E2. Engine failure before the EEP (or after the EXP).","src":"OM-A 4.2.4.2 (4b), 4.6.6.1","ref":"E2","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Do not enter the EDTO segment. Single-engine operation is on the mandatory-diversion list, and Annex 6 requires a twin to land at the nearest suitable aerodrome in point of time. That may be the departure airport or a take-off alternate within 60 min. After the EXP, same answer: nearest suitable aerodrome.","keys":[{"k":"Do not enter","m":["do not enter","don't enter","not enter"]},{"k":"Nearest by flying time","m":["flying time","nearest","least flying time","in point of time"]},{"k":"Departure or take-off alternate","m":["departure","take-off alternate","takeoff alternate"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-76","type":"recall","q":"E3. Engine failure exactly at the ETP: which alternate do you choose?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.8, 4.1.2.9","ref":"E3","prio":2,"a":"Both are equal in time by definition, so choose on: weather against landing minima, runway length and condition, approach aids, terrain along the path, RFFS, medical and handling facilities, and wind at the diversion level. Remember the ETP is about equal time; the CP is only about fuel.","keys":[{"k":"Equal time by definition","m":["equal"]},{"k":"Weather","m":["weather"]},{"k":"Runway","m":["runway"]},{"k":"Terrain / approach aids","m":["terrain","approach aid"]},{"k":"RFFS / facilities","m":["rffs","facilit","medical"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-77","type":"recall","q":"E4. Depressurisation at the ETP, both engines running. Level, speed, fuel?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.3, 4.6.6.1; FCOM PD.14.5","ref":"E4","prio":2,"a":"- Oxygen masks, emergency descent at MMO/VMO with speedbrakes to FL100, or MORA or OFP SHT if higher.\n- Mandatory diversion.\n- Cruise at LRC at FL100: critical fuel scenario (a), all engines. Above FL100 (up to FL150) only if supplemental oxygen covers the time.\n- Passenger oxygen lasts only minutes, so descend promptly.\n- Fuel is already in the OFP REQ CRIT figure.","keys":[{"k":"Emergency descent MMO/VMO","m":["mmo","vmo","emergency descent"]},{"k":"FL100 or MORA","m":["fl100","fl 100","mora"]},{"k":"LRC","m":["lrc","long range"]},{"k":"Scenario (a)","m":["scenario a","all engines","both engines"]},{"k":"Mandatory diversion","m":["mandatory","divert"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-78","type":"recall","q":"E5. Depressurisation plus engine failure: what do you fly and is the fuel enough?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.3 (f)","ref":"E5","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Emergency descent at MMO/VMO to FL100 (or MORA), then cruise at the approved OEI speed, 0.79M/330 KIAS, at FL100. This is scenario (b), usually the highest fuel flow: the critical fuel was calculated for exactly this. Monitor fuel against distance, and icing at FL100 (the anti-ice allowance is in the calculation).","keys":[{"k":"FL100","m":["fl100","fl 100"]},{"k":"0.79M/330 KIAS","m":["330"]},{"k":"Scenario (b)","m":["scenario b","critical fuel","highest"]},{"k":"Monitor icing","m":["icing","ice"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-79","type":"recall","q":"E6. Engine fire or severe damage in the EDTO segment.","src":"OM-A 4.6.5.2, 4.6.6.1","ref":"E6","prio":2,"a":"QRH memory items and checklist. If the fire persists, land as soon as possible. Engine fire is on the mandatory-diversion list, so divert to the nearest en-route alternate. It is time-critical, so fly the maximum practical speed rather than a fuel-saving schedule.","keys":[{"k":"QRH memory items","m":["memory","qrh"]},{"k":"Land ASAP if persists","m":["as soon as possible","asap"]},{"k":"Mandatory diversion","m":["mandatory","nearest"]},{"k":"Maximum speed","m":["maximum","max speed","time critical","time-critical"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-80","type":"recall","q":"E7. Cargo fire in the EDTO segment.","src":"OM-A 4.6.5.2, 4.6.6.1","ref":"E7","prio":1,"a":"Divert to the nearest alternate: mandatory, whatever the extinguisher's protection time. The PIC may choose a slightly farther aerodrome for operational reasons or better fire services. It is time-critical, so maximum speed. Class C suppression lasts 195 min, the system that limits the MDT. MAYDAY, have RFFS ready at the alternate, and prepare for an evacuation.","keys":[{"k":"Mandatory diversion nearest","m":["mandatory","nearest"]},{"k":"Maximum speed","m":["maximum","max speed","time critical","time-critical"]},{"k":"195 min suppression","m":["195"]},{"k":"MAYDAY / RFFS ready","m":["mayday","rffs","fire services"]},{"k":"Prepare evacuation","m":["evacuat"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-81","type":"recall","q":"E8. APU fire in the EDTO segment. What is the electrical impact?","src":"OM-A 4.6.6.1","ref":"E8","prio":3,"a":"Run the QRH. APU fire is on the mandatory-diversion list, so divert to the nearest en-route alternate. Electrically you are now down to two IDGs. That is acceptable after the EEP, but at least one more failure would leave you on standby power, so monitor the remaining sources closely.","keys":[{"k":"QRH","m":["qrh"]},{"k":"Mandatory diversion","m":["mandatory","divert"]},{"k":"Two IDGs left","m":["two idg","2 idg","idgs"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-82","type":"recall","q":"E9. Air-conditioning or electrical smoke in the EDTO segment.","src":"OM-A 4.6.6.1","ref":"E9","prio":3,"a":"Oxygen masks on and run the QRH smoke procedures. Air-conditioning smoke and electrical smoke are both on the mandatory-diversion list, so divert to the nearest en-route alternate. If the smoke can't be controlled, it becomes land as soon as possible at the nearest runway, not the planned alternate.","keys":[{"k":"QRH smoke procedure","m":["qrh","smoke"]},{"k":"Oxygen masks","m":["oxygen","mask"]},{"k":"Mandatory diversion","m":["mandatory","divert"]},{"k":"Uncontrolled: land ASAP","m":["as soon as possible","asap","nearest runway"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-83","type":"recall","q":"E10. One IDG fails. Before the EEP? After the EEP?","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.4, 4.6.6.1","ref":"E10","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Before the EEP:** do not enter; you no longer have the three sources (two IDGs plus APU generator).\n- **After the EEP:** a diversion is not necessarily required. Run the QRH, keep the APU generator on line, and assess. Both IDGs failing would be a mandatory diversion.","keys":[{"k":"Before: do not enter","m":["do not enter","don't enter","not enter"]},{"k":"Three sources needed","m":["three","3 sources"]},{"k":"No diversion required after EEP","m":["not necessarily required","no diversion","not required","continue","no requirement"]},{"k":"APU generator on line","m":["apu"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-84","type":"recall","q":"E11. Both IDGs fail in the EDTO segment.","src":"OM-A 4.6.6.1; FCOM L.10.8, 6.20.11, 6.20.12, 6.20.21","ref":"E11","prio":2,"a":"- Mandatory diversion.\n- Start and use the APU generator. Its electrical load limit (41,000 ft) is not the problem; starting it may be, because on battery power APU start attempts are not recommended above 25,000 ft, so a descent may come first.\n- If the APU generator isn't available, you are on battery and standby power: load shed and land within battery time, at least 30 min single battery, 60 min dual-battery installation.","keys":[{"k":"Mandatory diversion","m":["mandatory","divert"]},{"k":"APU generator","m":["apu"]},{"k":"APU start not above 25,000 ft on battery","m":["25000","25 000","fl250","fl 250"]},{"k":"Battery 30 min single","m":["30 min","30 minutes"]},{"k":"60 min dual battery","m":["60 min","60 minutes","dual"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-19","type":"num","q":"On battery power, above what altitude are APU start attempts not recommended (B737)?","src":"FCOM 6.20.21, L.10.8","ref":"E11","prio":2,"ans":25000,"unit":"ft","why":"25,000 ft; the APU generator load limit itself is 41,000 ft.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-20","type":"num","q":"Minimum standby battery time with the dual-battery installation (B737)?","src":"FCOM 6.20.11, 6.20.12","ref":"E11","prio":2,"ans":60,"unit":"min","why":"At least 30 min single battery, 60 min dual battery.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-85","type":"recall","q":"E12 / S1. The APU fails just after the EEP. What if it failed before the EEP?","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.4, 4.6.2.4.1 Notes 1, 2, 4.6.6.1","ref":"S1; E12","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **After the EEP:** continue. A single electrical source failure after the EEP does not necessarily require a diversion. Inform the company by ACARS and monitor; if both IDGs then fail, that is a mandatory diversion.\n- **Before the EEP:** three sources (2 IDGs + APU gen) are needed to enter. Two further start attempts; if it still won't start, re-route with ATC within 60 min (420 NM) of an alternate at or above landing minima, otherwise proceed to an en-route alternate within 60 min.\n- **APU-on-demand aircraft** need not start the APU, so it does not affect entry.","keys":[{"k":"After EEP: continue","m":["continue","not necessarily","no diversion"]},{"k":"Before: three sources needed","m":["three","3 sources"]},{"k":"Two further attempts","m":["two further","two more","2 more","3 attempts","three attempts"]},{"k":"Re-route within 60 min / 420 NM","m":["420","60 min","within 60"]},{"k":"APU on demand","m":["on demand","on-demand"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-86","type":"recall","q":"E13. Hydraulic failure in the EDTO segment.","src":"OM-A 4.6.6, 4.6.6.1","ref":"E13","prio":2,"a":"- **One system lost:** run the QRH. It is not on the mandatory list, so continuing may be acceptable after the PIC's assessment that there is no substantial loss of safety.\n- **Two systems lost:** on the mandatory-diversion list, so divert to the nearest en-route alternate by flying time.","keys":[{"k":"One: PIC assessment","m":["assess","may continue","continue"]},{"k":"Two: mandatory diversion","m":["two systems","mandatory"]},{"k":"Not on mandatory list for one","m":["not on the","not mandatory","substantial"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-87","type":"recall","q":"E14. Flight control failure (jammed stabiliser, trim runaway, flap asymmetry) in the segment.","src":"OM-A 4.6.6.1","ref":"E14","prio":3,"a":"Run the QRH non-normal checklist first. Flight control failures are on the OM-A mandatory-diversion list, so divert to the nearest en-route alternate by flying time. Before committing, check the landing distance and configuration (for example flap setting with asymmetry) against the alternate's runway length and condition.","keys":[{"k":"QRH","m":["qrh"]},{"k":"Mandatory diversion","m":["mandatory","divert"]},{"k":"Landing distance vs runway","m":["landing distance","runway"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-88","type":"recall","q":"E15. Fuel leak or higher-than-planned burn in the EDTO segment.","src":"OM-A 4.6.6.1 (5)","ref":"E15","prio":2,"a":"Confirm first: compare totaliser against fuel used, check engine indications, and run the QRH Fuel Leak NNC. If the loss or burn exceeds the reserves, it is a mandatory diversion. If an engine is shut down for a leak, single-engine operation is also a mandatory diversion.","keys":[{"k":"Totaliser vs fuel used","m":["totaliser","totalizer","fuel used"]},{"k":"QRH Fuel Leak","m":["fuel leak","qrh"]},{"k":"Beyond reserves: mandatory","m":["reserve","mandatory"]},{"k":"Engine shutdown also mandatory","m":["single engine","single-engine","shutdown"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-89","type":"recall","q":"E16. Your FOB at the CP will be below critical fuel. What do you do?","src":"OM-A 4.6.3, 4.6.5.1 (d)","ref":"E16","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"No diversion required, provided estimated fuel at destination is above the minimum needed to divert to the destination alternate. The CP is not a reclearance point. Tell the company so dispatch can review the performance factor or route reserves. Before the EEP, first recompute critical fuel with actual winds and weight; if a genuine shortfall shows before entry, re-routing or a closer alternate is the prudent answer.","keys":[{"k":"No diversion required","m":["no diversion","not required","no requirement"]},{"k":"Destination alternate fuel protected","m":["destination alternate"]},{"k":"Not a reclearance point","m":["reclearance"]},{"k":"Tell company / dispatch","m":["company","dispatch"]},{"k":"Before EEP recompute","m":["recompute","actual wind","re-route","closer"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-90","type":"recall","q":"E17. Why is the crossfeed valve checked on EDTO, and what if it fails or an imbalance develops?","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.2 (8), 4.6.2.4.2","ref":"E17","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"After an engine failure you must be able to feed the live engine from both tanks. So the crossfeed valve is checked before every EDTO flight and in the last hour of cruise. Found on the ground: an MEL item, likely no EDTO, re-plan non-EDTO. Found in flight: QRH, assess whether one tank alone is enough if an engine fails, and consider a re-route.","keys":[{"k":"Feed live engine from both tanks","m":["both tanks","live engine","feed"]},{"k":"Before every EDTO flight","m":["before every","before each","pre-flight","preflight"]},{"k":"Last hour of cruise","m":["last hour"]},{"k":"Ground: MEL, non-EDTO","m":["mel","non-edto","non edto"]},{"k":"Flight: QRH / re-route","m":["qrh","re-route","reroute"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-91","type":"recall","q":"E18. Cold fuel on a long sector: what are the limits and what do you do?","src":"FCOM L.10.11; FCTM 4.9, 4.10","ref":"E18","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Minimum tank fuel temperature before take-off and in flight is minus 43°C or 3°C above the fuel freezing point, whichever is higher; maximum 49°C. Specification freezing point: minus 47°C for Jet A-1, minus 40°C for Jet A. If fuel temperature nears the limit: increase speed (higher TAT), or descend or re-route to warmer air, coordinating with ATC.","keys":[{"k":"Minus 43°C","m":["43°c","43 c","43 degrees","minus 43"]},{"k":"3°C above freezing","m":["3°c above","3 c above","3 degrees","three degrees","3c above"]},{"k":"Whichever higher","m":["higher"]},{"k":"Jet A-1 freezes minus 47","m":["47°c","47 c","47 degrees","minus 47"]},{"k":"Increase speed / descend","m":["increase speed","speed","descend","warmer"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-21","type":"num","q":"Maximum fuel tank temperature (B737)?","src":"FCOM L.10.11","ref":"E18","prio":3,"ans":49,"unit":"°C","why":"Maximum 49°C.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"Jet A-1 specification freezing point?","src":"FCTM 4.9, 4.10","ref":"E18","prio":2,"o":["-47°C","-40°C","-43°C","-50°C"],"a":0,"why":"Jet A-1 minus 47°C; Jet A minus 40°C. The tank minimum is minus 43°C or 3°C above freezing, whichever higher.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-num-22","type":"num","q":"Jet A-1 (freezing point -47°C) loaded: minimum in-flight tank fuel temperature (°C)?","src":"FCOM L.10.11; FCTM 4.9","ref":"E18","prio":2,"ans":-43,"unit":"°C","why":"Higher of -43°C and (-47 + 3) = -44°C, so -43°C.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-92","type":"recall","q":"E19 / S2. EDTO alternate weather goes below dispatch minima before the EEP. And below landing minima? And after the EEP?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.13, 4.3.3.1, 4.6.2.4, 4.6.2.4.3, 4.7","ref":"S2; E19","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Below planning but above landing minima, before the EEP:** continue. Planning minima apply only at planning; airborne the test is normal landing minima for the window.\n- **Below landing minima before the EEP:** nominate another alternate (WX, NOTAMs, FIX page 420 NM ring, APU 10 min before the ring); tell the company by ACARS or SSB; if dep-dest is under 1632 NM you may continue with the APU running to the destination's 420 NM circle; otherwise re-route within 60 min; if no route, turn back.\n- **After the EEP:** no requirement to divert or re-route; keep monitoring.","keys":[{"k":"Planning minima only at planning","m":["planning","dispatch minima"]},{"k":"Airborne: landing minima","m":["landing minima"]},{"k":"Nominate another alternate","m":["another alternate","nominate","new alternate"]},{"k":"Re-route within 60 min / turn back","m":["within 60","60 min","turn back","re-route"]},{"k":"After EEP no requirement","m":["no requirement","not required","no diversion","continue"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-order-5","type":"order","q":"Before the EEP an EDTO alternate goes below landing minima. Order the OM-A actions.","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.4.3","ref":"E19","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["Nominate another alternate (WX, NOTAMs, FIX page 420 NM ring)","Inform the company by ACARS or SSB","If dep-dest < 1632 NM, continue with APU running to destination's 420 NM circle","Otherwise re-route to stay within 60 min (420 NM)","If no such route exists, turn back"],"why":"OM-A 4.6.2.4.3 sequence.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-93","type":"recall","q":"E20. An alternate closes by NOTAM mid-segment.","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.4.3, 4.7","ref":"E20","prio":2,"a":"Same logic as an alternate going below minima. After the EEP there is no forced diversion. Look for another usable aerodrome within range with Flight Dispatch's help, and check the remaining alternates still cover the rest of the segment in case something else fails.","keys":[{"k":"No forced diversion after EEP","m":["no forced","no diversion","not required","no requirement"]},{"k":"Find another aerodrome","m":["another"]},{"k":"Flight Dispatch help","m":["dispatch"]},{"k":"Check coverage of rest of segment","m":["cover","rest of the segment"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-94","type":"recall","q":"E21 / S5. Radio communication failure inside the EDTO segment.","src":"OM-A 4.5.7, 5.2, 5.5, 41.1.9.4, 4.6.6.1; FCOM L.10.7","ref":"S5; E21","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- Try every link first: the other VHF, HF with SELCAL, ACARS to Flight Dispatch or IOCC, relay via other aircraft on 123.45, and 121.5; SATCOM only where fitted (part of the fleet).\n- If still lost: squawk 7600 and the ICAO RCF procedure. Non-surveillance: keep last assigned speed and level (or MFA if higher) for 20 min after failing to report over a compulsory point, then the filed flight plan.\n- RVSM: no change; ATC applies 2000 ft.\n- RCF alone is not a mandatory diversion; if another failure forces one, fly the Doc 4444 contingency.","keys":[{"k":"HF / SELCAL / ACARS","m":["hf radio","high frequency","selcal","acars"]},{"k":"Relay 123.45 / 121.5","m":["12345","123.45","123 45","1215","121.5","relay"]},{"k":"7600","m":["7600"]},{"k":"20 min then flight plan","m":["20 min","20 minutes","flight plan"]},{"k":"Not a mandatory diversion","m":["not a mandatory","not mandatory","not on the"]},{"k":"RVSM 2000 ft","m":["2000"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-95","type":"recall","q":"E22. Loss of RVSM capability before the EDTO area.","src":"OM-A 4.6.5.1 (a)","ref":"E22","prio":2,"a":"Loss of RVSM capability before entering the area is a listed reason to re-route in the OM-A. Below FL290 the fuel burn rises and the critical fuel picture changes, so re-plan the levels with dispatch and confirm the fuel still covers trip and critical fuel before entering the EDTO segment.","keys":[{"k":"Listed re-route reason","m":["re-route","reroute","listed"]},{"k":"Below FL290 more burn","m":["fl290","fl 290","burn"]},{"k":"Re-plan with dispatch, confirm fuel","m":["dispatch","confirm fuel","fuel"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-96","type":"recall","q":"E23. A weather deviation would take you outside the area of operation.","src":"OM-A 4.6.4","ref":"E23","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"The PIC must not accept, or fly, a routing beyond 120 min or 816 NM of an alternate. Deviate within the area, or pick a route that keeps an alternate in range, and tell ATC and the company. Doc 4444 weather deviation rules still apply over water.","keys":[{"k":"Not beyond 120 min / 816 NM","m":["816","120"]},{"k":"Deviate within area","m":["within the area","keep an alternate","in range"]},{"k":"Tell ATC and company","m":["atc","company"]},{"k":"Doc 4444 weather deviation","m":["4444"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-97","type":"recall","q":"E24 / E25. Medical emergency or pilot incapacitation in the EDTO segment.","src":"OM-A 4.6.5.1 (e), (f)","ref":"E24; E25","prio":2,"a":"- **Medical emergency:** a listed diversion reason. Use MedLink or company medical advice if available, choose an alternate with the right medical facilities even if a little farther, declare PAN or MAYDAY as appropriate.\n- **Incapacitation:** crew below the minimum legal complement is a listed diversion reason. Secure the pilot, get cabin crew help, declare an emergency and divert to the nearest suitable alternate.","keys":[{"k":"Listed diversion reason","m":["listed","divert"]},{"k":"Medical facilities","m":["medical facilit","medlink","medical"]},{"k":"PAN / MAYDAY","m":["pan","mayday","emergency"]},{"k":"Below minimum complement","m":["complement","minimum crew"]},{"k":"Secure pilot, cabin crew help","m":["secure","cabin crew"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-98","type":"recall","q":"E26. A failure occurs after door close or during taxi on an EDTO flight.","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.3, 4.7.2.3","ref":"E26","prio":2,"a":"Do the NNC, then stop with the parking brake set and check the MEL/DDG for EDTO relief, because the EDTO MEL is more restrictive. If EDTO capability is lost, return to the bay and re-dispatch as a non-EDTO flight, re-planned within 60 min of an aerodrome. Remember the MEL does not apply once airborne.","keys":[{"k":"NNC","m":["nnc","qrh","non-normal"]},{"k":"Stop, parking brake","m":["parking brake","stop"]},{"k":"MEL/DDG for EDTO relief","m":["mel","ddg"]},{"k":"Re-dispatch non-EDTO","m":["non-edto","non edto","re-dispatch","redispatch"]},{"k":"MEL not airborne","m":["airborne"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-99","type":"recall","q":"E27. The verification flight has not been signed off by the EEP.","src":"OM-A 4.6.7, 4.2.4.1","ref":"E27","prio":2,"a":"Do not enter the EDTO segment; re-route the flight as non-EDTO, staying within 60 min of an aerodrome. After maintenance on an EDTO significant system or an IFSD, a verification flight can be a revenue EDTO flight only if it is signed off as complete before the EEP.","keys":[{"k":"Do not enter","m":["do not enter","don't enter","not enter"]},{"k":"Re-route non-EDTO","m":["non-edto","non edto","re-route","reroute"]},{"k":"Must be signed off before EEP","m":["before the eep","before eep","signed off"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-100","type":"recall","q":"E28. The aircraft arrives with an EDTO-significant MEL item.","src":"OM-A 4.3.8, 4.5.3.2","ref":"E28","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Check the EDTO MEL, which is built from the EDTO MMEL and is more restrictive than the normal MEL, with AIX network factors added. If there is no EDTO relief, the aircraft is downgraded to non-EDTO and the route is re-planned within 60 min of an aerodrome.","keys":[{"k":"Check EDTO MEL","m":["edto mel","mel"]},{"k":"More restrictive","m":["restrictive"]},{"k":"No relief: non-EDTO","m":["non-edto","non edto","downgrad"]},{"k":"Re-plan within 60 min","m":["60 min","within 60"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-101","type":"recall","q":"What is a verification flight?","src":"OM-A 4.6.7, 4.2.4.1","ref":"EDTO brief 11","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"After an IFSD or a significant EDTO system failure (or maintenance on an EDTO significant system), a ground check and, if required, a flight that proves the fix. It may be a revenue EDTO flight only if signed off as complete before the EEP; otherwise do not enter the segment. After an IFSD the aircraft needs one before its next EDTO flight.","keys":[{"k":"After IFSD / significant failure","m":["ifsd","shutdown","significant"]},{"k":"Proves the fix","m":["prove","fix","rectif"]},{"k":"Revenue if signed off before EEP","m":["before the eep","before eep","revenue"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-102","type":"recall","q":"S3. Before the EEP, fuel on board is less than required for the EDTO segment.","src":"OM-A 4.6.5.1, 4.6.3, 12.3","ref":"S3","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"Fuel consumption is a listed reason to divert or re-route before entering: do not enter unless FOB covers EDTO critical fuel from the remaining ETPs. First recompute with actual winds and weight. If still short: nominate a closer EDTO alternate (shortens the critical leg), re-route within 60 min (non-EDTO), or divert for fuel. FOB must never be less than remaining trip + destination alternate + final reserve. Know OM-A 4.6.3 (CP shortfall does not force a diversion) in case pushed, but before the EEP the safe answer is do not enter.","keys":[{"k":"Do not enter short","m":["do not enter","don't enter","not enter"]},{"k":"Recompute actual winds/weight","m":["recompute","actual wind","actual"]},{"k":"Closer alternate","m":["closer"]},{"k":"Re-route within 60 min","m":["within 60","60 min","non-edto","non edto"]},{"k":"Trip + alternate + final reserve","m":["final reserve"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-103","type":"recall","q":"S4. Engine failure before entering the EDTO segment: actions?","src":"OM-A 4.6.6.1, 4.3.6, 4.6.7","ref":"S4","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Do not enter. Single-engine operation is on the mandatory-diversion list, and Annex 6 requires a twin to land at the nearest suitable aerodrome in point of time.\n1. NNC.\n2. MAYDAY, squawk 7700.\n3. Strategy: obstacle (driftdown at FMC engine-out speed) if terrain matters, otherwise standard.\n4. Divert.\nAfterwards the aircraft needs a verification flight before its next EDTO flight.","keys":[{"k":"Do not enter","m":["do not enter","don't enter","not enter"]},{"k":"Nearest by flying time","m":["flying time","nearest","least flying time","in point of time"]},{"k":"MAYDAY 7700","m":["mayday","7700"]},{"k":"Obstacle or standard strategy","m":["obstacle","standard","driftdown"]},{"k":"Verification flight","m":["verification"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-104","type":"recall","q":"S6. Depressurisation at the ETP with both engines running: which alternate, what speed, what level?","src":"OM-A 4.3.6, 4.3.7.1, 4.3.7.3, 4.6.6.1","ref":"S6","prio":2,"a":"- **Level:** emergency descent at MMO/VMO with speedbrakes to FL100, or MORA if higher; above FL100 (up to FL150) only with supplemental oxygen.\n- **Alternate:** mandatory diversion; at the ETP both are equal in time, so choose on weather, runway, terrain, facilities and RFFS.\n- **Speed:** scenario (a), fuel-critical at low level, so LRC, not the high OEI speed.\n- **Fuel:** critical fuel is the greater of three scenarios, so the OFP already covers it with 15 min hold, two approaches, 5% wind and icing.","keys":[{"k":"FL100 or MORA","m":["fl100","fl 100","mora"]},{"k":"FL150 only with oxygen","m":["fl150","fl 150","supplemental"]},{"k":"Choose on weather/runway/RFFS","m":["weather","runway","rffs"]},{"k":"LRC not OEI speed","m":["lrc","long range"]},{"k":"Already in critical fuel","m":["critical fuel","covered","ofp"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-recall-105","type":"recall","q":"Passenger oxygen duration in a depressurisation over an EDTO route: what can you say?","src":"FCOM PD.14.5; Pending: SEPM","ref":"E4; S6","prio":3,"a":"Passenger oxygen lasts only minutes, so the descent must be prompt. The fleet has either a chemical generator system (Standard or Medium Capacity Descent) or a pulse system (Large Capacity Descent). The FCOM gives crew oxygen dispatch tables but no passenger duration; the figure is in the SEPM (pending), to check against terrain along the diversion.","keys":[{"k":"Only minutes, descend promptly","m":["minutes","prompt"]},{"k":"Chemical or pulse system","m":["chemical","pulse"]},{"k":"FCOM gives no pax duration","m":["no passenger","not in the fcom","sepm"]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"Explain EDTO.\" Then drills in.","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.19, 4.1.2.23, 4.3.5.4, 4.6.5.2, Annex A","ref":"E chain: EDTO brief 1, 2, 3","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What is threshold time and who sets it?","a":"Range in time to an en-route alternate beyond which EDTO approval is needed; set by the State of the Operator. DGCA: 60 min for twins at OEI speed, ISA, still air.","keys":[{"k":"Set by State","m":["state","dgca"]},{"k":"60 min threshold","m":["60 min","60min","60 minutes","sixty minutes","one hour","1 hour"]},{"k":"OEI speed","m":["one engine inoperative","one-engine","oei","single engine","engine out"]}]},{"q":"What is it in NM for your aircraft, and your maximum diversion time?","a":"420 NM at 0.79M/330 KIAS. MDT 120 min = 816 NM.","keys":[{"k":"420 NM","m":["420"]},{"k":"816 NM","m":["816"]},{"k":"0.79/330","m":["330"]}]},{"q":"What limits the maximum diversion time?","a":"DGCA approval, not above type-design capability or the most limiting time-limited system in the AFM minus 15 min. Cargo fire suppression 195 min.","keys":[{"k":"Minus 15 min","m":["minus 15","15 min","time limited","time-limited"]},{"k":"Cargo fire 195","m":["195","cargo"]}]},{"q":"Why not oxygen?","a":"Oxygen only covers the descent to FL100; the cargo fire agent must last the whole diversion.","keys":[{"k":"Only for descent","m":["descent","fl100","fl 100"]}]},{"q":"What does EDTO 90 on your OFP mean?","a":"The route needs at most 90 min (618 NM) from an ETP to an alternate.","keys":[{"k":"90 min ETP to alternate","m":["90 min","90min","90 minutes","ninety"]},{"k":"618 NM","m":["618"]}]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"You are cruising towards the EEP. The APU fails to start.\" Drill.","src":"OM-A 4.6.2.4, 4.6.2.4.1 Notes 1, 2, 4.6.6.1","ref":"S1; E10; E12","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What do you do?","a":"Two further start attempts (QRH). Three electrical sources are needed to enter.","keys":[{"k":"Two more attempts","m":["two further","two more","2 more"]},{"k":"Three sources","m":["three","3 sources"]}]},{"q":"It still won't start.","a":"Re-route with ATC within 60 min (420 NM) of an alternate at or above landing minima; otherwise go to an en-route alternate within 60 min.","keys":[{"k":"Re-route within 60 min / 420 NM","m":["420","60 min","within 60"]},{"k":"Landing minima","m":["landing minima","minima"]}]},{"q":"Now suppose it failed just after the EEP.","a":"Continue: one electrical source lost after the EEP does not necessarily require a diversion. Inform company, monitor.","keys":[{"k":"No diversion required after EEP","m":["not necessarily required","no diversion","not required","continue","no requirement"]}]},{"q":"And if both IDGs then fail as well?","a":"Mandatory diversion. With no APU generator you are on battery and standby power: load shed and land within battery time (at least 30 min single, 60 min dual battery).","keys":[{"k":"Mandatory diversion","m":["mandatory","divert"]},{"k":"Battery / standby power","m":["battery","standby"]},{"k":"30 / 60 min","m":["30 min","60 min","30 minutes","60 minutes"]}]},{"q":"Is there any MAX exception?","a":"Aircraft certified for APU on demand need not run the APU, so it does not affect entry.","keys":[{"k":"APU on demand","m":["on demand","on-demand"]}]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-chain-3","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"Critical fuel: how is it decided?\" Drill.","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.1, 4.3.7.3, 4.6.3","ref":"E5; E16; EDTO brief 6","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What are the three scenarios?","a":"Depressurisation; depressurisation plus engine failure; engine failure only.","keys":[{"k":"Depressurisation","m":["depressuri","decompression"]},{"k":"Engine failure","m":["engine failure"]}]},{"q":"Which is most limiting and why?","a":"(b) depressurisation plus engine failure: FL100, one engine at MCT, 0.79M/330 KIAS, highest fuel flow. (c) always least.","keys":[{"k":"Depress + engine (b)","m":["both","plus engine","and engine","scenario b"]},{"k":"Low level, one engine, fast","m":["fl100","fl 100","low level","mct","one engine"]}]},{"q":"What is added after reaching the alternate?","a":"Descend to 1,500 ft, hold 15 min, IFR approach and go-around, second approach and landing. Plus 5% contingency, 5% wind, icing, performance factor, APU and MEL.","keys":[{"k":"15 min hold at 1500","m":["15 min","1500"]},{"k":"Two approaches","m":["two approach","second approach","go-around","go around"]},{"k":"5% wind","m":["5 percent","five percent","wind"]}]},{"q":"How does that become block fuel?","a":"Normal fuel to the CP plus the worst scenario, versus the standard plan; block is the higher; difference shows as ADD.E and CRIT.","keys":[{"k":"Higher of two","m":["higher","greater"]},{"k":"ADD.E / CRIT","m":["adde","add e","add.e","crit"]}]},{"q":"At the CP you will be below critical fuel. Must you divert?","a":"No. The CP is not a reclearance point; no diversion required if destination-alternate fuel is protected. Tell the company.","keys":[{"k":"No diversion","m":["no diversion","not required","not a reclearance"]},{"k":"Destination alternate protected","m":["destination alternate"]}]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-chain-4","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"Engine failure in the middle of the EDTO segment over the Arabian Sea.\" Drill.","src":"OM-A 4.2.4.2 (4b), 4.6.5.3, 4.6.6.1, 4.6.7; Doc 4444 15.2.2.3","ref":"E1; E3","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"First actions?","a":"Memory items and QRH, MCT, choose strategy (obstacle if terrain, else standard or fixed speed).","keys":[{"k":"QRH / MCT","m":["qrh","mct","memory"]},{"k":"Strategy","m":["strategy","obstacle","standard","fixed"]}]},{"q":"Where do you go?","a":"Mandatory diversion to the nearest en-route alternate in terms of flying time, not distance.","keys":[{"k":"Nearest by flying time","m":["flying time","nearest","least flying time","in point of time"]}]},{"q":"You are exactly at the ETP. Which one?","a":"Equal time, so choose on weather vs landing minima, runway, approach aids, terrain, RFFS, facilities, wind at diversion level.","keys":[{"k":"Weather","m":["weather"]},{"k":"Runway / RFFS","m":["runway","rffs"]}]},{"q":"How do you leave the track?","a":"Doc 4444 contingency: turn at least 45° (16th ed.), 15 NM offset, 500 ft off levels, 121.5, MAYDAY 7700. Later amendments 30° / 5 NM.","keys":[{"k":"Offset","m":["offset","15 nm","15nm","5 nm"]},{"k":"MAYDAY / 7700","m":["mayday","7700"]}]},{"q":"What speed?","a":"0.79/290 or 0.79/310 on an EDTO 90 route; check ETA within 120 min, otherwise 330.","keys":[{"k":"290 / 310","m":["290","310"]},{"k":"Within 120 min","m":["120"]}]},{"q":"After landing?","a":"Tech Log entry, 72 h report to DGCA, verification flight before the next EDTO flight.","keys":[{"k":"72 h report","m":["72 h","72h","72 hours"]},{"k":"Verification flight","m":["verification"]}]}],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw an EDTO route and explain it.","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.5 to 4.1.2.10, 4.3.2, 4.3.3.2, 4.6.2.4, 4.6.3, 4.6.4","ref":"EDTO brief 10","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"A straight route departure to destination with three en-route alternates (departure and destination may count as alternates).\n- **60 min threshold rings** (420 NM for the MAX, OEI speed, ISA, still air) round each alternate. Track leaves the last ring at the **EEP**, enters the next ring at the **EXP**; between them the **EDTO segment** (re-entering a ring ends a segment).\n- **Maximum diversion rings** (120 min = 816 NM approved; OFP plans EDTO 90 = 618 NM) overlapping along the whole segment; a gap means no EDTO.\n- **ETPs** between each pair of adjacent alternates (equal time with wind, moves into wind).\n- **CP** at the ETP with the smallest fuel margin, usually the last; not a reclearance point.\n- **Validity windows** written by each alternate.\n- Procedures annotated: pre-EEP review, APU on 10 min before EEP and off after EXP, no forced diversion for alternate weather after EEP, nothing beyond 120 min / 816 NM.","checklist":["Track with departure, destination and en-route alternates; said dep/dest can count","60 min / 420 NM threshold rings drawn round alternates","EEP where track leaves the ring, EXP where it re-enters; segment marked between","120 min / 816 NM area-of-operation rings overlapping along the whole segment (EDTO 90 = 618 NM)","ETP between each adjacent pair; said it moves into wind","CP marked at usually the last ETP; said not a reclearance point","Validity window written for each alternate (earliest via previous ETP, latest via next ETP at LRC FL100)","APU on 10 min before EEP, off after EXP; no clearance beyond 120 min / 816 NM"],"topic":1},{"id":"t1-order-6","type":"order","q":"Order the steps for drawing the EDTO route on paper.","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.5 to 4.1.2.10, 4.3.3.2, 4.6.2.4","ref":"EDTO brief 10","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Route and alternates","Threshold (60 min / 420 NM) rings: mark EEP and EXP","Area of operation (MDT) rings, check overlap","ETPs between adjacent alternates","CP","Validity windows","Procedures annotated on the picture"],"why":"Draw-and-say order from the EDTO brief.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"How does the ETP move with wind?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.8","ref":"EDTO brief 10, 11","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Into wind: with a headwind towards the destination it moves towards the destination","Downwind: towards the departure with a headwind","It does not move; it is fixed by distance","Towards whichever alternate has better weather"],"a":0,"why":"The ETP moves into wind.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"Where is the EEP on the route?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.5","ref":"EDTO brief 2","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["First point more than 60 min from an en-route alternate at OEI speed, ISA, still air","Point 60 min after take-off","First ETP on the route","Point where the aircraft is 120 min from the destination"],"a":0,"why":"EEP = first point beyond the threshold time from an en-route alternate.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement about the maximum diversion time is correct?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.19","ref":"EDTO brief 2","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["It sizes the area of operation; it is not an operational limit on an actual diversion","It is the maximum time the crew may take for any real diversion","It equals the cargo fire suppression time","It is set by the aircraft manufacturer only"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 4.1.2.19: used only to size the area of operation.","topic":1},{"id":"t1-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"Validity window: latest ETA at an alternate is based on a diversion from:","src":"OM-A 4.3.3.2","ref":"EDTO brief 5","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["The ETP with the next alternate, at LRC at FL100 (or MORA)","The ETP with the previous alternate, at normal cruise","The CP at the OEI speed","The EEP at 0.79M/330 KIAS"],"a":0,"why":"Earliest: previous ETP, normal cruise. Latest: next ETP, LRC at FL100.","topic":1},{"id":"t10-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"Explain the principle of a VOR.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3; Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: VOR","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"VOR works by **phase comparison of two 30 Hz signals**.\n- VHF, 108.00 to 117.95 MHz.\n- CVOR: the reference 30 Hz is FM on a 9960 Hz subcarrier; the variable 30 Hz is AM.\n- The phase difference between them equals the magnetic bearing from the station (the radial).\n- DVOR reverses the roles, using a rotating Doppler antenna array.\n- Line of sight; total accuracy about ±5°, with a cone of confusion overhead.","keys":[{"k":"Phase comparison","m":["phase"]},{"k":"Two 30 Hz signals","m":["30 hz","30hz","thirty hertz","30 hertz"]},{"k":"Reference FM on 9960 Hz subcarrier","m":["9960","subcarrier","sub carrier"]},{"k":"Variable AM","m":["variable"]},{"k":"VHF 108 to 117.95 MHz","m":["108","117","vhf"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"What is the VOR frequency band?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3","ref":"Key facts: VOR","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["108.00 to 117.95 MHz","108.10 to 111.95 MHz","118.00 to 136.975 MHz","328.6 to 335.4 MHz"],"a":0,"why":"VOR uses 108.00 to 117.95 MHz. 108.10 to 111.95 MHz (odd tenths) is the localiser band.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"Between 108 and 112 MHz, how are VOR and ILS frequencies shared?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3","ref":"Key facts: VOR","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["VOR on even tenths, ILS on odd tenths","VOR on odd tenths, ILS on even tenths","VOR on 50 kHz offsets, ILS on whole tenths","They are not shared; VOR starts at 112 MHz"],"a":0,"why":"From 108 to 112 MHz VORs use even tenths and ILS localisers use odd tenths.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-1","type":"num","q":"CVOR reference signal: what is the subcarrier frequency that carries the 30 Hz FM reference?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3; Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: VOR","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":9960,"unit":"Hz","why":"In a CVOR the reference 30 Hz is FM on a 9960 Hz subcarrier; the variable 30 Hz is AM.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"In a conventional VOR, which signal is frequency modulated?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: VOR","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["The 30 Hz reference, on a 9960 Hz subcarrier","The 30 Hz variable signal","Both 30 Hz signals","Neither; both are AM"],"a":0,"why":"CVOR: reference 30 Hz FM on the 9960 Hz subcarrier, variable 30 Hz AM. A DVOR reverses the roles.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"What is the theoretical VHF line-of-sight range formula for a VOR?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: VOR","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Range in NM is about **1.23 × (√ aircraft height + √ station height)**, heights in feet.\n- VOR is VHF, so it is line of sight (space wave), not a surface wave.\n- Height of both the aircraft and the station aerial matter.\n- Example: at 10,000 ft with a station at sea level, 1.23 × 100 is about 123 NM.","keys":[{"k":"1.23 factor","m":["123","1 23"]},{"k":"Square root of heights","m":["root","sqrt"]},{"k":"Aircraft and station height","m":["station"]},{"k":"Line of sight","m":["line of sight","los"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-2","type":"num","q":"Using the VHF range formula, what is the theoretical VOR range for an aircraft at 10,000 ft and a station at sea level?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: VOR","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":123,"unit":"NM","tol":1,"why":"1.23 × (√10,000 + √0) = 1.23 × 100 = 123 NM.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"What is the approximate total accuracy of a VOR bearing?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: VOR","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["±5°","±1°","±2°","±10°"],"a":0,"why":"Total VOR system accuracy is about ±5°. (The ground station error alone is within ±2°.)","topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-3","type":"num","q":"What is the tolerance for a VOT (VOR test facility) check?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: VOR","prio":2,"ans":4,"unit":"°","why":"A VOT check should read within ±4°.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"Why is a Doppler VOR better than a conventional VOR? (key facts)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: VOR","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**DVOR is better because its large-aperture antenna reduces site error.**\n- The variable signal comes from a wide ring of antennas using the Doppler principle.\n- That wide aperture is far less affected by reflections from buildings and terrain (multipath).\n- So there is less scalloping and the radials are more stable.\n- The roles of the 30 Hz signals are reversed, but the aircraft receiver is unchanged.","keys":[{"k":"Large aperture / ring of antennas","m":["aperture","ring","array","antennas"]},{"k":"Reduces site error","m":["site error"]},{"k":"Less multipath / reflections","m":["multipath","reflection","scallop"]},{"k":"Doppler principle","m":["doppler"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"Explain how DME works.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.5.3.1.1, 3.5.3.2, 3.5.4.4.1","ref":"Key facts: DME","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**DME measures slant range by timing an interrogation and reply.**\n- UHF, 960 to 1215 MHz band, vertically polarised.\n- The aircraft interrogates; the ground station replies 63 MHz away after a fixed delay (50 µs on X channels).\n- The round-trip time minus the delay gives slant range.\n- Capacity is the area's peak traffic or 100 aircraft, whichever is less; beyond that the station answers the strongest interrogations.\n- The 737 has two DMEs.","keys":[{"k":"UHF 960 to 1215 MHz","m":["uhf","960","1215","962","1213"]},{"k":"Aircraft interrogates, ground replies","m":["interrogat"]},{"k":"Reply 63 MHz away","m":["63 mhz","63mhz","63 mega"]},{"k":"50 µs fixed delay","m":["50 micro","50 us","50us","50 µs","fixed delay","delay"]},{"k":"Slant range","m":["slant"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"What frequency band does DME use (Annex 10)?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.5.3.2","ref":"Key facts: DME","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["960 to 1215 MHz","108 to 117.95 MHz","1030 to 1090 MHz only","328.6 to 335.4 MHz"],"a":0,"why":"DME operates in the UHF 960 to 1215 MHz band (NR14 quotes 962 to 1213 MHz for the channels).","topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"What is the polarisation of DME transmissions?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.5.3.2","ref":"Key facts: DME","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Vertical","Horizontal","Circular","Elliptical"],"a":0,"why":"DME is vertically polarised; VOR, ILS and markers are horizontally polarised.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-4","type":"num","q":"By how many MHz does a DME ground reply differ from the aircraft interrogation frequency?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.5.4.4.1","ref":"Key facts: DME","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":63,"unit":"MHz","why":"The ground station replies 63 MHz away from the interrogation frequency.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-5","type":"num","q":"What is the fixed reply delay of a DME ground transponder on X channels?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.5.4.4.1","ref":"Key facts: DME","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":50,"unit":"µs","why":"The ground station replies after a fixed 50 µs delay on X channels; the airborne unit subtracts it.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"What is the design capacity of a DME ground station?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.5.3.5.1","ref":"Key facts: DME","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["The peak traffic of the area or 100 aircraft, whichever is less","Unlimited, it replies to all","50 aircraft","100 aircraft, or 200 if the area's peak traffic is higher"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10: peak traffic or 100 aircraft, whichever is less. Beyond that the station answers the strongest interrogations.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"When a DME ground station is saturated, which aircraft does it reply to?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.5.3.5.1","ref":"Key facts: DME","prio":3,"o":["The strongest interrogations","The first 100 to lock on","The aircraft closest in azimuth","It stops replying altogether"],"a":0,"why":"Beyond capacity the ground station answers the strongest interrogations, so distant (weaker) aircraft lose it first.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"When is DME slant-range error significant, and when can you ignore it?","src":"Standard theory; NR14","ref":"Key facts: DME","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Slant-range error is negligible when the distance in NM is well above the height in thousands of feet.**\n- DME measures slant range, not ground range.\n- It is largest close to and overhead the station: overhead at FL360 the DME reads about 6 NM, which is just your height.\n- Far out, slant and ground range are almost the same.","keys":[{"k":"Measures slant range","m":["slant"]},{"k":"Negligible when distance much greater than height","m":["negligible","well above","much greater","far"]},{"k":"Overhead reads height","m":["overhead","6 nm","6nm","height"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-6","type":"num","q":"Directly overhead a DME at FL360, approximately what does the DME read?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR14","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":6,"unit":"NM","tol":0.5,"why":"36,000 ft is about 6 NM (6076 ft per NM), and overhead the slant range equals your height.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-7","type":"num","q":"How many DMEs are fitted to the 737?","src":"OM-A Ch 3","ref":"Key facts: DME","prio":3,"ans":2,"why":"The 737 has two DMEs.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"VOR, DVOR, NDB: which would you prefer, and why?","src":"OM-A 28.4; Standard theory","ref":"NR5","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**DVOR first, then CVOR, then NDB.**\n- DVOR: a wide ring of antennas (Doppler principle) greatly reduces site error from reflections off buildings and terrain, so it is more accurate and stable.\n- CVOR: about ±5° overall.\n- NDB is least accurate: night effect, coastal refraction, thunderstorm and static interference, quadrantal error, mountain effect. No integrity monitoring the pilot can see beyond the ident.\n- AIX: NDB approaches are not flown at night (OM-A 28.4).","keys":[{"k":"DVOR first, NDB last","m":["dvor"]},{"k":"Site error reduced","m":["site error","reflection","multipath"]},{"k":"NDB errors: night effect","m":["night effect"]},{"k":"Coastal refraction / thunderstorm","m":["coastal","thunderstorm","static"]},{"k":"No monitoring beyond ident","m":["ident","monitor"]},{"k":"No NDB approaches at night","m":["not at night","no ndb","at night"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Under the AIX rule, when may NDB approaches NOT be flown?","src":"OM-A 28.4","ref":"NR5","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["At night","In IMC","Without DME","Below 1000 ft ceiling"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 28.4: NDB approaches are not flown at night.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"Explain the NDB/ADF: band and how the ADF finds bearing.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.4.4.1; Oral bank p.99","ref":"NR14","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**The ADF finds relative bearing to an NDB with a loop and a sense antenna.**\n- LF/MF, 190 to 1750 kHz.\n- The loop gives a figure-of-eight with two nulls (180° ambiguity).\n- The sense antenna (omnidirectional) combines with it to give a cardioid with a single null, removing the ambiguity.\n- Ground (surface) wave or sky wave, so it suffers night effect and coastal refraction.","keys":[{"k":"LF/MF 190 to 1750 kHz","m":["190","1750","low frequency","medium frequency","lf mf"]},{"k":"Loop antenna null","m":["loop"]},{"k":"Sense antenna","m":["sense"]},{"k":"Removes 180° ambiguity","m":["ambiguity","180"]},{"k":"Relative bearing","m":["relative"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"INS versus IRS: what is the difference?","src":"OM-A Ch 3; Standard theory","ref":"NR14","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**INS uses a gyro-stabilised platform; IRS is strapdown.**\n- INS: accelerometers on a platform kept level and north-aligned by gyros. It integrates acceleration once for velocity and again for distance.\n- IRS: ring laser gyros and accelerometers fixed to the airframe; a computer does the platform's job mathematically.\n- IRS has no moving parts, so it is more reliable and aligns faster.\n- The 737 has two ADIRUs.","keys":[{"k":"INS gyro-stabilised platform","m":["platform","gimbal","stabilised","stabilized"]},{"k":"Integrates acceleration twice","m":["integrat"]},{"k":"IRS strapdown","m":["strapdown","strap down","fixed to the airframe"]},{"k":"Ring laser gyros","m":["ring laser","laser"]},{"k":"More reliable, faster alignment","m":["reliable","faster","no moving parts"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"Explain a servo-assisted altimeter and its advantages.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR14","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**The capsule's movement is sensed electrically and a servo motor drives the pointers.**\n- An inductive E and I bar pick-off senses the aneroid capsule with no mechanical linkage.\n- The error signal drives a servo motor that turns the pointers and repositions the pick-off to null the signal.\n- Advantages: no friction or lag, so more accurate especially at high altitude; electrical output for the transponder (Mode C), ADC and autopilot.\n- Usually has a standby mode on failure.","keys":[{"k":"E and I bar pick-off","m":["e and i","e i bar","e-i","pick off","pickoff","pick-off","inductive"]},{"k":"Servo motor nulls signal","m":["servo","null"]},{"k":"No friction or lag","m":["friction","lag"]},{"k":"Electrical output (Mode C, ADC, autopilot)","m":["mode c","transponder","electrical output","adc"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"In a servo altimeter, what senses the aneroid capsule movement?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR14","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["An inductive E and I bar pick-off","A mechanical gear train and rocking shaft","A vibrating cylinder","A ring laser gyro"],"a":0,"why":"An E and I bar inductive pick-off senses the capsule with no mechanical linkage; its error signal drives the servo.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"What are the VOR/approach interception angle limits in PANS-OPS?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I 3.1.2; 1.2.4.2; II-5-5 5.7.6","ref":"NR47","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**PANS-OPS limits:**\n- Initial approach segment to the IF: maximum interception **90° for a precision approach, 120° for a non-precision approach**.\n- Straight-in non-precision approach: final approach track within **30°** of the runway centreline.\n- Reversal procedures: direct entry from within **±30°** of the outbound track.\n- ILS: do not descend on the glide path until within localiser tracking tolerance; stay within half-scale deflection once established.","keys":[{"k":"90° precision","m":["90 degrees","90 deg","90°","ninety"]},{"k":"120° non-precision","m":["120 degrees","120 deg","120°","120"]},{"k":"Straight-in within 30°","m":["30 degrees","30 deg","30°","thirty"]},{"k":"Half-scale on ILS","m":["half scale","half-scale","halfscale"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"PANS-OPS: maximum interception angle onto the IF in the initial approach segment for a NON-precision approach?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I 3.1.2","ref":"NR47","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["120°","90°","70°","45°"],"a":0,"why":"90° for a precision approach, 120° for a non-precision approach.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"PANS-OPS: maximum interception angle onto the IF in the initial approach segment for a PRECISION approach?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I 3.1.2","ref":"NR47","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["90°","120°","30°","45°"],"a":0,"why":"90° for a precision approach, 120° for a non-precision approach.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-8","type":"num","q":"For a straight-in non-precision approach, the final approach track must be within how many degrees of the runway centreline?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I 1.2.4.2","ref":"NR47","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":30,"unit":"°","why":"Doc 8168: within 30° of the runway centreline.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"What intercept technique do you use on a VOR radial, inbound and outbound?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR47","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Use 30° to 45°, smaller near the station, larger far out.**\n- Radials converge near the station, so the CDI moves fast: use smaller angles and lead the turn.\n- Far out the radials are far apart: larger angles, up to 90°.\n- Inbound, sensitivity keeps increasing as you close, so reduce the angle.\n- Outbound, sensitivity decreases, so a 45° intercept settles well.\n- Set the radial with FROM for outbound, the reciprocal with TO for inbound, so the CDI senses correctly.","keys":[{"k":"30° to 45°","m":["30 to 45","30 45","45 degrees","45 deg","45°","forty five"]},{"k":"Smaller angle near station","m":["smaller","lead"]},{"k":"Larger far out","m":["larger","far out","90 degrees","bigger"]},{"k":"Sensitivity increases inbound","m":["sensitiv"]},{"k":"FROM outbound, TO inbound","m":["from","reciprocal"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-9","type":"num","q":"If the wavelength is 2 cm, what is the frequency in GHz?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR52","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":15,"unit":"GHz","why":"f = c / λ = 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 0.02 = 15 × 10⁹ Hz = 15 GHz (SHF, Ku band). Memory aid: λ (m) = 300 / f (MHz).","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"Wavelength 2 cm: what is the frequency, and which band is it?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR52","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**15 GHz, which is SHF (Ku band).**\n- f = c / λ = 3 × 10⁸ m/s ÷ 0.02 m = 15 × 10⁹ Hz.\n- Memory aid: λ (m) = 300 / f (MHz).\n- So 3 cm is 10 GHz, and the weather radar's 3.2 cm is about 9.4 GHz.","keys":[{"k":"15 GHz","m":["15 ghz","15ghz","15 giga","15 x 10","15 times 10"]},{"k":"SHF","m":["shf","super high"]},{"k":"c / λ","m":["c over","300","speed of light","3 x 10","3 times 10"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-10","type":"num","q":"Using λ (m) = 300 / f (MHz), what is the frequency in GHz for a 3 cm wavelength?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR52","prio":3,"ans":10,"unit":"GHz","why":"300 / 0.03 = 10,000 MHz = 10 GHz.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"What is the SHF band, and what aviation equipment uses it?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR56","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**SHF is 3 to 30 GHz, wavelength 10 cm to 1 cm.**\n- Radio altimeter (4.2 to 4.4 GHz).\n- Weather radar (about 9.3 GHz).\n- MLS (about 5 GHz).\n- Surface movement radar and part of satcom.\n- Below it, UHF (300 MHz to 3 GHz): DME, SSR, glide path, GPS. Above it, EHF (30 to 300 GHz).","keys":[{"k":"3 to 30 GHz","m":["3 to 30","3 30 ghz","3-30"]},{"k":"10 cm to 1 cm","m":["10 cm","1 cm","centimetric","centimetre","centimeter"]},{"k":"Radio altimeter","m":["radio alt","rad alt","radalt"]},{"k":"Weather radar","m":["weather radar","wx radar","radar"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"What is the frequency range of the SHF band?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR56","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["3 to 30 GHz","300 MHz to 3 GHz","30 to 300 MHz","30 to 300 GHz"],"a":0,"why":"SHF is 3 to 30 GHz (10 cm to 1 cm). UHF is 300 MHz to 3 GHz; EHF is 30 to 300 GHz.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the equipment to its band.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR56","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Radio altimeter (4.2 to 4.4 GHz)","SHF"],["DME","UHF"],["VOR","VHF"],["NDB","LF/MF"],["Glide path","UHF (328.6 to 335.4 MHz)"],["Weather radar (about 9.3 GHz)","SHF (X band)"]],"why":"SHF 3 to 30 GHz holds RA and weather radar; UHF 300 MHz to 3 GHz holds DME, SSR, glide path and GPS.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"What is a BFO, and when do you need it?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR57","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Beat Frequency Oscillator: a circuit in the ADF receiver.**\n- It mixes a locally generated frequency, offset by about 1 kHz, with the incoming carrier to produce an audible beat tone.\n- Needed for NDBs whose ident is sent by keying an unmodulated carrier on and off (N0N A1A): without it you hear nothing, so select BFO/TONE to hear the ident.\n- NDBs with a modulated ident (N0N A2A) carry their own audio, so the BFO is only for fine tuning.","keys":[{"k":"Beat frequency oscillator","m":["beat frequency","beat"]},{"k":"About 1 kHz offset","m":["1 khz","1khz","1000 hz","offset"]},{"k":"Audible tone","m":["audible","tone"]},{"k":"A1A unmodulated keyed carrier","m":["a1a","unmodulated","keyed","keying"]},{"k":"A2A has own audio","m":["a2a","modulated ident"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"For which NDB emission must you select BFO to hear the ident?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR57","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["N0N A1A","N0N A2A","A3E","J3E"],"a":0,"why":"A1A keys an unmodulated carrier, which is inaudible without the BFO. A2A carries its own audio tone.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-11","type":"num","q":"By roughly how much does the BFO offset its locally generated frequency from the carrier?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR57","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":1,"unit":"kHz","why":"About 1 kHz, producing an audible beat tone.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"Explain polarisation and the types of radio wave (surface, space, sky).","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.3.1, 3.1.3.2.2, 3.1.5.2.2, 3.1.7; Standard theory","ref":"OB51","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Polarisation is the plane of the electric field.**\n- Annex 10: VOR, localiser, glide path and markers are horizontally polarised; DME is vertically polarised. NDBs radiate vertically polarised waves because LF/MF surface waves need it.\n- Surface (ground) wave: follows the earth's curvature; dominant at LF/MF, so NDBs work beyond line of sight.\n- Space wave: direct plus ground-reflected wave; VHF and above.\n- Sky wave: refracted back from the ionosphere; the main long-range path at HF.","keys":[{"k":"Plane of the electric field","m":["electric"]},{"k":"VOR/ILS horizontal","m":["horizontal"]},{"k":"Surface wave follows curvature (LF/MF)","m":["surface wave","ground wave","curvature"]},{"k":"Space wave / line of sight (VHF)","m":["space wave","direct wave","line of sight"]},{"k":"Sky wave from ionosphere (HF)","m":["sky wave","skywave","ionosphere"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"Explain skip distance, dead space and how sky waves change by day and night.","src":"Standard theory; Oral bank p.106","ref":"OB51","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Skip distance:** the shortest ground distance at which the first sky wave returns.\n- **Dead space:** the gap between the end of the surface wave and that first sky-wave return.\n- **At night** the D layer disappears and ionisation falls, so sky waves reflect from higher, skip distances get longer, and lower HF frequencies are needed (rule of thumb: about half the day frequency).\n- **Multihop:** a strong sky wave reflects between ionosphere and earth several times, giving very long HF ranges.","keys":[{"k":"Skip distance: first sky wave return","m":["skip"]},{"k":"Dead space: gap after surface wave","m":["dead space","gap"]},{"k":"D layer disappears at night","m":["d layer","d-layer"]},{"k":"Longer skip at night","m":["longer","increase"]},{"k":"Lower frequency at night (about half)","m":["half","lower frequenc"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"What are ducting and fading?","src":"Oral bank p.106, p.98","ref":"OB51","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"- **Ducting:** a temperature inversion bends VHF/UHF waves back towards the surface and traps them, giving abnormally long ranges and interference from distant stations.\n- **Fading:** two or more components of the same signal arrive by different paths with different phase and partly cancel: sky and surface waves at LF/MF/HF; direct and ground-reflected waves at VHF.","keys":[{"k":"Temperature inversion","m":["inversion"]},{"k":"Abnormally long VHF range","m":["long range","abnormal","trapped","trap"]},{"k":"Fading: different paths, phase","m":["different path","phase","multipath"]},{"k":"Partly cancel","m":["cancel"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"At night, how does the usable HF frequency compare with the day frequency (rule of thumb)?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB51","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["About half the day frequency","About double the day frequency","The same","About a quarter higher"],"a":0,"why":"At night the D layer disappears and ionisation falls, so lower HF frequencies are needed, about half the day frequency.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"Bank question: \"Which polarisation do you prefer, vertical or horizontal?\" The bank says vertical (more range, less attenuation). Is that right?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.3.1, 3.1.3.2.2","ref":"OB51","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Only for surface waves.** Vertical polarisation suits LF/MF surface waves, which is why NDBs radiate vertically polarised waves.\n- But VOR, localiser, glide path and marker beacons are **horizontally polarised** by Annex 10.\n- DME is vertically polarised.\n- So \"vertical is better\" is not a general rule; it depends on the wave type and the aid.","keys":[{"k":"Holds for surface waves only","m":["surface wave","ground wave"]},{"k":"VOR/ILS horizontal (Annex 10)","m":["horizontal"]},{"k":"NDB vertical","m":["ndb"]},{"k":"DME vertical","m":["dme"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["vor is vertical","ils is vertical","always vertical"],"why":"VOR and ILS are horizontally polarised by Annex 10"}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-16","type":"mcq","q":"What polarisation does Annex 10 specify for VOR and ILS localiser emissions?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.3.1, 3.1.3.2.2","ref":"OB51","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Horizontal","Vertical","Circular","Either, at the State's choice"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 makes VOR, LOC, GP and marker emissions horizontally polarised. The bank's \"vertical is better\" only holds for surface waves.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"What is a carrier wave? Can it carry information? What are sidebands?","src":"Oral bank p.4, p.106; Standard theory","ref":"OB52","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**A carrier is the RF wave on which information is impressed by modulation.**\n- Unmodulated, it carries no information except by keying it on and off (Morse, A1A), which is why an A1A NDB needs the BFO.\n- AM varies amplitude (VHF voice is AM); FM varies frequency and is less affected by static.\n- Modulating carrier f_c with tone f_m gives an upper sideband f_c + f_m and a lower sideband f_c − f_m; bandwidth is 2 × f_m.\n- Example: 500 kHz carrier, 1 kHz tone gives 499 and 501 kHz.\n- SSB on HF suppresses one sideband and the carrier.","keys":[{"k":"Information impressed by modulation","m":["modulat"]},{"k":"Keying on and off (A1A)","m":["keying","a1a","morse","on and off"]},{"k":"Upper and lower sidebands","m":["upper","lower","sideband"]},{"k":"Bandwidth 2 × f_m","m":["twice","2 x","2 times","double"]},{"k":"SSB on HF","m":["ssb","single sideband"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"Bank example: \"a 500 Hz carrier modulated with a 1 kHz tone\". What are the sidebands?","src":"Oral bank p.4; Standard theory","ref":"OB52","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"a":"**The bank's \"500 Hz\" is a slip for 500 kHz.** A 500 kHz carrier modulated with a 1 kHz tone gives sidebands at **499 kHz and 501 kHz**.\n- Upper sideband = f_c + f_m, lower = f_c − f_m.\n- Bandwidth = 2 × 1 kHz = 2 kHz.\n- A 500 Hz carrier with a 1 kHz tone makes no sense: the carrier must be far above the audio.","keys":[{"k":"500 kHz carrier","m":["500 khz","500khz","kilohertz"]},{"k":"499 kHz","m":["499"]},{"k":"501 kHz","m":["501"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["500 hz carrier"],"why":"Transcription slip; the carrier is 500 kHz"}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-12","type":"num","q":"A 500 kHz carrier is amplitude modulated with a 1 kHz tone. What is the upper sideband frequency?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB52","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":501,"unit":"kHz","why":"f_c + f_m = 500 + 1 = 501 kHz (lower sideband 499 kHz).","topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-13","type":"num","q":"A carrier is amplitude modulated with a 3 kHz audio tone. What bandwidth does the signal occupy?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB52","prio":3,"ans":6,"unit":"kHz","why":"Bandwidth = 2 × f_m = 6 kHz.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-17","type":"mcq","q":"Which modulation does VHF aviation voice use?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB52","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["AM","FM","SSB","Pulse"],"a":0,"why":"VHF voice is AM. FM is less affected by static; SSB is used on HF.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-21","type":"recall","q":"What is night effect, and how do you reduce it?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 6.2.3.5, 6.3.4; Oral bank p.99; OM-A 28.4","ref":"OB53","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Sky wave and surface wave arriving together at the ADF with a phase difference.**\n- At night the NDB sky wave returns strongly; their interaction produces bearing errors, the loop null becomes ill-defined and the needle wanders.\n- Worst around dusk and dawn, when the ionosphere is changing height.\n- To reduce it: use the nearest beacon within rated coverage; prefer lower-frequency beacons; average the needle swing; cross-check with other aids.\n- Company rule: no NDB approaches at night (OM-A 28.4).","keys":[{"k":"Sky wave plus surface wave","m":["sky wave","skywave"]},{"k":"Phase difference / bearing error","m":["phase","bearing error"]},{"k":"Worst at dusk and dawn","m":["dusk","dawn","twilight"]},{"k":"Use nearest beacon","m":["nearest","closest"]},{"k":"Lower frequency beacon","m":["lower frequenc","low frequenc"]},{"k":"Average the needle / cross-check","m":["average","cross check","crosscheck","cross-check"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["more power","higher power","powerful"],"why":"Annex 10: night-effect distances are independent of NDB power"}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-18","type":"mcq","q":"The bank says night effect is reduced by using an NDB with more power output. What does Annex 10 say?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 6.3.4 c)","ref":"OB53","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["Night-effect distances are independent of the NDB's power","Doubling the power halves night effect","Power only matters over the sea","Higher power removes night effect completely"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 Att C 6.3.4 c): the night-effect distances are independent of the power of the NDB. Use the nearest, lower-frequency beacon instead.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-14","type":"num","q":"Annex 10: at night over land, out to about what range (km) does the surface wave exceed the sky wave by 10 dB for a 200 kHz NDB?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 6.3.4","ref":"OB53","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":300,"unit":"km","why":"About 300 km at 200 kHz, 230 km at 300 kHz and 200 km at 400 kHz over land. Reliable night bearings are unlikely much beyond.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match the NDB frequency to the approximate night-time good-bearing range over land (Annex 10).","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 6.3.4","ref":"OB53","asked":true,"prio":3,"pairs":[["200 kHz","300 km"],["300 kHz","230 km"],["400 kHz","200 km"]],"why":"Lower frequency gives a longer range before the sky wave spoils bearings. These distances are independent of power.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-22","type":"recall","q":"NDB/ADF errors and factors affecting range.","src":"Oral bank p.99; Annex 10 Vol I 3.4.4.1","ref":"OB54","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Errors:** thunderstorms (the largest), precipitation static, night effect, station interference, mountain effect, coastal refraction, quadrantal error, dip error in turns. No failure warning apart from losing the ident.\n\n**Range factors:**\n- Power: range ∝ √power, so four times the power doubles the range.\n- Lower frequency gives more range.\n- More range over sea.\n- N0N A1A gives more range than N0N A2A.\n- Accuracy about ±5° for 95%, by day only.","keys":[{"k":"Thunderstorm (largest)","m":["thunderstorm","lightning"]},{"k":"Night effect, coastal, mountain, quadrantal","m":["night effect","coastal","mountain","quadrantal"]},{"k":"Range ∝ √power","m":["square root","root of power","sqrt","4 times","four times"]},{"k":"Lower frequency more range","m":["lower frequenc","low frequenc"]},{"k":"More over sea","m":["sea","water"]},{"k":"A1A more range than A2A","m":["a1a"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-19","type":"mcq","q":"Which is the largest source of ADF error?","src":"Oral bank p.99","ref":"OB54","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Thunderstorms","Quadrantal error","Dip error","Coastal refraction"],"a":0,"why":"Thunderstorms are listed as the largest ADF error.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-15","type":"num","q":"NDB range is proportional to the square root of power. By what factor must power increase to double the range?","src":"Oral bank p.99","ref":"OB54","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":4,"unit":"×","why":"Range ∝ √power, so four times the power doubles the range.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-20","type":"mcq","q":"NDB assigned frequency band (Annex 10)?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.4.4.1","ref":"OB54","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["190 to 1750 kHz","108 to 117.95 MHz","75 MHz","2 to 30 MHz"],"a":0,"why":"NDBs are assigned frequencies between 190 and 1750 kHz; most are below about 450 kHz.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-21","type":"mcq","q":"What is the typical NDB/ADF accuracy?","src":"Oral bank p.99","ref":"OB54","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["About ±5° for 95%, by day only","About ±1° at all times","About ±2° day and night","About ±10° for 95%, by day only"],"a":0,"why":"About ±5° for 95%, by day only.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-23","type":"recall","q":"How is an NDB approach flown on the 737?","src":"FCTM 5.55; OM-A 28.4","ref":"OB54","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**With FMC lateral guidance, monitored on raw ADF.**\n- Under IAN, the FMA shows FAC (final approach course) and G/P (glide path).\n- Raw ADF data is monitored throughout.\n- AIX does not fly NDB approaches at night (OM-A 28.4).","keys":[{"k":"FMC lateral guidance","m":["fmc"]},{"k":"IAN: FAC and G/P","m":["fac","ian","g p","glide path"]},{"k":"Raw ADF monitoring","m":["raw","monitor"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-24","type":"recall","q":"VOR errors: what are they, and what is the ground station accuracy?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.2.1, 3.3.3.2; Oral bank p.89","ref":"OB55","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Ground station error is within ±2° (0 to 40° elevation); total system about ±5°.**\nErrors:\n- beacon alignment;\n- site error (terrain and obstacles near the station);\n- propagation error (reflections en route);\n- airborne equipment error;\n- pilotage error;\n- plus the cone of confusion overhead.\nThe monitor shuts the station down or removes the ident for a bearing shift of more than 1°.","keys":[{"k":"Ground error ±2°","m":["2 degrees","2 deg","plus minus 2","two degrees"]},{"k":"Total ±5°","m":["5 degrees","5 deg","plus minus 5","five degrees"]},{"k":"Site error","m":["site"]},{"k":"Propagation error","m":["propagation","reflection"]},{"k":"Cone of confusion","m":["cone"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-16","type":"num","q":"Annex 10: VOR ground station bearing error must be within how many degrees (elevation 0 to 40°)?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.3.2","ref":"OB55","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2,"unit":"°","why":"Within ±2°. Total system accuracy is about ±5°.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-25","type":"recall","q":"What are scalloping and beam bending? (Bank: beam bending is a signal bending towards a denser medium.)","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 3.2.1, 3.2.2; NR42","ref":"OB55","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Both come from multipath reflections, not refraction.**\n- **Scalloping:** course roughness, a rapid oscillation of the indicated radial, from reflections off terrain, buildings, trees and power lines. Wind farms near a VOR are a growing source.\n- **Beam bending:** a slower, steadier displacement of the radial, from the same reflection mechanism.\n- The bank's \"bends towards a denser medium\" describes refraction; VOR and ILS beam bends come from reflections off terrain, buildings and hangars.","keys":[{"k":"Multipath reflections","m":["reflect","multipath"]},{"k":"Scalloping: rapid oscillation","m":["oscillat","roughness","rapid"]},{"k":"Beam bend: slow steady displacement","m":["steady","slow","displace"]},{"k":"Not refraction","m":["not refraction","refraction"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["denser medium","towards denser"],"why":"That is refraction; beam bends are caused by reflections"}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-22","type":"mcq","q":"What causes VOR beam bending?","src":"NR42; Annex 10 Vol I Att C 3.2.1","ref":"OB55","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Reflections (multipath) off terrain, buildings and hangars","Refraction towards a denser medium","Ionospheric sky wave","Doppler shift from aircraft speed"],"a":0,"why":"Beam bends and scalloping come from reflections. The bank's \"denser medium\" answer describes refraction.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-23","type":"mcq","q":"What is the VOR band as written in Annex 10 3.3.2.1?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.2.1","ref":"OB55","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["111.975 to 117.975 MHz, plus 108 to 111.975 MHz where allowed","108.10 to 111.95 MHz only","112 to 118 MHz only","118 to 137 MHz"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 gives 111.975 to 117.975 MHz, plus 108 to 111.975 MHz where allowed (even tenths, ILS on odd tenths).","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-26","type":"recall","q":"Doppler VOR vs conventional VOR: how do the signals differ, and why is DVOR better? (Bank: because FM is less subject to interference.)","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 3.2.1; Oral bank p.101","ref":"OB56","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"**In a DVOR the reference 30 Hz is AM and the variable 30 Hz is FM, the opposite of a CVOR.** The aircraft receiver is unchanged.\n- **Main reason it is better:** the variable signal comes from a large ring of antennas (Doppler principle), a wide aperture much less affected by reflections.\n- Annex 10: DVOR is more resistant to multipath and suits challenging sites.\n- Result: much smaller site error and scalloping, more stable radials.\n- The bank's \"FM is less subject to interference\" is only a minor point.","keys":[{"k":"DVOR reference AM, variable FM","m":["reference am","reference is am","variable fm","variable is fm","swapped","reversed"]},{"k":"Large ring / wide aperture","m":["aperture","ring","array"]},{"k":"Less multipath / site error","m":["multipath","site error","reflection"]},{"k":"Receiver unchanged","m":["receiver","same receiver","unchanged"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["fm is less","fm less subject"],"why":"Minor point; the main reason is the wide-aperture array reducing multipath"}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-24","type":"mcq","q":"In a Doppler VOR, which 30 Hz signal is AM?","src":"Oral bank p.101; Standard theory","ref":"OB56","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["The reference","The variable","Both","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"DVOR reverses the CVOR roles: reference 30 Hz AM, variable 30 Hz FM.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-25","type":"mcq","q":"What is the MAIN reason a DVOR is more accurate than a CVOR?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 3.2.1","ref":"OB56","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["Its large-aperture antenna array reduces site (multipath) error","FM is less subject to interference than AM","It transmits on a higher frequency","It has a more sensitive monitor"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 Att C 3.2.1: DVOR is more resistant to multipath. The FM argument in the bank is minor.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-27","type":"recall","q":"How do you work out where you are from the CDI, and why does VOR tracking get harder near the station?","src":"Standard theory; NR47","ref":"OB57","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**The CDI shows angular deviation, not distance.**\n- 1:60: distance off (NM) = angle off (°) × distance from station (NM) / 60. 5° off at 60 NM is 5 NM; at 6 NM it is only 0.5 NM.\n- So sensitivity rises as you close: reduce intercept angles and lead turns near the station.\n- Set FROM for outbound, TO for inbound, or a basic CDI gives reverse sensing.\n- Wind: bracket to find the drift.\n- Overhead: cone of confusion; hold heading until FROM is steady.","keys":[{"k":"Angular, not distance","m":["angular","angle"]},{"k":"1 in 60 rule","m":["1 in 60","1:60","one in sixty","1 60","160 rule"]},{"k":"Sensitivity increases near station","m":["sensitiv","converge"]},{"k":"Reverse sensing","m":["reverse sensing","reverse"]},{"k":"Cone of confusion overhead","m":["cone"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-17","type":"num","q":"You are 5° off a radial at 6 NM from the VOR. How far off track are you?","src":"Standard theory (1:60)","ref":"OB57","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":0.5,"unit":"NM","why":"1:60: 5 × 6 / 60 = 0.5 NM. The same 5° at 60 NM would be 5 NM.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-18","type":"num","q":"You are 4° off a radial at 45 NM from the VOR. How far off track are you?","src":"Standard theory (1:60)","ref":"OB57","prio":3,"ans":3,"unit":"NM","why":"1:60: 4 × 45 / 60 = 3 NM.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-recall-28","type":"recall","q":"What are the VOR/DME co-location criteria?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.5.2.5, 3.5.2.6.1, 3.5.3.1.2.1, Att C 7.2.3.3","ref":"OB58","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**A DME associated with a VOR must use the standard frequency pairing, be collocated within Annex 10 limits, and follow the associated identification rules.**\n- Antenna separation: not more than **80 m (260 ft)** in terminal areas, approach, or where the highest fixing accuracy is needed; not more than **600 m (2000 ft)** for other purposes.\n- Coverage at least that of the VOR, as far as practicable.\n- For RNAV, if separation exceeds 30 m (100 ft) the DME should have its own surveyed position published.","keys":[{"k":"Frequency pairing","m":["pairing","paired"]},{"k":"80 m terminal/approach","m":["80 m","80m","260 ft","260ft","80 metres","80 meters"]},{"k":"600 m other","m":["600 m","600m","2000 ft","2000ft","600 metres","600 meters"]},{"k":"Associated ident","m":["ident"]},{"k":"30 m RNAV published position","m":["30 m","30m","100 ft","100ft"]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-19","type":"num","q":"Maximum VOR/DME antenna separation in terminal areas or for approach (Annex 10)?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.5.2.6.1","ref":"OB58","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":80,"unit":"m","why":"Not more than 80 m (260 ft) where highest fixing accuracy is needed; 600 m (2000 ft) for other purposes.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-num-20","type":"num","q":"Maximum VOR/DME antenna separation for purposes other than terminal/approach (Annex 10)?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.5.2.6.1","ref":"OB58","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":600,"unit":"m","why":"Not more than 600 m (2000 ft); 80 m (260 ft) in terminal areas and approach.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-mcq-26","type":"mcq","q":"Above what VOR/DME separation should the DME have its own surveyed position published for RNAV use?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 7.2.3.3","ref":"OB58","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["30 m (100 ft)","80 m (260 ft)","600 m (2000 ft)","10 m (33 ft)"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 Att C 7.2.3.3: if separation exceeds 30 m (100 ft), because DME location errors become RNAV position errors.","topic":10},{"id":"t10-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"You are tracking inbound on a VOR radial for an arrival. Talk me through it.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.7; OM-A 25.4; Standard theory","ref":"S52","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"How does the VOR give you the radial?","a":"Phase comparison of two 30 Hz signals; in a CVOR reference is FM on a 9960 Hz subcarrier, variable is AM; DVOR reverses them.","keys":[{"k":"Phase comparison","m":["phase"]},{"k":"30 Hz","m":["30 hz","30hz"]}]},{"q":"Why does the needle get more sensitive as you close?","a":"The CDI shows angle, not distance; by 1:60 the same angle is fewer NM off track near the station, so reduce intercept angles and lead turns.","keys":[{"k":"Angular","m":["angle","angular"]},{"k":"1 in 60","m":["1 in 60","1:60","one in sixty","160"]}]},{"q":"The radial starts oscillating rapidly. What is that?","a":"Scalloping, from multipath reflections off terrain, buildings, trees, power lines or wind farms. A DVOR suffers far less.","keys":[{"k":"Scalloping","m":["scallop"]},{"k":"Reflections","m":["reflect","multipath"]}]},{"q":"Now the flag appears and the ident stops. Why?","a":"The station monitor has shut it down or removed ident: bearing shift over 1°, 15% drop in modulation, or monitor failure.","keys":[{"k":"Monitor","m":["monitor"]},{"k":"More than 1°","m":["1 degree","1 deg","one degree","more than 1"]}]},{"q":"What do you do?","a":"En route or on the arrival, continue on the FMC if within specification and tell ATC. On a VOR approach, go around (ground equipment failure).","keys":[{"k":"FMC if within spec","m":["fmc"]},{"k":"Tell ATC","m":["atc"]},{"k":"Go around on approach","m":["go around","go-around","missed"]}]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"DME drill: you are flying an arrival using DME.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.5.3.5.1, 3.5.4.4.1, 3.5.2.6.1","ref":"Key facts: DME","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"How does DME measure distance?","a":"The aircraft interrogates on UHF; the ground replies 63 MHz away after a 50 µs delay; round-trip time minus delay gives slant range.","keys":[{"k":"Interrogate and reply","m":["interrogat"]},{"k":"63 MHz","m":["63 mhz","63mhz","63 mega"]},{"k":"50 µs","m":["50 micro","50 us","50us","50 µs"]}]},{"q":"What does it read overhead at FL360?","a":"About 6 NM, because it is slant range, equal to your height.","keys":[{"k":"About 6 NM","m":["6 nm","6nm","6 nautical","six"]},{"k":"Slant range","m":["slant"]}]},{"q":"Lots of traffic: what happens when the station is saturated?","a":"Capacity is the area's peak traffic or 100 aircraft, whichever is less; beyond that it answers the strongest interrogations.","keys":[{"k":"100 aircraft","m":["100"]},{"k":"Strongest interrogations","m":["strongest"]}]},{"q":"The DME is co-located with the VOR. How close must they be?","a":"Not more than 80 m (260 ft) for terminal and approach use; 600 m (2000 ft) otherwise; standard pairing and associated ident.","keys":[{"k":"80 m","m":["80 m","80m","260"]},{"k":"600 m","m":["600","2000"]}]}],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw the ADF loop and sense antenna polar diagrams and show how the ambiguity is resolved.","src":"Oral bank p.99","ref":"OB54","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Loop:** figure-of-eight polar diagram with two nulls 180° apart (ambiguity).\n- **Sense:** omnidirectional circle.\n- **Combined:** a cardioid with a single null, which removes the 180° ambiguity.","checklist":["Loop drawn as a figure of eight","Two nulls marked, 180° apart","Sense antenna drawn as a circle","Combined pattern drawn as a cardioid","Single null on the cardioid marked","Labelled: ambiguity resolved"],"topic":10},{"id":"t10-draw-2","type":"draw","q":"Draw the CVOR polar diagram: how is the variable signal produced?","src":"Oral bank p.89; Standard theory","ref":"OB55","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- An **omnidirectional** reference signal (circle).\n- A rotating **figure of eight**.\n- Combined, they give a rotating **limaçon** (circle plus figure of eight), rotating 30 times a second, which produces the 30 Hz variable signal.\n- The phase difference between reference and variable equals the magnetic bearing from the station.","checklist":["Omnidirectional circle drawn","Figure of eight drawn","Combined limaçon (cardioid-like) shape drawn","Rotation at 30 Hz noted","Phase difference = magnetic bearing (radial) labelled"],"topic":10},{"id":"t11-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"What is the ILS localiser frequency band?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["108.10 to 111.95 MHz (odd tenths)","108.00 to 117.95 MHz","328.6 to 335.4 MHz","112.00 to 117.95 MHz (odd tenths)"],"a":0,"why":"LOC uses 108.10 to 111.95 MHz on odd tenths; VORs use the even tenths in that range.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"What is the ILS glide path frequency band?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["UHF 328.6 to 335.4 MHz","VHF 108.10 to 111.95 MHz","UHF 962 to 1213 MHz","75 MHz"],"a":0,"why":"GS is UHF 328.6 to 335.4 MHz, automatically paired with the LOC, which is why it isn't published.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"Why is the glide path frequency not published on the chart?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["It is paired with the localiser frequency and selected automatically","It is classified","It is the same as the localiser frequency","It changes daily"],"a":0,"why":"The GS frequency is paired with the LOC, so tuning the LOC tunes the GS.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"Explain ILS modulation: 90 Hz and 150 Hz on the localiser and glide path.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Deviation is measured as DDM, the difference in depth of modulation, between 90 Hz and 150 Hz.**\n- Localiser: 90 Hz predominates on the left, 150 Hz on the right (as seen on approach).\n- Glide path: 90 Hz above the path, 150 Hz below.\n- On the centreline or path the DDM is zero.","keys":[{"k":"DDM","m":["ddm","difference in depth","depth of modulation"]},{"k":"90 Hz left (LOC)","m":["90 hz left","90 left","left 90","90 hz on the left","90 hz predominates on the left"]},{"k":"150 Hz right (LOC)","m":["150 hz right","150 right","right 150","150 hz on the right"]},{"k":"90 above, 150 below (GS)","m":["90 above","above 90","90 hz above","150 below","below 150","150 hz below"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"On the glide path, which modulation predominates when you are BELOW the path?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["150 Hz","90 Hz","Equal 90 and 150 Hz","1020 Hz ident"],"a":0,"why":"GS: 90 Hz above, 150 Hz below.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"On the localiser, on approach, which modulation predominates to the LEFT of the centreline?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["90 Hz","150 Hz","Equal 90 and 150 Hz","30 Hz"],"a":0,"why":"LOC: 90 Hz predominates on the left, 150 Hz on the right (on approach).","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"What is the coverage of the localiser and the glide path?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1; Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.3.1","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Localiser:** ±10° to 25 NM, and ±35° to 17 NM.\n- **Glide path:** ±8° in azimuth to 10 NM, vertically from 1.75θ down to 0.45θ (to 0.30θ if the intercept procedure needs it); for 3° that is about 5.25° down to 1.35°.\n- Outside coverage the signals are unreliable, including false glide paths and back-beam or side signals.","keys":[{"k":"LOC ±10° to 25 NM","m":["25 nm","25nm","25 nautical"]},{"k":"LOC ±35° to 17 NM","m":["17 nm","17nm","17 nautical"]},{"k":"GS ±8° to 10 NM","m":["10 nm","10nm","10 nautical","8 degrees","8 deg"]},{"k":"GS 1.75θ to 0.45θ","m":["175","045","1 75","0 45"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-1","type":"num","q":"Localiser coverage within ±10° of the course extends to what range?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":25,"unit":"NM","why":"LOC: ±10° to 25 NM, ±35° to 17 NM.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-2","type":"num","q":"Localiser coverage between 10° and 35° either side of the course extends to what range?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":17,"unit":"NM","why":"LOC: ±10° to 25 NM, ±35° to 17 NM.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-3","type":"num","q":"Glide path azimuth coverage is ±8° to what range?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.3.1","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":10,"unit":"NM","why":"GS coverage is ±8° in azimuth to at least 10 NM (18.5 km).","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"Where are the localiser and glide path antennas sited?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Localiser:** beyond the stop end of the runway, on the extended centreline.\n- **Glide path:** 120 to 180 m to the side of the runway, abeam the touchdown point (touchdown zone).\n- The marker beacons lie on the approach path: OM about 3.9 NM, MM about 3500 ft, IM 75 to 450 m from the threshold.","keys":[{"k":"LOC beyond stop end","m":["stop end","far end","upwind end","end of the runway"]},{"k":"GS 120 to 180 m to the side","m":["120","180"]},{"k":"GS abeam touchdown","m":["touchdown","touch down"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"How far to the side of the runway is the glide path antenna typically sited?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["120 to 180 m","30 to 60 m","300 to 450 m","600 to 900 m"],"a":0,"why":"GS: 120 to 180 m to the side, abeam the touchdown point.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"What ILS ground tolerances does the OM-A give for CAT II/III?","src":"OM-A 45.1.14","ref":"Key facts: ILS ground tolerances","prio":2,"a":"- **Localiser:** within ±25 ft at the threshold for CAT II, ±10 ft for CAT III.\n- **Changeover to standby transmitter:** within 2 s (under 2 s for CAT II/III).\n- **Glide slope:** crosses the threshold at 50 ft (+10/−0 ft) for CAT II/III.\n- CAT II/III ILS also has tight beam-bend limits.","keys":[{"k":"±25 ft CAT II","m":["25 ft","25ft","25 feet"]},{"k":"±10 ft CAT III","m":["10 ft","10ft","10 feet"]},{"k":"Changeover 2 s","m":["2 s","2s","2 sec","two sec"]},{"k":"GS 50 ft at threshold","m":["50 ft","50ft","50 feet"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-4","type":"num","q":"For CAT II, the localiser must be within what tolerance at the threshold?","src":"OM-A 45.1.14","ref":"Key facts: ILS","prio":2,"ans":25,"unit":"ft","why":"±25 ft for CAT II, ±10 ft for CAT III.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-5","type":"num","q":"For CAT III, the localiser must be within what tolerance at the threshold?","src":"OM-A 45.1.14","ref":"Key facts: ILS","prio":2,"ans":10,"unit":"ft","why":"±10 ft for CAT III, ±25 ft for CAT II.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-6","type":"num","q":"Maximum changeover time to the standby ILS transmitter for CAT II/III?","src":"OM-A 45.1.14","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":2,"unit":"s","why":"Changeover must take under 2 s.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"What are the ILS critical and sensitive areas?","src":"OM-A Ch 0, 45.1.10, 45.1.15","ref":"Key facts: ILS critical and sensitive areas","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Critical area:** kept clear of vehicles and aircraft during ALL ILS operations.\n- **Sensitive area:** extends beyond the critical area and is controlled during LVP.\n- Aircraft or vehicles near the LOC or GS antennas distort the signal.\n- **The worst interference** comes from an aircraft taking off over the localiser: a brief LOC excursion and autopilot disturbance. That is why these areas are protected in LVP.","keys":[{"k":"Critical: clear during all ILS ops","m":["all ils","all operations","always"]},{"k":"Sensitive: controlled in LVP","m":["lvp","low visibility"]},{"k":"Sensitive extends beyond critical","m":["beyond","outside","larger"]},{"k":"Aircraft taking off over LOC is worst","m":["taking off","take off","takeoff","departing"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"Which ILS protected area must be kept clear during ALL ILS operations?","src":"OM-A Ch 0, 45.1.10","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Critical area","Sensitive area","Clearway","Runway strip"],"a":0,"why":"Critical area: clear during all ILS operations. Sensitive area: controlled during LVP.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"According to OM-A, what causes the worst ILS interference?","src":"OM-A 45.1.15","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["An aircraft taking off over the localiser","A vehicle near the glide path antenna","FM broadcast stations","Heavy rain"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 45.1.15: an aircraft taking off over the localiser, causing a brief LOC excursion and autopilot disturbance.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match each marker beacon to its audio frequency.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.4","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Outer marker","400 Hz"],["Middle marker","1300 Hz"],["Inner marker","3000 Hz"]],"why":"OM 400 Hz, MM 1300 Hz, IM 3000 Hz; all on a 75 MHz carrier.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match each marker beacon to its keying.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.5","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Outer marker","2 dashes per second, continuous"],["Middle marker","Alternate dots and dashes"],["Inner marker","6 dots per second, continuous"]],"why":"OM dashes, MM dots and dashes, IM dots. The faster, higher-pitched signals come closer to the threshold.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-match-3","type":"match","q":"Match each marker beacon to its cockpit light colour.","src":"NR30 (airborne convention)","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Outer marker","Blue"],["Middle marker","Amber"],["Inner marker","White"]],"why":"Blue, amber, white. The light colours are an airborne convention, not in Annex 10.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-match-4","type":"match","q":"Match the marker to its siting from the threshold.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.6","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Outer marker","7.2 km (3.9 NM); 6.5 to 11.1 km where needed"],["Middle marker","1050 m (3500 ft) ±150 m"],["Inner marker","75 to 450 m"]],"why":"OM about 1300 ft on a 3° GS, MM about 200 ft, IM meant to be crossed at the lowest CAT II DH.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"Outer marker: light colour, frequency, Morse and distance.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.2 to 3.1.7.6","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Outer marker: blue light, 400 Hz audio, 2 dashes per second, about 3.9 NM from the threshold.**\n- Carrier 75 MHz, horizontally polarised.\n- Sited 7.2 km (3.9 NM) from the threshold; 6.5 to 11.1 km (3.5 to 6 NM) where needed.\n- About 1300 ft on a 3° glide slope.\n- Purpose: height, distance and equipment-functioning check on intermediate and final approach.\n- Light colour is an airborne convention, not Annex 10.","keys":[{"k":"Blue","m":["blue"]},{"k":"400 Hz","m":["400 hz","400hz","400 hertz","400"]},{"k":"2 dashes per second","m":["dashes","dash"]},{"k":"75 MHz","m":["75 mhz","75mhz","75 mega"]},{"k":"3.9 NM / 7.2 km","m":["39 nm","3 9 nm","72 km","7 2 km","39nm","4 nm","4nm"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"Middle and inner markers: frequency, keying, colour and purpose.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.4 to 3.1.7.6","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Middle marker:** 1300 Hz, alternate dots and dashes, amber; 1050 m (3500 ft) ±150 m from the threshold, about 200 ft on a 3° GS. Warns that visual guidance is imminent in low visibility.\n- **Inner marker:** 3000 Hz, 6 dots per second, white; 75 to 450 m from the threshold, meant to be crossed at the lowest CAT II DH. Warns that the threshold is imminent.\n- All on 75 MHz.","keys":[{"k":"MM 1300 Hz","m":["1300"]},{"k":"MM amber, dots and dashes","m":["amber"]},{"k":"IM 3000 Hz","m":["3000"]},{"k":"IM white, dots","m":["white"]},{"k":"MM warns visual guidance imminent","m":["visual guidance","visual"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-7","type":"num","q":"What carrier frequency do all marker beacons use?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.2","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":75,"unit":"MHz","why":"All markers transmit on 75 MHz, horizontally polarised, uninterrupted carrier.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-8","type":"num","q":"Outer marker audio modulation frequency?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.4","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":400,"unit":"Hz","why":"OM 400 Hz, MM 1300 Hz, IM 3000 Hz.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-9","type":"num","q":"Middle marker audio modulation frequency?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.4","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1300,"unit":"Hz","why":"OM 400 Hz, MM 1300 Hz, IM 3000 Hz.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-10","type":"num","q":"Inner marker audio modulation frequency?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.4","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":3000,"unit":"Hz","why":"OM 400 Hz, MM 1300 Hz, IM 3000 Hz.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-11","type":"num","q":"Nominal distance of the outer marker from the threshold, in NM?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.6","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":3.9,"unit":"NM","tol":0.1,"why":"7.2 km (3.9 NM); 6.5 to 11.1 km (3.5 to 6 NM) where needed.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-12","type":"num","q":"Nominal distance of the middle marker from the threshold, in feet?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.6","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":3500,"unit":"ft","why":"1050 m (3500 ft) ±150 m.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Approximately what height on a 3° glide slope is the aircraft over the outer marker?","src":"NR30","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["1300 ft","200 ft","2500 ft","700 ft"],"a":0,"why":"About 1300 ft at the OM; about 200 ft at the MM.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"At what height is the inner marker meant to be crossed?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.6","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["The lowest CAT II DH","200 ft","1000 ft AAL","The CAT I DA"],"a":0,"why":"The IM is meant to be crossed at the lowest CAT II DH.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-match-5","type":"match","q":"Match the marker to its coverage length along the glide path.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.3","ref":"NR30","prio":3,"pairs":[["Inner","150 m ±50 m"],["Middle","300 m ±100 m"],["Outer","600 m ±200 m"]],"why":"Annex 10 3.1.7.3 coverage along the glide path.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"When must a marker beacon monitor send a warning to the control point?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.7","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Failure of modulation or keying, or power below 50%","Power below 90%","Only on total carrier failure","Bearing shift over 1°"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 3.1.7.7: modulation or keying failure, or power below 50%.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"Are the marker beacon cockpit light colours (blue, amber, white) specified in Annex 10?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"o":["No, they are an airborne convention","Yes, in 3.1.7.4","Only for the outer marker","Yes, in Annex 14"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 specifies frequency, modulation and keying; the light colours are an airborne convention.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"Can DME replace marker beacons? What are the conditions?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.6.5","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Yes.** Annex 10 allows DME instead of markers.\n- It must give equivalent distance information.\n- It must be published per Annex 15.\n- A DME replacing the middle marker must be frequency-paired with the localiser.\n- In practice most ILSs now use DME or published altitude/DME checks instead of markers. GPS fixes also replace them.","keys":[{"k":"Yes, allowed","m":["yes","allowed","can replace","permitted"]},{"k":"Equivalent distance information","m":["equivalent"]},{"k":"Published (Annex 15)","m":["publish","annex 15"]},{"k":"Paired with localiser","m":["paired","pairing"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-13","type":"num","q":"How many marker beacons does an installation normally have?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7.1","ref":"NR30","prio":3,"ans":2,"why":"Normally two; one or a third if the authority decides.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"What are the components of an ILS?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1; OM-A 45.1.14","ref":"NR9","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Localiser, glide path, range information, visual aids and ground monitors.**\n- **Localiser:** VHF 108.10 to 111.95 MHz odd tenths, antenna beyond the stop end, 90 Hz left / 150 Hz right, Morse ident.\n- **Glide path:** UHF 328.6 to 335.4 MHz paired with the LOC, antenna 120 to 180 m to the side abeam touchdown, 90 Hz above / 150 Hz below.\n- **Range:** OM, MM (and IM) on 75 MHz, now mostly replaced by DME.\n- **Visual aids:** approach lights, TDZ and centreline lights, PAPI.\n- **Monitors:** switch to standby or shut down; changeover under 2 s for CAT II/III.","keys":[{"k":"Localiser","m":["localiser","localizer","loc"]},{"k":"Glide path","m":["glide"]},{"k":"Markers / DME","m":["marker","dme"]},{"k":"Visual aids / lights","m":["light","papi","visual"]},{"k":"Monitor and standby","m":["monitor","standby"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"What is the ILS reference datum (point T)?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.1, 3.1.5.1.4, 3.1.5.1.5; OM-A 45.1.14, 27.5","ref":"NR15","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**A point at a specified height above the intersection of the runway centreline and the threshold, through which the downward extended straight portion of the glide path passes.**\n- Height 15 m (50 ft), tolerance +3 m (10 ft).\n- A Standard for CAT II/III, a Recommendation for CAT I.\n- Assumes up to 5.8 m (19 ft) between the GS antenna and the lowest part of the wheels.\n- OM-A: GS crosses the threshold at 50 ft (+10/−0) for CAT II/III; TCH about 50 ft.","keys":[{"k":"Above threshold on centreline","m":["threshold","centreline","centerline"]},{"k":"Extended straight glide path passes through","m":["extended","straight"]},{"k":"15 m / 50 ft","m":["50 ft","50ft","50 feet","15 m","15m"]},{"k":"+3 m / 10 ft tolerance","m":["10 ft","10ft","3 m","plus 10"]},{"k":"Antenna to wheels 5.8 m / 19 ft","m":["19 ft","19ft","58 m","5 8","wheel"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-14","type":"num","q":"What is the nominal height of the ILS reference datum (point T) above the threshold?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.1.4","ref":"NR15","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":50,"unit":"ft","why":"15 m (50 ft), tolerance +3 m (10 ft).","topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-15","type":"num","q":"The ILS reference datum height assumes up to how many feet between the aircraft's GS antenna and the lowest part of its wheels?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.1.4","ref":"NR15","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":19,"unit":"ft","why":"Up to 5.8 m (19 ft).","topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"Is the 15 m (50 ft) ILS reference datum height a Standard or a Recommendation?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.1.4, 3.1.5.1.5","ref":"NR15","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Standard for CAT II/III, Recommendation for CAT I","Standard for all categories","Recommendation for all categories","Standard for CAT I, Recommendation for CAT II/III"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 3.1.5.1.4 and 3.1.5.1.5.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-match-6","type":"match","q":"Match the ILS point to its definition (Annex 10).","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.1","ref":"NR15","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Point A","On the GP, 7.5 km (4 NM) from threshold"],["Point B","On the GP, 1050 m (3500 ft) from threshold"],["Point C","Where the straight GP is 30 m (100 ft) above the threshold plane"],["Point D","4 m above centreline, 900 m from threshold towards LOC"],["Point E","4 m above centreline, 600 m from the stop end"],["Point T","Reference datum, 15 m (50 ft) over threshold"]],"why":"Annex 10 uses these points to say where each facility category's accuracy limits apply.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-order-1","type":"order","q":"Put the ILS points in order along the approach, from furthest out to the runway's stop end.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.1","ref":"NR15","asked":true,"prio":3,"items":["Point A (4 NM)","Point B (3500 ft)","Point C (100 ft above threshold plane)","Point T (threshold)","Point D (900 m past threshold)","Point E (600 m from stop end)"],"why":"A at 7.5 km, B at 1050 m, C where GP is 100 ft high, T over the threshold, D 900 m along the runway towards the localiser, E 600 m from the stop end.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw the ILS reference points A, B, C, T, D, E along the approach and runway.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.1","ref":"NR15","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"Side view of a 3° glide path to the threshold and along the runway:\n- **A:** on the GP, 7.5 km (4 NM) out.\n- **B:** on the GP, 1050 m (3500 ft) out.\n- **C:** where the straight GP is 30 m (100 ft) above the threshold plane.\n- **T:** 15 m (50 ft) over the threshold, the reference datum.\n- **D:** 4 m (12 ft) above the centreline, 900 m (3000 ft) past the threshold.\n- **E:** 4 m (12 ft) above the centreline, 600 m (2000 ft) from the stop end.","checklist":["Glide path drawn to the threshold","A at 4 NM on the GP","B at 3500 ft on the GP","C at 100 ft height","T at 50 ft over the threshold","D 900 m along the runway at 12 ft","E 600 m from the stop end at 12 ft","LOC antenna beyond the stop end"],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"Is the middle marker mandatory for ILS approaches? What if the outer marker is out?","src":"OM-A Table 7.3.D","ref":"NR28","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**No, the middle marker is not mandatory.**\n- MM out: no effect on minima.\n- OM out: no effect if replaced by a height check at 1000 ft, or by DME or an altitude/DME check (OM-A Table 7.3.D).\n- In practice most ILSs now use DME instead of markers.","keys":[{"k":"MM not mandatory","m":["not mandatory","not required","no it is not","optional"]},{"k":"MM out: no effect on minima","m":["no effect","no change"]},{"k":"OM replaced by DME / altitude check","m":["dme","altitude check","height check"]},{"k":"Height check at 1000 ft","m":["1000 ft","1000ft","1000 feet","1000"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"The middle marker is unserviceable. What is the effect on ILS minima?","src":"OM-A Table 7.3.D","ref":"NR28","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["No effect","Add 50 ft to the DA","Raise RVR by 200 m","ILS not authorised"],"a":0,"why":"MM out has no effect on minima.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"What are marker locators (compass locators)?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR29","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Low-power NDBs at the outer marker (LOM) or middle marker (LMM).**\n- LF/MF, roughly 190 to 535 kHz, about 10 to 25 NM range.\n- Two-letter ident.\n- Uses: transition to the ILS and holding, lead-in to the localiser, a fix for marker-out cases, and the FAF for an NDB approach.","keys":[{"k":"Low-power NDB","m":["ndb","low power"]},{"k":"At OM or MM (LOM / LMM)","m":["lom","lmm","outer marker","middle marker"]},{"k":"190 to 535 kHz","m":["190","535"]},{"k":"10 to 25 NM","m":["25 nm","25nm","10 to 25"]},{"k":"Two-letter ident","m":["two letter","2 letter"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"What is the approximate range of a compass (marker) locator?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR29","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["10 to 25 NM","50 to 100 NM","2 to 5 NM","100 to 200 NM"],"a":0,"why":"About 10 to 25 NM, LF/MF roughly 190 to 535 kHz.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"What are the ILS errors?","src":"OM-A 45.1.14, 45.1.15; Standard theory","ref":"NR42","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Beam bends and scalloping:** reflections off terrain, buildings and hangars; limit how low autoland can be used; CAT II/III has tight beam-bend limits.\n- **Critical/sensitive area interference:** worst is an aircraft taking off over the localiser.\n- **False glide paths:** side lobes at about 6° and 9°; the 6° lobe has reverse sensing.\n- **Coverage limits:** LOC ±10° to 25 NM, ±35° to 17 NM; GS ±8° to 10 NM; back-beam unreliable.\n- **FM radio interference** with LOC receivers.\n- **GS not usable** below a published height on some ILSs.\n- **Equipment:** receiver or monitor faults; flag on loss of signal.","keys":[{"k":"Beam bends / scalloping","m":["bend","scallop"]},{"k":"Critical/sensitive area","m":["critical","sensitive"]},{"k":"False glide path","m":["false"]},{"k":"Coverage limits","m":["coverage","back beam","backbeam"]},{"k":"FM interference","m":["fm radio","fm broadcast","fm interference","fm station"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-16","type":"mcq","q":"At what angles do false glide paths form above a 3° ILS glide slope?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR42","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["About 6° and 9°","About 1.5° and 4.5°","About 4° and 5°","About 12° and 15°"],"a":0,"why":"Side lobes at about 2× and 3× the glide angle: 6° and 9°. The 6° lobe has reverse sensing. There is none below the true GS.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-17","type":"mcq","q":"Which false glide path lobe has reverse sensing?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR42","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["The 6° lobe","The 9° lobe","Both","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"The 6° (2×) lobe has reverse sensing.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"What does the Boeing FCTM say about false glide slope capture?","src":"FCTM 5.16","ref":"NR42","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Aircraft have captured false glide slopes with a steady \"on glide slope\" indication from an ILS transmitter left in test mode.**\n- Detect it: cross-check the FAF crossing altitude and VNAV path before capture; normal pitch and descent rate after capture; altitude against distance, about 300 ft HAT per NM on 3°.\n- If suspected, go around unless visual.\n- Intercepting from well above, a false capture can cause a sudden pitch-up and rapid speed loss.","keys":[{"k":"Transmitter in test mode","m":["test mode","test"]},{"k":"Cross-check FAF altitude / VNAV","m":["faf","vnav"]},{"k":"300 ft per NM","m":["300 ft","300ft","300 feet","300"]},{"k":"Go around unless visual","m":["go around","go-around","goaround"]},{"k":"Pitch-up, speed loss from above","m":["pitch up","pitch-up","pitchup","speed loss","airspeed"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"FCTM: how do you intercept the glide slope from above?","src":"FCTM 5.18","ref":"NR42","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Localiser captured first; then APP armed, landing configuration, MCP altitude no lower than 1000 ft AFE, and V/S −1000 to −1500 fpm.**\n- A false capture from well above a 3° GS can cause a sudden pitch-up and rapid loss of airspeed.\n- If not captured and stable by 1000 ft AFE, go around.","keys":[{"k":"LOC captured first","m":["loc","localiser","localizer"]},{"k":"APP armed, landing config","m":["arm app","app armed","approach armed","arm approach","landing config","landing flap"]},{"k":"MCP altitude not below 1000 ft AFE","m":["1000 ft","1000ft","1000 feet","1000"]},{"k":"V/S 1000 to 1500 fpm","m":["1500"]},{"k":"Go around if not stable by 1000 ft","m":["go around","go-around","goaround"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-order-2","type":"order","q":"FCTM glide slope capture from above: put the steps in order.","src":"FCTM 5.18","ref":"NR42","prio":2,"items":["Localiser captured","Arm APP","Landing configuration","Set MCP altitude no lower than 1000 ft AFE","V/S −1000 to −1500 fpm","Not captured and stable by 1000 ft AFE: go around"],"why":"FCTM 5.18 sequence.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-16","type":"num","q":"On a 3° slope, roughly how many feet of height above touchdown per NM?","src":"FCTM 5.16","ref":"NR42","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":300,"unit":"ft/NM","why":"About 300 ft HAT per NM; the FCTM uses it to detect a false glide slope.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-18","type":"mcq","q":"FCTM: V/S to set when intercepting the glide slope from above?","src":"FCTM 5.18","ref":"NR42","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["−1000 to −1500 fpm","−500 to −700 fpm","−2000 to −2500 fpm","−700 to −1000 fpm"],"a":0,"why":"V/S −1000 to −1500 fpm with MCP altitude no lower than 1000 ft AFE.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"What is the glide slope coverage, and how sensitive is the indication?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.3.1, 3.1.5.6.1, 3.1.5.6.2; Oral bank p.91","ref":"OB59","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Coverage:** ±8° in azimuth to at least 10 NM, vertically from 1.75θ down to 0.45θ (0.30θ if the intercept needs it): about 5.25° to 1.35° on a 3° path.\n**Sensitivity (CAT I):** DDM 0.0875 at 0.07θ to 0.14θ above and below, recommended 0.12θ below: about 0.36° on 3°.\n- 0.0875 DDM is half full scale, roughly one dot on a two-dot display.\n- So the full sector is only about ±0.7° on 3°.\n- GS is UHF so a narrow, accurate beam comes from a practical antenna.","keys":[{"k":"±8° to 10 NM","m":["8 degrees","8 deg","10 nm","10nm"]},{"k":"1.75θ to 0.45θ","m":["175","1 75","045","0 45"]},{"k":"0.0875 DDM","m":["00875","0 0875","0875"]},{"k":"0.12θ about 0.36°","m":["012","0 12","036","0 36"]},{"k":"About ±0.7° full sector","m":["07 degrees","0 7 degrees","07 deg","07°"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-19","type":"mcq","q":"The bank says one dot of glide path deflection is 0.14°. What does Annex 10 actually say?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.6.1, 3.1.5.6.2","ref":"OB59","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["0.14θ, a fraction of the glide angle (about 0.42° at 3°); recommended 0.12θ (about 0.36°)","0.14° exactly, regardless of glide angle","0.0875°","1.4°"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 gives the CAT I sensitivity as DDM 0.0875 at 0.07θ to 0.14θ, recommended 0.12θ. At 3° 0.14θ is 0.42°.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"Bank: \"1 dot deflection on the GP is 0.14°.\" Correct this.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.6.1, 3.1.5.6.2","ref":"OB59","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"**It is 0.14θ, a fraction of the glide angle, not 0.14°.**\n- Annex 10 CAT I: DDM 0.0875 at 0.07θ to 0.14θ above and below the path, recommended 0.12θ below.\n- On a 3° path, 0.14θ is 0.42° and 0.12θ is about 0.36°.\n- 0.0875 DDM is half full scale, roughly one dot on a two-dot display.","keys":[{"k":"Theta, not degrees","m":["theta","glide angle","fraction"]},{"k":"0.42° at 3°","m":["042","0 42"]},{"k":"0.12θ about 0.36°","m":["036","0 36","012","0 12"]},{"k":"0.0875 DDM","m":["0875"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["014 degrees","0 14 degrees","014 deg"],"why":"It is 0.14θ, a fraction of the glide angle"}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-17","type":"num","q":"Glide path vertical coverage upper limit is 1.75θ. For a 3° glide path, what angle is that?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.3.1","ref":"OB59","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":5.25,"unit":"°","tol":0.05,"why":"1.75 × 3 = 5.25°; lower limit 0.45 × 3 = 1.35°.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-18","type":"num","q":"Glide path vertical coverage lower limit is 0.45θ. For a 3° glide path, what angle is that?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.3.1","ref":"OB59","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":1.35,"unit":"°","tol":0.05,"why":"0.45 × 3 = 1.35° (0.30θ if the intercept procedure needs it).","topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-20","type":"mcq","q":"Why is the glide path transmitted on UHF?","src":"Oral bank p.91","ref":"OB59","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["A higher frequency allows a narrow, accurate beam from a practical antenna","UHF has more range than VHF","To avoid FM broadcast interference only","UHF is not affected by multipath"],"a":0,"why":"Oral bank p.91.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"Can you fly an ILS approach without the glide slope?","src":"Oral bank p.6; OM-A 28.7.1(e); FCTM 5.55","ref":"OB60","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Yes, as a localiser-only (GS out) approach, which is a non-precision approach to the LOC MDA, not the ILS DA.**\n- OM-A: at the OM/FAF be prepared to fly it without glide slope to the localiser MDA, if authorised for that approach.\n- On the MAX (IAN): G/P prompt to OFF so the FMC glide path is flown; FMA shows VOR/LOC and G/P.\n- Fly it as a CDFA to DDA = MDA + 50 ft.\n- Check the chart for the LOC minima line and any extra equipment such as DME.","keys":[{"k":"Yes, LOC only, non-precision","m":["loc only","localiser only","localizer only","non precision","nonprecision","npa"]},{"k":"MDA not DA","m":["mda"]},{"k":"IAN G/P (FMC glide path)","m":["ian","g p","fmc"]},{"k":"CDFA, DDA = MDA + 50","m":["cdfa","50 ft","50ft","plus 50","dda"]},{"k":"Check chart minima / DME","m":["chart","dme"]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-19","type":"num","q":"On a localiser-only CDFA, what is added to the MDA to get the DDA?","src":"OB60; S18","ref":"OB60","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":50,"unit":"ft","why":"DDA = MDA + 50 ft.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-21","type":"mcq","q":"On the MAX, flying an ILS with the glide slope out, what does the FMA show?","src":"FCTM 5.55","ref":"OB60","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["VOR/LOC and G/P","VOR/LOC and G/S","FAC and G/S","LNAV and VNAV PTH"],"a":0,"why":"With the G/S prompt OFF the FMC glide path is flown: VOR/LOC and G/P.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"Is the ILS reference point the same as MEHT?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.1; Annex 14 2.12 e)","ref":"OB61","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"**No.**\n- The ILS reference datum (point T) is above the threshold on the centreline, where the extended straight glide path passes; nominal 15 m (50 ft).\n- MEHT (minimum eye height over the threshold) belongs to visual approach slope indicators: published for PAPI, APAPI and T-VASIS/AT-VASIS as the eye height over the threshold of the on-slope signal.\n- They differ because the GS antenna, the wheels and the pilot's eyes are at different heights; Annex 10's 50 ft assumes up to 19 ft antenna to wheels.\n- The bank conflates them.","keys":[{"k":"No, different","m":["different","not the same","no it is not","differ"]},{"k":"Point T, 50 ft, glide path","m":["50 ft","50ft","point t","reference datum"]},{"k":"MEHT is PAPI/VASIS","m":["papi","vasis"]},{"k":"Eye height","m":["eye"]},{"k":"Antenna, wheels, eyes differ","m":["antenna","wheel"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["meht is 50","same as meht","yes it is meht"],"why":"The 50 ft is the ILS reference datum; MEHT is a PAPI/VASIS term"}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-22","type":"mcq","q":"MEHT is published for which installations?","src":"Annex 14 2.12 e)","ref":"OB61","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["PAPI, APAPI and T-VASIS/AT-VASIS","ILS glide paths","Marker beacons","Approach lighting systems"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 14 2.12 e): MEHT is a visual approach slope indicator term.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"Where does an ILS DME read zero?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 2.11.1, 2.11.1.1, 7.1.6.3","ref":"OB62","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"**At the DME, unless its delay is adjusted.**\n- Zero can be placed by siting the transponder close to the required point, or by adjusting its time delay so aircraft read zero at a specified point; if zero is not at the antenna, that must be published.\n- Where DME replaces markers it should be on the airport, zero near the runway, with less than about 20° between approach path and DME.\n- An ILS DME with a zero-range offset must be excluded from FMC (RNAV) solutions.\n- The bank's \"anywhere in the TMA\" is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"At the DME antenna unless adjusted","m":["antenna","where it is sited","at the dme","sited"]},{"k":"Delay adjusted","m":["delay","offset"]},{"k":"Must be published","m":["publish"]},{"k":"On the airport, under 20°","m":["airport","20 degrees","20 deg","aerodrome"]},{"k":"Excluded from RNAV","m":["rnav","fmc"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["anywhere in the tma"],"why":"Annex 10 wants it on the airport near the runway"}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-mcq-23","type":"mcq","q":"An ILS DME has a zero-range offset so it reads zero at the threshold. What is the consequence for the FMC?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 2.11.1","ref":"OB62","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["It must be excluded from RNAV (FMC) position solutions","None; the FMC corrects automatically","The FMC must use it as primary","The ILS cannot be flown with DME"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 Att C 2.11.1: a zero-range offset makes it unsuitable for RNAV.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-num-20","type":"num","q":"Where DME replaces markers, the angle between the approach path and the DME should be less than about how many degrees?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 2.11.1.1","ref":"OB62","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":20,"unit":"°","why":"Annex 10 Att C 2.11.1.1: less than about 20°.","topic":11},{"id":"t11-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"You are briefing an ILS approach. Tell me how the ILS works.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1; FCTM 5.16, 5.18, 5.55; OM-A 28.7.1(e)","ref":"NR9","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What are the components and frequencies?","a":"LOC VHF 108.10 to 111.95 MHz odd tenths; GS UHF 328.6 to 335.4 MHz paired; markers 75 MHz or DME; visual aids; monitors.","keys":[{"k":"LOC VHF odd tenths","m":["odd","108","111"]},{"k":"GS UHF paired","m":["328","335","paired"]}]},{"q":"How does the receiver know you are left or low?","a":"DDM between 90 Hz and 150 Hz: LOC 90 left, 150 right; GS 90 above, 150 below.","keys":[{"k":"DDM","m":["ddm","depth of modulation"]},{"k":"90 / 150 Hz","m":["150"]}]},{"q":"You're cleared to intercept from above. What's the risk?","a":"False glide path at about 6° (reverse sensing) and 9°; intercept from below normally, or per FCTM with LOC captured, MCP not below 1000 ft AFE, V/S −1000 to −1500.","keys":[{"k":"False GS 6° and 9°","m":["false","side lobe","sidelobe"]},{"k":"Reverse sensing","m":["reverse"]}]},{"q":"How do you confirm you're on the real one?","a":"FAF crossing altitude, VNAV path, altitude against DME (about 300 ft per NM), normal pitch and descent rate, PAPI.","keys":[{"k":"Altitude/DME check","m":["dme","300 ft","300"]},{"k":"FAF altitude / VNAV","m":["faf","vnav","outer marker"]}]},{"q":"At 1500 ft the GS flag appears. Now what?","a":"Continue only as a localiser-only non-precision approach to the LOC MDA if authorised (CDFA, DDA = MDA + 50); on the MAX, G/P via IAN; otherwise go around.","keys":[{"k":"LOC only to MDA","m":["mda","loc only","localiser only","localizer only"]},{"k":"Or go around","m":["go around","go-around"]}]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"LVP CAT II/III: ILS integrity drill.","src":"OM-A 45.1.10, 45.1.14, 45.1.15, 45.2.4.2, 25.4, Table 7.3.D","ref":"NR42","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"Why are critical and sensitive areas protected?","a":"Aircraft or vehicles near the LOC or GS antennas distort the signal; critical area clear in all ILS ops, sensitive area controlled in LVP.","keys":[{"k":"Signal distortion","m":["distort","interfere","reflect"]},{"k":"LVP","m":["lvp","low vis"]}]},{"q":"What's the worst case?","a":"An aircraft taking off over the localiser: brief LOC excursion and autopilot disturbance.","keys":[{"k":"Aircraft taking off over LOC","m":["taking off","take off","takeoff","departing"]}]},{"q":"What protects you if the transmitter goes out of tolerance?","a":"The ILS monitor switches to standby or shuts it down; changeover under 2 s; LOC within ±25 ft (CAT II) or ±10 ft (CAT III) at the threshold.","keys":[{"k":"Monitor","m":["monitor"]},{"k":"2 s changeover","m":["2 s","2s","2 sec","two sec"]}]},{"q":"The middle marker is unserviceable. Affect you?","a":"No effect on minima; markers are not mandatory and are mostly replaced by DME.","keys":[{"k":"No effect","m":["no effect","nothing","no change"]}]},{"q":"The localiser fails at 500 ft on autoland. Action?","a":"Go around: failure between 1000 ft and alert height, and ground equipment failure is an immediate go-around item.","keys":[{"k":"Go around","m":["go around","go-around","goaround"]}]}],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-draw-2","type":"draw","q":"Draw the ILS coverage: localiser in plan and glide path in profile.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1, 3.1.5.3.1; NR42","ref":"Key facts: ILS","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **LOC plan view:** ±10° to 25 NM and ±35° to 17 NM from the antenna beyond the stop end; 90 Hz on the left, 150 Hz on the right on approach.\n- **GS profile:** from 0.45θ to 1.75θ (about 1.35° to 5.25° for 3°), ±8° in azimuth to 10 NM; 90 Hz above, 150 Hz below; false lobes at about 6° and 9°.","checklist":["LOC antenna beyond the stop end","±10° sector to 25 NM","±35° sector to 17 NM","90 Hz left, 150 Hz right","GS antenna abeam touchdown","GS vertical coverage 0.45θ to 1.75θ","GS ±8° to 10 NM","False lobes at about 6° and 9° shown"],"topic":11},{"id":"t11-draw-3","type":"draw","q":"Draw a 3° ILS approach profile with the marker beacons.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.7","ref":"NR30","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- Glide path to point T at 50 ft over the threshold.\n- **OM:** 3.9 NM (7.2 km), about 1300 ft, 400 Hz dashes, blue.\n- **MM:** 3500 ft (1050 m), about 200 ft, 1300 Hz dot-dash, amber.\n- **IM:** 75 to 450 m, at the lowest CAT II DH, 3000 Hz dots, white.\n- All on 75 MHz; DME may replace them.","checklist":["OM at 3.9 NM, about 1300 ft","MM at 3500 ft, about 200 ft","IM at 75 to 450 m","Audio tones labelled (400 / 1300 / 3000 Hz)","Light colours labelled (blue / amber / white)","75 MHz noted","Threshold crossing 50 ft"],"topic":11},{"id":"t12-num-1","type":"num","q":"Maximum unambiguous range of a primary radar ≈ 81,000 / PRF. What is it for a PRF of 400 pps?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Radar and surveillance","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":202.5,"unit":"NM","tol":1,"why":"81,000 / 400 = 202.5 NM. Derived from c / (2 × PRF).","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"What is the formula for the maximum range of a primary radar?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Radar and surveillance","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["c / (2 × PRF), about 81,000 / PRF NM","c × PRF / 2","c / PRF, about 162,000 / PRF NM","PRF / (2 × c)"],"a":0,"why":"The pulse must return before the next is sent: range = c / (2 × PRF), about 81,000 / PRF in NM.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the SSR element to its value.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Radar and surveillance","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Ground interrogation","1030 MHz"],["Aircraft reply","1090 MHz"],["Mode A codes","4096"],["Mode C altitude steps","100 ft, referenced to 1013.25"],["Mode S address","24-bit"]],"why":"SSR interrogates on 1030 and replies on 1090 MHz.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-num-2","type":"num","q":"SSR ground interrogation frequency?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Radar and surveillance","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1030,"unit":"MHz","why":"Interrogation 1030 MHz, reply 1090 MHz.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-num-3","type":"num","q":"SSR transponder reply frequency?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Radar and surveillance","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1090,"unit":"MHz","why":"Interrogation 1030 MHz, reply 1090 MHz. ADS-B 1090 ES uses the same frequency.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-num-4","type":"num","q":"How many Mode A codes are available?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Radar and surveillance","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":4096,"why":"4096 codes (four octal digits, 8⁴).","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"Mode C reports altitude how?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Radar and surveillance","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Pressure altitude referenced to 1013.25 hPa, in 100 ft steps","QNH altitude in 25 ft steps","Radio altitude in 10 ft steps","GNSS altitude in 100 ft steps"],"a":0,"why":"Mode C: pressure altitude to 1013.25, in 100 ft steps.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"What size is the Mode S aircraft address?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Radar and surveillance","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["24-bit","12-bit","16-bit","32-bit"],"a":0,"why":"Mode S uses a 24-bit selective address, with extended squitter.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"Explain ADS-B on our aircraft.","src":"OM-A 3.3; FCOM 11.20; Oral bank p.87","ref":"Key facts: ADS-B","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**ADS-B broadcasts the aircraft's GNSS position and other data, about once a second, with no interrogation.**\n- Link: 1090 ES, the extended squitter of the Mode S transponder. UAT on 978 MHz is not used here.\n- ADS-B OUT transmits; ADS-B IN receives.\n- On the MAX the left GPS feeds transponder 1, the right GPS transponder 2.\n- STBY on the transponder panel disables the transponder, ADS-B OUT and TCAS together.\n- Failure: XPNDR/ATC FAIL, GPS-L/R INVALID.","keys":[{"k":"Broadcast, no interrogation","m":["broadcast","no interrogation"]},{"k":"GNSS position","m":["gnss","gps"]},{"k":"1090 ES","m":["1090","extended squitter"]},{"k":"OUT transmits, IN receives","m":["out","in receives"]},{"k":"STBY kills transponder, ADS-B, TCAS","m":["stby","standby"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"Which ADS-B link is used in India per OM-A?","src":"OM-A 3.3","ref":"Key facts: ADS-B","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["1090 ES","UAT 978 MHz","VDL Mode 4","HF data link"],"a":0,"why":"1090 ES; UAT on 978 MHz is not used.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"Selecting STBY on the transponder panel disables what?","src":"OM-A 3.3","ref":"Key facts: ADS-B","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["The transponder, ADS-B OUT and TCAS","The transponder only","ADS-B OUT only","TCAS only"],"a":0,"why":"STBY disables the transponder, ADS-B OUT and TCAS together.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"Which indications show ADS-B failure?","src":"OM-A 3.3","ref":"Key facts: ADS-B","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["XPNDR/ATC FAIL, GPS-L/R INVALID","TCAS OFF only","ADS-B FAIL on the FMA","No indication"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 3.3.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"Weather radar: what band, and what does it see and not see?","src":"OM-A 17.22","ref":"Key facts: Weather radar","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**X band, about 3.2 cm (about 9.3 GHz). It sees precipitation only.**\n- Wet hail is the best reflector; rain is good; dry snow and dry hail are poor.\n- It does not see cloud, clear-air turbulence, lightning or dry (clear-air) windshear.\n- Magenta means turbulence associated with precipitation (WX+T), within about 40 NM.\n- Heavy rain attenuates the beam, so the area behind a storm can look clear (radar shadow).","keys":[{"k":"X band","m":["x band","xband"]},{"k":"3.2 cm / 9.3 GHz","m":["32 cm","3 2 cm","93 ghz","9 3 ghz","94 ghz","9 ghz"]},{"k":"Precipitation only","m":["precipitation","water","rain"]},{"k":"Wet hail best","m":["wet hail"]},{"k":"Not CAT / cloud / dry windshear","m":["clear air","cat","cloud","dry"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"Which is the BEST weather radar reflector?","src":"OM-A 17.22","ref":"Key facts: Weather radar","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Wet hail","Dry hail","Dry snow","Ice crystals"],"a":0,"why":"Wet hail is the best reflector; dry snow and hail are poor.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"Which of these can normal airborne weather radar NOT detect?","src":"OM-A 17.22","ref":"Key facts: Weather radar","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Clear-air turbulence","Heavy rain","Wet hail","Turbulence in precipitation (WX+T)"],"a":0,"why":"Turbulence, cloud and clear-air windshear are invisible; WX+T needs precipitation.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"What are the rules for using weather radar on the ground?","src":"OM-A 17.22","ref":"Key facts: Weather radar","prio":2,"a":"**Keep it in STBY on the apron.**\n- Switch it ON only with the tilt at 5° or more up, and at least 200 m from other aircraft.\n- In the key facts this reads: STBY on the apron; ON only with the tilt at 5° or more up and 200 m from other aircraft.\n- Never operate it near refuelling.\n- Source: OM-A 17.22.","keys":[{"k":"STBY on apron","m":["stby","standby"]},{"k":"Tilt 5° up","m":["5 degree","5 deg","five degree","tilt up"]},{"k":"200 m from aircraft","m":["200 m","200m","200 metres","200 meters"]},{"k":"Not near refuelling","m":["refuel"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-num-5","type":"num","q":"Weather radar may be switched ON on the ground only with the tilt at least how many degrees up?","src":"OM-A 17.22","ref":"Key facts: Weather radar","prio":2,"ans":5,"unit":"°","why":"Tilt 5° or more up and 200 m from other aircraft.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-num-6","type":"num","q":"Minimum distance from other aircraft to operate weather radar on the ground?","src":"OM-A 17.22","ref":"Key facts: Weather radar","prio":2,"ans":200,"unit":"m","why":"200 m, with tilt 5° or more up.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"What is radar shadow, and what do PAC and REACT do?","src":"FCOM 10.40.22, 10.40.24, 11.10.35","ref":"Key facts: Weather radar","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Heavy rain attenuates the beam, so the area behind a storm can look clear: radar shadow.**\n- Collins WXR-2100: a Path Attenuation Compensation (PAC) alert over the attenuated region.\n- Honeywell RDR-4000: REACT magenta arcs where attenuation is significant; severe weather may lie there even with little or no reflectivity shown.","keys":[{"k":"Attenuation by heavy rain","m":["attenuat"]},{"k":"Area behind looks clear","m":["behind","shadow","clear"]},{"k":"PAC alert (Collins)","m":["pac","path attenuation"]},{"k":"REACT magenta arcs (Honeywell)","m":["react"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"Explain the radio altimeter.","src":"OM-A 45.1.21, Ch 3; FCOM 10.10.23; Standard theory","ref":"NR6","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**FMCW (frequency-modulated continuous wave) at 4.2 to 4.4 GHz (SHF).**\n- Transmit and receive antennas on the belly; the frequency difference between the signal sent and received is proportional to height.\n- Shown on the PFD below 2500 ft AGL; accuracy about ±1.5 ft or a few percent.\n- Reads about 0 ft at main-gear touchdown; the antenna offset is calibrated out.\n- Used for: CAT II/III DH, GPWS modes, autoland flare and retard, TCAS RA inhibits, windshear, callouts. Two fitted on the 737.","keys":[{"k":"FMCW","m":["fmcw","frequency modulated continuous","continuous wave"]},{"k":"4.2 to 4.4 GHz","m":["42 ghz","4 2 ghz","44 ghz","4 4 ghz","42 to 44","4 2 to 4 4"]},{"k":"Frequency difference proportional to height","m":["frequency difference","difference"]},{"k":"Displayed below 2500 ft","m":["2500"]},{"k":"0 ft at touchdown","m":["zero","0 ft","touchdown","calibrat"]},{"k":"Users: DH, GPWS, autoland, TCAS","m":["gpws","autoland","flare","decision height","tcas"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"What principle and band does the radio altimeter use?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR6","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["FMCW at 4.2 to 4.4 GHz","Pulse radar at 9.3 GHz","FMCW at 1030 MHz","Pulse at 4.2 to 4.4 MHz"],"a":0,"why":"FMCW in the 4.2 to 4.4 GHz SHF band.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-num-7","type":"num","q":"Below what height is the radio altitude shown on the PFD?","src":"FCOM 10.10.23","ref":"NR6","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":2500,"unit":"ft","why":"Displayed below 2500 ft AGL.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"What does the radio altimeter read at main-gear touchdown?","src":"OM-A 45.1.21","ref":"NR6","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["About 0 ft, the antenna offset being calibrated out","About 8 ft","About 20 ft","The antenna height above the wheels"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 45.1.21: about 0 ft at touchdown (main gear).","topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"Radio altimeter errors and failure: what happens?","src":"OM-A 45.1.5.2, 45.2.8.2","ref":"NR6","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Errors:**\n- uneven terrain before the threshold (hence company RA minima);\n- pitch and bank;\n- 5G C-band interference near airports.\n\n**Failure:** RA flag. Autoland, CAT II/III and some GPWS modes are lost. Go around if below 1000 ft in CAT II/III (OM-A 45.2.8.2).","keys":[{"k":"Uneven terrain before threshold","m":["terrain","uneven"]},{"k":"Pitch and bank","m":["pitch","bank"]},{"k":"5G interference","m":["5g interference","5g network","5g signal","c band","cband"]},{"k":"Lose autoland, CAT II/III, GPWS modes","m":["autoland","cat 2","cat ii","gpws"]},{"k":"Go around below 1000 ft in CAT II/III","m":["go around","go-around","1000"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"What is the 5G interference issue with radio altimeters?","src":"FCOM Bulletin IDC-1 R1","ref":"Key facts: Radio altimeter","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**5G uses the C band, next to the RA's 4.2 to 4.4 GHz band, and can interfere near airports.**\nFCOM Bulletin IDC-1 R1 (written for US airspace) lists possible effects:\n- RA failure, no computed data or erroneous data;\n- NO AUTOLAND, autopilot disconnect on ILS/GLS approaches, flare problems;\n- TO/GA unavailable;\n- RA-based GPWS alerts missing or false.\nThe Indian DGCA/DoT position on frequencies and runway buffer zones is still pending in our notes.","keys":[{"k":"C band","m":["c band","cband","c-band"]},{"k":"RA failure or erroneous data","m":["erroneous","failure","wrong"]},{"k":"Autoland / autopilot / flare affected","m":["autoland","autopilot","flare"]},{"k":"GPWS alerts missing or false","m":["gpws","egpws"]},{"k":"TO/GA unavailable","m":["toga","to ga","go around"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"Why do company RA minima exist at some airports?","src":"OM-A 45.1.5.2","ref":"Key facts: Radio altimeter","prio":2,"a":"**Because uneven terrain before the threshold affects the radio altimeter reading at DH.**\n- The RA measures height above whatever is below, not above the threshold.\n- Where the pre-threshold terrain rises or falls, the RA reading at DH would be misleading, so company RA values are published.","keys":[{"k":"Uneven terrain before threshold","m":["terrain","uneven"]},{"k":"Affects DH","m":["decision height","decision altitude","minima"]},{"k":"Company RA values","m":["company"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"What is FANS?","src":"OM-A Ch 40; FCOM 11.34, 5.20","ref":"NR36","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Future Air Navigation System: ICAO's late-1980s concept for satellite-based CNS/ATM.**\n- Avionics: FANS-1 (Boeing) and FANS-A (Airbus), together FANS 1/A.\n- Combines CPDLC, ADS-C (automatic contract position reports) and RNP navigation, over ACARS by satcom, VHF or HF data link.\n- Benefit: in oceanic and remote airspace, reduced separation, for example 30 NM lateral and 30 NM longitudinal with RNP 4 + CPDLC + ADS-C. Also better routes and fewer HF reports.\n- Operational approval is an OpsSpecs matter.","keys":[{"k":"Future Air Navigation System","m":["future air navigation"]},{"k":"CNS/ATM satellite-based","m":["cns","satellite"]},{"k":"CPDLC","m":["cpdlc","controller pilot data link","controller-pilot"]},{"k":"ADS-C","m":["ads c","adsc","contract"]},{"k":"RNP","m":["rnp"]},{"k":"30/30 NM separation","m":["30 nm","30nm","30 30","reduced separation"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"How is FANS data link worked on the 737?","src":"FCOM 11.34, 11.11","ref":"NR36","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Everything is on the CDU (a FANS MCDU) on aircraft with ATC data link installed.**\n- AFN logon on the ATC LOGON/STATUS page, which shows ADS and data link status.\n- ADS-C: the FMC can answer five ATC centres and one airline centre at once; periodic, event and on-demand reports, automatic. Crew can switch ADS off on LOGON/STATUS.\n- CPDLC: ATC UPLINK, REQUEST, REPORT, LOG and EMERGENCY pages (MAYDAY sends a position report).\n- Uplinks give a white ATC message on the engine display and a hi/lo chime.","keys":[{"k":"CDU / FANS MCDU","m":["cdu","mcdu"]},{"k":"AFN logon on LOGON/STATUS","m":["afn","logon","log on"]},{"k":"Five ATC + one airline","m":["five","5 atc","5 centres","5 centers"]},{"k":"Periodic, event, demand reports","m":["periodic","event","demand"]},{"k":"White ATC message and chime","m":["chime","white","engine display","eicas"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-num-8","type":"num","q":"With ADS-C, how many ATC centres can the 737 FMC report to at once?","src":"FCOM 11.34","ref":"NR36","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":5,"why":"Five ATC centres plus one airline centre.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"FANS 1/A combines which three elements?","src":"NR36; FCOM 11.34","ref":"NR36","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["CPDLC, ADS-C and RNP navigation","CPDLC, ADS-B and GPS","ACARS, TCAS and ADS-B","SSR, ADS-B and CPDLC"],"a":0,"why":"CPDLC + ADS-C + RNP over ACARS. The bank's \"CPDLC, ADS-B, GPS\" is wrong.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"Which data link path reaches polar regions where VHF and SATCOM do not?","src":"FCOM 5.20","ref":"NR36","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["HF data link (HFDL)","VHF data link","Inmarsat SATCOM","1090 ES"],"a":0,"why":"FCOM 5.20: HFDL where fitted also covers polar regions.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"What ACARS messages are allowed under the FCOM limitation?","src":"FCOM L, Communications","ref":"NR36","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"**Only messages that can't create an unsafe condition if delayed, misdelivered or corrupted.**\n- Allowed if verified per approved procedures: PDC, D-ATIS, oceanic clearances, weight and balance, and takeoff data.\n- ACARS is the airline data link for company messages; on the 737 it also carries FANS (CPDLC and ADS-C).","keys":[{"k":"No unsafe condition if delayed or corrupted","m":["unsafe","delayed","corrupt"]},{"k":"PDC / D-ATIS","m":["pdc","datis","d atis","pre departure"]},{"k":"Oceanic clearance","m":["oceanic"]},{"k":"W&B / takeoff data if verified","m":["verif","weight and balance","takeoff data","take off data"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-num-9","type":"num","q":"With RNP 4 + CPDLC + ADS-C, what lateral separation is possible in oceanic airspace (OM-A)?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"NR36","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":30,"unit":"NM","why":"30 NM lateral and 30 NM longitudinal.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"How do you calculate cloud top height using weather radar?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR44","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Beam-edge method with the 1:60 rule.**\n1. Tilt up until the cell's return just disappears: the top is now at the lower beam edge.\n2. Lower edge = tilt − half the beam width.\n3. Height above you (ft) = range (NM) × that angle (°) × 100.\n- Example: 40 NM, disappears at +4°, beam 3°: lower edge 2.5°, so 40 × 2.5 × 100 = 10,000 ft above you.\n- Cloud base: tilt down until it just appears, using the upper edge (tilt + half beam width).\n- Radar sees precipitation, not cloud, so the visible top can be several thousand feet higher.","keys":[{"k":"Tilt until return disappears","m":["disappear"]},{"k":"Lower edge = tilt minus half beam","m":["half the beam","half beam","half of the beam","minus half"]},{"k":"Range × angle × 100","m":["x 100","times 100","100 ft","100 feet","into 100"]},{"k":"1 in 60 rule","m":["1 in 60","1:60","one in sixty","160"]},{"k":"Sees precipitation, top may be higher","m":["precipitation","higher"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-num-10","type":"num","q":"Cell at 40 NM; return disappears at +4° tilt; beam width 3°. Height of the top above you?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR44","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":10000,"unit":"ft","why":"Lower edge = 4 − 1.5 = 2.5°. 40 × 2.5 × 100 = 10,000 ft.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-num-11","type":"num","q":"Cell at 30 NM; return disappears at +5° tilt; beam width 4°. Height of the top above you?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR44","prio":2,"ans":9000,"unit":"ft","why":"Lower edge = 5 − 2 = 3°. 30 × 3 × 100 = 9000 ft.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"To find the cloud BASE with weather radar, which beam edge do you use?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR44","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Upper edge: tilt + half the beam width","Lower edge: tilt − half the beam width","The beam centre","Upper edge: tilt − half the beam width"],"a":0,"why":"Tilt down until the return just appears; the base is at the upper beam edge.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"Explain the frequency and working of the weather radar.","src":"OM-A 17.22; FCOM 11.10.30, 11.20.17","ref":"NR45","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**X band, about 9.3 to 9.4 GHz (about 3.2 cm), a pulse radar.**\n- The band reflects well off raindrops but still passes through light precipitation.\n- A narrow pencil beam (about 3 to 4°) from a flat-plate antenna scans ahead; FCOM: 180° forward sweep.\n- Echo time gives range; antenna position gives bearing.\n- Strength in colour: green, yellow/amber, red, magenta.\n- Doppler processing detects turbulence (WX+T, within 40 NM) and predictive windshear.\n- Controls: tilt, gain, range, mode (WX, WX+T, MAP; AUTO/MAN, GC on newer panels).","keys":[{"k":"X band / 9.3 GHz","m":["x band","xband","93 ghz","9 3 ghz","94 ghz","9 4 ghz"]},{"k":"Pulse radar, echo time gives range","m":["pulse","echo"]},{"k":"Pencil beam 3 to 4°","m":["pencil","flat plate","3 to 4"]},{"k":"Colour scale to magenta","m":["magenta","red"]},{"k":"Doppler for turbulence / windshear","m":["doppler","turbulence","windshear"]},{"k":"Tilt / gain controls","m":["tilt","gain"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"What weather radar facts does the 737 FCOM give?","src":"FCOM 11.20.17, 10.10","ref":"NR45","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- The antenna sweeps **180°** forward.\n- Rain shows red (heaviest), amber, green.\n- Turbulence: precipitation moving at **5 m/s or more** toward or away from the antenna shows magenta, limited to **40 NM** whatever range is selected, and needs precipitation; CAT can't be detected.\n- MAP mode shows ground returns.\n- Must not be used for proximity warning or anti-collision.\n- At the 640 NM range, returns are limited to 320 NM.\n- The FCOM gives no operating frequency; X band is from OM-A.","keys":[{"k":"180° sweep","m":["180"]},{"k":"Turbulence 5 m/s","m":["5 ms","5 m s","5 metres","5 meters","5m s"]},{"k":"Turbulence limited to 40 NM","m":["40 nm","40nm","40 nautical"]},{"k":"Not for anti-collision","m":["anti collision","proximity","collision"]},{"k":"640 NM limited to 320 NM","m":["320"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-num-12","type":"num","q":"Weather radar turbulence (WX+T) detection is limited to what range, whatever range is selected?","src":"FCOM 11.20.17","ref":"NR45","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":40,"unit":"NM","why":"40 NM.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-num-13","type":"num","q":"FCOM: precipitation moving at what speed or more toward/away from the antenna is shown as turbulence?","src":"FCOM 11.20.17","ref":"NR45","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":5,"unit":"m/s","why":"5 m/s or more shows magenta.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-num-14","type":"num","q":"Through how many degrees does the 737 weather radar antenna sweep?","src":"FCOM 11.20.17","ref":"NR45","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":180,"unit":"°","why":"180° forward.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"With 640 NM range selected, to what range are weather returns limited?","src":"FCOM 10.10","ref":"NR45","prio":3,"o":["320 NM","640 NM","160 NM","40 NM"],"a":0,"why":"FCOM 10.10: returns limited to 320 NM.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"Weather radar: what is the approximate beam width of the pencil beam?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR45","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["About 3 to 4°","About 10°","About 1°","About 25°"],"a":0,"why":"A narrow pencil beam from a flat-plate antenna, about 3 to 4°.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match the weather radar to its feature.","src":"FCOM 11.20.17 to 11.20.21; 15.20.25","ref":"NR45","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Collins WXR-2100 MultiScan","AUTO mode: automatic tilt, Gain PLUS, PAC alert"],["Honeywell RDR-4000 IntuVue","3D memory to 320 NM and 60,000 ft, REACT"],["RDR-4000 flight-path weather","±4000 ft of the expected path"],["RDR-4000 MAN mode","Altitude slices 0 to 60,000 ft"]],"why":"Both types appear in the FCOM; RDR-4000 needs no tilt or gain inputs.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"Compare the two weather radars in our FCOM: Collins WXR-2100 and Honeywell RDR-4000.","src":"FCOM 11.20.17 to 11.20.21; 15.20.25","ref":"NR45","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Collins WXR-2100 MultiScan:** AUTO mode gives automatic tilt, more gain as OAT falls, and Gain PLUS: path attenuation alert, oceanic reflectivity compensation, overflight protection, and with WX+T predictive overflight and threat assessment.\n- **Honeywell RDR-4000 IntuVue:** builds a 3D memory from several scans: weather to 320 NM, ground level to 60,000 ft, ground clutter removed, no tilt or gain inputs. Flight-path weather is ±4000 ft of the expected path; other weather striped. MAN mode gives altitude slices. Hail/lightning icons and REACT.","keys":[{"k":"Collins automatic tilt / Gain PLUS","m":["automatic tilt","auto tilt","gain plus","multiscan"]},{"k":"Honeywell 3D memory","m":["3d memory","3d buffer","three dimensional","volumetric","memory"]},{"k":"320 NM / 60,000 ft","m":["320","60000"]},{"k":"±4000 ft flight path","m":["4000"]},{"k":"REACT / no tilt input","m":["react","no tilt"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"Weather radar errors and limitations.","src":"OM-A 17.22; FCOM 11.20.17","ref":"NR45","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Attenuation:** heavy rain hides what is behind it (radar shadow).\n- **Beam spreading** at long range.\n- **Ground clutter.**\n- Sees precipitation only: not cloud, CAT, lightning or dry windshear.\n- Turbulence detection needs precipitation and is limited to 40 NM.","keys":[{"k":"Attenuation / shadow","m":["attenuat","shadow"]},{"k":"Beam spreading","m":["spread","beam width"]},{"k":"Ground clutter","m":["clutter","ground return"]},{"k":"Precipitation only","m":["precipitation","clear air","cat"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"Primary radar vs secondary radar.","src":"OM-A 3.3; Standard theory","ref":"NR53","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Primary:** transmits a pulse and receives the echo off the target. No aircraft equipment. Range and bearing only. High power, range limited (fourth-power law). Clutter, but sees non-cooperative targets. Examples: weather radar, ground surveillance, SMR.\n- **Secondary (SSR):** ground interrogates on 1030 MHz, transponder replies on 1090 MHz. Range, bearing, identity (Mode A), pressure altitude (Mode C), data link and 24-bit address (Mode S). Low power, long range (one-way each direction). No clutter, but garbling and FRUIT. Examples: ATC SSR, TCAS, Mode S.\n- Our aircraft: weather radar is primary; two transponders with Mode S and ADS-B out.","keys":[{"k":"Primary: reflection, no aircraft equipment","m":["reflect","echo","no equipment","non cooperative"]},{"k":"SSR: interrogate 1030, reply 1090","m":["1030","1090","transponder"]},{"k":"SSR gives identity and altitude","m":["identity","mode a","mode c","altitude"]},{"k":"Primary high power, fourth-power law","m":["fourth power","high power","4th power"]},{"k":"SSR garbling / FRUIT","m":["garbl","fruit"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-16","type":"mcq","q":"Which of these is a disadvantage of SSR compared with primary radar?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR53","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Garbling and FRUIT","Weather clutter","Requires very high transmitter power","Gives range and bearing only"],"a":0,"why":"SSR has no clutter but suffers garbling and FRUIT. The other options describe primary radar.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-17","type":"mcq","q":"Why does a primary radar need high power for its range?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR53","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["The echo is weak: received power falls with the fourth power of range","It operates on HF","The transponder reply is weak","It uses a one-way path"],"a":0,"why":"Two-way reflected path: fourth-power law. SSR is one-way each direction, so low power gives long range.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"Compare ADS-B, ADS-C and Mode S.","src":"FCOM 11.34, 11.20; OM-A 3.3; Oral bank p.87","ref":"OB63","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Mode S:** secondary radar transponder with a 24-bit address; replies on 1090 MHz to interrogations on 1030 MHz.\n- **ADS-B:** broadcasts GNSS position about once a second to anyone, no interrogation and no logon. 1090 ES in India. Used for radar-like surveillance where ground stations exist.\n- **ADS-C:** after AFN logon, ATC sets contracts with the FMC for periodic, event or on-demand reports by data link; up to five ATC centres plus one airline. For oceanic and remote areas via SATCOM/HF. Crew sees ADS status and can switch it off.\n- ADS-B traffic display is for awareness only; collision avoidance is TCAS's job.","keys":[{"k":"Mode S 24-bit, interrogated","m":["24 bit","24bit","interrogat"]},{"k":"ADS-B broadcast, no logon","m":["broadcast","no logon","no log on"]},{"k":"ADS-C contract after logon","m":["contract","afn","logon","log on"]},{"k":"ADS-C oceanic via SATCOM/HF","m":["oceanic","remote","satcom"]},{"k":"ADS-B display not for avoidance","m":["awareness","tcas","not for avoidance"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-18","type":"mcq","q":"The bank says ADS-B requires the aircraft to send a logon message. Which system actually needs an AFN logon?","src":"FCOM 11.34","ref":"OB63","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["ADS-C and CPDLC","ADS-B","Mode S","TCAS"],"a":0,"why":"Logon (AFN) is for CPDLC and ADS-C; ADS-B needs none.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-19","type":"mcq","q":"The bank says with ADS-C the pilot is not aware of it. What is correct?","src":"FCOM 11.34","ref":"OB63","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Reports are automatic, but the crew sees ADS status and can switch it off on the LOGON/STATUS page","The crew must send each report manually","ADS-C cannot be switched off","ADS-C is only visible to the airline"],"a":0,"why":"FCOM 11.34.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-20","type":"mcq","q":"The bank (FAA text) says ADS-B is deployed throughout the \"NAS\". What does NAS mean there?","src":"Oral bank p.87","ref":"OB63","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"o":["The US National Airspace System","North Atlantic airspace","National Approach Service","Navigation Accuracy Standard"],"a":0,"why":"NAS is the US National Airspace System; the text is FAA material.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-21","type":"mcq","q":"On the MAX, which GPS feeds ADS-B position to transponder 1?","src":"FCOM 11.20","ref":"OB63","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Left GPS","Right GPS","Whichever is more accurate","The FMC blended position"],"a":0,"why":"Left GPS to transponder 1, right GPS to transponder 2.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"Bank: \"ADS-B: the aircraft sends a logon message; ADS-C: the pilot is not aware of it.\" Correct both.","src":"FCOM 11.34","ref":"OB63","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **ADS-B needs no logon.** It simply broadcasts GNSS position on 1090 ES to anyone listening.\n- **AFN logon is for CPDLC and ADS-C.**\n- **ADS-C reports are automatic,** but the crew sees the ADS status on the ATC LOGON/STATUS page and can switch ADS off.","keys":[{"k":"ADS-B no logon","m":["no logon","no log on","does not need","doesnt need","not need"]},{"k":"Logon is for CPDLC/ADS-C","m":["cpdlc","afn"]},{"k":"Crew can see/switch off ADS","m":["switch","status","turn off"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["ads b needs logon","ads b requires logon","adsb logon"],"why":"ADS-B needs no logon"}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"ACARS, CPDLC, FANS, ADS-B and Inmarsat: what bands do they use, and how does the crew work them?","src":"FCOM 5.20.10, 11.34; OM-A 3.3; Oral bank p.47; Standard theory","ref":"OB64","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Paths on our aircraft:** VHF; SATCOM where fitted; HF data link where fitted, which also reaches polar areas.\n- VHF data link: VHF COM band, 118 to 137 MHz.\n- HF data link: 3 to 30 MHz.\n- SATCOM: Inmarsat geostationary satellites in L band, about 1.5 to 1.6 GHz.\n- ADS-B: 1090 MHz.\n- ACARS carries company messages and, on the 737, FANS.\n- Crew interface: the CDU pages (ATC LOGON/STATUS, UPLINK, REQUEST, REPORT, LOG, EMERGENCY), with a white ATC message and chime. Not the Airbus DCDU.","keys":[{"k":"VHF 118 to 137 MHz","m":["118","137"]},{"k":"HF 3 to 30 MHz","m":["3 to 30","hf data","hfdl","high frequency"]},{"k":"SATCOM L band 1.5 to 1.6 GHz","m":["l band","lband","15 ghz","1 5","16 ghz"]},{"k":"ADS-B 1090","m":["1090"]},{"k":"CDU interface","m":["cdu","mcdu"]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-match-3","type":"match","q":"Match the data link path to its band.","src":"Standard theory; OM-A 3.3","ref":"OB64","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["VHF data link","118 to 137 MHz"],["HF data link","3 to 30 MHz"],["Inmarsat SATCOM","L band, about 1.5 to 1.6 GHz"],["ADS-B","1090 MHz"]],"why":"Standard theory band figures; ADS-B per OM-A 3.3.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-mcq-22","type":"mcq","q":"What is Inmarsat?","src":"Oral bank p.47","ref":"OB64","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["A satellite communications company providing voice and data via geostationary satellites","A GNSS augmentation system","A low-earth-orbit navigation constellation","An HF data link provider"],"a":0,"why":"Oral bank p.47; it is the SATCOM path for oceanic data link.","topic":12},{"id":"t12-recall-21","type":"recall","q":"Bank: \"FANS has three components: CPDLC, ADS-B and GPS.\" Correct it.","src":"NR36; FCOM 11.34","ref":"OB64","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"**FANS 1/A is CPDLC + ADS-C + RNP navigation, over ACARS** (satcom, VHF or HF data link).\n- It uses **ADS-C** (contract), not ADS-B.\n- ADS-C needs an AFN logon; ADS-B is a broadcast with no logon.","keys":[{"k":"CPDLC","m":["cpdlc"]},{"k":"ADS-C not ADS-B","m":["ads c","adsc","contract"]},{"k":"RNP navigation","m":["rnp"]},{"k":"Over ACARS","m":["acars"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["ads b","adsb"],"why":"FANS 1/A uses ADS-C, not ADS-B"}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"Thunderstorms ahead on your weather radar at night. Talk me through using it.","src":"OM-A 17.22; FCOM 10.40.22, 10.40.24, 11.20.17","ref":"NR45","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What band is it and what does it detect?","a":"X band, about 9.3 GHz (3.2 cm); precipitation only: wet hail best, dry snow/hail poor.","keys":[{"k":"X band","m":["x band","xband","93 ghz","9 3 ghz","94 ghz","9 4 ghz"]},{"k":"Precipitation","m":["precipitation","rain","water"]}]},{"q":"There's a clear area behind a red cell. Do you go through it?","a":"No: it may be radar shadow from attenuation. PAC alert (Collins) or REACT arcs (Honeywell) mark it.","keys":[{"k":"Attenuation / shadow","m":["attenuat","shadow"]},{"k":"PAC or REACT","m":["pac","react"]}]},{"q":"How high is the cell at 40 NM if the return disappears at +4° with a 3° beam?","a":"Lower edge 2.5°; 40 × 2.5 × 100 = 10,000 ft above you; the real top may be higher.","keys":[{"k":"10,000 ft","m":["10000","10 000","ten thousand"]}]},{"q":"Magenta appears. What is it and what are its limits?","a":"Turbulence in precipitation (WX+T), within 40 NM; CAT is not detected.","keys":[{"k":"Turbulence","m":["turbulence"]},{"k":"40 NM","m":["40 nm","40nm","40 nautical"]}]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"Surveillance chain: what does ATC see of you?","src":"OM-A 3.3; FCOM 11.34","ref":"NR53","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"Primary versus secondary radar?","a":"Primary: echo, range and bearing only, no equipment. SSR: interrogate 1030, reply 1090, identity and altitude.","keys":[{"k":"1030 / 1090","m":["1030","1090"]},{"k":"Echo vs transponder","m":["echo","reflect","transponder"]}]},{"q":"What does Mode S add?","a":"A 24-bit selective address, data link, and extended squitter, which carries ADS-B.","keys":[{"k":"24-bit","m":["24 bit","24bit"]},{"k":"Squitter","m":["squitter"]}]},{"q":"How is ADS-B different from ADS-C?","a":"ADS-B broadcasts GNSS position, no logon; ADS-C is contract reporting to ATC after AFN logon via data link, used oceanic.","keys":[{"k":"Broadcast","m":["broadcast"]},{"k":"Contract / logon","m":["contract","logon","log on"]}]},{"q":"ATC asks you to stop squawking. Consequence?","a":"STBY also disables TCAS and ADS-B OUT, so tell ATC.","keys":[{"k":"TCAS lost","m":["tcas"]},{"k":"ADS-B OUT lost","m":["ads b","adsb"]}]}],"topic":12},{"id":"t12-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw how weather radar tilt finds a cloud top.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR44","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- Aircraft with the radar beam (3 to 4° wide) tilted up.\n- The cell's top just touching the **lower** beam edge as the return disappears.\n- Lower edge angle = tilt − half beam width.\n- Height above aircraft = range (NM) × angle (°) × 100 ft.\n- Example: 40 NM, +4°, 3° beam: 2.5°, 10,000 ft.","checklist":["Beam drawn with upper and lower edges","Tilt angle from horizontal marked","Half beam width marked","Cloud top at lower beam edge","Range marked","Formula range × angle × 100"],"topic":12},{"id":"t13-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"How do you know you're on a false glideslope, and how do you make sure you capture the right one?","src":"OM-A 28.7.1, 27.5; FCTM 5.16; Standard theory","ref":"S35","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**False glideslopes are side lobes at about 2× and 3× the glide angle (6° and 9° on a 3° GS); the 6° lobe has reversed sensing. None lies below the true GS.**\nSigns:\n- rate of descent far above normal (about 5 × GS: roughly 700 fpm at 140 kt on 3°, double on 6°);\n- altitude/DME check failing; abnormal pitch; erratic GS.\nPrevention:\n- intercept from below;\n- check altitude at the FAP/OM against the chart and DME/altitude table;\n- cross-check VNAV path and PAPI.\nBoeing adds: an ILS left in test mode can give a steady on-glideslope indication; if in doubt, go around unless visual.","keys":[{"k":"6° and 9° side lobes","m":["6 and 9","6 degrees","9 degrees","side lobe","sidelobe"]},{"k":"6° lobe reverse sensing","m":["reverse"]},{"k":"High rate of descent","m":["rate of descent","descent rate","vertical speed","fpm"]},{"k":"Intercept from below","m":["from below","below"]},{"k":"Altitude/DME check at FAP/OM","m":["dme","fap","outer marker","faf"]},{"k":"Go around if in doubt","m":["go around","go-around","goaround","missed approach"]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-num-1","type":"num","q":"On a 3° glide slope at 140 kt groundspeed, roughly what rate of descent (rule: 5 × GS)?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S35","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":700,"unit":"fpm","why":"5 × 140 = 700 fpm. On a 6° false lobe it would be about double.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"Is there a false glideslope below the true glideslope?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S35","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["No, the false lobes are only above (about 6° and 9°)","Yes, at about 1.5°","Yes, a mirror image at 0°","Only on CAT I ILS"],"a":0,"why":"There is no false lobe below the true glideslope, which is why you intercept from below.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"Do Annex 10 documents describe the false glide path side-lobe angles?","src":"Standard theory; FCTM 5.16","ref":"S35","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"o":["No, the 6° and 9° figures are standard theory","Yes, Annex 10 3.1.5 gives 6° and 9°","Yes, but gives 4.5° and 7.5°","Only for CAT III"],"a":0,"why":"The reference notes the side-lobe angles are standard theory; Annex 10 doesn't describe them. Boeing adds the test-mode cause.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"You get a map shift on an ILS approach. What do you do?","src":"OM-A 30.6, 3.4.1, 25.4","ref":"S36","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**The ILS raw data is independent of the FMC position, so the ILS is still valid.** Continue if the LOC and GS raw data are good and agree with other cues.\n- Affected: the ND map and the EGPWS terrain display, both driven by FMC position. Expect false or missing terrain alerts.\n- Actions: check POS REF and GPS status; revert to raw data.\n- If there is any doubt, go around (instrument disagreement without visual reference).","keys":[{"k":"ILS raw data independent of FMC","m":["independent","raw data","raw"]},{"k":"Map and EGPWS terrain affected","m":["egpws","terrain"]},{"k":"False or missing terrain alerts","m":["false","missing","spurious"]},{"k":"Check POS REF / GPS","m":["pos ref","gps"]},{"k":"Go around if in doubt","m":["go around","go-around","goaround","missed approach"]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"During a map shift on an ILS, which display is NOT affected?","src":"OM-A 30.6","ref":"S36","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["ILS raw data (LOC and GS deviation)","ND map","EGPWS terrain display","FMC position-based waypoints"],"a":0,"why":"ILS raw data does not depend on FMC position; the map and EGPWS terrain do.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"You forgot to switch on the PWS during taxi. What happens on the 737?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"S37","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**There is no switch to forget: the 737 weather radar starts scanning for windshear automatically.**\n- It starts when the thrust levers are set for take-off (even with an engine off or the IRS not aligned), or in flight below 2300 ft RA (1800 ft RA with the RDR-4000).\n- Alerts are issued only below 1200 ft RA, about 12 s after scanning starts.\n- Pushing the EFIS WXR switch enables them before take-off.\n- The Airbus question (PWS switch to AUTO) doesn't translate. Reactive windshear is EGPWS mode 7 and needs no switch.","keys":[{"k":"Automatic, no switch","m":["automatic","no switch"]},{"k":"Thrust levers set for take-off","m":["thrust lever","takeoff thrust","take off thrust"]},{"k":"Below 2300 ft RA (1800 RDR-4000)","m":["2300","1800"]},{"k":"Alerts below 1200 ft RA","m":["1200"]},{"k":"EFIS WXR switch enables earlier","m":["wxr","efis"]},{"k":"Reactive is EGPWS mode 7","m":["mode 7","reactive"]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-num-2","type":"num","q":"PWS scanning starts automatically in flight below what radio altitude (WXR-2100 aircraft)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"S37","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2300,"unit":"ft RA","why":"2300 ft RA; 1800 ft RA on aircraft with the RDR-4000.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-num-3","type":"num","q":"PWS scanning starts automatically in flight below what radio altitude on aircraft with the RDR-4000?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"S37","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":1800,"unit":"ft RA","why":"1800 ft RA with the RDR-4000; 2300 ft RA otherwise.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-num-4","type":"num","q":"Predictive windshear alerts are issued only below what radio altitude?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"S37","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1200,"unit":"ft RA","why":"Below 1200 ft RA, about 12 s after scanning starts.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-num-5","type":"num","q":"About how many seconds after scanning starts are PWS alerts available?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"S37","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":12,"unit":"s","why":"About 12 s.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"Which EGPWS mode gives reactive windshear?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"S37","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Mode 7","Mode 4","Mode 5","Mode 1"],"a":0,"why":"Reactive windshear is EGPWS mode 7 and needs no switch.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"Windshear ahead at 120 kt on the take-off roll: what happens and what do you do?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25; QRH MAN.1.12, MAN.1.13; OM-A 17.3.6.4","ref":"S38","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**At 120 kt no NEW predictive alert will appear:** new PWS cautions are inhibited from 80 kt to 400 ft RA, new warnings from 100 kt to 50 ft RA; alerts already showing remain.\n- WINDSHEAR AHEAD warning before V1: reject. After V1: fly the escape manoeuvre.\n- Windshear encountered on the roll before V1: there may not be runway to stop if you reject at V1. At VR rotate normally towards 15°, then escape. If speed drops near VR and the runway is running out, rotate at least 2000 ft before the end, even at low speed.\n- Report it to ATC.","keys":[{"k":"Cautions inhibited 80 kt to 400 ft","m":["80 kt","80kt","80 knots","400 ft","400ft"]},{"k":"Warnings inhibited 100 kt to 50 ft","m":["100 kt","100kt","100 knots","50 ft","50ft"]},{"k":"Before V1 reject, after V1 escape","m":["reject","v1"]},{"k":"Rotate towards 15°","m":["15 degrees","15 deg","15°","fifteen"]},{"k":"2000 ft before end","m":["2000"]},{"k":"Report to ATC","m":["atc","report"]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the predictive windshear take-off inhibit to its band.","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"S38","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["New PWS cautions inhibited","80 kt to 400 ft RA"],["New PWS warnings inhibited","100 kt to 50 ft RA"],["Alerts already showing","Remain displayed"]],"why":"FCOM 15.20.25.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"Describe the windshear escape manoeuvre.","src":"QRH MAN.1.13, MAN.1.14; OM-A 17.3.6.4","ref":"S38","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**TO/GA, aggressively apply maximum thrust, wings level, rotate towards 15° pitch following F/D TO/GA guidance without exceeding the PLI, retract speedbrakes.**\n- Don't change flap or gear configuration until windshear is no longer a factor.\n- On the runway before V1 with windshear encountered: rotate at VR normally towards 15°; if speed decays near VR and the runway is running out, rotate at least 2000 ft before the end.\n- Report it to ATC.","keys":[{"k":"TO/GA, max thrust","m":["toga","to ga","maximum thrust","max thrust"]},{"k":"Wings level","m":["wings level"]},{"k":"Pitch towards 15°, not beyond PLI","m":["15 degrees","15 deg","15°","fifteen","pli"]},{"k":"Retract speedbrakes","m":["speedbrake","speed brake"]},{"k":"No config change","m":["flap","gear","configuration"]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-order-1","type":"order","q":"Windshear escape manoeuvre: put the actions in order.","src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"S38","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Push TO/GA","Aggressively apply maximum thrust","Wings level","Rotate towards 15° pitch following F/D, not exceeding PLI","Retract speedbrakes","Keep flap and gear until windshear no longer a factor"],"why":"QRH MAN.1.14 order.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-num-6","type":"num","q":"In a windshear on the take-off roll with runway running out, rotate at least how many feet before the runway end?","src":"QRH MAN.1.13; OM-A 17.3.6.4","ref":"S38","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2000,"unit":"ft","why":"At least 2000 ft before the end, even at low speed.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"WINDSHEAR AHEAD warning appears before V1. Action?","src":"QRH MAN.1.12","ref":"S38","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Reject the take-off","Continue and fly the escape manoeuvre","Continue and ignore it","Retract flaps"],"a":0,"why":"Before V1 reject; after V1 fly the escape manoeuvre.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"Transponder failure: how will you know, and what do you do?","src":"FCOM 11.10.25, 10.10.84; OM-A 3.3, Ch 3","ref":"S39","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Cockpit:** amber XPNDR (or ATC) FAIL light on the transponder panel, which also lights when ADS-B is inoperative, and TCAS FAIL on the ND.\n**Externally:** ATC reports loss of Mode C/S or ADS-B.\n**Actions:**\n- Select the other transponder (two are fitted).\n- Tell ATC and run the QRH.\n**If ATC asks you to stop squawking:** comply, but STBY also disables TCAS and ADS-B OUT, so tell ATC.","keys":[{"k":"XPNDR/ATC FAIL light","m":["xpndr","atc fail","fail light"]},{"k":"TCAS FAIL on ND","m":["tcas"]},{"k":"ATC reports loss","m":["atc report","atc tells","atc"]},{"k":"Select other transponder","m":["other transponder","second transponder","transponder 2","switch transponder"]},{"k":"STBY also kills TCAS and ADS-B","m":["stby","standby","ads b","adsb"]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"Lost IRS: what does the IRS supply and what disappears from the PFD?","src":"QRH 11.14 to 11.20; OM-A Ch 3; FCOM 11.20.6","ref":"S40","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**The IRS supplies attitude, heading, track, flight path vector, inertial vertical speed, ground speed and wind, and the map.**\n- Speed and altitude come from the air data side, so those remain.\n- The 737 has two ADIRUs plus the ISFD and standby compass.\n- In flight, run QRH IRS FAULT: transfer switch BOTH ON L or BOTH ON R.","keys":[{"k":"Attitude","m":["attitude"]},{"k":"Heading","m":["heading"]},{"k":"FPV / inertial VS / GS / wind","m":["flight path vector","fpv","vertical speed","ground speed","groundspeed","wind"]},{"k":"Map","m":["map"]},{"k":"Speed and altitude remain (air data)","m":["air data","adc","airspeed remain","speed and altitude"]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"IR fault in flight: what is the QRH action and the autopilot consequence?","src":"QRH 11.14 to 11.20","ref":"S40","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**IRS transfer switch BOTH ON L or BOTH ON R, to the working IRS.** It then feeds attitude and heading to both PFDs and NDs, AFDS guidance, the weather radar, and GPWS and windshear alerts.\n- **Fail-operational aircraft:** the autopilot on the working side may be used, but not for the approach.\n- **Fail-passive aircraft:** disengage the autopilot (it can't be re-engaged) and the autothrottle.","keys":[{"k":"Transfer switch BOTH ON L/R","m":["both on","transfer switch","transfer"]},{"k":"Working IRS feeds both sides","m":["working","both pfd","both sides"]},{"k":"Fail-operational: AP usable, not approach","m":["not for the approach","not for approach","fail operational"]},{"k":"Fail-passive: AP and A/T off","m":["fail passive","autothrottle","disengage"]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"How can an IRS be recovered in flight: ATT mode and Align In Motion?","src":"FCOM 11.20.12, 11.40.12","ref":"S40","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **ATT mode:** attitude only; needs periodic magnetic heading entries (POS INIT, SET IRS HDG).\n- **Align In Motion** (where ADIRUs are connected to the MMRs): restores NAV after an in-flight power loss. Hold straight and level for about 30 s; attitude returns within seconds and full navigation in about 10 to 30 min, but only with GPS.","keys":[{"k":"ATT mode attitude only","m":["att mode","attitude only","att"]},{"k":"Magnetic heading entries","m":["heading","set irs hdg","pos init"]},{"k":"Align In Motion","m":["align in motion","aim"]},{"k":"Straight and level 30 s","m":["30 s","30s","30 sec","straight and level"]},{"k":"Needs GPS, 10 to 30 min","m":["gps","10 to 30"]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"In IRS ATT mode, what does the IRS provide?","src":"FCOM 11.20.12, 11.40.12","ref":"S40","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Attitude only; heading must be entered periodically","Attitude, heading and position","Position only","Nothing; it is off"],"a":0,"why":"ATT mode gives attitude only and needs periodic magnetic heading entries.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"Align In Motion: how long must the aircraft be held straight and level?","src":"FCOM 11.20.12","ref":"S40","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["About 30 s","About 10 min","About 2 s","About 3 min"],"a":0,"why":"About 30 s; attitude returns within seconds, full NAV in about 10 to 30 min, only with GPS.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"IRS fault on a fail-passive aircraft: autopilot?","src":"QRH 11.14 to 11.20","ref":"S40","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Disengage autopilot (cannot re-engage) and autothrottle","Use AP on the working side for the approach","Keep both APs","Re-engage after transfer"],"a":0,"why":"Fail-passive: disengage AP and A/T. Fail-operational: AP on the working side but not for the approach.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"Localiser transmitter fails at 500 ft on an autoland. What do you see and what do you do?","src":"OM-A 45.2.4.2, 45.2.8.2, 25.4, 45.1.14; FCOM 4.20.24","ref":"S41","prio":1,"a":"**Go around.** A failure between 1000 ft and alert height (200 ft on the B737) means a go-around unless visual reference is established; ground equipment failure is also an immediate go-around item.\nWhat you see:\n- Before LAND 3 or LAND 2: autopilot disengages and/or F/D bars removed.\n- After LAND 3 or LAND 2: LOC scale amber and flashing, pointer blanks, amber line through the roll mode on the FMA, A/P stays engaged until you disengage it.\nBackground: the monitor shuts down an out-of-tolerance transmitter; changeover under 2 s.","keys":[{"k":"Go around","m":["go around","go-around","goaround","missed approach"]},{"k":"1000 ft to alert height","m":["1000","alert height"]},{"k":"AH 200 ft","m":["200 ft","200ft","200 feet"]},{"k":"Amber flashing LOC scale / pointer blanks","m":["amber","flash","blank"]},{"k":"FMA mode fail line","m":["fma","mode fail","line through"]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-num-7","type":"num","q":"What is the alert height on the B737?","src":"OM-A 45.2.4.2, 45.2.8.2","ref":"S41","prio":1,"ans":200,"unit":"ft","why":"Alert height 200 ft on the B737.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"After LAND 2 or LAND 3 is annunciated, the localiser fails. What happens to the autopilot?","src":"FCOM 4.20.24","ref":"S41","prio":2,"o":["It stays engaged until the crew disengages it; LOC scale amber and flashing","It disengages automatically","It reverts to LNAV","It holds the last heading and continues the autoland"],"a":0,"why":"FCOM 4.20.24: after LAND 3/LAND 2 the A/P stays engaged until you disengage it.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"On a VOR arrival the VOR monitor shuts the station down: what do you see, what do you do?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.7; OM-A 25.4","ref":"S52","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**You see the VOR flag and the bearing pointer removed or parked, and the ident stops.** DME may continue if it is separate.\n- On a VOR approach: go around (ground equipment failure).\n- En route or on an arrival: continue on the FMC if within the specification, and tell ATC.\n- The monitor warns a control point and removes ident and navigation components or stops radiation for: a bearing change of more than 1° at the monitor site; a 15% reduction in subcarrier or 30 Hz modulation; or failure of the monitor itself.","keys":[{"k":"Flag, pointer removed, ident stops","m":["flag","ident"]},{"k":"Go around on VOR approach","m":["go around","go-around","goaround","missed approach"]},{"k":"Continue on FMC, tell ATC","m":["fmc","atc"]},{"k":"Bearing change over 1°","m":["1 degree","1 deg","one degree","more than 1"]},{"k":"15% modulation reduction","m":["15 percent","15%","15 per"]},{"k":"Monitor failure also","m":["monitor fail","failure of the monitor","monitor itself"]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-num-8","type":"num","q":"VOR monitor action is triggered by a bearing change of more than how many degrees at the monitor site?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.7","ref":"S52","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1,"unit":"°","why":"More than 1°.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-num-9","type":"num","q":"VOR monitor action is triggered by what percentage reduction in the subcarrier or 30 Hz modulation?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.7","ref":"S52","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":15,"unit":"%","why":"A 15% reduction.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"Unreliable airspeed climbing out of Mumbai at night in heavy rain: your actions?","src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"S53","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Memory items (QRH Airspeed Unreliable):**\n- Autopilot disengage, autothrottle disengage, both F/D switches OFF.\n- Set gear-up pitch and thrust: flaps extended 10° and 80% N1; flaps up 4° and 75% N1.\n**Then:** check probe heat ON. Reliable: attitude, N1, ground speed, radio altitude. Stick shaker, overspeed and AIRSPEED LOW may be false.\n**QRH:** pitch and power tables, GPS ground speed, AOA indicator if fitted.\n**Around that:** declare an emergency, keep terrain and MSA in mind, plan the return.","keys":[{"k":"A/P, A/T off, F/Ds off","m":["autopilot","autothrottle","flight director","f d"]},{"k":"Flaps extended 10° / 80% N1","m":["80"]},{"k":"Flaps up 4° / 75% N1","m":["75"]},{"k":"Probe heat on","m":["probe heat"]},{"k":"Pitch and power tables","m":["table","pitch and power"]},{"k":"Declare emergency / MSA","m":["mayday","emergency","msa","terrain"]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-match-2","type":"match","q":"Airspeed Unreliable memory items: match configuration to pitch and thrust.","src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"S53","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Flaps extended","10° and 80% N1"],["Flaps up","4° and 75% N1"]],"why":"QRH 10.1 memory items.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-num-10","type":"num","q":"Airspeed unreliable, flaps extended: memory pitch attitude?","src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"S53","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":10,"unit":"°","why":"Flaps extended: 10° and 80% N1.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-num-11","type":"num","q":"Airspeed unreliable, flaps up: memory N1?","src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"S53","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":75,"unit":"%","why":"Flaps up: 4° and 75% N1.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-order-2","type":"order","q":"Unreliable airspeed: put the memory items in order.","src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"S53","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Autopilot disengage","Autothrottle disengage","F/D switches (both) OFF","Set gear-up pitch and thrust (10°/80% N1 flaps extended; 4°/75% N1 flaps up)"],"why":"QRH 10.1 memory items, then probe heat and the checklist.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"With unreliable airspeed, which indications remain reliable?","src":"QRH 10.1","ref":"S53","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Attitude, N1, ground speed, radio altitude","IAS, Mach, altitude, VS","Stick shaker, overspeed, AIRSPEED LOW","Pitot static altitude and IAS"],"a":0,"why":"QRH 10.1; stick shaker, overspeed and AIRSPEED LOW may be false.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"\"TOO LOW TERRAIN\" on a visual approach in good weather at an unfamiliar airport. What does it mean and what do you do?","src":"OM-A 25, 30.5, 30.8","ref":"S54","prio":2,"a":"**A Mode 4 or Terrain Clearance Floor alert: you are closer to the terrain than expected for your configuration and distance,** often from starting down too early on an unfamiliar visual.\n- It is a caution, not a PULL UP. Correct the flight path at once: check altitude against distance and PAPI.\n- If unsure, or you can't be stabilised by 500 ft on a visual, go around.\n- EGPWS TCF and RFCF (used at Mangalore and Calicut) protect exactly this case.\n- Report it if you modified the flight path.","keys":[{"k":"Mode 4 / TCF","m":["mode 4","tcf","terrain clearance floor"]},{"k":"Caution, not pull up","m":["caution","not a pull up","not pull up"]},{"k":"Check altitude v distance, PAPI","m":["papi","distance"]},{"k":"Stable by 500 ft or go around","m":["500"]},{"k":"Report it","m":["report"]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-num-12","type":"num","q":"On a visual approach, by what height must you be stabilised or go around?","src":"OM-A 25","ref":"S54","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":500,"unit":"ft","why":"Stabilised by 500 ft on a visual approach, otherwise go around.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"Which EGPWS functions protect against early descent at airports like Mangalore and Calicut?","src":"OM-A 30.5","ref":"S54","prio":2,"o":["TCF and RFCF","Mode 1 and Mode 2","Mode 6 callouts","Mode 7 windshear"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 30.5: TCF and RFCF.","topic":13},{"id":"t13-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"ILS in IMC, cleared for the approach from above the glide slope.","src":"FCTM 5.16; OM-A 30.6, 45.2.4.2, 25.4","ref":"S35","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What's the threat?","a":"False glide slope: side lobes at about 6° (reverse sensing) and 9°; none below the true GS.","keys":[{"k":"False GS","m":["false","side lobe","sidelobe"]},{"k":"6° reverse","m":["reverse","6 degrees","6 deg"]}]},{"q":"How do you detect a false capture?","a":"Abnormal descent rate (5 × GS) and pitch, altitude/DME and FAF crossing altitude disagree, VNAV path, PAPI, about 300 ft per NM.","keys":[{"k":"Descent rate / pitch","m":["descent","pitch","fpm"]},{"k":"Altitude/DME / 300 ft per NM","m":["dme","300"]}]},{"q":"Now the map shifts on short final. Is the ILS still good?","a":"Yes, raw ILS is independent of FMC position; map and EGPWS terrain may be wrong; check POS REF/GPS.","keys":[{"k":"Raw ILS independent","m":["independent","raw"]},{"k":"EGPWS terrain affected","m":["egpws","terrain"]}]},{"q":"At 500 ft on autoland the localiser fails. Action?","a":"Go around: failure between 1000 ft and alert height (200 ft), ground equipment failure.","keys":[{"k":"Go around","m":["go around","go-around","goaround","missed approach"]}]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"Take-off from Mumbai at night in heavy rain; you forgot the PWS switch.","src":"FCOM 15.20.25; QRH MAN.1.14, 10.1","ref":"S38","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"Does the 737 need a PWS switch?","a":"No; scanning starts automatically with take-off thrust set, or below 2300 ft RA in flight; EFIS WXR enables alerts before take-off.","keys":[{"k":"Automatic","m":["automatic","no switch"]},{"k":"Thrust levers","m":["thrust"]}]},{"q":"At 120 kt, would you get a new predictive alert?","a":"No: new cautions inhibited 80 kt to 400 ft RA, new warnings 100 kt to 50 ft RA.","keys":[{"k":"Inhibited","m":["inhibit"]},{"k":"80 kt / 100 kt","m":["80 kt","80kt","100 kt","100kt","80 knots","100 knots"]}]},{"q":"You encounter windshear after V1. Escape manoeuvre?","a":"TO/GA, max thrust, wings level, pitch towards 15° within PLI, speedbrakes in, no config change.","keys":[{"k":"TO/GA max thrust","m":["toga","to ga","max thrust","maximum thrust"]},{"k":"15° within PLI","m":["15 degrees","15 deg","15°","pli"]}]},{"q":"Climbing out, the airspeeds disagree. Memory items?","a":"A/P, A/T off, F/Ds off; flaps extended 10° and 80% N1, flaps up 4° and 75% N1; then probe heat and QRH tables.","keys":[{"k":"A/P A/T off","m":["autopilot","autothrottle"]},{"k":"10°/80%, 4°/75%","m":["80","75"]}]},{"q":"What else?","a":"Declare an emergency, keep terrain and MSA in mind, plan the return, report the windshear.","keys":[{"k":"Emergency","m":["mayday","emergency","pan"]},{"k":"MSA / terrain","m":["msa","terrain"]}]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t13-chain-3","type":"chain","q":"Cruise: an IRS fault and the transponder fail light.","src":"QRH 11.14 to 11.20; FCOM 11.20.12, 11.10.25; OM-A 3.3","ref":"S40","asked":true,"prio":2,"steps":[{"q":"What does the IRS give you?","a":"Attitude, heading, track, FPV, inertial VS, GS, wind and the map; speed and altitude are air data.","keys":[{"k":"Attitude, heading","m":["attitude","heading"]}]},{"q":"QRH action?","a":"IRS transfer switch BOTH ON the working side; fail-passive: AP and A/T off; fail-operational: AP on working side, not for approach.","keys":[{"k":"Both on","m":["both on","transfer"]}]},{"q":"Can you get it back?","a":"ATT mode (attitude, enter heading), or Align In Motion: 30 s straight and level, NAV in 10 to 30 min with GPS.","keys":[{"k":"ATT / Align in motion","m":["att","align in motion"]},{"k":"GPS needed","m":["gps"]}]},{"q":"Then XPNDR FAIL lights. What now?","a":"Select the other transponder, tell ATC, QRH; STBY would also lose TCAS and ADS-B OUT.","keys":[{"k":"Other transponder","m":["other transponder","second transponder","transponder 2"]},{"k":"Tell ATC","m":["atc"]}]}],"topic":13},{"id":"t14-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"Explain the principle of an inertial navigation system.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: INS / IRS","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"An INS measures accelerations and integrates them: **once for velocity, again for distance**.\n- **Platform** INS: gyros and accelerometers on a gimballed, stabilised platform.\n- **Strapdown** IRS: ring laser gyros and accelerometers fixed to the airframe; the computer does the platform's job.\n- RLG: counter-rotating laser beams, the **Sagnac** frequency difference measures rotation.\n- Errors: drift grows with time, about **1 to 2 NM/h**.","keys":[{"k":"integrate twice","m":["integrat","velocity and distance","velocity then distance"]},{"k":"accelerometers","m":["accelerometer"]},{"k":"platform vs strapdown","m":["strapdown","strap down","gimbal","platform"]},{"k":"drift 1 to 2 NM/h","m":["1 to 2 nm","1 to 2 nautical","2 nm per hour","2 nm/h","2nm per hour","drift"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"How does a ring laser gyro work, and what is a dither motor for?","src":"Standard theory; FCOM 11.20.6","ref":"Key facts: INS / IRS; NR10","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"A ring laser gyro has **no moving parts**. Two laser beams travel in opposite directions round a closed path. When the unit rotates, one beam's path gets longer and the other's shorter, giving a **frequency difference (Sagnac effect)** proportional to the rotation rate.\n- **Lock-in:** at very low rotation rates the two beams lock together and no rotation is sensed.\n- **Cure:** mechanical **dither**, a small vibration that keeps the gyro out of the lock-in zone.\n- The 737's ADIRUs use them: three RLGs and three accelerometers per IRS.","keys":[{"k":"counter-rotating beams","m":["counter rotating","opposite direction","two beams","two laser"]},{"k":"Sagnac / frequency difference","m":["sagnac","frequency difference","frequency shift"]},{"k":"lock-in at low rates","m":["lock in","lockin","lock-in"]},{"k":"dither","m":["dither"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"How does an IRS align, how long does it take on the 737, and where can it not align?","src":"FCOM SP.11.5; FCOM L.10.9; FCOM 11.20.7, 11.20.10","ref":"Key facts: INS / IRS","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Alignment is **levelling plus gyrocompassing**: it senses gravity to level and senses earth rate to find true north.\n- On the 737 it takes **about 5 to 17 minutes**, longer at higher latitudes.\n- It is **not possible near the poles**, where the horizontal earth-rate component is too small: do not attempt ADIRU alignment **above 78°15'** latitude.\n- Present position: GPS from the MMRs where available, otherwise FMC POS INIT or the ISDU keyboard.\n- The aircraft **must stay stationary**.","keys":[{"k":"levelling + gyrocompassing","m":["gyrocompass","gyro compass","earth rate","levelling","leveling"]},{"k":"5 to 17 min","m":["5 to 17","17 min","five to seventeen"]},{"k":"78 deg 15 min limit","m":["78 degrees","78 deg","7815","78 15","78°"]},{"k":"stationary","m":["stationary","not move","must not be moved","no movement"]},{"k":"position source","m":["gps","pos init","mmr","isdu"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-num-1","type":"num","q":"Above what latitude must ADIRU alignment not be attempted on the 737? (degrees, to nearest 0.25)","src":"FCOM L.10.9","ref":"Key facts: INS / IRS","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":78.25,"unit":"°","why":"78°15' (78.25°). Near the poles the horizontal earth-rate component is too small to gyrocompass.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"Typical 737 IRS full alignment time?","src":"FCOM SP.11.5; FCOM 11.20.7","ref":"Key facts: INS / IRS","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["3 to 10 min","5 to 17 min","About 20 min","30 s"],"a":1,"why":"5 to 17 minutes depending on latitude. 30 s is the fast realignment.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-num-2","type":"num","q":"How long does a 737 IRS fast realignment take (seconds)?","src":"FCOM 11.20.7; FCOM NP.21.1","ref":"Key facts: INS / IRS","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":30,"unit":"s","why":"30 s while parked; present position must be entered to return to NAV. A full alignment is recommended before each flight.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"Fast realignment versus full alignment on the 737: when and how?","src":"FCOM 11.20.7; FCOM NP.21.1","ref":"Key facts: INS / IRS","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Fast realignment** takes about **30 seconds** while parked. It re-levels and zeroes ground speed, and you must **enter present position** to return to NAV.\n- **Full alignment** (levelling and gyrocompassing) takes 5 to 17 min depending on latitude, aircraft stationary throughout.\n- The FCOM recommends **a full alignment before each flight**.","keys":[{"k":"30 s","m":["30 s","30s","30 sec","thirty sec"]},{"k":"enter present position","m":["present position","enter position","pos init"]},{"k":"full before each flight","m":["each flight","every flight"]},{"k":"5 to 17 min","m":["5 to 17","17 min"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"What is the typical drift rate of an INS/IRS?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: INS / IRS","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["0.1 to 0.2 NM/h","1 to 2 NM/h","5 to 10 NM/h","15 NM/h"],"a":1,"why":"About 1 to 2 NM/h, and it grows with time.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"How many ADIRUs does the 737 have, and what does each IRS contain?","src":"OM-A Ch 3; FCOM 11.20.6","ref":"Key facts: INS / IRS","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["2; three laser ring gyros and three accelerometers","3; three laser ring gyros and three accelerometers","2; two mechanical gyros and three accelerometers","3; one platform with two gyros"],"a":0,"why":"Two ADIRUs, each IRS with three laser ring gyros and three accelerometers.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-num-3","type":"num","q":"Maximum flight operating latitude of the 737 (degrees N or S), set by the ADIRU magnetic variation tables?","src":"FCOM L.10.2","ref":"Key facts: INS / IRS","prio":3,"ans":82,"unit":"°","why":"82°N and 82°S, with exceptions: 73°N between 80°W and 170°W, 60°S between 120°E and 160°E.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"What limits the 737's maximum flight operating latitude of 82°?","src":"FCOM L.10.2","ref":"Key facts: INS / IRS","trap":true,"prio":3,"o":["ADIRU magnetic variation tables","GPS satellite geometry","Fuel temperature","IRS alignment limit"],"a":0,"why":"The ADIRU magnetic variation tables. 78°15' is the separate alignment limit.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"How is the 737 FMC position kept accurate?","src":"OM-A Ch 40; FCOM 11.31.4","ref":"Key facts: INS / IRS","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["IRS position updated by GNSS through the MMR","GPS only, IRS for attitude only","DME/DME only","Pilot entry every 30 min"],"a":0,"why":"The FMC position is IRS updated by GNSS through the MMR (and radio aids).","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"Take me from IAS to TAS. What correction gives each speed?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Pitot-static","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**IAS → CAS → EAS → TAS.**\n- **CAS** = IAS corrected for **instrument and position error**.\n- **EAS** = CAS corrected for **compressibility**.\n- **TAS** = EAS corrected for **density**.\nRule of thumb: TAS is about 2% higher than CAS per 1000 ft.","keys":[{"k":"CAS: instrument + position","m":["instrument and position","position error","instrument error"]},{"k":"EAS: compressibility","m":["compressib"]},{"k":"TAS: density","m":["density"]},{"k":"sequence IAS CAS EAS TAS","m":["cas","eas"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-order-1","type":"order","q":"Put the airspeeds in order of correction, starting from the ASI reading.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Pitot-static","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["IAS","CAS (instrument and position error)","EAS (compressibility)","TAS (density)"],"why":"IAS → CAS → EAS → TAS.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match each ASI error to the speed you get after correcting it.","src":"Standard theory; Oral bank p.85","ref":"Key facts: Pitot-static; OB67","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Instrument and position error","CAS"],["Compressibility error","EAS"],["Density error","TAS"]],"why":"IAS corrected for instrument and position error is CAS; for compressibility, EAS; for density, TAS.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-num-4","type":"num","q":"Local speed of sound at mean sea level in ISA (kt)?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Pitot-static","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":661,"unit":"kt","tol":1,"why":"LSS = 38.94 √T(K) kt; at 288 K that is about 661 kt.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"Which formula gives the local speed of sound in knots?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Pitot-static","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["38.94 √T (T in K)","38.94 √T (T in °C)","661 × √T (T in K)","39 × T (T in K)"],"a":0,"why":"LSS = 38.94 √T with T in kelvin. It depends only on temperature.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"What is the crossover altitude?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Pitot-static","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Climbing at **constant CAS, Mach number rises** (LSS falls with temperature and TAS rises with altitude). The **crossover altitude** is where the **CAS and Mach schedules meet**: above it the aircraft climbs at constant Mach, and CAS then falls.","keys":[{"k":"constant CAS climb","m":["constant cas","constant ias"]},{"k":"Mach rises","m":["mach rises","mach increases","mach number increases","mach goes up"]},{"k":"CAS and Mach schedules meet","m":["meet","equal","same","switch"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"What happens to the instruments with a pitot blockage and with a static blockage?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Pitot-static","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Pitot blocked (drain also blocked):** the ASI behaves like an **altimeter**: it **over-reads in the climb** (and under-reads in the descent). \"ASI increasing in the climb in icing\" means pitot blocked.\n- **Static blocked:** the **altimeter freezes**, the **VSI shows zero**, and the ASI **under-reads in the climb** and **over-reads in the descent**.\n- A leak in the pitot line makes the ASI under-read.","keys":[{"k":"pitot: ASI acts as altimeter","m":["altimeter"]},{"k":"pitot: over-reads in climb","m":["over reads in climb","overreads in climb","over read in the climb","overreads in the climb","over reads in the climb","increases in climb","increase in the climb"]},{"k":"static: altimeter frozen","m":["frozen","freez","stuck"]},{"k":"static: VSI zero","m":["vsi zero","vsi at zero","vsi shows zero","vsi reads zero"]},{"k":"static: ASI under-reads in climb","m":["under reads in climb","underreads in climb","under reads in the climb","underreads in the climb","under read"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"In icing during the climb the ASI keeps increasing. What is blocked?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Pitot-static","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["Pitot (with drain)","Static port","Both pitot and static","Nothing, it is TAS rising"],"a":0,"why":"With the pitot and drain blocked, trapped pressure stays fixed while static falls, so the ASI over-reads like an altimeter.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"Static port blocked. During a descent the ASI will:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Pitot-static","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Over-read","Under-read","Read correctly","Read zero"],"a":0,"why":"Static blocked: ASI under-reads in the climb and over-reads in the descent; altimeter frozen, VSI zero.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"You are climbing out through cloud in icing and the ASI is creeping up. Talk me through it.","src":"Standard theory; FCOM Ch 3, Probe and Sensor Heat; NR58","ref":"Key facts: Pitot-static; OB65; NR58","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What is blocked?","a":"Pitot probe with its drain: trapped total pressure, falling static, so ASI over-reads.","keys":[{"k":"pitot blocked","m":["pitot"]},{"k":"acts like altimeter","m":["altimeter"]}]},{"q":"What would a static blockage show instead?","a":"Altimeter frozen, VSI zero, ASI under-reads in climb and over-reads in descent.","keys":[{"k":"altimeter frozen","m":["frozen","freez","stuck"]},{"k":"VSI zero","m":["zero"]},{"k":"ASI under-reads in climb","m":["under"]}]},{"q":"Are the static ports heated on the MAX?","a":"No. Pitots, TAT probe and alpha vanes are heated; static ports are not.","keys":[{"k":"static not heated","m":["not heated","no heat","unheated"]},{"k":"pitot heated","m":["pitot"]}]},{"q":"What does the Machmeter do with a static blockage in the climb?","a":"Under-reads in the climb (over-reads in the descent).","keys":[{"k":"under-reads in climb","m":["under"]}]},{"q":"What is your procedure?","a":"Unreliable airspeed: cross-check standby and the other side, fly the QRH pitch and thrust settings (S53).","keys":[{"k":"unreliable airspeed","m":["unreliable"]},{"k":"pitch and thrust","m":["pitch and thrust","pitch thrust","pitch and power"]}]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"Explain the two fundamental gyro properties.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR10","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Rigidity:** a spinning rotor **resists any change to the direction of its spin axis**. It increases with rotor **mass, radius and speed** (RPM).\n- **Precession:** a force applied to the rim takes effect **90° later in the direction of rotation**.\nRigidity lets a gyro hold a reference; precession is how we erect, torque and correct it.","keys":[{"k":"rigidity: resists change","m":["rigidity","resist"]},{"k":"mass, radius, speed","m":["mass","radius","rpm","speed of rotation","rotor speed"]},{"k":"precession","m":["precession"]},{"k":"90 degrees in direction of rotation","m":["90 degrees","90°","90 deg","ninety"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-num-5","type":"num","q":"Precession: a force applied to the gyro rim takes effect how many degrees later in the direction of rotation?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR10","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":90,"unit":"°","why":"90° later, in the direction of rotation.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Rigidity of a gyro increases with:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR10","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Rotor mass, radius and speed","Lower rotor speed","More gimbals","Higher latitude"],"a":0,"why":"Rigidity increases with mass, radius (moment of inertia) and rotor speed.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"What types of gyro are there? Give an example of each.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR10","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Space (free) gyro:** 2 degrees of freedom, fixed in space.\n- **Tied gyro:** 2 degrees, tied to a reference, e.g. the **directional gyro**.\n- **Earth gyro:** tied to the earth's vertical, e.g. the **attitude indicator**.\n- **Rate gyro:** **1 degree of freedom**, measures rate of turn, e.g. the **turn indicator**.\n- **Rate-integrating gyro.**\n- **Ring laser gyro:** no moving parts, Sagnac shift, used in the 737's ADIRUs.","keys":[{"k":"space/free gyro","m":["space gyro","free gyro","space"]},{"k":"tied: directional gyro","m":["directional gyro","tied"]},{"k":"earth: attitude indicator","m":["attitude indicator","artificial horizon","earth gyro"]},{"k":"rate: turn indicator","m":["turn indicator","rate gyro","turn and slip","turn coordinator"]},{"k":"ring laser gyro","m":["ring laser","rlg","laser gyro"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match the gyro type to its instrument.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR10","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Tied gyro","Directional gyro"],["Earth gyro","Attitude indicator"],["Rate gyro","Turn indicator"],["Ring laser gyro","737 ADIRU"]],"why":"DG is a tied gyro, the AI an earth gyro, the turn indicator a rate gyro; the ADIRUs use RLGs.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"What is gyro wander? Give the types.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR10","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Wander is any movement of the spin axis away from its reference.\n- **Real wander:** the axis actually moves, caused by **friction and imbalance**.\n- **Apparent wander:** the axis stays fixed in space but the **earth rotates** beneath it: **15°/h × sin(latitude)**.\n- **Transport wander:** caused by **moving over the earth** (the reference direction changes as you travel).","keys":[{"k":"real: friction/imbalance","m":["friction","imbalance","real wander"]},{"k":"apparent: earth rotation","m":["earth rotation","earths rotation","apparent"]},{"k":"15 deg/h x sin lat","m":["15 sin","sin lat","sine of latitude","sine latitude","15 degrees per hour","15 deg"]},{"k":"transport wander","m":["transport"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"Apparent wander of a directional gyro at latitude λ is:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR10","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["15°/h × sin λ","15°/h × cos λ","15°/h × tan λ","15°/h at all latitudes"],"a":0,"why":"Apparent wander = 15°/h × sin(latitude): zero at the equator, 15°/h at the poles. (Earth-rate horizontal component for alignment uses cos.)","topic":14},{"id":"t14-num-6","type":"num","q":"Apparent wander of an uncorrected gyro at 30° latitude (°/h)?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR10","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":7.5,"unit":"°/h","tol":0.1,"why":"15 × sin 30° = 7.5°/h.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between degrees of freedom and planes of freedom in a gyro?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR16","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Degree of freedom:** an axis about which the spin axis is free to move, **not counting the spin axis itself**. Gimbals provide it.\n- **Plane of freedom:** a plane in which the spin axis can move. In most textbooks the two give **the same count**.\n- **Free gyro:** 2 degrees (two gimbals), e.g. AI and DG. **Rate gyro:** 1 degree, spring-restrained, e.g. turn indicator.\n- Some books count the spin axis and call a free gyro **3 degrees**, so say which convention you use.","keys":[{"k":"excludes spin axis","m":["spin axis","not counting"]},{"k":"gimbals provide it","m":["gimbal"]},{"k":"same count usually","m":["same"]},{"k":"free gyro 2, rate gyro 1","m":["2 degrees","two degrees","1 degree","one degree"]},{"k":"3 degree convention","m":["3 degrees","three degrees"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"How many degrees of freedom does a rate gyro (turn indicator) have (spin axis not counted)?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR16","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["0","1","2","3"],"a":1,"why":"One degree of freedom, restrained by a spring. A free gyro has 2 (or 3 if a book counts the spin axis).","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"Describe the construction of the ASI and the altimeter.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR58","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **ASI:** a capsule fed with **pitot (total) pressure** inside, in a sealed case fed with **static**. The capsule expands with **dynamic pressure (½ρV²)**, and a linkage moves the pointer. Calibrated to **ISA MSL density**. Errors: instrument, position, density, compressibility.\n- **Altimeter:** **evacuated aneroid capsules** in a case fed with **static** pressure; they expand as pressure falls. A **subscale** sets the datum (QNH, QFE, 1013). A servo version also exists.","keys":[{"k":"ASI: pitot in capsule, static in case","m":["pitot","total pressure"]},{"k":"dynamic pressure","m":["dynamic pressure","half rho v","12 rho v","rho v squared"]},{"k":"ISA MSL calibration","m":["isa","sea level"]},{"k":"altimeter: evacuated aneroid","m":["aneroid","evacuated"]},{"k":"subscale datum","m":["subscale","sub scale","qnh"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Sketch the ASI: show what pressure goes where.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR58","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"A sealed instrument case with a flexible capsule inside.\n- Pitot (total) pressure line connected into the capsule.\n- Static line connected into the case around the capsule.\n- Capsule expansion (dynamic pressure = pitot minus static) driving a linkage and pointer over a dial.","checklist":["Sealed case drawn","Capsule inside the case","Pitot (total) pressure fed into the capsule","Static pressure fed into the case","Linkage from capsule to pointer","Labelled: deflection due to dynamic pressure (½ρV²)"],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"How do the VSI and IVSI work, and what are the IVSI's errors?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR58","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **VSI:** a capsule fed **directly with static**; the case fed with static through a **metering unit (calibrated leak)**. In a climb or descent the case pressure **lags** the capsule, and that difference is the rate of climb or descent.\n- **IVSI:** a VSI plus an **accelerometer unit** of small dashpots with weighted pistons. At the start of a vertical acceleration they pump air into the capsule side immediately, **removing the lag**.\n- **IVSI errors:** a **false climb in steep turns**, and **overreading in turbulence**.","keys":[{"k":"static to capsule, case via metering unit","m":["metering","calibrated leak","choke"]},{"k":"lag gives the rate","m":["lag"]},{"k":"IVSI dashpots/accelerometer","m":["dashpot","accelerometer","piston"]},{"k":"false climb in steep turns","m":["steep turn","false climb"]},{"k":"overreads in turbulence","m":["turbulence"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"How does a Machmeter work, and what errors does it have?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR58","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"It combines an **airspeed capsule (pitot minus static)** and an **altimeter capsule (static)** through a **ratio arm**. Mach is a function of **(Pt − Ps)/Ps**, so it needs **no temperature input**.\n- **Errors:** **instrument and position only**. No temperature or density error; compressibility is calibrated in.\n- **Pitot blocked:** under-reads in descent, over-reads in climb.\n- **Static blocked:** over-reads in descent, under-reads in climb.","keys":[{"k":"airspeed + altimeter capsule","m":["airspeed capsule","altimeter capsule","two capsules","ratio arm"]},{"k":"(Pt-Ps)/Ps","m":["pt ps ps","pitot minus static over static","dynamic over static","ratio"]},{"k":"no temperature input","m":["no temperature","not temperature","no temp","temperature not"]},{"k":"instrument and position errors only","m":["instrument and position"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"Which errors does a Machmeter suffer from?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR58","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Instrument and position only","Instrument, position and temperature","Density and compressibility","Instrument, position, density and compressibility"],"a":0,"why":"Mach is a function of (Pt − Ps)/Ps: no temperature or density error; compressibility is calibrated in.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"Machmeter with a blocked static source, in a climb, will:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR58","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Under-read","Over-read","Read correctly","Freeze"],"a":0,"why":"Static blocked: Machmeter under-reads in the climb and over-reads in the descent.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"Machmeter with a blocked pitot, in a descent, will:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR58","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Under-read","Over-read","Read correctly","Read zero"],"a":0,"why":"Pitot blocked: under-reads in descent, over-reads in climb.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"Where is the EGT sensor located, and why there?","src":"Standard theory; FCOM 7.10.8","ref":"NR63","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"EGT is measured by **thermocouple probes (chromel-alumel)**, several around the engine case, **averaged through a harness**.\n- The **HPT inlet is too hot** for probes to survive, so EGT is sensed **between the HP and LP turbines** (LPT inlet or its first nozzle stage).\n- GE90 (777): at the LPT inlet. CFM56-7 and LEAP-1B: LPT nozzle area just behind the HPT (station about 4.9 to 4.95).\n- It is the best measurable indicator of turbine health and margin.\n- Not visible on a walk-round: the probes sit on the core casing under the fan and core cowls.","keys":[{"k":"thermocouples","m":["thermocouple","chromel","alumel"]},{"k":"averaged","m":["averag","harness"]},{"k":"between HPT and LPT","m":["between the high","between hp and lp","between hpt and lpt","lpt inlet","low pressure turbine","lp turbine"]},{"k":"HPT inlet too hot","m":["too hot","too high"]},{"k":"under cowls, not seen on walk-round","m":["cowl","walk round","walkaround","walk around"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"What does the lower end of the amber EGT band on the 737 indicate?","src":"FCOM 7.10.8","ref":"NR63","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Maximum continuous EGT","Maximum take-off EGT","Maximum in-flight start EGT","Ground start limit"],"a":0,"why":"Lower end of the amber band is the maximum continuous EGT; the redlines are max take-off and max in-flight start EGT.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-num-7","type":"num","q":"Until about what N2 (%) is the EGT start-limit redline shown?","src":"FCOM 7.10.8","ref":"NR63","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":66,"unit":"%","why":"Shown until stable idle, about 66% N2.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"Explain static pressure. Where is it sensed on your aircraft?","src":"Standard theory; FCOM 10.20.8; FCOM Ch 3, Probe and Sensor Heat","ref":"OB65","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Static pressure is the **ambient pressure of the undisturbed air** at the aircraft's level, acting **equally in all directions**, independent of the aircraft's motion.\n- Sensed by **flush static ports** where airflow gives pressure closest to true static; the residual is **position (pressure) error**, varying with speed, attitude and configuration.\n- **MAX:** **six static ports and three pitot probes**. Four static ports and two pitots feed **four air data modules** for the two ADIRUs; the auxiliary pitot and alternate static ports feed the **ISFD**.\n- **Static ports are not heated.**","keys":[{"k":"ambient undisturbed air","m":["ambient","undisturbed"]},{"k":"flush static ports","m":["flush","static port"]},{"k":"position error","m":["position error","pressure error"]},{"k":"6 static, 3 pitot","m":["six static","6 static","three pitot","3 pitot"]},{"k":"air data modules / ADIRU","m":["air data module","adm","adiru"]},{"k":"not heated","m":["not heated","unheated","no heat"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-16","type":"mcq","q":"How many static ports and pitot probes does the 737 MAX have?","src":"FCOM 10.20.8","ref":"OB65","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["6 static, 3 pitot","4 static, 2 pitot","6 static, 2 pitot","4 static, 3 pitot"],"a":0,"why":"Six flush static ports and three pitots; four statics and two pitots feed the ADMs, the rest feed the ISFD.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-17","type":"mcq","q":"Which of these is NOT heated on the 737 MAX?","src":"FCOM Ch 3, Probe and Sensor Heat","ref":"OB65","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Static ports","Pitot probes","TAT probe","Alpha vanes"],"a":0,"why":"Pitots, TAT probe and alpha vanes are heated; static ports are not.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"Why is TAT higher than OAT?","src":"Standard theory; FCOM 10.20.8; FCTM 4.10","ref":"OB66","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Because of **ram rise**. The TAT probe brings the air (nearly) to rest, converting kinetic energy to heat by **adiabatic compression**: **TAT = SAT + ram rise**.\n- Size: about **(TAS/100)² °C**, e.g. about 20°C at 450 kt TAS.\n- **Recovery factor:** the fraction of the full ram rise the probe senses, close to 1.\n- On the MAX the ADIRUs compute TAT and SAT from one TAT probe. **Don't use TAT instead of OAT for take-off performance.**\n- Raising Mach raises TAT, about 0.5 to 0.7°C per 0.01 M.","keys":[{"k":"ram rise","m":["ram rise","ram effect","ram"]},{"k":"adiabatic compression / kinetic energy","m":["adiabatic","kinetic","compression"]},{"k":"TAT = SAT + ram rise","m":["sat","static air temperature"]},{"k":"recovery factor","m":["recovery factor"]},{"k":"(TAS/100) squared","m":["tas 100","tas over 100","tas divided by 100","squared"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-num-8","type":"num","q":"Approximate ram rise at 450 kt TAS (°C)?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB66","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":20,"unit":"°C","tol":1,"why":"(TAS/100)² = 4.5² ≈ 20°C.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-18","type":"mcq","q":"Approximately how much does TAT rise per 0.01 M increase?","src":"FCTM 4.10","ref":"OB66","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["0.5 to 0.7°C","2 to 3°C","0.05°C","5°C"],"a":0,"why":"About 0.5 to 0.7°C per 0.01 M.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"What are the ASI errors? What is density error?","src":"Oral bank p.85; Standard theory","ref":"OB67","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Instrument error:** manufacturing tolerances and friction.\n- **Position (pressure) error:** the static port doesn't sense true static; varies with speed, AoA and configuration. Correcting these two gives **CAS**.\n- **Compressibility error:** above about **300 kt TAS** air compresses in the pitot, so the ASI **over-reads**; corrected gives **EAS**.\n- **Density error:** the ASI is calibrated to **ISA sea level density**; at altitude the air is thinner, so it **under-reads TAS**. Corrected gives **TAS**.\n- Rule of thumb: TAS about **2% above CAS per 1000 ft**.","keys":[{"k":"instrument error","m":["instrument error","instrument"]},{"k":"position error","m":["position","pressure error"]},{"k":"compressibility over-reads","m":["compressib"]},{"k":"density: ISA SL calibration","m":["sea level","isa"]},{"k":"density: under-reads TAS","m":["under reads","underreads","under read","lower than tas","less than tas"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-num-9","type":"num","q":"Compressibility error becomes significant above about what TAS (kt)?","src":"Oral bank p.85","ref":"OB67","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":300,"unit":"kt","why":"Above about 300 kt TAS the air compresses in the pitot and the ASI over-reads.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-num-10","type":"num","q":"Rule of thumb: TAS exceeds CAS by about what percentage per 1000 ft?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB67","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2,"unit":"%","why":"About 2% per 1000 ft.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-19","type":"mcq","q":"A leak in the pitot line makes the ASI:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB67","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Under-read","Over-read","Freeze","Act like an altimeter"],"a":0,"why":"A pitot leak lowers the sensed total pressure, so the ASI under-reads.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"What are pressure altitude and density altitude?","src":"Oral bank p.109; Standard theory","ref":"OB68","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Pressure altitude:** the ISA altitude corresponding to the static pressure, i.e. the altimeter reading with **1013.25 hPa (29.92 in)** set. Flight levels and Mode C use it.\n- **Density altitude:** pressure altitude **corrected for non-standard temperature**: the ISA altitude with the same air density.\n- Rule of thumb: about **120 ft per °C** of ISA deviation. Hot days raise density altitude and cut engine and aerodynamic performance.","keys":[{"k":"1013.25 / 29.92 set","m":["1013","2992","29 92"]},{"k":"corrected for temperature","m":["temperature"]},{"k":"same density in ISA","m":["same density","same air density"]},{"k":"120 ft per deg C","m":["120 ft","120 feet","120"]},{"k":"hot = worse performance","m":["performance","hot"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-num-11","type":"num","q":"Density altitude rule of thumb: feet per °C of ISA deviation?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB68","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":120,"unit":"ft","why":"About 120 ft per °C.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"Hard iron and soft iron magnetism. What magnetism does an aircraft acquire if it is hammered for a long time?","src":"Standard theory; FCOM 10.20.8","ref":"OB69","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Hammering for a long time gives hard iron (permanent) magnetism**, aligned with the earth's field the airframe sat in during construction (riveting, vibration on one heading).\n- **Hard iron:** permanent, retained by ferrous material, **does not change with heading**.\n- **Soft iron:** temporary, **induced** by the earth's field and electrical currents, **changes with heading**, attitude and latitude.\n- Both cause **deviation**; a **compass swing** measures it, the compensator removes what it can, the residual goes on the **correction card**. Swing with systems running.","keys":[{"k":"hard iron = permanent","m":["hard iron","permanent"]},{"k":"soft iron = induced/temporary","m":["soft iron","induced","temporary"]},{"k":"deviation","m":["deviation"]},{"k":"compass swing","m":["swing"]},{"k":"correction card","m":["card"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-21","type":"recall","q":"What are the requirements of a direct reading compass?","src":"Oral bank p.88; FCOM 10.20.8","ref":"OB70","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"The DRC is the standby compass in transports (the MAX has a liquid-damped one with a correction card, used to validate heading). Requirements:\n- **Horizontality:** pendulous suspension below the pivot.\n- **Sensitivity:** strong magnets; liquid reduces pivot friction.\n- **Aperiodicity:** damping liquid, damping wires and short magnets so it settles quickly without oscillating.","keys":[{"k":"horizontality","m":["horizontal"]},{"k":"sensitivity","m":["sensitiv"]},{"k":"aperiodicity","m":["aperiodic","dead beat","deadbeat"]},{"k":"pendulous","m":["pendulous","below the pivot"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-22","type":"recall","q":"Explain the acceleration and turning errors of the direct reading compass.","src":"Oral bank p.88; Standard theory","ref":"OB70","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Both come from **dip**: the magnet system's CG is displaced from the pivot, so accelerations and turns tilt it and the **vertical field component** deflects it.\n- **Acceleration error:** maximum on **east and west** headings, zero on north and south. Northern hemisphere **ANDS**: accelerate north, decelerate south.\n- **Turning error:** maximum turning through **north and south**. Northern hemisphere **UNOS**: undershoot north, overshoot south. Reversed in the southern hemisphere.\n- Both greatest near the magnetic poles, zero at the magnetic equator.","keys":[{"k":"caused by dip","m":["dip","vertical component"]},{"k":"acceleration max E/W","m":["east and west","east west","easterly","e w"]},{"k":"ANDS","m":["ands","accelerate north"]},{"k":"UNOS","m":["unos","undershoot north"]},{"k":"reversed in southern hemisphere","m":["southern","reversed"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-20","type":"mcq","q":"On which headings is DRC acceleration error maximum?","src":"Oral bank p.88","ref":"OB70","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["East and west","North and south","All headings equally","Only north"],"a":0,"why":"Acceleration error is maximum on E and W, zero on N and S. Turning error is maximum through N and S.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-21","type":"mcq","q":"Northern hemisphere, turning onto north with a DRC, you should:","src":"Oral bank p.88","ref":"OB70","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Undershoot (roll out early)","Overshoot (roll out late)","Roll out on the reading","Roll out 90° early"],"a":0,"why":"UNOS: undershoot north, overshoot south.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-22","type":"mcq","q":"Northern hemisphere, accelerating on an easterly heading, the DRC shows:","src":"Oral bank p.88","ref":"OB70","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["An apparent turn towards north","An apparent turn towards south","No error","Heading freezes"],"a":0,"why":"ANDS: accelerate north (apparent turn towards north), decelerate south.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-match-3","type":"match","q":"Match the DRC error to where it is maximum or its mnemonic.","src":"Oral bank p.88; Standard theory","ref":"OB70","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Acceleration error","Max on E/W headings; ANDS"],["Turning error","Max turning through N/S; UNOS"],["Both errors","Greatest near magnetic poles"],["Magnetic equator","Both errors zero"]],"why":"Errors come from dip, which is zero at the magnetic equator.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-23","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between an INS and an IRS?","src":"Oral bank p.60; FCOM 11.20.6, 11.20.7, 10.20.8; NR14","ref":"OB71","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **INS (platform):** mechanical gyros and accelerometers on a **gyro-stabilised platform**, kept level and north-aligned by gimbals and torque motors.\n- **IRS (strapdown):** **ring laser gyros** and accelerometers **fixed to the airframe**; the computer does the platform's job mathematically.\n- IRS has **no moving parts**, so is more reliable with a longer life, and aligns faster (737: **5 to 17 min**).\n- Drift is the same order for both, about 1 to 2 NM/h; RLG lock-in is cured by dither.\n- MAX: two ADIRUs, IRS combined with air data in one unit.","keys":[{"k":"platform / gimbals","m":["platform","gimbal","stabilised","stabilized"]},{"k":"strapdown, fixed to airframe","m":["strapdown","strap down","fixed to the airframe","body fixed"]},{"k":"ring laser gyros","m":["ring laser","rlg","laser"]},{"k":"no moving parts","m":["no moving parts","moving parts"]},{"k":"computer does platform's job","m":["computer","mathematic","software"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-24","type":"recall","q":"Bank question: what is the IRS aligning time at middle latitudes?","src":"FCOM 11.20.7; Oral bank p.60","ref":"OB71","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"On the 737 it is **5 to 17 minutes depending on latitude** (FCOM), not the bank's \"3 to 10 min\". Longer at higher latitudes because the horizontal earth-rate component is weaker, and impossible above 78°15'. A platform INS took longer, about 20 min at mid-latitudes.","keys":[{"k":"5 to 17 min","m":["5 to 17","17 min"]},{"k":"depends on latitude","m":["latitude"]},{"k":"not above 78 deg 15","m":["78 deg","78 15","7815","78°"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["3 to 10"],"why":"Bank value; your FCOM says 5 to 17 min depending on latitude"}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-23","type":"mcq","q":"A platform INS typically takes about how long to align at mid-latitudes (bank figure)?","src":"Oral bank p.60","ref":"OB71","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["About 20 min","About 5 min","30 s","About 60 min"],"a":0,"why":"Bank: about 20 min for a platform INS; the 737 IRS takes 5 to 17 min.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"INS or IRS on your aircraft? Explain.\"","src":"FCOM 11.20.6, 11.20.7; Standard theory","ref":"OB71; OB72; OB73","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"Which do you have and how many?","a":"IRS (strapdown), two ADIRUs, each with three RLGs and three accelerometers.","keys":[{"k":"IRS / strapdown","m":["irs","strapdown","strap down"]},{"k":"two ADIRUs","m":["two","2 adiru"]},{"k":"3 RLG + 3 accelerometers","m":["three","3 ring","3 laser","3 rlg"]}]},{"q":"How does the RLG sense rotation?","a":"Counter-rotating laser beams; Sagnac frequency difference.","keys":[{"k":"Sagnac / frequency difference","m":["sagnac","frequency"]}]},{"q":"What is lock-in and the cure?","a":"Beams lock together at low rotation rates; cured by mechanical dither.","keys":[{"k":"lock-in","m":["lock"]},{"k":"dither","m":["dither"]}]},{"q":"How long does alignment take and why does it vary?","a":"5 to 17 min; gyrocompassing uses the horizontal earth rate, 15°/h × cos latitude, weaker towards the poles.","keys":[{"k":"5 to 17","m":["5 to 17","17 min"]},{"k":"cos latitude","m":["cos"]},{"k":"earth rate","m":["earth rate","earths rotation","earth rotation"]}]},{"q":"What errors grow with time?","a":"Unbounded position error from gyro drift, about 1 to 2 NM/h; Schuler errors are bounded (84 min).","keys":[{"k":"drift","m":["drift"]},{"k":"unbounded","m":["unbounded","grows","accumulat"]},{"k":"Schuler 84 min","m":["schuler","84 min"]}]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-order-2","type":"order","q":"Order the 737 IRS full alignment sequence.","src":"FCOM 11.20.7","ref":"OB72","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["MSU from OFF to NAV","Short power-up test (ON DC light)","ALIGN light on, alignment begins","Present position from MMR GPS or entered on POS INIT","ALIGN light extinguishes, IRS in NAV"],"why":"OFF to NAV, ON DC test, ALIGN, position (GPS or POS INIT), aircraft stationary throughout, then NAV.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-25","type":"recall","q":"How do you align the 737 IRS?","src":"FCOM 11.20.7; FCOM L.10.9","ref":"OB72","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **MSU from OFF to NAV.** A short power-up test (**ON DC** light), then the **ALIGN** light comes on and alignment begins.\n- **Present position:** on hybrid GPS-inertial ADIRUs it comes from the **MMR GPS automatically**; otherwise enter it on **POS INIT** (or the ISDU).\n- The aircraft **must stay stationary**.\n- **5 to 17 min** depending on latitude; not possible above 78°15'.\n- Fast realignment: 30 s, with position entry to return to NAV.","keys":[{"k":"OFF to NAV","m":["off to nav","to nav","nav position"]},{"k":"ALIGN light","m":["align light"]},{"k":"position from GPS or POS INIT","m":["pos init","gps","mmr"]},{"k":"stationary","m":["stationary","not move"]},{"k":"5 to 17 min","m":["5 to 17","17 min"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-26","type":"recall","q":"Why does IRS alignment time depend on latitude?","src":"Standard theory; FCOM L.10.9","ref":"OB72","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Alignment **levels** (senses gravity) and **gyrocompasses**: it finds true north by sensing the **earth's rotation**. The **horizontal component of earth rate is 15°/h × cos latitude**, so it gets **weaker towards the poles**. Alignment therefore takes longer at high latitude, and becomes **impossible** near the poles (737: not above 78°15').","keys":[{"k":"gyrocompassing","m":["gyrocompass","gyro compass","true north"]},{"k":"earth rate","m":["earth rate","earths rotation","earth rotation","15 degrees per hour","15 deg"]},{"k":"cos latitude","m":["cos"]},{"k":"weaker at poles, impossible","m":["pole","impossible","78 deg","78 15","7815"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-24","type":"mcq","q":"The horizontal component of earth rate used for gyrocompassing is:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB72","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["15°/h × cos latitude","15°/h × sin latitude","15°/h × tan latitude","15°/min × cos latitude"],"a":0,"why":"Horizontal earth rate = 15°/h × cos lat, so weaker towards the poles.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-27","type":"recall","q":"What position input errors will the 737 IRS accept during alignment?","src":"FCOM 11.20.7; Standard theory","ref":"OB72","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- If the entered position is **not within 4 NM** of the origin airport: **VERIFY POSITION**.\n- The IRS runs two tests: entered position against the **last stored position**, and entered **latitude against its own computed latitude**. Failing either flashes ALIGN and shows ENTER IRS POSITION.\n- A position that disagrees with the stored one is **accepted if you enter the same position again**.\n- A **latitude error is not**: two consecutive failed entries light **FAULT**.\n- **Longitude cannot be self-checked** (gyrocompassing finds latitude, not longitude), so the crew cross-checks it.","keys":[{"k":"4 NM check","m":["4 nm","4nm","four nm","4 nautical"]},{"k":"last stored position test","m":["stored","last position"]},{"k":"latitude compared","m":["latitude"]},{"k":"re-entry accepted","m":["same position again","again","re enter","reenter"]},{"k":"longitude not checked","m":["longitude"]},{"k":"FAULT light","m":["fault"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-num-12","type":"num","q":"If the entered IRS position is not within how many NM of the origin airport do you get VERIFY POSITION?","src":"FCOM 11.20.7","ref":"OB72","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":4,"unit":"NM","why":"4 NM.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-25","type":"mcq","q":"Which IRS entry error cannot be detected by the IRS itself?","src":"FCOM 11.20.7; Standard theory","ref":"OB72","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Longitude error","Latitude error","Disagreement with last stored position","No position entered"],"a":0,"why":"Gyrocompassing finds latitude but not longitude, so the crew must cross-check longitude.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-chain-3","type":"chain","q":"You arrive at the aircraft and want to align the IRS. Talk me through it.","src":"FCOM 11.20.7; FCOM NP.21.1","ref":"OB72","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"How do you start?","a":"MSU OFF to NAV; ON DC test; ALIGN light on.","keys":[{"k":"OFF to NAV","m":["nav"]},{"k":"ALIGN light","m":["align"]}]},{"q":"Where does present position come from?","a":"MMR GPS automatically on hybrid ADIRUs; else POS INIT or ISDU.","keys":[{"k":"GPS / MMR","m":["gps","mmr"]},{"k":"POS INIT","m":["pos init","isdu"]}]},{"q":"What if you enter a position 6 NM from the airport?","a":"VERIFY POSITION (not within 4 NM); stored position test fails, re-enter same position and it is accepted.","keys":[{"k":"4 NM","m":["4 nm","4nm","four"]},{"k":"re-enter","m":["again","re enter","reenter"]}]},{"q":"And a wrong latitude twice?","a":"Fails the latitude test twice: FAULT light.","keys":[{"k":"FAULT","m":["fault"]}]},{"q":"Ground stop of 20 minutes, IRS still on: options?","a":"Fast realignment, 30 s, enter present position; full alignment recommended before each flight.","keys":[{"k":"fast realignment 30 s","m":["30 s","30s","30 sec","fast"]},{"k":"full before each flight","m":["each flight","full alignment"]}]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-28","type":"recall","q":"What are the inputs and outputs of an INS?","src":"Oral bank p.62; FCOM 10.20.8","ref":"OB73","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Inputs:** **initial present position** (lat and long) during alignment; in flight, **TAS from the air data computer** for wind computation. On the MAX the ADIRU also takes air data module inputs and the alpha vanes.\n- **Principle:** accelerations integrated once for velocity, again for distance.\n- **Outputs:** **position, ground speed, track, drift, wind**, great-circle track and distance, cross-track error, time and distance to next waypoint, plus **attitude and heading**. On the 737 also the flight path vector, inertial vertical speed and the map.","keys":[{"k":"present position input","m":["present position","initial position","lat and long","latitude and longitude"]},{"k":"TAS input for wind","m":["tas","true airspeed"]},{"k":"position, GS, track","m":["ground speed","groundspeed","track"]},{"k":"wind / drift","m":["wind","drift"]},{"k":"attitude and heading","m":["attitude","heading"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-29","type":"recall","q":"What are bounded and unbounded INS errors?","src":"Standard theory; OM-A Ch 40; FCOM 11.31.4","ref":"OB73","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Bounded errors** oscillate and **do not grow**: the **Schuler loop**, period about **84 minutes**, e.g. velocity errors from initial levelling.\n- **Unbounded errors** **accumulate with time**: mainly position error from **gyro drift**, growing roughly linearly with time.\n- Hence IRS-only time limits for RNAV 10 and RNAV 5, and the FMC updating IRS position from **GPS and radio aids**.","keys":[{"k":"bounded: oscillate","m":["oscillat","bounded"]},{"k":"Schuler 84 min","m":["schuler","84 min","84 minutes"]},{"k":"unbounded: grows with time","m":["unbounded","grow","accumulat","increase with time"]},{"k":"gyro drift","m":["drift"]},{"k":"GPS/radio updating","m":["gps","radio","dme"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-num-13","type":"num","q":"Schuler period (minutes)?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB73","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":84,"unit":"min","why":"About 84 minutes; Schuler errors are bounded oscillations.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-30","type":"recall","q":"What are the IRS mode selector positions and lights on the 737?","src":"FCOM 11.10, IRS Mode Selector Unit","ref":"OB74","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **OFF:** alignment lost, power removed after 30 s.\n- **ALIGN.**\n- **NAV:** all IRS information.\n- **ATT:** attitude and heading only. Attitude after the ALIGN light goes out; heading only after a **magnetic heading is entered**; **no position or ground speed** until aligned on the ground.\n- **Lights:** ALIGN (steady aligning; flashing = failed position test or no position), FAULT (amber), ON DC, DC FAIL.","keys":[{"k":"OFF ALIGN NAV ATT","m":["att","align"]},{"k":"ATT: attitude + heading only","m":["attitude"]},{"k":"heading must be entered","m":["enter","magnetic heading"]},{"k":"no position in ATT","m":["no position","no ground speed","not position"]},{"k":"lights ALIGN FAULT ON DC","m":["fault","on dc","dc fail"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-match-4","type":"match","q":"Match the IRS mode selector position to what it gives.","src":"FCOM 11.10, IRS Mode Selector Unit","ref":"OB74","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["OFF","Alignment lost, power removed after 30 s"],["ALIGN","Alignment"],["NAV","All IRS information"],["ATT","Attitude, and heading once entered; no position"]],"why":"ATT gives attitude and (entered) heading only.","topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-31","type":"recall","q":"Can you switch an IRS off in flight and back on? GPS or IRS, which would you prefer?","src":"FCOM 11.20.12; FCOM 11.31.4; Oral bank p.61; NR48","ref":"OB74","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- If an IRS fails or is switched off, it can come back in **ATT mode** for attitude, with **periodic heading entries**.\n- On ADIRUs connected to the MMRs, **Align In Motion** restores NAV: hold **straight and level about 30 s**, full navigation in about **10 to 30 min**, **only with GPS**.\n- **GPS or IRS?** IRS is better short-term (smooth, no outages); GPS long-term (no drift). On the MAX you don't choose: the FMC uses IRS updated by GPS. Forced to choose one for a long flight: **GPS**, because IRS error grows with time.","keys":[{"k":"ATT mode","m":["att"]},{"k":"heading entries","m":["heading"]},{"k":"Align In Motion","m":["align in motion","aim"]},{"k":"needs GPS","m":["gps"]},{"k":"IRS short-term, GPS long-term","m":["short term","long term","drift"]}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-recall-32","type":"recall","q":"Bank question: an INS switched off in flight can only be used for attitude reference. True?","src":"FCOM 11.20.12","ref":"OB74","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"**True for a classic INS, not for your ADIRUs.** On 737 ADIRUs connected to the MMRs, **Align In Motion** restores **NAV**, using **GPS**: hold straight and level about 30 s, full navigation in about 10 to 30 min. Without that, ATT mode gives attitude and heading (with heading entries) only.","keys":[{"k":"Align In Motion","m":["align in motion"]},{"k":"restores NAV","m":["nav"]},{"k":"needs GPS/MMR","m":["gps","mmr"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["only attitude","only for attitude","only att"],"why":"True only for a classic INS; MMR-connected ADIRUs have Align In Motion"}],"topic":14},{"id":"t14-mcq-26","type":"mcq","q":"Align In Motion on MMR-connected ADIRUs: what is needed and how long to full NAV?","src":"FCOM 11.20.12","ref":"OB74","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Straight and level ~30 s, GPS; full NAV in about 10 to 30 min","Wings level 5 min; full NAV in 30 s","Any manoeuvre; no GPS needed; 17 min","Not possible in flight"],"a":0,"why":"Straight and level about 30 s, full navigation in about 10 to 30 min, only with GPS.","topic":14},{"id":"t15-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"What are the basic GPWS modes?","src":"FCOM 15.20.14; Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: GPWS / EGPWS","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Seven basic modes (alert names from the FCOM, numbering standard):\n1. **Excessive descent rate:** SINK RATE, PULL UP.\n2. **Excessive terrain closure rate:** TERRAIN TERRAIN, PULL UP.\n3. **Altitude loss after take-off:** DON'T SINK.\n4. **Unsafe terrain clearance:** TOO LOW GEAR / FLAPS / TERRAIN.\n5. **Below glideslope:** GLIDESLOPE.\n6. **Callouts and bank angle.**\n7. **Reactive windshear.**","keys":[{"k":"1 sink rate","m":["sink rate","descent rate"]},{"k":"2 terrain closure","m":["closure","terrain terrain"]},{"k":"3 don't sink / alt loss","m":["dont sink","don t sink","altitude loss"]},{"k":"4 too low gear/flaps","m":["too low","terrain clearance"]},{"k":"5 glideslope","m":["glideslope","glide slope"]},{"k":"6 callouts/bank angle","m":["callout","bank angle"]},{"k":"7 windshear","m":["windshear","wind shear"]}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the GPWS mode to its aural.","src":"FCOM 15.20.14; FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"Key facts: GPWS / EGPWS; OB75","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Mode 1 (excessive descent rate)","SINK RATE, PULL UP"],["Mode 2 (terrain closure)","TERRAIN TERRAIN, PULL UP"],["Mode 3 (altitude loss after take-off)","DON'T SINK"],["Mode 4 (unsafe terrain clearance)","TOO LOW GEAR / FLAPS / TERRAIN"],["Mode 5 (below glideslope)","GLIDESLOPE"],["Mode 7 (reactive windshear)","WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR"]],"why":"Mode numbers standard theory; alert names FCOM.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"Which GPWS mode gives DON'T SINK?","src":"FCOM 15.20.14","ref":"Key facts: GPWS / EGPWS; OB75","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Mode 3","Mode 1","Mode 2","Mode 4"],"a":0,"why":"Mode 3: altitude loss after take-off or go-around.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"Which GPWS mode gives the reactive WINDSHEAR warning?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25; Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: GPWS / EGPWS; OB75","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Mode 7","Mode 5","Mode 6","Mode 2"],"a":0,"why":"Mode 7 is reactive windshear.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"TOO LOW FLAPS is a mode 4 alert given at:","src":"FCOM 15.20.14","ref":"OB75","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Low airspeed","High airspeed","Any speed above 1000 ft","Only on go-around"],"a":0,"why":"Mode 4: TOO LOW GEAR, TOO LOW FLAPS (low airspeed), TOO LOW TERRAIN (high airspeed).","topic":15},{"id":"t15-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"What are the enhanced functions of EGPWS?","src":"OM-A 30.5","ref":"Key facts: GPWS / EGPWS","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"EGPWS adds a forward look using a terrain, obstacle and runway database:\n- **TAD / FLTA:** terrain on the ND and look-ahead alerts.\n- **Terrain Clearance Floor (TCF):** warns of descending too early on any approach.\n- **Runway Field Clearance Floor (RFCF):** for runways higher than their surroundings, e.g. **Mangalore and Calicut**.\n- **Geometric altitude:** GPS-based, removes temperature, pressure and altimeter-setting errors.","keys":[{"k":"TAD/FLTA","m":["tad","flta","look ahead","lookahead","terrain awareness display"]},{"k":"TCF","m":["tcf","terrain clearance floor"]},{"k":"RFCF","m":["rfcf","runway field clearance"]},{"k":"geometric altitude","m":["geometric"]}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"Runway Field Clearance Floor is specifically useful at which Indian airports?","src":"OM-A 30.5","ref":"Key facts: GPWS / EGPWS","prio":2,"o":["Mangalore and Calicut","Mumbai and Delhi","Leh and Srinagar","Chennai and Kolkata"],"a":0,"why":"RFCF protects runways higher than their surroundings, e.g. Mangalore and Calicut.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"What are the limitations of EGPWS, and what are the operating rules?","src":"OM-A 30.1, 30.6, 30.8; CAR Sec 2 Series I Part VII","ref":"Key facts: GPWS / EGPWS; NR1","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Limitations:**\n- **Database errors.**\n- A **corrupted FMC position** (map shift, spoofing) gives false or missed alerts.\n- With no terrain data or degraded navigation, protection is reduced.\n- The terrain display is **not for navigation**.\n**Rules:**\n- EGPWS **on from take-off to landing**.\n- **Report** EGPWS-driven path changes, copy to **DGCA within 24 h**.\n- CAR Sec 2 Series I Part VII.","keys":[{"k":"database errors","m":["database"]},{"k":"corrupted position","m":["position","map shift","spoof"]},{"k":"not for navigation","m":["not for navigation","awareness only","never navigate"]},{"k":"on take-off to landing","m":["take off to landing","takeoff to landing"]},{"k":"report DGCA 24 h","m":["24 h","24 hours","24h","dgca"]}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-num-1","type":"num","q":"Within how many hours must an EGPWS-driven path change report be copied to DGCA?","src":"OM-A 30.8","ref":"Key facts: GPWS / EGPWS","prio":2,"ans":24,"unit":"h","why":"Within 24 h.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between GPWS and EGPWS?","src":"OM-A 30.5","ref":"NR1","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**GPWS looks only downwards.** It uses the **radio altimeter**, barometric vertical speed, ILS glideslope deviation, and gear and flap position (modes 1 to 7). A steep cliff ahead gives almost no warning, because the RA sees only the ground below.\n**EGPWS adds a forward look:** it compares aircraft **position (FMC blended GPS/IRS/DME)** and altitude with a worldwide **terrain, obstacle and runway database**. It adds **TAD/FLTA**, **TCF**, **RFCF** and **geometric altitude**.","keys":[{"k":"GPWS: radio altimeter, looks down","m":["radio altim","rad alt","radalt","below","down"]},{"k":"GPWS inputs: baro VS, G/S, gear/flaps","m":["glideslope","glide slope","vertical speed","gear"]},{"k":"EGPWS: forward looking","m":["forward","ahead","look ahead","lookahead"]},{"k":"terrain database","m":["database"]},{"k":"position from FMC/GPS","m":["gps","fmc","position"]}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"What warnings does EGPWS give, and with what timing?","src":"FCOM 15.20.12","ref":"NR1","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Look-ahead caution:** \"CAUTION TERRAIN\" (or \"CAUTION OBSTACLE\"), **40 to 60 s** before projected impact, solid amber on the ND.\n- **Look-ahead warning:** \"TERRAIN TERRAIN, PULL UP\", **20 to 30 s**, solid red on the ND.\n- **TCF:** \"TOO LOW TERRAIN\", descending below an unsafe altitude too far from any database airport.\n- Plus the basic modes: SINK RATE, PULL UP, TERRAIN, DON'T SINK, TOO LOW GEAR/FLAPS, GLIDESLOPE, bank angle and callouts, WINDSHEAR.\n- Based on position, baro altitude, vertical flight path and ground speed.","keys":[{"k":"CAUTION TERRAIN","m":["caution terrain"]},{"k":"40 to 60 s","m":["40 to 60","60 s","60 sec"]},{"k":"TERRAIN TERRAIN PULL UP","m":["pull up"]},{"k":"20 to 30 s","m":["20 to 30","30 s","30 sec"]},{"k":"TOO LOW TERRAIN","m":["too low terrain"]}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"EGPWS look-ahead CAUTION TERRAIN is given about how long before projected impact?","src":"FCOM 15.20.12","ref":"NR1","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["40 to 60 s","20 to 30 s","60 to 90 s","10 to 15 s"],"a":0,"why":"Caution 40 to 60 s (solid amber); warning TERRAIN TERRAIN PULL UP 20 to 30 s (solid red).","topic":15},{"id":"t15-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"EGPWS look-ahead warning TERRAIN TERRAIN, PULL UP is given about how long before projected impact?","src":"FCOM 15.20.12","ref":"NR1","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["20 to 30 s","40 to 60 s","5 to 10 s","60 to 90 s"],"a":0,"why":"Warning at 20 to 30 s, solid red on the ND.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"TOO LOW TERRAIN (look-ahead) is given when:","src":"FCOM 15.20.12","ref":"NR1","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Descending below an unsafe altitude while too far from any airport in the database","Terrain closure rate is excessive","Gear is up below 500 ft","Below glideslope"],"a":0,"why":"This is the Terrain Clearance Floor style alert.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"What does the TERR INHIBIT switch do, and what inhibits look-ahead alerts automatically?","src":"FCOM 15.20.12; FCOM 15.10.12","ref":"NR1","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **TERR INHIBIT** inhibits all three look-ahead alerts: CAUTION TERRAIN, TERRAIN TERRAIN PULL UP and TOO LOW TERRAIN.\n- An **actual windshear warning inhibits look-ahead and radio-altitude alerts**.\n- With **no terrain data**, look-ahead alerting and the display are lost, but **radio-altitude-based alerts work normally**.\n- The database is correlated to **GPS position**, so a GPS error moves the terrain picture.","keys":[{"k":"inhibits look-ahead alerts","m":["look ahead","lookahead","caution terrain"]},{"k":"windshear inhibits","m":["windshear","wind shear"]},{"k":"basic RA modes still work","m":["radio altitude","radio altimeter","basic modes","still work"]},{"k":"GPS position error moves picture","m":["gps"]}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"Describe the EGPWS terrain colours on the ND.","src":"FCOM 15.10.12, Terrain Display","ref":"NR1","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**More than 500 ft above the highest terrain in range:** everything **green**, shaded by elevation (solid, high-density dotted, low-density dotted), black for no significant terrain.\n**Less than 500 ft above the highest terrain:** colour relative to your altitude:\n- **dotted red:** more than **2000 ft above** you;\n- **dotted amber:** **500 ft below** (250 ft gear down) to 2000 ft above;\n- **dotted green:** 2000 ft below to 500 ft below;\n- **solid amber:** look-ahead caution; **solid red:** look-ahead warning;\n- **dotted magenta:** no terrain data.\nNot shown: terrain more than 2000 ft below, or within 200 ft of the nearest runway elevation.","keys":[{"k":"red > 2000 ft above","m":["2000 ft above","2000 above","2000ft above","2000 feet above"]},{"k":"amber 500 below to 2000 above","m":["500 ft below","500 below","500ft below","500 feet below"]},{"k":"solid amber/red = caution/warning","m":["solid"]},{"k":"magenta = no data","m":["magenta"]},{"k":"green peaks mode when >500 ft clear","m":["green"]}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match the ND terrain colour (when within 500 ft of the highest terrain) to its meaning.","src":"FCOM 15.10.12, Terrain Display","ref":"NR1","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Dotted red","Terrain more than 2000 ft above you"],["Dotted amber","500 ft below (250 ft gear down) to 2000 ft above"],["Dotted green","2000 ft below to 500 ft below"],["Solid amber","Look-ahead caution active"],["Solid red","Look-ahead warning active"],["Dotted magenta","No terrain data"]],"why":"Colours show terrain relative to aircraft altitude.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw the EGPWS ND terrain colour bands relative to your altitude.","src":"FCOM 15.10.12, Terrain Display","ref":"NR1","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"A vertical scale centred on the aircraft altitude:\n- Above +2000 ft: dotted red.\n- From −500 ft (−250 ft gear down) to +2000 ft: dotted amber.\n- From −2000 ft to −500 ft: dotted green.\n- Below −2000 ft: not shown (black).\nPlus solid amber = caution, solid red = warning, dotted magenta = no data.","checklist":["Aircraft altitude reference line","Dotted red above +2000 ft","Dotted amber from −500 ft to +2000 ft","250 ft lower amber limit with gear down","Dotted green from −2000 ft to −500 ft","Nothing shown below −2000 ft","Solid amber/red and magenta noted"],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-num-2","type":"num","q":"Dotted red terrain on the ND means terrain more than how many feet above the aircraft?","src":"FCOM 15.10.12","ref":"NR1","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2000,"unit":"ft","why":"Dotted red: more than 2000 ft above you.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-num-3","type":"num","q":"With gear down, the lower limit of dotted amber terrain is how many feet below the aircraft?","src":"FCOM 15.10.12","ref":"NR1","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":250,"unit":"ft","why":"500 ft below normally, 250 ft with gear down.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-num-4","type":"num","q":"Terrain within how many feet of the nearest runway elevation is not displayed?","src":"FCOM 15.10.12","ref":"NR1","prio":3,"ans":200,"unit":"ft","why":"Terrain within 200 ft of the nearest runway elevation is not shown.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"Your aircraft has EGPWS. How is it better than GPWS?\"","src":"OM-A 30.5, 30.6; FCOM 15.20.12; FCOM 15.10.12","ref":"NR1","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What does GPWS use?","a":"Radio altimeter, baro vertical speed, glideslope deviation, gear and flap position: it looks only down.","keys":[{"k":"radio altimeter","m":["radio alt","rad alt","radalt"]},{"k":"looks down only","m":["down","below"]}]},{"q":"What does EGPWS add?","a":"Forward look: position and altitude against a terrain/obstacle/runway database; TAD/FLTA, TCF, RFCF, geometric altitude.","keys":[{"k":"database","m":["database"]},{"k":"look ahead","m":["look ahead","lookahead","forward"]},{"k":"TCF","m":["tcf","clearance floor"]}]},{"q":"Timings of the look-ahead alerts?","a":"CAUTION TERRAIN 40 to 60 s; TERRAIN TERRAIN PULL UP 20 to 30 s.","keys":[{"k":"40 to 60","m":["40 to 60"]},{"k":"20 to 30","m":["20 to 30"]}]},{"q":"Your GPS position is spoofed. Effect?","a":"Terrain picture moves with the position error: false or missed alerts. Display is awareness only.","keys":[{"k":"false or missed alerts","m":["false","missed"]},{"k":"not for navigation","m":["not for navigation","awareness"]}]},{"q":"What still protects you if the terrain data is lost?","a":"Radio-altitude-based basic modes still work normally.","keys":[{"k":"basic modes / RA based","m":["radio alt","basic mode","rad alt"]}]}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"What is TAWS? What is FANS?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR62","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **TAWS (Terrain Awareness and Warning System):** the generic ICAO/FAA term for a GPWS with a **forward-looking terrain function**.\n  - **Class A** (airliners): all GPWS modes plus forward-looking terrain alerts, premature descent alerting and a terrain display.\n  - **EGPWS is Honeywell's TAWS.**\n- **FANS (Future Air Navigation System):** **CPDLC + ADS-C + RNP**, over satellite, VHF or HF data link.","keys":[{"k":"generic term, forward looking","m":["forward","generic"]},{"k":"Class A","m":["class a"]},{"k":"EGPWS is Honeywell's TAWS","m":["honeywell","egpws"]},{"k":"FANS = CPDLC + ADS-C","m":["cpdlc","ads c","adsc"]}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"TAWS Class A (airliners) includes:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR62","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["All GPWS modes, forward-looking alerts, premature descent alerting and a terrain display","Forward-looking alerts only, no display","Modes 1 to 3 only","Terrain display only"],"a":0,"why":"Class A: all GPWS modes plus FLTA, PDA and a display.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"Give the GPWS modes with the alerts for each, as on your FCOM.","src":"FCOM 15.20.14, 15.20.25, 15.20.12","ref":"OB75","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Mode 1** excessive descent rate: SINK RATE, then PULL UP.\n- **Mode 2** terrain closure: TERRAIN, then PULL UP if not in landing configuration.\n- **Mode 3** altitude loss after take-off or go-around: DON'T SINK.\n- **Mode 4** unsafe terrain clearance not in landing configuration: TOO LOW GEAR, TOO LOW FLAPS (low speed), TOO LOW TERRAIN (high speed); gear and flap alerts have inhibit switches.\n- **Mode 5** below glideslope: GLIDESLOPE, louder and faster with deviation; **BELOW G/S P-INHIBIT** cancels it below 1000 ft RA.\n- **Mode 6** callouts and bank angle. **Mode 7** WINDSHEAR, enabled below 1500 ft RA.\n- Plus look-ahead alerts and obstacle alerts for obstacles of **100 ft or more**.","keys":[{"k":"sink rate","m":["sink rate"]},{"k":"don't sink","m":["dont sink","don t sink"]},{"k":"too low gear/flaps","m":["too low"]},{"k":"glideslope + P-inhibit","m":["p inhibit","inhibit","glideslope"]},{"k":"windshear below 1500 ft","m":["1500"]}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-num-5","type":"num","q":"Below what radio altitude can BELOW G/S P-INHIBIT cancel the GLIDESLOPE alert (ft)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.14","ref":"OB75","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":1000,"unit":"ft","why":"Below 1000 ft RA.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-num-6","type":"num","q":"GPWS mode 7 reactive windshear is enabled below what radio altitude (ft)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"OB75","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1500,"unit":"ft","why":"Below 1500 ft RA, detection starting at rotation.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-num-7","type":"num","q":"EGPWS obstacle alerts are given for obstacles of at least what height (ft)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.12","ref":"OB75","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":100,"unit":"ft","why":"Obstacles of 100 ft or more.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Mode 1 envelope (bank figure, generic): excessive descent rate is active between:","src":"Oral bank p.68","ref":"OB75","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["50 and 2450 ft RA","50 and 1800 ft RA","220 and 790 ft RA","50 and 700 ft RA"],"a":0,"why":"Bank: mode 1 50 to 2450 ft RA. The FCOM gives no envelope heights.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"Mode 2A (not landing configuration) envelope per the bank:","src":"Oral bank p.68","ref":"OB75","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["50 to 1800 ft RA","220 to 790 ft RA","50 to 2450 ft RA","50 to 700 ft RA"],"a":0,"why":"Bank: 2A 50 to 1800 ft; 2B (landing configuration) 220 to 790 ft. Not in the FCOM.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"Give the GPWS mode envelope heights. Where do they come from?","src":"Oral bank p.68, p.96, p.97; FCOM 15.20.14","ref":"OB75","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"a":"**The FCOM gives the alerts but no envelope heights**, so any heights are the bank's generic figures: mode 1 **50 to 2450 ft**, mode 2A 50 to 1800 ft, 2B 220 to 790 ft, mode 3 **50 to 700 ft**.\nThe bank contradicts itself: mode 3 \"700\" vs \"790 ft\", mode 4B \"500\" vs \"200 ft\", mode 5 \"100 to 500\" vs \"50 to 1000 ft\". Pick one and **say it is the generic envelope**.","keys":[{"k":"FCOM gives no envelope","m":["no envelope","not in the fcom","fcom gives no","fcom doesnt","fcom does not"]},{"k":"generic / bank figures","m":["generic","bank"]},{"k":"mode 1 50 to 2450","m":["2450"]},{"k":"mode 3 50 to 700","m":["700 ft","700"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["790"],"why":"Bank p.96 gives 790 ft for mode 3, contradicting its own p.68 (700 ft); say which source you use"}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"Bank question: GPWS works on which principle, primary radar?","src":"NR1; OM-A 30.5","ref":"OB75","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"**No.** GPWS is a **computer** using **radio altitude, barometric vertical speed, glideslope deviation, and gear and flap position** (and for EGPWS a **terrain database** with position). **Only its radio altimeter input is a radar.** So the honest answer is that GPWS is a warning computer, not a radar system.","keys":[{"k":"computer, not radar","m":["computer","not radar","not primary"]},{"k":"radio altitude input","m":["radio alt","rad alt","radalt"]},{"k":"other inputs","m":["vertical speed","glideslope","gear","flap"]},{"k":"only RA is a radar","m":["only"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["primary radar"],"why":"Bank answer; only the radio altimeter input is a radar"}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"What is EGPWS mode 6?","src":"FCOM 15.20.30, 15.20.35, 15.20.36; Oral bank p.96","ref":"OB76","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Mode 6 = advisory callouts**: radio altitude callouts, minimums callouts and the bank angle alert. **No warning level, no PULL UP.**\n- **Altitude callouts** set per aircraft, e.g. 2500, 1000, 500, then 400, 300, 200, 100, 50, 40, 30, 20, 10. With **Smart 500**, FIVE HUNDRED only on a non-ILS/GLS approach or when more than 2 dots off LOC or G/S.\n- **Minimums:** from the Captain's selector: APPROACHING MINIMUMS at **DH/MDA + 80 ft** (some aircraft), MINIMUMS at DH/MDA.\n- **BANK ANGLE, BANK ANGLE** (6B).","keys":[{"k":"advisory callouts","m":["callout","advisory"]},{"k":"minimums","m":["minimums"]},{"k":"bank angle","m":["bank angle"]},{"k":"no warning / no pull up","m":["no warning","no pull up","advisor"]}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-num-8","type":"num","q":"APPROACHING MINIMUMS is called at DH/MDA plus how many feet (on aircraft that have it)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.36","ref":"OB76","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":80,"unit":"ft","why":"DH/MDA + 80 ft.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"With Smart 500, the FIVE HUNDRED callout is given:","src":"FCOM 15.20.36","ref":"OB76","prio":3,"o":["Only on non-ILS/GLS approaches or when more than 2 dots off LOC or G/S","On every approach","Only on ILS approaches","Only below MDA"],"a":0,"why":"Smart 500 suppresses it on a stable ILS/GLS approach.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"Give the bank angle alert thresholds.","src":"FCOM 15.20.30","ref":"OB76","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"\"BANK ANGLE, BANK ANGLE\":\n- **5 to 30 ft AGL:** more than **10°**.\n- **30 to 130 ft:** threshold rises linearly from **10° to 35°**.\n- **Above 130 ft:** more than **35°, 40° or 45°**.\n- Then silent until bank is reduced to **30° or less**.","keys":[{"k":"10 deg low","m":["10 degrees","10°","10 deg","ten degrees"]},{"k":"35 deg at 130 ft","m":["35 deg","35°","35 degrees","thirty five"]},{"k":"130 ft","m":["130 ft","130 feet","130"]},{"k":"reset at 30 deg","m":["30 degrees or less","30°","30 deg","thirty degrees","30 or less"]}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"Bank question: at 150 ft RADALT the bank angle alert is at 40°. Correct?","src":"FCOM 15.20.30","ref":"OB76","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"a":"**Not as per the FCOM.** The threshold rises linearly from **10° at 30 ft to 35° at 130 ft**; **above 130 ft** it is **35°, 40° or 45°** (aircraft-dependent). So at 150 ft it is not a fixed 40°.","keys":[{"k":"35 at 130 ft","m":["130"]},{"k":"10 to 35 linear","m":["linear","10 to 35","10° to 35°"]},{"k":"35, 40 or 45 above","m":["45 deg","45°","45 degrees","40 or 45"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["150 ft"],"why":"Bank figure; FCOM threshold reaches 35° at 130 ft"}],"topic":15},{"id":"t15-num-9","type":"num","q":"Bank angle alert: the threshold reaches 35° at what radio height (ft)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.30","ref":"OB76","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":130,"unit":"ft","why":"10° at 30 ft rising linearly to 35° at 130 ft.","topic":15},{"id":"t15-num-10","type":"num","q":"Between 5 and 30 ft AGL, the BANK ANGLE alert triggers above how many degrees?","src":"FCOM 15.20.30","ref":"OB76","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":10,"unit":"°","why":"More than 10°.","topic":15},{"id":"t16-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"ACAS and TCAS: what are they, and what does TCAS need from the other aircraft?","src":"OM-A 31; Standard theory; FCOM 15.20.37","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS; NR60","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **ACAS** is the **ICAO** term (the concept); **TCAS** is the **FAA** term (the equipment; TCAS II meets ACAS II).\n- TCAS needs the intruder's **transponder**: **Mode C or S** for altitude. No working transponder means the intruder is invisible.\n- **RA coordination** happens only if **both aircraft have Mode S** (and TCAS).\n- TCAS II **interrogates on 1030 MHz** and **listens on 1090 MHz**.","keys":[{"k":"ACAS ICAO / TCAS FAA","m":["icao","faa"]},{"k":"needs transponder","m":["transponder"]},{"k":"Mode C or S for altitude","m":["mode c","mode s"]},{"k":"coordination via Mode S","m":["coordinat"]},{"k":"1030 / 1090 MHz","m":["1030","1090"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"TCAS II interrogates and listens on which frequencies?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Interrogates 1030 MHz, listens 1090 MHz","Interrogates 1090 MHz, listens 1030 MHz","Both on 1090 MHz","Interrogates 978 MHz, listens 1090 MHz"],"a":0,"why":"Same pair as SSR: 1030 up, 1090 down.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"How do you respond to a TA and an RA? Give the phraseology.","src":"OM-A 31","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **TA:** **no manoeuvre**; look for the traffic and prepare.\n- **RA:** follow it promptly with **minimum deviation**, **notify ATC**, **return promptly** to the clearance when clear. Following an RA is **not a violation**; follow it even against an ATC instruction.\n- **Phraseology:** \"**TCAS RA**\"; \"**CLEAR OF CONFLICT, RETURNING TO** (clearance)\"; \"**UNABLE, TCAS RA**\" when given a conflicting instruction.\n- **Written report within 24 h** in India.","keys":[{"k":"no manoeuvre on TA","m":["no manoeuvre","no maneuver","not manoeuvre","dont manoeuvre","don t manoeuvre"]},{"k":"minimum deviation","m":["minimum deviation","minimum"]},{"k":"TCAS RA call","m":["tcas ra"]},{"k":"clear of conflict returning","m":["clear of conflict"]},{"k":"unable TCAS RA","m":["unable"]},{"k":"report 24 h","m":["24 h","24 hours","24h"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-order-1","type":"order","q":"Order the actions for a TCAS RA on the 737.","src":"QRH MAN.1.9, MAN.1.10; OM-A 31","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["Disengage autopilot and autothrottle","Smoothly adjust pitch and thrust to satisfy the RA (minimum deviation, follow planned lateral path)","Notify ATC: \"TCAS RA\"","On CLEAR OF CONFLICT, return promptly to the clearance: \"CLEAR OF CONFLICT, RETURNING TO ...\"","Written report within 24 h"],"why":"QRH: disengage A/P and A/T, fly the RA; tell ATC; return promptly; report.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"What are the reporting and regulatory requirements for TCAS in India?","src":"OM-A 31; OM-A 41.2","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","prio":2,"a":"- **Written report within 24 h** after an RA.\n- The **flight plan must show ACAS**.\n- Regulation: **CAR Section 2 Series R Part IV**.\n- TCAS II (ACAS II) is required in India and for RVSM.","keys":[{"k":"24 h report","m":["24 h","24 hours","24h"]},{"k":"flight plan shows ACAS","m":["flight plan"]},{"k":"CAR Sec 2 Series R Part IV","m":["series r","part iv","part 4"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"Describe the TCAS symbols on the ND.","src":"FCOM 10.40.25; FCOM 15.20.37, 15.20.38; Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS; NR17","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Other traffic:** hollow **white** diamond.\n- **Proximate traffic** (within **6 NM and 1200 ft**, not expected to cause a TA or RA): filled **white** diamond.\n- **TA:** filled **amber circle**.\n- **RA:** filled **red square**.\n- Relative altitude in hundreds of feet; an **arrow** when the target climbs or descends at **500 fpm or more**.\n- **OFFSCALE** when a TA or RA is beyond the selected range.","keys":[{"k":"other: hollow diamond","m":["hollow"]},{"k":"proximate: filled diamond, 6 NM 1200 ft","m":["proximate","1200"]},{"k":"TA amber circle","m":["amber circle","yellow circle","circle"]},{"k":"RA red square","m":["red square","square"]},{"k":"arrow 500 fpm","m":["500 fpm","500 feet per minute","500 ft per min","arrow"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the TCAS symbol to its meaning.","src":"FCOM 10.40.25; FCOM 15.20.38","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Hollow white diamond","Other traffic"],["Filled white diamond","Proximate traffic (within 6 NM and 1200 ft)"],["Amber circle","Traffic Advisory"],["Red square","Resolution Advisory"]],"why":"Shapes standard theory, colours FCOM.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw the four TCAS ND symbols with an example data tag.","src":"FCOM 10.40.25; FCOM 15.20.37","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Hollow white diamond (other), filled white diamond (proximate), filled amber circle (TA), filled red square (RA), each with relative altitude in hundreds of feet (+ above, − below) and a vertical arrow when climbing or descending at 500 fpm or more.","checklist":["Hollow white diamond: other traffic","Filled white diamond: proximate","Amber circle: TA","Red square: RA","Relative altitude tag (+/− hundreds of ft) above or below symbol","Arrow for ≥500 fpm vertical speed"],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"Proximate traffic is shown for targets within:","src":"FCOM 15.20.38","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["6 NM and 1200 ft","6 NM and 2700 ft","3.3 NM and 850 ft","14 NM and 1200 ft"],"a":0,"why":"Within 6 NM and 1200 ft, not expected to cause a TA or RA: filled white diamond.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-num-1","type":"num","q":"A TCAS target shows a vertical arrow when climbing or descending at what rate or more (fpm)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.37","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":500,"unit":"fpm","why":"500 fpm or more.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-num-2","type":"num","q":"Per your FCOM, about how many seconds before CPA is a TA issued?","src":"FCOM 15.20.37","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":40,"unit":"s","why":"TA about 40 s, RA about 25 s before closest point of approach.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-num-3","type":"num","q":"Per your FCOM, about how many seconds before CPA is an RA issued?","src":"FCOM 15.20.37","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":25,"unit":"s","why":"RA about 25 s, TA about 40 s.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"What TCAS inhibits are there near the ground and by other warnings?","src":"FCOM 15.20.38","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS; NR60","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **INCREASE DESCENT** RAs inhibited below about **1500 ft RA**.\n- **DESCEND** RAs below about **1100 ft RA**.\n- **All RAs** below about **1000 ft RA**: with TA/RA selected, **TA ONLY** engages automatically and shows on the ND.\n- **All TCAS voice** below about **500 ft RA**.\n- **All TCAS alerts** are inhibited by **GPWS and windshear warnings**.","keys":[{"k":"increase descent 1500","m":["1500"]},{"k":"descend 1100","m":["1100"]},{"k":"all RAs 1000, TA ONLY","m":["1000"]},{"k":"voice 500","m":["500 ft","500 feet","500ft"]},{"k":"GPWS/windshear inhibit TCAS","m":["gpws","windshear","wind shear"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-order-2","type":"order","q":"Order the TCAS inhibits from the highest radio altitude to the lowest.","src":"FCOM 15.20.38","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["INCREASE DESCENT RAs inhibited (~1500 ft RA)","DESCEND RAs inhibited (~1100 ft RA)","All RAs inhibited, TA ONLY (~1000 ft RA)","All TCAS voice inhibited (~500 ft RA)"],"why":"1500, 1100, 1000, 500 ft RA.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-num-4","type":"num","q":"Below about what radio altitude are INCREASE DESCENT RAs inhibited (ft)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.38","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":1500,"unit":"ft","why":"About 1500 ft RA.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-num-5","type":"num","q":"Below about what radio altitude are DESCEND RAs inhibited (ft)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.38","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":1100,"unit":"ft","why":"About 1100 ft RA.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-num-6","type":"num","q":"Below about what radio altitude are all RAs inhibited and TA ONLY engaged automatically (ft)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.38","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1000,"unit":"ft","why":"About 1000 ft RA.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-num-7","type":"num","q":"Below about what radio altitude is all TCAS voice inhibited (ft)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.38","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":500,"unit":"ft","why":"About 500 ft RA.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"Which inhibits all TCAS alerts?","src":"FCOM 15.20.38","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["GPWS and windshear warnings","Master caution","A/P disconnect","Gear down"],"a":0,"why":"All TCAS alerts are inhibited by GPWS and windshear warnings.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"When would you select TA ONLY, and why after an engine failure?","src":"FCTM 7.19, 7.20; FCOM 15.20.38","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS; NR60","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Engine failure:** select TA ONLY because the aircraft **may not achieve a climb RA**.\n- Also (per operator policy): **closely spaced parallel approaches**, **take-off towards known traffic in sight**, or known **close proximity** to other aircraft.\n- Otherwise use **TA/RA from just before take-off until just after landing**.\n- Below about 1000 ft RA, TA ONLY engages automatically.","keys":[{"k":"engine failure","m":["engine"]},{"k":"may not achieve climb RA","m":["climb"]},{"k":"parallel approaches","m":["parallel"]},{"k":"TA/RA take-off to landing","m":["take off","takeoff","landing"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"Fly the 737 TCAS RA procedure. What about a climb RA in landing configuration?","src":"QRH MAN.1.9, MAN.1.10","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"If manoeuvring is needed:\n- **Disengage the autopilot and autothrottle**; smoothly adjust **pitch and thrust** to satisfy the RA.\n- **Follow the planned lateral path.**\n- **Don't use F/D pitch commands** until clear of conflict (F/D is not affected by TCAS).\n- **Never follow a DESCEND RA below 1000 ft AGL.**\n- **Climb RA in landing configuration:** **maximum thrust, flaps 15, gear up with a positive rate**.","keys":[{"k":"disengage A/P and A/T","m":["disengage","autopilot","a p"]},{"k":"pitch and thrust","m":["pitch"]},{"k":"follow lateral path","m":["lateral"]},{"k":"no descend RA below 1000 ft","m":["1000"]},{"k":"landing config: max thrust, flaps 15, gear up","m":["flaps 15","flap 15"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"Climb RA in landing configuration on the 737: what configuration change?","src":"QRH MAN.1.10","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Maximum thrust, flaps 15, gear up with positive rate","Maximum thrust, flaps 1, gear up immediately","TO/GA thrust, keep landing flaps and gear","Flaps 5, gear down"],"a":0,"why":"QRH: maximum thrust, flaps 15, gear up with a positive rate.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"Which TCAS version is on your 737, judged from the FCOM's RA aural table?","src":"FCOM 15.20.39; FCTM 7.19","ref":"Key facts: TCAS / ACAS; NR60","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["7.1","7.0","6.04a","TCAS I"],"a":0,"why":"The FCOM has LEVEL OFF, LEVEL OFF and no ADJUST VERTICAL SPEED, ADJUST: version 7.1 logic. The FCOM does not name the version.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"What is the TCAS TA/RA range?","src":"FCOM 15.20.37; Standard theory","ref":"NR17","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**TCAS alerts are triggered by time (tau), not distance.** TA about **20 to 48 s**, RA about **15 to 35 s** before closest approach, depending on sensitivity level (rises with altitude). At low closure rates a minimum range (**DMOD**) applies.\n- Your FCOM: **TA about 40 s, RA about 25 s.**\n- **Surveillance range:** the standard requires at least **14 NM**; typical TCAS II tracks about **30 to 40 NM ahead** (less behind and to the sides), so \"40 NM forward\" is a fair answer. The FCOM gives no NM figure.","keys":[{"k":"time not distance (tau)","m":["tau","time"]},{"k":"TA ~40 s, RA ~25 s","m":["40 s","40 sec","25 s","25 sec"]},{"k":"DMOD","m":["dmod","minimum range"]},{"k":"min 14 NM","m":["14 nm","14nm","14 nautical"]},{"k":"~40 NM forward","m":["40 nm","40nm","40 nautical"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-num-8","type":"num","q":"Minimum TCAS surveillance range required by the standard (NM)?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR17","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":14,"unit":"NM","why":"At least 14 NM; typical TCAS II tracks 30 to 40 NM ahead.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"What does the TCAS vertical display range switch do on the 737?","src":"FCOM 15.10.15","ref":"NR17","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"Altitude range switch on the transponder panel:\n- **N (normal):** **2700 ft below to 2700 ft above.**\n- **ABV:** 2700 ft below to **7000 ft above**.\n- **BLW:** **7000 ft below** to 2700 ft above.\nSo on the 737 it is **7000 ft, not the 9900 ft** some generic texts quote. Some panels mark it A and B.","keys":[{"k":"normal 2700","m":["2700"]},{"k":"ABV 7000 above","m":["7000"]},{"k":"BLW 7000 below","m":["below"]},{"k":"not 9900","m":["not 9900","9900"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["9900"],"why":"Generic text; 737 ABV/BLW is 7000 ft"}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"TCAS vertical display limits in NORMAL on the 737:","src":"FCOM 15.10.15","ref":"NR17","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["±2700 ft","±7000 ft","±9900 ft","±1200 ft"],"a":0,"why":"N: 2700 ft above and below. ABV/BLW extend one side to 7000 ft.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"With ABV selected, the 737 TCAS display covers:","src":"FCOM 15.10.15","ref":"NR17","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["2700 ft below to 7000 ft above","2700 ft below to 9900 ft above","7000 ft below to 7000 ft above","Unlimited above"],"a":0,"why":"ABV: 2700 below to 7000 above. Not 9900 ft.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between TCAS I and TCAS II? Which do you have?","src":"OM-A 31; OM-A Ch 3; OM-A 41.2; FCOM 15.20.39","ref":"NR60","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **TCAS I:** **Traffic Advisories only**, no avoidance manoeuvre; smaller aircraft.\n- **TCAS II:** TAs plus **vertical Resolution Advisories** (climb, descend, adjust). Two TCAS II aircraft **coordinate RAs via Mode S**. ICAO calls it **ACAS II**; required in India and for RVSM.\n- **TCAS III:** horizontal RAs; **never fielded**.\n- **Ours:** **TCAS II with 2 transponders**, version 7.1 logic from the FCOM aural table.","keys":[{"k":"TCAS I TA only","m":["ta only","traffic advisor","advisories only"]},{"k":"TCAS II vertical RAs","m":["vertical"]},{"k":"coordination Mode S","m":["coordinat","mode s"]},{"k":"TCAS III horizontal, not fielded","m":["horizontal","never"]},{"k":"ours TCAS II","m":["tcas ii","tcas 2","acas ii","acas 2"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"What are the TCAS versions, and what changed in 7.1?","src":"FCOM 15.20.39; FCTM 7.19","ref":"NR60","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **6.04a:** older.\n- **7.0:** adapted to **RVSM 1000 ft separation**, fewer nuisance alerts.\n- **7.1:** **ADJUST VERTICAL SPEED, ADJUST** replaced by **LEVEL OFF, LEVEL OFF** (crews on 7.0 sometimes increased VS wrongly; it always meant reduce), and **improved reversal logic** when the other aircraft doesn't follow its RA. **Mandatory in Europe from December 2015.**\n- Our FCOM lists LEVEL OFF and reversals CLIMB, CLIMB NOW / DESCEND, DESCEND NOW: 7.1 logic.","keys":[{"k":"7.0 for RVSM","m":["rvsm"]},{"k":"LEVEL OFF replaces ADJUST VS","m":["level off"]},{"k":"adjust vertical speed removed","m":["adjust vertical speed","adjust"]},{"k":"reversal logic","m":["reversal"]},{"k":"Europe Dec 2015","m":["2015"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"Which RA aural was replaced in TCAS 7.1?","src":"FCTM 7.19; FCOM 15.20.39","ref":"NR60","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["ADJUST VERTICAL SPEED, ADJUST","CLIMB, CLIMB","MONITOR VERTICAL SPEED","CLEAR OF CONFLICT"],"a":0,"why":"Replaced by LEVEL OFF, LEVEL OFF (reduce to 0 fpm).","topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"TCAS 7.1 became mandatory in Europe from:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR60","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["December 2015","January 2010","March 2020","June 2005"],"a":0,"why":"December 2015.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"What does LEVEL OFF, LEVEL OFF require?","src":"FCOM 15.20.39","ref":"NR60","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Reduce vertical speed to 0 fpm","Climb at 1500 fpm","Maintain current vertical speed","Descend at 1500 fpm"],"a":0,"why":"Reduce to 0 fpm.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"What does the Boeing FCTM say about flying TCAS?","src":"FCTM 7.19, 7.20","ref":"NR60","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- Fly the **QRH Traffic Avoidance** manoeuvre; **don't manoeuvre on a TA alone**.\n- The **flight director is not affected by TCAS**: follow F/D commands only if they also satisfy the RA.\n- **Never manoeuvre opposite to an RA**: TCAS may be coordinating with the other aircraft.\n- **Windshear, GPWS and stall warnings take precedence** over TCAS; the **stick shaker always** takes priority.\n- Use TA/RA from just before take-off to just after landing.","keys":[{"k":"no manoeuvre on TA","m":["ta alone","not on a ta","no manoeuvre","dont manoeuvre"]},{"k":"F/D not affected","m":["flight director","f d not","fd not","fd commands"]},{"k":"never opposite","m":["opposite"]},{"k":"windshear/GPWS/stall take precedence","m":["precedence","priority"]},{"k":"stick shaker","m":["stick shaker","shaker"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"Can a TCAS RA be generated against a Mode C-only intruder?","src":"FCOM 15.20.37","ref":"NR60","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Yes, but not coordinated","No, only Mode S intruders","Only a TA","Only above FL290"],"a":0,"why":"RAs can be generated against Mode C intruders; coordinated RAs need both aircraft to have TCAS.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"What is tau in TCAS?","src":"FAA Introduction to TCAS II Version 7.1, Table 3; FCOM 15.20.37","ref":"NR61","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"TCAS works on **time, not distance**:\n- **Range tau = range ÷ closure rate**: time to **CPA**.\n- **Vertical tau = vertical separation ÷ vertical closure rate**: time to co-altitude.\n- A TA or RA is issued when **both** are below the threshold for the current **sensitivity level** (rises with altitude).\n- For slow closures, fixed thresholds apply: **DMOD** (minimum range) and **ZTHR** (altitude threshold).\n- TA tau is larger than RA tau: warning first, manoeuvre if needed. Tau is the time until the intruder becomes a threat.","keys":[{"k":"range / closure rate","m":["closure rate","range divided","range over"]},{"k":"time to CPA","m":["cpa","closest point","closest approach"]},{"k":"vertical tau","m":["vertical"]},{"k":"sensitivity level","m":["sensitivity"]},{"k":"DMOD / ZTHR","m":["dmod","zthr"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match own altitude to TCAS v7.1 sensitivity level and TA/RA tau.","src":"FAA Introduction to TCAS II Version 7.1, Table 3","ref":"NR61","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Below 1000 ft AGL","SL2: TA 20 s, no RA"],["1000 to 2350 ft AGL","SL3: TA 25 s, RA 15 s"],["2350 to 5000 ft","SL4: TA 30 s, RA 20 s"],["5000 to 10,000 ft","SL5: TA 40 s, RA 25 s"],["10,000 to 20,000 ft","SL6: TA 45 s, RA 30 s"],["Above 20,000 ft","SL7: TA 48 s, RA 35 s"]],"why":"FAA v7.1 Table 3. FCOM summary: TA about 40 s, RA about 25 s.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-num-9","type":"num","q":"TCAS v7.1 RA tau above 20,000 ft (s)?","src":"FAA Introduction to TCAS II Version 7.1, Table 3","ref":"NR61","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":35,"unit":"s","why":"SL7: TA 48 s, RA 35 s.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-num-10","type":"num","q":"TCAS v7.1 TA tau above 20,000 ft (s)?","src":"FAA Introduction to TCAS II Version 7.1, Table 3","ref":"NR61","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":48,"unit":"s","why":"SL7: TA 48 s, RA 35 s.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"Below 1000 ft AGL, TCAS sensitivity level 2 gives:","src":"FAA Introduction to TCAS II Version 7.1, Table 3","ref":"NR61","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["TA only, tau 20 s","TA 25 s, RA 15 s","RA only","No alerts"],"a":0,"why":"SL2 is TA only, 20 s.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"What is DMOD?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR61","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["A minimum range threshold used when closure is slow","The TA tau at SL7","Vertical display limit","Mode S data link"],"a":0,"why":"DMOD ensures a slowly converging aircraft still triggers an alert; ZTHR is the fixed altitude threshold.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"You're descending through 6000 ft and get \"TRAFFIC, TRAFFIC\". Take me through it.","src":"FCOM 15.20.37, 15.20.38; FCTM 7.19; QRH MAN.1.9; OM-A 31","ref":"NR61; NR60; OB78","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What is it and what do you do?","a":"A TA: amber circle. No manoeuvre; look out, prepare.","keys":[{"k":"TA","m":["traffic advisory","its a ta","a ta ","amber circle"]},{"k":"no manoeuvre","m":["no manoeuvre","no maneuver","not manoeuvre"]}]},{"q":"How does TCAS decide to alert? Range?","a":"Tau, time to CPA: range over closure rate; FCOM TA ~40 s, RA ~25 s.","keys":[{"k":"tau","m":["tau","time"]},{"k":"40 and 25 s","m":["40 s","40 sec","25 s","25 sec"]}]},{"q":"Now \"CLIMB, CLIMB\" but ATC says descend. Actions?","a":"Follow the RA: disengage A/P and A/T, pitch and thrust, \"UNABLE, TCAS RA\"; never opposite.","keys":[{"k":"follow RA","m":["follow"]},{"k":"disengage A/P A/T","m":["disengage"]},{"k":"unable TCAS RA","m":["unable"]}]},{"q":"GPWS sounds during the RA?","a":"GPWS/windshear/stall take precedence; GPWS warnings inhibit TCAS.","keys":[{"k":"GPWS priority","m":["gpws","precedence","priority"]}]},{"q":"After CLEAR OF CONFLICT?","a":"Return promptly to clearance, \"CLEAR OF CONFLICT, RETURNING TO...\", written report within 24 h.","keys":[{"k":"return promptly","m":["return"]},{"k":"report 24 h","m":["24 h","24 hours","24h"]}]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"TCAS TA and RA \"range\": what do 3.3 NM and 2.1 NM mean?","src":"FCOM 15.20.37; Oral bank p.51","ref":"OB77","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"TCAS works on **time to closest approach (tau)**, not distance. FCOM: **TA about 40 s, RA about 25 s** before CPA.\nThe bank's distances are those times at a **300 kt closure rate**: **40 s × 300 kt = 3.3 NM**; **25 s × 300 kt = 2.1 NM**. At other closures the distance changes, and at slow closures a fixed minimum range (DMOD) applies. Tau also varies with sensitivity level: TA 20 to 48 s, RA 15 to 35 s.","keys":[{"k":"time not distance","m":["time","tau"]},{"k":"300 kt closure","m":["300 kt","300 knots","300kt"]},{"k":"40 s and 25 s","m":["40 s","25 s","40 sec","25 sec"]},{"k":"DMOD at slow closure","m":["dmod","minimum range","slow"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-num-11","type":"num","q":"At a 300 kt closure, how far apart (NM) are the aircraft 40 s before CPA (the bank's TA distance)?","src":"Oral bank p.51; FCOM 15.20.37","ref":"OB77","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":3.3,"unit":"NM","tol":0.05,"why":"40/3600 × 300 = 3.3 NM.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-num-12","type":"num","q":"At a 300 kt closure, the distance (NM) 25 s before CPA (the bank's RA distance)?","src":"Oral bank p.51; FCOM 15.20.37","ref":"OB77","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2.1,"unit":"NM","tol":0.05,"why":"25/3600 × 300 ≈ 2.1 NM.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"Bank question: if the Mode S transponder is switched off, the aircraft becomes a ghost plane. Correct?","src":"FCOM 15.20.37; Standard theory","ref":"OB77","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Any aircraft without an operating transponder (A, C or S) is invisible to TCAS**, not just Mode S. **Altitude and vertical motion appear only for Mode S or C intruders.** A **Mode A-only** target can give a **TA but no RA**. Coordinated RAs need both aircraft with TCAS (Mode S).","keys":[{"k":"any transponder off = invisible","m":["any","no transponder","without"]},{"k":"altitude needs Mode C or S","m":["mode c","mode s"]},{"k":"Mode A: TA no RA","m":["mode a"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["only mode s"],"why":"Not just Mode S; any aircraft with no operating transponder is invisible"}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"A Mode A-only intruder (no altitude reporting) can generate:","src":"Standard theory; FCOM 15.20.37","ref":"OB77","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["A TA but no RA","TA and RA","RA only","Nothing at all"],"a":0,"why":"No altitude means no RA; it can give a TA.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"Bank question: list the RA commands, including TRAFFIC, TRAFFIC.","src":"FCOM 15.20.37, 15.20.39","ref":"OB77","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"**TRAFFIC, TRAFFIC is the TA aural, not an RA.** RA aurals include CLIMB, CLIMB; DESCEND, DESCEND; LEVEL OFF, LEVEL OFF (7.1); reversals CLIMB, CLIMB NOW and DESCEND, DESCEND NOW; and CLEAR OF CONFLICT at the end.","keys":[{"k":"traffic traffic is a TA","m":["traffic advisory","is a ta","ta aural","the ta"]},{"k":"climb / descend","m":["climb","descend"]},{"k":"level off","m":["level off"]},{"k":"clear of conflict","m":["clear of conflict"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["traffic traffic is an ra","traffic traffic ra"],"why":"TRAFFIC, TRAFFIC is the TA aural"}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"When will you not follow a TCAS RA? What are the warning priorities?","src":"FCTM 7.19, 7.20; FCOM 15.20.38; QRH MAN.1.9","ref":"OB78","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Priorities:** **windshear, GPWS and stall warnings take precedence** over TCAS; the **stick shaker always** has priority. GPWS and windshear warnings **inhibit all TCAS alerts**.\n**Don't follow (or stop following) an RA when:**\n- stick shaker or stall warning;\n- windshear or GPWS warning;\n- it would mean a **DESCEND RA below 1000 ft AGL**.\nTCAS itself limits RAs near the ground (1500, 1100, 1000 ft).\n**Otherwise always follow it**, even against ATC (\"UNABLE, TCAS RA\"), never opposite.","keys":[{"k":"stick shaker / stall","m":["stick shaker","stall","shaker"]},{"k":"windshear / GPWS","m":["windshear","gpws","wind shear"]},{"k":"descend RA below 1000 ft","m":["1000"]},{"k":"otherwise always follow, even vs ATC","m":["always follow","even against","atc","unable"]},{"k":"never opposite","m":["opposite"]}],"topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"Which has the highest priority among these on the 737?","src":"FCTM 7.19","ref":"OB78","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Stick shaker","TCAS RA","TCAS TA","ATC instruction"],"a":0,"why":"Stick shaker always takes priority; windshear, GPWS and stall warnings take precedence over TCAS.","topic":16},{"id":"t16-mcq-16","type":"mcq","q":"ATC instructs you to descend while you have a CLIMB RA. You:","src":"OM-A 31; FCTM 7.19","ref":"OB78","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Follow the RA and say \"UNABLE, TCAS RA\"","Follow ATC","Level off as a compromise","Disconnect TCAS"],"a":0,"why":"Follow the RA; never manoeuvre opposite to it.","topic":16},{"id":"t17-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"What is the company guidance on windshear: danger, avoidance, prevention, reporting?","src":"OM-A 17.3.6.4","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Most dangerous below 500 ft**; you have only **5 to 10 s** to recognise it.\n- **Avoid:** thunderstorms, **virga**, **LLWAS** alerts, **PIREPs**.\n- **Prevention:** **full take-off thrust** (not reduced), the **longest runway**, **minimum landing flap plus a wind correction**.\n- **Report:** wind, height, and **speed gained or lost**.","keys":[{"k":"below 500 ft","m":["500 ft","500 feet","500ft"]},{"k":"5 to 10 s","m":["5 to 10","10 s","10 sec"]},{"k":"avoid: TS, virga, LLWAS, PIREPs","m":["virga","llwas","pirep","thunderstorm"]},{"k":"full take-off thrust","m":["full thrust","full take off thrust","full takeoff thrust","not reduced","no derate"]},{"k":"longest runway / min flap","m":["longest runway","minimum flap","minimum landing flap"]}],"topic":17},{"id":"t17-num-1","type":"num","q":"Below what height is windshear most dangerous (ft)?","src":"OM-A 17.3.6.4","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":500,"unit":"ft","why":"Most dangerous below 500 ft.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"How long do you typically have to recognise windshear?","src":"OM-A 17.3.6.4","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","prio":2,"o":["5 to 10 s","20 to 30 s","1 to 2 s","About 1 min"],"a":0,"why":"5 to 10 s.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"For a take-off with possible windshear, which thrust setting?","src":"OM-A 17.3.6.4","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Full rated take-off thrust (not reduced)","Assumed temperature derate","Climb thrust","Maximum derate"],"a":0,"why":"Full take-off thrust, longest runway.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"What windshear systems does the 737 have? Describe the reactive warning.","src":"FCOM 15.20.25; QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"Key facts: Windshear; NR31","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Two systems:\n- **Reactive:** the **GPWS (mode 7)**, enabled **below 1500 ft RA**, detection from **rotation**. **Two-tone siren**, then \"**WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR**\", with red **WINDSHEAR** on both attitude indicators. It detects shear you are **already in** from air data and inertial data, and works in **dry microbursts**.\n- **Predictive:** the **weather radar**, alerts only below 1200 ft RA.","keys":[{"k":"reactive = GPWS mode 7","m":["gpws","mode 7","egpws"]},{"k":"below 1500 ft RA","m":["1500"]},{"k":"from rotation","m":["rotation"]},{"k":"siren + WINDSHEAR x3","m":["siren","windshear windshear"]},{"k":"predictive = weather radar","m":["weather radar","radar"]}],"topic":17},{"id":"t17-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"Describe 737 predictive windshear: when does the radar scan and when does it alert?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25; QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"Key facts: Windshear; NR65","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"From the **weather radar**:\n- **Scans automatically** with thrust levers set for take-off, or in flight **below 2300 ft RA** (**1800 ft** with the RDR-4000). Pushing WXR enables PWS before take-off.\n- **Alerts only below 1200 ft RA**: **warning** for windshear within **1.5 NM** ahead on approach (close ahead on take-off); **caution** within **3 NM**.\n- **Inhibits:** new cautions **80 kt to 400 ft RA**, new warnings **100 kt to 50 ft RA**.\n- Alerts available about **12 s** after scanning starts.","keys":[{"k":"scan below 2300 ft","m":["2300"]},{"k":"alerts below 1200 ft","m":["1200"]},{"k":"warning 1.5 NM","m":["1.5","15 nm","one and a half"]},{"k":"caution 3 NM","m":["3 nm","3nm","three nm","3 nautical"]},{"k":"inhibits 80 kt-400 ft / 100 kt-50 ft","m":["80 kt","80 knots","100 kt","100 knots","400 ft"]}],"topic":17},{"id":"t17-num-2","type":"num","q":"Predictive windshear: the radar scans automatically in flight below what radio altitude (ft, standard radar)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2300,"unit":"ft","why":"2300 ft RA (1800 ft with the RDR-4000).","topic":17},{"id":"t17-num-3","type":"num","q":"Predictive windshear alerts are given only below what radio altitude (ft)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1200,"unit":"ft","why":"Below 1200 ft RA.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-num-4","type":"num","q":"Reactive windshear (GPWS) is enabled below what radio altitude (ft)?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1500,"unit":"ft","why":"Below 1500 ft RA, detection from rotation.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-num-5","type":"num","q":"PWS warning on approach: windshear within how many NM directly ahead?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":1.5,"unit":"NM","why":"GO AROUND, WINDSHEAR AHEAD: within 1.5 NM.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-num-6","type":"num","q":"PWS caution (MONITOR RADAR DISPLAY): windshear within how many NM ahead?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":3,"unit":"NM","why":"Within 3 NM ahead, take-off or approach.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"During take-off and landing, new PWS cautions are inhibited:","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["From 80 kt to 400 ft RA","From 100 kt to 50 ft RA","From 60 kt to 1000 ft RA","Below 1500 ft RA"],"a":0,"why":"New cautions 80 kt to 400 ft RA; new warnings 100 kt to 50 ft RA. Alerts already showing remain.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"During take-off and landing, new PWS warnings are inhibited:","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["From 100 kt to 50 ft RA","From 80 kt to 400 ft RA","From V1 to 35 ft","Never"],"a":0,"why":"New warnings 100 kt to 50 ft RA.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"PWS scan altitude with the RDR-4000 radar:","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","prio":3,"o":["Below 1800 ft RA","Below 2300 ft RA","Below 1200 ft RA","Below 1500 ft RA"],"a":0,"why":"1800 ft RA with the RDR-4000, 2300 ft with the standard radar.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-num-7","type":"num","q":"How many seconds after scanning starts are PWS alerts available?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","prio":3,"ans":12,"unit":"s","why":"About 12 s.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-order-1","type":"order","q":"Order the 737 windshear recovery actions (manual flight).","src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"Key facts: Windshear; OB80","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["Disengage autopilot, push TO/GA","Aggressively apply maximum thrust","Disengage autothrottle","Roll wings level and rotate towards 15°","Retract speedbrakes","Follow F/D TO/GA guidance, not exceeding the PLI","Don't change flap or gear until windshear is no longer a factor"],"why":"QRH windshear escape manoeuvre, manual flight.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-num-8","type":"num","q":"Windshear recovery: initial pitch target (degrees)?","src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"Key facts: Windshear","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":15,"unit":"°","why":"Roll wings level and rotate towards 15°, not exceeding the PLI.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"What is the windshear mode in GPWS? What is predictive windshear?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"NR31","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Reactive windshear = GPWS/EGPWS mode 7.** It detects shear already happening from **air data and inertial data** (airspeed against ground speed, vertical acceleration, angle of attack). Active **below about 1500 ft RA** on take-off and approach; red WINDSHEAR and \"WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR\".\n- **Predictive windshear (PWS)** comes from the **weather radar**. Its **Doppler** measures the wind change across a microburst **ahead (up to about 5 NM)**, so you are warned **before** you enter it. Alerts below about **1200 ft**: WINDSHEAR AHEAD (warning) or MONITOR RADAR DISPLAY (caution). It **needs precipitation**.","keys":[{"k":"reactive = mode 7","m":["mode 7","mode seven"]},{"k":"air data / inertial","m":["air data","inertial","ground speed","angle of attack"]},{"k":"predictive = weather radar Doppler","m":["doppler","weather radar","radar"]},{"k":"ahead ~5 NM, before entry","m":["ahead","5 nm","before"]},{"k":"needs precipitation","m":["precipitation","moisture","rain"]}],"topic":17},{"id":"t17-num-9","type":"num","q":"Predictive windshear looks ahead up to about how many NM?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR31","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":5,"unit":"NM","why":"Up to about 5 NM ahead.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"Predictive vs reactive windshear: which comes from the weather radar and which from EGPWS?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25; OM-A 17.3.6.4","ref":"NR65","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Predictive (PWS): weather radar.** Doppler sees the shear **ahead**, warns **before** entry; alerts below about 1200 ft; **needs precipitation**.\n- **Reactive (RWS): EGPWS (GPWS computer, mode 7) on the 737.** Detects shear you are **already in**; below about 1500 ft; **works in dry microbursts** where the radar sees nothing.\n- **Airbus is different:** reactive windshear comes from the **FAC**, not EGPWS. Say which aircraft you are describing.\n- Recovery: QRH windshear escape manoeuvre immediately.","keys":[{"k":"PWS from weather radar","m":["weather radar","radar"]},{"k":"RWS from EGPWS/GPWS","m":["egpws","gpws"]},{"k":"ahead vs already in","m":["ahead","already in","already"]},{"k":"dry microburst","m":["dry"]},{"k":"Airbus: FAC","m":["fac","flight augmentation"]}],"topic":17},{"id":"t17-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the windshear alert to its source and level.","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"NR65","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR, WINDSHEAR (with siren)","Reactive warning, GPWS"],["WINDSHEAR AHEAD","Predictive warning on take-off, radar"],["GO AROUND, WINDSHEAR AHEAD","Predictive warning on approach (within 1.5 NM), radar"],["MONITOR RADAR DISPLAY","Predictive caution (within 3 NM), radar"]],"why":"Reactive from GPWS mode 7; predictive from the weather radar.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"On the A320, where does reactive windshear detection come from?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR65","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["FAC (Flight Augmentation Computer)","EGPWS","Weather radar","FWC"],"a":0,"why":"Airbus uses the FAC; the 737 uses the GPWS computer (mode 7). Say which aircraft you mean.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"Which windshear system works in a dry microburst?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"NR65","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Reactive (GPWS)","Predictive (weather radar)","Both equally","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"PWS needs moisture or particulates; reactive detects the shear you are in regardless.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"What are the PWS alert priorities and limitations, and how is the ND affected?","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"NR65","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Display:** windshear symbol with **amber radials**; the radar picture **pops up automatically** in expanded MAP, centre MAP, expanded VOR or expanded APP.\n- **Priority:** PWS alerts are **inhibited by an actual windshear warning**, look-ahead terrain alerts and radio-altitude alerts.\n- **Limits:** needs **moisture or particulates**; detects microbursts and similar shear, **not all types**, so keep using traditional avoidance. **Caution or warning depends on where the shear is, not its strength.**\n- RDR-4000: 10 to 60 s warning before the encounter.","keys":[{"k":"amber radials / pop-up","m":["radial","pop up","popup","automatically"]},{"k":"inhibited by reactive WS/terrain","m":["inhibit"]},{"k":"needs moisture","m":["moisture","particulate","precipitation"]},{"k":"not all types","m":["not all"]},{"k":"location not strength","m":["not its strength","not strength","location","where"]}],"topic":17},{"id":"t17-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"A PWS alert is a warning rather than a caution because:","src":"FCOM 15.20.25","ref":"NR65","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["The shear is closer / directly ahead","The shear is stronger","The aircraft is heavier","Gear is down"],"a":0,"why":"Caution or warning depends on where the shear is, not its strength.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"Short final, heavy rain showers around. \"MONITOR RADAR DISPLAY\". Talk me through it.","src":"FCOM 15.20.25; QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"NR65; OB80","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What is that alert?","a":"Predictive windshear caution from the weather radar: shear within 3 NM ahead.","keys":[{"k":"predictive caution","m":["predictive","caution"]},{"k":"3 NM","m":["3 nm","3nm","three"]}]},{"q":"What if it becomes GO AROUND, WINDSHEAR AHEAD?","a":"PWS warning, shear within 1.5 NM directly ahead: go around, windshear escape.","keys":[{"k":"1.5 NM","m":["1.5","15 nm","one and a half"]},{"k":"go around","m":["go around"]}]},{"q":"Now the siren and WINDSHEAR x3. Recovery?","a":"A/P off, TO/GA, max thrust, A/T off, wings level, rotate towards 15°, speedbrakes in, follow F/D within PLI.","keys":[{"k":"TO/GA","m":["toga","to ga"]},{"k":"max thrust","m":["maximum thrust","max thrust"]},{"k":"15 degrees","m":["15 deg","15°","fifteen"]},{"k":"PLI","m":["pli","pitch limit"]}]},{"q":"Pitch limit?","a":"Intermittent stick shaker or initial buffet is the upper limit; may be needed for terrain clearance.","keys":[{"k":"stick shaker","m":["shaker"]},{"k":"buffet","m":["buffet"]}]},{"q":"Configuration?","a":"Don't change flap or gear, don't chase lost speed, until clear of the shear.","keys":[{"k":"no flap/gear change","m":["flap","gear"]}]}],"topic":17},{"id":"t17-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between a microburst and windshear?","src":"FCOM SP.16.27; Oral bank p.85; OM-A 17.3.6.1, 17.3.6.4, 17.3.6.12","ref":"OB79","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Windshear:** a **change of wind speed and/or direction over a short distance** along the flight path; horizontal or vertical. Sources: thunderstorms, fronts, jet streams, inversions, terrain.\n- **Microburst:** a **small, intense downburst** from a convective cloud that spreads out on hitting the ground: **under 4 km across**, lasting **about 5 min**. Gusts over 50 kt and downdrafts over 1500 fpm are not unusual.\n- On approach: **headwind (speed gain), then downdraft, then tailwind (speed loss)**.\n- **Every microburst contains windshear, but not all windshear is a microburst.**","keys":[{"k":"change speed/direction short distance","m":["short distance","change of wind","change in wind"]},{"k":"microburst = intense downburst","m":["downburst","downdraft"]},{"k":"<4 km, ~5 min","m":["4 km","4km","5 min"]},{"k":"headwind then tailwind","m":["headwind","tailwind"]},{"k":"microburst contains windshear","m":["not all windshear","every microburst","contains"]}],"topic":17},{"id":"t17-order-2","type":"order","q":"Order what an aircraft meets flying through a microburst on approach.","src":"OM-A 17.3.6.4","ref":"OB79","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["Headwind (speed gain, tendency to go high)","Downdraft","Tailwind (speed loss, pitch down, rapid sink)"],"why":"First a headwind, then the downdraft, then a tailwind as the outflow reverses.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Typical microburst size and duration:","src":"Oral bank p.85","ref":"OB79","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Under 4 km across, about 5 min","About 20 km across, 1 h","Under 1 km, 30 s","About 10 km, 30 min"],"a":0,"why":"Under 4 km across, lasting about 5 min.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"In thunderstorm downdrafts, what values are not unusual?","src":"OM-A 17.3.6.1, 17.3.6.12","ref":"OB79","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Gusts over 50 kt, downdrafts over 1500 fpm","Gusts over 20 kt, downdrafts over 500 fpm","Gusts over 100 kt, downdrafts over 6000 fpm","Gusts over 30 kt, downdrafts over 300 fpm"],"a":0,"why":"Gusts over 50 kt and downdrafts over 1500 fpm.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw a microburst and an aircraft's approach path through it.","src":"OM-A 17.3.6.4; Oral bank p.85","ref":"OB79","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"A convective cloud with a narrow column of descending air hitting the ground and spreading outwards both ways. An approach path enters the outflow first (headwind, speed gain, above path), passes the downdraft core (sink), then exits into the tailwind (speed loss, below path).","checklist":["Downdraft column from the cloud","Outflow spreading out at the surface in both directions","Approach path drawn through it","Headwind segment: speed gain, above path","Downdraft core: sink","Tailwind segment: speed loss, below path","Scale: under 4 km across"],"topic":17},{"id":"t17-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"How do you avoid windshear, and how do you recover on the 737 (manual flight)?","src":"FCOM SP.16.27; QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"OB80","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Avoid:** watch for thunderstorms, virga, PIREPs and LLWAS alerts. If windshear is confirmed, **delay the take-off or don't continue the approach**.\n**Recovery (manual):**\n- **disengage the A/P and push TO/GA**;\n- **aggressively apply maximum thrust**, disengage the A/T;\n- **roll wings level, rotate towards 15°**;\n- **retract the speedbrakes**;\n- **follow the F/D without exceeding the PLI**.\nDon't change flap or gear, don't chase lost speed, until clear. **Intermittent stick shaker or initial buffet is the upper pitch limit.**","keys":[{"k":"delay take-off / discontinue approach","m":["delay","discontinue","dont continue","don t continue","not continue"]},{"k":"TO/GA","m":["toga","to ga"]},{"k":"max thrust","m":["maximum thrust","max thrust","full thrust"]},{"k":"wings level, 15 deg","m":["15 deg","15°","15 degrees","fifteen"]},{"k":"speedbrakes","m":["speedbrake","speed brake"]},{"k":"PLI / stick shaker limit","m":["pli","pitch limit","stick shaker","shaker"]}],"topic":17},{"id":"t17-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"Windshear recovery with the autopilot engaged on the 737: what do you do?","src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"OB80","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Push TO/GA**, **verify TO/GA mode and GA thrust**, **retract the speedbrakes**, and **monitor**. Be ready to **disengage and fly manually** if the shear exceeds AFDS capability. Don't change flap or gear, don't try to regain lost speed until clear of the shear.","keys":[{"k":"push TO/GA","m":["toga","to ga"]},{"k":"verify mode and thrust","m":["verify"]},{"k":"speedbrakes","m":["speedbrake","speed brake"]},{"k":"ready to fly manually","m":["manual","disengage"]}],"topic":17},{"id":"t17-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"Bank answer: \"autopilot disengage, open full throttle, climb till the stick shaker activates\". Correct?","src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"OB80","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Not quite.** On the 737 the **A/P can stay engaged with TO/GA** (automatic recovery: push TO/GA, verify, speedbrakes in, monitor). Manually: **rotate towards 15°** and follow the F/D within the **PLI**. **Intermittent stick shaker or initial buffet is the upper pitch limit**, which you may need for terrain clearance: **a limit, not a target** to climb to.","keys":[{"k":"A/P can stay engaged","m":["stay engaged","remain engaged","can stay","autopilot engaged"]},{"k":"15 deg / PLI","m":["15 deg","15°","fifteen","pli"]},{"k":"shaker is a limit not a target","m":["limit","not a target"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["climb till the stick shaker","climb to the stick shaker","until stick shaker"],"why":"Shaker is the upper limit you may have to use, not a target"}],"topic":17},{"id":"t17-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"During windshear recovery, what is the upper pitch limit?","src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"OB80","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Intermittent stick shaker or initial buffet","20° nose up always","The F/D bar regardless","15° exactly"],"a":0,"why":"Rotate towards 15°; intermittent stick shaker or initial buffet is the upper limit.","topic":17},{"id":"t17-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"During windshear recovery, configuration changes:","src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"OB80","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Don't change flap or gear until windshear is no longer a factor","Retract gear immediately","Select flaps 1","Extend speedbrakes"],"a":0,"why":"Don't change flap or gear; retract speedbrakes only.","topic":17},{"id":"t18-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"Does the 737 MAX have an ECAM or FWC? How are alerts generated?","src":"FCOM 15.20.1, 15.20.2; FCOM 10.20.2; FCOM 4.20.1","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"**No.** The 737 MAX has **no FWC, SDAC or ECAM**. **Each system raises its own alert**: red lights, amber cautions through the **master caution system**, aurals, and messages on the PFD, ND and engine display. The crew works the **QRH**.\n- **Two Display Processing Computers** drive the displays.\n- The **MCP** replaces the FCU.\n- The **dual FMC with FCCs A and B and the autothrottle** does the FMGC's job.","keys":[{"k":"no ECAM/FWC","m":["no ecam","no fwc","doesnt have","does not have","no central"]},{"k":"each system own alert / master caution","m":["master caution","own alert","each system"]},{"k":"QRH","m":["qrh"]},{"k":"DPCs","m":["display processing","dpc"]},{"k":"MCP / FMC / FCC","m":["mcp","fmc","fcc"]}],"topic":18},{"id":"t18-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the Airbus term to its 737 MAX equivalent.","src":"FCOM 4.20.1, 11.30.1; FCOM 10.20.2; FCTM 1.4; FCOM 15.20.2","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: Airbus to 737 MAX equivalents","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["FCU","Mode Control Panel (MCP)"],["FMGC","Dual FMC + AFDS (FCCs A and B) + autothrottle"],["DMC","Two Display Processing Computers"],["ECAM procedures","QRH non-normal checklists"],["ECAM E/WD","Engine display"],["FWC / SDAC","No central computer: master caution system, GPWS, SMYDs"]],"why":"Boeing has no central warning computer; each system alerts on its own.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"Give the 737 MAX equivalents of the Airbus FWC/SDAC, DMC and FMA.","src":"FCOM 15.20.2, 15.20.7, 15.20.11, 15.20.25; FCOM 10.20.2, 10.15.6; FCOM 4.20.6","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: Airbus to 737 MAX equivalents","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **FWC/SDAC:** no central warning computer. A **master caution system** collects cautions and stores simple faults; the **GPWS** raises ground proximity and reactive windshear alerts; **two SMYD computers** handle stall warning.\n- **DMC:** **two Display Processing Computers**. In AUTO, DPC 1 drives the Captain's displays, DPC 2 the F/O's. One DPC can drive all four DUs, switching automatically after a single failure, with **DSPLY SOURCE 1 or 2** on the PFD.\n- **FMA:** above the attitude indicator: autothrottle, roll and pitch columns, A/P status below.","keys":[{"k":"master caution system","m":["master caution"]},{"k":"SMYD for stall","m":["smyd"]},{"k":"two DPCs","m":["dpc","display processing"]},{"k":"DSPLY SOURCE","m":["dsply source","display source"]},{"k":"FMA above ADI","m":["above the attitude","above attitude","autothrottle roll pitch"]}],"topic":18},{"id":"t18-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"Which 737 computers handle stall warning?","src":"FCOM 15.20.7","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: Airbus to 737 MAX equivalents","prio":3,"o":["Two SMYD computers","The GPWS","The DPCs","FCC A only"],"a":0,"why":"Two Stall Management Yaw Damper (SMYD) computers.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"On the 737 MAX, if one DPC fails:","src":"FCOM 10.20.2, 10.15.6","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: Airbus to 737 MAX equivalents","prio":3,"o":["The other DPC drives all four DUs automatically; DSPLY SOURCE 1 or 2 shows on the PFD","The Captain's displays go blank","The ISFD takes over the PFD","The crew must select the ECAM page"],"a":0,"why":"One DPC can drive all four DUs, switching automatically after a single failure.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"What are the levels of warning on the 737?","src":"FCOM 15.20.1, 15.20.2; FCTM 1.2","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: What are the levels of warning?","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Warning (red):** conditions needing **immediate attention**: red lights in the primary field of view, e.g. fires, A/P and A/T disengage, gear unsafe. Fire: two **master FIRE WARN** lights and the **bell**; pushing either cancels both and resets.\n- **Caution (amber):** **timely attention**. **MASTER CAUTION** and the **system annunciator** light together; push to cancel, push the annunciator to **recall**. Simple faults in redundant systems are stored and show only on recall.\n- **Advisory:** amber text.\n- **Information:** **blue** status lights; **green** fully extended configuration.","keys":[{"k":"warning red, immediate","m":["red","immediate"]},{"k":"FIRE WARN + bell","m":["fire warn","bell"]},{"k":"caution amber, timely","m":["amber","timely"]},{"k":"annunciator recall","m":["recall","annunciator","six pack","six light"]},{"k":"advisory / information blue","m":["advisory","blue"]}],"topic":18},{"id":"t18-order-1","type":"order","q":"Order the 737 alert levels from highest to lowest priority.","src":"FCOM 15.20.1; FCTM 1.2, 1.3","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: What are the levels of warning?","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Warning (red, immediate attention)","Caution (amber, timely attention)","Advisory (amber text)","Information (blue status / green configuration)"],"why":"Red first priority, amber second; pilots prioritise warnings over cautions.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"Give the 737 warning aurals and what each means.","src":"FCOM 15.20.1, 15.20.2","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: What are the levels of warning?","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Clacker:** airspeed limit.\n- **A/P disconnect tone.**\n- **Intermittent horn:** take-off configuration, cabin altitude.\n- **Steady horn:** landing gear.\n- **Voice:** GPWS, windshear.\n- **Stick shaker:** stall.\n- **Fire bell** with the master FIRE WARN lights.","keys":[{"k":"clacker = airspeed","m":["clacker"]},{"k":"intermittent horn = T/O config / cabin alt","m":["intermittent horn","take off config","takeoff config","cabin alt"]},{"k":"steady horn = gear","m":["steady horn","gear"]},{"k":"voice = GPWS/windshear","m":["voice","gpws"]},{"k":"stick shaker = stall","m":["shaker","stall"]}],"topic":18},{"id":"t18-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match the 737 aural to its meaning.","src":"FCOM 15.20.1, 15.20.2","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: What are the levels of warning?","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Clacker","Airspeed limit"],["Intermittent horn","Take-off configuration or cabin altitude"],["Steady horn","Landing gear"],["Bell","Fire"],["Stick shaker","Stall"],["Voice","GPWS or windshear"]],"why":"From the FCOM warning systems chapter.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"On the 737, the master FIRE WARN lights: pushing either one:","src":"FCOM 15.20.2","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: What are the levels of warning?","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Cancels both and resets the system","Cancels only that side","Discharges the bottle","Recalls stored faults"],"a":0,"why":"Pushing either cancels both and resets the system for further alerts.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"A simple fault in a redundant 737 system:","src":"FCOM 15.20.1, 15.20.2","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: What are the levels of warning?","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Is stored and shows only on recall","Gives a red warning","Lights MASTER CAUTION immediately","Is not recorded"],"a":0,"why":"Stored; appears only when the annunciator is pushed to recall.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-match-3","type":"match","q":"Match the 737 display colour to its meaning.","src":"FCTM 1.2, 1.3","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: What are the levels of warning?","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Red","First priority (warning)"],["Amber","Second priority (caution)"],["Green","Engaged or active"],["White","Current information"],["Magenta","Targets and FMC path"],["Cyan","Inactive data"]],"why":"FCTM colour philosophy.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"On the 737 displays, magenta indicates:","src":"FCTM 1.2, 1.3","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: What are the levels of warning?","prio":2,"o":["Targets and the FMC path","Engaged modes","Cautions","Inactive data"],"a":0,"why":"Magenta: targets and FMC path; cyan inactive; green engaged.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"What priorities and inhibits exist between the 737's alerting systems?","src":"FCOM 15.20.11, 15.20.25, 15.20.38; FCTM 1.3, 7.19","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: What are the levels of warning?","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- An **actual windshear warning inhibits look-ahead terrain and radio-altitude alerts**.\n- **Predictive windshear is inhibited** by actual windshear, look-ahead terrain or radio-altitude alerts.\n- **GPWS and windshear warnings inhibit all TCAS alerts.**\n- The stick shaker always has priority.\n- Pilots **prioritise warnings over cautions**.\nNote: there is no single complete aural priority table in the available sources.","keys":[{"k":"windshear inhibits terrain","m":["windshear"]},{"k":"PWS inhibited by others","m":["predictive"]},{"k":"GPWS/windshear inhibit TCAS","m":["tcas"]},{"k":"warnings over cautions","m":["warnings over cautions","warnings before cautions"]}],"topic":18},{"id":"t18-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"Describe the 737 FMA.","src":"FCOM 4.20.6; FCOM 4.10.21; FCOM 4.20.4","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: FMA","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Layout:** **autothrottle, roll, pitch** from left to right, above the attitude indicator; **A/P status** below.\n- **Engaged modes** in **large green** letters at the top; **armed modes** in **smaller white** letters below.\n- A **box** around each newly engaged mode for **10 s**; it **flashes for 10 s** when CWS R or CWS P engages.\n- With both F/Ds on, the **master FCC (MA light)** drives both FMAs.","keys":[{"k":"A/T, roll, pitch","m":["autothrottle roll pitch","thrust roll pitch","a t roll pitch"]},{"k":"engaged green","m":["green"]},{"k":"armed white","m":["white"]},{"k":"box 10 s","m":["10 s","10 sec","ten sec","box"]},{"k":"master FCC","m":["master fcc","ma light"]}],"topic":18},{"id":"t18-order-2","type":"order","q":"Order the 737 FMA columns from left to right.","src":"FCOM 4.20.6","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: FMA","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["Autothrottle","Roll","Pitch"],"why":"A/T, roll, pitch; A/P status below.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-num-1","type":"num","q":"For how many seconds is a box drawn around a newly engaged FMA mode?","src":"FCOM 4.10.21","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: FMA","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":10,"unit":"s","why":"10 s (flashing for 10 s for CWS R or CWS P).","topic":18},{"id":"t18-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"On the 737 FMA, armed modes are shown in:","src":"FCOM 4.20.6","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: FMA","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Smaller white letters below the engaged mode","Large green letters","Amber","Cyan"],"a":0,"why":"Engaged: large green at the top; armed: smaller white below.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"What A/P status annunciations appear on the 737 FMA, and what does SINGLE CH mean?","src":"FCOM 4.10.21; FCOM 4.20.5, 4.20.6","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: FMA","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **CMD** and **FD** (green); **CWS P** and **CWS R** (amber); **SINGLE CH**, **LAND 3** and **LAND 2** (green); **NO AUTOLAND** (amber).\n- **LAND 3, LAND 2 and NO AUTOLAND** appear **only on fail-operational aircraft**.\n- **SINGLE CH:** on a **single-A/P ILS** it shows after LOC capture and **stays**; on a **dual-A/P ILS** it **goes out after the pitch monitor test**.","keys":[{"k":"CMD / FD green","m":["cmd"]},{"k":"CWS amber","m":["cws"]},{"k":"LAND 3 / LAND 2 fail-operational","m":["land 3","land 2","fail operational"]},{"k":"SINGLE CH","m":["single ch","single channel"]},{"k":"pitch monitor test","m":["pitch monitor"]}],"topic":18},{"id":"t18-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"LAND 3, LAND 2 and NO AUTOLAND annunciations appear on:","src":"FCOM 4.20.6","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: FMA","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Fail-operational aircraft only","All 737s","Fail-passive aircraft only","Single A/P approaches"],"a":0,"why":"Only on fail-operational aircraft.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"CWS P / CWS R on the FMA are shown in:","src":"FCOM 4.10.21","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: FMA","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Amber","Green","White","Magenta"],"a":0,"why":"CWS P and CWS R are amber; CMD and FD green.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"What is the 737 policy on FMA callouts and MCP selections?","src":"FCOM NP.11.3; FCTM 1.22, 1.45","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: FMA","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Verify every AFDS change on the FMA**; announcing FMA and thrust mode changes is **good CRM practice** (FCOM).\n- **Announce, evaluate and address unexpected FMAs**; the **PM calls based on the FMAs**.\n- With the **A/P engaged, the PF makes MCP selections** and both pilots monitor.\n- Company-specific FMA callout wording is not in the available sources.","keys":[{"k":"verify every change","m":["verify"]},{"k":"good CRM practice","m":["crm"]},{"k":"announce, evaluate, address unexpected","m":["unexpected","announce"]},{"k":"PF makes MCP selections with A/P on","m":["pf makes","pf selects","pilot flying"]}],"topic":18},{"id":"t18-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"What is Boeing's automation philosophy? What do you do if automation doesn't do what you expect?","src":"FCTM 1.4, 1.6, 1.44, 1.45","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: Automation philosophy","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- \"**Use the appropriate level of automation for the situation.**\"\n- If automation doesn't do what you expect, **reduce the level** until the path is under control: **HDG SEL** if it turns the wrong way in the hold, **LVL CHG** if VNAV levels off unexpectedly, **manual flight** for an immediate level-off. **Re-engage** higher levels once control is assured.\n- **Not following the F/D? Turn it off.**\n- Intervention follows **ART: Ask, Recommend, Take control**.","keys":[{"k":"appropriate level","m":["appropriate level"]},{"k":"reduce the level","m":["reduce","lower level","step down"]},{"k":"HDG SEL / LVL CHG examples","m":["hdg sel","heading select","lvl chg","level change"]},{"k":"F/D off if not following","m":["turn it off","fd off","flight director off","switch off"]},{"k":"ART","m":["ask recommend","take control"]}],"topic":18},{"id":"t18-order-3","type":"order","q":"Order the 737 FCTM intervention steps (ART).","src":"FCTM 1.6","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: Automation philosophy","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Ask","Recommend","Take control"],"why":"ART: Ask, Recommend, Take control.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-match-4","type":"match","q":"Unexpected automation: match the problem to the FCTM's suggested lower level.","src":"FCTM 1.44, 1.45","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: Automation philosophy","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["LNAV turns the wrong way in the hold","HDG SEL"],["VNAV levels off unexpectedly","LVL CHG"],["Immediate level-off needed","Manual flight"],["Not following the F/D","Turn the F/D off"]],"why":"Reduce the level of automation until the path is under control.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"What backs up NAV on the 737? What happens with a single and a dual FMC failure?","src":"FCOM 11.30.1, 11.32.7, 11.32.8; QRH 11.4 to 11.6; FCTM 5.41","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: What backs up NAV?","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- The **dual FMC** is certified as a **sole source** navigation system; the second FMC is a full backup.\n- **Single FMC failure:** FMC alert, **SINGLE FMC OPERATION**; select **BOTH ON L or BOTH ON R** to the good FMC.\n- **Dual FMC failure:** **VTK** on both NDs, **LNAV and VNAV disengage**. QRH: resume **conventional navigation**, check position against terrain, set **gross weight, speed and N1 bugs manually**.\n- Without external updates the FMC uses a **corrected IRS position**; use raw VOR, ILS and ADF.","keys":[{"k":"sole source dual FMC","m":["sole source","second fmc","dual fmc"]},{"k":"BOTH ON L / R","m":["both on"]},{"k":"LNAV/VNAV disengage","m":["lnav","vnav"]},{"k":"conventional navigation / raw data","m":["conventional","raw","vor","adf"]},{"k":"bugs set manually","m":["bug","n1","manually"]}],"topic":18},{"id":"t18-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Single FMC failure on the 737: crew action?","src":"FCOM 11.32.7","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: What backs up NAV?","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Select BOTH ON L or BOTH ON R to the good FMC","Revert to conventional navigation immediately","Switch IRS to ATT","Select ALTN on the DPC"],"a":0,"why":"SINGLE FMC OPERATION; select BOTH ON to the good FMC.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"Dual FMC failure: what do you see on the NDs?","src":"FCOM 11.32.8","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: What backs up NAV?","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["VTK, with LNAV and VNAV disengaging","MAP FAIL only","IRS FAULT","Nothing; ISFD takes over"],"a":0,"why":"VTK on both NDs; LNAV and VNAV disengage.","topic":18},{"id":"t18-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"Cruise, FMC message: SINGLE FMC OPERATION. Talk me through it.","src":"FCOM 11.30.1, 11.32.7, 11.32.8; QRH 11.4 to 11.6; FCTM 5.41","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels: What backs up NAV?","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What has happened and what do you do?","a":"One FMC failed; select BOTH ON L or BOTH ON R to the good FMC.","keys":[{"k":"both on","m":["both on"]}]},{"q":"Is the remaining FMC good enough?","a":"Yes: dual FMC is certified sole-source; the second is a full backup.","keys":[{"k":"sole source / full backup","m":["sole source","backup","back up"]}]},{"q":"Now the second FMC fails. Indications?","a":"VTK on both NDs; LNAV and VNAV disengage.","keys":[{"k":"LNAV VNAV disengage","m":["lnav","vnav"]},{"k":"VTK","m":["vtk"]}]},{"q":"Actions?","a":"QRH: conventional navigation, check position against terrain, set GW, speed and N1 bugs manually; raw VOR/ILS/ADF.","keys":[{"k":"conventional nav","m":["conventional","raw"]},{"k":"bugs manually","m":["bug","manual"]}]},{"q":"Where does position come from without updates?","a":"Corrected IRS position; accuracy may degrade.","keys":[{"k":"IRS","m":["irs"]}]}],"topic":18},{"id":"t18-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"Examiner (from an Airbus background): \"Where is your ECAM? What are your warning levels?\"","src":"FCOM 15.20.1, 15.20.2; FCOM 4.20.1, 4.20.6; FCTM 1.2, 1.4","ref":"737 automation, FMA and warning levels","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"Where is the ECAM?","a":"No ECAM/FWC; each system alerts itself via lights, master caution, aurals and display messages; QRH for procedures.","keys":[{"k":"no ECAM","m":["no ecam","no fwc","dont have","don t have","does not have"]},{"k":"QRH","m":["qrh"]}]},{"q":"Levels of warning?","a":"Warning red (immediate), caution amber (timely), advisory amber text, information blue/green.","keys":[{"k":"red warning","m":["red"]},{"k":"amber caution","m":["amber"]}]},{"q":"How is a caution displayed and cancelled?","a":"MASTER CAUTION and system annunciator together; push to cancel, annunciator to recall.","keys":[{"k":"master caution","m":["master caution"]},{"k":"recall","m":["recall"]}]},{"q":"What replaces the FCU and FMGC?","a":"MCP; dual FMC plus FCCs A and B plus autothrottle.","keys":[{"k":"MCP","m":["mcp","mode control"]},{"k":"FMC + FCC","m":["fmc","fcc"]}]},{"q":"How do you confirm automation modes?","a":"FMA: A/T, roll, pitch; engaged green, armed white; verify every change.","keys":[{"k":"FMA","m":["fma"]},{"k":"verify","m":["verify","announce"]}]}],"topic":18},{"id":"t19-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"Define TORA, TODA, ASDA and LDA.","src":"Annex 14 Ch 1; Att A 3.5","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"TORA is the length of runway declared available and suitable for the ground run of an aeroplane taking off.\n- **TODA = TORA + clearway**, if provided.\n- **ASDA = TORA + stopway**, if provided.\n- **LDA** is the runway length available for the landing ground run.\n- A displaced threshold reduces the LDA, but only for approaches made to that threshold.","keys":[{"k":"TORA: ground run","m":["ground run","take off run","takeoff run","take-off run"]},{"k":"TODA = TORA + clearway","m":["plus clearway","+ clearway","tora clearway","and clearway","with clearway"]},{"k":"ASDA = TORA + stopway","m":["plus stopway","+ stopway","tora stopway","and stopway","with stopway"]},{"k":"LDA: landing ground run","m":["landing ground run","landing distance available","landing run"]},{"k":"Displaced threshold reduces LDA","m":["displaced threshold"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"What is a clearway? Give its Annex 14 dimensions and requirements.","src":"Annex 14 Ch 1; 3.6; 9.9","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"A clearway is a defined rectangular area on the ground or water, under the control of the appropriate authority, over which an aeroplane may make part of its initial climb to a specified height.\n- Starts at the end of the TORA.\n- Length not more than half the TORA.\n- At least 75 m each side of the extended centreline, so 150 m wide.\n- Ground must not project above a 1.25% upward plane from the end of the TORA.\n- Objects that may endanger aeroplanes are obstacles and should be removed; essential navaids must be frangible and mounted low.\n- No strength requirement. The specified height is the 35 ft screen height, from the performance rules.","keys":[{"k":"Initial climb to a specified height","m":["initial climb","specified height"]},{"k":"Starts at end of TORA","m":["end of the tora","end of tora","end of take off run"]},{"k":"Max half the TORA","m":["half the tora","half of tora","half tora","50% of tora","half of the tora","half"]},{"k":"150 m wide (75 m each side)","m":["150 m","150m","75 m","75m","150 metres","75 metres"]},{"k":"1.25% slope","m":["1.25"]},{"k":"Under control of the appropriate authority","m":["appropriate authority","under the control","controlled by"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"What is a stopway, and what does Annex 14 require of it?","src":"Annex 14 Ch 1; 3.7","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"A stopway is a defined rectangular area at the end of the TORA, prepared so an aircraft can be stopped in an abandoned take-off.\n- Same width as the runway.\n- Able to support the aeroplane in an abandoned take-off without structural damage.\n- A paved stopway's friction must be at or above that of the runway.\n- It adds to the ASDA, not the TODA. Unlike a clearway, it has a strength requirement.","keys":[{"k":"End of the TORA","m":["end of the tora","end of tora","end of the runway","end of runway"]},{"k":"Stop in an abandoned take-off","m":["abandoned","rejected","aborted","stop"]},{"k":"Same width as runway","m":["same width","width of the runway","runway width"]},{"k":"Supports aircraft without structural damage","m":["structural damage","support the aeroplane","support the aircraft","bear the weight","strength"]},{"k":"Friction not less than runway","m":["friction"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"Maximum length of a clearway?","src":"Annex 14 3.6.2","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Half the TORA","One-third of the TORA","Equal to the stopway","300 m"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 14 3.6.2: clearway length should not exceed half the TORA.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-num-1","type":"num","q":"Minimum total width of a clearway (75 m each side of the extended centreline)?","src":"Annex 14 3.6.3","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":150,"unit":"m","why":"At least 75 m each side of the extended centreline, so 150 m.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-num-2","type":"num","q":"Clearway ground must not rise above an upward plane of what gradient from the end of the TORA?","src":"Annex 14 3.6.4","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":1.25,"unit":"%","why":"Annex 14 3.6.4: plane sloping up at 1.25% from the end of the TORA.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"Width of a stopway?","src":"Annex 14 3.7.1","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Same as the runway","150 m","75 m each side of centreline","Half the runway width"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 14 3.7.1: same width as the runway it is associated with.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"Which of these must be able to support the aeroplane without structural damage?","src":"Annex 14 3.7.3","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Stopway","Clearway","Both stopway and clearway","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"Stopway: 3.7.3. Annex 14 sets no strength requirement for a clearway.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"Where does the 'specified height' over a clearway (35 ft) come from?","src":"Annex 14 Ch 1","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Take-off performance rules (screen height)","Annex 14 clearway definition","Doc 8168 OIS","The 1.25% clearway plane"],"a":0,"why":"The specified height is the 35 ft screen height of take-off performance; Annex 14 does not state it.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-num-3","type":"num","q":"Annex 14 Fig A-1: 2000 m runway, 300 m stopway, 580 m clearway. What is the TODA?","src":"Annex 14 Att A, Fig A-1","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2580,"unit":"m","why":"TODA = TORA + clearway = 2000 + 580.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-num-4","type":"num","q":"Annex 14 Fig A-1: 2000 m runway, 300 m stopway, 580 m clearway. What is the ASDA?","src":"Annex 14 Att A, Fig A-1","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2300,"unit":"m","why":"ASDA = TORA + stopway = 2000 + 300.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match each declared distance to its make-up.","src":"Annex 14 Ch 1","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["TORA","Runway available for take-off ground run"],["TODA","TORA + clearway"],["ASDA","TORA + stopway"],["LDA","Runway available for landing ground run"]],"why":"Annex 14 Ch 1 declared distances.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw the declared distances: runway, stopway and clearway, with TORA, TODA, ASDA and LDA.","src":"Annex 14 Ch 1; Att A Fig A-1","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Runway (TORA) from start to end of the paved runway; stopway beyond it at runway width; clearway starting at the end of the TORA, wider (150 m), able to extend past the stopway. Arrows: TORA = runway; ASDA = TORA + stopway; TODA = TORA + clearway; LDA from the (possibly displaced) threshold. Example from Annex 14 Fig A-1: 2000 m runway, 300 m stopway, 580 m clearway, ASDA 2300 m, TODA 2580 m.","checklist":["TORA drawn as the runway length","Stopway at the end, same width as runway","Clearway starting at end of TORA, 150 m wide","Clearway shown able to extend beyond the stopway","ASDA = TORA + stopway","TODA = TORA + clearway","LDA from threshold, shortened by a displaced threshold"],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"A displaced threshold reduces the LDA for which approaches?","src":"Annex 14 Att A 3.5","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Only approaches made to that threshold","Approaches in both directions","Only non-precision approaches","It never affects LDA"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 14 Att A 3.5: LDA reduced only for approaches to that threshold.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"Describe the colours of runway lighting: threshold, edge, centreline, end and touchdown zone.","src":"Annex 14 Ch 5; OM-A 45.1.17","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Threshold:** fixed green towards the approach, wing bars where needed.\n- **Edge:** variable white, may show yellow over the last 600 m or one-third, whichever is less; red towards the approach before a displaced threshold.\n- **Centreline:** white to 900 m from the end, alternate red and white from 900 m to 300 m, red over the last 300 m.\n- **Runway end:** red.\n- **TDZ:** pairs of white barrettes for 900 m from the threshold.","keys":[{"k":"Threshold green","m":["green"]},{"k":"Edge white, yellow last 600 m","m":["600 m","600m","600 metres","one third","one-third","1/3"]},{"k":"Centreline red/white from 900 m","m":["900 m","900m","900 metres","alternate red and white","red and white","red white"]},{"k":"Centreline red last 300 m","m":["300 m","300m","300 metres","last 300"]},{"k":"End lights red","m":["end red","red end","end lights red","end lights are red","runway end red"]},{"k":"TDZ white barrettes","m":["barrette","touchdown zone","tdz"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"Describe precision approach lighting for CAT I and for CAT II/III.","src":"Annex 14 5.3.4.10, 5.3.4.14, 5.3.4.22, 5.3.4.36; OM-A 7.3","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **CAT I:** variable white centreline over 900 m, with a single crossbar 30 m long at 300 m from the threshold.\n- **CAT II/III:** centreline over 900 m, crossbars at 150 m and 300 m, plus two side rows of red barrettes for the inner 270 m (240 m allowed).\n- Approach light classes for minima (OM-A 7.3): FALS 720 m or more, IALS 420 to 719 m, BALS 210 to 419 m, NALS under 210 m.","keys":[{"k":"900 m centreline","m":["900 m","900m","900 metres"]},{"k":"CAT I crossbar at 300 m","m":["300 m","300m","300 metres"]},{"k":"CAT II/III crossbar at 150 m","m":["150 m","150m","150 metres"]},{"k":"Red side row barrettes","m":["red barrette","side row","red side"]},{"k":"Side rows 270 m","m":["270 m","270m","270 metres"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"Describe taxiway lighting, stop bars and runway guard lights.","src":"Annex 14 5.3.17.7, 5.3.18.7, 5.3.20, 5.3.23","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Taxiway edge:** blue. **Taxiway centreline:** green.\n- **Exit centreline:** alternates green and yellow from the runway to the edge of the ILS critical/sensitive area or the inner transitional surface, whichever is farther, then all green.\n- **Stop bar:** red lights across the taxiway, required at runway-holding positions for RVR below 550 m unless other aids or procedures are used.\n- **Runway guard lights:** two pairs of yellow lights flashing alternately.","keys":[{"k":"Edge blue","m":["blue"]},{"k":"Centreline green","m":["green"]},{"k":"Exit alternate green/yellow","m":["green and yellow","yellow and green","green yellow","alternate"]},{"k":"Stop bar red","m":["red"]},{"k":"Stop bar below RVR 550 m","m":["550"]},{"k":"Guard lights yellow flashing","m":["flashing","yellow"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"How does a PAPI work? What do you see on, above and below the slope?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.26, 5.3.5.25","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"PAPI has 4 units in a wing bar.\n- **On slope:** 2 red (nearest the runway), 2 white.\n- **Slightly high:** 1 red, 3 white. **High:** all white.\n- **Slightly low:** 3 red, 1 white. **Low:** all red.\n- APAPI uses 2 units.","keys":[{"k":"4 units","m":["4 units","four units","4 lights","four lights","four boxes"]},{"k":"On slope 2 red 2 white","m":["2 red 2 white","two red two white","2 red and 2 white","two red and two white"]},{"k":"High: all white / more white","m":["all white","3 white","three white"]},{"k":"Low: all red / more red","m":["all red","3 red","three red"]},{"k":"Red nearest runway","m":["nearest the runway","nearest to the runway","inner","closest to the runway"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"What are the approach light classes used for minima, and their lengths?","src":"OM-A 7.3","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"From the OM-A (not Annex 14):\n- **FALS** (full): 720 m or more.\n- **IALS** (intermediate): 420 to 719 m.\n- **BALS** (basic): 210 to 419 m.\n- **NALS** (no approach lights): less than 210 m.\nThe class sets the minimum RVR for the approach.","keys":[{"k":"FALS 720 m","m":["720"]},{"k":"IALS 420 m","m":["420"]},{"k":"BALS 210 m","m":["210"]},{"k":"NALS below 210 m","m":["nals","no approach light","less than 210","below 210"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"What are the secondary power switch-over times for runway lighting?","src":"Annex 14 Table 8-1","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"- **CAT II/III runway:** 1 s for the inner 300 m of the approach lights, threshold, runway end, centreline and TDZ lights, and all stop bars; 15 s for the rest of the approach lights and the runway edge lights.\n- **CAT I and non-precision:** 15 s throughout.\n- **Take-off runway, RVR below 800 m:** 1 s for runway end, centreline and stop bars; 15 s for edge lights (1 s if there are no centreline lights).","keys":[{"k":"1 second for critical lights","m":["1 s","1s","1 sec","one second","1 second"]},{"k":"15 seconds otherwise","m":["15 s","15s","15 sec","fifteen second","15 second"]},{"k":"Inner 300 m approach lights","m":["300 m","300m","inner 300"]},{"k":"CAT I: 15 s throughout","m":["cat i","cat 1","cat one"]},{"k":"Take-off RVR below 800 m","m":["800"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"Colour of threshold lights seen from the approach?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.10","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Green","White","Red","Yellow"],"a":0,"why":"Fixed unidirectional green towards the approach.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"Colour of runway end lights?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.11","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Red","Green","Yellow","White"],"a":0,"why":"Runway end lights are red; at least six recommended.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"Runway edge lights may show yellow over what portion?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.9.7","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Last 600 m or one-third, whichever is less","Last 900 m","Last 300 m","Last one-half"],"a":0,"why":"Caution zone: last 600 m or one-third of the runway, whichever is less.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Colour of runway edge lights seen from the approach before a displaced threshold?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.9.6","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Red","Green","White","Yellow"],"a":0,"why":"Edge lights between the runway start and a displaced threshold show red towards the approach.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"Runway centreline lights over the last 300 m to the runway end are:","src":"Annex 14 5.3.12.7","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Red","Alternate red and white","White","Yellow"],"a":0,"why":"White to 900 m from the end, alternate red/white 900 to 300 m, red last 300 m.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"Runway centreline lights from 900 m to 300 m from the runway end are:","src":"Annex 14 5.3.12.7","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Alternate red and white","Red","White","Alternate green and yellow"],"a":0,"why":"Alternate red and white from 900 m to 300 m.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"Nominal spacing of runway centreline lights?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.12.5","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["About 15 m","About 60 m","About 7.5 m only","About 50 m"],"a":0,"why":"About 15 m; about 30 m allowed for RVR 350 m or more with proven serviceability; old 7.5 m installations need not be replaced.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"Maximum spacing of runway edge lights on an instrument runway?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.9","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["60 m","100 m","30 m","150 m"],"a":0,"why":"60 m instrument, 100 m non-instrument.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"Taxiway edge lights are:","src":"Annex 14 5.3.17, 5.3.18","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Blue","Green","Yellow","White"],"a":0,"why":"Edge blue, centreline green.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"Taxiway centreline lights are:","src":"Annex 14 5.3.17.7","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Green","Blue","Yellow","White"],"a":0,"why":"Green; exit centreline alternates green and yellow near the runway.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-16","type":"mcq","q":"Runway guard lights are:","src":"Annex 14 5.3.23","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Two pairs of yellow lights flashing alternately","A row of red lights across the taxiway","Steady green lights","Flashing white lights"],"a":0,"why":"Runway guard lights: two pairs of yellow flashing alternately.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-17","type":"mcq","q":"Below what RVR are stop bars required at runway-holding positions (unless other aids/procedures)?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.20","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["550 m","800 m","350 m","175 m"],"a":0,"why":"Required for RVR below 550 m unless other aids or procedures are used.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-18","type":"mcq","q":"PAPI shows 3 red and 1 white. You are:","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.26","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Slightly low","Slightly high","On slope","Well below"],"a":0,"why":"Slightly low: 3 red, 1 white.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-19","type":"mcq","q":"PAPI shows 1 red and 3 white. You are:","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.26","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Slightly high","Slightly low","On slope","Well above"],"a":0,"why":"Slightly high: 1 red, 3 white.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-20","type":"mcq","q":"CAT I precision approach lighting crossbar is at what distance from the threshold?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.4.10","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["300 m","150 m","450 m","900 m"],"a":0,"why":"One 30 m crossbar at 300 m.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-21","type":"mcq","q":"Approach light class for a 500 m system?","src":"OM-A 7.3","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["IALS","FALS","BALS","NALS"],"a":0,"why":"IALS is 420 to 719 m.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-22","type":"mcq","q":"Minimum length for FALS?","src":"OM-A 7.3","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["720 m","900 m","420 m","600 m"],"a":0,"why":"FALS: 720 m or more.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-23","type":"mcq","q":"Secondary power switch-over time for runway edge lights on a CAT II/III runway?","src":"Annex 14 Table 8-1","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["15 s","1 s","5 s","30 s"],"a":0,"why":"Edge lights and outer approach lights: 15 s; critical lights 1 s.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-24","type":"mcq","q":"Touchdown zone lights extend from the threshold for:","src":"Annex 14 5.3.13","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["900 m (to the midpoint if runway under 1800 m)","600 m","300 m","1200 m"],"a":0,"why":"Pairs of white barrettes for 900 m.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-num-5","type":"num","q":"Length of the approach lighting centreline for a precision CAT I system?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.4.10","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":900,"unit":"m","why":"Centreline over 900 m.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-num-6","type":"num","q":"CAT II/III approach lighting red side-row barrettes extend how far from the threshold (standard value)?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.4.22","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":270,"unit":"m","why":"270 m, 240 m allowed.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-num-7","type":"num","q":"Minimum number of runway end lights recommended?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.11","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":6,"unit":"lights","why":"At least six red lights.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match the light to its colour.","src":"Annex 14 Ch 5","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Threshold","Green"],["Runway end","Red"],["Taxiway edge","Blue"],["Taxiway centreline","Green (exit: green/yellow)"],["Runway guard lights","Flashing yellow"],["Runway edge (caution zone)","Yellow"]],"why":"Annex 14 Ch 5 colour scheme.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-match-3","type":"match","q":"Match the PAPI picture to the aircraft position.","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.26","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["4 white","High"],["3 white, 1 red","Slightly high"],["2 white, 2 red","On slope"],["1 white, 3 red","Slightly low"],["4 red","Low"]],"why":"PAPI: red nearest the runway grows as you go low.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-order-1","type":"order","q":"Order the approach light classes from longest to shortest.","src":"OM-A 7.3","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["FALS (720 m or more)","IALS (420 to 719 m)","BALS (210 to 419 m)","NALS (less than 210 m)"],"why":"OM-A 7.3 classes.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-order-2","type":"order","q":"Runway centreline lights colour, from the threshold to the runway end.","src":"Annex 14 5.3.12.7","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["Variable white","Alternate red and white (900 m to 300 m from end)","Red (last 300 m)"],"why":"Distances are counted from the runway end.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"How is a runway designation number derived, and how are parallel runways marked?","src":"Annex 14 5.2.2.4, 5.2.2.5","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"The designation is a two-digit number: the whole number nearest one-tenth of the magnetic bearing of the runway, viewed from the direction of approach. A single digit gets a leading zero (e.g. 09).\n- Two parallel runways add L and R.\n- Three parallel runways add L, C and R.","keys":[{"k":"Two-digit number","m":["two digit","2 digit","two-digit"]},{"k":"Nearest one-tenth of the bearing","m":["one tenth","1/10","tenth","divided by 10","divide by 10","nearest 10"]},{"k":"Magnetic","m":["magnetic"]},{"k":"Leading zero","m":["leading zero","zero","09"]},{"k":"L, C, R for parallels","m":["left","l and r","l c r","lcr","l r","centre","center"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"Describe threshold stripes and the aiming point marking.","src":"Annex 14 5.2.4.4, 5.2.4.5, Table 5-1, 5.2.5.3","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Threshold stripes** start 6 m from the threshold. Number by runway width: 18 m 4, 23 m 6, 30 m 8, 45 m 12, 60 m 16.\n- **Aiming point:** two conspicuous white stripes, starting no closer to the threshold than 150 m (LDA under 800 m), 250 m (800 to under 1200 m), 300 m (1200 to under 2400 m), 400 m (2400 m or more).\n- With a visual approach slope indicator, the aiming point starts at the slope origin.","keys":[{"k":"Stripes start 6 m from threshold","m":["6 m","6m","6 metres","six metres"]},{"k":"45 m runway: 12 stripes","m":["12 stripes","twelve stripes","twelve","45 m 12","45m 12"]},{"k":"Aiming point 400 m for LDA 2400 m+","m":["400 m","400m","400 metres","400"]},{"k":"Two white stripes","m":["two","2 stripes","white"]},{"k":"Starts at VASI/PAPI origin","m":["origin","papi","slope indicator"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"Describe touchdown zone markings.","src":"Annex 14 5.2.6.3, 5.2.6.4","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Touchdown zone markings are pairs of rectangular markings at 150 m spacing from the threshold. The number of pairs depends on LDA:\n- under 900 m: 1; 900 to under 1200 m: 2; 1200 to under 1500 m: 3; 1500 to under 2400 m: 4; 2400 m or more: 6.\n- Pairs within 50 m of the aiming point are left out.","keys":[{"k":"Pairs","m":["pairs","pair"]},{"k":"150 m spacing","m":["150 m","150m","150 metres"]},{"k":"6 pairs for LDA 2400 m+","m":["6 pairs","six pairs","2400"]},{"k":"Depends on LDA","m":["lda","landing distance"]},{"k":"Omitted within 50 m of aiming point","m":["50 m","50m","50 metres","aiming point"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"Describe runway-holding position markings, Pattern A and Pattern B.","src":"Annex 14 5.2.1.5, 5.2.10.3, 5.4.2.15; OM-A 45.1.18","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Taxiway markings are yellow.\n- **Pattern A:** two solid and two dashed lines; the holding position closest to the runway.\n- **Pattern B:** used for the farther holding positions where there are two or three, such as the CAT II/III holding position at a precision approach runway.\n- The associated sign shows the runway designator plus the category, e.g. \"27 CAT II\".","keys":[{"k":"Yellow","m":["yellow"]},{"k":"Pattern A: two solid, two dashed","m":["two solid","2 solid","solid and dashed","solid lines"]},{"k":"Pattern A closest to runway","m":["closest","nearest"]},{"k":"Pattern B: CAT II/III holding position","m":["cat ii","cat 2","cat iii","cat 3","farther","further"]},{"k":"Sign: designator + CAT","m":["27 cat","designator","cat ii sign"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"What other runway markings are there: centreline, side stripes, displaced threshold and chevrons?","src":"Annex 14 5.2.3.3, 5.2.7, 5.2.4.7, 5.2.4.9, 7.3","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Centreline:** white stripes and gaps, stripe plus gap 50 to 75 m.\n- **Side stripes** along each edge.\n- **Displaced threshold:** arrows leading to a white transverse stripe.\n- **Chevrons**, preferably yellow, on a paved pre-threshold area longer than 60 m that aircraft must not use.","keys":[{"k":"Centreline stripe + gap 50 to 75 m","m":["50 to 75","50-75","75 m","75m"]},{"k":"Side stripes","m":["side stripe"]},{"k":"Displaced threshold: arrows","m":["arrow"]},{"k":"Chevrons: unusable pre-threshold area","m":["chevron"]},{"k":"Chevrons yellow","m":["yellow"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-match-4","type":"match","q":"Match runway width to number of threshold stripes.","src":"Annex 14 5.2.4.5","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["18 m","4"],["23 m","6"],["30 m","8"],["45 m","12"],["60 m","16"]],"why":"Annex 14 5.2.4.5.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-match-5","type":"match","q":"Match LDA to the aiming point distance from threshold.","src":"Annex 14 Table 5-1","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Under 800 m","150 m"],["800 to under 1200 m","250 m"],["1200 to under 2400 m","300 m"],["2400 m or more","400 m"]],"why":"Annex 14 Table 5-1.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-match-6","type":"match","q":"Match LDA to the number of touchdown zone marking pairs.","src":"Annex 14 5.2.6.3","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Under 900 m","1"],["900 to under 1200 m","2"],["1200 to under 1500 m","3"],["1500 to under 2400 m","4"],["2400 m or more","6"]],"why":"Annex 14 5.2.6.3, 5.2.6.4.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-num-8","type":"num","q":"Threshold stripes start how far from the threshold?","src":"Annex 14 5.2.4.4","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":6,"unit":"m","why":"6 m from the threshold.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-num-9","type":"num","q":"How many threshold stripes on a 45 m wide runway?","src":"Annex 14 5.2.4.5","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":12,"unit":"stripes","why":"45 m: 12 stripes.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-num-10","type":"num","q":"Aiming point marking starts how far from the threshold on a runway with LDA 3000 m (no VASI)?","src":"Annex 14 Table 5-1","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":400,"unit":"m","why":"LDA 2400 m or more: 400 m.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-num-11","type":"num","q":"Spacing between touchdown zone marking pairs?","src":"Annex 14 5.2.6.3","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":150,"unit":"m","why":"Pairs at 150 m spacing.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-num-12","type":"num","q":"How many pairs of touchdown zone markings for LDA 2400 m or more?","src":"Annex 14 5.2.6.4","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":6,"unit":"pairs","why":"2400 m or more: 6 pairs.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-25","type":"mcq","q":"Runway heading 087° magnetic. Designation?","src":"Annex 14 5.2.2.4","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["09","08","9","87"],"a":0,"why":"Nearest whole number to one-tenth of the magnetic bearing, two digits with a leading zero.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-26","type":"mcq","q":"Which holding position marking is the one closest to the runway?","src":"Annex 14 5.2.10.3","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Pattern A (two solid, two dashed)","Pattern B","Ladder marking","Chevrons"],"a":0,"why":"Pattern A is the holding position closest to the runway; Pattern B the farther ones.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-27","type":"mcq","q":"Chevron markings on a pre-threshold area mean:","src":"Annex 14 7.3","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Paved area over 60 m not to be used by aircraft","Displaced threshold usable for take-off","Stopway","Clearway"],"a":0,"why":"Chevrons (preferably yellow) on a paved pre-threshold area longer than 60 m that aircraft must not use.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-28","type":"mcq","q":"Colour of taxiway markings (including holding position markings)?","src":"Annex 14 5.2.1.5","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Yellow","White","Red","Blue"],"a":0,"why":"Taxiway markings are yellow; runway markings white.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-draw-2","type":"draw","q":"Draw the runway markings on a 3000 m, 45 m wide precision runway.","src":"Annex 14 5.2","ref":"NR25","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Threshold stripes (12, starting 6 m in), designation number, white centreline (stripe + gap 50 to 75 m), side stripes, aiming point (two white stripes at 400 m, or at the PAPI origin), touchdown zone pairs at 150 m spacing (6 pairs, omitting any within 50 m of the aiming point), yellow Pattern A and Pattern B holding positions on the taxiway.","checklist":["12 threshold stripes starting 6 m from threshold","Two-digit designator","Centreline stripes","Aiming point at 400 m (or PAPI origin)","TDZ pairs at 150 m spacing, 6 pairs","Side stripes","Yellow Pattern A nearest the runway, Pattern B farther"],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"Describe T-VASIS: layout and what the pilot sees.","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.4, 5.3.5.7, 5.3.5.9","ref":"NR41","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"T-VASIS has 20 light units: two wing bars of four units, one each side of the runway, each bisected by a longitudinal line of six lights (three fly-up units on the threshold side, three fly-down units beyond the wing bar).\n- **On slope:** white wing bars only.\n- **Above:** wing bars plus 1 to 3 white fly-down lights.\n- **Below:** wing bars plus 1 to 3 white fly-up lights.\n- **Well below:** wing bars and all three fly-up lights red.\n- ICAO recommended discontinuing T-VASIS and AT-VASIS as standard systems from 1 January 2020.","keys":[{"k":"20 units","m":["20 units","20 light","twenty","20 lights"]},{"k":"Wing bars of 4","m":["wing bar"]},{"k":"Fly-up / fly-down lights","m":["fly up","fly-up","fly down","fly-down","flyup","flydown"]},{"k":"On slope: wing bar only","m":["wing bars only","wing bar only","only wing bar","only the wing bar"]},{"k":"Well below: red","m":["red"]},{"k":"Discontinued from 2020","m":["2020","discontinu"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"What is a pulsating VASI (PVASI/PLASI)? Is it an ICAO system?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.2","ref":"NR41","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"It is not an ICAO standard system: Annex 14 lists only T-VASIS, AT-VASIS, PAPI and APAPI.\nIt is a single unit:\n- pulsing white above the path;\n- steady white on the path;\n- steady red slightly below;\n- pulsing red well below.\nUsed mostly at small or military aerodromes.","keys":[{"k":"Not ICAO standard","m":["not an icao","not icao","non icao","not a standard","not standard"]},{"k":"Single unit","m":["single unit","one unit","single light"]},{"k":"Pulsing white above","m":["pulsing white","pulsating white","flashing white"]},{"k":"Steady white on path","m":["steady white"]},{"k":"Red below","m":["steady red","pulsing red","red"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-29","type":"mcq","q":"How many light units in a T-VASIS?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.7","ref":"NR41","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["20","10","4","16"],"a":0,"why":"Two wing bars of 4 plus two lines of 6: 20 units. AT-VASIS has 10.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-30","type":"mcq","q":"How many light units in an AT-VASIS?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.8","ref":"NR41","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["10","20","2","12"],"a":0,"why":"One wing bar of 4 with a longitudinal line of 6, on one side.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-31","type":"mcq","q":"On a T-VASIS you see the white wing bars and two white fly-down lights. You are:","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.9","ref":"NR41","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Above the slope","Below the slope","On slope","Well below"],"a":0,"why":"Fly-down lights show above slope; more the higher you are.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-32","type":"mcq","q":"On a T-VASIS the wing bars and all three fly-up lights show red. You are:","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.9","ref":"NR41","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Well below slope","Slightly below","Well above","On slope"],"a":0,"why":"Well below: wing bars and fly-up lights red.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-33","type":"mcq","q":"Which visual approach slope systems are listed as standard in Annex 14?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.2","ref":"NR41","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["T-VASIS, AT-VASIS, PAPI, APAPI","PAPI, APAPI, PVASI, HAPI","VASI, PLASI, PAPI","PAPI only"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 14 5.3.5.2. Pulsating VASI is not an ICAO standard.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-34","type":"mcq","q":"From what date did ICAO recommend discontinuing T-VASIS and AT-VASIS as standard systems?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.4","ref":"NR41","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["1 January 2020","1 January 2015","1 November 2018","1 January 2025"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 14 5.3.5.4.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-35","type":"mcq","q":"How many units does an APAPI have?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.25","ref":"NR41","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["2","4","3","6"],"a":0,"why":"APAPI: 2 units; PAPI: 4.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-match-7","type":"match","q":"Match the T-VASIS position to the picture.","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.9","ref":"NR41","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Above slope","Wing bars + white fly-down lights"],["On slope","Wing bars only"],["Below slope","Wing bars + white fly-up lights"],["Well below","Wing bars and 3 fly-up lights red"]],"why":"Annex 14 5.3.5.9.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-match-8","type":"match","q":"Match the pulsating VASI signal to the position.","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.2","ref":"NR41","asked":true,"prio":3,"pairs":[["Pulsing white","Above path"],["Steady white","On path"],["Steady red","Slightly below"],["Pulsing red","Well below"]],"why":"Single-unit PVASI; not an ICAO standard system.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-draw-3","type":"draw","q":"Draw a T-VASIS layout.","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5.7","ref":"NR41","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"Two wing bars of four units, one each side of the runway. Through each wing bar runs a longitudinal line of six units: three fly-up units on the threshold side, three fly-down units beyond the wing bar. Total 20 units.","checklist":["Wing bar each side of runway","4 units per wing bar","3 fly-up units on threshold side","3 fly-down units beyond the wing bar","Total 20 units"],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-draw-4","type":"draw","q":"Draw the approach and runway lighting for a CAT II/III runway.","src":"Annex 14 5.3.4.22, 5.3.4.23, 5.3.4.26, 5.3.4.36, 5.3.13.2, 5.3.13.3; OM-A 45.1.17","ref":"OB81","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Approach:** 900 m centreline, lights every 30 m starting 30 m from threshold; crossbars at 150 m and 300 m (the 300 m crossbar 15 m each side of centreline); two side rows of red barrettes over the inner 270 m (240 m allowed).\n- **Threshold:** green.\n- **TDZ:** pairs of white barrettes for 900 m (midpoint if runway under 1800 m), pairs 30 m or 60 m apart, each barrette at least 3 lights no more than 1.5 m apart.\n- **Centreline:** about 15 m spacing; white, then alternate red/white over the last 900 m, red over the last 300 m (counted from the runway end).\n- **Edges:** white, yellow over last 600 m or one-third. **End:** red.","checklist":["900 m approach centreline","Crossbars at 150 m and 300 m","Red side-row barrettes over inner 270 m","Green threshold","TDZ white barrettes for 900 m","Centreline red/white last 900 m, red last 300 m (from far end)","Edge yellow last 600 m or one-third","Red end lights"],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"Describe CAT II/III approach lighting in detail for the drawing.","src":"Annex 14 5.3.4.10, 5.3.4.22, 5.3.4.23, 5.3.4.26, 5.3.4.36","ref":"OB81","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- Centreline 900 m long, lights every 30 m starting 30 m from the threshold.\n- Crossbars at 150 m and 300 m; the 300 m crossbar reaches 15 m each side of the centreline.\n- Two side rows of red barrettes for the inner 270 m (240 m allowed).\n- CAT I by contrast has only the 300 m crossbar, 30 m long.","keys":[{"k":"900 m","m":["900 m","900m","900 metres"]},{"k":"Lights every 30 m","m":["30 m","30m","30 metres","every 30"]},{"k":"Crossbars 150 and 300 m","m":["150 m","150m","150 metres","150"]},{"k":"Red side rows 270 m","m":["270"]},{"k":"CAT I only 300 m crossbar","m":["only the 300","single crossbar","one crossbar","cat i","cat 1"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"Describe touchdown zone lighting on a CAT II/III runway.","src":"Annex 14 5.3.13, 5.3.13.2, 5.3.13.3","ref":"OB81","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- Pairs of white barrettes from the threshold for 900 m, or only to the midpoint on runways under 1800 m.\n- Pairs 30 m or 60 m apart, 30 m advised for low minima.\n- Each barrette at least 3 lights, no more than 1.5 m apart.\n- TDZ lights are required on CAT II/III runways.","keys":[{"k":"White barrettes","m":["white barrette","white","barrette"]},{"k":"900 m","m":["900"]},{"k":"Midpoint if under 1800 m","m":["1800","midpoint","mid point","half"]},{"k":"30 m or 60 m spacing","m":["30 m","30m","60 m","60m"]},{"k":"At least 3 lights per barrette","m":["3 lights","three lights","1.5 m","1.5m"]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-mcq-36","type":"mcq","q":"On the CAT II/III drawing, the 900 m and 300 m centreline colour-code distances are measured from:","src":"OM-A 45.1.17","ref":"OB81","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["The runway end","The threshold","The touchdown zone","The aiming point"],"a":0,"why":"Counted from the far (stop) end: red/white from 900 m to go, red for the last 300 m.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-num-13","type":"num","q":"TDZ barrette pairs on a CAT II/III runway: recommended longitudinal spacing for low minima?","src":"Annex 14 5.3.13.3","ref":"OB81","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":30,"unit":"m","why":"Pairs 30 m or 60 m apart, 30 m advised for low minima.","topic":19},{"id":"t19-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"Draw and explain TORA, TODA and ASDA.\"","src":"Annex 14 Ch 1; 3.6; 3.7; Att A 3.5","ref":"NR19","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"Define TODA and ASDA.","a":"TODA = TORA + clearway; ASDA = TORA + stopway.","keys":[{"k":"TODA + clearway","m":["clearway"]},{"k":"ASDA + stopway","m":["stopway"]}]},{"q":"What are the clearway's dimensions?","a":"Starts at end of TORA, max half the TORA, 150 m wide (75 m each side), 1.25% plane.","keys":[{"k":"Half TORA","m":["half"]},{"k":"150 m wide","m":["150","75"]},{"k":"1.25%","m":["1.25"]}]},{"q":"Does a clearway need to bear the aircraft's weight?","a":"No, Annex 14 sets no strength requirement. A stopway must support the aeroplane without structural damage.","keys":[{"k":"No strength requirement","m":["no strength","no requirement","not required","need not","does not"]},{"k":"Stopway must","m":["stopway"]}]},{"q":"How wide is a stopway, and what about friction?","a":"Same width as the runway; paved stopway friction at or above the runway's.","keys":[{"k":"Same width","m":["same width","runway width"]},{"k":"Friction","m":["friction"]}]},{"q":"What reduces the LDA?","a":"A displaced threshold, only for approaches made to that threshold.","keys":[{"k":"Displaced threshold","m":["displaced threshold"]}]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"You are on short final on a CAT II/III runway at night. Tell me what lights you see.\"","src":"Annex 14 Ch 5, Table 8-1; OM-A 45.1.17","ref":"NR24","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What approach lights?","a":"900 m centreline, crossbars at 150 m and 300 m, red side-row barrettes over the inner 270 m.","keys":[{"k":"900 m","m":["900"]},{"k":"Crossbars 150/300","m":["150","300"]},{"k":"Red side rows","m":["red","side row"]}]},{"q":"Threshold and TDZ?","a":"Green threshold; white TDZ barrettes for 900 m.","keys":[{"k":"Green threshold","m":["green"]},{"k":"TDZ white","m":["white","barrette"]}]},{"q":"Rollout: how do you know the end is coming?","a":"Centreline alternates red and white from 900 m to go, all red the last 300 m; edges yellow over the last 600 m or one-third; red end lights.","keys":[{"k":"Red/white 900 m","m":["900"]},{"k":"Red 300 m","m":["300"]},{"k":"Yellow edges","m":["yellow"]}]},{"q":"Vacating: what lights lead you off?","a":"Exit centreline alternate green and yellow to the edge of the ILS critical/sensitive area, then green; blue edges.","keys":[{"k":"Green/yellow","m":["green and yellow","yellow and green","green yellow","alternate"]},{"k":"Blue edges","m":["blue"]}]},{"q":"If power fails, how quickly are the centreline lights back?","a":"1 s on a CAT II/III runway; edge lights 15 s.","keys":[{"k":"1 s","m":["1 s","1s","one second","1 sec"]}]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t19-chain-3","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"On approach you see a visual slope indicator. Talk me through it.\"","src":"Annex 14 5.3.5","ref":"NR41","asked":true,"prio":2,"steps":[{"q":"PAPI on slope?","a":"4 units: 2 red nearest the runway, 2 white.","keys":[{"k":"2 red 2 white","m":["2 red","two red"]}]},{"q":"Slightly low?","a":"3 red, 1 white; low: all red.","keys":[{"k":"3 red","m":["3 red","three red"]}]},{"q":"What about a T-VASIS?","a":"20 units, wing bars with fly-up and fly-down lights; on slope wing bars only.","keys":[{"k":"20 units","m":["20","twenty"]},{"k":"Wing bar only on slope","m":["wing bar"]}]},{"q":"And a pulsating VASI?","a":"Not ICAO standard; single unit: pulsing white above, steady white on, steady red slightly below, pulsing red well below.","keys":[{"k":"Not ICAO","m":["not icao","not an icao","not standard","non standard"]},{"k":"Pulsing","m":["puls"]}]}],"topic":19},{"id":"t2-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"What is RVSM, why was it introduced and what does it give us?","src":"OM-A 41.1.1, 41.1.2, 41.1.3","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"RVSM is 1000 ft vertical separation between FL290 and FL410 inclusive.\n- **History:** 2000 ft was used above FL290 from the 1950s because of altimeter errors. ICAO's target level of safety is 2.5 x 10^-9 fatal accidents per flight hour.\n- **Introduction:** the NAT first, in 1997; India on 27 Nov 2003.\n- **Benefits:** 6 more levels, more airspace capacity, fuel-optimal levels and less ATC workload.","keys":[{"k":"1000 ft between FL290 and FL410","m":["1000 ft","1000ft","1000 feet","thousand feet"]},{"k":"FL290 to FL410","m":["290"]},{"k":"Upper limit FL410","m":["410"]},{"k":"Previously 2000 ft due to altimeter errors","m":["altimeter error","2000 ft","2000ft","2000 feet"]},{"k":"6 extra levels","m":["6 more","six more","6 extra","six extra","6 additional","six additional"]},{"k":"India 27 Nov 2003","m":["2003"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"When did India introduce RVSM?","src":"OM-A 41.1.3","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["27 Nov 2003","1 Jan 1997","27 Nov 2005","24 Jan 2002"],"a":0,"why":"India introduced RVSM on 27 Nov 2003. The NAT was first, in 1997.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"Which region introduced RVSM first, and when?","src":"OM-A 41.1.3","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","prio":3,"o":["North Atlantic, 1997","Europe, 2002","India, 2003","Pacific, 2000"],"a":0,"why":"The NAT introduced RVSM first in 1997.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"What is ICAO's target level of safety for RVSM?","src":"OM-A 41.1.3","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","prio":3,"o":["2.5 x 10^-9 fatal accidents per flight hour","5 x 10^-9 fatal accidents per flight hour","2.5 x 10^-7 fatal accidents per flight hour","1 x 10^-9 fatal accidents per flight hour"],"a":0,"why":"The TLS is 2.5 x 10^-9 fatal accidents per flight hour.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-1","type":"num","q":"How many extra flight levels does RVSM create between FL290 and FL410?","src":"OM-A 41.1.2; Oral bank p.64","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":6,"unit":"levels","why":"RVSM adds 6 levels: 13 usable levels between FL290 and FL410 instead of 7.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"Differentiate RVSM approved, non-RVSM approved and approved non-RVSM.","src":"OM-A 41.1.5","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Three different statuses:\n- **RVSM approved:** airworthiness approval, crew training, State approval and the required equipment.\n- **Non-RVSM approved:** no approval, or the mandatory equipment has failed.\n- **Approved non-RVSM:** delivery or maintenance ferries, humanitarian, State or military flights. Approval comes from the Watch Supervisory Officer of the entry ACC, requested 4 to 72 h before departure. File item 18 STS/.../APVD NON-RVSM. ATC applies 2000 ft separation.","keys":[{"k":"Approved = airworthiness + training + State approval + equipment","m":["state approval","airworthiness","crew training"]},{"k":"Non-RVSM approved = no approval or equipment failed","m":["equipment failed","failed","no approval"]},{"k":"Approved non-RVSM = ferry, humanitarian, State/military","m":["ferry","humanitarian","military","state aircraft"]},{"k":"Watch Supervisory Officer of entry ACC","m":["watch supervisory","supervisory officer","wso"]},{"k":"4 to 72 h before departure","m":["72 h","72h","72 hours","seventy two"]},{"k":"2000 ft separation","m":["2000 ft","2000ft","2000 feet"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match each RVSM status to its meaning.","src":"OM-A 41.1.5","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["RVSM approved","Airworthiness, crew training, State approval and equipment"],["Non-RVSM approved","No approval, or mandatory equipment failed"],["Approved non-RVSM","Ferry, humanitarian, State or military flight cleared through RVSM with 2000 ft"]],"why":"These three terms are a favourite trap; approved non-RVSM aircraft get 2000 ft separation.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"How far ahead must an approved non-RVSM flight request its approval?","src":"OM-A 41.1.5","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","prio":3,"o":["4 to 72 h before departure","1 to 24 h before departure","At least 7 days before","2 to 48 h before departure"],"a":0,"why":"Request it from the Watch Supervisory Officer of the entry ACC 4 to 72 h before departure.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"How does an approved non-RVSM flight show its status in the flight plan?","src":"OM-A 41.1.5","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Item 18 STS/.../APVD NON-RVSM","Item 10 W","Item 18 RMK/NONRVSM","Item 10 R"],"a":0,"why":"Item 18 STS/.../APVD NON-RVSM. Item 10 W is for RVSM-approved aircraft.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"What do you file in the flight plan to show RVSM approval?","src":"OM-A 41.1.6.1","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Item 10: W","Item 10: R","Item 18: RVSM/YES","Item 10: Y"],"a":0,"why":"An approved aircraft files W in item 10. R is PBN approval.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-2","type":"num","q":"What vertical separation does ATC apply to an approved non-RVSM aircraft in RVSM airspace?","src":"OM-A 41.1.5","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2000,"unit":"ft","why":"Approved non-RVSM flights get 2000 ft.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-3","type":"num","q":"RVSM altitude alerting tolerance on a newer type certificate is plus or minus how many feet?","src":"OM-A 41.2","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":200,"unit":"ft","why":"Altitude alert is +/-200 ft on newer type certificates, +/-300 ft on older ones.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-4","type":"num","q":"RVSM altitude alerting tolerance on an OLDER type certificate is plus or minus how many feet?","src":"OM-A 41.2","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":300,"unit":"ft","why":"+/-300 ft on older type certificates; +/-200 ft on newer ones.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-5","type":"num","q":"The automatic altitude control system must hold the level within plus or minus how many feet?","src":"OM-A 41.2","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":50,"unit":"ft","why":"Automatic altitude control holds +/-50 ft in straight and level flight.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-6","type":"num","q":"On the ground before an RVSM flight, altimeters set to QNH must read the known elevation within how many feet?","src":"OM-A 41.1.6.2","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":75,"unit":"ft","why":"Ground check: within 75 ft of the known elevation on QNH.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-7","type":"num","q":"In RVSM cruise the two primary altimeters must agree within how many feet?","src":"OM-A 41.1.6.3","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":200,"unit":"ft","why":"Checked about hourly: primaries within 200 ft.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-8","type":"num","q":"What is the level overshoot limit in RVSM airspace?","src":"OM-A 41.1.6.3","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","prio":3,"ans":150,"unit":"ft","why":"The overshoot limit is 150 ft.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"What altimeter checks do you do before and during RVSM?","src":"OM-A 41.1.6.2, 41.1.6.3","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **On the ground:** set QNH; altimeters must show the known elevation within 75 ft.\n- **Near entry:** record the initial primary and standby readings.\n- **In flight, about hourly:** the two primary altimeters must agree within 200 ft.\n- **Level-off:** overshoot limit 150 ft.\n- If the primaries disagree by more than 200 ft, tell ATC \"UNABLE RVSM DUE EQUIPMENT\".","keys":[{"k":"Ground: 75 ft of elevation","m":["75 ft","75ft","75 feet","seventy five"]},{"k":"In flight: primaries within 200 ft","m":["200 ft","200ft","200 feet"]},{"k":"About hourly","m":["hourly","every hour","one hour","1 hour"]},{"k":"Record primary and standby near entry","m":["standby","record"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"Define TVE, ASE, FTE and AAD, and which values are reportable.","src":"OM-A 41.3","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **TVE (total vertical error) = ASE + FTE.**\n- **ASE:** altimetry system error.\n- **FTE:** flight technical error.\n- **AAD:** assigned altitude deviation.\n- **Reportable height-keeping errors:** TVE 300 ft or more, ASE 245 ft or more, AAD 300 ft or more.\n- **Envelopes:** basic, ASE mean 80 ft or less and mean + 3 SD 200 ft or less; full, mean 120 ft or less and mean + 3 SD 245 ft or less.","keys":[{"k":"TVE = ASE + FTE","m":["ase plus fte","ase + fte","ase fte","ase and fte"]},{"k":"AAD = assigned altitude deviation","m":["assigned altitude deviation"]},{"k":"TVE 300 ft","m":["300"]},{"k":"ASE 245 ft","m":["245"]},{"k":"Basic and full envelopes","m":["envelope","basic","full"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match each height-keeping error to its reportable threshold.","src":"OM-A 41.3","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["TVE","300 ft or more"],["ASE","245 ft or more"],["AAD","300 ft or more"]],"why":"TVE = ASE + FTE. ASE 245 ft is the odd one out.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"What is the RVSM 'full envelope' ASE requirement?","src":"OM-A 41.3","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Mean 120 ft or less, mean + 3 SD 245 ft or less","Mean 80 ft or less, mean + 3 SD 200 ft or less","Mean 50 ft or less, mean + 3 SD 150 ft or less","Mean 200 ft or less, mean + 3 SD 300 ft or less"],"a":0,"why":"Basic: 80 ft and 200 ft. Full: 120 ft and 245 ft.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"What is the RVSM 'basic envelope' ASE requirement?","src":"OM-A 41.3","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Mean 80 ft or less, mean + 3 SD 200 ft or less","Mean 120 ft or less, mean + 3 SD 245 ft or less","Mean 75 ft or less, mean + 3 SD 300 ft or less","Mean 100 ft or less, mean + 3 SD 250 ft or less"],"a":0,"why":"Basic: ASE mean 80 ft or less, mean + 3 SD 200 ft or less.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-9","type":"num","q":"In RVSM, within how many feet of level-off do you reduce vertical speed to 1500 fpm to avoid nuisance TCAS RAs?","src":"OM-A 41.1.9.5","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2000,"unit":"ft","why":"Reduce to 1500 fpm within 2000 ft of level-off.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Who monitors RVSM height-keeping in this region, and how?","src":"OM-A 41.1.8","ref":"Key facts: RVSM","prio":3,"o":["MAAR Bangkok, using ground HMUs or portable GMUs","EUROCONTROL, using Mode S only","AAI Delhi, using radar altitude reports","ICAO Montreal, using ADS-C reports"],"a":0,"why":"MAAR in Bangkok, with ground height-monitoring units (HMU) or portable GPS monitoring units (GMU).","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-10","type":"num","q":"Radio failure in IMC, surveillance (radar) airspace: how long do you keep the last speed and level?","src":"OM-A 5.2; Doc 4444 15.3.3","ref":"Key facts: Contingencies and communication failure","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":7,"unit":"min","why":"7 min from the latest of reaching the level, setting 7600 or missing a report; then the filed flight plan.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-11","type":"num","q":"Radio failure in IMC, non-surveillance airspace: how long do you keep the last speed and level after failing to report over a compulsory point?","src":"OM-A 5.2; Doc 4444 15.3.3","ref":"Key facts: Contingencies and communication failure","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":20,"unit":"min","why":"20 min, then the filed flight plan.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-12","type":"num","q":"After radio failure, you must land within how many minutes of the ETA or EAT, whichever is later?","src":"OM-A 5.2","ref":"Key facts: Contingencies and communication failure","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":30,"unit":"min","why":"Land within 30 min of ETA or EAT, whichever is later.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-order-1","type":"order","q":"Radio failure, IMC: put the arrival actions in order.","src":"OM-A 5.2","ref":"NR26","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Proceed to the destination aid and hold","Begin descent at the last acknowledged EAT, or the flight-plan ETA","Fly the normal instrument approach","Land within 30 min of ETA or EAT, whichever is later"],"why":"The Doc 4444 15.3.3 / OM-A 5.2 arrival sequence.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"In radar airspace after radio failure, the 7 min starts from the latest of which events?","src":"OM-A 5.2","ref":"Key facts: Contingencies and communication failure","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Reaching the level, setting 7600, or missing a compulsory report","Setting 7600 only","The last ATC transmission received","Passing the next significant point"],"a":0,"why":"The latest of reaching the level, setting 7600 or missing a report.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-13","type":"num","q":"Oceanic weather deviation without clearance: by how many feet do you change level?","src":"OM-A 41.1.9.3","ref":"Key facts: Contingencies and communication failure","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":300,"unit":"ft","why":"Change level by 300 ft once more than 5 NM off track.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-14","type":"num","q":"Per your OM-A, beyond how many NM off track do you make the 300 ft level change during a weather deviation without clearance?","src":"OM-A 41.1.9.3, 17.3.7.2","ref":"Key facts: Contingencies and communication failure","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"ans":5,"unit":"NM","why":"OM-A 41.1.9.3 says more than 5 NM. Your Doc 4444 copy (16th ed, 15.2.3.3) says 10 NM, the older value.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-match-3","type":"match","q":"Weather deviation without clearance (oceanic, more than 5 NM off track): match the case to the action.","src":"OM-A 41.1.9.3","ref":"Key facts: Contingencies and communication failure","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Track 000 to 179, deviating left","Descend 300 ft"],["Track 000 to 179, deviating right","Climb 300 ft"],["Track 180 to 359, deviating left","Climb 300 ft"],["Track 180 to 359, deviating right","Descend 300 ft"]],"why":"Eastbound (000 to 179): left down, right up. Westbound: left up, right down.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-15","type":"num","q":"SLOP: maximum offset where route spacing is 23 NM or more?","src":"Doc 4444 16.5.2","ref":"Key facts: Contingencies and communication failure (SLOP)","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2,"unit":"NM","why":"Up to 2 NM right of centreline, in tenths of a NM.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"SLOP where route spacing is between 6 and 23 NM: what is the maximum offset?","src":"Doc 4444 16.5.2","ref":"Key facts: Contingencies and communication failure (SLOP)","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["0.5 NM right","1 NM right","2 NM right","0.5 NM left or right"],"a":0,"why":"Up to 0.5 NM, right of centreline only.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-match-4","type":"match","q":"Match the squawk or frequency to its use.","src":"OM-A 17.54","ref":"Key facts: Contingencies and communication failure","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["7500","Unlawful interference"],["7600","Communication failure"],["7700","Emergency"],["121.5 MHz","VHF emergency frequency"],["2182 kHz","HF/MF emergency frequency"],["243.0 MHz","UHF (military) emergency frequency"]],"why":"Emergency frequencies 121.5 MHz, 243.0 MHz, 2182 kHz.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between MAYDAY and PAN PAN, and where do you find other States' contingency procedures?","src":"OM-A 17.54","ref":"Key facts: Contingencies and communication failure","prio":3,"a":"- **MAYDAY:** distress, grave and imminent danger, needing immediate assistance.\n- **PAN PAN:** urgency.\n- **Codes:** 7700 emergency, 7600 comm failure, 7500 unlawful interference.\n- **Frequencies:** 121.5 MHz, 243.0 MHz, 2182 kHz.\n- **Other States:** contingency procedures are in the Jeppesen ATC and Emergency pages.","keys":[{"k":"MAYDAY = grave and imminent danger","m":["grave","imminent"]},{"k":"PAN PAN = urgency","m":["urgency"]},{"k":"Squawk 7700","m":["7700"]},{"k":"121.5","m":["121.5","1215","121 decimal 5"]},{"k":"Jeppesen ATC / Emergency pages","m":["jeppesen"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"When do you tell ATC 'UNABLE RVSM', and what does ATC then do?","src":"OM-A 41.1.7, 41.1.9, 41.3.5.2","ref":"NR7","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Call \"UNABLE RVSM DUE EQUIPMENT\" (or \"TURBULENCE\") as soon as you can no longer meet the requirements, for example:\n- failure of all automatic altitude control;\n- loss of altimeter redundancy;\n- primary altimeters more than 200 ft apart;\n- loss of thrust that forces a descent;\n- greater than moderate turbulence.\n\nATC then applies 2000 ft separation or moves you out of RVSM airspace. In severe turbulence also watch TCAS and turn the lights on.","keys":[{"k":"Unable RVSM due equipment/turbulence","m":["unable rvsm"]},{"k":"Loss of automatic altitude control","m":["altitude control","autopilot","altitude hold"]},{"k":"Loss of altimeter redundancy / split over 200 ft","m":["redundancy","200 ft","200ft","200 feet","altimeter"]},{"k":"Engine/thrust loss forcing descent","m":["thrust","engine"]},{"k":"Greater than moderate turbulence","m":["turbulence"]},{"k":"ATC applies 2000 ft or moves you out","m":["2000 ft","2000ft","2000 feet","out of rvsm"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"Weather deviation in oceanic RVSM airspace without a clearance: what do you do?","src":"OM-A 41.1.9.3; Doc 4444 15.2.3.3","ref":"NR7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Get a clearance first:** open with \"WEATHER DEVIATION REQUIRED\" (or PAN PAN) and keep trying.\n- **If none:** once more than 5 NM off track, change level by 300 ft. Below that, stay at the assigned level.\n  - Track 000 to 179: left, descend; right, climb.\n  - Track 180 to 359: left, climb; right, descend.\n- **Returning:** be back at the assigned level within about 10 NM of the centreline.\n- **Version note:** my Doc 4444 copy (15.2.3.3, Table 15-1) triggers the level change at 10 NM, the older value; OM-A uses 5 NM.","keys":[{"k":"Weather deviation required / PAN","m":["weather deviation required","pan pan"]},{"k":"More than 5 NM off track","m":["5 nm","5nm","5 nautical","five nautical","five nm"]},{"k":"300 ft level change","m":["300"]},{"k":"000 to 179 left descend, right climb","m":["left descend","descend left","left down"]},{"k":"Back at level within about 10 NM","m":["10 nm","10nm","10 nautical","ten nautical"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["10 nm off track","more than 10 nm"],"why":"Older Doc 4444 trigger; current OM-A 41.1.9.3 is more than 5 NM off track"}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"You cannot maintain your assigned level in oceanic airspace (engine failure or depressurisation) and cannot get a revised clearance. What does Doc 4444 say?","src":"Doc 4444 15.2.2.3, 15.2.2.4; OM-A 41.3.5.2, 17.3.7.2","ref":"NR7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"First try for a revised clearance with MAYDAY or PAN PAN. If not possible, Doc 4444 15.2.2.3 (my 16th edition copy):\n- Turn **at least 45°** left or right to acquire a same- or opposite-direction track offset **15 NM** from the centreline. Choose the side by the track system, the alternate, terrain, any SLOP and adjacent levels.\n- If you can't hold the level, minimise the descent rate and pick a level 500 ft off the normal levels (1000 ft above FL410). If you can, change by 500 ft once 10 NM off track.\n- Broadcast on 121.5 (backup 123.45), watch ACAS, all exterior lights and transponder on.\n- Later amendments: 30° and 5 NM. Quote the Jeppesen ATC pages version.","keys":[{"k":"Turn at least 45 degrees","m":["45 deg","forty five"]},{"k":"Offset 15 NM","m":["15 nm","15nm","15 nautical","fifteen"]},{"k":"Level 500 ft off normal levels","m":["500"]},{"k":"Broadcast 121.5 / 123.45","m":["121.5","1215","123.45","12345"]},{"k":"Lights, transponder, ACAS","m":["lights","tcas","acas"]},{"k":"Later amendment 30 degrees / 5 NM","m":["30 deg","thirty deg","5 nm","5nm"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"Doc 4444 oceanic contingency (cannot maintain level): which figures are in your 16th edition copy, Chapter 15 dated 10 Nov 2016?","src":"Doc 4444 15.2.2.3","ref":"NR7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["Turn at least 45°, offset 15 NM","Turn at least 30°, offset 5 NM","Turn 90°, offset 25 NM","Turn at least 30°, offset 15 NM"],"a":0,"why":"The 16th edition copy has 45° and 15 NM; later amendments reduced it to 30° and 5 NM. Quote the version in your Jeppesen ATC pages.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"Wake turbulence, severe turbulence and radio failure in RVSM: what changes?","src":"OM-A 41.1.9.1, 41.1.9.2; Doc 4444 16.5","ref":"NR7","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Wake turbulence (oceanic):** offset up to 2 NM and tell ATC (OM-A 41.1.9.2). Doc 4444 SLOP: right of centreline only, tenths of a NM, up to 2 NM where spacing is 23 NM or more, 0.5 NM where 6 to 23 NM; crew decides, no need to tell ATC for SLOP itself.\n- **Severe turbulence:** tell ATC, watch TCAS, lights on; ATC applies 2000 ft.\n- **Radio failure:** no change to the RCF procedure; ATC applies 2000 ft.","keys":[{"k":"Wake: offset up to 2 NM","m":["2 nm","2nm","2 nautical","two nautical","two nm"]},{"k":"Tell ATC for wake offset","m":["tell atc","inform atc","advise atc"]},{"k":"Severe turbulence: 2000 ft","m":["2000"]},{"k":"TCAS and lights","m":["tcas","lights"]},{"k":"Radio failure: normal RCF, 2000 ft","m":["radio failure","rcf","no change"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw the weather deviation level-change rule for an eastbound track (000 to 179) and a westbound track (180 to 359).","src":"OM-A 41.1.9.3","ref":"NR7","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Two tracks drawn as arrows, each with a 5 NM band either side:\n- **Eastbound (000 to 179):** deviation to the LEFT (north side) labelled \"descend 300 ft\"; deviation to the RIGHT labelled \"climb 300 ft\".\n- **Westbound (180 to 359):** LEFT labelled \"climb 300 ft\"; RIGHT labelled \"descend 300 ft\".\n- Inside 5 NM of centreline: \"stay at assigned level\".\n- Return: \"back at level within about 10 NM of centreline\".","checklist":["Two tracks, eastbound 000 to 179 and westbound 180 to 359","5 NM trigger band marked each side","000 to 179: left descend, right climb","180 to 359: left climb, right descend","300 ft level change labelled","Assigned level inside 5 NM; back on level within about 10 NM on return"],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-draw-2","type":"draw","q":"Draw the Doc 4444 contingency when you cannot maintain level on an oceanic route.","src":"Doc 4444 15.2.2.3","ref":"NR7","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Plan view of the original track with an arrow turning off by at least 45° (16th edition copy) and joining a parallel offset track 15 NM from centreline (same or opposite direction). Label: \"later amendments: 30° / 5 NM\". Side view: the new level 500 ft off the normal levels (1000 ft above FL410). Annotations: broadcast 121.5 (123.45 backup), lights, transponder, watch ACAS; choose side by track system, alternate, terrain, SLOP and adjacent levels.","checklist":["Original centreline and turn-off of at least 45°","Offset track 15 NM from centreline (same or opposite direction)","Note of 30° / 5 NM later amendment","Level 500 ft off normal levels (1000 ft above FL410)","121.5 / 123.45 broadcast, lights, transponder, ACAS","Factors for choosing the side"],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"What airspaces are there in India?","src":"AIP India ENR 1.4; OM-A 17.3.1.6, 41.1.3","ref":"NR11","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Classes (AIP India ENR 1.4):** C, designated parts of controlled airspace; D, the rest of controlled airspace (CTRs, TMAs, CTAs); E, ATS route segments outside controlled airspace; F, designated ATS route segments outside controlled airspace; G, everything else. A and B are \"under consideration\".\n- **Four FIRs:** Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai; the last three include oceanic airspace.\n- **Special use:** Prohibited, Restricted and Danger areas (ENR 5.1); conditional routes open only in published hours.\n- **RVSM:** FL290 to FL410.\n- **AIX:** no operations to uncontrolled aerodromes without DFO approval.","keys":[{"k":"Class C and D controlled","m":["class c","class d"]},{"k":"E, F and G","m":["class g","class e","class f"]},{"k":"A and B not implemented","m":["under consideration","not implemented","a and b"]},{"k":"Four FIRs","m":["four fir","4 fir","delhi mumbai kolkata chennai"]},{"k":"Prohibited, restricted, danger","m":["prohibited","restricted","danger"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-match-5","type":"match","q":"Match the Indian airspace class to its use (AIP India ENR 1.4).","src":"AIP India ENR 1.4","ref":"NR11","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["C","Designated parts of controlled airspace"],["D","Rest of controlled airspace (CTRs, TMAs, CTAs)"],["E","ATS route segments outside controlled airspace"],["F","Designated ATS route segments outside controlled airspace"],["G","Everything else"],["A and B","Under consideration, not implemented"]],"why":"India uses C to G; A and B are not yet implemented.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-match-6","type":"match","q":"Match the Indian FIR to its ICAO code.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR11","asked":true,"prio":3,"pairs":[["Delhi","VIDF"],["Mumbai","VABF"],["Kolkata","VECF"],["Chennai","VOMF"]],"why":"Mumbai, Kolkata and Chennai include oceanic airspace.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"What instrumentation is required for RVSM?","src":"OM-A 41.2","ref":"NR21","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Two independent altitude measurement systems:** cross-coupled static source with ice protection, display, digital encoded output, SSEC/PEC, outputs to autopilot and alerting.\n- **One SSR transponder with altitude reporting** (Mode C/S), fed from the altimetry system in use.\n- **Altitude alerting:** +/-300 ft older type certificates, +/-200 ft newer.\n- **Automatic altitude control:** holds +/-50 ft.\n- **ACAS II**, listed \"to improve safety\".","keys":[{"k":"Two independent altimetry systems","m":["two independent","2 independent","two altimet","2 altimet"]},{"k":"SSR transponder with altitude reporting","m":["transponder","mode c","mode s"]},{"k":"Altitude alert 200/300 ft","m":["alert"]},{"k":"Altitude hold +/-50 ft","m":["50 ft","50ft","50 feet","fifty feet"]},{"k":"ACAS II","m":["acas","tcas"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"What is the minimum equipment at RVSM entry, and what if something fails before entry?","src":"OM-A 41.3.4, 41.2","ref":"NR21","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Required at entry:** 2 primary altimetry systems, 1 automatic altitude control system and 1 altitude alerting system.\n- If any of these fails before entry, get a new clearance to avoid RVSM airspace.\n- ACAS II is in the CAR list \"to improve safety\" but is not in the entry list.\n- **Checks:** on the ground, altimeters within 75 ft of known elevation; in flight, primaries within 200 ft.","keys":[{"k":"2 altimetry systems","m":["2 primary","two primary","2 altimet","two altimet"]},{"k":"1 altitude control","m":["altitude control","autopilot","altitude hold"]},{"k":"1 alerting system","m":["alerting"]},{"k":"New clearance to avoid RVSM","m":["new clearance","avoid rvsm","re clearance","reclearance"]},{"k":"ACAS not in entry list","m":["acas","tcas"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"If you can't maintain RVSM, what is the RT, and how do you get back in?","src":"OM-A 41.1.7, 41.1.6.1","ref":"NR22","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Telling ATC:** \"(Callsign) UNABLE RVSM DUE EQUIPMENT\" or \"UNABLE RVSM DUE TURBULENCE\".\n- **ATC check:** \"CONFIRM RVSM APPROVED\"; reply \"AFFIRM RVSM\" or \"NEGATIVE RVSM\" (State aircraft add \"STATE AIRCRAFT\").\n- **ATC refusal:** \"UNABLE ISSUE CLEARANCE INTO RVSM AIRSPACE, MAINTAIN FL...\".\n- **Getting back in:** \"READY TO RESUME RVSM\"; ATC may ask \"REPORT ABLE TO RESUME RVSM\".\n- **Flight plan:** an approved aircraft files W in item 10.","keys":[{"k":"Unable RVSM due equipment","m":["unable rvsm"]},{"k":"Affirm RVSM / Negative RVSM","m":["affirm rvsm","negative rvsm"]},{"k":"Confirm RVSM approved","m":["confirm rvsm"]},{"k":"Ready to resume RVSM","m":["ready to resume","resume rvsm"]},{"k":"Item 10 W","m":["item 10"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-match-7","type":"match","q":"Match the RVSM phraseology to who says it.","src":"OM-A 41.1.7","ref":"NR22","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["UNABLE RVSM DUE EQUIPMENT","Pilot, cannot meet RVSM"],["CONFIRM RVSM APPROVED","ATC, checking status"],["AFFIRM RVSM / NEGATIVE RVSM","Pilot, reply to the status check"],["UNABLE ISSUE CLEARANCE INTO RVSM AIRSPACE, MAINTAIN FL...","ATC, refusal"],["READY TO RESUME RVSM","Pilot, requesting re-entry"]],"why":"Standard RVSM RT from OM-A 41.1.7.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"Radio failure: what do you do first, and what is the procedure in radar and non-radar airspace?","src":"OM-A 5.2; Doc 4444 15.3.3","ref":"NR26","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **First:** squawk 7600; try the other radio and the previous frequency, 121.5, a relay through another aircraft, and ACARS/company.\n- **VMC:** stay VMC, land at the nearest suitable aerodrome, report arrival.\n- **IMC, radar:** keep the last speed and level (or minimum flight altitude if higher) for **7 min** from the latest of reaching the level, setting 7600 or missing a report; then the filed flight plan. If vectored, rejoin the route by the next significant point.\n- **IMC, non-radar:** keep them for **20 min** after missing a compulsory report, then the flight plan.","keys":[{"k":"Squawk 7600","m":["7600"]},{"k":"Try other means: 121.5, relay, ACARS","m":["121.5","1215","relay","acars"]},{"k":"VMC: land nearest suitable","m":["nearest suitable","remain vmc","stay vmc"]},{"k":"Radar: 7 min","m":["7 min","7min","seven min"]},{"k":"Non-radar: 20 min","m":["20 min","20min","twenty min"]},{"k":"Vectored: rejoin by next significant point","m":["significant point","rejoin"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"Radio failure: what is the arrival procedure?","src":"OM-A 5.2; Doc 4444 15.3.3","ref":"NR26","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"1. Proceed to the destination aid and hold.\n2. Begin descent at the last acknowledged EAT, or the flight-plan ETA if no EAT.\n3. Fly the normal instrument approach.\n4. Land within 30 min of ETA or EAT, whichever is later.\n\nThis is word for word Doc 4444 15.3.3.","keys":[{"k":"Hold over destination aid","m":["hold"]},{"k":"Descent at EAT or ETA","m":["expected approach","eat or","at eat","last eat","at the eta"]},{"k":"Normal instrument approach","m":["instrument approach","normal approach"]},{"k":"Land within 30 min","m":["30 min","30min","thirty min"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"Radio failure during a SID: what do you do?","src":"OM-A 5.2; Doc 4444 6.3.2.5, 15.3.3","ref":"NR26","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- Continue the SID as cleared, laterally and with its altitude restrictions, to its end.\n- Then apply the radar (7 min) or non-radar (20 min) rule and climb to the flight-planned level after that time.\n- Many Indian SIDs and airports publish their own RCF instructions on the chart or in AIP AD 2.22. Doc 4444 6.3.2.5 says the action is set by regional agreement and published in the SID or AIP, and those override the general rule.\n- So check the chart notes and the Jeppesen ATC pages for India.","keys":[{"k":"Continue SID with restrictions","m":["continue the sid","continue sid","complete the sid","follow the sid"]},{"k":"Then 7 / 20 min rule","m":["7 min","20 min","7min","20min"]},{"k":"Climb to flight-planned level","m":["flight plan level","flight planned level","planned level"]},{"k":"Chart / AIP AD 2.22 RCF overrides","m":["ad 2.22","ad 222","chart","aip"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"Weather ahead in RVSM airspace, Delhi to Kolkata, ATC not responding. What do you do?","src":"OM-A 41.1.9.3, 17.3.7.2; Doc 4444 15.2.3.3","ref":"S7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"1. **Try hard for a clearance:** other frequencies, previous or next sector, 121.5, relay.\n2. **If none, PIC's emergency authority:** declare PAN or MAYDAY; broadcast position and intentions on 121.5 and 123.45; watch TCAS; exterior lights on.\n3. **Level change once more than 5 NM off track.** Delhi to Kolkata is about 110°M, so 000 to 179: deviate left (north), descend 300 ft; right (south), climb 300 ft. (My Doc 4444 copy says 10 NM.)\n4. **Return** to track and level when clear; tell ATC when contact is regained.\n\nOn a radar continental route the table is only the no-comms fallback.","keys":[{"k":"Try for clearance: 121.5, relay","m":["121.5","1215","relay"]},{"k":"PAN or MAYDAY","m":["pan pan","mayday"]},{"k":"Broadcast 121.5 and 123.45","m":["123.45","12345"]},{"k":"More than 5 NM: 300 ft","m":["300"]},{"k":"Left descend, right climb (track 110)","m":["left descend","descend left","north descend","left down"]},{"k":"Lights and TCAS","m":["lights","tcas"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"In the Doc 4444 contingency your alternate lies more than 30° off track. Can you turn further?","src":"Doc 4444 15.2.2.3, 15.2.2.3.1; OM-A 17.3.7.2","ref":"S8","trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Yes. The turn is a minimum, not a maximum.**\n- My 16th edition copy says turn at least 45° to an offset of 15 NM; later amendments reduced this to at least 30° and 5 NM.\n- For an opposite-direction offset the turn may continue beyond 180° to re-intercept the offset track (15.2.2.3.1).\n- Then turn towards the alternate, keeping clear of the track system, watching TCAS, lights on and listening on 121.5.\n- For weather, deviate as far as needed; 300 ft level change once more than 5 NM off track.\n- Quote the version in the Jeppesen ATC pages.","keys":[{"k":"Yes, the turn is a minimum","m":["minimum","at least"]},{"k":"Beyond 180° for opposite direction","m":["180"]},{"k":"45/15 vs 30/5 versions","m":["45 deg","forty five","15 nm","30 deg","5 nm"]},{"k":"Keep clear of track system","m":["track system","clear of"]},{"k":"TCAS, lights, 121.5","m":["tcas","lights","121.5","1215"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"One autopilot is inoperative before entering RVSM airspace. Can you enter?","src":"OM-A 41.3.4, 41.1.7, 41.3.5.2","ref":"S9","prio":2,"a":"**Yes, you can enter.**\n- At RVSM entry you need 2 primary altimetry systems, 1 automatic altitude control system and 1 altitude alerting system. One autopilot with altitude hold meets the altitude control requirement.\n- **Before flight:** check the MEL for dispatch relief.\n- **In flight:** if the remaining autopilot fails, tell ATC \"UNABLE RVSM DUE EQUIPMENT\".","keys":[{"k":"Yes, can enter","m":["yes","can enter"]},{"k":"One altitude control system needed","m":["one altitude control","1 altitude control","one autopilot","1 autopilot","one ap"]},{"k":"2 altimetry + 1 alerting","m":["2 primary","two primary","alerting","2 altimet","two altimet"]},{"k":"Check MEL","m":["mel"]},{"k":"If second AP fails: unable RVSM","m":["unable rvsm"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"TCAS fails: on the ground, before entering RVSM, and after entering. What do you do?","src":"OM-A 31, 41.2, 41.3.4; CAR Sec 2 Series R Part IV","ref":"S10","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **On the ground:** an MEL question. The Indian flight plan must show serviceable ACAS, and ACAS II is required in India under CAR Sec 2 Series R Part IV. Dispatch only if the MEL allows; amend the flight plan and inform ATC.\n- **Before RVSM entry:** ACAS II is in the CAR equipment list \"to improve safety\" but not in the OM-A entry list (2 altimetry, 1 altitude control, 1 alerting). Inform ATC and let them decide; increase lookout; tech log entry.\n- **After entry:** continue, inform ATC, good lookout.\n- Say both: the two lists differ on ACAS.","keys":[{"k":"Ground: MEL","m":["mel"]},{"k":"ACAS required in India (CAR)","m":["car sec","series r","required in india","mandatory in india"]},{"k":"Not in RVSM entry list","m":["entry list","not required at entry","not in the entry","not mandatory"]},{"k":"Inform ATC","m":["inform atc","tell atc","advise atc"]},{"k":"Lookout and tech log","m":["lookout","tech log"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-21","type":"recall","q":"Engine failure in cruise in RVSM or oceanic airspace: heading, altitude, offset and calls?","src":"OM-A 4.3.6, 17.3.7.2, 41.3.5.2; Doc 4444 15.2.2.3, 15.2.2.4","ref":"S11","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"1. **Aviate:** NNC and memory items; MCT; choose a strategy.\n2. **Doc 4444 15.2.2.3 (16th edition copy):** turn at least 45° towards the alternate and away from traffic to a 15 NM offset (30° and 5 NM in later amendments). If you can't hold the level, minimise descent rate; final level 500 ft off normal levels. Lights, transponder, TCAS.\n3. **Calls:** MAYDAY, squawk 7700; broadcast on 121.5 and 123.45; get a clearance as soon as possible; remind ATC you are a twin with an engine shut down.\n4. **Divert** to the nearest suitable aerodrome.","keys":[{"k":"MCT and NNC","m":["mct","maximum continuous","nnc","memory items"]},{"k":"Turn 45 degrees / 15 NM offset","m":["45 deg","forty five","15 nm","15nm","offset"]},{"k":"Level 500 ft off normal","m":["500"]},{"k":"MAYDAY, 7700","m":["mayday","7700"]},{"k":"121.5 and 123.45","m":["121.5","1215","123.45","12345"]},{"k":"Divert nearest suitable","m":["divert","nearest suitable"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-22","type":"recall","q":"How do you fly the engine-out driftdown on the 737?","src":"FCTM 4.12 to 4.14, 4.16","ref":"S11","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- Disconnect the A/T and set CON thrust manually.\n- Select ENG OUT on the FMC CRZ page.\n- Set the engine-out MAX altitude and target speed on the MCP.\n- Let the speed bleed back, then engage LVL CHG.\n- After level-off keep MCT and accelerate to single-engine LRC.\n- On an EDTO route the operator's approved speeds replace the FMC engine-out speed.","keys":[{"k":"A/T off, CON thrust","m":["con thrust","autothrottle","a t off"]},{"k":"ENG OUT on CRZ page","m":["eng out","engine out"]},{"k":"MAX altitude on MCP","m":["max alt","maximum altitude","mcp"]},{"k":"LVL CHG after speed bleeds","m":["lvl chg","level change"]},{"k":"Single-engine LRC after level-off","m":["lrc","long range cruise"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-23","type":"recall","q":"RNP 10 route, no GPS, and one IRS inoperative. Can you enter?","src":"OM-A Ch 40 (RNAV 10)","ref":"S12","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**No.** RNAV 10 (RNP 10) needs two long-range navigation systems operational at oceanic entry (INS, IRS or GNSS).\n- With GPS unavailable and one IRS failed you have only one LRNS. The B737 RNAV 10 list is 2 FMC, 2 CDU, 2 PFD/ND and 2 IRS.\n- **Actions:** tell ATC \"unable RNP 10\", request a routing within ground-navaid coverage, otherwise divert or return.\n- With two IRS and no GPS you would still be legal, but only within the IRS time limit from the last radio update.","keys":[{"k":"No, cannot enter","m":["cannot","can not","not permitted","no you"]},{"k":"Two LRNS required","m":["two long range","2 long range","two lrns","2 lrns"]},{"k":"Only one LRNS left","m":["one lrns","1 lrns","only one"]},{"k":"Unable RNP 10 to ATC","m":["unable rnp"]},{"k":"Re-route via navaid coverage or divert","m":["ground","navaid","divert","return"]},{"k":"Two IRS OK within time limit","m":["time limit"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-24","type":"recall","q":"Your altimeters are split by 250 ft at FL370. What do you do?","src":"OM-A 41.1.6.3, 41.1.7, 41.3.5.2; FCOM 10.10.54; QRH 10.14","ref":"S13","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**The 200 ft in-flight limit is breached.**\n1. Identify the faulty side with the standby altimeter.\n2. Keep the AP and transponder on the good side; the transponder must be fed from the system controlling the aircraft.\n3. Run the QRH ALT DISAGREE checklist. The amber ALT DISAGREE shows on both PFDs when altitudes differ by more than 200 ft for more than 5 s. If it stays after checking baro settings, the aircraft does not meet RVSM.\n4. Tell ATC \"UNABLE RVSM DUE EQUIPMENT\".\n5. Tech log entry.","keys":[{"k":"200 ft limit breached","m":["200"]},{"k":"Standby altimeter to find faulty side","m":["standby"]},{"k":"AP and transponder on good side","m":["good side","transponder"]},{"k":"ALT DISAGREE checklist","m":["alt disagree","altitude disagree","qrh"]},{"k":"Unable RVSM due equipment","m":["unable rvsm"]},{"k":"Tech log","m":["tech log","techlog"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"When does ALT DISAGREE appear on the 737 PFDs?","src":"FCOM 10.10.54","ref":"S13","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Altitudes differ by more than 200 ft for more than 5 s","Altitudes differ by more than 300 ft for more than 10 s","Altitudes differ by more than 75 ft on the ground","Altitudes differ by more than 245 ft for more than 5 s"],"a":0,"why":"Amber ALT DISAGREE on both PFDs: more than 200 ft for more than 5 s. It does not show which side is wrong.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-order-2","type":"order","q":"Altimeters split by 250 ft at FL370: order the actions.","src":"OM-A 41.1.6.3, 41.1.7; QRH 10.14","ref":"S13","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Identify the faulty side with the standby altimeter","Keep AP and transponder on the good side","Run the QRH ALT DISAGREE checklist","Tell ATC UNABLE RVSM DUE EQUIPMENT","Make a tech log entry"],"why":"S13 sequence: diagnose, protect the good side, checklist, ATC, record.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-25","type":"recall","q":"What was MNPS airspace, what is it called now, and could you fly the North Atlantic tracks?","src":"OM-A Ch 0, Ch 42, 42.0, 17.3.2 (2), 1.8 g (vii); Oral bank p.73 to p.81","ref":"OB1","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- MNPS (Minimum Navigation Performance Specification) airspace is now **NAT HLA** (North Atlantic High Level Airspace). OM-A Chapter 42 is titled \"NAT HLA / MNPS\".\n- **For AIX: not applicable.** The company has no NAT HLA or polar approval; a ferry through the NAT needs specific DGCA approval. It would appear as a special authorisation in the OpsSpecs.\n- **Bank's description:** FL285 to FL420; needs two fully serviceable LRNS, RVSM approval, HF, MEL entries, trained crew and operator approval.\n- **Tracks:** published twice a day to suit the winds.","keys":[{"k":"Now NAT HLA","m":["hla","high level airspace"]},{"k":"AIX not approved","m":["not applicable","no approval","not approved"]},{"k":"DGCA approval for ferry","m":["dgca"]},{"k":"FL285 to FL420","m":["285","420"]},{"k":"Two LRNS","m":["two long range","2 long range","two lrns","2 lrns"]},{"k":"Tracks published twice daily","m":["twice","two times"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"The oral bank says an airline 'has to seek MNPS approval to fly over the NAT'. What is the correct position for your operator?","src":"OM-A 42.0","ref":"OB1","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["The airspace is now NAT HLA, and AIX holds no NAT HLA approval","AIX holds MNPS approval through its OpsSpecs","MNPS approval is automatic with RVSM approval","MNPS is still the current name and needs only RNP 10"],"a":0,"why":"MNPS is outdated (now NAT HLA) and OM-A 42.0 says it is not applicable to AIX.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-26","type":"recall","q":"What were the MNPS-era NAT separation standards and why do operators want the approval?","src":"Oral bank p.76, p.77, p.79, p.81; OM-A 40.14.3.2","ref":"OB1","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"- **Separation (bank, MNPS-era):** longitudinal 10 min by Mach number technique, reducing towards 5 min when the leader is faster; 15 min without a common reporting point; lateral 60 NM. Current NAT values are in NAT Doc 007, not in my sources.\n- **Why the approval:** without it you are kept below FL290 or above FL410, which costs fuel and payload.\n- **Track system:** eastbound overnight from North America, westbound in daytime.\n- **The India equivalent:** oceanic RNAV 10 with two LRNS, or RNP 4 with CPDLC and ADS-C.","keys":[{"k":"10 min Mach number technique","m":["10 min","10min","mach number"]},{"k":"Lateral 60 NM","m":["60 nm","60nm","60 nautical","sixty"]},{"k":"Kept below FL290 or above FL410","m":["below fl290","below 290","above 410"]},{"k":"Fuel and payload penalty","m":["fuel","payload"]},{"k":"India: RNAV 10 / RNP 4","m":["rnav 10","rnp 4","rnp 10"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-27","type":"recall","q":"What is SLOP, why was it introduced, and what are the limits?","src":"Doc 4444 16.5 Note 1, 16.5.2, 16.5.3, 16.5.4; Oral bank p.82","ref":"OB2","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **SLOP (strategic lateral offset procedure):** a crew-decided offset to the right of the centreline, only where the AIP authorises it.\n- **Why:** GNSS keeps everyone within metres of the centreline, so a level bust on the same route is far more likely to end in a collision. Offsets reduce lateral overlap; also used for wake.\n- **Limits:** right only, in tenths of a NM; up to 2 NM where route spacing is 23 NM or more; up to 0.5 NM where 6 to 23 NM.\n- **Rules:** only with automatic offset tracking; ATC need not be told; coordinate on 123.45 MHz.","keys":[{"k":"Right of centreline only","m":["right"]},{"k":"Only where AIP authorises","m":["aip"]},{"k":"GNSS accuracy raises collision risk","m":["gps","gnss","accura","overlap"]},{"k":"Up to 2 NM, tenths of NM","m":["2 nm","2nm","two nm","tenth"]},{"k":"0.5 NM where spacing 6 to 23 NM","m":["0.5 nm","05 nm","0.5nm","05nm","half a","point 5"]},{"k":"Automatic offset, ATC need not be told","m":["automatic","need not","no need"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-28","type":"recall","q":"How do you fly a lateral offset on the 737 MAX, and why can't SLOP be flown manually?","src":"FCOM 11.42.13, 11.10.1; Doc 4444 16.5.4","ref":"OB2","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Entry:** OFFSET prompt on the RTE page (or INIT/REF INDEX) opens the LATERAL OFFSET page. Offset up to 99.9 NM left or right, entered as e.g. L10 or 10L, with optional start and end waypoints.\n- **Not valid on:** approach procedures, DME arcs, heading legs, holds (except PPOS) or discontinuities.\n- **Indication:** the white OFST light on the CDU means LNAV is guiding on a lateral offset.\n- **Why automatic:** Doc 4444 requires automatic offset tracking for SLOP, which this LNAV offset provides.","keys":[{"k":"OFFSET prompt / LATERAL OFFSET page","m":["lateral offset","offset prompt","rte page"]},{"k":"Up to 99.9 NM","m":["99.9","999","99 point 9"]},{"k":"Entered as L10 / 10L","m":["l10","10l"]},{"k":"OFST light","m":["ofst"]},{"k":"Not on approach, arcs, holds","m":["approach","dme arc","hold","heading leg"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"The oral bank says SLOP is 'only applicable to the NAT' and 'always 0, 1, 2 miles'. What is correct?","src":"Doc 4444 16.5.2, 16.5.3; OM-A 17.3.7.3","ref":"OB2","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["Any oceanic or remote continental airspace where the AIP authorises it, in tenths of a NM, max 0.5 NM where routes are 6 to 23 NM apart","NAT only, offsets of 0, 1 or 2 NM right","Any airspace, offsets of 0, 1 or 2 NM left or right","Oceanic airspace only, 1 NM or 2 NM right, ATC must approve"],"a":0,"why":"Doc 4444 16.5.2 and 16.5.3: tenths of a NM, right only, where the AIP authorises. The bank's 'centreline before leaving' rule is not in Doc 4444; follow each region's AIP.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-29","type":"recall","q":"How is CPDLC used in oceanic airspace, and what do you do if it fails?","src":"OM-A Ch 0 def 34, 40.14.3.2; Doc 4444 14.1.1, 14.2.1, 14.3.1, 14.3.3.1, 14.3.6.2, 14.3.6.4; FCOM 11.34","ref":"OB3","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **What it is:** controller-pilot communication by data link, replacing HF voice for routine clearances, requests and reports beyond VHF range.\n- **Logon:** early enough, where and when the AIP says. Only the current data authority holds the link.\n- **Messages:** standard elements, avoid long multi-element messages, answer in the medium used; one response covers all elements.\n- **Transfer:** voice and CPDLC transfer together.\n- **Failure:** revert to voice prefixed \"CPDLC FAILURE\"; treat outstanding messages as not delivered and restart the dialogue.\n- **Oceanic value:** RNP 4 with CPDLC and ADS-C supports 30 NM lateral and longitudinal separation.","keys":[{"k":"Data link replacing HF voice","m":["data link","datalink","replac"]},{"k":"Logon / current data authority","m":["logon","log on","data authority"]},{"k":"Reply in medium used","m":["same medium","medium"]},{"k":"Failure: voice, 'CPDLC FAILURE'","m":["cpdlc failure","revert to voice","voice"]},{"k":"Outstanding messages not delivered, restart","m":["not delivered","restart"]},{"k":"RNP 4 + ADS-C: 30 NM","m":["30 nm","30nm","30 30","thirty"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-16","type":"mcq","q":"The oral bank lists FANS as 'CPDLC, ADS-B, GPS'. What is the error?","src":"OM-A 40.14.33; NR36","ref":"OB3","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["The FANS surveillance element is ADS-C (contract), not ADS-B","FANS has no data link element","FANS uses ACARS voice, not CPDLC","Nothing; that list is correct"],"a":0,"why":"RNP 4 lists CPDLC, GNSS and ADS-C.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-recall-30","type":"recall","q":"Which cruising levels apply to your track, inside and above RVSM (India)?","src":"OM-A 17.3.1.4, 17.3.1.5 a; Oral bank p.64, p.66","ref":"OB4","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Indian table, magnetic track:\n- **000 to 179:** odd levels, FL290, 310, 330, 350, 370, 390, 410, then FL450.\n- **180 to 359:** even levels, FL300, 320, 340, 360, 380, 400, then FL430.\n- **Below FL290 (IFR):** odd thousands eastbound, even westbound; VFR adds 500 ft.\n- **Above FL410:** 2000 ft separation returns, so same-direction levels are 4000 ft apart.\n- **Exceptions:** an ATC clearance or the AIP (e.g. one-way routes) overrides; IFR outside controlled airspace still uses the table.","keys":[{"k":"000 to 179 odd: FL290, 310...","m":["odd level","odd flight","odd fl","odd number"]},{"k":"180 to 359 even: FL300, 320...","m":["even level","even flight","even fl","even number"]},{"k":"Above FL410: FL430 / FL450","m":["430","450"]},{"k":"Magnetic track","m":["magnetic"]},{"k":"VFR adds 500 ft","m":["500"]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-17","type":"mcq","q":"Magnetic track 095°, above RVSM airspace: what is the first available level above FL410?","src":"OM-A 17.3.1.4","ref":"OB4","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["FL450","FL430","FL420","FL440"],"a":0,"why":"000 to 179 uses FL410 then FL450 (4000 ft apart same direction). FL430 is westbound.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-mcq-18","type":"mcq","q":"Magnetic track 270°: which of these is a correct RVSM cruising level?","src":"OM-A 17.3.1.4","ref":"OB4","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["FL360","FL370","FL350","FL410"],"a":0,"why":"180 to 359 uses even levels: FL300, 320, 340, 360, 380, 400.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-num-16","type":"num","q":"How many usable flight levels are there from FL290 to FL410 inclusive with RVSM?","src":"Oral bank p.64; OM-A 41.1.2","ref":"OB4","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":13,"unit":"levels","why":"13 levels with RVSM against 7 before, i.e. 6 more.","topic":2},{"id":"t2-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: 'Explain RVSM.'","src":"OM-A 41.1.1 to 41.3.5.2; FCOM 10.10.54","ref":"NR21, NR22, S13","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What is RVSM?","a":"1000 ft separation from FL290 to FL410 inclusive; India since 27 Nov 2003.","keys":[{"k":"1000 ft","m":["1000"]},{"k":"FL290 to FL410","m":["290","410"]}]},{"q":"What equipment does it need?","a":"Two independent altimetry systems, SSR transponder with altitude reporting, altitude alerting (200/300 ft), automatic altitude control (+/-50 ft), ACAS II to improve safety.","keys":[{"k":"Two altimetry systems","m":["two independent","2 independent","two altimet","2 altimet"]},{"k":"Transponder","m":["transponder"]},{"k":"Altitude alert","m":["alert"]},{"k":"Altitude hold 50 ft","m":["50 ft","50ft","50 feet"]}]},{"q":"What must work at the entry point?","a":"2 primary altimetry, 1 altitude control and 1 alerting system. Else get a clearance to avoid RVSM.","keys":[{"k":"2 altimetry","m":["2 primary","two primary","2 altimet","two altimet"]},{"k":"1 altitude control","m":["altitude control","autopilot"]},{"k":"1 alerting","m":["alerting"]}]},{"q":"What checks do you do?","a":"Ground: within 75 ft of elevation on QNH. Flight: primaries within 200 ft, about hourly. Record primary and standby near entry.","keys":[{"k":"75 ft","m":["75 ft","75ft","75 feet"]},{"k":"200 ft","m":["200"]},{"k":"Hourly","m":["hour"]}]},{"q":"Now your altimeters split by 250 ft. Call?","a":"Standby to find the bad side, AP/transponder on good side, ALT DISAGREE checklist, 'UNABLE RVSM DUE EQUIPMENT', tech log.","keys":[{"k":"Unable RVSM due equipment","m":["unable rvsm"]},{"k":"Standby altimeter","m":["standby"]},{"k":"ALT DISAGREE","m":["alt disagree","qrh"]}]},{"q":"What does ATC do then?","a":"Applies 2000 ft separation or moves you out of RVSM airspace.","keys":[{"k":"2000 ft","m":["2000"]},{"k":"Out of RVSM","m":["out of rvsm","outside rvsm","below fl290"]}]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: 'Delhi to Kolkata in RVSM, a line of CBs ahead, ATC not answering.'","src":"OM-A 41.1.9.3, 17.3.7.2; Doc 4444 15.2.3.3","ref":"S7, NR7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What first?","a":"Keep trying for a clearance: other frequencies, previous or next sector, 121.5, relay through another aircraft.","keys":[{"k":"121.5","m":["121.5","1215"]},{"k":"Relay","m":["relay"]},{"k":"Other frequency","m":["frequenc"]}]},{"q":"Still nothing. Now?","a":"PIC emergency authority: PAN or MAYDAY, broadcast position and intentions on 121.5 and 123.45, TCAS, exterior lights on.","keys":[{"k":"PAN or MAYDAY","m":["pan pan","mayday"]},{"k":"123.45","m":["123.45","12345"]},{"k":"Lights and TCAS","m":["lights","tcas"]}]},{"q":"You deviate left. Which way do you change level, and when?","a":"Track about 110°M, so 000 to 179 group: left means descend 300 ft, once more than 5 NM off track (OM-A). My Doc 4444 copy says 10 NM.","keys":[{"k":"Descend","m":["descend"]},{"k":"300 ft","m":["300"]},{"k":"5 NM","m":["5 nm","5nm","five"]}]},{"q":"And deviating right?","a":"Climb 300 ft.","keys":[{"k":"Climb 300 ft","m":["climb"]}]},{"q":"Coming back?","a":"Return to track and back at the assigned level within about 10 NM of the centreline; tell ATC when contact is regained.","keys":[{"k":"Assigned level","m":["assigned level","original level","cleared level"]},{"k":"10 NM","m":["10 nm","10nm","ten"]}]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-chain-3","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: 'Engine failure at FL370 on an oceanic route.'","src":"Doc 4444 15.2.2.3, 15.2.2.4; FCTM 4.12 to 4.14; OM-A 4.3.6","ref":"S11, NR7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"First actions?","a":"Aviate: NNC and memory items, MCT, choose a strategy.","keys":[{"k":"MCT / CON","m":["mct","con thrust","maximum continuous"]},{"k":"NNC / memory items","m":["nnc","memory","checklist"]}]},{"q":"You can't get a clearance. Lateral?","a":"Doc 4444 (my copy): turn at least 45° to a 15 NM offset, towards the alternate and away from traffic. Later amendments: 30° and 5 NM.","keys":[{"k":"45 degrees","m":["45 deg","forty five"]},{"k":"15 NM","m":["15 nm","15nm","fifteen"]}]},{"q":"Vertical?","a":"Minimise the descent rate; final level 500 ft off the normal levels; driftdown once clear of the track.","keys":[{"k":"500 ft off normal levels","m":["500"]},{"k":"Driftdown","m":["driftdown","drift down"]}]},{"q":"Calls and lights?","a":"MAYDAY, 7700, broadcast on 121.5 and 123.45, lights and transponder on, watch TCAS; tell ATC you're a twin with an engine shut down.","keys":[{"k":"MAYDAY / 7700","m":["mayday","7700"]},{"k":"121.5 / 123.45","m":["121.5","1215","123.45","12345"]},{"k":"Lights","m":["lights"]}]},{"q":"How do you fly the driftdown on the 737?","a":"A/T off, CON thrust, ENG OUT on CRZ page, MAX altitude on MCP, speed bleeds back, then LVL CHG; after level-off MCT and single-engine LRC.","keys":[{"k":"ENG OUT page","m":["eng out","engine out"]},{"k":"LVL CHG","m":["lvl chg","level change"]},{"k":"CON thrust","m":["con thrust","continuous thrust"]}]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t2-chain-4","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: 'You lose both radios in IMC just after take-off on a SID.'","src":"OM-A 5.2; Doc 4444 6.3.2.5, 15.3.3","ref":"NR26","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"Immediate actions?","a":"Squawk 7600, try the other radio, previous frequency, 121.5, relay, ACARS.","keys":[{"k":"7600","m":["7600"]},{"k":"121.5 / relay","m":["121.5","1215","relay","acars"]}]},{"q":"What lateral and vertical profile?","a":"Continue the SID as cleared with its restrictions to its end; check chart or AIP AD 2.22 RCF notes, which override.","keys":[{"k":"Continue the SID","m":["the sid","sid as cleared","complete sid","continue sid"]},{"k":"Chart / AIP notes override","m":["chart","aip","ad 2.22","ad 222"]}]},{"q":"Then?","a":"Radar airspace: keep the last level for 7 min, then climb to the flight-planned level and follow the flight plan. Non-radar: 20 min.","keys":[{"k":"7 min","m":["7 min","7min","seven"]},{"k":"20 min","m":["20 min","20min","twenty"]}]},{"q":"At destination?","a":"Hold over the aid, descend at EAT or ETA, normal approach, land within 30 min of ETA or EAT whichever is later.","keys":[{"k":"EAT","m":["expected approach","eat or","at eat","last eat"]},{"k":"30 min","m":["30 min","30min","thirty"]}]}],"topic":2},{"id":"t20-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"What is compressor stall and what is surge? What causes them?","src":"Oral bank p.12, p.13; FCTM 4.3","ref":"OB82","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"A compressor blade's angle of attack depends on the axial velocity of the air and the blade's rotational speed. Too high an angle of attack and the blade stalls.\n- **Surge:** the stall spreads until airflow through the whole engine breaks down; in severe cases flow reverses with a bang and flame from the intake or exhaust.\n- **Causes:** abrupt acceleration with too much fuel; operation away from design RPM; disturbed intake flow (crosswind, tailwind, high angle of attack); contaminated or damaged blades. Engine icing can also cause it.","keys":[{"k":"Blade angle of attack too high","m":["angle of attack","aoa"]},{"k":"Axial velocity vs rotational speed","m":["axial velocity","axial","rotational speed","rpm"]},{"k":"Surge: whole-engine flow breakdown / reversal","m":["whole engine","reverse","reversal","breaks down","breakdown"]},{"k":"Too much fuel on acceleration","m":["too much fuel","excess fuel","rapid acceleration","abrupt acceleration","slam"]},{"k":"Disturbed intake flow","m":["crosswind","tailwind","intake","disturbed"]},{"k":"Damaged or contaminated blades","m":["damaged","contaminated","dirty","fod"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"What are the indications of a compressor stall, and what is the 737 action?","src":"Oral bank p.12; QRH 7.2","ref":"OB82","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Indications:** bangs, vibration, rising EGT, and N1/N2 not following the thrust lever.\n**Action: Engine Limit or Surge or Stall NNC (QRH 7.2):**\n- A/T off, retard the thrust lever until indications stay within limits.\n- If stable, advance slowly and run at a surge-free setting; with reduced thrust set the transponder to TA (TA ONLY).\n- If not stable, or EGT keeps rising: engine start lever CUTOFF.","keys":[{"k":"Bangs / vibration","m":["bang","vibration","noise"]},{"k":"Rising EGT","m":["egt"]},{"k":"N1/N2 not following thrust lever","m":["not following","n1","n2","mismatch"]},{"k":"A/T off, retard thrust lever","m":["retard","a/t off","at off","autothrottle off","close the thrust","reduce thrust"]},{"k":"Transponder TA only","m":["ta only","transponder ta","ta mode","tcas ta"]},{"k":"Not stable: start lever CUTOFF","m":["cutoff","cut off","shut down","shutdown"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"How do you prevent compressor surge on take-off in a crosswind or tailwind?","src":"FCTM 3.16, 4.3; FCOM 7.20","ref":"OB82","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Use a rolling take-off. Surge can occur with a crosswind over 20 kt or a tailwind over 10 kt if take-off thrust is set before brake release. Engine icing can also cause a stall, so use engine anti-ice as required. On ground starts the EEC detects an impending stall and cuts fuel and ignition.","keys":[{"k":"Rolling take-off","m":["rolling"]},{"k":"Crosswind over 20 kt","m":["20 kt","20kt","20 knots","20 kts"]},{"k":"Tailwind over 10 kt","m":["10 kt","10kt","10 knots","10 kts"]},{"k":"Not setting TO thrust before brake release","m":["brake release","before releasing","on the brakes"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-num-1","type":"num","q":"Above what crosswind can surge occur if take-off thrust is set before brake release (737 FCTM)?","src":"FCTM 3.16","ref":"OB82","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":20,"unit":"kt","why":"Crosswind over 20 kt or tailwind over 10 kt: use a rolling take-off.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-num-2","type":"num","q":"Above what tailwind can surge occur if take-off thrust is set before brake release (737 FCTM)?","src":"FCTM 3.16","ref":"OB82","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":10,"unit":"kt","why":"Tailwind over 10 kt or crosswind over 20 kt.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-order-1","type":"order","q":"Order the Engine Limit or Surge or Stall NNC actions.","src":"QRH 7.2","ref":"OB82","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Autothrottle off","Retard thrust lever until indications stay within limits","If stable: advance slowly to a surge-free setting","If not stable or EGT keeps rising: start lever CUTOFF"],"why":"QRH 7.2.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"What is a bypass engine and what is bypass ratio? Advantages and disadvantages of high bypass?","src":"Oral bank p.13; Standard theory","ref":"OB83","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"In a bypass engine not all the intake air passes through the core: the fan drives a cold stream around the core that gives thrust without burning fuel.\n- **Bypass ratio** = mass of air bypassing the core divided by mass through the core. At 5:1, of 6 kg entering, 1 kg goes through the core and 5 kg bypass.\n- **Advantages:** a large mass at low jet velocity gives higher propulsive efficiency, lower SFC and less noise.\n- **Disadvantages:** large fan diameter and weight; thrust lapses more with speed and altitude.","keys":[{"k":"Cold stream around the core","m":["around the core","bypass the core","bypasses the core","cold stream","not through the core"]},{"k":"Ratio of bypass mass to core mass","m":["ratio","divided by","mass"]},{"k":"Higher propulsive efficiency","m":["propulsive efficiency","efficien"]},{"k":"Lower SFC","m":["sfc","fuel consumption"]},{"k":"Less noise","m":["noise","quieter"]},{"k":"Disadvantage: size/weight, thrust lapse","m":["weight","diameter","lapse","heavier"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["cooling of turbines","cools the turbine","turbine cooling"],"why":"Bank error: turbines are cooled by compressor bleed air, not the bypass stream"}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"Describe the 737 MAX engine (LEAP-1B) spool layout.","src":"FCOM 7.20","ref":"OB83","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"The LEAP-1B is a dual-rotor, axial-flow turbofan.\n- **N1 rotor:** fan, LP compressor and LP turbine.\n- **N2 rotor:** HP compressor and HP turbine.\n- The starter drives N2.\n- The EEC sets thrust by commanding N1 and adjusting fuel flow.\nThe bypass ratio is not given in the FCOM.","keys":[{"k":"Dual rotor / two spool","m":["dual rotor","dual-rotor","two spool","twin spool","two-spool","2 spool"]},{"k":"N1: fan, LPC, LPT","m":["fan"]},{"k":"N2: HPC, HPT","m":["high pressure","hp compressor","hpc","hp turbine"]},{"k":"Starter drives N2","m":["starter drives n2","starter on n2","start drives n2","n2 starter"]},{"k":"EEC commands N1","m":["eec","fadec"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-num-3","type":"num","q":"Bypass ratio 5:1. Of 6 kg of air entering, how many kg pass through the core?","src":"Oral bank p.13","ref":"OB83","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":1,"unit":"kg","why":"5 kg bypass, 1 kg through the core.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"Which rotor does the 737 MAX starter drive?","src":"FCOM 7.20","ref":"OB83","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["N2","N1","Both","The fan only"],"a":0,"why":"The starter drives N2 (HP compressor and HP turbine).","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"How are the turbines of a high-bypass engine cooled?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB83","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Air bled from the compressor","The bypass (fan) stream","Fuel circulation","Ram air from the nacelle"],"a":0,"why":"Bank error: bypass does not 'promote cooling of turbines'. Turbine cooling uses compressor bleed air.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"Explain the working of a jet engine: the Brayton cycle.","src":"Oral bank p.14, p.15; FCOM 7.20","ref":"OB84","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"The jet engine works on the Brayton, or constant-pressure, cycle:\n1. Air enters through the intake.\n2. The compressor compresses it.\n3. Fuel burns in the combustion chamber at roughly constant pressure.\n4. Hot gas expands through the turbine, which drives the compressor (and fan).\n5. The remaining energy leaves through the exhaust as thrust.\nThrust = mass flow times change in velocity, plus any pressure thrust. On the 737 MAX the EEC sets thrust by commanding N1.","keys":[{"k":"Brayton / constant pressure","m":["brayton","constant pressure"]},{"k":"Intake, compression","m":["compress"]},{"k":"Combustion","m":["combustion","burn"]},{"k":"Turbine drives compressor","m":["turbine"]},{"k":"Exhaust / thrust","m":["exhaust","thrust"]},{"k":"Thrust = mass flow x velocity change","m":["mass flow","change in velocity","velocity"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-order-2","type":"order","q":"Order the Brayton cycle stages.","src":"Oral bank p.15","ref":"OB84","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["Intake","Compression","Combustion at constant pressure","Expansion through the turbine","Exhaust"],"why":"Suck, squeeze, bang, blow.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"In the Brayton cycle, combustion takes place at roughly constant:","src":"Oral bank p.15","ref":"OB84","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Pressure","Volume","Temperature","Velocity"],"a":0,"why":"Brayton is the constant-pressure cycle.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"What are wet, hot and hung starts?","src":"Oral bank p.27; FCOM 7.20; FCOM NP.21.30","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Wet start (no light-off):** EGT does not rise after fuel is introduced, e.g. from an ignition failure. On a ground start the EEC turns off ignition and fuel 15 s after the start lever goes to IDLE.\n- **Hot start:** EGT rises fast towards or above the start limit. Causes: too much fuel, early start-lever movement, starter dropping out early, blocked intake, tailwind. The EEC detects an impending hot start (flashing white box) and cuts fuel and ignition; if the limit is exceeded the EGT display turns red.\n- **Hung start:** light-off occurs but N2 stalls below self-sustaining (idle) speed, often with high EGT for that RPM.","keys":[{"k":"Wet: no EGT rise / no light-off","m":["no light","egt does not rise","egt doesnt rise","no egt rise","no rise in egt","no ignition"]},{"k":"EEC cuts at 15 s","m":["15 s","15s","15 sec","15 second","fifteen second"]},{"k":"Hot: EGT exceeds/nears limit","m":["exceed","start limit","egt rises","high egt","egt limit"]},{"k":"Hung: N2 stagnates below idle","m":["below idle","self sustaining","self-sustaining","stagnat","hang","does not accelerate"]},{"k":"Hot start causes","m":["too much fuel","tailwind","early","blocked"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"When do you abort an engine start on the 737 MAX, and what is the procedure?","src":"FCOM NP.21.30; QRH 7.1; FCOM L.10.8; FCOM 7.20","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Abort if:** N1 or N2 does not increase or increases very slowly after EGT rises; no oil pressure by stable idle; EGT has not risen by 15 s; EGT quickly nears or exceeds the start limit.\n**QRH Aborted Engine Start:** start lever CUTOFF, then motor the engine for 60 s (if the start switch is already OFF, wait for N2 below 20%, then GRD and motor 60 s).\n- Starter limits: 3 min per attempt, 10 s between attempts. After three consecutive aborted starts, 15 min cooling.\n- EEC abnormal-start protection works on ground starts only.","keys":[{"k":"No EGT rise by 15 s","m":["15 s","15s","15 sec","15 second"]},{"k":"No oil pressure by stable idle","m":["oil pressure"]},{"k":"Start lever CUTOFF","m":["cutoff","cut off"]},{"k":"Motor 60 s","m":["60 s","60s","60 sec","60 second","one minute","1 minute"]},{"k":"N2 below 20% before GRD","m":["20%","20 percent","20 %","below 20"]},{"k":"15 min cooling after 3 aborts","m":["15 min","15 minute","three consecutive","3 consecutive","three aborted","3 aborted"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["blowout cycle 2 min","2 min blowout","cooling period 5 min","5 min cooling","2 minutes","5 minutes"],"why":"Bank error: 737 MAX motors 60 s per QRH 7.1; 15 min cooling only after three aborted starts"}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-num-4","type":"num","q":"On a 737 MAX ground start, how long after the start lever goes to IDLE does the EEC cut fuel and ignition if there is no light-off?","src":"FCOM 7.20","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":15,"unit":"s","why":"15 s with no EGT rise: wet start protection.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-num-5","type":"num","q":"QRH Aborted Engine Start: how long do you motor the engine after CUTOFF?","src":"QRH 7.1","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":60,"unit":"s","why":"Motor 60 s.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-num-6","type":"num","q":"Starter duty: maximum time per start attempt (737 MAX)?","src":"FCOM NP.21.30","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":3,"unit":"min","why":"3 min per attempt, 10 s between attempts.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-num-7","type":"num","q":"Cooling period required after three consecutive aborted starts (737 MAX)?","src":"FCOM L.10.8","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":15,"unit":"min","why":"15 minutes.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-num-8","type":"num","q":"Aborted start with start switch already OFF: N2 must be below what before selecting GRD to motor?","src":"QRH 7.1","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":20,"unit":"%","why":"Wait for N2 below 20%, then GRD and motor 60 s.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"The 737 MAX EEC hot-start and wet-start protection operates:","src":"FCOM 7.20","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["On ground starts only","On ground and in-flight starts","In flight only","Only with APU bleed"],"a":0,"why":"Abnormal-start protection works on ground starts only.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"EEC detects an impending hot start on the ground. What does the EGT display show?","src":"FCOM 7.20","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Flashing white box","Red EGT","Amber box","Magenta box"],"a":0,"why":"Impending hot start: flashing white box, EEC cuts fuel and ignition. Red if the start limit is actually exceeded.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"A hung start is:","src":"Oral bank p.27","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Light-off, but N2 stalls below self-sustaining speed","No light-off after fuel is introduced","EGT exceeds the start limit","Starter fails to engage"],"a":0,"why":"Hung start: light-off but N2 stagnates below idle, often with high EGT for the RPM.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the abnormal start to its definition.","src":"Oral bank p.27","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Wet start","No light-off: EGT does not rise"],["Hot start","EGT rises fast towards or above the start limit"],["Hung start","N2 stalls below self-sustaining speed"]],"why":"Oral bank p.27.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"What are vortex generators and why are they fitted?","src":"Oral bank p.13; Standard theory","ref":"OB86","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Vortex generators are rows of small, thin aerofoil-shaped blades standing about 2.5 cm into the airflow.\n- Each sheds a small vortex that mixes high-energy free-stream air into the boundary layer.\n- This re-energises the boundary layer and delays separation.\n- Uses: better control effectiveness and a higher stall angle, or reducing shock-induced separation on high-speed wings.","keys":[{"k":"Small blades / vanes","m":["blade","vane","small aerofoil","small airfoil","fins"]},{"k":"Shed a vortex","m":["vortex","vortices"]},{"k":"Energise the boundary layer","m":["boundary layer","energi"]},{"k":"Delay separation","m":["separation","delay"]},{"k":"Control effectiveness / shock separation","m":["control","shock","stall angle"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"Why are jet wings swept? Advantages and disadvantages?","src":"Oral bank p.29, p.59","ref":"OB87","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Main advantage: higher MCRIT.** Only the velocity component normal to the leading edge drives compressibility; sweep reduces it, delaying shock formation and drag rise.\n- Other advantages: less gust response, higher stalling angle of attack, some directional and lateral stability.\n- **Disadvantages:** lower CLmax (higher approach speeds, need for high-lift devices); spanwise flow and a tendency to tip stall; less efficient lift.","keys":[{"k":"Higher critical Mach","m":["mcrit","critical mach","mach critical"]},{"k":"Normal component of velocity","m":["normal","perpendicular","component"]},{"k":"Delays shock / drag rise","m":["shock","drag rise","drag divergence","wave drag"]},{"k":"Less gust response","m":["gust"]},{"k":"Lower CLmax","m":["clmax","cl max","lower lift","less lift","high lift"]},{"k":"Tip stall / spanwise flow","m":["tip stall","spanwise"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"Why does a swept wing pitch up at the stall?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB87","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"The tips stall first because of spanwise flow. Lift is lost from the tips, which on a swept wing lie behind the CG, so the centre of pressure moves forward and inboard. That gives a nose-up pitch at the stall, which deepens the stall. This is not deep stall: deep stall is a T-tail problem where the tail sits in the wing wake.","keys":[{"k":"Tip stall first","m":["tip stall","tips stall","tip"]},{"k":"Tips behind the CG","m":["behind the cg","aft of the cg","behind cg","aft of cg"]},{"k":"CP moves forward and inboard","m":["forward and inboard","moves forward","cp forward","centre of pressure","center of pressure"]},{"k":"Nose-up pitch","m":["pitch up","nose up","pitch-up","nose-up"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["deep stall"],"why":"Bank error: deep stall is a T-tail wake problem, not swept-wing pitch-up"},{"m":["centre of pressure moves aft","cp moves aft","moves rearward","moves backward","moves aft"],"why":"The CP moves forward and inboard, which is why the nose pitches up"}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"A swept wing tip-stalls. The centre of pressure moves:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB87","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Forward and inboard","Aft and outboard","Aft and inboard","Forward and outboard"],"a":0,"why":"Tips (behind the CG) lose lift, so CP moves forward and inboard: nose-up pitch.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match the term to its correct meaning.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB87","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Swept-wing pitch-up","Tip stall moves CP forward and inboard"],["Deep stall","T-tail in the stalled wing's wake"],["Shock stall","Compressibility (shock-induced) separation"]],"why":"Bank p.59 confuses these; they are three different things.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"What is aquaplaning, what types are there, and at what speed does it start?","src":"Oral bank p.14; OM-A 17.21; Standard theory","ref":"OB88","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Aquaplaning is when the tyre rides on a film of water and loses contact with the runway: braking becomes negligible and directional control is lost.\n- **Dynamic:** standing water lifts the tyre.\n- **Viscous:** a thin film on a smooth or rubber-coated surface.\n- **Reverted rubber:** a locked wheel; friction heat makes steam.\n- **Speed:** dynamic aquaplaning starts at about 9 times the square root of tyre pressure (psi) in kt; about 7.7 root p for a non-rotating tyre at touchdown.\n- Once started it can continue in much shallower water.","keys":[{"k":"Tyre loses contact, rides on water","m":["loses contact","lose contact","film of water","rides on","lifts"]},{"k":"Dynamic","m":["dynamic"]},{"k":"Viscous","m":["viscous"]},{"k":"Reverted rubber","m":["reverted"]},{"k":"9 root p","m":["9 root","9 times","9 x","9 sqrt","9 square root","9√p","9√"]},{"k":"Continues in shallower water","m":["shallower","continue","persists"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-num-9","type":"num","q":"Tyre pressure 196 psi. Dynamic aquaplaning speed using 9√p?","src":"Oral bank p.14","ref":"OB88","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":126,"unit":"kt","tol":1,"why":"9 x 14 = 126 kt.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-num-10","type":"num","q":"Tyre pressure 144 psi. Aquaplaning speed for a non-rotating tyre at touchdown (7.7√p)? (nearest kt)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB88","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":92,"unit":"kt","tol":1,"why":"7.7 x 12 = 92.4 kt.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"Which type of aquaplaning involves a locked wheel and steam from friction heat?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB88","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Reverted rubber","Dynamic","Viscous","Slush"],"a":0,"why":"Reverted rubber: locked wheel, steam.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Which 737 brake system(s) provide hydroplane protection?","src":"FCOM 14.20","ref":"OB88","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Normal and alternate","Normal only","Alternate only","Neither; only autobrake"],"a":0,"why":"Normal and alternate both give skid, locked-wheel, touchdown and hydroplane protection; anti-skid works even with both hydraulic systems lost.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"Once dynamic aquaplaning has started:","src":"OM-A 17.21","ref":"OB88","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["It can continue in much shallower water","It stops as soon as the water gets shallower","It stops below 9√p","Reverse thrust prevents it"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 17.21: it can continue in much shallower water, so plan a distance margin.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"What is Q-feel, and how does the 737 give elevator feel?","src":"Oral bank p.17; FCOM 9.20","ref":"OB89","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"With fully powered hydraulic controls, air loads don't reach the pilot, so feel must be made artificially. Q-feel makes column force proportional to dynamic pressure (q = half rho V squared).\n**737 elevator feel computer:**\n- Makes simulated aerodynamic forces from airspeed (elevator pitot system) and stabiliser position.\n- Fed to the column through the elevator feel and centering unit.\n- Uses hydraulic A or B pressure, whichever is higher.\n- Elevator Feel Shift near the stall raises system A pressure, about doubling column forces.","keys":[{"k":"Artificial feel for powered controls","m":["artificial","powered","hydraulic"]},{"k":"Dynamic pressure","m":["dynamic pressure","half rho","1/2 rho","rho v"]},{"k":"Elevator pitot airspeed","m":["pitot","airspeed"]},{"k":"Stabiliser position","m":["stabiliser","stabilizer","stab position"]},{"k":"Hydraulic A or B, higher","m":["a or b","whichever is higher","system a","system b"]},{"k":"Feel shift doubles force near stall","m":["feel shift","double"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["downspring","bob weight","bobweight"],"why":"Bank error: downsprings and bob weights are stick-force devices on simpler aircraft, not the 737 Q-feel"}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"What happens if the 737 elevator feel system fails (FEEL DIFF PRESS)?","src":"FCOM 9.20; QRH 9.8","ref":"OB89","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"FEEL DIFF PRESS lights if one hydraulic system or one elevator pitot fails.\n- Column forces can be **significantly higher** than normal, especially in the flare.\n- The autopilot can disengage on pitch changes.\n- Do not autoland.\n- Continue normal operation.","keys":[{"k":"FEEL DIFF PRESS light","m":["feel diff","diff press"]},{"k":"One hydraulic system or one pitot failed","m":["pitot","hydraulic"]},{"k":"Forces significantly higher","m":["higher","heavier","increase"]},{"k":"Don't autoland","m":["autoland"]},{"k":"A/P may disengage","m":["disengage","autopilot"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["decrease","lighter","reduced stick force","lower stick force","less force"],"why":"Bank error: on the 737 a feel fault can make column forces significantly higher (QRH 9.8)"}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"The 737 elevator feel computer uses which inputs?","src":"FCOM 9.20","ref":"OB89","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Elevator pitot airspeed and stabiliser position","Static pressure and flap position","AOA and Mach","Downspring and bob weight"],"a":0,"why":"Airspeed from the elevator pitot system and stabiliser position, with hydraulic A or B.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"Elevator Feel Shift near the stall does what to column forces?","src":"FCOM 9.20","ref":"OB89","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["About doubles them","Halves them","Removes them","Adds a stick shaker only"],"a":0,"why":"It raises system A pressure to the feel unit, about doubling column forces.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"Hydraulic supply for the 737 elevator feel computer?","src":"FCOM 9.20","ref":"OB89","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["System A or B, whichever is higher","System A only","System B only","Standby system"],"a":0,"why":"Higher of A or B.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"What is fly-by-wire? What are its advantages?","src":"Oral bank p.22, p.23, p.87","ref":"OB90","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"In fly-by-wire, pilot inputs become electrical signals and flight control computers command the surface actuators. Computers can also stabilise the aeroplane and keep it inside the flight envelope.\n**Advantages:**\n- less weight (so less fuel) and lower maintenance;\n- precise control and envelope protection;\n- common cockpits and handling across a family, so shorter conversions and cross-crew qualification.\nSpectrum: conventional (cables and rods), then hydraulically powered with cable signalling, then FBW.","keys":[{"k":"Electrical signals","m":["electric","signal"]},{"k":"Computers command actuators","m":["computer"]},{"k":"Envelope protection","m":["envelope"]},{"k":"Less weight","m":["weight","lighter"]},{"k":"Common cockpit / cross-crew","m":["common","cross crew","cross-crew","commonality","family"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"Is the 737 MAX fly-by-wire?","src":"FCOM 9.20; FCTM 8.39","ref":"OB90","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"Mostly no: it is conventional.\n- Cables run to hydraulic PCUs for the elevators, ailerons and rudder, with manual reversion if both hydraulic systems fail.\n- But the **spoilers are fly-by-wire**: wheel position sensors signal the Spoiler Control Electronics (SCE), which commands electrically controlled spoiler actuators.\n- Lose electrical power to the SCE and all spoilers are inoperative.\n- In manual reversion the FBW spoilers make initial wheel forces higher.","keys":[{"k":"Mostly conventional / cables","m":["conventional","cable"]},{"k":"Hydraulic PCUs","m":["pcu","power control unit","hydraulic"]},{"k":"Manual reversion","m":["manual reversion","manual"]},{"k":"Spoilers are FBW","m":["spoiler"]},{"k":"Spoiler Control Electronics","m":["sce","spoiler control electronics"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["not fly by wire at all","no fly by wire at all"],"why":"The 737 MAX spoilers are fly-by-wire through the SCE (FCOM 9.20)"}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"On the 737 MAX, loss of electrical power to the Spoiler Control Electronics results in:","src":"FCOM 9.20","ref":"OB90","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["All spoilers inoperative","Spoilers revert to cable control","Only ground spoilers lost","No effect"],"a":0,"why":"FCOM 9.20.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-order-3","type":"order","q":"Order the flight control architectures from simplest to most advanced.","src":"Oral bank p.87","ref":"OB90","asked":true,"prio":3,"items":["Conventional (cables and rods)","Hydraulically powered with cable signalling","Fly-by-wire"],"why":"Oral bank p.87 spectrum.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"What are the main differences between Airbus and Boeing?","src":"FCTM 1.3; FCOM 9.20; Standard theory","ref":"OB91","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Controls:** Boeing uses a control wheel and column, linked and back-driven, so each pilot sees the other's and the autopilot's inputs; pilot input can override the A/P and A/T. Airbus uses sidesticks, not linked or back-driven.\n- **Protection:** Airbus normal law gives envelope protection; protections are lost in alternate and direct law.\n- **Feel:** Boeing columns have artificial feel (737 elevator feel computer); Airbus sidesticks have spring feel only.\n- **Thrust levers:** Boeing A/T back-drives the levers; Airbus levers stay in detents.\n- Both mount engines on underwing pylons.","keys":[{"k":"Wheel/column vs sidestick","m":["sidestick","side stick","yoke","column"]},{"k":"Back-driven / linked","m":["back driven","back-driven","backdriven","linked"]},{"k":"Airbus envelope protection / laws","m":["normal law","protection","law"]},{"k":"Artificial feel vs spring feel","m":["feel"]},{"k":"Thrust levers move vs detents","m":["detent","thrust lever"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"Where are the engines mounted on both the 737 and A320?","src":"Oral bank p.18 (corrected)","ref":"OB91","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["On pylons under the wing, ahead of the leading edge","Boeing forward of the wing, Airbus under the wing","On the rear fuselage","Boeing under the wing, Airbus on the wing"],"a":0,"why":"Bank error p.18. Both use underwing pylons ahead of the leading edge.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-16","type":"mcq","q":"Which Boeing types are fly-by-wire?","src":"Standard theory; FCOM 9.20","ref":"OB91","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["777 and 787 (and the 737 MAX spoilers)","None","Only the 747-8","All Boeing types"],"a":0,"why":"Bank says Boeing is not FBW. The 777 and 787 are FBW, and the 737 MAX has FBW spoilers.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-17","type":"mcq","q":"In a Boeing, the autothrottle moves the thrust levers. In an Airbus:","src":"FCTM 1.3; Standard theory","ref":"OB91","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Levers stay fixed in detents","Levers are also back-driven","There is no autothrust","Levers move only in alternate law"],"a":0,"why":"Airbus thrust levers stay in detents.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"Explain the variable incidence (trimming) tailplane. Advantages and disadvantages?","src":"Oral bank p.25, p.26","ref":"OB92","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Trim by changing the whole tailplane's incidence until its load equals the balancing load the elevator was giving, so the elevator returns to about neutral.\n**Advantages:** less trim drag; full elevator range stays available; large trim authority for wide CG and speed ranges.\n**Disadvantage:** heavier and more complex than a trim tab.","keys":[{"k":"Whole tailplane incidence changes","m":["incidence","whole tailplane","whole stabiliser","entire tailplane","moving tailplane"]},{"k":"Elevator returns to neutral","m":["neutral","streamline"]},{"k":"Less trim drag","m":["drag"]},{"k":"Full elevator range available","m":["full elevator","elevator range","elevator authority"]},{"k":"Large trim authority (CG/speed)","m":["authority","cg range","wide cg"]},{"k":"Heavier / complex","m":["heavier","complex","weight"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"How is stabiliser trim done on the 737?","src":"FCOM 9.20","ref":"OB92","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- A movable stabiliser driven by one electric trim motor (main electric or autopilot trim), or by cables from the manual trim wheels. Two independent brakes hold it.\n- Authority: main electric -0.45 to 14.0 units flaps extended, 3.85 to 14.0 flaps up; autopilot -0.45 to 14.0; manual -0.70 to 16.4.\n- STAB TRIM PRI and B/U cutout switches disconnect autopilot, main electric and STS inputs.\n- Outside the green band, an intermittent horn sounds when take-off is attempted.","keys":[{"k":"Electric trim motor","m":["electric","motor"]},{"k":"Manual trim wheels via cables","m":["trim wheel","manual","cable"]},{"k":"Two brakes","m":["brake"]},{"k":"Cutout switches","m":["cutout","cut out","cut-out"]},{"k":"Green band / take-off horn","m":["green band","horn","takeoff warning","take off warning","take-off warning"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"What is the 737 Speed Trim System, and how do the column cutouts interact with MCAS?","src":"FCOM 9.20","ref":"OB92","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"The Speed Trim System has two functions:\n- **speed trim**: speed stability;\n- **MCAS**: pitch stability augmentation.\nIt works only with the autopilot off.\n- STAB TRIM PRI and B/U cutout switches disconnect autopilot, main electric and STS inputs.\n- Aft column does **not** interrupt MCAS nose-down trim; forward column interrupts MCAS nose-up.","keys":[{"k":"Speed trim: speed stability","m":["speed stability"]},{"k":"MCAS: pitch stability augmentation","m":["mcas","pitch stability","augmentation"]},{"k":"A/P off only","m":["autopilot off","a/p off","ap off","manual flight"]},{"k":"Cutout switches stop STS","m":["cutout","cut out","cut-out","pri and b/u"]},{"k":"Aft column does not stop MCAS nose-down","m":["aft column","does not interrupt","doesnt interrupt","not interrupt"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-match-3","type":"match","q":"Match the 737 trim mode to its authority (units).","src":"FCOM 9.20","ref":"OB92","asked":true,"prio":3,"pairs":[["Main electric, flaps up","3.85 to 14.0"],["Main electric, flaps extended","-0.45 to 14.0"],["Autopilot","-0.45 to 14.0"],["Manual trim wheel","-0.70 to 16.4"]],"why":"FCOM 9.20.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-18","type":"mcq","q":"737 manual (trim wheel) stabiliser trim range?","src":"FCOM 9.20","ref":"OB92","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["-0.70 to 16.4 units","-0.45 to 14.0 units","3.85 to 14.0 units","0 to 17.0 units"],"a":0,"why":"Manual has the widest range.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-19","type":"mcq","q":"Does pulling the column aft interrupt MCAS nose-down trim?","src":"FCOM 9.20","ref":"OB92","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["No; aft column does not interrupt MCAS nose-down","Yes, always","Only with flaps up","Only above 250 kt"],"a":0,"why":"Aft column does not interrupt MCAS nose-down; forward column interrupts MCAS nose-up. Use the cutout switches.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-20","type":"mcq","q":"The Speed Trim System (including MCAS) operates:","src":"FCOM 9.20","ref":"OB92","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Only with the autopilot off","Only with the autopilot on","Always","Only on approach"],"a":0,"why":"FCOM 9.20.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-21","type":"recall","q":"How does the 737 pressurisation system work?","src":"Oral bank p.19; FCOM 2.40","ref":"OB93","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"A roughly constant inflow of conditioned bleed air from the packs; cabin pressure is set by how much is let out.\n- Pressure and ventilation are controlled by modulating the **outflow valve** and the **overboard exhaust valve**. There is no separate cabin inlet valve.\n- Two identical automatic controllers (AUTO, ALTN) plus MAN, all driving the outflow valve by DC motor.\n- Cabin altitude is rate-controlled, up to 8,000 ft at the 41,000 ft certified ceiling.","keys":[{"k":"Constant inflow from packs","m":["pack","bleed","inflow"]},{"k":"Outflow valve controls pressure","m":["outflow"]},{"k":"Overboard exhaust valve","m":["overboard exhaust"]},{"k":"AUTO, ALTN, MAN","m":["altn","alternate","auto","manual"]},{"k":"8,000 ft cabin at 41,000 ft","m":["8000","8 000","8 thousand","eight thousand"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-recall-22","type":"recall","q":"What do the positive and negative relief valves do on the 737, and what are the differential limits?","src":"FCOM 2.40, 15.10","ref":"OB93","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Two positive pressure relief valves** limit the differential to 9.1 psi.\n- **A negative relief valve** stops outside pressure exceeding cabin pressure, e.g. in a fast descent.\n- Cruise differential: 7.45 psid at or below FL280; 7.80 psid FL280 to FL370; 8.35 psid above FL370.\n- AUTO FAIL if differential over 8.75 psi, cabin rate over 2000 sea-level fpm, or cabin altitude over 15,800 ft; it switches to ALTN.\n- Cabin altitude warning above 10,000 ft.","keys":[{"k":"Positive relief 9.1 psi","m":["9.1"]},{"k":"Negative relief: outside > cabin","m":["negative","outside pressure","ambient"]},{"k":"8.35 psid above FL370","m":["8.35"]},{"k":"AUTO FAIL 8.75 psi / 15,800 ft","m":["8.75","15800","15 800"]},{"k":"Cabin altitude warning 10,000 ft","m":["10000","10 000","ten thousand"]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-21","type":"mcq","q":"737 positive pressure relief valves limit the differential to:","src":"FCOM 2.40","ref":"OB93","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["9.1 psi","8.35 psi","8.75 psi","7.80 psi"],"a":0,"why":"Two positive relief valves: 9.1 psi.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-22","type":"mcq","q":"737 cruise differential above FL370?","src":"FCOM 2.40","ref":"OB93","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["8.35 psid","7.80 psid","7.45 psid","9.1 psid"],"a":0,"why":"8.35 psid above FL370.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-23","type":"mcq","q":"737 cruise differential at or below FL280?","src":"FCOM 2.40","ref":"OB93","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["7.45 psid","7.80 psid","8.35 psid","6.50 psid"],"a":0,"why":"7.45 psid at or below FL280.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-24","type":"mcq","q":"737 cruise differential between FL280 and FL370?","src":"FCOM 2.40","ref":"OB93","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["7.80 psid","7.45 psid","8.35 psid","8.75 psid"],"a":0,"why":"7.80 psid FL280 to FL370.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-25","type":"mcq","q":"Which valve prevents outside pressure exceeding cabin pressure in a fast descent?","src":"FCOM 2.40","ref":"OB93","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Negative relief valve","Positive relief valve","Outflow valve","Overboard exhaust valve"],"a":0,"why":"Negative relief valve.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-26","type":"mcq","q":"AUTO FAIL trips (controller not responding) at cabin altitude above:","src":"FCOM 2.40","ref":"OB93","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["15,800 ft","10,000 ft","14,000 ft","8,000 ft"],"a":0,"why":"Differential over 8.75 psi, rate over 2000 fpm, or cabin over 15,800 ft.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-27","type":"mcq","q":"737 cabin altitude warning comes on above:","src":"FCOM 15.10","ref":"OB93","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["10,000 ft","8,000 ft","14,000 ft","15,800 ft"],"a":0,"why":"Cabin altitude warning above 10,000 ft.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-mcq-28","type":"mcq","q":"Which valve sets the 737's cabin pressure?","src":"FCOM 2.40","ref":"OB93","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Outflow valve (with overboard exhaust valve)","Cabin inlet valve","Pack valve","Positive relief valve"],"a":0,"why":"No separate inlet valve; packs set inflow, outflow valve modulates.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-num-11","type":"num","q":"737 cabin altitude at the 41,000 ft certified ceiling (normal schedule)?","src":"FCOM 2.40","ref":"OB93","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":8000,"unit":"ft","why":"Up to 8,000 ft at 41,000 ft.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-num-12","type":"num","q":"AUTO FAIL trigger: cabin rate exceeding how many sea-level fpm?","src":"FCOM 2.40","ref":"OB93","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":2000,"unit":"fpm","why":"Plus or minus 2000 sea-level fpm.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-num-13","type":"num","q":"AUTO FAIL trigger: differential pressure over how many psi?","src":"FCOM 2.40","ref":"OB93","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":8.75,"unit":"psi","why":"8.75 psi.","topic":20},{"id":"t20-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"During the take-off roll you hear a loud bang and see EGT rising on No.2.\"","src":"FCTM 3.16; QRH 7.2; Oral bank p.12","ref":"OB82","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What is it likely to be?","a":"Compressor stall or surge: bangs, vibration, rising EGT, N1/N2 not following the lever.","keys":[{"k":"Stall/surge","m":["stall","surge"]}]},{"q":"What causes it on the ground?","a":"Disturbed intake flow (crosswind over 20 kt, tailwind over 10 kt with thrust set on brakes), too much fuel, damaged blades, icing.","keys":[{"k":"Crosswind/tailwind","m":["crosswind","tailwind"]},{"k":"Rolling take-off prevention","m":["rolling","brake"]}]},{"q":"Airborne, what's the NNC?","a":"Engine Limit or Surge or Stall: A/T off, retard the lever until within limits.","keys":[{"k":"A/T off, retard","m":["retard","a/t off","autothrottle off","reduce"]}]},{"q":"Indications stabilise at reduced thrust. Anything else?","a":"Advance slowly to a surge-free setting; transponder TA ONLY.","keys":[{"k":"TA only","m":["ta only","transponder ta","tcas ta"]}]},{"q":"And if EGT keeps rising?","a":"Start lever CUTOFF.","keys":[{"k":"CUTOFF","m":["cutoff","cut off","shut"]}]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"You're starting No.1 and EGT hasn't moved.\"","src":"FCOM 7.20; QRH 7.1; FCOM NP.21.30; FCOM L.10.8","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What is this called?","a":"A wet start (no light-off).","keys":[{"k":"Wet start","m":["wet start","no light"]}]},{"q":"What does the EEC do?","a":"On the ground it cuts ignition and fuel 15 s after start lever IDLE.","keys":[{"k":"15 s","m":["15"]}]},{"q":"What's the QRH action?","a":"Aborted Engine Start: start lever CUTOFF, motor 60 s.","keys":[{"k":"CUTOFF","m":["cutoff","cut off"]},{"k":"Motor 60 s","m":["60"]}]},{"q":"How many attempts and cooling?","a":"3 min per attempt, 10 s between; after three consecutive aborted starts, 15 min cooling.","keys":[{"k":"3 min","m":["3 min","three min"]},{"k":"15 min after three","m":["15 min","15 minute"]}]},{"q":"Would the EEC protect you in an in-flight start?","a":"No. The abnormal-start protection works on ground starts only.","keys":[{"k":"Ground only","m":["ground"]}]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t20-chain-3","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"On approach the FEEL DIFF PRESS light comes on.\"","src":"FCOM 9.20; QRH 9.8","ref":"OB89","asked":true,"prio":2,"steps":[{"q":"What does Q-feel do?","a":"Makes column force proportional to dynamic pressure, since hydraulic controls don't feed air loads back.","keys":[{"k":"Dynamic pressure","m":["dynamic pressure"]}]},{"q":"What drives the 737 feel computer?","a":"Elevator pitot airspeed and stabiliser position, using hydraulic A or B, whichever is higher.","keys":[{"k":"Pitot","m":["pitot"]},{"k":"Stabiliser","m":["stabili"]}]},{"q":"Why did the light come on?","a":"One hydraulic system or one elevator pitot has failed.","keys":[{"k":"Hydraulic or pitot","m":["hydraulic","pitot"]}]},{"q":"Effect on your flare?","a":"Column forces can be significantly higher; A/P may disengage; no autoland.","keys":[{"k":"Higher forces","m":["higher","heavier"]},{"k":"No autoland","m":["autoland"]}]}],"topic":20},{"id":"t21-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"What is the ITCZ, and how does it affect India?","src":"Oral bank p.7; Standard theory","ref":"OB94","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"The ITCZ is the low-pressure belt where the north-east and south-east trade winds converge near the equator.\n- It follows the sun with a lag: north in the northern summer, south in January.\n- Over India in July and August it lies from Punjab and Haryana to the head of the Bay of Bengal, where it is called the **monsoon trough** (axis of the monsoon trough).\n- Strong convergence gives deep convection, lines of embedded CBs, heavy showers, turbulence and icing.","keys":[{"k":"Convergence of NE and SE trades","m":["trade wind","trades","converge"]},{"k":"Low pressure belt","m":["low pressure","low-pressure"]},{"k":"Follows the sun with a lag","m":["follows the sun","lag","moves north","shifts north"]},{"k":"Monsoon trough over India","m":["monsoon trough"]},{"k":"Punjab to Bay of Bengal","m":["punjab","haryana","bay of bengal"]},{"k":"Embedded CBs, convection","m":["cbs","cumulonimbus","convection","thunderstorm"]}],"topic":21},{"id":"t21-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"What is the OM-A guidance for thunderstorms near the ITCZ or monsoon trough?","src":"OM-A 17.3.6.1","ref":"OB94","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Avoid the lower two-thirds of thunderstorms. Downdrafts spreading out at the surface give violent windshear. Expect lines of embedded CBs with heavy showers, turbulence and icing along the monsoon trough, so plan deviations early with the weather radar.","keys":[{"k":"Avoid lower two-thirds","m":["two thirds","two-thirds","2/3","lower two"]},{"k":"Downdrafts at surface","m":["downdraft","downdraught","outflow"]},{"k":"Violent windshear","m":["windshear","wind shear"]}],"topic":21},{"id":"t21-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"Over India in July and August, the ITCZ is called the:","src":"Oral bank p.7","ref":"OB94","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Monsoon trough","Western disturbance","Subtropical jet","Norwester line"],"a":0,"why":"It lies from Punjab/Haryana to the head of the Bay of Bengal as the monsoon trough.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"Where does the ITCZ lie in January?","src":"Oral bank p.7","ref":"OB94","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["South of the equator","Over north India","Over the Himalayas","Along 27°N"],"a":0,"why":"It follows the sun with a lag: south in January, north in the northern summer.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"In July and August over India, the monsoon trough lies from:","src":"Oral bank p.7","ref":"OB94","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Punjab and Haryana to the head of the Bay of Bengal","Kerala to Gujarat","Kashmir to Assam","Mumbai to Chennai"],"a":0,"why":"Oral bank p.7.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"What is the subtropical jet stream over India?","src":"Oral bank p.7; OM-A 17.3.6.3; Standard theory","ref":"OB95","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Winter (December to February):** a westerly jet over north India, around 27°N and about 12 km, typically 100 to 120 kt.\n- In summer it moves north of the Himalayas, and a tropical easterly jet sets up over the peninsula.\n- **Hazard:** clear air turbulence. Jets over 110 kt at the core often have significant turbulence in the sloping tropopause above the core, in the front below it, and on the low-pressure side, with strong windshear.\n- Strong headwinds westbound and tailwinds eastbound in winter.","keys":[{"k":"Westerly, winter","m":["westerly","winter"]},{"k":"Around 27 N","m":["27 n","27 degrees","27°","27 deg"]},{"k":"About 12 km","m":["12 km","12km","12 kilometre","40000","40 000"]},{"k":"100 to 120 kt","m":["100","120"]},{"k":"CAT","m":["clear air turbulence","clear air","cat risk","turbulence"]},{"k":"Moves north of Himalayas in summer / TEJ","m":["north of the himalaya","easterly jet","tropical easterly"]}],"topic":21},{"id":"t21-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"Typical position of the winter subtropical jet over north India?","src":"Oral bank p.7","ref":"OB95","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["About 27°N at about 12 km","About 15°N at about 9 km","About 40°N at about 15 km","About 5°N at about 16 km"],"a":0,"why":"Around 27°N, about 12 km, 100 to 120 kt.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-num-1","type":"num","q":"Above what core speed do jets often have significant turbulence (OM-A)?","src":"OM-A 17.3.6.3","ref":"OB95","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":110,"unit":"kt","why":"Jets over 110 kt at the core: turbulence above the core in the sloping tropopause, in the front below, and on the low-pressure side.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"Where is CAT most likely around a strong jet (over 110 kt core)?","src":"OM-A 17.3.6.3","ref":"OB95","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Sloping tropopause above the core, front below it, and the low-pressure side","Only directly in the core","Only on the high-pressure side","Only below 10,000 ft"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 17.3.6.3.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"In the Indian summer, which jet sets up over the peninsula?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB95","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Tropical easterly jet","Subtropical westerly jet","Polar front jet","Low-level westerly jet"],"a":0,"why":"The STJ moves north of the Himalayas and a tropical easterly jet appears.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"What are Norwesters (Kalbaisakhi)?","src":"Oral bank p.7; OM-A 17.3.6.1, 17.3.6.4; Standard theory","ref":"OB96","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Norwesters are violent pre-monsoon thunderstorms and squall lines over eastern and north-eastern India (Bengal, Jharkhand, Odisha, Assam) in March, April and May.\n- They usually arrive from the north-west, hence the name.\n- They form from strong surface heating and lifting over high ground such as the Chhota Nagpur plateau, with moisture from the Bay of Bengal.\n- Hazards: severe squalls, hail, heavy rain, lightning, microbursts and windshear, mostly afternoon and evening.","keys":[{"k":"Pre-monsoon thunderstorms / squalls","m":["pre monsoon","pre-monsoon","squall","thunderstorm"]},{"k":"East / north-east India","m":["bengal","east","assam","odisha"]},{"k":"March to May","m":["march","april","pre monsoon months"]},{"k":"From the north-west","m":["north west","north-west","northwest"]},{"k":"Chhota Nagpur / Bay of Bengal moisture","m":["chhota nagpur","chota nagpur","bay of bengal","moisture"]},{"k":"Hail, microbursts, windshear","m":["hail","microburst","windshear","wind shear"]}],"topic":21},{"id":"t21-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"Norwesters (Kalbaisakhi) occur mainly in:","src":"Oral bank p.7","ref":"OB96","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["March to May, east and north-east India","December to February, north-west India","July to August, west coast","October to November, south-east coast"],"a":0,"why":"Pre-monsoon, eastern India.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"Which high ground helps trigger Norwesters?","src":"Oral bank p.7","ref":"OB96","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Chhota Nagpur plateau","Western Ghats","Deccan plateau","Aravalli range"],"a":0,"why":"Heating and lifting over the Chhota Nagpur plateau with Bay of Bengal moisture.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"What are western disturbances, and why is visibility so poor in north India in winter?","src":"Oral bank p.7; Standard theory","ref":"OB97","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Western disturbances are extratropical low-pressure systems, often old occluded fronts, that come from the west in the subtropical westerlies to north-west India, mainly in winter.\n- Weather: cloud, rain over the plains, snow over the Himalayas, then a cold spell behind them.\n- **Fog:** after one passes, moist ground, clear skies, light winds and long nights give **radiation fog** across the Indo-Gangetic plain, so low-visibility operations, especially December and January.","keys":[{"k":"Extratropical lows from the west","m":["extratropical","low pressure","from the west","westerlies"]},{"k":"Occluded fronts","m":["occlu","front"]},{"k":"Rain on plains, snow on Himalayas","m":["snow","rain"]},{"k":"Radiation fog","m":["radiation fog"]},{"k":"Moist ground, clear sky, light wind, long night","m":["moist","clear sk","light wind","long night"]},{"k":"Indo-Gangetic plain, Dec/Jan","m":["indo gangetic","indo-gangetic","december","january"]}],"topic":21},{"id":"t21-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"The winter fog over the Indo-Gangetic plain after a western disturbance is mainly:","src":"Oral bank p.7","ref":"OB97","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Radiation fog","Advection fog","Frontal fog","Sea fog"],"a":0,"why":"Moist ground, clear skies, light winds and long nights: radiation fog.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"Which rain does the north-east monsoon bring in winter?","src":"Oral bank p.7","ref":"OB97","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Rain over the south-east coast","Rain over the west coast","Snow over the Himalayas","Rain over Rajasthan"],"a":0,"why":"The retreating monsoon's rain over the south-east coast.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-order-1","type":"order","q":"Sequence of a western disturbance and its aftermath over north India.","src":"Oral bank p.7","ref":"OB97","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Low from the west brings cloud, rain on plains, snow on hills","Disturbance passes; cold spell","Clear skies, light winds, moist ground, long night","Radiation fog over the Indo-Gangetic plain"],"why":"Oral bank p.7.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"What are Rossby waves and cut-off lows?","src":"Oral bank p.7; Standard theory","ref":"OB98","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Rossby waves:** very long waves (thousands of km) in the upper westerlies. They meander between troughs and ridges and steer surface lows and highs.\n- **Cut-off low:** a deep trough in the upper westerlies can pinch off into a closed low separate from the main flow. It moves slowly, often towards the equator, and gives prolonged unsettled weather.\n- Temperature is not their defining feature: they are planetary-scale waves in the wind flow.","keys":[{"k":"Long waves, thousands of km","m":["long wave","thousands","planetary"]},{"k":"Upper westerlies","m":["upper westerl","westerlies","upper wind"]},{"k":"Troughs and ridges","m":["trough","ridge"]},{"k":"Steer surface systems","m":["steer"]},{"k":"Cut-off low: closed low pinched off","m":["pinch","closed low","cut off","cut-off","separate"]},{"k":"Slow, prolonged bad weather","m":["slow","prolonged"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["very cold","cold wave","cold high altitude"],"why":"Bank error: Rossby waves are planetary waves in the upper westerlies; temperature is not their defining feature"}],"topic":21},{"id":"t21-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"Best description of Rossby waves?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB98","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Very long planetary waves in the upper westerlies","Very cold, high-altitude waves","Mountain lee waves","Sea-breeze fronts"],"a":0,"why":"Bank p.7 says 'very cold, high altitude waves'; wrong.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"A cut-off low typically:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB98","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Moves slowly, often towards the equator, with prolonged unsettled weather","Moves quickly eastward and clears in hours","Forms only over the ocean in summer","Is a surface thermal low"],"a":0,"why":"Standard theory.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the Indian weather system to its season.","src":"Oral bank p.7","ref":"OB94","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Norwesters (Kalbaisakhi)","March to May"],["Monsoon trough over north India","July and August"],["Subtropical westerly jet over north India","December to February"],["Western disturbance fog","December and January"]],"why":"Oral bank p.7.","topic":21},{"id":"t21-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"It's January, you're flying Mumbai to Delhi for an early morning arrival.\"","src":"Oral bank p.7; OM-A 17.3.6.3","ref":"OB97","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What weather do you expect at Delhi?","a":"Possible radiation fog over the Indo-Gangetic plain, especially after a western disturbance: low-visibility operations.","keys":[{"k":"Radiation fog","m":["radiation fog","fog"]},{"k":"LVO","m":["low vis","lvo","low-vis"]}]},{"q":"What is a western disturbance?","a":"Extratropical low, often an old occluded front, arriving from the west in the subtropical westerlies.","keys":[{"k":"Extratropical low","m":["extratropical","low"]},{"k":"From the west","m":["west"]}]},{"q":"Why does fog form after it passes?","a":"Moist ground, clear skies, light winds and long nights.","keys":[{"k":"Moist ground","m":["moist"]},{"k":"Clear sky / light wind","m":["clear","light wind"]}]},{"q":"En route at FL370, any jet?","a":"Subtropical westerly jet around 27°N, about 12 km, 100 to 120 kt; CAT risk if core over 110 kt.","keys":[{"k":"STJ","m":["subtropical","jet"]},{"k":"CAT","m":["clear air","turbulence"]}]}],"topic":21},{"id":"t21-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"April afternoon, approaching Kolkata. Radar shows a line of cells to the north-west.\"","src":"Oral bank p.7; OM-A 17.3.6.1, 17.3.6.4","ref":"OB96","asked":true,"prio":2,"steps":[{"q":"What is this likely to be?","a":"A Norwester (Kalbaisakhi) squall line, pre-monsoon.","keys":[{"k":"Norwester","m":["norwester","kalbaisakhi","nor wester"]}]},{"q":"How does it form?","a":"Strong surface heating, lifting over the Chhota Nagpur plateau, moisture from the Bay of Bengal.","keys":[{"k":"Heating","m":["heating"]},{"k":"Moisture","m":["moisture","bay of bengal"]}]},{"q":"Hazards?","a":"Severe squalls, hail, heavy rain, lightning, microbursts and windshear.","keys":[{"k":"Hail","m":["hail"]},{"k":"Windshear/microburst","m":["windshear","microburst","wind shear"]}]},{"q":"How do you avoid it?","a":"Avoid the lower two-thirds of thunderstorms; follow thunderstorm and windshear guidance.","keys":[{"k":"Lower two-thirds","m":["two thirds","two-thirds","2/3"]}]}],"topic":21},{"id":"t3-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"What is PBN, and what do the accuracy and integrity figures in an RNP value mean?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **PBN:** an approval built from a navigation specification plus navaid infrastructure, used within an airspace concept.\n- **RNP = RNAV + on-board performance monitoring and alerting.** In practice RNP needs GNSS.\n- **Accuracy:** the number is the lateral accuracy in NM for 95% of flight time.\n- **Integrity:** containment of 2 x RNP at 99.999%.\n- **Errors:** TSE = NSE + FTE + PDE; FTE is the largest, PDE about 0.","keys":[{"k":"Nav spec + infrastructure + airspace concept","m":["navigation specification","nav spec","infrastructure","airspace concept"]},{"k":"RNP = RNAV + monitoring and alerting","m":["monitoring and alerting","on board monitoring","onboard monitoring","alerting"]},{"k":"95% of flight time","m":["95 percent","95 per","ninety five"]},{"k":"2 x RNP at 99.999%","m":["99.999","99999","twice","2 x rnp","2 times"]},{"k":"TSE = NSE + FTE + PDE","m":["tse","total system error"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-1","type":"num","q":"An RNP value is the lateral accuracy in NM that must be met for what percentage of the flight time?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":95,"unit":"%","why":"95% of the flight time.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"What is the RNP integrity (containment) requirement?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["2 x RNP at 99.999%","1 x RNP at 95%","2 x RNP at 95%","3 x RNP at 99.9%"],"a":0,"why":"Accuracy is 1 x RNP for 95% of the time; containment is 2 x RNP at 99.999%.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"TSE = NSE + FTE + PDE. Which component is normally the largest?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["FTE (flight technical error)","NSE (navigation system error)","PDE (path definition error)","They are equal"],"a":0,"why":"FTE is the largest; PDE is about 0.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the DGCA 2014 Ops Circular number to its PBN specification.","src":"DGCA Ops Circulars 6 to 14 of 2014","ref":"Key facts: PBN","prio":3,"pairs":[["6 of 2014","RNAV 10"],["7 of 2014","RNAV 5"],["8 of 2014","RNAV 1 / 2"],["9 of 2014","RNP 1"],["10 of 2014","RNP 4"],["11 of 2014","RNP APCH"]],"why":"Ops Circulars 6 to 14 of 2014: also 12 APV baro-VNAV, 13 RNP AR, 14 RNP 2.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"Which DGCA 2014 Ops Circular covers RNP AR?","src":"DGCA Ops Circulars 6 to 14 of 2014","ref":"Key facts: PBN","prio":3,"o":["13 of 2014","11 of 2014","12 of 2014","14 of 2014"],"a":0,"why":"11 RNP APCH, 12 APV baro-VNAV, 13 RNP AR, 14 RNP 2.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"How is PBN capability shown in the ICAO flight plan?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: PBN","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Item 10 R, with PBN/ codes in item 18","Item 10 W, with RNP/ codes in item 18","Item 18 PBN/ only","Item 10 G only"],"a":0,"why":"Item 10 R plus item 18 PBN/ codes (RNP 4 = L1). The approved specs are in the AOC OpsSpecs.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"What is the item 18 PBN/ code for RNP 4?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: PBN","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["L1","A1","B1","D1"],"a":0,"why":"RNP 4 is filed as PBN/L1.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match the PBN spec to its B737 equipment requirement.","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["RNAV 10","2 FMC, 2 CDU, 2 PFD/ND, 2 IRS (two LRNS)"],["RNAV 5","1 FMC, 2 IRS, and 1 GPS or VOR/DME or DME/DME"],["RNAV 1 / 2","1 GPS or DME/DME"],["RNP 1","Two GPS"],["RNP 4","GNSS mandatory, 2 LRNS"]],"why":"RNAV 10 needs the most hardware because there are no ground aids.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-match-3","type":"match","q":"Match the spec to its cross-track limit.","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["RNAV 10","5 NM"],["RNAV 5","2.5 NM"],["RNAV 2","1 NM"],["RNAV 1","0.5 NM"],["RNP 2","1 NM"]],"why":"Cross-track limit is half the accuracy value.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-2","type":"num","q":"RNAV 10 with GNSS: what is the maximum FDE outage allowed?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":34,"unit":"min","why":"With GNSS the FDE outage limit is 34 min; without GNSS the IRS time limit applies.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-3","type":"num","q":"RNAV 5 on IRS alone: how long after the last radio update may you continue?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2,"unit":"h","why":"IRS alone: 2 h from the last update.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-4","type":"num","q":"RNP 4 with CPDLC and ADS-C supports what lateral and longitudinal separation?","src":"OM-A Ch 40, 40.14.3.2","ref":"Key facts: PBN","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":30,"unit":"NM","why":"30 NM lateral and longitudinal.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"What are the RNP APCH minima types, and what do you check before the approach?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN (RNP APCH)","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Minima:** LNAV (MDA; add 50 ft for DDA), LNAV/VNAV (DA, baro-VNAV), LPV (SBAS).\n- **Before the approach:**\n  - RAIM check.\n  - Never DIRECT TO the FAF.\n  - DIRECT TO the IF only if the track change is 45° or less.\n  - Established by the FAF.\n- **Temperature:** outside the chart range, LNAV only.\n- **Lowest APV/baro-VNAV DH:** 250 ft.","keys":[{"k":"LNAV MDA + 50 ft DDA","m":["lnav","mda"]},{"k":"LNAV/VNAV DA","m":["lnav vnav","baro vnav","barovnav"]},{"k":"LPV (SBAS)","m":["lpv"]},{"k":"RAIM check","m":["raim"]},{"k":"No DIRECT TO the FAF","m":["faf"]},{"k":"IF only with 45° or less","m":["45 deg","forty five"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-5","type":"num","q":"RNP APCH: you may go DIRECT TO the IF only if the track change is not more than how many degrees?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN (RNP APCH)","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":45,"unit":"°","why":"DIRECT TO the IF only with 45° or less; never DIRECT TO the FAF.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-6","type":"num","q":"RNP APCH lateral scaling on the final approach segment is plus or minus how many NM?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN (RNP APCH)","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":0.3,"unit":"NM","why":"Initial 1.0, final 0.3, missed approach 1.0 NM. FTE limit is half of that.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-order-1","type":"order","q":"RNP APCH lateral scaling: put the segments in order of flight with their values.","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN (RNP APCH)","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Initial: +/-1.0 NM","Final: +/-0.3 NM","Missed approach: +/-1.0 NM"],"why":"1.0, 0.3, 1.0 NM. FTE limit is half of each.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-7","type":"num","q":"Baro-VNAV vertical deviation limit on an RNP APCH: plus or minus how many feet?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN (RNP APCH)","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":75,"unit":"ft","why":"Baro-VNAV deviation +/-75 ft.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-8","type":"num","q":"What is the lowest DH for an APV baro-VNAV approach?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN (RNP APCH)","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":250,"unit":"ft","why":"Lowest APV/baro-VNAV DH is 250 ft.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"What do you add to the LNAV MDA to get the DDA?","src":"OM-A Ch 40; 28.2.1","ref":"Key facts: PBN (RNP APCH)","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["50 ft","100 ft","30 ft","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"DDA = MDA + 50 ft, flown as a CDFA.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"What are the advantages of RNP approaches?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN (RNP APCH)","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- Straight-in approaches to runways without an ILS.\n- A stable 3D path (CDFA), instead of dive and drive.\n- Lower minima than a conventional non-precision approach.\n- Fewer ground aids needed.\n- Shorter tracks, so less fuel and time.","keys":[{"k":"Runways without ILS","m":["without ils","without an ils","no ils"]},{"k":"Stable 3D path","m":["3d path","3 d","stable","constant descent"]},{"k":"Lower minima","m":["lower minima","lower mini"]},{"k":"Fewer ground aids","m":["ground aid","ground navaid","fewer"]},{"k":"Shorter tracks","m":["shorter","fuel"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"Which set lists RNP path terminators from the reference?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: PBN (fixes and routes)","prio":3,"o":["IF, TF, RF, HA, HF, HM","VA, VM, VI, CA, CF, DF","IF, VA, FM, RF, VI, HM","TF, DF, CI, VR, HA, FA"],"a":0,"why":"IF initial fix, TF track to fix, RF radius to fix, HA/HF/HM holding legs.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"A new AIRAC cycle becomes effective today. When may you use it?","src":"OM-A 3.2","ref":"Key facts: PBN (fixes and routes)","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Only if the flight starts at or after 0001Z on the effective date; never switch in flight","From local midnight; switch in flight if needed","Any time on the effective date, including in flight","Only after the first flight of the day completes"],"a":0,"why":"A new cycle is used only if the flight starts at or after 0001Z on the effective date. Never switch cycles in flight.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-match-4","type":"match","q":"GPS constellation: match each item to its value.","src":"Standard theory; NR64","ref":"Key facts: GNSS","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Number of satellites","24 or more"],["Orbital planes","6"],["Altitude","20,200 km"],["Orbital period","12 h"],["Inclination","55°"]],"why":"Standard GPS figures; the bank's 56° inclination is wrong.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-9","type":"num","q":"What is the GPS L1 frequency?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: GNSS","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1575.42,"unit":"MHz","why":"L1 is 1575.42 MHz (C/A code); L2 and L5 are also used.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"What polarisation does the GPS signal use?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: GNSS","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Right-hand circular","Left-hand circular","Horizontal","Vertical"],"a":0,"why":"GPS uses right-hand circular polarisation.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-match-5","type":"match","q":"Match the GPS function to the number of satellites needed.","src":"Standard theory; NR12","ref":"Key facts: GNSS","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["2D fix","3"],["3D fix (x, y, z, clock)","4"],["RAIM fault detection","5"],["FDE (detect and exclude)","6"]],"why":"Baro-aiding saves one satellite.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"What is the largest natural source of GPS error?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: GNSS","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Ionospheric delay","Tropospheric delay","Multipath","Receiver noise"],"a":0,"why":"The ionosphere is the largest natural error.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-match-6","type":"match","q":"RAIM horizontal alert limit (HAL): match the phase to its value.","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: GNSS","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Approach","0.3 NM"],["Terminal","1 NM"],["En route","2 NM"]],"why":"HPL greater than HAL triggers an alert.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-match-7","type":"match","q":"Match the augmentation system to its description.","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: GNSS","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["ABAS","On-board: RAIM, IRS hybrid"],["SBAS","Geostationary satellites plus ground reference stations; LPV"],["GBAS","Airport ground station for GLS"]],"why":"Three augmentation types.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-10","type":"num","q":"SBAS broadcasts corrections at least every how many seconds?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: GNSS","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":5,"unit":"s","why":"Corrections at least every 5 s.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-11","type":"num","q":"SBAS supports LPV approaches down to what DH?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: GNSS","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":200,"unit":"ft","why":"LPV down to 200 ft.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"What is GAGAN, and can it give you a position fix without GPS?","src":"Web sources; FCOM 10.10.18, NP.21.42; OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: GNSS","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **GAGAN:** GPS Aided GEO Augmented Navigation, India's SBAS, built by ISRO and AAI.\n- **No fix on its own:** it only corrects GPS; it cannot give a fix alone.\n- **First LPV approach in India:** IndiGo, ATR 72-600, Kishangarh, 28 Apr 2022.\n- **Supports LPV** down to about 200 ft.\n- **Your MAX:** the FCOM describes IAN and, on some aircraft, GLS, but no SBAS or LPV function. OM-A lists LNAV and LNAV/VNAV.","keys":[{"k":"GPS Aided GEO Augmented Navigation","m":["gps aided","geo augmented"]},{"k":"India's SBAS","m":["sbas","satellite based augmentation"]},{"k":"ISRO and AAI","m":["isro","aai"]},{"k":"Cannot give a fix alone","m":["cannot","can not","only corrects","no fix"]},{"k":"First LPV 2022 Kishangarh","m":["2022","kishangarh"]},{"k":"MAX: no LPV in FCOM","m":["no lpv","no sbas","not lpv","ian"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"Where and when was India's first LPV approach flown?","src":"Web sources","ref":"Key facts: GNSS","prio":3,"o":["Kishangarh, 28 Apr 2022, IndiGo ATR 72-600","Delhi, 1 Jan 2020, Air India A320","Bengaluru, 15 Aug 2021, IndiGo A320","Kishangarh, 28 Apr 2019, Alliance Air ATR"],"a":0,"why":"IndiGo ATR 72-600 at Kishangarh, 28 Apr 2022.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"If there were no GPS, would GAGAN give you a position fix by itself?","src":"Web sources","ref":"Key facts: GNSS","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["No: GAGAN only corrects GPS","Yes, from its geostationary satellites","Yes, but only in 2D","Only below 10,000 ft"],"a":0,"why":"GAGAN cannot give a fix alone; it only corrects GPS.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"What is RNP? Is RNP 1 better than RNP 10?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"NR2","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **RNP = RNAV + on-board performance monitoring and alerting.** The number is the lateral accuracy in NM met 95% of the flight time; integrity is containment of 2 x RNP at 99.999%.\n- **RNP 1 is far more accurate** (1 NM against 10 NM), so it is used for SIDs, STARs and terminal routes.\n- **\"Better\" depends on the job.** RNP 10 (strictly RNAV 10, since it has no on-board alerting) is for oceanic and remote areas with no ground aids, so it needs two independent LRNS.\n- **B737:** RNP 1 needs two GPS; RNAV 10 needs 2 FMC, 2 CDU and 2 IRS.","keys":[{"k":"RNAV + monitoring and alerting","m":["monitoring","alerting"]},{"k":"Lateral accuracy 95%","m":["95 percent","95 per","ninety five"]},{"k":"RNP 1 more accurate, terminal","m":["more accurate","terminal","sid"]},{"k":"RNP 10 is really RNAV 10","m":["rnav 10","rnav10"]},{"k":"Oceanic, two LRNS","m":["oceanic","two long range","2 long range","two lrns","2 lrns"]},{"k":"RNP 1 needs two GPS","m":["two gps","2 gps","dual gps"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"How many satellites are required for RAIM?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"NR12","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **5 to detect** a faulty satellite (RAIM fault detection).\n- **6 to detect and exclude** it (FDE), so navigation continues.\n- **Baro-aided RAIM** uses the altimeter as one \"satellite\", so one fewer.\n- **Basic fix:** 4 for 3D (x, y, z and clock); 3 for 2D.\n- **Alerts:** RAIM compares HPL with HAL (0.3 NM approach, 1 NM terminal, 2 NM en route); HPL greater than HAL gives an alert.\n- **Prediction:** before flight for RNP APCH; a predicted outage of more than 5 min means revising the plan.","keys":[{"k":"5 for detection","m":["5 sat","five sat","5 for","five for","detect"]},{"k":"6 for exclusion (FDE)","m":["6 sat","six sat","exclu","fde"]},{"k":"Baro-aiding saves one","m":["baro"]},{"k":"4 for 3D fix","m":["4 sat","four sat","3d fix","3 d"]},{"k":"HPL vs HAL","m":["hpl","hal","protection level","alert limit"]},{"k":"Predicted outage over 5 min","m":["predict"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"How many satellites does RAIM need to DETECT and EXCLUDE a faulty satellite (FDE) without baro-aiding?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"NR12","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["6","5","4","7"],"a":0,"why":"5 to detect, 6 to detect and exclude; baro-aiding saves one.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"When does RAIM generate an integrity alert?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"NR12","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["When HPL is greater than HAL","When HAL is greater than HPL","When fewer than 4 satellites are visible","When ANP is less than RNP"],"a":0,"why":"HPL (protection level) exceeding HAL (alert limit) triggers the alert.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"You are navigating with triple INS and all three fail. How will you navigate?","src":"AIX Fly-Safe Issue 17 (Nov 2023)","ref":"NR32","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **The sheet's answer: GPS.** With independent GPS, use GPS, then radio aids (VOR/DME, DME/DME, ADF), ATC radar vectors, and DR with standby compass and clock.\n- **Company source (Fly-Safe 17):** FMS sensor order is GPS, then IRS, DME/DME, VOR/DME, DR. Modern IRSs are GPS-updated, so with GPS and IRS gone you are down to DME/DME, VOR/DME and DR.\n- Declare an emergency and land at the nearest suitable aerodrome.","keys":[{"k":"GPS","m":["gps"]},{"k":"Radio aids VOR/DME, DME/DME, ADF","m":["vor","dme","adf"]},{"k":"Radar vectors","m":["radar","vector"]},{"k":"DR with standby compass","m":["dead reckoning","standby compass","compass"]},{"k":"Emergency, nearest suitable","m":["emergency","nearest suitable","mayday"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"On YOUR 737, what happens if you lose both IRS?","src":"FCTM 4.17; FCOM 11.20.6, 11.31.4","ref":"NR32","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **The 737 has two ADIRUs, not three.** Each IRS uses three laser ring gyros and three accelerometers, and is the only source of attitude and heading apart from the ISFD and the standby compass.\n- With both IRS lost, attitude and heading come from the ISFD; FMC navigation is badly degraded.\n- **FCTM:** with neither IRU in NAV, PFD and map heading/track are invalid; GPS-L True Track on the CDU PROGRESS page can be used to update heading for an IRS in ATT mode.\n- Navigate on raw-data VOR/DME and ADF, standby compass and radar vectors; declare an emergency and land at the nearest suitable aerodrome.","keys":[{"k":"Two ADIRUs, not three","m":["two adiru","2 adiru","two irs","2 irs","not three"]},{"k":"ISFD for attitude","m":["isfd","standby"]},{"k":"GPS-L true track on PROGRESS page","m":["progress","true track"]},{"k":"Raw data VOR/DME, ADF","m":["raw data","vor","adf"]},{"k":"Emergency, nearest suitable","m":["emergency","nearest suitable","mayday"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"How many ADIRUs does the 737 MAX have?","src":"FCOM 11.20.6","ref":"NR32","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["Two","Three","One plus a standby IRS","Four"],"a":0,"why":"Two ADIRUs. The oral bank's 'triple IRS' does not apply to your fleet.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"What equipment does the B737 need for RNAV 5, RNP 2, RNAV 1 / RNP 1 and RNAV 10?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"NR37","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **RNAV 5 (B-RNAV):** 1 FMC, 1 CDU, 2 IRS and 1 GPS or VOR/DME or DME/DME. 5 NM; cross-track 2.5 NM; IRS alone 2 h from last update.\n- **RNP 2:** 1 GPS, 1 FMC, 2 IRS (oceanic: 2 FMC, 2 CDU). Cross-track 1 NM.\n- **RNAV 1:** 1 GPS or DME/DME. **RNP 1:** two GPS. Cross-track 0.5 NM.\n- **RNAV 10 (RNP 10):** 2 FMC, 2 CDU, 2 PFD/ND, 2 IRS: two independent LRNS. Cross-track 5 NM; IRS time limit, or FDE outage 34 min or less.","keys":[{"k":"RNAV 5: 1 FMC, 2 IRS, GPS or VOR/DME or DME/DME","m":["rnav 5","rnp 5"]},{"k":"RNP 2: 1 GPS","m":["rnp 2","one gps","1 gps"]},{"k":"RNP 1: two GPS","m":["two gps","2 gps","dual gps"]},{"k":"RNAV 10: 2 FMC, 2 CDU, 2 IRS","m":["2 fmc","two fmc","dual fmc"]},{"k":"Two LRNS","m":["two long range","2 long range","two lrns","2 lrns","two independent"]},{"k":"FDE 34 min","m":["34 min","34min","thirty four"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"Why is RNP 10 more restrictive on equipment than RNP 5, even though it is less accurate?","src":"OM-A Ch 40; FCTM 1.37","ref":"NR37","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- RNP 10 is flown over ocean or remote land, for hours, with **no ground navaids** to cross-check or update.\n- Usually no radar and only HF voice.\n- One failure could leave you unable to navigate with nothing outside to fall back on, so **two independent long-range systems** plus IRS time limits are required.\n- **RNAV 5 is the opposite:** within navaid and radar coverage with VHF to ATC, so a single RNAV system is enough; a VOR or DME is always there as backup.\n- **FCTM:** RNP 10 suits FMCs with GPS updating, or without GPS if radio-updated within the previous six hours.","keys":[{"k":"No ground navaids","m":["no ground","no navaid","without navaid","no vor"]},{"k":"No radar, HF only","m":["no radar","hf voice","hf radio","only hf"]},{"k":"Two independent LRNS","m":["two long range","2 long range","two lrns","2 lrns","two independent","dual"]},{"k":"IRS time limit","m":["time limit","six hours","6 hours","6 h"]},{"k":"RNAV 5 has ground aids as backup","m":["backup","back up","ground aid","coverage"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-12","type":"num","q":"Boeing FCTM: an FMC without GPS updating is suitable for RNP 10 if it received the last radio update within how many hours?","src":"FCTM 1.37","ref":"NR37","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":6,"unit":"h","why":"'...received the last radio update within the previous six hours.'","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"What are WAAS and LAAS?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"NR48","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **WAAS (Wide Area Augmentation System):** the US SBAS. Ground reference stations measure GPS errors, master stations compute corrections and integrity data, and geostationary satellites broadcast them on L1. Supports LPV to about 200 ft. Equivalents: EGNOS, GAGAN, MSAS.\n- **LAAS (Local Area Augmentation System):** the US name for GBAS. An airport ground station broadcasts corrections by VHF data link (VDB, 108 to 118 MHz). Supports GLS (CAT I, aiming for CAT II/III).\n- No PBN navigation specification depends on GBAS.","keys":[{"k":"WAAS = US SBAS","m":["sbas","satellite based","wide area"]},{"k":"Reference + master stations + geostationary satellites","m":["geostationary","master station","reference station"]},{"k":"LPV ~200 ft","m":["lpv","200"]},{"k":"LAAS = GBAS","m":["gbas","ground based","local area"]},{"k":"VHF data broadcast 108 to 118 MHz","m":["vhf","vdb","108"]},{"k":"GLS approaches","m":["gls"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-match-8","type":"match","q":"Match the SBAS to its region.","src":"Standard theory; NR48","ref":"NR48","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["WAAS","USA"],["EGNOS","Europe"],["GAGAN","India"],["MSAS","Japan"]],"why":"All are SBAS: geostationary satellites broadcasting GPS corrections.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"What type of FMS does your 737 MAX have?","src":"FCOM 11.30.1, 11.31.4, 11.40.9; OM-A 3.2","ref":"NR48","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Two FMCs with two CDUs** (Boeing FMCS). Certified as a \"sole source\" navigation system, so it may operate outside radio navaid coverage; the second FMC is a full backup. FMC Source Select: NORMAL (left primary), BOTH ON L, BOTH ON R.\n- **Position:** IRS updated by GPS (multi-mode receivers), then DME/DME, VOR/DME, LOC/DME, LOC; with no GPS or DME, IRS only with a learned IRS error correction.\n- **Databases:** 28-day AIRAC nav database (0001Z changeover) and a performance database.\n- **Software:** OP PROGRAM on the IDENT page.","keys":[{"k":"Dual FMC, dual CDU","m":["two fmc","2 fmc","dual fmc"]},{"k":"Sole source navigation","m":["sole source"]},{"k":"Source select NORMAL / BOTH ON L/R","m":["both on","source select"]},{"k":"IRS updated by GPS, then DME/DME","m":["dme dme","dme/dme","dmedme","gps"]},{"k":"28-day AIRAC","m":["28 day","28day","airac"]},{"k":"IDENT page OP PROGRAM","m":["ident","op program"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between hybrid GPS-IRS and GPS updating on the 737?","src":"FCOM 11.20, 11.20.1, 11.31.4","ref":"NR48","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Hybrid GPS-IRS ADIRU:** blends GPS and inertial data; it coasts through GPS outages with very low drift, so no GPS INVALID message appears. The FMC uses hybrid GPS first, then raw GPS.\n- **Without hybrid:** the FMC uses the GPS position directly and reverts to radio or IRS updating as soon as GPS is lost.\n- **Sensors:** two GPS in the multi-mode receivers; left GPS feeds ADS-B position to transponder 1, right to transponder 2.","keys":[{"k":"Hybrid blends GPS and inertial","m":["blend","hybrid"]},{"k":"Coasts through outages, low drift","m":["coast","low drift"]},{"k":"No GPS INVALID with hybrid","m":["gps invalid"]},{"k":"Non-hybrid reverts to radio/IRS","m":["revert","radio updating","irs updating"]},{"k":"GPS feeds ADS-B per side","m":["ads b","adsb","transponder"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-order-2","type":"order","q":"Put the 737 FMC position update sources in priority order.","src":"FCOM 11.31.4","ref":"NR48","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["GPS (hybrid, then raw)","DME/DME (two or more DMEs)","VOR/DME","LOC/DME","LOC","IRS only (with learned error correction)"],"why":"FCOM 11.31.4 update priority.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"How does GPS give position information?","src":"Standard theory; NR64","ref":"NR64","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Segments:** space (24+ satellites, 6 planes at 55°, about 20,200 km, 12 h, atomic clocks); control (monitor and master stations upload clock and ephemeris corrections); user (receiver).\n- **Ranging:** each satellite broadcasts time and ephemeris on L1 (1575.42 MHz). The receiver measures travel time: range = speed of light x time, a pseudorange because its clock is cheap.\n- **Four satellites** solve latitude, longitude, altitude and clock error: a 3D fix in WGS-84.\n- **Velocity** from Doppler.\n- **Accuracy** typically under 10 m; ionosphere is the largest error.","keys":[{"k":"Space, control, user segments","m":["segment","control segment","space segment"]},{"k":"Time of travel x speed of light","m":["speed of light","travel time","time of travel"]},{"k":"Pseudorange","m":["pseudo range","pseudorange"]},{"k":"Four satellites, clock error","m":["four sat","4 sat","four satellites","clock error"]},{"k":"WGS-84","m":["wgs"]},{"k":"Ionosphere largest error","m":["ionosph"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-16","type":"mcq","q":"Why does GPS need a fourth satellite for a 3D fix?","src":"Standard theory; NR64","ref":"NR64","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["To solve the receiver clock error","To provide RAIM","To correct ionospheric delay","To compute velocity"],"a":0,"why":"Four unknowns: x, y, z and receiver clock bias.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between GPS jamming and spoofing, and how do you recognise them?","src":"AIX Fly-Safe Issue 17 (Nov 2023)","ref":"NR64","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Weak signal:** like a 60 W bulb seen from 20,000 km, so easily jammed in the L1 band (1575.42 MHz +/-10 MHz).\n- **Jamming** drowns the signal: you lose GPS.\n- **Spoofing** feeds false signals: position or time is wrong. Done by carry-off (sync, then overpower) or meaconing (re-broadcasting real signals).\n- **Signs:** big jump in EPU/ANP (1 to 2 NM up to 60 NM); wrong UTC on the clocks; GPS position shift or wrong FMS position; ND/PFD warnings; other aircraft reporting the same.","keys":[{"k":"Jamming = loss of GPS","m":["drown","lose gps","loss of gps","denial"]},{"k":"Spoofing = false position/time","m":["false","fake","wrong position"]},{"k":"Carry-off / meaconing","m":["carry off","meaconing"]},{"k":"ANP / EPU jump","m":["anp","epu"]},{"k":"Wrong UTC / clock","m":["utc","clock","time"]},{"k":"Map / position shift","m":["map shift","position shift","shift"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-order-3","type":"order","q":"Company (Fly-Safe 17) actions for GPS spoofing: put them in order.","src":"AIX Fly-Safe Issue 17 (Nov 2023)","ref":"NR64","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["Revert to heading mode","Deselect GPS as soon as possible","Confirm IRS integrity","Fly the OFP tracks","Report to ATC","Stay on IRS only until clear of the area","Report to maintenance and DGCA after the flight"],"why":"Deselect GPS early because the IRS is not contaminated immediately.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"Both GPS lost during pushback at Delhi. Can you fly the RNAV SID?","src":"OM-A Ch 40, 28.8, 3.3","ref":"S14","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**It depends on the SID's navigation specification; read the chart notes.**\n- **RNAV 1:** sensors may be GNSS, DME/DME or DME/DME/IRU. The B737 needs one GPS or DME/DME. Without GPS confirm the FMC position within 1000 ft at the start of the take-off roll.\n- **RNP 1 or \"GNSS required\":** not permitted; RNP 1 needs two GPS.\n- **Actions:** inform ATC and request a conventional SID or radar vectors; check the MEL; ADS-B OUT loses its position source (XPNDR/ATC FAIL, GPS-L/R INVALID).","keys":[{"k":"Depends on nav spec / chart","m":["depends","chart","nav spec","navigation specification"]},{"k":"RNAV 1: DME/DME acceptable","m":["dme dme","dme/dme","dmedme"]},{"k":"Position check 1000 ft on take-off roll","m":["1000 ft","1000ft","1000 feet","runway update","position update"]},{"k":"RNP 1: not permitted (two GPS)","m":["not permitted","two gps","2 gps","cannot"]},{"k":"Ask for conventional SID / vectors","m":["conventional","vector"]},{"k":"MEL and ADS-B","m":["mel","ads b","adsb"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"GPS PRIMARY LOST en route Mumbai to Delhi. How do you navigate?","src":"OM-A 3.4.1, Ch 40","ref":"S15","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **What happens:** the FMC reverts to IRS with radio updating (DME/DME, VOR/DME) and ANP grows. Cross-check the position with raw data.\n- **Check the route spec:** RNAV 5 accepts VOR/DME, DME/DME and IRS (2 h since the last ground update), so you can continue. For an RNP spec, tell ATC you can't meet it, with your proposed action, and ask for conventional routing or vectors.\n- **Approach:** plan an ILS at Delhi; an RNP approach needs GNSS.\n- **ADS-B OUT** is affected too; inform ATC.","keys":[{"k":"FMC reverts to IRS + radio updating","m":["radio updat","dme dme","irs"]},{"k":"Cross-check raw data","m":["raw data","cross check"]},{"k":"RNAV 5 OK on DME/VOR/IRS","m":["rnav 5","rnav5"]},{"k":"RNP route: tell ATC","m":["inform atc","tell atc","advise atc","unable rnp"]},{"k":"Plan ILS, not RNP approach","m":["ils"]},{"k":"ADS-B affected","m":["ads b","adsb"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"Bengaluru to Singapore with GPS jamming and no navaids. Can you continue?","src":"OM-A Ch 40; DGCA ANSS AC 01 of 2023","ref":"S16","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **RNAV 10 with two IRS:** the oceanic legs are RNAV 10 or RNP 4; RNAV 10 accepts two IRS as the two LRNS within the IRS time limit.\n- **Before oceanic entry:** update and check position against the last navaids; work out the IRS-only time to the next navaid or radar coverage. Automatic DME/DME updating reduces the baseline by 0.3 h.\n- **RNP 4 needs GNSS,** so you are not RNP 4 capable; tell ATC.\n- **En route:** report the loss, position reports by HF or CPDLC, monitor ANP; if the IRS time limit will be exceeded, \"unable RNAV 10\" and re-route.\n- **Report the interference** per DGCA ANSS AC 01 of 2023 and AIP India.","keys":[{"k":"RNAV 10 OK on two IRS","m":["two irs","2 irs","rnav 10"]},{"k":"IRS time limit","m":["time limit"]},{"k":"Position check before oceanic entry","m":["update","last navaid","check position"]},{"k":"RNP 4 needs GNSS: not capable","m":["rnp 4"]},{"k":"HF / CPDLC reports","m":["hf voice","hf radio","by hf","cpdlc"]},{"k":"Report interference (ANSS AC 01/2023)","m":["report","anss"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"Suspected GPS spoofing over the Middle East. How do you know, and what do you do?","src":"AIX Fly-Safe Issue 17 (Nov 2023); DGCA ANSS AC 01 of 2023","ref":"S17","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Source:** not in the OM-A; AIX Fly-Safe 17 and DGCA ANSS AC 01 of 2023.\n- **Signs:** GPS position jumps away from IRS (map shift); GPS-L/R INVALID, VERIFY POSITION, UNABLE REQD NAV PERF-RNP; rising ANP; odd ground speed or wind; clock or date jumps; false EGPWS alerts; ATC says your ADS-B position is wrong.\n- **Actions:** heading mode; deselect GPS early (IRS not contaminated immediately); confirm IRS integrity; fly OFP tracks cross-checking DME/DME and VOR; report to ATC; IRS only until clear; report to maintenance and DGCA.\n- **Reporting:** since November 2025 DGCA requires a report within 10 minutes; tech log entry.","keys":[{"k":"Map shift / GPS-IRS disagreement","m":["shift","jump","disagree"]},{"k":"Clock / date jump","m":["clock","date","utc"]},{"k":"Heading mode","m":["heading mode","hdg sel","heading select"]},{"k":"Deselect GPS","m":["deselect","inhibit gps","gps off"]},{"k":"Confirm IRS integrity","m":["irs"]},{"k":"Report ATC/DGCA within 10 min","m":["report","10 min","10min"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"During GPS spoofing, which clock do you trust, and why?","src":"FCOM 10.20.12, 11.40.12; AIX Fly-Safe Issue 17","ref":"S17","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Not the flight deck clocks.**\n- On the MAX each AUX-display clock shows UTC and date generated automatically from GPS, and the FMC also takes UTC from GPS, so spoofing can move both.\n- Use a personal watch time-checked before entering the risk area (Fly-Safe 17), or get a time check from ATC or ACARS.\n- Before a known risk area also do a full IRS alignment.","keys":[{"k":"Not the flight deck clocks","m":["not the","don't trust","dont trust","do not trust"]},{"k":"Clocks and FMC take UTC from GPS","m":["from gps","gps time","gps derived"]},{"k":"Personal watch","m":["watch","personal"]},{"k":"Time check from ATC / ACARS","m":["atc","acars"]},{"k":"Full IRS alignment before area","m":["full align","alignment"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-13","type":"num","q":"Since November 2025, within how many minutes must DGCA be told of a spoofing occurrence?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S17","prio":3,"ans":10,"unit":"min","why":"Pilots and controllers report spoofing within 10 minutes of the occurrence.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-21","type":"recall","q":"OAT is 1°C above the chart maximum for a baro-VNAV RNP approach. Can you fly it?","src":"OM-A Ch 40 Baro-VNAV; 28.2.1","ref":"S18","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Only to LNAV minima.**\n- The chart temperature range applies to LNAV/VNAV minima. AIX aircraft have no temperature compensation, so outside the range use LNAV minima only, as a CDFA to DDA = MDA + 50 ft.\n- **Why:** a hot day makes the true path steeper than charted; the maximum temperature matches the 3.5° maximum path angle.\n- A cold day makes the path shallower and closer to obstacles.","keys":[{"k":"LNAV minima only","m":["lnav only","lnav minima","only lnav"]},{"k":"Temperature range limits LNAV/VNAV","m":["lnav vnav","baro vnav","temperature"]},{"k":"No temperature compensation","m":["compensation"]},{"k":"DDA = MDA + 50 ft, CDFA","m":["50 ft","50ft","50 feet","cdfa","dda"]},{"k":"Hot = steeper path","m":["steeper"]},{"k":"Cold = shallower, near obstacles","m":["shallower","obstacle"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-17","type":"mcq","q":"A hot day above the baro-VNAV chart maximum makes the true vertical path...","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"S18","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Steeper than charted","Shallower than charted","Unchanged","Lower, closer to obstacles"],"a":0,"why":"Hot: steeper (max temperature matches the 3.5° max path angle). Cold: shallower and closer to obstacles.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-22","type":"recall","q":"GPS PRIMARY LOST during an RNP approach. Do you continue? Missed approach on GPS?","src":"OM-A Ch 40 RNP APCH, 25.4","ref":"S19","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Do not continue on instruments;** RNP APCH requires GNSS.\n- **Discontinue if:** nav display flagged invalid; loss-of-integrity alert; integrity alerting not available before the FAF; or excessive FTE. ANP greater than RNP means an immediate go-around.\n- **Only exception:** land if the required visual reference is already established.\n- **Missed approach:** fly the published missed approach. LNAV on IRS/DME updating is usable while ANP stays within the missed-approach RNP (1.0 NM); otherwise conventional navaids checked pre-flight, or radar vectors. Tell ATC.\n- That is why the pre-flight requires alternative means to land and conventional missed-approach equipment.","keys":[{"k":"Do not continue / go around","m":["go around","goaround","discontinue","do not continue"]},{"k":"RNP APCH requires GNSS","m":["gnss","requires gps"]},{"k":"Unless visual reference established","m":["visual"]},{"k":"Missed approach LNAV while ANP within 1.0 NM","m":["1.0 nm","10 nm","1 nm","anp"]},{"k":"Conventional navaids / radar vectors","m":["conventional","vector","raw data"]},{"k":"Tell ATC","m":["atc"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-23","type":"recall","q":"ANP exceeds RNP en route. What do you see and what do you do?","src":"FCOM 11.31.10, 10.10.76; QRH 11.22; OM-A Ch 40","ref":"S20","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Indication:** UNABLE REQD NAV PERF-RNP on the CDU scratchpad once the time to alert has elapsed, amber on the ND map, amber FMC lights on the main panel. Run the QRH UNABLE REQD NAV PERF-RNP checklist.\n- **Actions:**\n  1. Cross-check FMC position against raw data.\n  2. Check sensors (GPS status, DME updating).\n  3. Tell ATC you can't meet the spec, with your proposed action.\n  4. Revert to conventional navigation or radar vectors.\n  5. Tech log entry.","keys":[{"k":"UNABLE REQD NAV PERF-RNP","m":["unable reqd","unable required","unable rnp","nav perf"]},{"k":"Amber FMC light / ND","m":["amber","fmc light"]},{"k":"QRH checklist","m":["qrh","checklist"]},{"k":"Cross-check raw data","m":["raw data","cross check"]},{"k":"Tell ATC","m":["atc"]},{"k":"Conventional nav / vectors","m":["conventional","vector"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-24","type":"recall","q":"Spoofing triggers a 'PULL UP' at FL350 on a clear day. What do you do?","src":"OM-A 30.6, 30.8; QRH MAN.1.3, MAN.1.4; FCOM 15.20.12","ref":"S21","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Why:** EGPWS terrain look-ahead uses the FMC blended position, so a corrupted position can give false alerts or no alerts.\n- **Default:** a PULL UP is flown immediately.\n- **Boeing exception:** a look-ahead terrain warning in climb, cruise or descent that can be immediately verified as above the minimum safe altitude can be considered unreliable. At FL350 that is immediate.\n- Then the company GNSS-interference actions. TERR INHIBIT inhibits look-ahead alerts; radio-altitude alerts still work.\n- **Report:** tell ATC; a written report whenever the flight path is modified by an EGPWS alert, copy to DGCA within 24 h.","keys":[{"k":"Look-ahead uses FMC/GPS position","m":["look ahead","lookahead","fmc position","position"]},{"k":"Default: fly the pull up","m":["immediately","fly the pull up","respond"]},{"k":"Verified above MSA: unreliable","m":["minimum safe","msa","unreliable"]},{"k":"TERR INHIBIT","m":["terr inhibit","terrain inhibit"]},{"k":"Report, DGCA within 24 h","m":["24 h","24h","24 hours","report"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-14","type":"num","q":"After an EGPWS alert modifies your flight path, within how many hours must a copy of the report reach DGCA?","src":"OM-A 30.8","ref":"S21","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":24,"unit":"h","why":"Written report required; copy to DGCA within 24 h.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-25","type":"recall","q":"RAIM prediction shows an outage at your ETA for an RNP approach. Can you dispatch?","src":"OM-A Ch 40 RNP APCH, 28.8","ref":"S51","prio":2,"a":"**Not planning on that approach.**\n- A predicted loss of RAIM of more than 5 minutes continuous means the plan must be revised.\n- **Options:** delay departure; plan a different approach at destination; nominate an alternate with an approach that does not need GPS (required anyway).\n- **Re-check** RAIM if the ETA changes by more than 15 minutes.","keys":[{"k":"Revise plan","m":["revise","not plan","cannot plan"]},{"k":"Outage more than 5 min","m":["5 min","5min","five min"]},{"k":"Delay or different approach","m":["delay","different approach","other approach","ils"]},{"k":"Alternate not needing GPS","m":["alternate"]},{"k":"Re-check if ETA changes 15 min","m":["15 min","15min","fifteen"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-15","type":"num","q":"RAIM prediction: a predicted continuous outage longer than how many minutes means you must revise the plan?","src":"OM-A Ch 40 RNP APCH","ref":"S51","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":5,"unit":"min","why":"More than 5 minutes continuous; re-check if ETA changes by more than 15 minutes.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-26","type":"recall","q":"What is RNAV? What were B-RNAV and P-RNAV, and what are Q routes?","src":"Oral bank p.56, p.58; OM-A 40.8.1, 40.9.1; FCOM 11.30.1","ref":"OB5","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **RNAV:** navigation on any desired flight path within coverage of ground or space-based aids, or self-contained aids, or both; no need to overfly stations.\n- **B-RNAV:** old European en-route name, now **RNAV 5**; +/-5 NM for 95% of the time.\n- **P-RNAV:** old European terminal name; RNAV 1 and RNAV 2 harmonise P-RNAV with US-RNAV; +/-1 NM for 95%.\n- **Sensors:** GPS, DME/DME (best ground fix), VOR/DME, IRS. **2D/3D/4D:** lateral; plus vertical; plus time. Your FMC is 4D (LNAV, VNAV, RTA).\n- **Q routes:** India's RNAV 5 routes (bank: Q1 Delhi to Mumbai); current list in AIP ENR 3.","keys":[{"k":"Any desired flight path","m":["desired flight path","any desired","without overflying","not overfly"]},{"k":"B-RNAV = RNAV 5","m":["rnav 5","rnav5"]},{"k":"P-RNAV = RNAV 1","m":["rnav 1","rnav1"]},{"k":"Sensors GPS, DME/DME, VOR/DME, IRS","m":["dme dme","dme/dme","dmedme"]},{"k":"4D = time / RTA","m":["4d rnav","4 d","rta","required time"]},{"k":"Q routes = RNAV 5 routes","m":["q route","q1 delhi","q routes"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-match-9","type":"match","q":"Match the old European RNAV name to its PBN equivalent.","src":"OM-A 40.8.1, 40.9.1; Oral bank p.56","ref":"OB5","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["B-RNAV","RNAV 5 (+/-5 NM)"],["P-RNAV","RNAV 1 (+/-1 NM)"],["2D RNAV","Lateral only"],["4D RNAV","Lateral, vertical and time"]],"why":"RNAV 1 and 2 harmonise European P-RNAV with US-RNAV.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-18","type":"mcq","q":"The oral bank says 'if the aircraft is outside coverage, RNAV shifts to DR mode'. What does your 737 FMC actually do?","src":"FCOM 11.31.4","ref":"OB5","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Navigates on IRS only, applying a learned IRS error correction; DR is the last resort after that","Switches straight to DR using air data","Stops navigating and flags LNAV invalid","Uses the last GPS position frozen"],"a":0,"why":"FCOM 11.31.4: with no GPS or DME, IRS only with a learned correction.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-27","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between RNAV and RNP? What is ANP, and what happens when it exceeds RNP?","src":"Oral bank p.57, p.58; OM-A 0.15, 40.2.6, Ch 40; FCOM 11.31, 11.31.10","ref":"OB6","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **RNP = RNAV plus on-board performance monitoring and alerting.** With that requirement it is an RNP spec; without, an RNAV spec.\n- **ANP (actual navigation performance):** the FMC's own estimate of its position accuracy (also EPU or EPE), not the actual error. The actual position lies within the ANP circle. The FMC uses it for a gross error check before accepting sensor updates.\n- **RNP value:** defaults for oceanic, en route, terminal and approach, or from the database, or crew entry. ANP should not exceed RNP.\n- **If ANP exceeds RNP:** UNABLE REQD NAV PERF-RNP after the time to alert, amber map and FMC lights; tell ATC and ask for a revised clearance.","keys":[{"k":"RNP = RNAV + monitoring and alerting","m":["monitoring","alerting"]},{"k":"ANP = FMC estimate of accuracy","m":["estimate","actual navigation performance"]},{"k":"Also EPU / EPE","m":["epu","epe"]},{"k":"ANP exceeds RNP: alert","m":["exceed","greater than","more than"]},{"k":"UNABLE REQD NAV PERF","m":["unable reqd","unable required","nav perf","unable rnp"]},{"k":"Tell ATC","m":["atc"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-19","type":"mcq","q":"The oral bank writes 'ANP < RNP [message on CDU] [inform ATC]'. What is wrong?","src":"FCOM 11.31","ref":"OB6","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["The inequality is reversed: the alert comes when ANP exceeds RNP","Nothing; ANP below RNP triggers the alert","The message appears on the ND only, not the CDU","ATC need not be informed"],"a":0,"why":"FCOM 11.31: ANP should not exceed RNP; the alert is for ANP > RNP.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-20","type":"mcq","q":"The oral bank says RNP 10 routes require 'HF radio, CPDLC, ADS-B'. What does the B737 actually need for RNAV 10?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"OB6","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Two LRNS (2 FMC, 2 CDU, 2 IRS); no transponder requirement stated","HF, CPDLC and ADS-B","One GPS and one IRS","ADS-C and CPDLC only"],"a":0,"why":"CPDLC plus ADS-C is the RNP 4 add-on for 30/30 NM separation; ADS-B is not part of it.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-21","type":"mcq","q":"The oral bank says the lowest sensor the FAA supports for RNP is DME/DME. For your fleet, what does RNP 1 need?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"OB6","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Two GPS","DME/DME","One GPS or DME/DME","Two IRS"],"a":0,"why":"RNP 1 needs two GPS; RNP 4 and RNP APCH need GNSS.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-28","type":"recall","q":"What are the GPS errors and limitations?","src":"Standard theory; NR64; Oral bank p.92, p.93","ref":"OB7","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Receiver clock bias:** cheap quartz clock vs atomic satellite clocks; solved by a fourth satellite.\n- **Ionospheric delay:** the largest natural error. Tropospheric delay is smaller.\n- **Satellite clock and ephemeris errors:** corrected by uploads from the control segment.\n- **Multipath:** reflections near the antenna.\n- **Geometry (DOP):** best with one satellite overhead and others well spread.\n- **Integrity:** basic GPS has no integrity warning, hence RAIM, FDE, SBAS and GBAS.\n- **Interference:** very weak signal, easily jammed or spoofed.","keys":[{"k":"Receiver clock error","m":["clock"]},{"k":"Ionosphere largest","m":["ionosph"]},{"k":"Ephemeris","m":["ephemeris","orbit"]},{"k":"Multipath","m":["multipath","multi path","reflection"]},{"k":"Geometry / DOP","m":["dop","geometry","dilution"]},{"k":"No integrity: RAIM","m":["integrity","raim"]},{"k":"Jamming / spoofing","m":["jam","spoof","interference"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-22","type":"mcq","q":"The oral bank lists Selective Availability as a GPS error. What is the current position?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I, Att D","ref":"OB7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["SA was discontinued at midnight on 1 May 2000","SA is still applied but reduced to 30 m","SA is applied only in wartime and is active now","SA was replaced by RAIM in 2010"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 Vol I Att D: SA discontinued 1 May 2000.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-23","type":"mcq","q":"The oral bank says receiver time bias 'is removed by using three satellites at a time'. What is correct?","src":"Standard theory; NR64","ref":"OB7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["A fourth satellite removes it (x, y, z and time)","Three satellites are correct","Five satellites (RAIM) remove it","SBAS removes it"],"a":0,"why":"Four satellites solve the four unknowns.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-num-16","type":"num","q":"What is the inclination of the GPS orbital planes? (The bank says 56°.)","src":"Standard theory; NR64","ref":"OB7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"ans":55,"unit":"°","why":"55°; the bank's 56° is wrong.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-29","type":"recall","q":"What is differential GPS? How do GAGAN (SBAS) and LAAS (GBAS) improve GPS?","src":"Oral bank p.94; Annex 10 Vol I 3.7.3.4.4.1, 3.7.3.5.4.1; OM-A Ch 40","ref":"OB8","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **DGPS:** a ground station at a surveyed position compares its GPS position or ranges with the truth, works out the error and broadcasts a correction that the aircraft applies (bank: local range about 70 NM).\n- **SBAS (WAAS, EGNOS, GAGAN, MSAS):** reference stations, master stations computing corrections and integrity, geostationary satellites broadcasting them at least every 5 s, on the GPS L1 carrier (1575.42 MHz), so no separate receiver.\n- **GAGAN:** corrects GPS, cannot fix alone; LPV to about 200 ft; in service since 2022.\n- **GBAS (LAAS):** airport reference receivers; corrections on a VHF data broadcast (108 to 117.975 MHz) for GLS.","keys":[{"k":"Surveyed reference station computes error","m":["surveyed","known position","reference station"]},{"k":"Broadcasts correction","m":["correction"]},{"k":"SBAS via geostationary satellites","m":["geostationary","sbas"]},{"k":"Same L1 carrier, no separate receiver","m":["1575","l1 carrier","l1 frequency","same frequency","same carrier"]},{"k":"GBAS VHF data broadcast","m":["vhf","vdb"]},{"k":"GLS / LPV","m":["gls","lpv"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-24","type":"mcq","q":"The oral bank calls GAGAN 'a planned implementation'. What is correct?","src":"Web sources (topic 3 key facts)","ref":"OB8","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["It is in service; the first LPV approach in India was flown in April 2022","It is still planned for 2028","It was cancelled in favour of GBAS","It is in service only for military users"],"a":0,"why":"In service; first LPV 28 Apr 2022 at Kishangarh.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-25","type":"mcq","q":"The oral bank says the aircraft needs 'a GSAT receiver for receiving the WAAS'. What is correct?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.7.3.4.4.1","ref":"OB8","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"o":["SBAS is broadcast on the GPS L1 carrier, so an SBAS-capable GPS receiver uses it directly","A separate GSAT receiver is required","WAAS is received on VHF 108 to 118 MHz","WAAS uses the ILS receiver"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.7.3.4.4.1: SBAS on 1575.42 MHz.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-mcq-26","type":"mcq","q":"On what band does GBAS broadcast its corrections?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.7.3.5.4.1","ref":"OB8","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["VHF data broadcast, 108 to 117.975 MHz","GPS L1, 1575.42 MHz","UHF 328 to 335 MHz","HF 2 to 30 MHz"],"a":0,"why":"GBAS VDB in 108 to 117.975 MHz.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-30","type":"recall","q":"What is the FMS? What are its inputs, outputs and the navigation database contents?","src":"FCOM 11.30.1, 11.31, 11.31.4; OM-A 3.2; Oral bank p.54, p.55","ref":"OB9","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Does:** automatic navigation, in-flight performance optimisation, fuel monitoring and flight deck displays; LNAV and VNAV.\n- **Inputs:** crew entries on two CDUs; IRS, GPS from the MMRs, auto-tuned nav radios, air data, fuel; navigation and performance databases.\n- **Outputs:** pitch, roll and thrust commands to AP, A/T and FD; map and route data to the displays.\n- **Nav database:** waypoints, airways, navaids, airports, runways, SIDs, STARs, holds and approaches in ARINC 424; 28-day AIRAC.\n- **Preflight:** IDENT, POS INIT, RTE, then performance and takeoff data.\n- **Bank error:** the FMC does not \"aggregate\" triple IRS; two ADIRUs, IRS updated by one source in priority order.","keys":[{"k":"LNAV / VNAV, performance","m":["lnav","vnav","performance"]},{"k":"Inputs: IRS, GPS, radios, air data, fuel","m":["air data","irs","fuel"]},{"k":"Outputs to AP/AT/FD","m":["autopilot","autothrottle","flight director"]},{"k":"Nav database contents","m":["waypoint","sid","airway"]},{"k":"ARINC 424 / AIRAC","m":["arinc","airac","28 day"]},{"k":"Preflight IDENT, POS INIT, RTE","m":["ident","pos init"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-order-4","type":"order","q":"FMC preflight: put the pages in order.","src":"FCOM 11.31 (Preflight)","ref":"OB9","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["IDENT","POS INIT","RTE","Performance data","Takeoff data"],"why":"FCOM preflight order; required items are initial position, route, performance data and takeoff data.","topic":3},{"id":"t3-recall-31","type":"recall","q":"Does the FMS fly great circle or rhumb line tracks?","src":"FCOM 11.31 (RTE LEGS, descent page)","ref":"OB10","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Great circle.**\n- The FMC computes great-circle legs between waypoints, so RTE LEGS directions may differ from chart values.\n- Across a route discontinuity it also builds a great-circle path.\n- Because a great circle's direction changes as you fly (except along meridians and the equator), the displayed course changes along a long leg.\n- Courses are shown magnetic, or true when selected; variation comes from a database table.","keys":[{"k":"Great circle","m":["great circle"]},{"k":"Leg direction differs from chart","m":["differ","chart"]},{"k":"Course changes along a long leg","m":["chang"]},{"k":"Except meridians and equator","m":["meridian","equator"]},{"k":"Magnetic via database variation table","m":["magnetic","variation"]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: 'Explain GPS.'","src":"Standard theory; OM-A Ch 40; FCOM 10.10.18","ref":"NR64, NR12, NR48","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"How does it give you a position?","a":"Ranging: travel time x speed of light gives a pseudorange to each satellite; four satellites solve x, y, z and clock error; WGS-84.","keys":[{"k":"Travel time x speed of light","m":["speed of light","travel time"]},{"k":"Four satellites","m":["four sat","4 sat","fourth"]},{"k":"Clock error","m":["clock"]}]},{"q":"What frequency and polarisation?","a":"L1 1575.42 MHz (C/A), L2 and L5 too; right-hand circular polarisation.","keys":[{"k":"1575.42 MHz","m":["1575"]},{"k":"Right-hand circular","m":["right hand","rhcp"]}]},{"q":"Biggest error?","a":"Ionospheric delay; also troposphere, multipath, satellite clock/ephemeris, geometry (DOP).","keys":[{"k":"Ionosphere","m":["ionosph"]}]},{"q":"How many satellites for RAIM?","a":"5 to detect, 6 to detect and exclude (FDE); baro-aiding saves one.","keys":[{"k":"5 detect","m":["5 sat","five sat","5 to detect","five to detect","5 for"]},{"k":"6 exclude","m":["six","6 sat","6 for","fde"]}]},{"q":"What is GAGAN, and can it fix your position alone?","a":"India's SBAS (ISRO and AAI); it only corrects GPS and cannot give a fix alone.","keys":[{"k":"SBAS","m":["sbas","augment"]},{"k":"Cannot fix alone","m":["cannot","can not","only correct","no fix"]}]},{"q":"Can your MAX fly an LPV?","a":"No SBAS/LPV function in the FCOM; IAN and, on some aircraft, GLS. OM-A lists LNAV and LNAV/VNAV.","keys":[{"k":"No LPV","m":["no lpv","not lpv","no sbas","cannot"]},{"k":"LNAV / LNAV-VNAV","m":["lnav"]}]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: 'You're cleared for an RNP approach.'","src":"OM-A Ch 40, 25.4, 28.2.1","ref":"S18, S19, Key facts: PBN (RNP APCH)","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What do you check before?","a":"RAIM; never DIRECT TO the FAF; DIRECT TO the IF only with 45° or less track change; established by the FAF; temperature within chart range.","keys":[{"k":"RAIM","m":["raim"]},{"k":"No direct to FAF","m":["faf"]},{"k":"Temperature","m":["temperature","temp"]}]},{"q":"What are the lateral and vertical limits?","a":"Scaling +/-1.0 NM initial, +/-0.3 NM final, +/-1.0 NM missed; FTE half of that; baro-VNAV +/-75 ft.","keys":[{"k":"0.3 NM final","m":["0.3 nm","03 nm","0.3nm","03nm","point 3"]},{"k":"75 ft vertical","m":["75 ft","75ft","75 feet"]}]},{"q":"OAT is 1°C above the chart maximum. Now?","a":"LNAV minima only, as a CDFA to DDA = MDA + 50 ft; no temperature compensation.","keys":[{"k":"LNAV only","m":["lnav"]},{"k":"MDA + 50 ft","m":["50 ft","50ft","50 feet","dda"]}]},{"q":"GPS PRIMARY LOST on final. Continue?","a":"No, discontinue (go around) unless visual reference is established; RNP APCH needs GNSS. ANP > RNP is an immediate go-around.","keys":[{"k":"Go around","m":["go around","discontinue"]},{"k":"Unless visual","m":["visual"]}]},{"q":"Missed approach on what?","a":"Published missed approach in LNAV on IRS/DME while ANP stays within 1.0 NM; else conventional navaids or radar vectors; tell ATC.","keys":[{"k":"ANP within 1.0 NM","m":["1.0 nm","10 nm","1 nm","anp"]},{"k":"Conventional / vectors","m":["conventional","vector"]}]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-chain-3","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: 'Your position jumps 20 NM on the map over the Middle East.'","src":"AIX Fly-Safe Issue 17; OM-A 30.6, 30.8; QRH MAN.1.4; FCOM 10.20.12, 15.20.12","ref":"S17, S21","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What is happening?","a":"Likely GPS spoofing: false signals giving wrong position or time (carry-off or meaconing). Jamming would just lose GPS.","keys":[{"k":"Spoofing","m":["spoof"]},{"k":"False signal","m":["false","fake"]}]},{"q":"What else might you see?","a":"ANP/EPU jump, GPS-L/R INVALID or VERIFY POSITION, UNABLE REQD NAV PERF-RNP, clock or date jump, odd wind/GS, false EGPWS, ATC reports wrong ADS-B.","keys":[{"k":"ANP jump","m":["anp","epu"]},{"k":"Clock jump","m":["clock","utc","date"]},{"k":"False EGPWS","m":["egpws","gpws","terrain"]}]},{"q":"Actions?","a":"Heading mode, deselect GPS early, confirm IRS integrity, fly OFP tracks with DME/VOR cross-checks, tell ATC, IRS only until clear, report to maintenance and DGCA.","keys":[{"k":"Heading mode","m":["heading"]},{"k":"Deselect GPS","m":["deselect"]},{"k":"IRS","m":["irs"]}]},{"q":"You get a PULL UP at FL350. Respond?","a":"Default is to fly it; but a look-ahead warning in cruise immediately verified above MSA can be considered unreliable. TERR INHIBIT; RA-based alerts still work.","keys":[{"k":"Above MSA: unreliable","m":["msa","minimum safe","unreliable"]},{"k":"TERR INHIBIT","m":["terr inhibit","terrain inhibit"]}]},{"q":"Which clock do you trust, and how do you report?","a":"Personal watch checked before the area, or ATC/ACARS time (flight deck clocks are GPS-fed). Report to DGCA within 10 minutes; tech log; EGPWS report copy to DGCA within 24 h.","keys":[{"k":"Personal watch","m":["watch"]},{"k":"10 minutes","m":["10 min","10min","ten min"]}]}],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw the RNP containment concept: the RNP value and the integrity boundary around the desired track.","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN","prio":2,"a":"A desired track line with two pairs of parallel boundaries:\n- Inner: +/-1 x RNP, labelled \"95% of flight time (accuracy)\".\n- Outer: +/-2 x RNP, labelled \"containment, 99.999% (integrity)\".\n- A note: TSE = NSE + FTE + PDE, FTE largest.\n- A note: alert (UNABLE REQD NAV PERF-RNP) when ANP exceeds RNP.","checklist":["Desired track line","1 x RNP boundary labelled 95%","2 x RNP boundary labelled 99.999%","TSE = NSE + FTE + PDE noted","Alerting when ANP exceeds RNP noted"],"topic":3},{"id":"t3-draw-2","type":"draw","q":"Draw an RNP APCH profile with the lateral scaling and minima.","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"Key facts: PBN (RNP APCH)","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Plan and side view: IAF to IF to FAF to MAPt to missed approach.\n- Lateral scaling labelled +/-1.0 NM initial, +/-0.3 NM final, +/-1.0 NM missed.\n- No DIRECT TO the FAF; DIRECT TO the IF only with 45° or less.\n- Side view: continuous descent path; LNAV MDA (DDA = MDA + 50 ft), LNAV/VNAV DA (lowest 250 ft), LPV.\n- Baro-VNAV +/-75 ft band; temperature range note (outside: LNAV only).","checklist":["IAF, IF, FAF, MAPt and missed approach drawn","Scaling 1.0 / 0.3 / 1.0 NM labelled","Direct-to rules at IF (45°) and FAF (never)","MDA with DDA = MDA + 50 ft","LNAV/VNAV DA, lowest 250 ft","Baro-VNAV +/-75 ft","Temperature limit note"],"topic":3},{"id":"t4-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"What are the components of block fuel under your fuel policy?","src":"OM-A 12.1.3","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Block fuel is built up from these components [OM-A 12.1.3]:\n- **Taxi and APU** fuel\n- **Trip** fuel\n- **Contingency:** 5% of trip, minimum 5 min hold at 1,500 ft above destination\n- **Destination alternate** fuel\n- **Final reserve:** 30 min hold at 1,500 ft above the alternate\n- **Additional** fuel (engine failure or depressurisation at the critical point, EDTO)\n- **Discretionary** fuel at the captain's decision.\nMinimum diversion fuel is alternate fuel plus final reserve.","keys":[{"k":"Taxi","m":["taxi"]},{"k":"Trip","m":["trip"]},{"k":"Contingency","m":["contingency"]},{"k":"Alternate","m":["alternate"]},{"k":"Final reserve","m":["final reserve","reserve fuel"]},{"k":"Additional","m":["additional"]},{"k":"Discretionary","m":["discretionary","extra fuel"]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-num-1","type":"num","q":"Contingency fuel is what percentage of trip fuel?","src":"OM-A 12.1.3","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":5,"unit":"%","why":"Contingency is 5% of trip fuel, with a minimum of 5 min holding at 1,500 ft above the destination.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-num-2","type":"num","q":"Minimum contingency fuel: how many minutes of holding?","src":"OM-A 12.1.3 c","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":5,"unit":"min","why":"At least 5 min at holding speed, 1,500 ft above the destination.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-num-3","type":"num","q":"Final reserve fuel: how many minutes of holding?","src":"OM-A 12.1.3 e","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":30,"unit":"min","why":"30 min at holding speed, 1,500 ft above the destination alternate elevation.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-num-4","type":"num","q":"At what height above the alternate is final reserve fuel calculated?","src":"OM-A 12.1.3 e","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1500,"unit":"ft","why":"Final reserve is a 30 min hold at 1,500 ft above the destination alternate elevation.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"Minimum diversion fuel (MDF) is:","src":"OM-A 12.1.3","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","prio":1,"o":["Alternate fuel + final reserve","Alternate fuel + contingency","Trip + final reserve","Alternate fuel + contingency + final reserve"],"a":0,"why":"MDF = alternate fuel + final reserve.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"How many destination alternates must you plan, and when do you need two?","src":"OM-A 12.1.3; OM-A 12.1.6","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","prio":1,"a":"**At least one destination alternate on every flight** under AIX policy.\n- **Two alternates** are required when the destination weather is below planning minima or there is no MET information for the destination.\n- With two, plan the alternate fuel for the **farther** of the two.\n- AIX does not operate to isolated aerodromes, so the no-alternate isolated case does not arise [OM-A 12.1.6].\n- CAR Sec 8 Series O Part II is the regulatory base.","keys":[{"k":"One on every flight","m":["every flight","always one","at least one"]},{"k":"Two if below planning minima","m":["planning minima","below minima"]},{"k":"Or no MET info","m":["no met","no weather","no meteorological"]},{"k":"Fuel for the farther one","m":["farther","further","farthest","furthest"]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"When must you check fuel in flight, and what is the continuance rule?","src":"OM-A 12.2.1; OM-A 12.3","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","prio":2,"a":"**In-flight fuel checks** are done at top of climb, at top of descent and every 30 minutes [OM-A 12.2.1].\n- **Continuance rule:** fuel on board must stay at or above remaining trip fuel + alternate fuel + final reserve [OM-A 12.3].\n- If a check shows you will not meet it, re-plan: a nearer alternate, a reclearance or a diversion.\n- If you may land with less than final reserve, the MINIMUM FUEL and MAYDAY FUEL declarations apply [OM-A 12.2.2].","keys":[{"k":"Top of climb","m":["toc","top of climb"]},{"k":"Top of descent","m":["tod","top of descent"]},{"k":"Every 30 min","m":["30 min","30min","30 minutes","half an hour","half hour"]},{"k":"Remaining trip + alternate + final reserve","m":["final reserve","reserve"]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-num-5","type":"num","q":"How often (minutes) must in-flight fuel checks be made, in addition to TOC and TOD?","src":"OM-A 12.2.1","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","prio":2,"ans":30,"unit":"min","why":"Checks at TOC, TOD and every 30 minutes.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"What is reclearance in flight (decision point procedure) and what is its benefit?","src":"OM-A 12.1.8","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Reclearance** plans the flight to a nearer refuelling aerodrome, with the option to continue to the real destination if fuel at the decision point allows [OM-A 12.1.8].\n- **Decision point** at 80 to 87% of the stage.\n- **Block fuel** is the greater of F1 (to the destination) and F2 (to the refuelling airport).\n- **Minimum reclearance contingency:** 300 kg.\n- Filed in flight plan item 18 as **RIF/**.\n- **Benefit:** it saves the contingency fuel for the leg before the decision point, allowing more payload or less fuel burn.","keys":[{"k":"Decision point 80 to 87%","m":["80 to 87","80 87","decision point"]},{"k":"Greater of F1 and F2","m":["greater","f1 and f2","f1 or f2"]},{"k":"Min contingency 300 kg","m":["300 kg","300kg","300 kilo"]},{"k":"Item 18 RIF","m":["rif","item 18"]},{"k":"Saves contingency fuel","m":["saves contingency","contingency fuel","more payload","less fuel"]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-num-6","type":"num","q":"Minimum reclearance contingency fuel?","src":"OM-A 12.1.8","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","prio":2,"ans":300,"unit":"kg","why":"OM-A sets a minimum reclearance contingency of 300 kg.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"Where in the stage is the reclearance decision point placed?","src":"OM-A 12.1.8","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","prio":2,"o":["50 to 60% of the stage","80 to 87% of the stage","90 to 95% of the stage","At top of descent"],"a":1,"why":"The decision point is at 80 to 87% of the stage.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"Which flight plan item and indicator shows a reclearance?","src":"OM-A 12.1.8","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","prio":3,"o":["Item 15, DCT","Item 18, RIF/","Item 18, RMK/","Item 19, R/"],"a":1,"why":"Reclearance goes in item 18 as RIF/.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"When is a take-off alternate required, and how far away may it be?","src":"OM-A 7.6; OM-A 45.1.1","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Required when** the departure weather is below landing minima, or a return to the departure aerodrome is not possible [OM-A 7.6].\n- **Distance:** within 1 hour at one-engine-inoperative cruise speed, in ISA still air at the actual take-off mass. For the MAX that is 420 NM.\n- **Weather** there, actual and forecast, must be at or above the applicable minima (for LVTO, at or above CAT I or the applicable approach minima) [OM-A 45.1.1].\n- Only an EDTO-approved flight may use the first alternate within its maximum diversion time.","keys":[{"k":"Departure below landing minima","m":["below landing minima","landing minima"]},{"k":"Return not possible","m":["return","cannot return"]},{"k":"Within 1 h OEI speed","m":["1 hour","one hour","60 min","1 hr","oei","one engine"]},{"k":"420 NM for the MAX","m":["420"]},{"k":"Weather at or above minima","m":["at or above","above minima"]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-num-7","type":"num","q":"Maximum distance to a take-off alternate for the 737 MAX (1 h at OEI cruise speed)?","src":"OM-A 7.6","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":420,"unit":"NM","why":"1 h at one-engine-inoperative cruise speed, which OM-A gives as 420 NM for the MAX.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"What limits apply to fuel tankering on the 737 MAX?","src":"OM-A 12.4","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","prio":3,"a":"**Tankering is limited** by OM-A 12.4:\n- Landing fuel is limited to **3000 kg** on the B737 MAX if the next sector is unknown.\n- Landing weight must be at least **500 kg below MLW**.\nIn the viva give both numbers and the condition: the 3000 kg cap applies when the next sector is unknown, and the MLW margin applies to every tankering sector.","keys":[{"k":"3000 kg landing fuel","m":["3000 kg","3000kg","3000","3 tonnes","3 tons"]},{"k":"Next sector unknown","m":["next sector","unknown"]},{"k":"500 kg below MLW","m":["500 kg","500kg","below mlw","maximum landing weight"]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-num-8","type":"num","q":"Tankering: maximum landing fuel on the B737 MAX when the next sector is unknown?","src":"OM-A 12.4","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","prio":3,"ans":3000,"unit":"kg","why":"OM-A 12.4 limits landing fuel to 3000 kg.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-num-9","type":"num","q":"Tankering: landing weight must be at least how far below MLW?","src":"OM-A 12.4","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","prio":3,"ans":500,"unit":"kg","why":"At least 500 kg below MLW.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between the MMEL and the MEL?","src":"OM-A Ch 0","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **MMEL (Master MEL):** established for the aircraft type by the organisation responsible for the type design, with the approval of the State of Design.\n- **MEL:** prepared by the operator for its own aircraft, in conformity with the MMEL **or more restrictive** than it [OM-A Ch 0].\n- So the MEL can never be less restrictive than the MMEL. The MEL covers inoperative items; a missing item is an airworthiness matter.","keys":[{"k":"MMEL by type design organisation","m":["type design","manufacturer","boeing"]},{"k":"State of Design approval","m":["state of design"]},{"k":"MEL by operator","m":["operator"]},{"k":"Same or more restrictive","m":["more restrictive","conformity"]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"The MMEL is approved by:","src":"OM-A Ch 0","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["The State of the Operator","The State of Registry","The State of Design","The operator's CAMO"],"a":2,"why":"MMEL: established by the type design organisation with approval of the State of Design. The operator's MEL conforms to it or is more restrictive.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"Does your policy allow a flight with no destination alternate, and can a second runway count as the alternate?","src":"OM-A 12.1.3; OM-A 7.5; OM-A 12.1.6","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","prio":3,"a":"**AIX policy:** at least one destination alternate on every flight [OM-A 12.1.3].\n- Whether CAR Sec 8 Series O Part II itself allows \"no destination alternate\" cases is still to be confirmed from the CAR text.\n- A second runway at the same airport is used in OM-A 7.5 only to **reduce planning minima**, not as the alternate.\n- AIX does not operate to isolated aerodromes [OM-A 12.1.6].\nSo in the viva: always one alternate for AIX; quote the CAR once checked.","keys":[{"k":"At least one alternate always","m":["at least one","every flight","always"]},{"k":"Separate runway reduces planning minima","m":["planning minima","reduce"]},{"k":"No isolated aerodromes","m":["isolated"]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"You arrive at destination with 12 minutes of extra fuel over your planned landing fuel. What does that mean and what do you do?","src":"OM-A 12.1.3; OM-A 12.2.1; OM-A 12.2.2","ref":"S42","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**It is about 12 minutes of holding or delay before you must leave for the alternate.** Planned landing fuel is minimum diversion fuel (alternate + final reserve) plus what is left of contingency [OM-A 12.1.3].\n- Set a **diversion decision fuel** equal to minimum diversion fuel and ask ATC for the expected delay [OM-A 12.2.1].\n- If the delay will exceed about 12 minutes, **divert in good time** rather than eat into alternate fuel.\n- If you commit to land at destination instead, declare **MINIMUM FUEL** once any change could put you below final reserve [OM-A 12.2.2].","keys":[{"k":"About 12 min of holding before diverting","m":["12 min","12 minutes","twelve"]},{"k":"Diversion decision fuel = MDF","m":["decision fuel","minimum diversion fuel","mdf","alternate fuel"]},{"k":"Ask ATC expected delay","m":["delay","expected approach","eat"]},{"k":"Divert in good time","m":["divert"]},{"k":"Committed: MINIMUM FUEL","m":["minimum fuel"]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"Where do you declare MINIMUM FUEL, destination or alternate? When does it become MAYDAY?","src":"OM-A 12.2.2.1; OM-A 12.2.2.2","ref":"S43","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**MINIMUM FUEL** is declared wherever you are **committed to land at a specific aerodrome** and any change to the existing clearance may result in landing with less than planned final reserve [OM-A 12.2.2.1].\n- Usually at the alternate after a diversion, or at destination once you have decided not to divert.\n- It is **not an emergency**; it warns that one is possible with any more delay.\n**MAYDAY MAYDAY MAYDAY FUEL** when the calculated usable fuel on landing at the **nearest suitable aerodrome** is less than planned final reserve [OM-A 12.2.2.2].","keys":[{"k":"Committed to land at a specific aerodrome","m":["committed"]},{"k":"Any change may land below final reserve","m":["final reserve","less than"]},{"k":"Not an emergency","m":["not an emergency","not emergency"]},{"k":"MAYDAY FUEL","m":["mayday"]},{"k":"Nearest suitable aerodrome","m":["nearest suitable","nearest"]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"MAYDAY FUEL is declared when calculated usable fuel on landing at the nearest suitable aerodrome is less than:","src":"OM-A 12.2.2.2","ref":"S43","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Contingency fuel","Planned final reserve","Minimum diversion fuel","Alternate fuel"],"a":1,"why":"MAYDAY MAYDAY MAYDAY FUEL: calculated usable fuel on landing at the nearest suitable aerodrome below planned final reserve.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"Is a MINIMUM FUEL declaration an emergency?","src":"OM-A 12.2.2.1","ref":"S43","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Yes, it is a distress call","Yes, it is an urgency (PAN) call","No, it warns an emergency is possible with further delay","No, it is only a company message"],"a":2,"why":"MINIMUM FUEL is not an emergency; it tells ATC that any further delay may lead to one.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"At preflight you find the standby magnetic compass missing. Can you go?","src":"OM-A Ch 3","ref":"S44","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Normally no-go until engineering resolves it.**\n- The standby compass is **listed equipment** for the B737: 1 required [OM-A Ch 3].\n- **Check the MEL, ATA 34.** If there is no relief, it is a no-go.\n- The MEL covers **inoperative** items. A **missing** item is an airworthiness matter, not an MEL deferral.\n- The exact AIX MEL ATA 34 entry and its conditions are still to be confirmed from the MEL.","keys":[{"k":"Required equipment, 1 required","m":["required","listed"]},{"k":"Check the MEL","m":["mel"]},{"k":"ATA 34","m":["ata 34","ata34"]},{"k":"Missing is not inoperative, airworthiness","m":["missing","airworthiness"]},{"k":"Call engineering","m":["engineering","engineer","ame"]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"On the take-off roll you realise V2 was never inserted. What do you see and what do you do?","src":"FCOM 10.10.10; FCOM 4.20.14","ref":"S45","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Below 80 kt reject; at high speed continue.**\n- **Indication:** the amber NO VSPD flag shows on the ground unless V1 and VR are both set [FCOM 10.10.10].\n- **Why it matters:** V2 is the MCP speed. After lift-off the F/D holds 15° until climb rate is sufficient, then commands MCP speed + 20 kt, so its pitch target is wrong [FCOM 4.20.14].\n- **Continuing:** rotate at the briefed VR (PM calls it from the take-off data card), fly pitch manually towards 15° and V2 + 15 to 20, and enter the speeds when workload allows.\n- The AIX SOP for this case is still to be confirmed.","keys":[{"k":"NO VSPD flag","m":["no vspd","vspd"]},{"k":"Low speed reject (below 80 kt)","m":["80 kt","80kt","80 knots","reject","rto"]},{"k":"High speed continue","m":["continue"]},{"k":"Rotate at briefed VR","m":["rotate","briefed vr"]},{"k":"Fly 15 degrees pitch, V2+15 to 20","m":["15 deg","15°","pitch","v2 15","v2 plus"]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-num-10","type":"num","q":"After lift-off, what pitch (degrees) does the F/D hold until a sufficient climb rate?","src":"FCOM 4.20.14","ref":"S45","prio":3,"ans":15,"unit":"°","why":"The F/D holds 15° until a sufficient climb rate, then commands MCP speed + 20 kt.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"You have diverted, declared MINIMUM FUEL and are now holding at the alternate. What do you do?","src":"OM-A 12.2.2; OM-A 12.2.1","ref":"S46","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Keep ATC updated** with your fuel state in minutes and ask for priority.\n- The moment the **calculated landing fuel at the nearest suitable aerodrome drops below final reserve**, declare **MAYDAY FUEL** [OM-A 12.2.2].\n- **Keep a nearer or better option in hand:** other runways or aerodromes, including closed or military fields ATC may offer.\n- The PIC must always keep enough usable fuel to land somewhere safely with final reserve [OM-A 12.2.1].","keys":[{"k":"Fuel in minutes to ATC","m":["minutes","fuel state"]},{"k":"Ask for priority","m":["priority"]},{"k":"Below final reserve: MAYDAY FUEL","m":["mayday"]},{"k":"Nearest suitable aerodrome","m":["nearest"]},{"k":"Other options incl military/closed","m":["military","other runway","other aerodrome","closed"]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"You are approaching destination and ATC announces holding. Talk me through your fuel.\"","src":"OM-A 12.1.3; OM-A 12.2.1; OM-A 12.2.2","ref":"S42; S43; S46","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What fuel do you plan to land with at destination?","a":"Minimum diversion fuel (alternate + final reserve) plus remaining contingency.","keys":[{"k":"Alternate fuel","m":["alternate"]},{"k":"Final reserve","m":["final reserve"]},{"k":"Contingency","m":["contingency"]}]},{"q":"ATC gives 20 minutes of delay and you have 12 minutes extra. What now?","a":"Set diversion decision fuel = MDF; the delay exceeds the extra, so divert in good time.","keys":[{"k":"Decision fuel","m":["decision fuel","mdf","minimum diversion"]},{"k":"Divert","m":["divert"]}]},{"q":"You divert. At the alternate there is more delay. When do you call MINIMUM FUEL?","a":"When committed to land there and any change may land you below planned final reserve.","keys":[{"k":"Committed","m":["committed"]},{"k":"Below final reserve","m":["final reserve"]}]},{"q":"And when MAYDAY?","a":"When calculated usable fuel on landing at the nearest suitable aerodrome is below planned final reserve.","keys":[{"k":"MAYDAY FUEL","m":["mayday"]},{"k":"Nearest suitable","m":["nearest"]},{"k":"Final reserve","m":["final reserve"]}]},{"q":"What is final reserve on your fleet?","a":"30 min holding at 1,500 ft above the alternate.","keys":[{"k":"30 min","m":["30 min","30 minutes","thirty"]},{"k":"1500 ft","m":["1500"]}]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"Departure weather is below your landing minima. Can you take off?\"","src":"OM-A 7.6; OM-A 45.1.1; OM-A 4.3.2.1","ref":"S28; Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","asked":true,"prio":2,"steps":[{"q":"What do you need?","a":"A take-off alternate.","keys":[{"k":"Take-off alternate","m":["take off alternate","takeoff alternate","alternate"]}]},{"q":"How far can it be?","a":"Within 1 h at OEI cruise speed in ISA still air at actual take-off mass: 420 NM for the MAX.","keys":[{"k":"1 h OEI","m":["1 hour","one hour","60 min","oei","one engine"]},{"k":"420 NM","m":["420"]}]},{"q":"All take-off alternates within 1 hour are deteriorating. Now?","a":"Do not depart. Wait for departure weather to reach landing minima, or have OCC find another qualifying alternate.","keys":[{"k":"Don't depart","m":["not depart","dont depart","wait"]},{"k":"OCC another alternate","m":["occ","another alternate"]}]},{"q":"Could an EDTO flight do differently?","a":"Yes, an EDTO-approved flight may use the first available alternate within its maximum diversion time.","keys":[{"k":"EDTO","m":["edto"]},{"k":"Maximum diversion time","m":["diversion time"]}]}],"topic":4},{"id":"t4-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match each fuel component to its definition.","src":"OM-A 12.1.3","ref":"Key facts: Fuel policy, alternates and dispatch","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Contingency","5% of trip, minimum 5 min hold at 1,500 ft above destination"],["Final reserve","30 min hold at 1,500 ft above the alternate"],["Additional","Engine failure or depressurisation at the critical point, EDTO"],["MDF","Alternate fuel + final reserve"]],"why":"From the fuel policy component list.","topic":4},{"id":"t4-order-1","type":"order","q":"Order the in-flight fuel escalation, from routine to distress.","src":"OM-A 12.2.1; OM-A 12.2.2","ref":"S42; S43","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Routine fuel checks at TOC, TOD and every 30 min","Set diversion decision fuel and ask ATC for expected delay","Committed to an aerodrome: declare MINIMUM FUEL","Calculated landing fuel at nearest suitable aerodrome below final reserve: MAYDAY FUEL"],"why":"Checks, then a decision fuel, then MINIMUM FUEL when committed, then MAYDAY FUEL when below final reserve.","topic":4},{"id":"t5-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"Who computes your aerodrome operating minima, and how are they expressed below 800 m visibility?","src":"OM-A 7.1","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Jeppesen computes them**, on the 11-0 / 21-0 pages, and they are **never lower than State minima** [OM-A 7.1].\n- Below **800 m visibility**, minima must be given as **RVR**.\n- 2D: MDA/H plus visibility; 3D: DA/H plus visibility or RVR [OM-A 7.1.1 to 7.1.3].\n- The PIC still checks that actual reported conditions are at or above minima even when ATC has cleared the approach [OM-A 7.2 Note].","keys":[{"k":"Jeppesen","m":["jeppesen","jepp"]},{"k":"Never below State minima","m":["state minima","not lower","never lower","never below"]},{"k":"Below 800 m as RVR","m":["800 m","800m","800 metre","800 meter"]},{"k":"RVR","m":["rvr"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"What are aerodrome operating minima, who sets them, and what factors must the operator consider?","src":"OM-A Ch 0 def 24; OM-A 7.1; OM-A 45.1.4.1; Oral bank p.11","ref":"OB11","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**AOM are the limits of usability of an aerodrome** for take-off (RVR/visibility, cloud if needed), 2D landing (visibility/RVR, MDA/H) and 3D landing (visibility/RVR and DA/H for the category) [OM-A Ch 0 def 24].\n- Set under CAR Sec 8 Series C Part I and Series O Part II; computed by Jeppesen to Doc 9365 or State minima, whichever is higher [OM-A 7.1], from AIS data (AAI or defence) [OM-A 45.1.4.1].\n- **Factors:** aircraft type, performance and handling; crew composition and experience; runway dimensions; equipment; obstacles in approach, missed approach and climb-out; OCA/H; met reporting; CDFA or step-down technique [Oral bank p.11].","keys":[{"k":"Limits of usability","m":["usability","limits"]},{"k":"Take-off and landing","m":["take off","takeoff","landing"]},{"k":"Jeppesen / State, whichever higher","m":["jeppesen","state","higher"]},{"k":"Aircraft performance and equipment","m":["performance","equipment","aircraft type"]},{"k":"Crew composition and experience","m":["crew"]},{"k":"Obstacles / OCA/H","m":["obstacle","oca","och"]},{"k":"Met reporting","m":["met","weather report"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"What is restricted AOM in your company?","src":"OM-A 7.2 (f); OM-A 7.2.2","ref":"OB11","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Restricted AOM is a new-captain rule** [OM-A 7.2.2]:\n- DA/MDA **+100 ft** and VIS/RVR **+400 m**, the increment capped at 5000 m.\n- Applies to **landing minima only**, at the destination, not to alternates.\n- Applies until the PIC has **100 h in command on type**, or if the PIC does not meet Ch 24.\n- **Restricted captains may not do LVTO** [OM-A 7.2 (f), 7.2.2].","keys":[{"k":"+100 ft","m":["100 ft","100ft","100 feet"]},{"k":"+400 m","m":["400 m","400m","400 metre","400 meter"]},{"k":"100 h in command on type","m":["100 h","100 hours","100hr","100 hrs","in command"]},{"k":"Landing only, not alternates","m":["landing","not alternate","destination"]},{"k":"No LVTO","m":["lvto","low visibility take"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"TRAP: Is restricted AOM a rule for non-scheduled operators, lifted with 200 h on type and 10 h in 90 days?","src":"OM-A 7.2.2","ref":"OB11","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"**No, not for AIX.** That is the oral bank's version for non-scheduled operators, and those NSOP criteria are not in our OM-A.\n- In AIX, restricted AOM is a **new-captain rule**: +100 ft and +400 m until the captain has **100 h in command on type** [OM-A 7.2.2].\n- Landing minima at destination only; restricted captains do not do LVTO.","keys":[{"k":"New-captain rule","m":["new captain","captain"]},{"k":"100 h in command on type","m":["100 h","100 hours","100hr","100 hrs"]},{"k":"+100 ft / +400 m","m":["100 ft","400 m","400m"]},{"k":"NSOP figures not in OM-A","m":["not in","nsop","non scheduled"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["200 h","200 hours","200hr","200 hrs"],"why":"Bank's non-scheduled operator figure; AIX uses 100 h in command on type"},{"m":["10 h in 90","10 hours in 90"],"why":"Bank's NSOP criterion, not in the OM-A"}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-1","type":"num","q":"Restricted AOM: DA/MDA increment?","src":"OM-A 7.2.2","ref":"OB11","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":100,"unit":"ft","why":"+100 ft and +400 m until 100 h in command on type.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-2","type":"num","q":"Restricted AOM: visibility/RVR increment?","src":"OM-A 7.2.2","ref":"OB11","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":400,"unit":"m","why":"+400 m (increment capped at 5000 m).","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-3","type":"num","q":"Restricted AOM applies until the captain has how many hours in command on type?","src":"OM-A 7.2.2","ref":"OB11","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":100,"unit":"h","why":"100 h in command on type.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"The B737 approach category is:","src":"OM-A 7.2.3","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["B (VAT 91 to 120 kt)","C (VAT 121 to 140 kt)","D (VAT 141 to 165 kt)","C (VAT 141 to 165 kt)"],"a":1,"why":"The B737 is Category C, VAT 121 to 140 kt.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"How does Annex 6 classify approach operations? Give Type A, Type B and the CAT I, II, III limits.","src":"OM-A Ch 0","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**By 2D vs 3D, and by Type A and B** [OM-A Ch 0]:\n- **Type A:** MDH or DH **250 ft or more**.\n- **Type B:** DH **below 250 ft**:\n  - **CAT I:** DH 200 ft or more, visibility 800 m or RVR 550 m or more.\n  - **CAT II:** DH 100 to 200 ft, RVR 300 m or more.\n  - **CAT III:** DH below 100 ft or none, RVR below 300 m.\n- The new nomenclature no longer splits CAT III into A, B and C.","keys":[{"k":"Type A 250 ft or more","m":["250"]},{"k":"Type B below 250 ft","m":["type b"]},{"k":"CAT I 200 ft, 550 m","m":["200 ft","550"]},{"k":"CAT II 100 to 200 ft, 300 m","m":["300 m","300m","100 to 200"]},{"k":"CAT III below 100 ft","m":["below 100","less than 100"]},{"k":"No more IIIA/B/C split","m":["no longer","single cat","not split"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"Under the Annex 6 classification, a Type A operation has a minimum descent height or decision height of:","src":"OM-A Ch 0","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["200 ft or more","250 ft or more","300 ft or more","Below 250 ft"],"a":1,"why":"Type A: MDH/DH 250 ft or more. Type B: DH below 250 ft.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the approach category (Annex 6) to its DH and RVR.","src":"OM-A Ch 0","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["CAT I","DH 200 ft or more; vis 800 m or RVR 550 m or more"],["CAT II","DH 100 to 200 ft; RVR 300 m or more"],["CAT III","DH below 100 ft or none; RVR below 300 m"],["Type A","MDH/DH 250 ft or more"]],"why":"Annex 6 classification as given in OM-A Ch 0.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"What are your company lowest minima for CAT I, APV and NPA, and the system minima by aid?","src":"OM-A 7.3.1","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Lowest minima** [OM-A 7.3.1]:\n- **CAT I:** 200 ft and 550 m.\n- **APV:** 250 ft and 600 m.\n- **NPA:** 250 ft and 750 m.\n**System minima:** ILS 200 ft, LOC 250 ft, VOR 300 ft, VOR/DME 250 ft, NDB 350 ft.\nNot flying CDFA on an NPA adds 400 m [OM-A 7.2.7].","keys":[{"k":"CAT I 200 ft / 550 m","m":["550"]},{"k":"APV 250 ft / 600 m","m":["600"]},{"k":"NPA 250 ft / 750 m","m":["750"]},{"k":"VOR 300 ft","m":["vor 300","300"]},{"k":"NDB 350 ft","m":["350"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-4","type":"num","q":"CAT I minimum RVR?","src":"OM-A 7.3.1","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":550,"unit":"m","why":"CAT I: 200 ft and 550 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-5","type":"num","q":"APV minimum RVR?","src":"OM-A 7.3.1","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":600,"unit":"m","why":"APV: 250 ft and 600 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-6","type":"num","q":"NPA minimum RVR (CDFA flown)?","src":"OM-A 7.3.1","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":750,"unit":"m","why":"NPA: 250 ft and 750 m; without CDFA add 400 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match the facility to its system minimum.","src":"OM-A 7.3.1","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["ILS","200 ft"],["LOC","250 ft"],["VOR","300 ft"],["VOR/DME","250 ft"],["NDB","350 ft"]],"why":"System minima from OM-A 7.3.1.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-7","type":"num","q":"System minimum for an NDB approach?","src":"OM-A 7.3.1","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","prio":2,"ans":350,"unit":"ft","why":"ILS 200, LOC 250, VOR 300, VOR/DME 250, NDB 350.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"Give the CAT II RVR minima by DH band, and the CAT III minima.","src":"OM-A 7.3.6; OM-A 7.3.6.2","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**CAT II** [OM-A 7.3.6]:\n- DH 100 to 120 ft: RVR **300 m**\n- DH 121 to 140 ft: RVR **400 m**\n- DH 141 to 199 ft: RVR **450 m**\n**CAT III:**\n- No rollout guidance (fail-passive): DH below 100 ft, RVR **175 m**.\n- Fail-operational: DH below 50 ft or none, RVR **50 m** on the B737 [OM-A 7.3.6.2].\n**RVRs required:** CAT I TDZ; CAT II TDZ and mid; CAT III all three.","keys":[{"k":"CAT II 300 m at DH 100 to 120","m":["300"]},{"k":"400 m at 121 to 140","m":["400"]},{"k":"450 m at 141 to 199","m":["450"]},{"k":"Fail-passive 175 m","m":["175"]},{"k":"Fail-op 50 m","m":["50 m","50m","50 metre","50 meter"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-8","type":"num","q":"CAT II minimum RVR with DH 100 to 120 ft?","src":"OM-A 7.3.6","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":300,"unit":"m","why":"DH 100 to 120 ft: 300 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-9","type":"num","q":"CAT II minimum RVR with DH 121 to 140 ft?","src":"OM-A 7.3.6","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","prio":2,"ans":400,"unit":"m","why":"DH 121 to 140 ft: 400 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-10","type":"num","q":"CAT II minimum RVR with DH 141 to 199 ft?","src":"OM-A 7.3.6","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","prio":2,"ans":450,"unit":"m","why":"DH 141 to 199 ft: 450 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-11","type":"num","q":"CAT III without rollout guidance (fail-passive): minimum RVR?","src":"OM-A 7.3.6.2","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":175,"unit":"m","why":"DH below 100 ft, RVR 175 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-12","type":"num","q":"CAT III fail-operational on the B737: minimum RVR?","src":"OM-A 7.3.6.2; OM-A 45.1.1","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":50,"unit":"m","why":"DH below 50 ft or no DH, RVR 50 m for the B737 (75 m for the A320).","topic":5},{"id":"t5-match-3","type":"match","q":"Which RVR readings are required for each category?","src":"OM-A 7.3.6","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","prio":2,"pairs":[["CAT I","TDZ"],["CAT II","TDZ and mid"],["CAT III","TDZ, mid and rollout"]],"why":"OM-A 7.3.6: CAT I TDZ; CAT II TDZ and mid; CAT III all three.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-match-4","type":"match","q":"Match the operation to the minimum visual reference required at DH/MDA.","src":"OM-A 7.3.7","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["CAT I and NPA","Any one listed element"],["CAT II","3 consecutive lights plus a lateral element"],["CAT III, DH below 100 ft","3 consecutive lights"],["CAT III, DH below 50 ft","1 centreline light"],["No DH","None required before touchdown"]],"why":"OM-A 7.3.7 visual reference table.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"What visual reference do you need at minima for CAT I, CAT II and CAT III?","src":"OM-A 7.3.7","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**It depends on the category** [OM-A 7.3.7]:\n- **CAT I and NPA:** any one listed element, distinctly visible and identifiable.\n- **CAT II:** 3 consecutive lights plus a lateral element.\n- **CAT III with DH below 100 ft:** 3 consecutive lights.\n- **CAT III with DH below 50 ft:** 1 centreline light.\n- **No DH:** no visual reference needed before touchdown.","keys":[{"k":"CAT I any listed element","m":["any","one element","listed"]},{"k":"CAT II 3 lights + lateral","m":["lateral"]},{"k":"3 consecutive lights","m":["3 consecutive","three consecutive","3 lights","three lights"]},{"k":"DH below 50: 1 centreline light","m":["1 centreline","one centreline","one centerline","1 centerline","single"]},{"k":"No DH: none","m":["none","no visual","not required"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"What is alert height, and what is it on the B737?","src":"FCTM 5.14; FCTM 5.17; OM-A 45.2.4.2","ref":"NR59","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Alert height** is a height above the runway, above which a CAT III approach must be discontinued and a missed approach initiated if a specified failure occurs [FCTM 5.14].\n- Normally used for **fail-operational** CAT III; most authorities need no visual reference below it.\n- **B737: 200 ft** [OM-A 45.2.4.2].\n- Boeing: AFDS design provides an AH of at least 200 ft HAT, which operators may lower [FCTM 5.17]. It is an operator setting, not something the crew changes in flight.","keys":[{"k":"Above it, failure means go-around","m":["above which","go around","missed approach","discontinue"]},{"k":"Specified failure","m":["failure"]},{"k":"Fail-operational CAT III","m":["fail operational","fail op","cat 3","cat iii"]},{"k":"200 ft on the B737","m":["200 ft","200ft","200 feet"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"TRAP: Your OM-A definitions say alert height is \"typically 100 ft or less\". What alert height do you quote for the 737?","src":"OM-A 45.2.4.2; FCTM 5.17; OM-A Ch 0","ref":"Flag 1: Alert height","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"**200 ft for the B737.**\n- The OM-A Ch 0 definition says \"typically 100 ft or less\", but OM-A 45.2.4.2 sets AH by type: **200 ft for B737**, 100 ft for A320, set by the manufacturer.\n- Boeing: AFDS design provides an AH of at least 200 ft HAT, which may be modified to a lower value by operators [FCTM 5.17].\n- So it is an **operator setting**, not something the crew changes in flight.","keys":[{"k":"200 ft","m":["200 ft","200ft","200 feet"]},{"k":"OM-A 45.2.4.2 by type","m":["45 2 4 2","type","manufacturer"]},{"k":"Operator may lower, not crew","m":["operator"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["100 ft or less","100 feet or less","100ft or less"],"why":"Generic Ch 0 definition; OM-A 45.2.4.2 sets 200 ft for the B737"}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-13","type":"num","q":"Alert height for the B737 per your OM-A?","src":"OM-A 45.2.4.2","ref":"NR59","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"ans":200,"unit":"ft","why":"OM-A 45.2.4.2: 200 ft for B737 (100 ft for A320).","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"What is the \"no switching below 1000 ft\" rule?","src":"OM-A 45.2.8","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","prio":2,"a":"**A failure between 1000 ft and alert height (or DH) means a go-around, unless visual reference is already established** [OM-A 45.2.8].\n- **Downgrade decisions** (for example CAT III to CAT II, or CAT II to CAT I) are made **above 1000 ft** [OM-A 45.2.8.3].\n- So you do not reconfigure or change category in the last 1000 ft; you go around and re-plan.","keys":[{"k":"1000 ft","m":["1000"]},{"k":"Failure below: go around","m":["go around","goaround","missed"]},{"k":"Unless visual","m":["unless visual","visual"]},{"k":"Downgrade above 1000 ft","m":["downgrade","above 1000"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"What MEL items and crew recency do you need for low visibility operations?","src":"OM-A 45.1.3; OM-A 45.1.1.6; OM-A 45.1.14.1","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Aircraft MEL items for LVO** [OM-A 45.1.3]: **wipers** (in precipitation), **window heat**, **anti-skid** and **reversers**, plus anything the AFM/MEL requires.\n**Crew recency** [OM-A 45.1.1.6]: **6 approaches, including 3 simulator landings, in the last 6 months**.\n**Chart:** CAT II/III must be stated in the Jeppesen title; a plain \"ILS RWY\" is CAT I [OM-A 45.1.14.1].","keys":[{"k":"Wipers","m":["wiper"]},{"k":"Window heat","m":["window heat"]},{"k":"Anti-skid","m":["anti skid","antiskid"]},{"k":"Reversers","m":["reverser"]},{"k":"6 approaches in 6 months","m":["6 approaches","six approaches","6 months","six months"]},{"k":"3 simulator landings","m":["3 sim","three sim","simulator"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-14","type":"num","q":"LVO recency: how many approaches in the last 6 months?","src":"OM-A 45.1.1.6","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","prio":2,"ans":6,"unit":"approaches","why":"6 approaches, including 3 simulator landings, in the last 6 months.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"How do you know from the Jeppesen chart that CAT II/III is available?","src":"OM-A 45.1.14.1; Doc 8168 Vol I Att B 5.2","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","prio":2,"o":["A plain \"ILS RWY\" title covers all categories","The title says \"CAT II/III\" explicitly","Only from the 10-9 airport chart","Only from NOTAM"],"a":1,"why":"The title must say CAT II/III; a plain \"ILS RWY\" is CAT I.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"What is an LVTO, and what are the take-off RVR thresholds you must know?","src":"OM-A 7.6; OM-A 45.1.1; OM-A Table 7.5.A","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**LVTO is a take-off with RVR below 400 m.** The AIX minimum is **125 m** [OM-A 7.6, 45.1.1], a DGCA OpsSpecs authorisation.\n- **No take-off below 550 m RVR / 800 m visibility unless LVP is in force.**\n- Lighting rows in Table 7.5.A run from **500 m** (day, adequate visual reference) down to **125 m**.\n- Below 200 m all three RVRs are required; crew may not assess TDZ RVR.","keys":[{"k":"Below 400 m","m":["400"]},{"k":"AIX minimum 125 m","m":["125"]},{"k":"LVP below 550 m / 800 m","m":["550","lvp","low visibility procedure"]},{"k":"Table 7.5.A lighting rows","m":["lighting","lights","500 m","500m"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-15","type":"num","q":"LVTO is defined as take-off with RVR below:","src":"OM-A 7.6; OM-A 45.1.1","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":400,"unit":"m","why":"LVTO = RVR below 400 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-16","type":"num","q":"AIX lowest LVTO RVR?","src":"OM-A 7.6","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":125,"unit":"m","why":"AIX is authorised for LVTO down to 125 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-17","type":"num","q":"Below what RVR may you not take off unless LVP is in force?","src":"OM-A 45.1.1.4.2","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":550,"unit":"m","why":"No take-off below 550 m RVR / 800 m visibility unless LVP is in force.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"What does a low visibility take-off need from the aerodrome, the aircraft and the crew?","src":"OM-A 7.6; OM-A 45.1.3.1; OM-A 45.1.2.2; Annex 14 5.3.12.3, 5.3.20, 5.3.17.1","ref":"OB12","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**LVTO: RVR below 400 m, AIX down to 125 m under DGCA OpsSpecs; LVP below 550 m RVR / 800 m visibility** [OM-A 7.6, 1.8].\n- **Aerodrome:** LVP; centreline lights below about 400 m; stop bars below 350 m (and 350 to 550 m unless other aids); taxiway centreline lights and SMGCS below 350 m [Annex 14].\n- **Aircraft:** wipers, window heat, anti-skid, reversers, plus AFM/MEL items; appropriate thrust rating [OM-A 45.1.3.1].\n- **Crew:** LVTO trained and qualified; no restricted captains; no supervised take-off [OM-A 45.1.2.2, 7.2.2].\n- **Take-off alternate** if departure is below landing minima [OM-A 7.6].","keys":[{"k":"LVP","m":["lvp","low visibility procedure"]},{"k":"Centreline lights / stop bars","m":["centreline","centerline","stop bar"]},{"k":"Wipers, window heat, anti-skid, reversers","m":["anti skid","antiskid","window heat","reverser","wiper"]},{"k":"Crew trained and qualified","m":["trained","qualified"]},{"k":"No restricted captain","m":["restricted"]},{"k":"Take-off alternate","m":["alternate"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"TRAP: Does LVTO need FD, autopilot, RA, weather radar, two VHF COM and two VHF NAV, approved by the State of Registry?","src":"OM-A 45.1.3.1; OM-A 1.8","ref":"OB12","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"**No, that is the oral bank's list.** For AIX:\n- The OM-A aircraft list is **wipers, window heat, anti-skid and reversers**, plus anything the AFM/MEL requires [OM-A 45.1.3.1].\n- LVTO is a **DGCA OpsSpecs authorisation**, given as **State of the Operator**, not the State of Registry [OM-A 1.8].\n- Crew: LVTO trained and qualified, no restricted captains.","keys":[{"k":"Wipers, window heat, anti-skid, reversers","m":["anti skid","antiskid","window heat","reverser"]},{"k":"AFM/MEL items","m":["afm","mel"]},{"k":"DGCA OpsSpecs","m":["opsspec","ops spec","dgca"]},{"k":"State of the Operator","m":["state of the operator","state of operator"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["state of registry"],"why":"LVTO is approved by DGCA as State of the Operator (OpsSpecs)"},{"m":["weather radar","two vhf"],"why":"Bank's list; OM-A 45.1.3.1 lists wipers, window heat, anti-skid, reversers plus AFM/MEL items"}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-order-1","type":"order","q":"Order the take-off lighting rows of Table 7.5.A from the highest RVR to the lowest.","src":"OM-A Table 7.5.A","ref":"OB12","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["500 m: day, adequate visual reference","400 m: edge lights or CL markings","300 m: edge lights and CL markings","200 m: edge and CL lights","150 m: plus relevant RVR","125 m: HI edge lights, CL lights at 15 m or less, relevant RVR"],"why":"Each lower RVR row needs more lighting and RVR information.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-match-5","type":"match","q":"Match the take-off RVR to the lighting it needs (Table 7.5.A).","src":"OM-A Table 7.5.A","ref":"OB12","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["400 m","Edge lights or CL markings"],["300 m","Edge lights and CL markings"],["200 m","Edge and CL lights"],["125 m","HI edge lights, CL lights at 15 m or less, relevant RVR"]],"why":"OM-A Table 7.5.A rows.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"For a 125 m LVTO, the centreline light spacing must be:","src":"OM-A Table 7.5.A","ref":"S23","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["30 m or less","15 m or less","60 m or less","Not specified"],"a":1,"why":"125 m row: high-intensity edge lights, CL lights at 15 m spacing or less, relevant RVR.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"What is the approach ban, and where is the approach ban point?","src":"OM-A 7.2.5; Doc 8168 Vol I Definitions (IAP)","ref":"OB14","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Two gates** [OM-A 7.2.5 a, b]:\n- **Don't commence** if the reported RVR/visibility is below the minimum. Check before the IAF, where the approach procedure starts [Doc 8168 Vol I].\n- **Once started,** if it drops below, you may not continue **below 1000 ft above the aerodrome or into the final approach segment**: from the FAF for an NPA, the FAP for a precision approach.\n- **Past that gate:** continue to DA/H or MDA/H; land only if the required visual reference is established and maintained [7.2.5 c, d].\n- **TDZ RVR always controls**; mid and stop-end too if reported (mid minimum 125 m). No RVR: use CMV.","keys":[{"k":"Don't commence below minima","m":["commence","start"]},{"k":"1000 ft above aerodrome","m":["1000"]},{"k":"Final approach segment / FAF / FAP","m":["final approach segment","faf","fap"]},{"k":"Past gate continue to DA/MDA","m":["continue to","decision alt","mda"]},{"k":"TDZ controlling","m":["tdz","touchdown"]},{"k":"Mid 125 m","m":["125"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"TRAP: The bank says the approach ban point is the IAF. Is that right?","src":"OM-A 7.2.5 b","ref":"OB14","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"**No.** The IAF is where you **check the minima before commencing**, because the approach procedure starts there.\n- The **approach ban point** is **1000 ft above the aerodrome or the final approach segment**: the FAF for an NPA, the FAP for a precision approach [OM-A 7.2.5 b].\n- If conditions drop below minima after that gate, you may continue to DA/MDA and land only with the required visual reference.","keys":[{"k":"IAF is the check before commencing","m":["check","before commenc","commence"]},{"k":"1000 ft above aerodrome","m":["1000"]},{"k":"FAF for NPA","m":["faf","final approach fix"]},{"k":"FAP for precision","m":["fap","final approach point"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["ban point is the iaf","ban point iaf","at the iaf"],"why":"The IAF is where you check before commencing; the ban point is 1000 ft or the FAF/FAP"}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-18","type":"num","q":"Approach ban: minimum mid-point RVR when reported?","src":"OM-A 7.2.5","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":125,"unit":"m","why":"TDZ RVR is controlling; mid minimum 125 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-19","type":"num","q":"Approach ban gate: height above aerodrome below which you may not continue if RVR drops below minima?","src":"OM-A 7.2.5 b","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1000,"unit":"ft","why":"1000 ft above the aerodrome or the final approach segment.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"What is CMV, and what factors do you use?","src":"OM-A 7.2.8; OM-A Table 7.2.A","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**CMV (converted meteorological visibility) converts reported visibility to an RVR equivalent** when RVR is not available [OM-A 7.2.8].\n- **HI approach and runway lights:** day **1.5**, night **2**.\n- **Other lighting:** day **1**, night **1.5**.\n- **No lighting:** day 1, night not applicable.\n- **Never used:** for take-off; for any required RVR minimum below 800 m; when RVR is reported.\n- Why: met visibility is measured away from the approach path, and RVR depends on light intensity and background, so it differs by day and night.","keys":[{"k":"Converts visibility to RVR","m":["convert","rvr"]},{"k":"HI day 1.5 / night 2","m":["1 5","night 2","2 at night","double"]},{"k":"Other day 1 / night 1.5","m":["other light","day 1"]},{"k":"Not for take-off","m":["take off","takeoff"]},{"k":"Not below 800 m","m":["800"]},{"k":"Not when RVR reported","m":["reported"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-match-6","type":"match","q":"Match the lighting and time of day to the CMV factor.","src":"OM-A Table 7.2.A","ref":"OB13","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["HI lights, day","1.5"],["HI lights, night","2"],["Other lights, day","1"],["Other lights, night","1.5"],["No lights, night","Not applicable"]],"why":"OM-A Table 7.2.A.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"CMV may be used when:","src":"OM-A 7.2.8 b, Note 1","ref":"OB13","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["RVR is reported but varying","The required minimum is 550 m","No RVR is reported and the required minimum is 800 m or more","For take-off in fog"],"a":2,"why":"Not used for take-off, for any RVR minimum below 800 m, or when RVR is reported. \"RVR more than 1500 m\" does not count as a reported value.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"RVR isn't reported. Jaipur needs 900 m RVR and met visibility is 500 m. Can you start the approach?","src":"OM-A 7.2.8; OM-A Table 7.2.A","ref":"OB13","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"**It depends on day or night: always say which.**\n- **Night, HI approach and runway lights:** 500 x 2 = **1000 m**, above 900 m: **yes**.\n- **Day:** 500 x 1.5 = **750 m**, below 900 m: **no** [OM-A 7.2.8, Table 7.2.A].\n- CMV is allowed here because the required minimum (900 m) is not below 800 m and no RVR is reported [OM-A 7.2.8 b].\n- The bank's \"yes, 500 x 2\" assumes night without saying so.","keys":[{"k":"Night yes: 1000 m","m":["1000","night"]},{"k":"Day no: 750 m","m":["750","day"]},{"k":"Factor 2 / 1.5","m":["factor"]},{"k":"Allowed: minimum not below 800 m, no RVR","m":["800","not reported","no rvr"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["yes 500 x 2","simply yes"],"why":"Bank assumes night; by day 500 x 1.5 = 750 m, below 900 m"}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-20","type":"num","q":"Met visibility 500 m, night, HI approach and runway lights, no RVR reported. CMV?","src":"OM-A 7.2.8; OM-A Table 7.2.A","ref":"OB13","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1000,"unit":"m","why":"500 x 2 = 1000 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-21","type":"num","q":"Met visibility 500 m, day, HI approach and runway lights, no RVR reported. CMV?","src":"OM-A 7.2.8; OM-A Table 7.2.A","ref":"OB13","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":750,"unit":"m","why":"500 x 1.5 = 750 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"What visibility do you need for a visual approach?","src":"OM-A 7.3.5","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**The higher of the NPA minima for that runway or 3200 m** [OM-A 7.3.5].\n- If the runway has **only a circling approach**, you need **5 km ground visibility**.\n- PAPI is required at night for a visual approach [OM-A 7.3 PAPI table].\n- You must keep the runway environment in sight throughout; if you lose it, go around [OM-A 7.3.7].","keys":[{"k":"Higher of NPA minima or 3200 m","m":["3200","3 2 km","3.2 km"]},{"k":"NPA minima","m":["npa","non precision"]},{"k":"Circling only: 5 km","m":["5 km","5km","5000","five km"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-22","type":"num","q":"Visual approach: minimum visibility (when NPA minima are lower)?","src":"OM-A 7.3.5","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":3200,"unit":"m","why":"The higher of NPA minima or 3200 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-21","type":"recall","q":"What are the stabilised approach criteria?","src":"OM-A 25","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Stable by 1000 ft on an instrument approach, or 500 ft on a visual approach** [OM-A 25]:\n- Speed **VAPP -5 to +10 kt**.\n- Rate of descent **1000 fpm or less**.\n- On an ILS, **within 1 dot**.\nIf not stable at the gate, go around.","keys":[{"k":"1000 ft instrument","m":["1000 ft","1000ft","1000 feet"]},{"k":"500 ft visual","m":["500 ft","500ft","500 feet"]},{"k":"VAPP -5/+10","m":["vapp","10 kt","10kt","plus 10","5 to 10"]},{"k":"1000 fpm max","m":["fpm","feet per minute","rate of descent"]},{"k":"Within 1 dot","m":["1 dot","one dot"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"Stabilised approach speed tolerance in your OM-A:","src":"OM-A 25","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["VAPP -5 to +5 kt","VAPP -5 to +10 kt","VAPP 0 to +20 kt","VREF to VREF+20 kt"],"a":1,"why":"VAPP -5 to +10 kt.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-22","type":"recall","q":"Describe runway centreline, edge, TDZ and exit taxiway lighting colour coding.","src":"OM-A 45.1.17","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"[OM-A 45.1.17]\n- **Runway centreline:** coded over the last **900 m**: alternate **red and white for 600 m**, then **red for the last 300 m**.\n- **Edge lights:** last **600 m yellow**.\n- **TDZ lights:** in the first **900 m**.\n- **Exit taxiway centreline:** alternate **green and yellow** up to the edge of the ILS critical or sensitive area.","keys":[{"k":"Centreline coded last 900 m","m":["900"]},{"k":"Red/white alternating 600 m","m":["red and white","red white","alternate"]},{"k":"Last 300 m red","m":["300"]},{"k":"Edge last 600 m yellow","m":["yellow","amber"]},{"k":"Exit green/yellow to ILS area","m":["green","critical","sensitive"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-order-2","type":"order","q":"Walking a centreline light row from mid-runway to the stop end, order the colours.","src":"OM-A 45.1.17","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","prio":2,"items":["White","Alternate red and white (from 900 m to go)","Red (last 300 m)"],"why":"Centreline lights are coded over the last 900 m: alternate red/white for 600 m, then red for the last 300 m.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-23","type":"num","q":"Runway edge lights: how many metres at the end are yellow?","src":"OM-A 45.1.17","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","prio":2,"ans":600,"unit":"m","why":"Last 600 m yellow.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw the runway lighting colour coding (centreline, edge, TDZ, exit taxiway).","src":"OM-A 45.1.17","ref":"Key facts: All-weather operations","prio":3,"a":"A runway plan showing:\n- **TDZ lights** in the first 900 m.\n- **Centreline** white, then alternate red/white from 900 m to go to 300 m to go, then all red for the last 300 m.\n- **Edge lights** white, last 600 m yellow.\n- **Exit taxiway centreline** alternate green/yellow from the runway to the edge of the ILS critical/sensitive area, then green.","checklist":["TDZ lights in first 900 m","Centreline coded only in the last 900 m","Red/white alternate for 600 m","Last 300 m centreline red","Edge lights last 600 m yellow","Exit taxiway green/yellow to the ILS critical/sensitive area edge"],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-23","type":"recall","q":"What are the DH and RVR limits for CAT IIIA, IIIB and IIIC (old classification), and what does your OM-A use now?","src":"OM-A Ch 0; OM-A 7.3.6.2; OM-A 45.1.1","ref":"NR27","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Old (pre-2018) classification:**\n- **IIIA:** DH below 100 ft or no DH, RVR 175 m or more.\n- **IIIB:** DH below 50 ft or no DH, RVR below 175 m but not below 50 m.\n- **IIIC:** no DH and no RVR limit (not used).\nICAO now uses a single \"CAT III\" [OM-A Ch 0].\n**OM-A** [7.3.6.2, 45.1.1]: CAT III fail-passive DH below 100 ft, RVR 175 m; CAT III fail-operational (IIIB) DH below 50 ft or no DH, RVR **50 m** for the B737 (75 m for the A320).","keys":[{"k":"IIIA below 100 ft, 175 m","m":["175"]},{"k":"IIIB below 50 ft","m":["below 50","less than 50","50 ft"]},{"k":"IIIB down to 50 m","m":["50 m","50m","50 metre","50 meter"]},{"k":"IIIC no DH no RVR","m":["no rvr","no limit","iiic","cat 3c"]},{"k":"Now a single CAT III","m":["single","one cat"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-24","type":"recall","q":"What are the aircraft, crew and aerodrome requirements for a CAT IIIB (fail-operational) approach?","src":"OM-A 7.3.6.2; OM-A 45.1.1; OM-A 7.3.7; OM-A 45.2.4.2","ref":"NR27","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Aircraft:** certified **fail-operational** autoland with **rollout guidance**; LVO MEL items (wipers, window heat, anti-skid, reversers); DH set on the RA.\n- **Crew:** both pilots CAT III qualified and current (6 approaches incl. 3 sim landings in 6 months); **captain in the left seat**.\n- **Aerodrome:** CAT III ILS and lighting; **LVP** in force; stop bars; **ATC clearance** for CAT III.\n- **RVR:** all three (TDZ, mid, rollout) reported, each at or above minimum.\n- **Visual ref:** DH below 50 ft one centreline light; no DH none. **AH 200 ft** [OM-A 45.2.4.2].","keys":[{"k":"Fail-operational with rollout","m":["fail operational","fail op","rollout"]},{"k":"Both crew qualified and current","m":["qualified","current"]},{"k":"LVP","m":["lvp","low visibility procedure"]},{"k":"All three RVRs","m":["all three","three rvr","3 rvr"]},{"k":"Radio altimeter DH","m":["radio alt","rad alt","radio altimeter"]},{"k":"Alert height 200 ft","m":["alert height","200"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-25","type":"recall","q":"What are the B737 autoland limitations (flaps, engines, glideslope, wind)?","src":"FCOM L.10.6; FCOM L.10.7","ref":"NR27","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"[FCOM L, Autopilot/Flight Director System]\n- Autoland only with **flaps 30 or 40** and **both engines operative**.\n- Exception, fail-operational aircraft under FAA rules: **flaps 30 with one engine operative**.\n- **Glideslope 2.5° to 3.25°**.\n- **Wind (FAA rules):** headwind **25 kt**; crosswind **20 kt** fail-passive or **25 kt** fail-operational; tailwind **15 kt**.","keys":[{"k":"Flaps 30 or 40","m":["30 or 40","flaps 30","flap 30"]},{"k":"Both engines","m":["both engines","two engines"]},{"k":"GS 2.5 to 3.25 deg","m":["2 5","3 25","325","two and a half"]},{"k":"Headwind 25 kt","m":["headwind 25","25 kt head","25 knots head"]},{"k":"Crosswind 20 / 25 kt","m":["crosswind 20","20 kt cross","20 knots"]},{"k":"Tailwind 15 kt","m":["tailwind 15","15 kt tail","15 knots","15 kt"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-24","type":"num","q":"Autoland maximum tailwind (FAA rules)?","src":"FCOM L.10.7","ref":"NR27","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":15,"unit":"kt","why":"Headwind 25, crosswind 20 (fail-passive) or 25 (fail-op), tailwind 15 kt.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-25","type":"num","q":"Autoland maximum crosswind for a fail-passive aircraft (FAA rules)?","src":"FCOM L.10.7","ref":"NR27","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":20,"unit":"kt","why":"20 kt fail-passive, 25 kt fail-operational.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-26","type":"num","q":"Autoland maximum headwind (FAA rules)?","src":"FCOM L.10.7","ref":"NR27","prio":2,"ans":25,"unit":"kt","why":"Headwind 25 kt.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"Autoland glideslope angle limits on the 737:","src":"FCOM L.10.7","ref":"NR27","prio":2,"o":["2.0° to 3.0°","2.5° to 3.25°","2.5° to 3.5°","3.0° only"],"a":1,"why":"Glideslope 2.5° to 3.25°.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-26","type":"recall","q":"Define fail-passive and fail-operational.","src":"FCTM 5.11; OM-A 7.3.6.2","ref":"NR59","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Fail-operational:** \"an AFDS capable of completing an ILS approach, autoland, and rollout following the failure of any single system component after passing alert height\" [FCTM 5.11]. FMA shows LAND 3 or LAND 2 with ROLLOUT armed.\n- **Fail-passive:** \"an AFDS which in the event of a failure, causes no significant deviation of airplane flight path or attitude. A DA(H) is used as approach minimums\" [FCTM 5.11]. The landing is not completed automatically after a failure: the pilot takes over or goes around.\n- **Minima:** fail-passive CAT III DH below 100 ft, RVR 175 m; fail-operational DH below 50 ft or none, RVR 50 m [OM-A 7.3.6.2].","keys":[{"k":"Fail-op completes approach, autoland and rollout","m":["complet","rollout","continue"]},{"k":"After single failure below AH","m":["single","alert height"]},{"k":"Fail-passive: no significant deviation","m":["no significant deviation","significant deviation","no deviation"]},{"k":"Fail-passive uses DA(H)","m":["da h","decision height","decision alt"]},{"k":"Pilot takes over or goes around","m":["take over","takes over","go around"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-27","type":"recall","q":"On a fail-operational 737, what do LAND 3, LAND 2 and NO AUTOLAND mean?","src":"FCOM 4.20; FCTM 5.17","ref":"NR59","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"[FCOM 4.20]\n- **LAND 3:** two autopilots, three inertial sources and their sensors normal. A single fault cannot prevent an automatic landing.\n- **LAND 2:** a failure above AH has reduced redundancy, but it can still autoland and roll out; it is now **fail-passive**.\n- **NO AUTOLAND:** FLARE and ROLLOUT will not arm.\n- A single failure **below AH** leaves LAND 3 showing; the failure shows only below 40 kt after the autopilots disengage.\n- If it degrades above AH, do not continue below the higher minima unless visual [FCTM 5.17].","keys":[{"k":"LAND 3: 2 AP, 3 inertial sources","m":["three inertial","3 inertial","two autopilot","2 autopilot"]},{"k":"LAND 2: reduced redundancy, fail-passive","m":["reduced","fail passive"]},{"k":"NO AUTOLAND: FLARE/ROLLOUT won't arm","m":["flare","will not arm","wont arm"]},{"k":"Failure below AH: still LAND 3","m":["below alert","below ah","still land 3"]},{"k":"Degraded above AH: higher minima","m":["higher minima"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-match-7","type":"match","q":"Match the fail-operational 737 annunciation to its meaning.","src":"FCOM 4.20","ref":"NR59","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["LAND 3","Two A/Ps, three inertial sources normal: a single fault cannot prevent autoland"],["LAND 2","Reduced redundancy; still autolands and rolls out (fail-passive)"],["NO AUTOLAND","FLARE and ROLLOUT will not arm"],["No LAND 3/LAND 2 shown at all","Fail-passive aircraft"]],"why":"FCOM 4.20 status annunciations.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-28","type":"recall","q":"How does the 737 dual autopilot set up for an autoland approach, and what is the single-channel minimum use height?","src":"FCOM 4.20; FCOM 4.20.4; FCOM L","ref":"NR59","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Dual A/P setup** [FCOM 4.20]:\n- The **second VHF NAV must be tuned to the ILS** and its A/P engaged in CMD **before 800 ft RA**.\n- The second A/P then engages automatically **after LOC and G/S capture, below 1500 ft RA**.\n- With one RA inoperative the autopilot disengages 2 s after LOC and G/S capture, so dual-A/P CAT II/III needs both RAs [FCOM 4.20.4].\n**Single-channel A/P minimum use height** (EASA certification): **111 ft AGL** [FCOM L].","keys":[{"k":"Second NAV on ILS","m":["second nav","both nav","vhf nav"]},{"k":"CMD before 800 ft RA","m":["800"]},{"k":"Engages after LOC and GS capture","m":["capture"]},{"k":"Below 1500 ft RA","m":["1500"]},{"k":"Single channel 111 ft","m":["111"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-27","type":"num","q":"Second A/P must be in CMD before what RA height for a dual-channel approach?","src":"FCOM 4.20","ref":"NR59","prio":2,"ans":800,"unit":"ft","why":"Before 800 ft RA; it engages after LOC/GS capture below 1500 ft RA.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-num-28","type":"num","q":"Single-channel A/P minimum use height (EASA certification)?","src":"FCOM L","ref":"NR59","prio":3,"ans":111,"unit":"ft","why":"111 ft AGL.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-29","type":"recall","q":"Can the 737 autoland with one engine inoperative?","src":"FCOM L; FCTM 5.14; FCOM 4.20","ref":"NR59","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Only a fail-operational aircraft, under FAA rules, with flaps 30, and not to the lowest minima.**\n- AFM limitation: autoland with flaps 30 or 40 and **both engines** operative. Exception marked \"Airplanes operating under FAA Rules\": **flaps 30 with one engine operative** [FCOM L].\n- FCTM: a fail-operational approach with one engine out can autoland with flaps 30 only, performance permitting [FCTM 5.14].\n- If the APU is the second electrical source, LAND 3 can still show, but an **approach and autoland to a DH below 50 ft is prohibited** [FCOM 4.20].\n- So a fail-passive aircraft has no engine-out autoland.","keys":[{"k":"Fail-operational only","m":["fail operational","fail op"]},{"k":"FAA rules","m":["faa"]},{"k":"Flaps 30","m":["flaps 30","flap 30"]},{"k":"No DH below 50 ft","m":["below 50","50 ft","prohibited"]},{"k":"Fail-passive: no","m":["fail passive"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-30","type":"recall","q":"TRAP: Can every B737 in your fleet use the fail-operational CAT III minima (RVR 50 m, DH below 50 ft)?","src":"OM-A 7.3.6.2; OM-A 45.1.1; FCOM 4.20; FCOM L.10.6, L.10.7","ref":"Flag 3: B737 CAT III status","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"**No, only the fail-operational aircraft.** OM-A 7.3.6.2 and 45.1.1 list B737 fail-operational minima, but the fleet has both systems [FCOM 4.20].\n- **Fail-operational aircraft** show LAND 3 / LAND 2 / NO AUTOLAND and may use RVR 50 m, DH below 50 ft or no DH.\n- **Fail-passive aircraft** have no such annunciations and are limited to **DH below 100 ft, RVR 175 m**.\n- Autoland limits for both: flaps 30 or 40, both engines, GS 2.5° to 3.25° [FCOM L.10.6, L.10.7].","keys":[{"k":"Only fail-operational aircraft","m":["only fail operational","fail operational","fail op"]},{"k":"LAND 3 annunciation tells you","m":["land 3","annunciation"]},{"k":"Fail-passive: DH below 100, 175 m","m":["175"]},{"k":"Fleet has both","m":["both","mixed"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["all 737","every 737","whole fleet"],"why":"Fail-passive aircraft are limited to DH below 100 ft, RVR 175 m"}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"In a crosswind autoland on a fail-passive 737, what happens at touchdown?","src":"FCTM 5.17","ref":"NR59","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["It decrabs automatically and tracks the centreline","It touches down in a crab; disengage the A/P immediately after touchdown","It goes around automatically","ROLLOUT engages"],"a":1,"why":"Fail-passive touches down in a crab and the autopilot must be disengaged immediately after touchdown.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-31","type":"recall","q":"Delhi CAT I: visibility drops to 300 m while you are on the LOC intercept heading. What do you do? And if you are already established on the GS?","src":"OM-A 7.2.5; OM-A 45.2.8.3; OM-A 7.1.1","ref":"S22","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**On the intercept heading** you are normally above 1000 ft and outside the FAP, so the **approach ban** stops you continuing the CAT I approach [OM-A 7.2.5]. Options:\n- hold for improvement;\n- upgrade to CAT II/III if aircraft, both crew, LVP and ATC allow, decided **above 1000 ft** [OM-A 45.2.8.3];\n- divert.\n**Established on the GS**, past the FAP or below 1000 ft: you may continue to DA; land only with the required visual reference [OM-A 7.2.5 c, d].\nBelow 800 m minima are RVR, and **TDZ RVR is controlling** [OM-A 7.1.1, 7.2.5 e].","keys":[{"k":"Approach ban","m":["approach ban","ban"]},{"k":"Hold / upgrade / divert","m":["hold","divert","upgrade"]},{"k":"Upgrade decided above 1000 ft","m":["above 1000","1000"]},{"k":"On GS past FAP: continue to DA","m":["continue","decision alt"]},{"k":"Land only if visual","m":["visual"]},{"k":"TDZ RVR controlling","m":["tdz","touchdown"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-32","type":"recall","q":"RVR is 125/125/125 and you can see 3 lights. Can you take off? What if TDZ RVR is not reported?","src":"OM-A 7.6; OM-A Table 7.5.A; OM-A 45.1.1","ref":"S23","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**125/125/125: yes, if everything else is in place.** AIX LVTO goes down to 125 m [OM-A 7.6]. The 125 m row needs:\n- HI edge lights and centreline lights at **15 m spacing or less**, and relevant RVR [Table 7.5.A];\n- LVP in force; both pilots LVTO trained and current;\n- a **take-off alternate** within 60 min at OEI cruise speed, weather at or above CAT I or applicable approach minima; ATC informed [OM-A 45.1.1].\n**TDZ not reported: no.** AIX crews may not assess TDZ RVR themselves [Table 7.5.A Note 1], and below 200 m all three RVRs are required.","keys":[{"k":"Yes at 125 m","m":["yes","125"]},{"k":"CL lights 15 m spacing","m":["15 m","15m","15 metre","15 meter"]},{"k":"LVP and trained crew","m":["lvp","trained"]},{"k":"Take-off alternate","m":["alternate"]},{"k":"TDZ not reported: no","m":["not authoris","not authoriz","cannot assess","can't assess","not allowed"]},{"k":"Below 200 m all three","m":["all three","200"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-33","type":"recall","q":"TRAP: TDZ RVR isn't reported for your LVTO. Can the crew assess it themselves, as EASA allows?","src":"OM-A Table 7.5.A Note 1","ref":"S23","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"**No, not in AIX.** EASA allows a pilot assessment when TDZ RVR isn't reported, but **AIX crews are not authorised to assess TDZ RVR** [OM-A Table 7.5.A Note 1].\n- Below 200 m, all three RVRs must be reported and each must meet the minimum.\n- So with TDZ not reported you do not take off. Give the AIX answer, not the EASA one.","keys":[{"k":"No pilot assessment in AIX","m":["not authoris","not authoriz","cannot","not allowed","not permitted"]},{"k":"EASA allows it, AIX doesn't","m":["easa"]},{"k":"Table 7.5.A Note 1","m":["note 1","7 5 a"]},{"k":"Below 200 m all three","m":["all three","200"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["pilot assess","crew assess","count the lights"],"why":"EASA allows it; AIX OM-A Table 7.5.A Note 1 does not"}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-34","type":"recall","q":"LVTO: TDZ RVR is 200 m and the others are not reported. Can you go? And below 200 m?","src":"OM-A Table 7.5.A; OM-A 45.1.1; OM-A 7.6","ref":"S24","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**TDZ 200 m, others not reported: permitted.** At 200 m you need **edge plus centreline lights** [Table 7.5.A], plus all the LVTO conditions (LVP, trained crew, take-off alternate) because RVR is below 400 m [OM-A 45.1.1].\n**Below 200 m:** all three RVRs must be reported and each must meet the minimum. Example: 300/150/NR is treated as 150 m, and all three are needed [OM-A 7.6]. With mid and end not reported you cannot go.","keys":[{"k":"200 m TDZ only: yes","m":["yes","permitted"]},{"k":"Edge and CL lights","m":["edge","centreline","centerline"]},{"k":"LVTO conditions","m":["lvp","trained","alternate"]},{"k":"Below 200 m all three required","m":["all three","three rvr"]},{"k":"Lowest governs","m":["lowest","150"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-35","type":"recall","q":"After pushback the visibility drops to 500 m. What now?","src":"OM-A 45.1.1.4.2; OM-A 7.6; OM-A 45.1.1","ref":"S25","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Look at the RVR.** With visibility under 800 m, the 550 m RVR criterion applies [OM-A 45.1.1.4.2].\n- **RVR 550 m or more:** you may depart without LVP. If conditions are below landing minima and no take-off alternate was filed, ask ATC and OCC to file one, provided weather and NOTAMs meet the criteria [OM-A 7.6].\n- **RVR below 550 m:** no take-off unless LVP is in force. Below 400 m it is an LVTO: trained crew, take-off alternate, Table 7.5.A lighting.\n- **Re-check** performance and thrust, and brief a low-visibility RTO.","keys":[{"k":"Check RVR","m":["rvr"]},{"k":"550 m criterion","m":["550"]},{"k":"Below 550 needs LVP","m":["lvp","low visibility procedure"]},{"k":"Take-off alternate via ATC/OCC","m":["alternate","occ"]},{"k":"Below 400 m is LVTO","m":["400","lvto"]},{"k":"Re-brief RTO / performance","m":["rto","reject","performance"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-36","type":"recall","q":"Delhi RVR is 500 m and your co-pilot is not CAT II/III qualified. Can you fly the approach?","src":"OM-A 45.1.1.4.1; OM-A 45.2.1; OM-A 7.2.5","ref":"S26","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**No.** 500 m is below the CAT I minimum of **550 m**, so a CAT II approach (300 m) is needed.\n- CAT II requires **both pilots qualified and current** [OM-A 45.1.1.4.1, 45.2.1]. If both are not qualified, you are limited to CAT I minima.\n- The **approach ban** stops you commencing [OM-A 7.2.5].\n- **Options:** hold for improvement if fuel allows, or divert.","keys":[{"k":"No","m":["cannot","can't","not permitted","unable"]},{"k":"Below CAT I 550 m","m":["550"]},{"k":"Both crew must be qualified","m":["both"]},{"k":"Approach ban","m":["approach ban","ban"]},{"k":"Hold or divert","m":["hold","divert"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-37","type":"recall","q":"CAT III conditions at departure and you get an engine fire after take-off. Return or go to the take-off alternate?","src":"OM-A 7.6; OM-A 45.1.1; FCOM L.10.7; FCOM 4.20.25; OM-A 17.1.2.3","ref":"S27","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Normally the take-off alternate: that is why it exists** [OM-A 7.6, 45.1.1].\n- **Engine-out autoland:** limited to flaps 30 or 40 with both engines. Only fail-operational aircraft under FAA rules may autoland on one engine (flaps 30) [FCOM L.10.7], and even then not to a DH below 50 ft [FCOM 4.20.25].\n- So if departure weather is below what you can fly on one engine, your minima there are not met: go to the take-off alternate (within 60 min at OEI speed, weather at or above CAT I).\n- **If the fire persists or it is grave:** MAYDAY; the PIC may land wherever is safest [OM-A 17.1.2.3].","keys":[{"k":"Take-off alternate","m":["alternate"]},{"k":"No engine-out autoland (fail-passive)","m":["fail passive","both engines","one engine"]},{"k":"Fail-op: not below DH 50 ft","m":["below 50","50 ft"]},{"k":"Within 60 min OEI","m":["60 min","1 hour","one hour","oei"]},{"k":"Grave: MAYDAY, land anywhere safe","m":["mayday","emergency","safest"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-38","type":"recall","q":"All your take-off alternates within 1 hour are deteriorating. What do you do?","src":"OM-A 7.6; OM-A 45.1.1; OM-A 4.3.2.1","ref":"S28","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Don't depart until the requirement is met.** A take-off alternate is required when departure weather is below company landing minima; it must be within **1 h at OEI cruise speed** (ISA, still air, actual take-off mass), with actual and forecast weather at or above minima [OM-A 7.6, 45.1.1].\n- Wait for departure weather to rise to landing minima (then none is needed).\n- Have OCC find another qualifying alternate.\n- Only an EDTO-approved flight may use the first alternate within its maximum diversion time [OM-A 4.3.2.1].\n- Any weather validity window is still to be confirmed from CAR Sec 8 Series C Part I.","keys":[{"k":"Don't depart","m":["not depart","dont depart","do not go","don't go","wait"]},{"k":"1 h OEI","m":["1 hour","one hour","60 min","oei"]},{"k":"Wait for departure weather","m":["departure weather","landing minima","improve"]},{"k":"OCC finds another","m":["occ","another alternate"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-39","type":"recall","q":"You are given three RVRs. When can you fly a CAT II approach? Try 350/150/100 and 300/200/NR.","src":"OM-A 7.3.6.3; OM-A 7.2.5 e; OM-A 45.1.5.2","ref":"S29","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**CAT II needs TDZ 300 m, mid 125 m, rollout 125 m.** TDZ and mid must be reported, and every reported value is controlling [OM-A 7.3.6.3, 7.2.5 e].\n- **350/150/100:** rollout below 125 m: **no**.\n- **300/200/NR:** **yes**, if the rest is in place.\n- Also: DH set by **radio altimeter**, not lower than the highest of AFM, aid, OCH, crew DH or 100 ft [OM-A 45.1.5.2]; LVP in force; both crew qualified.","keys":[{"k":"TDZ 300 m","m":["300"]},{"k":"Mid and rollout 125 m","m":["125"]},{"k":"Every reported value controls","m":["every reported","all reported","controlling"]},{"k":"350/150/100: no","m":["rollout below","rollout is below","100 is below","no go"]},{"k":"300/200/NR: yes","m":["yes","can fly","acceptable"]},{"k":"RA DH, LVP, crew","m":["radio alt","lvp","qualified"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"CAT II, RVR 350/150/100. Can you start the approach?","src":"OM-A 7.3.6.3; OM-A 7.2.5 e","ref":"S29","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Yes: TDZ is above 300 m","Yes: only TDZ and mid count","No: rollout is below 125 m and every reported RVR is controlling","No: mid is below 200 m"],"a":2,"why":"Every reported value is controlling; rollout 100 m is below the 125 m minimum.","topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-40","type":"recall","q":"Only the PAPI is visible at minima: can you continue? And can you fly an ILS with PAPI unserviceable?","src":"OM-A 7.3.7; OM-A 7.3 PAPI table","ref":"S30","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**PAPI only at minima:** for CAT I, APV and NPA, any **one listed element** is acceptable, and PAPI is on the list [OM-A 7.3.7]. So legally yes. Make sure you can assess position and rate, and go around if the runway environment doesn't follow.\n- OM-A 7.3.7 adds no \"below 100 ft\" condition.\n- For **CAT II**, PAPI is **not enough**: 3 consecutive lights and a lateral element.\n**PAPI U/S on an ILS:** you can fly it. For CAT I PAPI is only **recommended**; it is **required at night for APV/NPA** and for a **visual approach** [OM-A 7.3 PAPI table].","keys":[{"k":"Yes, PAPI is a listed element","m":["yes","listed","one element"]},{"k":"Go around if runway doesn't follow","m":["go around"]},{"k":"Not enough for CAT II","m":["cat ii","cat 2","lateral"]},{"k":"ILS OK with PAPI U/S","m":["recommended","can fly"]},{"k":"Required at night NPA/visual","m":["night"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-41","type":"recall","q":"1000 ft of the runway is closed (displaced threshold). Where do you start the descent?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S31","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Read the NOTAM:** it normally publishes a temporarily displaced threshold. The ILS GS is often unusable (it still aims at the original touchdown point), and PAPI may be relocated or withdrawn.\n- **Shift the profile:** the aim point moves **1000 ft (about 0.16 NM)** down the runway, so on a 3° path every height and distance check moves the same way: start final descent about 0.16 NM later.\n- **Recompute landing distance** on the reduced LDA.\n- **Brief** which approach is still valid (often LOC-only or RNP).","keys":[{"k":"Read NOTAM","m":["notam"]},{"k":"GS unusable","m":["glideslope","glide slope","glidepath","glide path"]},{"k":"Shift 0.16 NM","m":["0 16","016","16 nm","later"]},{"k":"Recompute landing distance","m":["landing distance","lda"]},{"k":"LOC-only or RNP","m":["loc","rnp"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-42","type":"recall","q":"Chennai VOR 07, dual RA failure, no AP/FD, RVR 550 m. Can you fly the CDFA?","src":"OM-A 7.3.1; OM-A Table 7.3.C; OM-A 7.2.5; OM-A 28.2; OM-A 7.2.7","ref":"S32","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**No, the RVR is too low.** An NPA minimum is **750 m**, more if the procedure doesn't meet 7.3.3 criteria [OM-A 7.3.1, Table 7.3.C]. 550 m is below that, so the **approach ban** applies [OM-A 7.2.5].\nThe rest could be flown:\n- A CDFA can be flown **manually without AP/FD**, as a 2D CDFA with a calculated rate of descent [OM-A 28.2].\n- RA failure doesn't change NPA minima, which are barometric; it removes RA callouts and degrades EGPWS.\n- Not flying CDFA adds 400 m [OM-A 7.2.7].","keys":[{"k":"No: RVR too low","m":["too low","below minim","cannot","can't"]},{"k":"NPA minimum 750 m","m":["750"]},{"k":"Approach ban","m":["approach ban","ban"]},{"k":"CDFA manual 2D OK","m":["manual","2d cdfa","calculated"]},{"k":"RA doesn't change baro minima","m":["barometric","baro"]},{"k":"No CDFA +400 m","m":["400"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-43","type":"recall","q":"You lose sight of the runway during a visual circuit. What do you do?","src":"OM-A 7.3.7; OM-A Ch 6; OM-A 7.4","ref":"S33","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Go around immediately.** Climb straight ahead or towards the runway to a safe altitude (MSA), and tell ATC.\n- A visual approach requires the runway environment to stay in sight throughout [OM-A 7.3.7]; obstacle clearance is the commander's responsibility [OM-A Ch 6].\n- Then follow ATC or the missed approach of the instrument approach for that runway.\n- **If it were a circling approach (ICAO):** climbing turn towards the landing runway, then the missed approach for the instrument approach flown.\n- **AIX:** circling is not authorised [OM-A 7.4].","keys":[{"k":"Go around immediately","m":["go around","goaround","missed"]},{"k":"Climb to MSA / safe altitude","m":["msa","safe altitude"]},{"k":"Tell ATC","m":["atc"]},{"k":"Circling: turn towards runway","m":["towards the runway","toward the runway","towards runway","climbing turn"]},{"k":"AIX: no circling","m":["not authoris","not authoriz","no circling"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-recall-44","type":"recall","q":"Radio altimeter fails on short final during a CAT II approach. What do you do?","src":"OM-A 45.1.1.4.1; OM-A 45.2.8.2; OM-A 45.2.8.3; FCOM 4.20.4","ref":"S34","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Go around.** CAT II requires the DH set by radio altimeter [OM-A 45.1.1.4.1]. Any failure between 1000 ft and DH means a go-around unless visual reference is already established (\"no switching below 1000 ft\") [OM-A 45.2.8.2].\n- **Next approach:** re-plan for CAT I minima if the RVR allows; the downgrade decision is made above 1000 ft [OM-A 45.2.8.3].\n- **737 detail:** two independent RAs feed their own FCCs; with one RA inoperative the autopilot disengages **2 seconds after LOC and G/S capture** [FCOM 4.20.4], so dual-A/P CAT II/III needs both.","keys":[{"k":"Go around","m":["go around","goaround","missed"]},{"k":"CAT II DH by RA","m":["radio alt","rad alt","radio altimeter"]},{"k":"No switching below 1000 ft","m":["1000"]},{"k":"Re-plan CAT I","m":["cat i","cat 1","cat one"]},{"k":"AP disengages 2 s after capture","m":["2 sec","two sec","disengage"]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"Tell me about CAT III. What is fail-passive and fail-operational?\"","src":"FCTM 5.11; FCTM 5.17; OM-A 45.2.4.2; OM-A 7.3.6.2; FCOM L; FCOM 4.20","ref":"NR59; NR27","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"Define fail-operational.","a":"AFDS that can complete the approach, autoland and rollout after any single failure below alert height.","keys":[{"k":"Completes autoland and rollout","m":["rollout","complet"]},{"k":"Single failure","m":["single"]}]},{"q":"What is alert height and what is it on your aircraft?","a":"Height above which a specified failure means go-around; 200 ft on the B737.","keys":[{"k":"Failure above it: go around","m":["go around","above which","missed"]},{"k":"200 ft","m":["200"]}]},{"q":"You see LAND 2 at 600 ft. Now?","a":"Redundancy reduced: fail-passive. Don't continue below the higher (fail-passive) minima unless visual.","keys":[{"k":"Fail-passive","m":["fail passive"]},{"k":"Higher minima","m":["higher minima","175"]}]},{"q":"What are the minima for each?","a":"Fail-passive DH below 100 ft, RVR 175 m; fail-operational DH below 50 ft or none, RVR 50 m.","keys":[{"k":"175 m","m":["175"]},{"k":"50 m","m":["50 m","50m","50 metre","50 meter"]}]},{"q":"An engine fails on the approach. Can you still autoland?","a":"Only fail-operational under FAA rules, flaps 30, and not to a DH below 50 ft.","keys":[{"k":"Flaps 30","m":["flaps 30","flap 30"]},{"k":"FAA","m":["faa"]},{"k":"Not below DH 50","m":["below 50","50 ft"]}]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"You are cleared for the CAT I ILS at Delhi and RVR starts dropping.\"","src":"OM-A 7.2.5; OM-A 45.2.8.3; OM-A 45.1.1.4.1","ref":"S22; S26; OB14","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"When may you not start the approach?","a":"If reported RVR/visibility is below minima: check before the IAF.","keys":[{"k":"Below minima","m":["below minima","below the minimum"]},{"k":"Before IAF","m":["iaf","commence"]}]},{"q":"RVR drops to 300 m on the intercept heading. Continue?","a":"No: approach ban. Hold, upgrade to CAT II/III (decided above 1000 ft) or divert.","keys":[{"k":"Approach ban","m":["ban"]},{"k":"Hold/upgrade/divert","m":["hold","upgrade","divert"]}]},{"q":"What if you were already past the FAP?","a":"Continue to DA; land only with the required visual reference.","keys":[{"k":"Continue to DA","m":["continue","decision alt"]},{"k":"Visual reference","m":["visual"]}]},{"q":"Which RVR controls?","a":"TDZ always; mid and rollout too if reported.","keys":[{"k":"TDZ","m":["tdz","touchdown"]},{"k":"Others if reported","m":["reported"]}]},{"q":"Your F/O is not CAT II qualified and RVR is 500 m. Can you upgrade?","a":"No: CAT II needs both crew qualified; 500 m is below CAT I 550 m, so hold or divert.","keys":[{"k":"Both crew","m":["both"]},{"k":"550","m":["550"]},{"k":"Hold or divert","m":["hold","divert"]}]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t5-chain-3","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: \"Low visibility take-off. RVR is 150 m at Delhi.\"","src":"OM-A 7.6; OM-A Table 7.5.A; OM-A 45.1.1","ref":"S23; S24; OB12","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What is an LVTO and what is your company minimum?","a":"Take-off with RVR below 400 m; AIX minimum 125 m.","keys":[{"k":"Below 400 m","m":["400"]},{"k":"125 m","m":["125"]}]},{"q":"What lighting do you need at 150 m?","a":"Edge and centreline lights, plus relevant RVR (125 m needs HI edge and CL at 15 m or less).","keys":[{"k":"Edge and CL","m":["edge","centreline","centerline"]},{"k":"Relevant RVR","m":["rvr"]}]},{"q":"TDZ RVR stops being reported. Can you assess it yourself?","a":"No: AIX crews may not assess TDZ RVR; below 200 m all three are required.","keys":[{"k":"No","m":["not","cannot","can't"]},{"k":"All three below 200","m":["all three","200"]}]},{"q":"What else must be in place?","a":"LVP in force, trained crew, no restricted captain, take-off alternate within 1 h at OEI speed.","keys":[{"k":"LVP","m":["lvp"]},{"k":"Take-off alternate","m":["alternate"]},{"k":"Trained crew","m":["trained","qualified"]}]}],"topic":5},{"id":"t6-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"Compare MEA, MOCA, route MORA and grid MORA.","src":"Dispatch Training Tech Bulletin Nav 1; OM-A Ch 0","ref":"Key facts: Minimum flight altitudes (MEA, MOCA, MORA, MSA); OB19","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **MEA** (State): obstacle clearance **plus nav signal coverage**, across the airway width; 2000 ft mountainous, 1000 ft elsewhere. Plain figure.\n- **MOCA** (State): **obstacle clearance only**, airway width, same 2000/1000 ft; no nav coverage (USA: only within 22 NM of a VOR). Figure with a **T**.\n- **Route MORA** (Jeppesen): **10 NM either side** of the centreline; 2000 ft above terrain over 5000 ft, 1000 ft at 5000 ft or lower. Figure with an **a**.\n- **Grid MORA**: the whole lat/long square; large figure, **green below 10,000 ft, magenta at or above**.\n- None but the MEA guarantees nav signal coverage.","keys":[{"k":"MEA includes nav signal","m":["signal","reception","navigation coverage","nav coverage"]},{"k":"MOCA obstacle only","m":["obstacle clearance only","only obstacle","obstruction clearance only","obstacle only"]},{"k":"MORA 10 NM each side","m":["10 nm","10nm","10 nautical","ten nm","ten nautical"]},{"k":"MOCA marked T","m":["with a t"," t ","t suffix","letter t"]},{"k":"MORA marked a","m":["with an a","with a a","a suffix","letter a","small a"]},{"k":"grid square","m":["grid square","square","lat long","latitude"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match each minimum altitude to who sets it.","src":"Dispatch Training Tech Bulletin Nav 1","ref":"Key facts: Minimum flight altitudes (MEA, MOCA, MORA, MSA)","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["MEA","Published by the State (CAA)"],["MOCA","Published by the State (CAA)"],["Route MORA","Derived by Jeppesen"],["Grid MORA","Published by the CAA, or derived by Jeppesen"]],"why":"MEA and MOCA are State-published; route MORA is a Jeppesen product; grid MORA can be either.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match each minimum altitude to its chart symbol.","src":"Dispatch Training Tech Bulletin Nav 1; Oral bank p.112","ref":"Key facts: Minimum flight altitudes (MEA, MOCA, MORA, MSA); OB20","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["MEA","Plain figure"],["MOCA","Figure with a T"],["Route MORA","Figure with an a"],["Grid MORA","Large figure in the lat/long square"]],"why":"e.g. 4300 / 3900T for MEA over MOCA, 2600a for a route MORA.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-3","type":"match","q":"Match each minimum altitude to the width it protects.","src":"Dispatch Training Tech Bulletin Nav 1; OM-A Ch 0","ref":"Key facts: Minimum flight altitudes (MEA, MOCA, MORA, MSA)","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["MEA","Airway width"],["MOCA","Airway width (whole segment)"],["Route MORA","10 NM either side of the centreline, and 10 NM beyond the fixes"],["Grid MORA","The whole grid square"],["MSA","25 NM circle round the navaid"]],"why":"Route MORA extends 10 NM beyond the radio fixes too (OM-A Ch 0).","topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-4","type":"match","q":"Match each altitude to the obstacle clearance it gives.","src":"Dispatch Training Tech Bulletin Nav 1; OM-A Ch 0; OM-A 6","ref":"Key facts: Minimum flight altitudes (MEA, MOCA, MORA, MSA)","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["MEA / MOCA","2000 ft mountainous, 1000 ft elsewhere"],["Route MORA","2000 ft if terrain above 5000 ft, 1000 ft if 5000 ft or lower"],["MSA","1000 ft within 25 NM"],["AIX minimum where nothing is published","2000 ft within 10 NM mountainous, 1500 ft elsewhere"]],"why":"Learn the 5000 ft breakpoint for MORA and the 25 NM radius for MSA.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"Which of these minimum altitudes guarantees navigation signal coverage?","src":"Dispatch Training Tech Bulletin Nav 1","ref":"Key facts: Minimum flight altitudes (MEA, MOCA, MORA, MSA)","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["MEA","MOCA","Route MORA","Grid MORA"],"a":0,"why":"Only the MEA includes signal reception. MOCA covers nav only within 22 NM of a VOR, and only in the USA.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"On a Jeppesen en-route chart, a grid MORA of 12,800 ft is printed in which colour?","src":"Dispatch Training Tech Bulletin Nav 1; Oral bank p.112","ref":"Key facts: Minimum flight altitudes (MEA, MOCA, MORA, MSA); OB20","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Green","Magenta (maroon)","Blue","Black"],"a":1,"why":"Green below 10,000 ft, magenta at or above 10,000 ft.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-1","type":"num","q":"ICAO definition of mountainous terrain used in the bulletin: what change in elevation within 10 NM?","src":"Dispatch Training Tech Bulletin Nav 1","ref":"Key facts: Minimum flight altitudes (MEA, MOCA, MORA, MSA)","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":3000,"unit":"ft","why":"A 3000 ft change in elevation within 10 NM. Note NR40: Doc 8168 leaves designation to the State (AIP).","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"Why is MOCA always equal to or lower than MEA, and when would you use it?","src":"Dispatch Training Tech Bulletin Nav 1","ref":"Key facts: Minimum flight altitudes (MEA, MOCA, MORA, MSA)","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**MOCA gives obstacle clearance only**; the MEA adds signal reception, restricted areas and ATC needs, so MOCA can never be higher.\n- Flying at MOCA may put you **below the MEA and below radar coverage**, without guaranteed nav reception.\n- So it's mainly for **emergencies**, for example descending to get out of icing.\n- A figure followed by **m** is in metres, not feet.","keys":[{"k":"obstacle clearance only","m":["obstacle clearance only","only obstacle","obstacle only"]},{"k":"no nav / radar guarantee","m":["below radar","radar coverage","no nav","signal","reception"]},{"k":"emergency use","m":["emergenc"]},{"k":"icing example","m":["icing","ice"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"Define MSA. What clearance and radius?","src":"OM-A Ch 0 def 21; Dispatch Training Tech Bulletin Nav 1","ref":"Key facts: Minimum flight altitudes (MEA, MOCA, MORA, MSA); OB19","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Minimum Sector Altitude**: the lowest altitude giving **1000 ft obstacle clearance within 25 NM** of the approach navaid or reference point.\n- **Emergency use only**; no navaid reception is guaranteed.\n- May be divided into sectors (shown on the approach chart).\n- In the USA it's called **Minimum Safe Altitude**.\n- If comms fail while vectored below it, climb to MSA.","keys":[{"k":"1000 ft","m":["1000 ft","1000ft","1000 feet","1000"]},{"k":"25 NM","m":["25 nm","25nm","25 nautical","twenty five"]},{"k":"emergency use","m":["emergenc"]},{"k":"reference navaid / point","m":["navaid","facility","reference point","beacon"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-2","type":"num","q":"MSA: obstacle clearance radius around the reference point?","src":"OM-A Ch 0 def 21","ref":"Key facts: Minimum flight altitudes (MEA, MOCA, MORA, MSA)","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":25,"unit":"NM","why":"1000 ft clearance within 25 NM.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw the Jeppesen en-route depiction of MEA, MOCA, route MORA and grid MORA on an airway segment.","src":"Dispatch Training Tech Bulletin Nav 1; Oral bank p.112","ref":"Key facts: Minimum flight altitudes (MEA, MOCA, MORA, MSA); OB20","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- Airway between two fixes, MEA as a plain figure (e.g. **4300**) above the line.\n- MOCA below it with a **T** (e.g. **3900T**).\n- Route MORA with an **a** (e.g. **2600a**), protecting **10 NM each side** and 10 NM beyond the fixes.\n- Grid MORA: large figure in the lat/long square (large 12, small 8 = 12,800 ft), **green below 10,000 ft, magenta at or above**.\n- Bar-triangle-bar marks an MEA change; X marks a mileage break.","checklist":["Airway between two fixes","MEA plain figure","MOCA with T suffix","Route MORA with a suffix","10 NM corridor each side of centreline","Grid MORA large/small figure in square","Colour rule: green below 10,000 ft, magenta at or above"],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"DA versus MDA: which approaches use which, and what is the AIX rule for NPAs?","src":"OM-A 7.2.7, 28.2.1; OM-A Ch 0","ref":"Key facts: Approach procedures and charts","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **DA/H** is for **3D** approaches (DA referenced to MSL, DH to threshold).\n- **MDA/H** is for **2D** approaches and circling.\n- **CDFA is mandatory for NPAs** at AIX; not flying CDFA adds **400 m** to the RVR.\n- Decide at a **DDA = MDA + 50 ft** so the go-around starts early enough not to sink below the MDA.","keys":[{"k":"DA for 3D","m":["3d","three d","precision"]},{"k":"MDA for 2D","m":["2d","two d","non precision","nonprecision"]},{"k":"CDFA mandatory","m":["cdfa","continuous descent","canpa"]},{"k":"+400 m","m":["400 m","400m","400 metre","400 meter"]},{"k":"DDA = MDA + 50 ft","m":["50 ft","50ft","50 feet","plus 50","+50"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-3","type":"num","q":"AIX: RVR increment if an NPA is not flown as CDFA?","src":"OM-A 7.2.7, 28.2.1","ref":"Key facts: Approach procedures and charts","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":400,"unit":"m","why":"CDFA is mandatory; otherwise add 400 m.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-order-1","type":"order","q":"Order the AIX approach priority tiers, highest first.","src":"OM-A 28.10","ref":"Key facts: Approach procedures and charts","prio":2,"items":["Tier 1: ILS and APV","Tier 2: 2D NPA","Tier 3: charted visual"],"why":"OM-A 28.10 approach priority.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"What approach restrictions does the AIX OM-A impose?","src":"OM-A 28.4 to 28.6","ref":"Key facts: Approach procedures and charts","prio":2,"a":"Three restrictions \\[OM-A 28.4 to 28.6\\]:\n- **No NDB approaches at night.**\n- **No circling.**\n- **No touch-and-go.**\nThese sit alongside the CDFA rule for all NPAs.\nAnd on a go-around before the MAPt, do not turn before the MAPt (OM-A 27).","keys":[{"k":"no NDB at night","m":["ndb"]},{"k":"no circling","m":["circl"]},{"k":"no touch and go","m":["touch and go","touchandgo","touch go"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"What RNAV substitution is allowed on a conventional procedure?","src":"OM-A 28.8","ref":"Key facts: Approach procedures and charts","prio":2,"a":"\\[OM-A 28.8\\]\n- **Allowed:** fixes, holds and DME arcs on conventional procedures, **up to the FAF**.\n- **Not allowed:** substituting the **final approach navaid**, or flying a LOC course without raw LOC.\n- **Alternate:** must have an approach that **does not need GPS**.\nSo RNAV can replace the conventional fixes and arcs, but final approach guidance must come from the real navaid.","keys":[{"k":"fixes, holds, arcs","m":["fix","hold","dme arc","arc"]},{"k":"up to the FAF","m":["faf","final approach fix"]},{"k":"not the final navaid","m":["final approach navaid","final navaid","final approach aid","not on final"]},{"k":"raw LOC needed","m":["raw loc","raw data","raw localiser","raw localizer"]},{"k":"alternate without GPS","m":["gps","gnss"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"Glide slope threshold crossing height, per OM-A 45.1.14?","src":"OM-A 45.1.14; OM-A 27.5","ref":"Key facts: Approach procedures and charts","prio":2,"o":["50 ft (+10/-0 ft)","35 ft (+5/-0 ft)","50 ft (+/-10 ft)","60 ft (+0/-10 ft)"],"a":0,"why":"TCH is about 50 ft (OM-A 27.5); the GS crosses the threshold at 50 ft +10/-0.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"Define MEA as your OM-A does.","src":"OM-A Ch 0 def 16; OM-A Ch 6","ref":"Key facts: En-route charts and altitudes; OB19","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Minimum En-route Altitude**: the lowest altitude between radio fixes, set by the State, that gives:\n- **obstacle clearance**;\n- **signal reception** (navigation radio coverage);\n- clearance of danger, prohibited and restricted areas;\n- and meets **ATC** needs.\nIt covers the **full airway width**: at least **1000 ft above obstacles within at least 5 NM either side**.","keys":[{"k":"obstacle clearance","m":["obstacle"]},{"k":"signal reception","m":["signal","reception","nav coverage"]},{"k":"restricted/prohibited areas","m":["restricted","prohibited","danger"]},{"k":"ATC","m":["atc","air traffic"]},{"k":"airway width / 5 NM","m":["airway width","5 nm","5nm","5 nautical","full width"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"Define MVA. Can it be below the MEA?","src":"OM-A Ch 0 def 22, 6.3","ref":"Key facts: En-route charts and altitudes; OB19","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**Minimum Vectoring Altitude**: the lowest altitude ATC uses for **radar vectors**.\n- **Yes, it may be below the MEA.**\n- ATC provides obstacle clearance while vectoring you.\n- If comms fail while being vectored, **climb to the MSA**.","keys":[{"k":"radar vectors","m":["radar","vector"]},{"k":"may be below MEA","m":["below the mea","below mea","lower than mea","lower than the mea"]},{"k":"ATC gives obstacle clearance","m":["atc provide","atc gives","atc responsible","obstacle clearance"]},{"k":"comms fail: climb to MSA","m":["msa","minimum sector"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"Where no minimum altitude is published, what minimum altitude does AIX use?","src":"OM-A 6.1.2","ref":"Key facts: En-route charts and altitudes; OB19","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"\\[OM-A 6.1.2\\]\n- **2000 ft above the highest obstacle within 10 NM** over high or mountainous terrain (**7000 ft and above**).\n- **1500 ft** above the highest obstacle within 10 NM elsewhere.\n- The bank's \"2000 ft within 4 NM\" (1000 ft elsewhere) is the **US FAA** figure, not your OM-A.","keys":[{"k":"2000 ft mountainous","m":["2000 ft","2000ft","2000 feet","2000"]},{"k":"1500 ft elsewhere","m":["1500 ft","1500ft","1500 feet","1500"]},{"k":"within 10 NM","m":["10 nm","10nm","10 nautical","ten nm","ten nautical"]},{"k":"mountainous 7000 ft+","m":["7000 ft","7000ft","7000 feet","7000"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["4 nm","4nm","four nm","4 nautical"],"why":"US FAA figure from the bank; OM-A uses 10 NM"}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"AIX minimum altitude where none is published, over non-mountainous terrain?","src":"OM-A 6.1.2","ref":"Key facts: En-route charts and altitudes; OB19","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["1500 ft above the highest obstacle within 10 NM","1000 ft above the highest obstacle within 4 NM","2000 ft above the highest obstacle within 10 NM","1000 ft above the highest obstacle within 5 NM"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 6: 1500 ft elsewhere, 2000 ft in mountainous areas (terrain 7000 ft or more), within 10 NM. 1000 ft / 4 NM is the FAA rule.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"How do you correct minimum altitudes for low pressure and cold temperature?","src":"OM-A 6.4","ref":"Key facts: En-route charts and altitudes","prio":2,"a":"\\[OM-A 6.4\\]\n- **Low pressure:** add **30 ft per hPa**.\n- **Cold temperature:** add **4% of height per 10°C below ISA**.\n- **Tell ATC** when you apply corrections.\nBoth errors make the aircraft lower than the altimeter shows, so published minimum altitudes are raised to keep the intended obstacle clearance.","keys":[{"k":"30 ft per hPa","m":["30 ft per","30ft per","30 feet per","30 ft/hpa"]},{"k":"4% per 10 deg below ISA","m":["4%","4 percent","4 per cent","four percent"]},{"k":"below ISA","m":["isa","10 degrees","10°c","10 deg"]},{"k":"tell ATC","m":["atc","inform","tell"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-4","type":"num","q":"Cold temperature correction (OM-A 6.4): percent of height per 10°C below ISA?","src":"OM-A 6.4","ref":"Key facts: En-route charts and altitudes","prio":2,"ans":4,"unit":"%","why":"4% of height per 10°C below ISA; low pressure 30 ft per hPa.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-5","type":"num","q":"Low pressure correction (OM-A 6.4): feet per hPa?","src":"OM-A 6.4","ref":"Key facts: En-route charts and altitudes","prio":2,"ans":30,"unit":"ft","why":"30 ft per hPa; tell ATC when applied.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"What altimeter settings do you use, and what is the transition layer?","src":"OM-A 17.3.3","ref":"Key facts: En-route charts and altitudes","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"\\[OM-A 17.3.3\\]\n- **QNH** for take-off, approach and landing.\n- Set **STD passing the TA** in the climb.\n- Set **QNH passing the TL** in the descent.\n- **Transition layer:** the airspace between TA and TL; its **depth varies with QNH**.\n- Some routes (e.g. Delhi to Srinagar) use **Area QNH** in climb and descent.\n- The OM-A gives no TA/TL values; quote AIP India.","keys":[{"k":"QNH for takeoff/landing","m":["qnh"]},{"k":"STD at TA in climb","m":["transition altitude"," ta ","standard","1013"]},{"k":"QNH at TL in descent","m":["transition level"," tl "]},{"k":"layer depth varies with QNH","m":["varies","depth","changes with qnh","depends on qnh"]},{"k":"Area QNH routes","m":["area qnh","srinagar"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"What projection are Jeppesen en-route charts?","src":"Standard theory; Oral bank p.102","ref":"Key facts: En-route charts and altitudes; OB20","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Lambert conformal conic","Mercator","Polar stereographic","Transverse Mercator"],"a":0,"why":"Lambert conformal conic, scale within about ±1% across the sheet (standard theory; Jeppesen legend pending).","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"What are the ICAO maximum holding speeds?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.1.1.1; Table II-6-2-1","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Enter and fly the hold at or below \\[Table II-6-2-1\\]:\n- **Up to 14,000 ft: 230 kt** (turbulence 280 kt).\n- **Above 14,000 to 20,000 ft: 240 kt** (turbulence 280 kt or M0.80, whichever is less).\n- **Above 20,000 to 34,000 ft: 265 kt** (same turbulence limit).\n- **Above 34,000 ft: M0.83.**\nTurbulence speeds need prior ATC clearance unless the holding area is published to accommodate them.","keys":[{"k":"230 kt to 14,000","m":["230"]},{"k":"240 kt to 20,000","m":["240"]},{"k":"265 kt to 34,000","m":["265"]},{"k":"M0.83 above","m":["0.83","083","point 83","point eight three"]},{"k":"turbulence 280 kt","m":["280"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-5","type":"match","q":"Match the level band to the ICAO normal maximum holding speed.","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, Table II-6-2-1","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Up to 14,000 ft","230 kt"],["Above 14,000 to 20,000 ft","240 kt"],["Above 20,000 to 34,000 ft","265 kt"],["Above 34,000 ft","M0.83"]],"why":"Turbulence: 280 kt up to 14,000 ft; 280 kt or M0.80, whichever is less, to 34,000 ft (with ATC clearance).","topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-6","type":"num","q":"Maximum holding speed (normal) between FL200 and FL340?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, Table II-6-2-1","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":265,"unit":"kt","why":"265 kt above 20,000 to 34,000 ft.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-7","type":"num","q":"Maximum holding speed in turbulence up to 14,000 ft (with ATC clearance)?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, Table II-6-2-1","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":280,"unit":"kt","why":"280 kt; above that 280 kt or M0.80, whichever is less.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"Maximum holding speed in turbulence between 14,000 and 34,000 ft?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, Table II-6-2-1","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["280 kt or M0.80, whichever is less","280 kt or M0.83, whichever is less","265 kt","300 kt or M0.80, whichever is less"],"a":0,"why":"280 kt or M0.80, whichever is less, with prior ATC clearance.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"What bank angle, outbound timing and timing start point are used in a hold?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.1.2, 2.1.4, 2.3.1","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Bank:** 25° or 3°/s, **whichever needs the lesser bank** \\[2.1.2\\].\n- **Outbound leg:** **1 min at or below 14,000 ft, 1½ min above**; where a distance is specified, fly to the limiting DME instead \\[2.3.1\\].\n- **Timing starts over or abeam the fix, whichever is later**; if abeam can't be determined, start when the outbound turn is complete \\[2.1.4\\].","keys":[{"k":"25 deg or 3 deg/s","m":["25°","25 deg","25 degree","rate one","rate 1","3 degrees per second","3°/s"]},{"k":"1 min to 14,000","m":["1 min","one min","1min"]},{"k":"1.5 min above","m":["1.5","15 min","one and a half","1 and a half","90 sec"]},{"k":"abeam, whichever later","m":["abeam"]},{"k":"DME limit","m":["dme"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-8","type":"num","q":"Outbound leg time in a hold above 14,000 ft?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.3.1","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":1.5,"unit":"min","why":"1 min at or below 14,000 ft, 1.5 min above.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"How do you correct for wind in a hold?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.1.3, 2.3.2; FCTM 4.23","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Adjust **both heading and timing**, during the entry and in the pattern, so the **inbound track is regained before the fix** \\[2.1.3; 2.3.2\\].\n- Correct drift on the outbound leg (heading).\n- Adjust later **outbound leg times** to get the proper **inbound timing** (1 or 1.5 min) \\[FCTM 4.23\\].\n- The FMC/LNAV does this automatically, but strong winds or excess speed may take it outside protected airspace.","keys":[{"k":"heading and timing","m":["heading and tim","heading and time","both heading"]},{"k":"regain inbound before fix","m":["inbound track","before the fix","regain"]},{"k":"adjust outbound time","m":["outbound tim","outbound leg","outbound time"]},{"k":"inbound timing target","m":["inbound tim","inbound time"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"Describe the three holding entries and how you choose one.","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.2.2 to 2.2.9.1","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Choose on **heading** relative to the three sectors of Figure II-6-2-1, with **5° flexibility** at the boundaries \\[2.2.2\\]:\n- **Sector 1, parallel:** at the fix turn onto the outbound heading, then turn towards the holding side to intercept inbound or return to the fix.\n- **Sector 2, offset (teardrop):** at the fix take up a track **30° from the reciprocal of the inbound track**, on the holding side, then turn to intercept inbound.\n- **Sector 3, direct:** at the fix turn and follow the pattern.\nStill-air outbound entry time should not exceed 1 min (1½ min above 14,000 ft). Left-hand holds are the mirror image.","keys":[{"k":"parallel","m":["parallel"]},{"k":"offset / teardrop 30°","m":["offset","teardrop","tear drop"]},{"k":"direct","m":["direct"]},{"k":"30 deg from reciprocal","m":["30°","30 deg","30 degree","thirty"]},{"k":"based on heading, 5° flexibility","m":["5°","5 deg","5 degree","five degree","heading"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-6","type":"match","q":"Match the holding entry sector to the entry.","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.2.4 to 2.2.6","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Sector 1","Parallel"],["Sector 2","Offset (teardrop)"],["Sector 3","Direct"]],"why":"Sectors of Figure II-6-2-1, judged on heading, 5° flexibility at the boundaries.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-order-2","type":"order","q":"Put the parallel (Sector 1) entry in order.","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.2.4","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["Arrive over the fix","Turn onto the outbound heading (parallel to inbound, non-holding side)","Fly outbound for the appropriate time (1 or 1.5 min still air)","Turn towards the holding side","Intercept the inbound track or return to the fix","Continue in the holding pattern"],"why":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.2.4 and 2.2.9.1.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-order-3","type":"order","q":"Put the offset (Sector 2) entry in order.","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.2.5","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["Arrive over the fix","Take up a track 30° from the reciprocal of the inbound track, on the holding side","Fly outbound for the appropriate time (1 or 1.5 min still air)","Turn in the direction of the hold to intercept the inbound track","Fly inbound to the fix and continue in the pattern"],"why":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.2.5 and 2.2.9.1.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-order-4","type":"order","q":"Put the direct (Sector 3) entry in order.","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.2.6; 2.1.4","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Arrive over the fix","Turn in the holding direction onto the outbound heading","Start timing over or abeam the fix, whichever is later","Fly outbound 1 or 1.5 min","Turn inbound and regain the inbound track before the fix"],"why":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.2.6, 2.1.4.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-9","type":"num","q":"Offset entry: angle of the outbound track from the reciprocal of the inbound track?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.2.5","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":30,"unit":"°","why":"30° on the holding side.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-10","type":"num","q":"Flexibility either side of a holding entry sector boundary?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.2.2","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":5,"unit":"°","why":"5° either side of each boundary.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-draw-2","type":"draw","q":"Draw a standard right-hand holding pattern with the three entry sectors.","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, Fig II-6-2-1; 2.2.2 to 2.2.6","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- Fix at the inbound end; **inbound track** towards the fix; **right turns**; outbound leg parallel on the right (holding side).\n- Leg time **1 min at or below 14,000 ft, 1½ min above**.\n- Sector boundaries drawn through the fix as in Figure II-6-2-1 (the **70°** line appears only in the figure, not the text).\n- **Sector 1 parallel**, **Sector 2 offset** (30° from the reciprocal), **Sector 3 direct**.\n- 5° flexibility at the boundaries. Left-hand hold is the mirror image.","checklist":["Fix at end of inbound leg","Right turns, holding side shown","Inbound track arrow to fix","Sector boundary at 70° line (figure only)","Sector 1 parallel, Sector 2 offset, Sector 3 direct labelled","Offset track 30° from reciprocal drawn","Leg time 1 / 1.5 min annotated","5° boundary flexibility noted"],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-draw-3","type":"draw","q":"Draw the three entries flown into a right-hand hold (parallel, offset, direct).","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.2.4 to 2.2.6, 2.2.9.1","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Parallel:** over the fix, turn to fly outbound on the non-holding side parallel to inbound, then turn back through the holding side to intercept inbound or the fix.\n- **Offset:** over the fix, fly 30° off the reciprocal into the holding side, then turn (right) to intercept inbound.\n- **Direct:** over the fix, turn right straight into the pattern.\n- Each outbound entry leg 1 min (1½ above 14,000 ft) still air.","checklist":["Parallel: outbound on non-holding side","Parallel: turn back across to inbound/fix","Offset: 30° track on holding side","Offset: turn to intercept inbound","Direct: straight into pattern","Entry legs 1 / 1.5 min"],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"How does the 737 MAX fly a hold?","src":"FCTM 4.23; FCOM 11.43","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Start slowing 3 min before the fix** to cross it at or below the maximum holding speed \\[FCTM 4.23\\].\n- If the FMC holding speed is above the maximum, hold at the **flaps 1 manoeuvre speed** (about **10% more fuel**); stay clean in icing or turbulence.\n- The **FMC picks the entry** (parallel, teardrop, direct) from the angle between the leg into the fix and the inbound course; **LNAV up to 30° bank**.\n- HOLD page defaults to 1.0 or 1.5 min legs and right turns; **BEST SPEED may exceed the regulatory maximum**.\n- Strong winds or excess speed can take it outside protected airspace.","keys":[{"k":"slow 3 min before","m":["3 min","three min","3min"]},{"k":"flaps 1 speed","m":["flaps 1","flap 1","flaps one"]},{"k":"FMC picks entry","m":["fmc"]},{"k":"LNAV 30 deg bank","m":["30°","30 deg","30 degree","thirty degree"]},{"k":"best speed can exceed max","m":["best speed","exceed"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"The FMC HOLD page shows BEST SPEED. What must you remember?","src":"FCOM 11.43","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","prio":2,"o":["It may exceed the regulatory maximum holding speed","It is always the ICAO maximum holding speed","It is the flaps 1 manoeuvre speed","It is only valid above FL340"],"a":0,"why":"FCOM 11.43: BEST SPEED may exceed the regulatory maximum; fly at or below the ICAO limit.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"Holding fuel: what are final reserve and contingency, and what is the minimum holding level clearance?","src":"OM-A 12.1.3, 23.9; Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.4.3","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Final reserve:** **30 min** at holding speed, **1500 ft above the destination alternate** elevation \\[OM-A 12.1.3 e\\].\n- **Contingency:** at least **5 min** at holding speed, 1500 ft above the destination \\[OM-A 12.1.3 c\\].\n- In the hold, monitor the FMC holding information for the time left before you must divert \\[OM-A 23.9\\].\n- **Minimum holding level:** at least **300 m (984 ft)** obstacle clearance in the holding area, reducing across the buffer area \\[II-6-2, 2.4.3\\]; up to 600 m over mountainous terrain.","keys":[{"k":"30 min final reserve","m":["30 min","30min","thirty min"]},{"k":"1500 ft above","m":["1500 ft","1500ft","1500 feet","1500"]},{"k":"5 min contingency","m":["5 min","5min","five min"]},{"k":"300 m / 984 ft","m":["300 m","300m","984","300 metre"]},{"k":"divert time from FMC","m":["divert","fmc"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-11","type":"num","q":"Minimum obstacle clearance in the holding area (Doc 8168), in feet?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2, 2.4.3","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":984,"unit":"ft","why":"300 m (984 ft), reducing across the buffer area.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"What is Grid MORA on the Jeppesen chart?","src":"Jeppesen legend; OM-A 17.19, Ch 6","ref":"NR3","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"A **minimum off-route altitude for each lat/long square** (1° × 1° on en-route charts), clearing all terrain and man-made obstacles in that square.\n- **Clearance (Jeppesen legend):** **1000 ft** where the highest point is **5000 ft or lower**, **2000 ft** where it is higher.\n- Shown in thousands and hundreds: large 12, small 8 = **12,800 ft**.\n- **No navigation or radio coverage** is guaranteed.\n- Use it off an airway: weather deviation, emergency, or doubtful radar vectors.","keys":[{"k":"per lat/long square","m":["square","grid","lat long","latitude"]},{"k":"1000 ft up to 5000","m":["1000 ft","1000ft","1000 feet","1000"]},{"k":"2000 ft above 5000","m":["2000 ft","2000ft","2000 feet","2000"]},{"k":"5000 ft breakpoint","m":["5000"]},{"k":"no nav/comm coverage","m":["no nav","no radio","not guarantee","no guarantee","coverage"]},{"k":"off airway use","m":["off airway","off route","offroute","deviation","emergenc"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["3000 ft","3000ft","3000 feet"],"why":"OM-A Ch 0 definition says 3000 ft; quote the Jeppesen legend (1000/2000 ft)"}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"Grid MORA: what terrain clearance where the highest point in the square is above 5000 ft?","src":"Jeppesen legend; OM-A Ch 0","ref":"NR3; OB20","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["2000 ft","1000 ft","3000 ft","1500 ft"],"a":0,"why":"Jeppesen legend: 1000 ft at 5000 ft or lower, 2000 ft above. The OM-A Ch 0 '3000 ft' and the bank's flat '1000 ft' are both wrong.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-12","type":"num","q":"Grid MORA printed as a large 12 with a small 8. What altitude?","src":"Jeppesen legend","ref":"NR3","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":12800,"unit":"ft","why":"Thousands and hundreds of feet: 12,800 ft (magenta, as it is 10,000 ft or above).","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"What is CANPA?","src":"OM-A Ch 0, 7.2.7, 28.2.1","ref":"NR4","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Constant Angle Non-Precision Approach**: the older European name for what ICAO now calls **CDFA** (Continuous Descent Final Approach).\n- The NPA final segment is flown at a **constant angle (about 3°)**, **no level-off**, from the FAF altitude to about **50 ft over the threshold**.\n- **All AIX NPAs are flown CDFA**; otherwise add **400 m** to the RVR.\n- Decision at a **DDA = MDA + 50 ft**.","keys":[{"k":"= CDFA","m":["cdfa","continuous descent"]},{"k":"constant ~3°","m":["3°","3 deg","three deg","constant angle","3 degree"]},{"k":"no level-off","m":["no level","without level","no step"]},{"k":"50 ft over threshold","m":["50 ft","50ft","50 feet","threshold"]},{"k":"+400 m otherwise","m":["400 m","400m","400 metre"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between ILS X, ILS Y and ILS Z to the same runway?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, 5.1.6, 5.1.7","ref":"NR13","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"If two or more procedures to the same runway **can't be told apart by navaid type**, each gets a **single-letter suffix, starting with Z** (e.g. VOR Z RWY 20, VOR Y RWY 20) \\[5.1.6\\].\n- Typically **different missed approaches** for a common approach, or **different aircraft categories** \\[5.1.7\\].\n- In practice it can also mark a different initial/intermediate segment, minima or required equipment.\n- **Brief the exact letter, read it back** (\"ILS Z RWY 27\"), and **check the FMC procedure letter** matches.","keys":[{"k":"same runway, same navaid type","m":["same runway","same type","same navaid","can't be distinguished","cannot be distinguished"]},{"k":"starts with Z","m":["with z","from z","z first","start at z","starting z"]},{"k":"different missed approach","m":["missed approach"]},{"k":"aircraft category","m":["category","categories"]},{"k":"brief/read back/check FMC","m":["read back","readback","fmc","brief"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-21","type":"recall","q":"What speed do you maintain on a DME arc? (The sheet says 230 KIAS.)","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, Table II-5-1-2; Table II-6-2-1","ref":"NR18","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"**There is no separate DME arc speed in PANS-OPS.** The arc is part of the initial approach, designed for your category's **initial approach speed range**: **Cat C 160 to 240 kt** (Cat D 185 to 250).\n- **230 kt is the holding speed limit up to 14,000 ft**, not an arc speed.\n- So: fly the **chart speed** for the arc, otherwise **ATC's**, otherwise stay within **240 kt max for Cat C**, and respect the **FL100 speed limit**.\n- Faster means a larger turn radius and you can leave protected airspace.","keys":[{"k":"no specific arc speed","m":["no separate","no specific","no dme arc speed","not specified","no arc speed"]},{"k":"Cat C 160 to 240 kt","m":["160","240"]},{"k":"230 is holding speed","m":["holding"]},{"k":"chart / ATC speed","m":["chart","atc"]},{"k":"protected area / turn radius","m":["protected","turn radius","radius"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["230 is the arc speed","arc speed is 230","230 kias on the arc"],"why":"230 kt is the holding speed limit up to 14,000 ft, not an arc speed"}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Initial approach speed range for a Cat C aircraft (procedure design)?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, Table II-5-1-2","ref":"NR18; OB17","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["160 to 240 kt","185 to 250 kt","120 to 180 kt","115 to 160 kt"],"a":0,"why":"Cat C initial 160 to 240 kt; Cat D 185 to 250; 115 to 160 is Cat C final.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-22","type":"recall","q":"Can you fly an ILS approach without the glideslope?","src":"OM-A 7.3.1, 28.7.1; FCTM 5.10, 5.44, 5.55, 5.56","ref":"NR20","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Yes, as a localiser-only (GS out) approach, which is a non-precision approach.**\n- Lateral guidance from the **LOC**; vertical profile from the **altitude/DME table**, flown **CDFA**.\n- Decide at **DDA = MDA + 50 ft**, using the **LOC minima** (LOC system minimum **250 ft**).\n- **Not allowed:** GS without LOC, or the ILS minima.\n- **Boeing:** fly it with IAN, **G/S prompt OFF**; FMA shows **VOR/LOC and G/P**. MAP is normally the threshold. VNAV PTH with step-down fixes FAF to MAP is not recommended without approval.","keys":[{"k":"yes, LOC only","m":["loc only","localiser only","localizer only","gs out","glideslope out","glide slope out"]},{"k":"NPA / LOC minima","m":["non precision","nonprecision","npa","loc minima"]},{"k":"DDA = MDA + 50","m":["mda","dda"]},{"k":"250 ft LOC minimum","m":["250"]},{"k":"IAN, G/S prompt off","m":["ian","g/s prompt","gs prompt","integrated approach"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-23","type":"recall","q":"What is CANPA (CDFA), and how is it useful?","src":"OM-A Ch 0, 28.2, 28.2.1","ref":"NR33","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Flying the NPA final at a **constant angle (about 3°)** with **no level-off**, FAF to about 50 ft over the threshold.\n**Benefits** \\[OM-A 28.2.1\\]:\n- **stabilised**, same as an ILS profile;\n- better situational awareness, **lower workload**;\n- **less fuel and noise**;\n- **lower CFIT risk**;\n- **lower minima** (otherwise +400 m).\nFlown with **VNAV path** or a calculated rate: about **5 × GS** for 3°, checked against the altitude/DME table.","keys":[{"k":"stabilised","m":["stabilis","stabiliz","like an ils","same as ils"]},{"k":"lower workload","m":["workload"]},{"k":"fuel and noise","m":["fuel","noise"]},{"k":"CFIT","m":["cfit","terrain","obstacle"]},{"k":"lower minima / +400 m","m":["400","minima"]},{"k":"5 × GS","m":["5 times","five times","5 x","x 5","times 5","5×"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-order-5","type":"order","q":"ICAO's three ways to fly an NPA final, from preferred to least preferred.","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-1, 1.8","ref":"NR33","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["CDFA: continuous descent, at MDA/H continue visually or go around","Constant angle descent: level off at MDA/H if not visual and fly to the MAPt","Step-down: descend immediately to each step altitude (gradient below 15%)"],"why":"Doc 8168 says CDFA 'whenever possible' because it reduces workload and error.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"A CDFA flown with a manually calculated rate of descent counts as which operation?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-1, 1.8.2.2 Note; II-5-2, 2.5","ref":"NR33; OB16","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["2D","3D","APV","Precision"],"a":0,"why":"With on-board VNAV guidance it counts as 3D; with a hand-calculated rate it is 2D.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-24","type":"recall","q":"Why do you add 50 ft to the MDA to get the DDA on a CANPA?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-1, 1.8.2.3, 1.8.2.4; OM-A 7.2.7, 28.2.1; FCTM 5.48","ref":"NR34","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**So the go-around doesn't sink below the MDA.**\n- ICAO: in a CDFA, if not visual approaching the MDA/H, the missed approach climb must start **high enough above the MDA/H that the aircraft does not descend through it**; never fly level at or near the MDA/H \\[1.8.2.3\\].\n- The operator may set an increment; if it does, **no RVR increase** is needed. AIX sets **50 ft** \\[1.8.2.4; OM-A 7.2.7\\].\n- At 700 to 800 fpm the aircraft keeps **sinking for a few seconds** after pitch-up and thrust.\n- An MDA has **no height-loss allowance**, unlike a DA. Boeing FCTM 5.48 says the same.","keys":[{"k":"not descend below MDA","m":["below the mda","below mda","through the mda","through mda","bust"]},{"k":"height loss in go-around","m":["height loss","sink","keeps descending","momentum"]},{"k":"MDA has no allowance","m":["no allowance","no margin","not built in","no height loss"]},{"k":"no RVR penalty","m":["rvr","no penalty"]},{"k":"50 ft","m":["50 ft","50ft","50 feet","fifty"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-25","type":"recall","q":"Why isn't 50 ft added to the DH on an ILS, when it is for a CANPA?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-2, 2.3.4, 2.4.2; II-5-5, 5.7.4, Table II-5-5-3","ref":"NR35","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**A DA/H already allows for height loss** after the missed approach starts \\[II-5-2, 2.3.4\\].\n- The designer adds a **height-loss/altimeter margin** to the obstacle when calculating OCA/H: **Cat C 71 ft** with radio altimeter, **150 ft** with pressure altimeter \\[Table II-5-5-3\\].\n- An **MDA/H has no such margin**: it's a floor not to be descended below without visual references, so the operator adds the allowance.\n- The precision path is also tightly protected (half-scale deflection).\n- **DA is a decision point with protected height loss; MDA is a floor.**","keys":[{"k":"DA includes height loss","m":["height loss","already allow","built in","builtin"]},{"k":"OCA/H margin","m":["oca","och","margin"]},{"k":"Cat C 71 / 150 ft","m":["71","150"]},{"k":"MDA is a floor","m":["floor","must not descend","not descend below","no margin"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-7","type":"match","q":"Height-loss/altimeter margin (Table II-5-5-3): match category to radio altimeter / pressure altimeter margin.","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-5, 5.7.4, Table II-5-5-3","ref":"NR35","asked":true,"prio":3,"pairs":[["Cat A (90 kt)","42 ft / 130 ft"],["Cat B (120 kt)","59 ft / 142 ft"],["Cat C (140 kt), B737","71 ft / 150 ft"],["Cat D (165 kt)","85 ft / 161 ft"]],"why":"Added to the obstacle height to get OCA/H for precision approaches.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-13","type":"num","q":"Height-loss margin for Cat C using a pressure altimeter (precision approach)?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, Table II-5-5-3","ref":"NR35","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":150,"unit":"ft","why":"Cat C: 71 ft radio altimeter, 150 ft pressure altimeter.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-26","type":"recall","q":"What is MORA and how is it calculated?","src":"OM-A Ch 0; Jeppesen legend","ref":"NR39","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Route MORA (Jeppesen):** clears all terrain and obstacles within **10 NM either side** of the airway centreline, **including 10 NM beyond the radio fixes**.\n- **Grid MORA:** same clearance for each 1° lat/long square.\n- **Calculation:** highest terrain or obstacle, then add **1000 ft if it's 5000 ft or lower**, **2000 ft if above 5000 ft**, and **round up to the next 100 ft**.\n- It does **not** guarantee navaid reception or radio coverage (the MEA does).","keys":[{"k":"10 NM each side","m":["10 nm","10nm","10 nautical","ten nm","ten nautical"]},{"k":"beyond the fixes","m":["beyond"]},{"k":"+1000 / +2000","m":["1000","2000"]},{"k":"5000 ft breakpoint","m":["5000"]},{"k":"round up 100 ft","m":["round","100 ft","next 100"]},{"k":"no nav coverage","m":["no nav","not guarantee","no guarantee","reception","coverage"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-27","type":"recall","q":"What is a mountainous area?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, 3.2.4.1, 3.2.4.2, Table A-3-6; OM-A 6","ref":"NR40","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Identified by the State and promulgated in the AIP** \\[Doc 8168 3.2.4.2\\], so AIP India is the authority; Doc 8168 Vol I gives no terrain-change figure.\n- En-route MOC is normally **1000 ft**; in mountainous areas: terrain 3000 to 5000 ft **1476 ft (450 m)**, above 5000 ft **1969 ft (600 m)**.\n- Holding: up to a total of **600 m** over high terrain.\n- Winds **20 kt or more**: MOC may increase **up to 100%**.\n- **OM-A:** 1500 ft over 3000 to 5000 ft terrain, 2000 ft above 5000 ft.","keys":[{"k":"State / AIP","m":["state","aip"]},{"k":"450 m / 1476 ft","m":["450","1476","1500"]},{"k":"600 m / 1969 ft","m":["600","1969","2000"]},{"k":"20 kt wind, up to 100%","m":["100%","100 percent","double","20 kt","20kt"]},{"k":"normal 1000 ft","m":["1000"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"Who defines a 'mountainous area' for the purpose of extra MOC, according to Doc 8168 Vol I?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, 3.2.4.2; Dispatch Training Tech Bulletin Nav 1","ref":"NR40","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["The State, promulgated in the AIP","ICAO: 3000 ft elevation change within 10 NM","The operator in its OM-A","Jeppesen, on the en-route chart"],"a":0,"why":"Doc 8168 Vol I 3.2.4.2 leaves designation to the State (AIP). The '3000 ft within 10 NM' figure comes from the Dispatch bulletin, not Doc 8168 Vol I.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-8","type":"match","q":"Match the OM-A wind (Bernoulli) altimeter error to the wind speed over mountains.","src":"OM-A 6; Doc 8168 Vol I, 1.13","ref":"NR40","asked":true,"prio":3,"pairs":[["20 kt","about 53 ft"],["40 kt","about 201 ft"],["60 kt","about 455 ft"]],"why":"OM-A 6. Doc 8168 allows MOC up to +100% where winds of 20 kt or more cross mountainous areas.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-28","type":"recall","q":"How do you fly a CANPA if no FAF is published, and how is the FAF crossing height calculated?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-1, 1.8.3.1, 1.10.4, 1.10.5; OM-A 28.2","ref":"NR43","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"ICAO: the constant angle runs **from the FAF, or an optimum point on procedures without an FAF**, to about 50 ft over the threshold \\[1.8.3.1\\].\n- **FAF height** = (distance from threshold in NM × **318**) + **50 ft**; add threshold elevation for altitude. Use the chart angle if not 3°.\n- **No FAF:** find where a 3° path from your platform altitude meets final: distance = (altitude − threshold elevation − 50) / 318 NM. Start descent there by DME or timing; cross-check the altitude/distance table.\n- Must pass at or above every **step-down fix** minimum.\n- Rate: **GS × 5** for 3°.","keys":[{"k":"optimum point","m":["optimum"]},{"k":"318 ft per NM","m":["318"]},{"k":"+50 ft TCH","m":["50 ft","50ft","50 feet","plus 50","+ 50"]},{"k":"step-down fixes","m":["step down","stepdown","sdf"]},{"k":"DME or timing","m":["dme","timing"]},{"k":"GS × 5","m":["5 times","five times","5 x","x 5","times 5","× 5"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-14","type":"num","q":"No-FAF CDFA: platform 2000 ft, threshold elevation 50 ft, 3° path. Distance from the threshold to start descent?","src":"OM-A 28.2; Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-1, 1.8.3.1","ref":"NR43","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":6.0,"unit":"NM","tol":0.2,"why":"(2000 − 50 − 50) / 318 ≈ 6.0 NM.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-15","type":"num","q":"A 3° glide path descends about how many feet per NM?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-1, 1.10.5; II-5-5, 5.2.3.1","ref":"NR43; OB15","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":318,"unit":"ft","tol":3,"why":"3° ≈ 5.2% ≈ 318 ft per NM.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-num-16","type":"num","q":"FAF 5 NM from the threshold, 3° path, TCH 50 ft. Height above threshold at the FAF?","src":"OM-A 28.2","ref":"NR43","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":1640,"unit":"ft","tol":10,"why":"(5 × 318) + 50 = 1640 ft above threshold; add threshold elevation for the altitude.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-29","type":"recall","q":"Chennai NDB RWY 07: visibility minima 3400 m Cat C, 5000 m Cat D. Visibility is 3400 m. Can a Cat B aircraft fly it?","src":"OM-A 7.2.3, 7.2.5, 28.4","ref":"NR46","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Yes.**\n- Minima for a lower category are **never higher** than for a higher one: slower aircraft need less visual segment and a smaller manoeuvring area. If only C and D are shown, Cat B may use the **more conservative Cat C** minima.\n- 3400 m is **at** the minimum, not below; the rule is **do not commence if below** \\[OM-A 7.2.5\\].\n- Cat B VAT 91 to 120 kt, Cat C 121 to 140 kt; the B737 is Cat C.\n- AIX doesn't fly NDB approaches at night or circle.","keys":[{"k":"yes","m":["yes","can fly","can commence"]},{"k":"lower cat never higher minima","m":["lower category","never higher","slower","less visual"]},{"k":"use Cat C minima","m":["cat c","category c"]},{"k":"at minimum, not below","m":["not below","at the minimum","at minimum","equal"]},{"k":"do not commence if below","m":["commence"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-30","type":"recall","q":"In Chennai, which way does the runway slope?","src":"SkyVector VOMM; AIP India AD 2 VOMM","ref":"NR50","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **RWY 07/25:** threshold 07 about **43 ft**, threshold 25 about **54 ft**, so it **rises from west to east**.\n- **RWY 07 is slightly upslope; RWY 25 slightly downslope.**\n- About 11 ft over roughly 3.6 km, about **0.1%**: negligible for performance.\n- RWY 12/30: about 44 to 48 ft, rising slightly towards the 30 end.\n- Aerodrome elevation 54 ft (AIP).","keys":[{"k":"07 upslope","m":["07 is upslope","07 upslope","upslope on 07","07 up","rises from west to east","rises west to east"]},{"k":"25 downslope","m":["25 downslope","25 is downslope","downslope on 25","25 down"]},{"k":"43 / 54 ft","m":["43","54"]},{"k":"about 0.1%","m":["0.1","point 1","negligible"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-31","type":"recall","q":"Why is circle-to-land not allowed south of RWY 27 in Mumbai?","src":"OM-A 7.4; Standard theory","ref":"NR51","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"**The circling area is protected only where obstacle clearance can be guaranteed, so the State prohibits circling in any sector where obstacles or prohibited airspace prevent it.** The usual reasons south of the field:\n- **Obstacles:** dense high-rise (Kurla, BKC, central Mumbai) penetrating the circling protection area.\n- **Prohibited airspace** to the south-east (BARC/Trombay).\n- **Conflicting traffic** on the crossing runway's paths.\nFor AIX it is academic: **circling is not authorised** \\[OM-A 7.4\\]. (Exact AIP note not confirmed; check the Jeppesen circling note.)","keys":[{"k":"obstacle clearance not guaranteed","m":["obstacle","high rise","highrise","buildings"]},{"k":"prohibited airspace / BARC","m":["prohibited","barc","trombay"]},{"k":"crossing runway traffic","m":["traffic","crossing runway"]},{"k":"AIX no circling","m":["not authorised","not authorized","no circling","circling not"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-32","type":"recall","q":"What are the segments of an instrument approach, and the obstacle clearance in each?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-1 to II-5-7; Att A 3.3.4.3","ref":"OB15","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Up to five: **arrival, initial, intermediate, final, missed approach**, plus a circling area \\[II-5-1, 1.2.3.1\\].\n- **Initial (IAF to IF):** MOC **300 m (984 ft)**, primary area; intercept max 90° precision, 120° NPA.\n- **Intermediate (IF to FAF/FAP):** **150 m (492 ft)**.\n- **Final NPA:** **75 m (246 ft)** with a FAF, 90 m (295 ft) without; optimum 5.2% (3°). Precision/APV: obstacle surfaces with height-loss margin.\n- **Missed approach:** initial, intermediate (until **50 m (164 ft)** clearance), final; nominal gradient **2.5%**.\nThe bank's 1000/500 ft are rounded 300 m/150 m.","keys":[{"k":"five segments named","m":["arrival","initial","intermediate"]},{"k":"initial 300 m / 984 ft","m":["300 m","300m","984","1000 ft"]},{"k":"intermediate 150 m / 492 ft","m":["150 m","150m","492","500 ft"]},{"k":"final 75 m / 246 ft","m":["75 m","75m","246"]},{"k":"missed approach 50 m / 2.5%","m":["2.5","164","50 m"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-order-6","type":"order","q":"Put the segments of an instrument approach in order.","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-1, 1.2.3.1, 1.2.3.2","ref":"OB15","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["Arrival (en-route to IAF)","Initial (IAF to IF)","Intermediate (IF to FAF/FAP)","Final (FAF/FAP to MAPt)","Missed approach"],"why":"Each begins and ends at a fix; a precision final starts at the FAP.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-9","type":"match","q":"Match the approach segment to its minimum obstacle clearance (primary area).","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-3, II-5-4, II-5-7; Att A 3.3.4.3","ref":"OB15","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Initial","300 m (984 ft)"],["Intermediate","150 m (492 ft)"],["Final NPA with FAF","75 m (246 ft)"],["Final NPA without FAF","90 m (295 ft)"],["Missed approach intermediate phase","50 m (164 ft)"]],"why":"Doc 8168 Vol I II-5-3 to II-5-7 and Att A 3.3.4.3.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-draw-4","type":"draw","q":"Draw the instrument approach segments with their obstacle clearance.","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-1 to II-5-7","ref":"OB15","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Profile view from en-route to the runway:\n- **Arrival** to the **IAF**.\n- **Initial** IAF to IF: MOC **300 m (984 ft)**, tapering to zero at the secondary area edge.\n- **Intermediate** IF to FAF/FAP: **150 m (492 ft)**.\n- **Final** FAF to MAPt: **75 m** (246 ft) with FAF; optimum 3° / 5.2%.\n- **Missed approach** from MAPt: initial, intermediate (to 50 m clearance), final; 2.5% gradient.","checklist":["IAF, IF, FAF/FAP, MAPt labelled","Initial MOC 300 m / 984 ft","Intermediate MOC 150 m / 492 ft","Final MOC 75 m / 246 ft","3° / 5.2% final path","Missed approach phases and 2.5% gradient"],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"Maximum intercept angle at the IF from the initial segment for a non-precision approach?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-3, 3.1.2, 3.1.3","ref":"OB15","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["120°","90°","30°","45°"],"a":0,"why":"90° for a precision approach, 120° for an NPA.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-33","type":"recall","q":"Classify NPA, APV and precision approaches. Where do LNAV, LNAV/VNAV and LPV fit?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, Definitions (IAP), Att A 3.3.4.4 to 3.3.4.5.1; OM-A Ch 0","ref":"OB16","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **NPA:** designed for **2D Type A**. **LNAV only** (to an MDA) is an NPA.\n- **APV:** PBN procedure for **3D Type A**. **LNAV/VNAV** is APV with baro-VNAV (chart temperature limits, no remote altimeter setting).\n- **Precision:** **ILS, MLS, GLS or SBAS CAT I**, for 3D Type A or B.\n- **LPV** (SBAS): to APV-I criteria it's APV; to **SBAS CAT I criteria it's precision**. No temperature limits.\n- **Type A:** MDH/DH **250 ft or more**; **Type B:** DH below 250 ft.","keys":[{"k":"NPA 2D","m":["2d","two d"]},{"k":"APV 3D type A","m":["apv"]},{"k":"precision incl SBAS CAT I","m":["sbas cat","sbas category","gls","mls"]},{"k":"LNAV/VNAV baro","m":["baro"]},{"k":"LPV can be precision","m":["lpv"]},{"k":"type A/B 250 ft","m":["250"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["lpv is only apv","lpv is always apv"],"why":"Bank says LPV is APV; to SBAS CAT I criteria it is a precision approach"}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"An LPV approach designed to SBAS CAT I criteria is classified as:","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, Att A 3.3.4.5.1","ref":"OB16","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["A precision approach","An APV","A non-precision approach","A 2D Type A operation"],"a":0,"why":"Bank says LPV is APV; Doc 8168 lists SBAS CAT I as precision (Att A 3.3.4.5.1).","topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-10","type":"match","q":"Match the approach to its classification.","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, Att A 3.3.4.4, 3.3.4.5; OM-A Ch 0","ref":"OB16","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["LNAV only","NPA (2D)"],["LNAV/VNAV","APV (baro-VNAV)"],["LPV to APV-I criteria","APV"],["LPV to SBAS CAT I criteria","Precision"],["ILS CAT II","Precision, Type B"]],"why":"Type B is DH below 250 ft (CAT I, II, III per OM-A Ch 0).","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-34","type":"recall","q":"What are the aircraft approach categories and their speeds?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-1, 1.4; Table II-5-1-2; OM-A 7.2.3","ref":"OB17","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"Set by **VAT** (1.3 VSO or 1.23 VS1G at **maximum certificated landing mass**), not the day's VREF.\n- **A** below 91 kt; **B** 91 to 120; **C 121 to 140**; **D** 141 to 165; **E** 166 to 210.\n- **737 MAX: Cat C.** Initial 160 to 240 kt, final 115 to 160, max circling 180.\n- The category is permanent; a permanently lower landing mass with State approval can change it.\n- OM-A 7.2.3 prints D as 141 to below 161 kt; Doc 8168 says below 166. Quote Doc 8168.","keys":[{"k":"VAT","m":["vat","threshold speed","speed at threshold"]},{"k":"max landing mass","m":["max","certificated","landing mass","landing weight"]},{"k":"C 121 to 140","m":["121","140"]},{"k":"737 = Cat C","m":["cat c","category c"]},{"k":"1.3 VSO","m":["1.3","13 vso","1.23"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["161"],"why":"OM-A 7.2.3 prints D as below 161 kt; Doc 8168 says below 166 kt"}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-11","type":"match","q":"Match approach category to its VAT band.","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-1, 1.4.3","ref":"OB17","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["A","below 91 kt"],["B","91 to 120 kt"],["C","121 to 140 kt"],["D","141 to 165 kt"],["E","166 to 210 kt"]],"why":"VAT at maximum certificated landing mass. The B737 is Cat C.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"Upper VAT limit of Category D per Doc 8168?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-1, 1.4.3; OM-A 7.2.3","ref":"OB17","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Below 166 kt (141 to 165)","Below 161 kt (141 to 160)","Below 151 kt","Below 181 kt"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 7.2.3 prints 141 to below 161; Doc 8168 says below 166. Quote Doc 8168 for D; Cat C is identical in both.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"Maximum circling speed for a Cat C aircraft (procedure design)?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, Table II-5-1-2","ref":"OB17","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["180 kt","205 kt","135 kt","160 kt"],"a":0,"why":"A 100, B 135, C 180, D 205 kt.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-35","type":"recall","q":"Apart from the X/Y/Z suffix, how are approach charts named?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, Att B 5.1.4 to 5.3.1; OM-A 45.1.14.1","ref":"OB18","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"The title names **the navaid giving final approach lateral guidance**; if two are used, **only the last one** (NDB as FAF, VOR on final = **VOR RWY 06**).\n- **Combined charts:** types joined by **\"or\"**, max **three** per chart (ILS or LOC RWY 27L).\n- **CAT II/III** shown in the title (ILS RWY 27L CAT II); a plain ILS RWY is CAT I.\n- **Circling-only:** last navaid plus a letter **from A** (VOR-A).\n- Suffix letters (from Z) for different missed approaches, categories or arrivals.","keys":[{"k":"final approach navaid","m":["final approach","final lateral","last navaid","last one"]},{"k":"'or', max three","m":[" or loc","joined by or","three"]},{"k":"CAT II/III in title","m":["cat ii","cat 2","cat iii","cat 3","category 2","category ii"]},{"k":"circling letter from A","m":["vor-a","vor a","letter a","from a","starting a","starting with a"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-16","type":"mcq","q":"A circling-only VOR approach chart is titled:","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, Att B 5.3.1","ref":"OB18; NR13","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["VOR-A","VOR Z","VOR CIRCLING","VOR RWY 00"],"a":0,"why":"Circling-only charts use the last navaid plus a letter starting from A; Z/Y suffixes are for straight-in procedures to the same runway.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-36","type":"recall","q":"Define MRA, MCA and MAA.","src":"Oral bank p.105, p.112; Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-1, 1.10.4","ref":"OB19","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **MRA (Minimum Reception Altitude):** lowest altitude at which an **airway intersection can be identified**.\n- **MCA (Minimum Crossing Altitude):** lowest altitude to **cross a fix when flying towards a higher MEA**. In PANS-OPS a CDFA must clear every step-down fix MCA, and the FAF is never crossed below its MCA.\n- **MAA (Maximum Authorised Altitude):** upper limit, protecting against **co-frequency interference**.","keys":[{"k":"MRA identify intersection","m":["intersection","identif"]},{"k":"MCA higher MEA","m":["higher mea","towards a higher","toward a higher"]},{"k":"MAA maximum","m":["maximum author","maximum altitude"]},{"k":"co-frequency interference","m":["interference","co frequency","cofrequency"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-12","type":"match","q":"Match the abbreviation to its definition.","src":"Oral bank p.105, p.107, p.112; OM-A Ch 0","ref":"OB19","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["MRA","Lowest altitude at which an airway intersection can be identified"],["MCA","Lowest altitude to cross a fix towards a higher MEA"],["MAA","Maximum altitude, protects against co-frequency interference"],["MVA","Lowest altitude for radar vectors, may be below MEA"],["MAPt","Where the missed approach must start if not visual"]],"why":"Oral bank definitions and OM-A Ch 0.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-37","type":"recall","q":"Define DA/DH, MDA/MDH and MAPt.","src":"OM-A Ch 0 defs 5, 15; Oral bank p.107","ref":"OB19","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **DA/DH:** for **3D** approaches. **DA referenced to MSL**, **DH to the threshold** elevation.\n- **MDA/MDH:** for **2D** approaches and **circling**. MDH referenced to **aerodrome elevation**, or threshold elevation if that is **more than 7 ft below** aerodrome elevation.\n- **MAPt:** the point where the **missed approach must start** if the required visual reference isn't established.","keys":[{"k":"DA to MSL","m":["msl","mean sea level"]},{"k":"DH to threshold","m":["threshold"]},{"k":"MDA for 2D / circling","m":["2d","circling","non precision"]},{"k":"7 ft rule","m":["7 ft","7ft","7 feet","seven f"]},{"k":"MAPt definition","m":["missed approach must","missed approach point","start the missed","initiate"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-17","type":"mcq","q":"Bank says MEA/MOCA protect '4 NM either side' and minimum IFR altitude is 2000 ft within 4 NM in mountains. What does your OM-A say?","src":"OM-A Ch 0 def 16, 6.1.2","ref":"OB19","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["MEA corridor at least 5 NM each side; own minimum altitudes 2000/1500 ft within 10 NM","Same as the bank: 4 NM either side, 2000/1000 ft within 4 NM","MEA corridor 10 NM each side; 2000/1000 ft within 5 NM","MEA corridor 5 NM each side; 1000 ft within 4 NM everywhere"],"a":0,"why":"The bank's figures are US FAA. OM-A Ch 0 def 16 and 6.1.2.","topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-38","type":"recall","q":"What does a block altitude clearance sound like?","src":"Doc 4444 12.3.2.3","ref":"OB19","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"A block clearance lets you operate anywhere between two levels: **\"MAINTAIN BLOCK (level) TO (level)\"** \\[Doc 4444 12.3.2.3\\].\n- Used e.g. for turbulence or when level cannot be held.\n- You may climb or descend freely within the block without further clearance.\n- Report as required by ATC.","keys":[{"k":"maintain block","m":["maintain block","block"]},{"k":"level to level","m":["level to","fl to","to level"]},{"k":"between two levels","m":["between","anywhere"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-recall-39","type":"recall","q":"On the Jeppesen en-route chart, what do the main symbols and colours mean?","src":"Oral bank p.112, p.113; Doc 4444 4.11.1.1","ref":"OB20","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Altitudes:** MEA over MOCA with **T** (4300/3900T); route MORA with **a**; **G** = GPS MEA; Grid MORA **green below 10,000 ft, maroon/magenta above**.\n- **Points:** **filled triangle = compulsory** reporting point; **open triangle = on request**.\n- **Airway marks:** **X = mileage break**; bar-triangle-bar = MEA change; bent line with two mileages = **changeover point**.\n- **Airspace:** shaded uncontrolled, unshaded controlled.\n- **Airports:** **blue IFR, green VFR**; circle military; anchor water; (H) heliport.\n- Projection **Lambert conformal conic**.","keys":[{"k":"filled triangle compulsory","m":["filled","solid","compulsory"]},{"k":"open triangle on request","m":["open triangle","hollow","on request"]},{"k":"X = mileage break","m":["mileage break"]},{"k":"COP","m":["changeover","change over","cop"]},{"k":"blue IFR / green VFR","m":["blue"]},{"k":"T = MOCA","m":["moca"]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-13","type":"match","q":"Match the Jeppesen en-route symbol to its meaning.","src":"Oral bank p.112, p.113","ref":"OB20","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Filled triangle","Compulsory reporting point"],["Open triangle","On-request reporting point"],["X on the airway","Mileage break"],["Bar-triangle-bar","MEA change"],["Bent line with two mileages","Changeover point"],["Figure with T","MOCA"]],"why":"From the oral bank (Jeppesen legend pending).","topic":6},{"id":"t6-match-14","type":"match","q":"Match the Jeppesen en-route airport/airspace depiction to its meaning.","src":"Oral bank p.112, p.113","ref":"OB20","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Blue airport","IFR"],["Green airport","VFR"],["Shaded airspace","Uncontrolled"],["Unshaded airspace","Controlled"],["Anchor","Water aerodrome"],["(H)","Heliport"]],"why":"From the oral bank (Jeppesen legend pending).","topic":6},{"id":"t6-mcq-18","type":"mcq","q":"Bank says: 'Grid MORA gives 1000 ft terrain clearance over the entire grid'. Correct?","src":"Jeppesen legend; Oral bank p.105","ref":"OB20","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Incomplete: 2000 ft where the highest point is above 5000 ft","Correct as stated","Wrong: it is always 2000 ft","Wrong: it is 3000 ft"],"a":0,"why":"The bank itself gives 2000 ft above 5000 ft on p.105 (NR3, NR39).","topic":6},{"id":"t6-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: 'Show me the en-route minimum altitudes on this chart.'","src":"Dispatch Training Tech Bulletin Nav 1; OM-A Ch 0, 6.4; Jeppesen legend","ref":"Key facts: Minimum flight altitudes (MEA, MOCA, MORA, MSA); NR3; NR39","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What is the figure 4300 above the airway, and 3900T below it?","a":"4300 is the MEA (obstacle clearance plus nav signal across the airway); 3900T is the MOCA (obstacle clearance only).","keys":[{"k":"MEA","m":["mea","en route","enroute"]},{"k":"MOCA","m":["moca","obstruction","obstacle clearance"]}]},{"q":"Why is the MOCA lower, and when would you use it?","a":"It ignores signal reception; may be below radar/nav coverage, so emergency use, e.g. descending out of icing.","keys":[{"k":"no nav signal","m":["signal","reception","coverage"]},{"k":"emergency","m":["emergenc","icing"]}]},{"q":"You divert 25 NM off the airway for weather. What is your minimum altitude?","a":"The Grid MORA for the square (route MORA covers only 10 NM each side): 1000 ft clearance up to 5000 ft terrain, 2000 ft above.","keys":[{"k":"grid MORA","m":["grid mora","grid"]},{"k":"1000/2000 ft","m":["1000","2000"]},{"k":"route MORA only 10 NM","m":["10 nm","10nm","10 nautical"]}]},{"q":"Does that guarantee radio coverage?","a":"No. Only the MEA guarantees nav signal coverage.","keys":[{"k":"no guarantee","m":["not guarantee","no guarantee","doesn't","does not","nope"]},{"k":"only MEA","m":["mea"]}]},{"q":"A very cold day well below ISA, and QNH is low. What do you do?","a":"Apply corrections: 30 ft per hPa low pressure, 4% of height per 10°C below ISA, and tell ATC.","keys":[{"k":"30 ft per hPa","m":["30 ft","30ft","30 feet"]},{"k":"4% per 10°C","m":["4%","4 percent","4 per cent"]},{"k":"tell ATC","m":["atc"]}]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: 'You're cleared to hold at the VOR at FL150.'","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I, II-6-2; FCTM 4.23; FCOM 11.43","ref":"Holding procedures and entries","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What is your maximum holding speed and leg time?","a":"240 kt (above 14,000 to 20,000 ft); 1.5 min outbound legs above 14,000 ft.","keys":[{"k":"240 kt","m":["240"]},{"k":"1.5 min","m":["1.5","one and a half","90 sec","1 and a half"]}]},{"q":"You're arriving in Sector 2. Which entry and how?","a":"Offset (teardrop): at the fix, track 30° from the reciprocal of the inbound track on the holding side, then turn to intercept inbound.","keys":[{"k":"offset/teardrop","m":["offset","teardrop","tear drop"]},{"k":"30 deg","m":["30"]}]},{"q":"When do you start timing?","a":"Over or abeam the fix, whichever is later; if abeam can't be determined, when the outbound turn is complete.","keys":[{"k":"abeam","m":["abeam"]},{"k":"whichever later","m":["later"]}]},{"q":"Moderate turbulence. Can you go faster?","a":"Yes, with ATC clearance: 280 kt or M0.80, whichever is less. On the 737 hold at flaps 1 manoeuvre speed only when clean speed is too high, but stay clean in turbulence.","keys":[{"k":"280 or M0.80","m":["280"]},{"k":"ATC clearance","m":["atc","clearance"]}]},{"q":"The FMC shows BEST SPEED 255 kt. Fly it?","a":"No: BEST SPEED may exceed the regulatory maximum; hold at or below 240 kt here.","keys":[{"k":"don't fly it","m":["not","don't","dont","never"]},{"k":"exceeds max","m":["exceed","maximum","240"]}]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t6-chain-3","type":"chain","q":"Examiner: 'ILS RWY 27, the glideslope fails on the approach.'","src":"OM-A 7.2.7, 7.3.1, 28.2.1, 28.7.1; FCTM 5.55; Doc 8168 Vol I, II-5-2, 2.3.4","ref":"NR20; NR34; NR35","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"Can you continue?","a":"Yes, as a localiser-only (GS out) approach: a non-precision approach using LOC minima.","keys":[{"k":"LOC only","m":["loc","localiser","localizer"]},{"k":"NPA","m":["non precision","npa","nonprecision"]}]},{"q":"How do you fly the vertical profile?","a":"CDFA from the altitude/DME table, or IAN with the G/S prompt OFF (FMA VOR/LOC and G/P); rate about 5 × GS for 3°.","keys":[{"k":"CDFA","m":["cdfa","continuous","constant angle"]},{"k":"IAN / G/S off","m":["ian","g/s","prompt","table"]}]},{"q":"What minimum do you set and why?","a":"DDA = MDA + 50 ft, so the go-around doesn't sink below the MDA; LOC minima (system minimum 250 ft).","keys":[{"k":"MDA + 50","m":["50"]},{"k":"not below MDA","m":["below","sink"]}]},{"q":"Why no 50 ft on the ILS DA?","a":"The DA already includes height loss in the OCA/H (Cat C 71 ft radio, 150 ft pressure altimeter); the MDA has no margin.","keys":[{"k":"height loss built in","m":["height loss","built in","already"]},{"k":"OCA/H","m":["oca","och","margin"]}]},{"q":"And if you don't fly it CDFA?","a":"Add 400 m to the RVR; CDFA is mandatory for AIX NPAs.","keys":[{"k":"+400 m","m":["400"]},{"k":"CDFA mandatory","m":["mandatory","cdfa"]}]}],"topic":6},{"id":"t7-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"What is a repetitive flight plan (RPL)?","src":"Doc 4444 Ch 1; OM-A Ch 0; OM-A 16.0","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"A repetitive flight plan is a flight plan for a series of frequently recurring, regularly operated individual flights with identical basic features, submitted by an operator for retention and repetitive use by ATS units.\n- AIX applies it to flights operated regularly at the same time and day within a set period, such as a summer or winter schedule.\n- Detail is in the Jeppesen ATC section.\n- Where RPL arrangements exist, the normal rule to file a flight plan before departure doesn't apply.","keys":[{"k":"Series of recurring flights","m":["recurring","regularly operated","series of"]},{"k":"Identical basic features","m":["identical","same basic features","same features"]},{"k":"Retained by ATS units","m":["retention","retained","ats unit","kept by ats","stored by ats"]},{"k":"Submitted by the operator","m":["operator"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"When can an RPL be used?","src":"Doc 4444 16.4.1","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Only when all the conditions are met:\n- IFR flights only.\n- Same day(s) of consecutive weeks on at least ten occasions, or every day for at least ten consecutive days.\n- Flight plan elements highly stable.\n- Covers the whole flight, departure to destination.\n- Every ATS authority concerned accepts RPLs; international use needs bilateral, multilateral or regional agreement.","keys":[{"k":"IFR only","m":["ifr"]},{"k":"At least ten occasions / ten days","m":["ten occasions","10 occasions","ten consecutive","10 consecutive","ten days","10 days","ten times","10 times"]},{"k":"Highly stable elements","m":["stable"]},{"k":"Whole flight dep to dest","m":["whole flight","entire flight","departure to destination","complete flight"]},{"k":"All ATS authorities accept / agreement","m":["accept","agreement","bilateral","multilateral"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"How is an RPL submitted, changed or cancelled?","src":"Doc 4444 16.4.3.1, 16.4.3.2, 16.4.3.5, 16.4.4","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Submit:** listings (form or electronic) at least two weeks ahead, as the AIP publishes; operator keeps alternate and Item 19 data available on request.\n- **Permanent change:** amendment listing at least 7 days before it takes effect.\n- **Type/wake category, speed or level change:** notify for each flight no later than 30 min before departure; a level change alone may be given by RTF on first contact.\n- **Callsign, departure aerodrome, route or destination change:** cancel the RPL for that day and file an individual flight plan.\n- **Delay of 30 min or more:** tell the departure ATS unit immediately or the RPL may be cancelled automatically.","keys":[{"k":"Two weeks ahead","m":["two weeks","2 weeks","14 days","fortnight"]},{"k":"Amendments 7 days before","m":["7 days","seven days"]},{"k":"Notify 30 min before departure","m":["30 min","30 minutes","thirty min"]},{"k":"Route/dest change: cancel and file individual FPL","m":["individual flight plan","file a new","cancel the rpl","cancel rpl","fresh flight plan","new flight plan"]},{"k":"Item 19 / alternate data on request","m":["item 19","alternate"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-num-1","type":"num","q":"How far in advance must RPL listings be submitted? (weeks)","src":"Doc 4444 16.4.3.1","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2,"unit":"weeks","why":"At least two weeks ahead, as published in the AIP.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-num-2","type":"num","q":"Permanent RPL changes: amendment listings must reach ATS at least how many days before they take effect?","src":"Doc 4444 16.4.4.1.1","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":7,"unit":"days","why":"Amendment listings at least 7 days before the change takes effect.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-num-3","type":"num","q":"A flight on an RPL is delayed. Beyond what delay past off-block time must the departure ATS unit be told, or the RPL may be cancelled? (min)","src":"Doc 4444 16.4.4.2.3","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":30,"unit":"min","why":"A delay of 30 min or more: tell the departure ATS unit immediately or the RPL may be cancelled automatically.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"An RPL flight needs to change its destination for one day. What do you do?","src":"Doc 4444 16.4.4.2.2","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Cancel the RPL for that day and file an individual flight plan","Notify ATS by RTF on first contact","Notify ATS no later than 30 min before departure","Send an amendment listing 7 days ahead"],"a":0,"why":"A change of callsign, departure aerodrome, route or destination means cancelling the RPL for that day and filing an individual flight plan. Only a level change alone may go by RTF.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"Minimum operating pattern for a flight to qualify for an RPL?","src":"Doc 4444 16.4.1","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Same day(s) of consecutive weeks on at least 10 occasions, or every day for at least 10 consecutive days","Same day(s) of consecutive weeks on at least 5 occasions, or daily for 7 days","At least 20 occasions in a season","Daily for at least 30 consecutive days"],"a":0,"why":"Doc 4444 sets ten occasions on the same day(s) of consecutive weeks, or ten consecutive days.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"Tell me about ATS route designators in the flight plan.","src":"Doc 4444 App 2 Item 15; OM-A 3.4.1(a)","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- In Item 15 a route designator has **2 to 7 characters**, e.g. BCN1, B1, R14, UB10, KODAP2A.\n- A significant point designator has **2 to 5 characters**.\n- Between a lower and an upper route in the same direction, the transition point need not be entered.\n- The rules for the letters themselves are in Annex 11 Appendix 1.\n- AIX: both pilots check the FMS route against the airway designator, including its start and end waypoints.","keys":[{"k":"Route designator 2 to 7 characters","m":["2 to 7","two to seven","seven characters","7 characters"]},{"k":"Significant point 2 to 5","m":["2 to 5","two to five","five characters","5 characters"]},{"k":"Annex 11 Appendix 1","m":["annex 11"]},{"k":"Both pilots check FMS route vs airway","m":["both pilots","start and end waypoint","check the fms","cross check","crosscheck"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"In Item 15 of the ICAO flight plan, how many characters can an ATS route designator have?","src":"Doc 4444 App 2, Item 15","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["2 to 7","2 to 5","3 to 5","1 to 6"],"a":0,"why":"Route designator 2 to 7 characters (e.g. KODAP2A); a significant point designator is 2 to 5.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"What is surface movement radar and how does ATC use it?","src":"Annex 14 9.8.1, 9.8.7, 9.8.8; Doc 4444 8.10.2, 8.7.3.2; Annex 14 Att A 23.2.1","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- Every aerodrome must have a surface movement guidance and control system (SMGCS). SMR is recommended where operations are intended in RVR below 350 m, or where traffic density needs it.\n- SMR should show all aircraft and vehicles on the manoeuvring area. It augments visual observation: monitoring compliance with clearances, confirming the runway is clear, locating traffic.\n- It can give directional taxi information, normally not specific headings.\n- For 2.5 NM final approach spacing the controller must see the runway and exits, visually or by SMR, MLAT or SMGCS.\n- A-SMGCS levels 1 and 2: surveillance and runway incursion alerting; levels 3 and 4 add routing and guidance.","keys":[{"k":"RVR below 350 m","m":["350 m","350m","350 metres","350 meters","350"]},{"k":"Aircraft and vehicles on manoeuvring area","m":["manoeuvring area","maneuvering area","vehicles"]},{"k":"Augments visual observation / runway clear","m":["augment","runway is clear","runway clear","visual observation"]},{"k":"Directional taxi info, not headings","m":["directional","not specific heading","not heading"]},{"k":"A-SMGCS levels","m":["a smgcs","asmgcs","level 1","levels 1","routing","incursion"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-num-4","type":"num","q":"SMR is recommended where operations are intended in RVR below what value? (m)","src":"Annex 14 9.8.7","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":350,"unit":"m","why":"Annex 14 recommends SMR where operations are intended in RVR below 350 m, or where traffic density needs it.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-num-5","type":"num","q":"For reduced final approach spacing, the controller must be able to see the runway and its exits (visually or by SMR/MLAT/SMGCS). What spacing is that? (NM)","src":"Doc 4444 8.7.3.2","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":3,"ans":2.5,"unit":"NM","why":"2.5 NM final approach spacing needs the runway and exits visible, by eye or by SMR, MLAT or SMGCS.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the A-SMGCS levels to what they provide.","src":"Annex 14 Att A 23.2.1; Doc 4444 8.10.2","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","asked":true,"prio":3,"pairs":[["Levels 1 and 2","Surveillance and runway incursion alerting"],["Levels 3 and 4","Adds routing and guidance"],["SMR","Shows all aircraft and vehicles on the manoeuvring area"]],"why":"A-SMGCS levels 1 and 2 give surveillance and incursion alerting; 3 and 4 add routing and guidance.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"AIX: below what take-off RVR (or visibility) must LVP be in force?","src":"OM-A 45.1.1.4, 45.2.8.2","ref":"Key facts: Flight plans, route designators and surface movement radar","prio":2,"o":["550 m RVR or 800 m visibility","400 m RVR or 800 m visibility","350 m RVR or 550 m visibility","800 m RVR or 1500 m visibility"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A requires LVP in force for take-off below 550 m RVR or 800 m visibility; a CAT II/III approach isn't started until cleared with LVP in use.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"What is an ELT? Name the types.","src":"OM-A 0.15","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Equipment that broadcasts distinctive signals on designated frequencies, activated automatically by impact or manually, depending on type.\n- **ELT(AF)** automatic fixed: permanently attached.\n- **ELT(AP)** automatic portable: rigidly attached but readily removable.\n- **ELT(AD)** automatic deployable: deployed and activated by impact (sometimes hydrostatic sensors); manual deployment also provided.\n- **ELT(S)** survival: removable, stowed for ready use, manually activated by survivors.","keys":[{"k":"Distinctive signals on designated frequencies","m":["designated frequenc","distinctive signal","broadcast"]},{"k":"Automatic fixed","m":["automatic fixed","elt af","eltaf"]},{"k":"Automatic portable","m":["automatic portable","elt ap","eltap"]},{"k":"Automatic deployable","m":["deployable","elt ad","eltad"]},{"k":"Survival","m":["survival","elt s","elts"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match the ELT type to its description.","src":"OM-A 0.15","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["ELT(AF)","Automatically activated, permanently attached"],["ELT(AP)","Automatically activated, rigidly attached but readily removable"],["ELT(AD)","Deployed and activated by impact or hydrostatic sensor; manual deployment too"],["ELT(S)","Removable, stowed for ready use, manually activated by survivors"]],"why":"The four ELT types as defined in the OM-A.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-match-3","type":"match","q":"Match the distress frequency to its band.","src":"OM-A 17.54","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["VHF","121.5 MHz"],["UHF","243.0 MHz"],["HF (marine)","500 kHz, 2182 kHz, 8364 kHz"]],"why":"MAYDAY goes on the current VHF frequency or 121.5 MHz; UHF 243.0 MHz; HF 500, 2182 and 8364 kHz.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"Flight plan Item 19, R/ U V E. What does 'U' indicate?","src":"Doc 4444 App 2, Item 19; 9.2.2.4","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["243.0 MHz available","121.5 MHz available","ELT carried","UHF radio with 8.33 kHz spacing"],"a":0,"why":"R/: U = 243.0 MHz, V = 121.5 MHz, E = ELT. ATS passes these to the RCC.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"What are the AIX company rules on 121.5 MHz, the ELT and flight tracking?","src":"OM-A 3.5.1, 5.1.1, 17.33, 1.1.7.3.1, Ch 17 App E; FCOM 5.20, 10.20","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- Radio equipment must allow communication on 121.5 MHz.\n- Monitor 121.5 MHz (VHF 2 recommended) on long over-water flights and flights that require an ELT, unless other VHF duties or equipment prevent it.\n- Crew must know where the ELT, crash axe and torch are and how to use them.\n- If you hear an ELT broadcast, report it to ATC.\n- Flight tracking: ACARS or ADS-B; without coverage report position, speed and altitude at least every 15 min.\n- CVR and DFDR underwater locator beacons last 90 days.","keys":[{"k":"Monitor 121.5 on VHF 2","m":["vhf 2","vhf2","121.5","1215"]},{"k":"Report heard ELT to ATC","m":["report it to atc","report to atc","tell atc","inform atc"]},{"k":"Position report every 15 min without coverage","m":["15 min","15 minutes","fifteen min"]},{"k":"ULB 90 days","m":["90 days","ninety days"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-num-6","type":"num","q":"How long do the CVR and DFDR underwater locator beacons last? (days)","src":"FCOM 5.20, 10.20","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","prio":2,"ans":90,"unit":"days","why":"CVR and DFDR underwater locator beacons last 90 days.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-num-7","type":"num","q":"Flight tracking without ACARS or ADS-B coverage: report position, speed and altitude at least every how many minutes?","src":"OM-A Ch 17 App E","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","prio":2,"ans":15,"unit":"min","why":"Without coverage, report position, speed and altitude at least every 15 min.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"Tell me about the 737 ELT: preflight and the QRH item.","src":"FCOM NP Preliminary Preflight; QRH 1.4","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","prio":3,"a":"- **Preflight:** ELT switch guard closed and ELT light out.\n- **QRH ELT:** the ELT is on; notify ATC if the transmission is erroneous.\n- And if you hear any ELT broadcast in flight, report it to ATC.","keys":[{"k":"Guard closed","m":["guard closed","guard"]},{"k":"ELT light out","m":["light out","light extinguished","light off"]},{"k":"QRH: notify ATC if erroneous","m":["notify atc","tell atc","inform atc","erroneous"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"What are the alerting phases?","src":"Doc 4444 Ch 1, 9.2.2.1","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","prio":1,"a":"Three phases:\n- **INCERFA, Uncertainty:** uncertainty exists as to the safety of the aircraft and its occupants.\n- **ALERFA, Alert:** apprehension exists as to their safety.\n- **DETRESFA, Distress:** reasonable certainty of grave and imminent danger or need for immediate assistance.\n- If no report arrives within a reasonable time after it's expected, ATS tries to get one within 30 min, after which the Uncertainty Phase can be declared.","keys":[{"k":"INCERFA uncertainty","m":["incerfa","uncertainty"]},{"k":"ALERFA alert / apprehension","m":["alerfa","apprehension"]},{"k":"DETRESFA distress / grave and imminent","m":["detresfa","grave and imminent","imminent danger"]},{"k":"30 min before uncertainty","m":["30 min","30 minutes","thirty min"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-order-1","type":"order","q":"Put the alerting phases in order of increasing seriousness.","src":"Doc 4444 Ch 1","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","prio":1,"items":["INCERFA (Uncertainty)","ALERFA (Alert)","DETRESFA (Distress)"],"why":"Uncertainty, then apprehension (Alert), then reasonable certainty of grave and imminent danger (Distress).","topic":7},{"id":"t7-match-4","type":"match","q":"Match the alerting phase to its meaning.","src":"Doc 4444 Ch 1","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","prio":2,"pairs":[["INCERFA","Uncertainty exists as to the safety of the aircraft and occupants"],["ALERFA","Apprehension exists as to their safety"],["DETRESFA","Reasonable certainty of grave and imminent danger or need for immediate assistance"]],"why":"Definitions from Doc 4444 Chapter 1.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-num-8","type":"num","q":"An expected report hasn't arrived. ATS tries to obtain it; after how many minutes can the Uncertainty Phase be declared?","src":"Doc 4444 9.2.2.1","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","prio":2,"ans":30,"unit":"min","why":"ATS tries to get a report within 30 min, after which the Uncertainty Phase can be declared.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"An aircraft with two-way radio and nothing to report should report 'Operations normal' how long after its last contact?","src":"Doc 4444 9.2.1.2","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","prio":3,"o":["20 to 40 min","10 to 20 min","30 to 60 min","Every 60 min"],"a":0,"why":"Doc 4444 gives 20 to 40 min after the last contact.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"What is VDF, and how can ATS help a lost aircraft?","src":"OM-A 0.14, 10.2, 7.3.1 Note 4; Doc 4444 15.4.1.3, 8.6.2.4.2","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **VDF** is VHF direction finding.\n- To help a lost pilot, ATS can use surveillance, a direction-finder, navaids or another aircraft.\n- DF bearings can help identify an aircraft but aren't the sole means unless prescribed.\n- If you intercept a distress call: acknowledge, record the position, take a bearing on the transmission, inform the RCC or ATS; you may proceed to the position.\n- AIX: VDF approaches are not authorised without explicit training.","keys":[{"k":"VHF direction finding","m":["vhf direction","direction finding","direction finder"]},{"k":"Surveillance / navaids / another aircraft","m":["surveillance","another aircraft","navaid","radar"]},{"k":"Not sole means of identification","m":["not the sole","not sole","sole means"]},{"k":"VDF approach needs training","m":["training","not authorised","not authorized"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-order-2","type":"order","q":"You intercept a distress call. Put your actions in order.","src":"OM-A 10.2","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Acknowledge the call","Record the position","Take a bearing on the transmission","Inform the RCC or ATS","Proceed to the position if able"],"why":"OM-A 10.2: acknowledge, record the position, take a bearing, inform the RCC or ATS; you may proceed to the position.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"You see a craft in distress. What do you do?","src":"OM-A 10.0, 10.1, 10.3.1","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- SAR is covered by ICAO Annex 12 and CAR Section 9 Series S Part 1.\n- Keep it in sight and fix its position.\n- Report type, position, UTC, persons, weather, survivors' condition and access route.\n- The first aircraft on scene takes charge until a SAR aircraft arrives.\n- To direct a surface craft: circle it once, cross close ahead at low altitude rocking the wings or opening and closing the throttle.","keys":[{"k":"Keep in sight","m":["in sight","keep sight","keep it in view"]},{"k":"Fix position","m":["fix its position","fix the position","position"]},{"k":"Report details (UTC, persons, weather)","m":["utc","persons","weather","survivors"]},{"k":"First on scene takes charge","m":["first aircraft","takes charge","on scene commander","first on scene"]},{"k":"Annex 12","m":["annex 12"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-order-3","type":"order","q":"Directing a surface craft to a distress position: put the manoeuvres in order.","src":"OM-A 10.3.1","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","prio":3,"items":["Circle the surface craft once","Cross close ahead of it at low altitude","Rock the wings or open and close the throttle while crossing"],"why":"Circle once, then cross close ahead at low altitude rocking the wings or opening and closing the throttle.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"You hear an ELT signal on 121.5 while en route over the sea.","src":"OM-A 1.1.7.3.1, 10.0, 10.1, 10.2; Doc 4444 Ch 1","ref":"Key facts: Search and rescue: ELT, alerting phases, DF","asked":true,"prio":2,"steps":[{"q":"What do you do first?","a":"Report it to ATC; if it is a distress call, acknowledge, record position, take a bearing, inform the RCC or ATS.","keys":[{"k":"Report to ATC","m":["atc","rcc"]}]},{"q":"Which alerting phase would ATS be in when they're certain of grave and imminent danger?","a":"DETRESFA, the Distress phase.","keys":[{"k":"DETRESFA","m":["detresfa","distress"]}]},{"q":"You spot survivors. Your role?","a":"Keep them in sight, fix position, report type, position, UTC, persons, weather, survivors' condition. First aircraft on scene takes charge until a SAR aircraft arrives.","keys":[{"k":"Keep in sight / fix position","m":["sight","position"]},{"k":"Take charge until SAR arrives","m":["take charge","takes charge","first aircraft"]}]},{"q":"Which ICAO Annex and CAR cover SAR?","a":"Annex 12; CAR Section 9 Series S Part 1.","keys":[{"k":"Annex 12","m":["annex 12","12"]},{"k":"CAR Section 9 Series S","m":["series s","section 9"]}]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"Name the ICAO Annexes and their subjects.","src":"Doc 4444 12.2.1, Foreword; OM-A 0.15, 10.0, 35.1, 40.11.8; Annex 14 1.1, 9.3, Foreword, 1.4.4","ref":"Key facts: ICAO Annexes and CAR references","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- 1 Personnel Licensing\n- 2 Rules of the Air\n- 3 Meteorological Service for International Air Navigation\n- 5 Units of measurement\n- 6 Operation of Aircraft\n- 7 Nationality and registration marks\n- 9 Facilitation\n- 10 Aeronautical Telecommunications\n- 11 Air Traffic Services\n- 12 Search and Rescue\n- 13 Aircraft Accident and Incident Investigation\n- 14 Aerodromes\n- 15 Aeronautical Information Services\n- 17 Security; 18 Dangerous goods; 19 Safety Management","keys":[{"k":"Annex 2 Rules of the Air","m":["rules of the air"]},{"k":"Annex 6 Operation of Aircraft","m":["operation of aircraft"]},{"k":"Annex 10 Telecommunications","m":["telecommunication"]},{"k":"Annex 11 ATS","m":["air traffic services","annex 11"]},{"k":"Annex 12 SAR","m":["search and rescue"]},{"k":"Annex 13 Accident investigation","m":["accident","investigation"]},{"k":"Annex 14 Aerodromes","m":["aerodromes","aerodrome"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-match-5","type":"match","q":"Match the ICAO Annex to its subject (set 1).","src":"Doc 4444 12.2.1, 11.4.3.2.3, App 2, 16.6.3; OM-A 40.7.7; Annex 14 1.1","ref":"Key facts: ICAO Annexes and CAR references","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Annex 1","Personnel Licensing"],["Annex 2","Rules of the Air"],["Annex 3","Meteorological Service"],["Annex 6","Operation of Aircraft"],["Annex 7","Nationality and registration marks"],["Annex 9","Facilitation"]],"why":"Annex subjects as stated in Doc 4444, Annex 14 and the OM-A.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-match-6","type":"match","q":"Match the ICAO Annex to its subject (set 2).","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Foreword; Doc 4444 Foreword; OM-A 10.0, 40.11.8; Annex 14 9.3","ref":"Key facts: ICAO Annexes and CAR references","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Annex 10","Aeronautical Telecommunications"],["Annex 11","Air Traffic Services"],["Annex 12","Search and Rescue"],["Annex 13","Aircraft Accident and Incident Investigation"],["Annex 14","Aerodromes"],["Annex 15","Aeronautical Information Services"]],"why":"Annex subjects as stated in Doc 4444, Annex 14 and the OM-A.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-match-7","type":"match","q":"Match the ICAO Annex to its subject (set 3).","src":"OM-A 0.15, 35.1; Annex 14 Foreword, 1.4.4","ref":"Key facts: ICAO Annexes and CAR references","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Annex 5","Units of measurement"],["Annex 17","Security"],["Annex 18","Dangerous goods"],["Annex 19","Safety Management"]],"why":"Annex subjects as stated in the OM-A and Annex 14.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-match-8","type":"match","q":"Match the Annex 10 volume to its subject.","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Foreword","ref":"Key facts: ICAO Annexes and CAR references","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Vol I","Radio Navigation Aids"],["Vol II","Communication Procedures"],["Vol III","Communication Systems"],["Vol IV","Surveillance Radar and Collision Avoidance Systems"],["Vol V","Radio Frequency Spectrum Utilization"]],"why":"From the Annex 10 Vol I Foreword.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"The OM-A cites Annex 6 for which of these?","src":"OM-A 2.1.1, 4.1.2.11, 30.1","ref":"Key facts: ICAO Annexes and CAR references","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Fatigue (Part I), EDTO critical fuel (Attachment C) and GPWS","RFFS category","Units of measurement","Dangerous goods Technical Instructions"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A cites Annex 6 for fatigue (Part I), EDTO critical fuel (Attachment C) and GPWS. RFFS category comes from Annex 14 Table 9-1.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"Where does the RFFS (fire-fighting) category come from?","src":"OM-A 17.1.2.3","ref":"Key facts: ICAO Annexes and CAR references","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Annex 14, Table 9-1","Annex 6, Attachment C","Annex 13, Chapter 5","Annex 11, Appendix 1"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 14 (Aerodromes) Table 9-1 gives the RFFS category.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"Which CARs cover the main operational subjects?","src":"OM-A 0.1.1.1, 2.1.1, 4.1.1.1, 12.1.1, 41.1.1, 40.1.5, 39.0, 7.1, 31.1, 47.1.1, 10.0","ref":"Key facts: ICAO Annexes and CAR references","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"As written in the OM-A:\n- Operations Manual: Section 8 Series O Part VII\n- Fuel policy, nav and comm equipment: Section 8 Series O Part II\n- FDTL: Section 7 Series J Part III\n- EDTO: Section 8 Series S Part I\n- RVSM: Section 8 Series S Part II; PBN: Series S Part IV; EFB: Series S Part VIII\n- AWO/CAT II/III: Section 8 Series C Part I\n- ACAS: Section 2 Series R Part IV\n- Rules of the Air: Section 9 Series C Part I; SAR: Section 9 Series S Part 1","keys":[{"k":"Ops Manual 8 O VII","m":["part vii","part 7","series o"]},{"k":"RVSM 8 S II","m":["rvsm"]},{"k":"PBN 8 S IV","m":["pbn"]},{"k":"EDTO 8 S I","m":["edto"]},{"k":"AWO 8 C I","m":["awo","cat ii","cat 2","all weather"]},{"k":"Section 8 is operations","m":["section 8"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-match-9","type":"match","q":"Match the subject to its CAR (Section 8 set).","src":"OM-A 0.1.1.1, 12.1.1, 4.1.1.1, 41.1.1, 40.1.5, 39.0","ref":"Key facts: ICAO Annexes and CAR references","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Operations Manual","Section 8, Series O, Part VII"],["Fuel policy","Section 8, Series O, Part II"],["EDTO","Section 8, Series S, Part I"],["RVSM","Section 8, Series S, Part II"],["PBN","Section 8, Series S, Part IV"],["EFB","Section 8, Series S, Part VIII"]],"why":"CAR references as written in the OM-A.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-match-10","type":"match","q":"Match the subject to its CAR (other sections).","src":"OM-A 2.1.1, 30.1, 47.1.1, 35.1.1, 17.1.11.2.1, 36.7.1","ref":"Key facts: ICAO Annexes and CAR references","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["Flight duty and fatigue","Section 7, Series J, Part III"],["GPWS","Section 2, Series I, Part VII"],["Rules of the Air","Section 9, Series C, Part I"],["Dangerous goods","Section 11, Series C, Part I"],["Accidents and incidents","Section 5, Series C, Part I"],["Unruly passengers","Section 3, Series M, Part VI"]],"why":"CAR references as written in the OM-A.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-match-11","type":"match","q":"Match the subject to its CAR (Section 9 and misc).","src":"OM-A 6.0, 47.3.7.1, 47.3.6.5.2, 11.0.1, 24.0, 17.3.10.1, 7.1","ref":"Key facts: ICAO Annexes and CAR references","asked":true,"prio":3,"pairs":[["Minimum flight altitudes","Section 9, Series R, Part I"],["Unlawful interference","Section 9, Series E, Part I"],["Communication failure","Section 9, Series D, Part III"],["Training and qualification","Section 8, Series F, Part II"],["Breath analyser","Section 5, Series F, Part III"],["AOM / AWO / CAT II and III","Section 8, Series C, Part I"]],"why":"CAR references as written in the OM-A.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Which CAR covers ACAS?","src":"OM-A 31.1; OM-A 47.3.2 (conflicting note)","ref":"Key facts: ICAO Annexes and CAR references","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Section 2, Series R, Part IV (OM-A 31.1)","Section 2, Series I, Part VII","Section 8, Series S, Part II","Section 9, Series D, Part III"],"a":0,"why":"Quote OM-A 31.1: Section 2 Series R Part IV. A note at OM-A 47.3.2 gives Section 2 Series I Part VIII, which conflicts; 31.1 is the one to quote. Series I Part VII is GPWS.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"Which CAR covers CAT II/III airworthiness (as opposed to AWO operations)?","src":"OM-A 7.1, 45.1.1.1, 45.1.1.4","ref":"Key facts: ICAO Annexes and CAR references","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Section 8, Series B, Part I","Section 8, Series C, Part I","Section 2, Series R, Part IV","Section 8, Series O, Part II"],"a":0,"why":"AOM/AWO/CAT II and III operations are Section 8 Series C Part I; the CAT II/III airworthiness CAR is Section 8 Series B Part I.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between ATC and FIS?","src":"OM-A Ch 0 defs 47, 50, FIS, FIC, FIR definitions; Doc 4444 9.1.3, 8.11 Note, 9.1.4.1","ref":"OB21","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"Both are air traffic services, but they're different services.\n- **ATC** prevents collisions (between aircraft, and with obstructions on the manoeuvring area) and expedites traffic, using clearances.\n- **FIS** gives advice and information for the safe and efficient conduct of flights, and issues no clearances.\n- FIS and alerting service are provided throughout an FIR by a flight information centre; FIS passes SIGMET, AIRMET, special air-reports, volcanic ash, SPECI and amended TAF.\n- FIS doesn't relieve the PIC; any suggested change is the PIC's decision.\n- Advisory service (Class F) sits between: 'advise' or 'suggest', never a clearance.","keys":[{"k":"ATC prevents collisions, expedites","m":["prevent collision","prevents collision","preventing collision","expedite"]},{"k":"ATC issues clearances","m":["clearance"]},{"k":"FIS gives advice and information","m":["advice","information"]},{"k":"FIS no clearances","m":["no clearance","not clearance","cannot clear","doesnt issue clearance","does not issue clearance","no clearances"]},{"k":"PIC responsibility remains","m":["pic","pilot in command","commander"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["part of atc","if atc is busy","when atc is busy"],"why":"Bank error: FIS is a separate air traffic service, provided where ATC isn't, and cannot clear you to land or descend."}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement about FIS is correct?","src":"OM-A Ch 0 def 50; Doc 4444 9.1.4.1.3, 8.11 Note","ref":"OB21","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["FIS is a separate air traffic service that gives advice and information and issues no clearances","FIS is a part of ATC used when ATC is busy","FIS can clear an aircraft to descend in Class G","FIS relieves the PIC of responsibility for any change it suggests"],"a":0,"why":"The bank says 'FIS is a part of ATC, used if ATC is busy': wrong. FIS is a separate air traffic service and cannot clear you to land or descend; the PIC keeps responsibility.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-order-4","type":"order","q":"List the air traffic services from least to most controlling (as the OM-A ATS family).","src":"OM-A Ch 0 def 50; Doc 4444 9.1.4.1","ref":"OB21","asked":true,"prio":3,"items":["Flight information service","Alerting service","Air traffic advisory service","Air traffic control (area, approach, aerodrome)"],"why":"ATS family: FIS, alerting service, air traffic advisory service, ATC. Advisory sits between FIS and ATC.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"Describe airspace classes A to G.","src":"Doc 4444 Ch 1, 9.1.4.1.1, 5.10.1.1 Note; Oral bank p.39","ref":"OB22","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- A to E are controlled; F has air traffic advisory service; G has FIS only.\n- ATC separates IFR from IFR in A to E, IFR from VFR only in B and C, VFR from VFR only in B.\n- **A:** IFR only; all separated. **B:** IFR and VFR, all separated.\n- **C:** IFR separated from all; VFR separated from IFR and given traffic on VFR; VFR 250 kt below 10,000 ft.\n- **E:** IFR controlled; VFR needs no clearance or radio.\n- **F:** IFR advisory plus FIS. **G:** FIS only.","keys":[{"k":"A to E controlled","m":["a to e","controlled"]},{"k":"F advisory","m":["advisory"]},{"k":"G FIS only","m":["fis only","flight information only","g is fis"]},{"k":"VFR from VFR only in B","m":["vfr from vfr","only in b","vfr separated from vfr"]},{"k":"250 kt below 10,000 ft","m":["250 kt","250 knots","250kt","10000"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"Describe Class D airspace in detail, and which classes India uses.","src":"Oral bank p.39; AIP ENR 1.4 (NR11); OM-A 17.48, 17.3.1.6 A, 17.3.1.5 c","ref":"OB22","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Class D:** IFR and VFR allowed. IFR separated from IFR and given traffic on VFR; VFR given traffic on all. 250 kt below 10,000 ft AMSL for both. Clearance and two-way radio for both.\n- **India:** C, D, E, F and G are used; A and B are under consideration (AIP ENR 1.4). C is designated parts of controlled airspace; E route segments outside controlled airspace; F designated route segments outside controlled airspace.\n- **AIX in E, F, G:** file IFR, use advisory service, keep comms, report position, ask for traffic before level changes, look out. Company policy: plan within controlled airspace.\n- **AIX speed:** max 250 kt below 5000 ft AAL; 250 kt recommended below 10,000 ft AAL.","keys":[{"k":"IFR separated from IFR only","m":["ifr from ifr","ifr separated from ifr"]},{"k":"Traffic info on VFR","m":["traffic information","traffic info","given traffic"]},{"k":"Clearance and two-way radio","m":["two way","2 way","radio"]},{"k":"India uses C D E F G","m":["c d e f","cdefg","c to g","class c"]},{"k":"250 kt","m":["250"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["only d e f and g","d e f and g","defg"],"why":"Bank omits C. The AIP extract lists C (designated parts of controlled airspace) as well as D, E, F and G."}],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"Which airspace classes are used in India?","src":"AIP ENR 1.4 (NR11); OM-A 17.48","ref":"OB22","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["C, D, E, F and G (A and B under consideration)","D, E, F and G only","A, C, D, E and G","A to G, all classes"],"a":0,"why":"The bank says only D, E, F and G. The AIP extract also lists C; A and B are under consideration.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"In which airspace classes does ATC separate VFR from VFR?","src":"Doc 4444 5.10.1.1 Note","ref":"OB22","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["B only","B and C","A to E","A, B and C"],"a":0,"why":"IFR from IFR in A to E; IFR from VFR in B and C; VFR from VFR only in B.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"AIX speed limit policy in the terminal area?","src":"OM-A 17.48","ref":"OB22","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Maximum 250 kt below 5000 ft AAL; 250 kt recommended below 10,000 ft AAL","Maximum 250 kt below 10,000 ft AMSL in all classes","Maximum 230 kt below 5000 ft AAL","Maximum 250 kt below 3000 ft AAL; 280 kt below 10,000 ft"],"a":0,"why":"AIX: maximum 250 kt below 5000 ft AAL; 250 kt recommended below 10,000 ft AAL; plus AIP, SID and STAR limits.","topic":7},{"id":"t7-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw a table of airspace classes A to G showing who is separated from whom.","src":"Doc 4444 5.10.1.1 Note; Oral bank p.39","ref":"OB22","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"A grid of classes A to G against flight rules:\n- A: IFR only, all separated.\n- B: IFR and VFR, all separated.\n- C: IFR separated from all; VFR from IFR, traffic info on VFR.\n- D: IFR from IFR, traffic on VFR; VFR gets traffic on all; clearance and radio for both.\n- E: IFR controlled; VFR no clearance or radio.\n- F: advisory for IFR, FIS. G: FIS only.\n- A to E bracketed as controlled; 250 kt below 10,000 ft for VFR in C, and both in D and E.","checklist":["A to E marked controlled","A: IFR only","B: VFR separated from VFR","C: IFR separated from VFR","D: traffic info only between IFR and VFR","E: VFR no clearance","F advisory, G FIS only","250 kt below 10,000 ft noted"],"topic":7},{"id":"t7-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"What is VOLMET?","src":"Doc 4444 9.1.3.5.1 c; OM-A 17.1.5.2; standard theory (channels, ATIS contrast)","ref":"OB23","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- A continuous or frequent broadcast of current METAR and TAF for a group of aerodromes, provided where traffic needs it.\n- D-VOLMET is the data link version.\n- HF for long range, VHF regionally.\n- AIX: monitor destination, destination alternate and en-route alternate weather en route via VOLMET, VHF, company frequency and ACARS.\n- Contrast ATIS: one aerodrome's arrival and departure information, not a group.","keys":[{"k":"Broadcast","m":["broadcast"]},{"k":"METAR and TAF","m":["metar","taf"]},{"k":"Group of aerodromes","m":["group of aerodromes","several aerodromes","group of airports","multiple aerodromes","number of aerodromes"]},{"k":"D-VOLMET data link","m":["d volmet","dvolmet","data link","datalink"]}],"topic":7},{"id":"t8-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"Define TODA, ASDA, clearway and stopway.","src":"Annex 14 Ch 1; 3.6.1 to 3.6.4; 3.7.1, 3.7.3","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **TODA** = TORA + clearway. **ASDA** = TORA + stopway. LDA is for landing.\n- **Clearway:** a defined rectangular area under the control of the appropriate authority, starting at the end of the TORA, no longer than half the TORA, at least 150 m wide (75 m each side of the centreline), ground not above a 1.25% upslope.\n- **Stopway:** same width as the runway, able to support the aeroplane in an abandoned take-off without structural damage.","keys":[{"k":"TODA = TORA + clearway","m":["tora plus clearway","tora + clearway","tora and clearway"]},{"k":"ASDA = TORA + stopway","m":["tora plus stopway","tora + stopway","tora and stopway"]},{"k":"Clearway max half TORA","m":["half the tora","half of tora","half tora","50 percent","50%"]},{"k":"Clearway 150 m wide / 1.25% slope","m":["150 m","150m","150 metres","75 m","125 percent","1.25","125"]},{"k":"Stopway supports aircraft in abandoned TO","m":["without structural damage","abandoned","rejected","support"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-1","type":"num","q":"Maximum length of a clearway as a fraction of TORA? (answer in %)","src":"Annex 14 3.6.1 to 3.6.4","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":50,"unit":"%","why":"Clearway is no longer than half the TORA.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-2","type":"num","q":"Minimum width of a clearway? (m)","src":"Annex 14 3.6.1 to 3.6.4","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":150,"unit":"m","why":"At least 150 m wide, 75 m each side of the extended centreline.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-3","type":"num","q":"Maximum upslope of the ground in a clearway? (%)","src":"Annex 14 3.6.1 to 3.6.4","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":1.25,"unit":"%","why":"Ground in the clearway must not project above a 1.25% upslope.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"What is the width of a stopway?","src":"Annex 14 3.7.1, 3.7.3","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Same width as the runway","At least 150 m","75 m each side of the centreline","Half the runway width"],"a":0,"why":"A stopway has the same width as the runway; 150 m is the clearway width.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"What are the V1 and V2 limits, and what is a balanced field?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **V1** is not less than VMCG and not more than VR.\n- **V2** is at least 1.13 VSR and at least 1.1 VMCA.\n- **Balanced field:** TODR = ASDR, the minimum field length for that weight.","keys":[{"k":"V1 not less than VMCG","m":["vmcg"]},{"k":"V1 not more than VR","m":["vr","rotation"]},{"k":"V2 1.13 VSR","m":["113","1.13"]},{"k":"V2 1.1 VMCA","m":["11 vmca","1.1 vmca","vmca"]},{"k":"Balanced: TODR = ASDR","m":["todr equals asdr","todr = asdr","asdr equals todr","asdr = todr","todr and asdr","equal"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"V2 must be at least:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["1.13 VSR and 1.1 VMCA","1.2 VSR and 1.1 VMCA","1.13 VSR and 1.05 VMCA","1.1 VSR and 1.2 VMCA"],"a":0,"why":"V2 at least 1.13 VSR and at least 1.1 VMCA.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"Describe the take-off segments for a twin, with gradients.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Gross gradients, one engine out:\n- **1st:** gear retracting, more than 0%.\n- **2nd:** gear up, V2, 2.4%. Usually the limiting segment.\n- **3rd:** level acceleration and flap retraction.\n- **Final:** 1.2%.\n- **Net path** = gross minus 0.8%, clearing obstacles by 35 ft.","keys":[{"k":"1st segment positive","m":["more than 0","positive","above zero","greater than 0"]},{"k":"2nd segment 2.4%","m":["2.4","24 percent","two point four"]},{"k":"3rd level acceleration","m":["level","acceleration","flap retraction"]},{"k":"Final 1.2%","m":["1.2","12 percent","one point two"]},{"k":"Net = gross minus 0.8%, 35 ft","m":["0.8","08 percent","point eight","35 ft","35 feet","35ft"]},{"k":"2nd segment limiting","m":["limiting"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-order-1","type":"order","q":"Put the take-off flight path segments in order.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["1st: gear retracting, gradient above 0%","2nd: gear up at V2, 2.4%","3rd: level acceleration and flap retraction","Final: en-route configuration, 1.2%"],"why":"Twin-engine take-off segments.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-4","type":"num","q":"Twin: minimum gross climb gradient in the 2nd segment? (%)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":2.4,"unit":"%","why":"2nd segment, gear up, V2: 2.4% gross. Usually the limiting segment.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-5","type":"num","q":"Twin: minimum gross gradient in the final take-off segment? (%)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":1.2,"unit":"%","why":"Final segment 1.2% gross.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-6","type":"num","q":"Net take-off flight path = gross minus what? (%)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":0.8,"unit":"%","why":"Net = gross minus 0.8% for a twin; the net path clears obstacles by 35 ft.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-7","type":"num","q":"By how much must the net take-off flight path clear obstacles? (ft)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":35,"unit":"ft","why":"Net path clears obstacles by 35 ft.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"Which take-off segment is usually limiting?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["2nd","1st","3rd","Final"],"a":0,"why":"The 2nd segment (gear up, V2, 2.4%) is usually limiting.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the climb requirement (twin) to its gradient.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["2nd segment (gross)","2.4%"],["Final segment (gross)","1.2%"],["Approach climb, OEI, approach config","2.1%"],["Landing climb, all engines, landing config","3.2%"]],"why":"Standard twin-engine climb gradient requirements.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-8","type":"num","q":"Approach climb gradient, one engine inoperative, approach configuration (twin)? (%)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":2.1,"unit":"%","why":"Approach climb OEI: 2.1%.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-9","type":"num","q":"Landing climb gradient, all engines, landing configuration? (%)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":3.2,"unit":"%","why":"Landing climb all engines: 3.2%.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"How do you convert a climb gradient to a rate of climb?","src":"OM-A 17.40","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **fpm = gradient % × ground speed in kt.**\n- 1% is about 60 ft/NM.\n- E.g. 2.4% at 150 kt GS is about 360 fpm.","keys":[{"k":"Gradient times ground speed","m":["ground speed","groundspeed","gs"]},{"k":"1% = 60 ft/NM","m":["60 ft","60ft","60 feet"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-10","type":"num","q":"Climb gradient 3.3% at 200 kt ground speed. Rate of climb? (fpm)","src":"OM-A 17.40","ref":"Key facts: Performance","prio":2,"ans":660,"unit":"fpm","tol":10,"why":"fpm = gradient % x GS = 3.3 x 200 = 660 fpm.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"Give the wake turbulence categories and the radar separation behind a Heavy.","src":"OM-A 17.3.7","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Heavy:** 136 t or more. **Medium:** 7 to 136 t (the B737). **Light:** 7 t or less.\n- Radar separation behind a Heavy: Heavy 4 NM, Medium 5 NM, Light 6 NM.","keys":[{"k":"Heavy 136 t","m":["136"]},{"k":"Light 7 t","m":["7 t","7t","7 tonnes","7000"]},{"k":"737 is Medium","m":["medium"]},{"k":"4 / 5 / 6 NM","m":["4 5 6","5 nm","5nm","6 nm","4 nm"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-match-2","type":"match","q":"Radar wake separation behind a Heavy: match the follower to the distance.","src":"OM-A 17.3.7","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Heavy behind Heavy","4 NM"],["Medium behind Heavy","5 NM"],["Light behind Heavy","6 NM"]],"why":"OM-A wake separation behind a Heavy.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-11","type":"num","q":"Minimum mass for the Heavy wake category? (t)","src":"OM-A 17.3.7","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":136,"unit":"t","why":"Heavy: 136 t or more. Medium 7 to 136 t. Light 7 t or less.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"What are the AIX rules for contaminated runways, and what is GRF?","src":"OM-A 17.21, 17.29","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- No reduced (assumed temperature) thrust on contaminated runways.\n- Standing water: more than 25% of the area covered by more than 3 mm.\n- V1 may be reduced to minimum V1 for more stopping margin; maximum take-off power obtainable.\n- **GRF:** runway condition codes RWYCC 0 to 6 by runway thirds, reported in the RCR.","keys":[{"k":"No reduced thrust","m":["no reduced","no assumed","no atm","not allowed","full thrust","no derate"]},{"k":"25% covered by >3 mm","m":["25","3 mm","3mm"]},{"k":"RWYCC 0 to 6","m":["rwycc","0 to 6","zero to six"]},{"k":"By runway thirds","m":["thirds","third"]},{"k":"RCR","m":["rcr","runway condition report"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"When is a runway reported as having standing water?","src":"OM-A 17.21","ref":"Key facts: Performance","prio":2,"o":["More than 25% of the area covered by more than 3 mm","More than 50% covered by more than 3 mm","More than 25% covered by more than 13 mm","Any area covered by more than 1 mm"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A: standing water is more than 25% of the area covered by more than 3 mm.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"Under GRF, how are runway conditions reported?","src":"OM-A 17.29","ref":"Key facts: Performance","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["RWYCC 0 to 6 for each runway third, in the RCR","Braking action good/medium/poor for the whole runway","Friction coefficient 0.1 to 0.5 per runway half","RWYCC 1 to 5 for touchdown zone only"],"a":0,"why":"GRF uses RWYCC 0 to 6 by runway thirds, reported in the Runway Condition Report.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"What are ACN and PCN?","src":"Annex 14 Ch 1, 2.6.2, 2.6.3, 2.6.8","ref":"Key facts: ACN/PCN and pavement strength","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **ACN:** a number expressing the relative effect of an aircraft on a pavement for a specified standard subgrade category. Calculated at the CG giving the critical load on the critical gear, normally aft CG at maximum apron (ramp) mass.\n- **PCN:** a number expressing the bearing strength of a pavement for unrestricted operations.\n- ACN equal to or less than PCN: the aircraft can use the pavement, subject to any tyre pressure or all-up mass limit.\n- Required for apron mass above 5700 kg; at or below that, max allowable mass and tyre pressure are reported instead.","keys":[{"k":"ACN: effect of aircraft on pavement","m":["effect","aircraft classification"]},{"k":"PCN: bearing strength of pavement","m":["bearing strength","pavement strength","pavement classification"]},{"k":"ACN <= PCN to operate","m":["less than or equal","equal to or less","acn less than pcn","less than pcn","not exceed"]},{"k":"Aft CG, max ramp mass","m":["aft cg","ramp mass","apron mass","maximum apron"]},{"k":"5700 kg threshold","m":["5700"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-12","type":"num","q":"ACN-PCN reporting is required for pavements used by aircraft with an apron mass above what? (kg)","src":"Annex 14 2.6.2, 2.6.8","ref":"Key facts: ACN/PCN and pavement strength","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":5700,"unit":"kg","why":"Above 5700 kg ACN-PCN; at or below 5700 kg the aerodrome reports max allowable mass and tyre pressure (e.g. 4 000 kg/0.50 MPa).","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"At what CG and mass is an aircraft's ACN calculated?","src":"Annex 14 Ch 1","ref":"Key facts: ACN/PCN and pavement strength","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["The CG giving the critical load on the critical gear, normally aft CG at maximum apron (ramp) mass","Forward CG at MTOW","Mid CG at MLW","Aft CG at MZFW"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 14 Ch 1: ACN is calculated at the CG that gives the critical load on the critical gear, normally aft CG at maximum ramp mass.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"Read me 'PCN 80/R/B/W/T' and name the five parts of a PCN report.","src":"Annex 14 2.6.6, Example 1; 2.6.3","ref":"Key facts: ACN/PCN and pavement strength","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Strength 80, rigid pavement, medium-strength subgrade, no tyre pressure limit, technically evaluated.\n- **1 PCN** number: bearing strength.\n- **2 Pavement type:** R rigid / F flexible.\n- **3 Subgrade:** A high, B medium, C low, D ultra low.\n- **4 Tyre pressure:** W unlimited, X 1.75 MPa, Y 1.25 MPa, Z 0.50 MPa.\n- **5 Evaluation:** T technical / U using aircraft experience.\nThen compare the aircraft's ACN for rigid pavement on subgrade B: 80 or less means unrestricted use.","keys":[{"k":"R = rigid","m":["rigid"]},{"k":"B = medium subgrade","m":["medium"]},{"k":"W = no tyre pressure limit","m":["unlimited","no limit","no tyre pressure","no tire pressure","no pressure limit"]},{"k":"T = technical evaluation","m":["technical"]},{"k":"Compare ACN rigid subgrade B","m":["acn","80 or less","compare"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-order-2","type":"order","q":"Put the parts of a PCN report in their published order.","src":"Annex 14 2.6.6","ref":"Key facts: ACN/PCN and pavement strength","asked":true,"prio":1,"items":["PCN number","Pavement type (R/F)","Subgrade strength (A to D)","Tyre pressure (W to Z)","Evaluation method (T/U)"],"why":"e.g. PCN 80/R/B/W/T.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-match-3","type":"match","q":"PCN tyre pressure category: match code to limit.","src":"Annex 14 2.6.6","ref":"Key facts: ACN/PCN and pavement strength","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["W","Unlimited"],["X","1.75 MPa"],["Y","1.25 MPa"],["Z","0.50 MPa"]],"why":"Annex 14 2.6.6 tyre pressure categories.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-match-4","type":"match","q":"PCN subgrade category: match code to strength and value.","src":"Annex 14 2.6.6","ref":"Key facts: ACN/PCN and pavement strength","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["A","High: K 150 MN/m3, CBR 15"],["B","Medium: K 80 MN/m3, CBR 10"],["C","Low: K 40 MN/m3, CBR 6"],["D","Ultra low: K 20 MN/m3, CBR 3"]],"why":"Rigid pavements use K; flexible pavements use CBR.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"In a PCN report, what does 'U' in the last position mean?","src":"Annex 14 2.6.6","ref":"Key facts: ACN/PCN and pavement strength","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Evaluated using aircraft experience","Unlimited tyre pressure","Ultra-low subgrade","Unpaved surface"],"a":0,"why":"Evaluation: T technical evaluation, U using aircraft experience. Unlimited tyre pressure is W; ultra-low subgrade is D.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"What if the ACN is greater than the PCN?","src":"Annex 14 2.6.7; Att A 20.1","ref":"Key facts: ACN/PCN and pavement strength","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"Annex 14 recommends that States set criteria; Attachment A gives a simple overload method:\n- **Flexible pavement:** occasional movements with ACN up to 10% above PCN.\n- **Rigid or composite:** up to 5% above PCN (PCN 80/R allows ACN up to 84). Unknown structure: use 5%.\n- Overload movements no more than about 5% of total annual movements.\n- Not normally allowed on pavement showing distress, during thaw, or when water may weaken the subgrade.\n- Tolerated because pavements don't fail suddenly; occasional small overloads only shorten pavement life.","keys":[{"k":"Flexible 10%","m":["10 percent","10%","ten percent"]},{"k":"Rigid 5%","m":["5 percent","5%","five percent"]},{"k":"~5% of annual movements","m":["annual","movements"]},{"k":"Not on distressed pavement / thaw","m":["distress","thaw","water"]},{"k":"Shortens pavement life","m":["shorten","pavement life","life"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-13","type":"num","q":"Overload on a rigid pavement with PCN 80/R: maximum ACN for occasional movements under the Annex 14 Attachment A method?","src":"Annex 14 Att A 20.1","ref":"Key facts: ACN/PCN and pavement strength","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":84,"why":"Rigid or composite: up to 5% above PCN, so 80 x 1.05 = 84.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"Annex 14 Attachment A overload guidance for a flexible pavement?","src":"Annex 14 Att A 20.1","ref":"Key facts: ACN/PCN and pavement strength","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Occasional movements with ACN up to 10% above PCN","Up to 5% above PCN","Up to 15% above PCN","No overload permitted"],"a":0,"why":"Flexible 10%; rigid or composite 5%; unknown structure 5%.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Where does an AIX crew find runway strength?","src":"OM-A Ch 0, 24.7","ref":"Key facts: ACN/PCN and pavement strength","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["Airport Data section of the Jeppesen manual","OM-A Chapter 14","FCOM Limitations","Company NOTAMs only"],"a":0,"why":"ACN and PCN appear only in the OM-A abbreviations; runway strength comes from the Jeppesen Airport Data section.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"What is a cold-soaked wing, and what factors affect it?","src":"OM-A 0.15, 15.2, 15.3, 17.3.6.2","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- The wings hold very cold fuel, just after a high-altitude flight or after refuelling with very cold fuel.\n- With visible moisture or high humidity, ice or frost can form at ambient -2°C to +15°C if the structure stays at 0°C or below.\n- Factors: fuel temperature and quantity, tank type and location, time at altitude, temperature of uplifted fuel, time since refuelling.\n- Clear ice is very hard to see, especially in poor light or on wet wings, so a special check may be needed in rain or high humidity.\n- OM-A: only 3 mm of frost on the underside of the wing tank area is acceptable.","keys":[{"k":"Very cold fuel after high-altitude flight","m":["cold fuel","high altitude","after flight"]},{"k":"-2 to +15 °C","m":["15","minus 2","-2"]},{"k":"Structure at or below 0 °C","m":["0 degrees","zero degrees","below freezing","0 c","0c"]},{"k":"Clear ice hard to see","m":["clear ice","hard to see","difficult to see","hard to detect"]},{"k":"3 mm underside","m":["3 mm","3mm","1/8","one eighth"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"Cold-soaked wings: frost can form at what ambient temperature range if the structure stays at or below 0°C?","src":"OM-A 0.15","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["-2°C to +15°C","-10°C to +5°C","0°C to +10°C","-5°C to +20°C"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 0.15: -2°C to +15°C with visible moisture or high humidity.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"When can a 737 take off with cold-soaked fuel frost (CSFF) on the wings?","src":"FCOM SP.3, SP.3.1","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Lower wing:** allowed when OAT at or above +4°C, no precipitation or visible moisture (fog under 1 mile counts), tank fuel at or above -16°C, and leading edge devices, control and tab surfaces, winglets and balance panel cavities clean. Up to 1/8 in (3 mm); more than 3 mm: de-ice.\n- **Upper wing, painted CSFF area:** frost only inside the painted lines, same conditions; otherwise remove all upper-wing contamination.\n- **Upper wing, no painted area:** not allowed; de-ice.\n- The painted area and the FCOM procedure don't by themselves give approval; regulatory approval is needed.","keys":[{"k":"OAT +4 °C or above","m":["4 degrees","plus 4","+4","4c","4 c"]},{"k":"Fuel -16 °C or above","m":["16"]},{"k":"No precipitation / visible moisture","m":["precipitation","visible moisture","moisture"]},{"k":"3 mm / 1/8 in lower wing","m":["3 mm","3mm","1/8","one eighth","18 inch"]},{"k":"Upper only in painted area","m":["painted"]},{"k":"Regulatory approval needed","m":["approval"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-14","type":"num","q":"737 CSFF: minimum OAT for take-off with cold-soaked fuel frost on the lower wing? (°C)","src":"FCOM SP.3","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":4,"unit":"°C","why":"OAT at or above +4°C, no precipitation or visible moisture, fuel at or above -16°C.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-15","type":"num","q":"737 CSFF: minimum tank fuel temperature for take-off with CSFF? (°C)","src":"FCOM SP.3","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":-16,"unit":"°C","why":"Tank fuel at or above -16°C.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-16","type":"num","q":"Maximum CSFF thickness allowed on the lower wing (critical surfaces clean)? (mm)","src":"FCOM SP.3; OM-A 17.3.6.2","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":3,"unit":"mm","why":"Up to 1/8 in (3 mm); more than 3 mm: de-ice.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"737 with no painted CSFF area on the upper wing, frost present there. Can you take off?","src":"FCOM SP.3","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["No, de-ice","Yes, if under 3 mm","Yes, if OAT is above +4°C","Yes, if fuel is above -16°C"],"a":0,"why":"Upper wing without a painted CSFF area: not allowed; de-ice.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"What are the 737 rules for a cold-soaked engine on the ground?","src":"FCOM SP.16.4, SP.16.8","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- Soaked 1 h or more below -40°C: don't start or motor the engine; maintenance heats the HMU (3 h or more: also service the starter).\n- Oil pressure may read high for several minutes and OIL FILTER BYPASS may light. Stay at idle until normal; if it stays high once oil temperature stabilises, shut down.\n- Oil temperature at least 31°C before take-off thrust.\n- Idle time: about 3 min at 0°C, 6 min at -18°C, 9 min at -40°C, 10 min at -54°C.","keys":[{"k":"1 h below -40 °C: no start","m":["40","one hour","1 h","1 hour"]},{"k":"Heat the HMU","m":["hmu","heat"]},{"k":"High oil pressure / filter bypass","m":["oil pressure","filter bypass","bypass"]},{"k":"Oil temp 31 °C before TO thrust","m":["31"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-17","type":"num","q":"737: minimum oil temperature before setting take-off thrust after a cold-soaked start? (°C)","src":"FCOM SP.16.4, SP.16.8","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","prio":2,"ans":31,"unit":"°C","why":"Oil temperature at least 31°C before take-off thrust.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-match-5","type":"match","q":"Cold-soaked 737 engine: match OAT to approximate idle warm-up time.","src":"FCOM SP.16.4, SP.16.8","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","prio":3,"pairs":[["0°C","About 3 min"],["-18°C","About 6 min"],["-40°C","About 9 min"],["-54°C","About 10 min"]],"why":"Idle time needed to reach 31°C oil temperature before take-off thrust.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"What are the 737 fuel temperature limits, and what do you do if fuel gets cold in cruise?","src":"FCOM L.10.11; FCTM 4.9, 4.10; QRH 12.16","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Minimum:** -43°C or 3°C above the fuel freezing point, whichever is higher. **Maximum** 49°C. Fuel icing inhibitor doesn't change the minimum.\n- Freezing point: spec maximum -40°C for Jet A, -47°C for Jet A-1; use spec value unless measured; mixed fuel: most conservative until 3 consecutive refuellings.\n- Fuel cools about 3°C per hour (up to 12°C per hour in extremes).\n- To raise TAT: change level, deviate, or increase Mach (each 0.01 Mach adds 0.5 to 0.7°C TAT). Fuel takes 15 min to 1 h to respond. May need to descend as low as FL250.","keys":[{"k":"-43 °C","m":["43"]},{"k":"3 °C above freezing point","m":["3 degrees above","3c above","3 above","three degrees above"]},{"k":"Jet A-1 -47 °C","m":["47"]},{"k":"Increase Mach / descend / deviate","m":["increase mach","descend","deviate","change level","speed up"]},{"k":"Max 49 °C","m":["49"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-18","type":"num","q":"737 minimum fuel tank temperature (absolute floor, ignoring the freezing point rule)? (°C)","src":"FCOM L.10.11","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":-43,"unit":"°C","why":"-43°C or 3°C above the fuel freezing point, whichever is higher.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-19","type":"num","q":"737 maximum fuel tank temperature? (°C)","src":"FCOM L.10.11","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","prio":2,"ans":49,"unit":"°C","why":"Maximum fuel temperature 49°C.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-20","type":"num","q":"Specification maximum freezing point of Jet A-1? (°C)","src":"FCTM 4.9, 4.10","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":-47,"unit":"°C","why":"Jet A-1 -47°C; Jet A -40°C.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-21","type":"num","q":"Specification maximum freezing point of Jet A? (°C)","src":"FCTM 4.9, 4.10","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":-40,"unit":"°C","why":"Jet A -40°C; Jet A-1 -47°C.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-22","type":"num","q":"Jet A-1 loaded (freezing point not measured). What is the effective 737 minimum fuel temperature? (°C)","src":"FCOM L.10.11; FCTM 4.9, 4.10","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"ans":-43,"unit":"°C","why":"Higher of -43°C and (-47 + 3) = -44°C, so -43°C.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-23","type":"num","q":"Jet A loaded (spec freezing point used). What is the effective 737 minimum fuel temperature? (°C)","src":"FCOM L.10.11; FCTM 4.9, 4.10","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"ans":-37,"unit":"°C","why":"Higher of -43°C and (-40 + 3) = -37°C, so -37°C.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"Each 0.01 Mach increase raises TAT by roughly how much?","src":"FCTM 4.10","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","prio":3,"o":["0.5 to 0.7°C","1 to 2°C","0.1 to 0.2°C","3°C"],"a":0,"why":"FCTM: each 0.01 Mach adds 0.5 to 0.7°C TAT; fuel takes 15 min to 1 h to respond.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"Mixed fuel types in the tank, freezing point not measured. Which freezing point do you use?","src":"FCTM 4.9, 4.10","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","prio":3,"o":["The most conservative (highest) value until 3 consecutive refuellings","The average of the two","The value of the fuel just loaded","Jet A-1 value, always"],"a":0,"why":"FCTM: for mixed fuel use the most conservative value until 3 consecutive refuellings.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"How does cold soak relate to EDTO and threshold time?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.23, 4.1.3, 4.2.4, 4.6.2.5","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Threshold time:** time to an en-route alternate beyond which EDTO approval is needed: 60 min for twins and for the 737-8 MAX.\n- Where the APU needn't run during EDTO, the operator needs a programme for cold-soak in-flight APU start reliability.\n- 737: check start about 20 min before descent on inbound sectors to India, after at least 1 h 30 min cruise 'to ensure adequate cold soak'; APU runs 5 min, two further start attempts allowed.","keys":[{"k":"Threshold 60 min","m":["60 min","60 minutes","sixty min","one hour"]},{"k":"APU in-flight start reliability","m":["apu"]},{"k":"20 min before descent","m":["20 min","20 minutes","twenty min"]},{"k":"After 1 h 30 min cruise","m":["1 h 30","90 min","1.5 hour","one and a half","1 hour 30"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-24","type":"num","q":"EDTO threshold time for the 737-8 MAX? (min)","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.23, 4.1.3","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":60,"unit":"min","why":"60 min for twins and for the 737-8 MAX.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"You arrive at the aircraft after a long high-altitude sector, light drizzle, OAT +6°C. There's frost under the wing.","src":"OM-A 0.15, 15.9.5; FCOM SP.3","ref":"Key facts: Cold soak","asked":true,"prio":2,"steps":[{"q":"What is this and why has it formed?","a":"Cold-soaked fuel frost: very cold fuel keeps the wing structure at or below 0°C, so frost forms at -2 to +15°C with moisture.","keys":[{"k":"Cold-soaked fuel","m":["cold soak","cold fuel"]},{"k":"Structure at or below 0","m":["0","freezing"]}]},{"q":"Can you go with it on the lower wing?","a":"Not with drizzle: lower-wing CSFF needs no precipitation or visible moisture, OAT at or above +4°C, fuel at or above -16°C, critical surfaces clean. Up to 3 mm then; more than 3 mm: de-ice.","keys":[{"k":"No precipitation","m":["precipitation","moisture","drizzle"]},{"k":"3 mm","m":["3 mm","3mm","1/8"]}]},{"q":"What about the upper wing?","a":"Only inside a painted CSFF area, with regulatory approval; without a painted area: de-ice.","keys":[{"k":"Painted area","m":["painted"]},{"k":"De-ice otherwise","m":["de ice","deice","remove"]}]},{"q":"After de-icing, can you use assumed temperature thrust?","a":"No: AIX bans ATM after de-icing/anti-icing; a derate is acceptable.","keys":[{"k":"No ATM","m":["no","not"]},{"k":"Derate OK","m":["derate"]}]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"Forward versus aft CG: what's the effect on take-off performance?","src":"OM-A 14.1.1, 14.1.4; FCTM 3.5, 3.15, 4.6; FCOM PI.10.5","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- CG must stay within forward and aft limits at taxi, take-off, in flight and landing; limits from the C of A or Flight Manual.\n- **Forward CG:** standard data and FMC/QRH speeds assume the most forward CG limit. More nose-up trim (e.g. 8 units at 6% MAC, 60 t, flaps 1/5).\n- **Aft CG:** less nose-up trim, more lift at a given AoA: lower VR and V2, shorter field length, higher field-limited weight, usually less drag and better climb. Fewer trim units (3 at 36% MAC).\n- **Aft CG at light weight:** nose can pitch up, nosewheel steering weakens: forward column pressure to 80 kt, reduced thrust, rolling take-off.\n- Cruise CG entry can change maximum altitude capability.","keys":[{"k":"Data assume forward CG","m":["most forward","forward cg limit","assume forward"]},{"k":"Aft: lower VR/V2, shorter field","m":["lower vr","lower v2","shorter","lower speeds"]},{"k":"Aft: less drag, better climb","m":["less drag","better climb","less trim drag"]},{"k":"Light aft: nose pitch-up, column forward to 80 kt","m":["80 kt","80 knots","pitch up","nosewheel steering","forward pressure"]},{"k":"Forward: more nose-up trim","m":["more nose up","more trim","more units"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"737 FMC/QRH take-off speeds assume which CG?","src":"FCTM 3.5","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["The most forward CG limit","The most aft CG limit","25% MAC","The actual CG entered on TAKEOFF REF"],"a":0,"why":"Standard data and FMC/QRH speeds assume the most forward CG limit, which is conservative.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"What does an alternate forward CG limit allow, and what's the catch?","src":"FCTM 3.15","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload","prio":3,"o":["Higher performance-limited TOW or more thrust reduction; needs AFM data and regulatory approval, and FMC/QRH speeds are invalid","A more forward CG for heavier cargo; no approval needed","Lower V2 with standard FMC speeds","Extended aft limit for light weight"],"a":0,"why":"FCTM 3.15: alternate forward CG limit gives higher TOW or more thrust reduction, needs AFM data and regulatory approval; FMC/QRH speeds then invalid.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-16","type":"mcq","q":"737 take-off trim at 60 t, flaps 1/5: roughly how many units at 6% MAC versus 36% MAC?","src":"FCOM PI.10.5","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["8 units forward, 3 units aft","3 units forward, 8 units aft","5 units at both","12 units forward, 6 aft"],"a":0,"why":"FCOM PI.10.5 example: 8 units at 6% MAC, 3 units at 36% MAC. Forward CG needs more nose-up trim.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-17","type":"mcq","q":"737 flaps up: stabiliser trim range, manual wheel versus electric?","src":"FCOM Flight Controls","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload","prio":3,"o":["Manual -0.70 to 16.4 units; electric 3.85 to 14.0 units","Manual 0 to 17 units; electric 0 to 15 units","Both 3.85 to 14.0 units","Manual 3.85 to 14.0 units; electric -0.70 to 16.4 units"],"a":0,"why":"At extreme CGs the manual trim wheels give more range than electric trim.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"How is take-off trim set from the CG, and why does it matter?","src":"FCOM NP Amplified; FCOM Flight Controls; FCTM 8.46","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- Enter the CG on TAKEOFF REF; the FMC computes take-off trim. Set it and check it's in the green band.\n- Outside the green band an intermittent horn sounds when take-off is attempted.\n- A stabiliser mistrimmed from wrong weights or CG is a tail strike risk, so challenge load sheet numbers.","keys":[{"k":"CG entered on TAKEOFF REF","m":["takeoff ref","take off ref","fmc"]},{"k":"Green band","m":["green band"]},{"k":"Intermittent horn","m":["horn"]},{"k":"Tail strike risk","m":["tail strike","tailstrike"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"Define DOW, traffic load, ZFW, TOW and LW.","src":"OM-A 14.1.1","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **DOW:** total weight ready for the operation, excluding usable fuel and traffic load.\n- **Traffic load:** passengers, baggage and cargo, including non-revenue load.\n- **ZFW** = DOW + traffic load.\n- **TOW** = ZFW + take-off fuel.\n- **LW** = TOW - trip fuel.","keys":[{"k":"DOW excludes usable fuel and traffic load","m":["excluding usable fuel","without fuel","excludes fuel","usable fuel"]},{"k":"ZFW = DOW + traffic load","m":["dow plus traffic","dow + traffic","dow and traffic"]},{"k":"TOW = ZFW + take-off fuel","m":["zfw plus","zfw + ","zfw and"]},{"k":"LW = TOW - trip fuel","m":["minus trip","less trip","trip fuel"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-match-6","type":"match","q":"737-8 structural limits: match limit to value.","src":"FCOM L.10.5","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["MTOW","81,419 or 82,644 kg (by aircraft)"],["MLW","69,308 kg"],["MZFW","65,952 kg"]],"why":"FCOM L.10.5 737-8 structural weight limits.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-25","type":"num","q":"737-8 MLW? (kg)","src":"FCOM L.10.5","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":69308,"unit":"kg","why":"MLW 69,308 kg.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-26","type":"num","q":"737-8 MZFW? (kg)","src":"FCOM L.10.5","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":65952,"unit":"kg","why":"MZFW 65,952 kg.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"What is the limiting take-off mass?","src":"OM-A 14.1.7.9","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"The least of:\n- runway RTOW (performance-limited take-off weight);\n- the en-route performance limit;\n- MLW plus burn to destination;\n- the landing distance limit at destination and alternate.\nAnd structurally, payload is what's left of the lowest limiting mass after DOW and fuel: check against MZFW, MTOW and MLW.","keys":[{"k":"Runway RTOW","m":["rtow","runway"]},{"k":"En-route limit","m":["en route","enroute"]},{"k":"MLW + trip burn","m":["mlw plus","landing weight plus","mlw +","burn"]},{"k":"Landing distance at dest/alternate","m":["landing distance","alternate"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"Work the allowed traffic load for the OM-A sample 737-8 load sheet: DOW 44,555, take-off fuel 7,800, trip 5,000.","src":"OM-A 14.4","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload (worked example)","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"Allowed traffic load is the lowest of:\n- MZFW: 65,952 - 44,555 = **21,397 kg**\n- MTOW: 81,419 - 44,555 - 7,800 = 29,064 kg\n- MLW: 69,308 - 44,555 - 2,800 landing fuel = 21,953 kg\nSo the flight is ZFW-limited at 21,397 kg. Actual traffic load 9,450 kg (110 pax at 75 kg = 8,250 plus 1,200 in the holds), so underload = 11,947 kg.","keys":[{"k":"MZFW limit 21,397","m":["21397"]},{"k":"MTOW limit 29,064","m":["29064"]},{"k":"MLW limit 21,953","m":["21953"]},{"k":"ZFW-limited","m":["zfw limited","mzfw limited","zero fuel"]},{"k":"Underload 11,947","m":["11947"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-27","type":"num","q":"Load sheet: MZFW 65,952 kg, DOW 44,555 kg. Allowed traffic load by MZFW? (kg)","src":"OM-A 14.4","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload (worked example)","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":21397,"unit":"kg","why":"65,952 - 44,555 = 21,397 kg.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-28","type":"num","q":"Load sheet: MTOW 81,419 kg, DOW 44,555 kg, take-off fuel 7,800 kg. Allowed traffic load by MTOW? (kg)","src":"OM-A 14.4","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload (worked example)","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":29064,"unit":"kg","why":"81,419 - 44,555 - 7,800 = 29,064 kg.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-29","type":"num","q":"Load sheet: MLW 69,308 kg, DOW 44,555 kg, take-off fuel 7,800 kg, trip fuel 5,000 kg. Allowed traffic load by MLW? (kg)","src":"OM-A 14.4","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload (worked example)","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":21953,"unit":"kg","why":"Landing fuel = 7,800 - 5,000 = 2,800 kg. 69,308 - 44,555 - 2,800 = 21,953 kg.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-30","type":"num","q":"Load sheet: 110 passengers at 75 kg plus 1,200 kg in the holds. Traffic load? (kg)","src":"OM-A 14.4","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload (worked example)","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":9450,"unit":"kg","why":"110 x 75 = 8,250 + 1,200 = 9,450 kg.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-31","type":"num","q":"Allowed traffic load 21,397 kg, actual traffic load 9,450 kg. Underload? (kg)","src":"OM-A 14.4","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload (worked example)","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":11947,"unit":"kg","why":"21,397 - 9,450 = 11,947 kg, matching the sample sheet.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-18","type":"mcq","q":"In the OM-A sample 737-8 load sheet, which limit restricts the traffic load?","src":"OM-A 14.4","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload (worked example)","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["MZFW","MTOW","MLW","Runway RTOW"],"a":0,"why":"MZFW gives 21,397 kg, lower than MLW (21,953) and MTOW (29,064). The flight is ZFW-limited.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"The load sheet arrives: DOW 44,555 kg, 110 pax plus 1,200 kg bags, take-off fuel 7,800, trip 5,000.","src":"OM-A 14.1.1, 14.4; FCTM 8.46; FCOM Flight Controls","ref":"Key facts: CG effects and payload","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"What's the traffic load and ZFW?","a":"Traffic load 9,450 kg; ZFW = 44,555 + 9,450 = 54,005 kg.","keys":[{"k":"9,450","m":["9450"]},{"k":"54,005","m":["54005"]}]},{"q":"Which structural limit restricts payload?","a":"MZFW: 65,952 - 44,555 = 21,397 kg, lower than MLW (21,953) and MTOW (29,064).","keys":[{"k":"MZFW","m":["mzfw","zero fuel"]},{"k":"21,397","m":["21397"]}]},{"q":"What's the underload?","a":"21,397 - 9,450 = 11,947 kg.","keys":[{"k":"11,947","m":["11947"]}]},{"q":"The CG on the sheet looks odd. Why challenge it?","a":"Wrong weights or CG give a mistrimmed stabiliser, a tail strike risk; trim must be in the green band or the take-off config horn sounds.","keys":[{"k":"Tail strike","m":["tail strike","tailstrike"]},{"k":"Trim / green band","m":["trim","green band"]}]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"What is V1?","src":"FCTM 3.28, 3.29","ref":"OB24","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"V1 has two halves:\n- the **maximum** speed at which the pilot must take the first action (brakes, thrust, speedbrakes) to stop within the accelerate-stop distance;\n- the **minimum** speed, after an engine failure, at which the take-off can be continued to the required height within the take-off distance.\n- AFM data assume the engine fails (VEF) one second before V1. Runway-limited, a continued take-off crosses the end at 35 ft; fail 2 s before V1 and go, and you cross at about 15 to 20 ft.\n- 'Equally possible to stop or go' is true only of a balanced V1.","keys":[{"k":"Maximum speed for first action to stop","m":["maximum speed","max speed","first action"]},{"k":"Within accelerate-stop distance","m":["accelerate stop","asda","stop within"]},{"k":"Minimum speed to continue","m":["minimum speed","min speed","continue"]},{"k":"VEF 1 s before V1","m":["one second","1 second","1 s ","1s"]},{"k":"Balanced only for equal go/stop","m":["balanced"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["equally possible","equal possibility"],"why":"Bank definition: only true of a balanced V1. V1 is the two-part (max stop / min go) speed."}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-21","type":"recall","q":"What happens to V1 on a wet or contaminated runway?","src":"FCTM 3.24, 3.31; OM-A 17.21","ref":"OB24","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Wet:** braking is worse, so wet-runway performance uses a **reduced V1** (and lower weight), with screen height reduced to **15 ft**. Dry V1 and weight on a wet runway can put a stop beyond the runway end.\n- **Contaminated:** slush, standing water or snow add rolling resistance and reduce friction, so weight reductions by contaminant depth.\n- AIX: V1 may be reduced to minimum V1 for stopping margin; no assumed-temperature thrust; maximum take-off power obtainable.\n- Lower limit is still VMCG, which is why ATM is banned on contaminated runways and derate helps.","keys":[{"k":"Reduced V1","m":["reduced v1","lower v1","v1 reduce","v1 is reduced","v1 decreases"]},{"k":"15 ft screen height wet","m":["15 ft","15ft","15 feet"]},{"k":"Weight reduction by depth","m":["weight reduction","lower weight","depth"]},{"k":"No ATM","m":["no assumed","no atm","no reduced thrust"]},{"k":"VMCG lower limit","m":["vmcg"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-32","type":"num","q":"737 wet-runway performance: screen height at the end of the take-off distance? (ft)","src":"FCTM 3.31","ref":"OB24","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":15,"unit":"ft","why":"Wet runway: reduced V1 and screen height 15 ft; dry 35 ft.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-19","type":"mcq","q":"AFM take-off data assume the engine fails how long before V1?","src":"FCTM 3.28, 3.29","ref":"OB24","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["1 second","2 seconds","3 seconds","At V1 exactly"],"a":0,"why":"VEF is one second before V1. Fail 2 s before V1 and go, and you cross the runway end at about 15 to 20 ft instead of 35 ft.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-22","type":"recall","q":"What are the limits on V1? Give the sequence of take-off speeds.","src":"Standard theory (Part 25/CS-25); Oral bank p.21; FCTM 3.25, 3.26","ref":"OB25","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"- V1 not less than VEF plus the speed gained with the critical engine out during the recognition time, and not less than VMCG.\n- Not more than VR, and not more than VMBE.\n- So V1 lies between VMCG and the lower of VR and VMBE.\n- **Sequence:** VMCG ≤ VEF < V1 ≤ VR < VLOF < V2, with VMU ≤ VLOF.\n- 737: QRH and FMC give a **balanced V1**. Unbalanced speeds (improved climb, max V1, min V1, clearway/stopway) need operator-supplied speeds.","keys":[{"k":"Not less than VMCG","m":["vmcg"]},{"k":"Not more than VR","m":["vr"]},{"k":"Not more than VMBE","m":["vmbe","brake energy"]},{"k":"Sequence VEF V1 VR VLOF V2","m":["vlof","v2"]},{"k":"737 balanced V1","m":["balanced"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["vmcg > vef","vmcg greater than vef","v1 > vr","v1 greater than vr"],"why":"Bank inequality signs are reversed: each speed is higher than the one before it."}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-order-3","type":"order","q":"Put the take-off speeds in ascending order.","src":"Standard theory; FCTM 3.26; Oral bank p.21","ref":"OB25","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"items":["VMCG","VEF","V1","VR","VLOF","V2"],"why":"VMCG ≤ VEF < V1 ≤ VR < VLOF < V2. The bank wrote it with reversed signs.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-20","type":"mcq","q":"V1 lies between:","src":"Oral bank p.21; standard theory","ref":"OB25","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["VMCG and the lower of VR and VMBE","VMCA and V2","VEF and VLOF","VMU and VR"],"a":0,"why":"V1 not less than VMCG (and VEF plus recognition gain), not more than VR or VMBE.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-21","type":"mcq","q":"When are the 737 QRH/FMC take-off speeds NOT valid?","src":"FCTM 3.25","ref":"OB25","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["When unbalanced speeds are used (improved climb, max or min V1 policy, clearway/stopway)","On any wet runway","At any derate","Whenever assumed temperature is used"],"a":0,"why":"QRH and FMC give a balanced V1; unbalanced speeds come from the operator.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Draw the V1 graph (distance required against engine-failure speed) and explain it.","src":"FCTM 3.25; Oral bank p.22","ref":"OB26","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- X axis: engine failure speed; Y axis: distance required.\n- **ASDR rises** with failure speed (more energy to stop).\n- **TODR falls** with failure speed (less distance left to accelerate on one engine to 35 ft).\n- They cross at the **balanced V1**: ASDR = TODR, minimum field length for that weight.\n- Failure below V1: stop. Above V1: go.\n- Field required is the higher of the two curves at the chosen V1.","checklist":["Axes labelled: failure speed vs distance","ASDR line rising","TODR line falling","Crossing point marked balanced V1","ASDR = TODR at crossing = minimum field length","Below V1 stop, above V1 go","Field required = higher curve at chosen V1"],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-23","type":"recall","q":"Explain the V1 graph.","src":"FCTM 3.25; Oral bank p.22","ref":"OB26","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- Plot distance required against engine-failure speed.\n- ASDR rises with failure speed: more energy to stop.\n- TODR falls with failure speed: less distance left to accelerate on one engine to 35 ft.\n- Where they cross is the balanced V1: ASDR = TODR, the minimum field length for that weight.\n- Failure below V1: stop, not enough distance to reach screen height on one engine. Above V1: go, can't stop in the ASDA.\n- Field required is the higher of the two curves at the chosen V1.","keys":[{"k":"ASDR rises","m":["asdr rise","asdr increase","accelerate stop increase","asdr goes up"]},{"k":"TODR falls","m":["todr fall","todr decrease","todr reduce","todr goes down"]},{"k":"Balanced V1 at crossing","m":["balanced","cross"]},{"k":"Minimum field length","m":["minimum field","shortest field","min field"]},{"k":"Below V1 stop, above go","m":["below v1","above v1"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-24","type":"recall","q":"How does weight affect V1?","src":"Oral bank p.22; standard theory","ref":"OB27","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Field length not limiting:** heavier means higher V1 (and VR, V2) from QRH/FMC, and you can still stop in the distance available.\n- **Field length limiting:** more weight raises the minimum V1 (needed to reach 35 ft on one engine) and lowers the maximum V1 (needed to stop). The usable V1 range narrows until, at the field-limit weight, only the balanced V1 remains.\n- That's why the bank says 'greater weight gives lower V1' for a limiting field: it refers to the stop-limited maximum V1.","keys":[{"k":"Not limiting: heavier = higher V1","m":["higher v1","v1 increase","increases v1","v1 goes up"]},{"k":"Limiting: min V1 up, max V1 down","m":["narrow","minimum v1","maximum v1"]},{"k":"Only balanced V1 at field limit","m":["balanced"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-25","type":"recall","q":"What is screen height? What is it for jets and propeller aircraft, dry and wet?","src":"FCTM 3.28, 3.31; Oral bank p.29; standard theory","ref":"OB28","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"- The height the aeroplane must reach at the end of the take-off distance; with an engine failure at VEF it marks the end of the TODR.\n- **737:** 35 ft dry; 15 ft wet with wet-runway performance.\n- It depends on the **performance class**, not engine type: large Class A (CS-25/Part 25) aeroplanes use 35 ft, including turboprops; 50 ft is the small-aeroplane (Class B) figure.","keys":[{"k":"Height at end of TOD","m":["end of the take off","end of takeoff","end of take off","end of tod","end of todr"]},{"k":"35 ft dry","m":["35 ft","35ft","35 feet"]},{"k":"15 ft wet","m":["15 ft","15ft","15 feet"]},{"k":"By performance class, not engine type","m":["class a","performance class","certification","class b"]},{"k":"50 ft Class B","m":["50 ft","50ft","50 feet"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["swept wing","lower cl","propeller 50","prop 50"],"why":"Bank reason is wrong: the 35/50 ft split is by certification (performance) class, not wing sweep or engine type."}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-22","type":"mcq","q":"Why is the screen height 35 ft for some aircraft and 50 ft for others?","src":"Standard theory; FCTM 3.28","ref":"OB28","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["Certification/performance class: Class A (CS-25) 35 ft, Class B 50 ft","Jets 35 ft because of the swept wing's lower CL","Propeller aircraft need more height for slipstream","Wet runways use 50 ft"],"a":0,"why":"Bank says the jet's swept wing gives a lower CL. Wrong reason: it's by performance class; Class A turboprops also use 35 ft.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-26","type":"recall","q":"What is assumed temperature (reduced) thrust on the 737?","src":"FCTM 3.20, 3.21; FCOM 11.32; OM-A 15.9.5; FCOM L.10.8","ref":"OB29","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- Lower take-off thrust reduces EGT, improves reliability and extends engine life. Used when performance limits and noise abatement procedures permit.\n- Enter a SEL TEMP higher than OAT on N1 LIMIT or TAKEOFF REF 2 ('D-TO'). Maximum reduction **25%** below any certified rating.\n- **Not an operating limit:** VMCG/VMCA are based on full rated thrust, so you may advance to full rated thrust at any time.\n- Allowed on wet runways with wet data. **Not** with standing water, slush, snow or ice, or suspected windshear.\n- AIX: no ATM after de-icing/anti-icing; no ATM with anti-skid inoperative.","keys":[{"k":"SEL TEMP above OAT","m":["sel temp","assumed temp","higher than oat","above oat"]},{"k":"Max 25% reduction","m":["25 percent","25%","twenty five"]},{"k":"Not an operating limit; VMCG at full thrust","m":["not an operating limit","full rated","full thrust"]},{"k":"Not on contaminated / windshear","m":["contaminat","slush","standing water","windshear","wind shear"]},{"k":"Reduces EGT / engine life","m":["egt","engine life","reliability"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-27","type":"recall","q":"What is derated thrust, and how does it differ from assumed temperature?","src":"FCOM PD.11.5, 11.32; FCTM 3.21, 3.22","ref":"OB29","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Fixed derate (TO-1, TO-2):** a lower certified rating selected in the FMC. TO-1 is 10%, TO-2 is 20%. Needs performance data for that rating.\n- **Is an operating limit:** VMCG, VMCA and stab trim are based on the derated thrust.\n- Allowed on wet **and** contaminated runways; may raise a VMCG-limited weight because VMCG is lower.\n- **Combined:** select the derate first, then an assumed temperature ('D-TO 1', 'D-TO 2').\n- In THR HLD you advance thrust levers manually. Below 800 ft RA a second TO/GA push only sets N1 bugs to full GA; above 800 ft RA TO/GA advances the levers.","keys":[{"k":"Lower certified rating TO-1/TO-2","m":["certified rating","to 1","to1","to 2","to2"]},{"k":"10% / 20%","m":["10 percent","10%","20 percent","20%"]},{"k":"Is an operating limit","m":["operating limit"]},{"k":"VMCG based on derated thrust (lower)","m":["vmcg","lower vmcg"]},{"k":"OK on contaminated","m":["contaminat"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["thrust returns to the maximum","returns to max","goes to full thrust"],"why":"Bank: pressing TOGA does not return thrust to max. On the 737 the levers are in THR HLD; extra thrust needs a manual advance."},{"m":["reduce noise","reduces noise"],"why":"Bank: reduced thrust isn't a noise measure. Boeing allows it only where noise procedures permit; less thrust means a lower climb path."}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-match-7","type":"match","q":"Match the 737 thrust option to its property.","src":"FCOM 11.32, PD.11.5; FCTM 3.21","ref":"OB29","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Assumed temperature (ATM)","Max 25% reduction; not an operating limit"],["TO-1","10% derate"],["TO-2","20% derate"],["Derate","Is an operating limit: VMCG/VMCA at derated thrust"]],"why":"FCOM 11.32, PD.11.5; FCTM 3.21.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-33","type":"num","q":"737 assumed temperature: maximum thrust reduction below any certified rating? (%)","src":"FCOM 11.32","ref":"OB29","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":25,"unit":"%","why":"Maximum reduction 25% below any certified rating.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-23","type":"mcq","q":"737, take-off roll in THR HLD, you need more thrust. What happens if you press TO/GA below 800 ft RA?","src":"FCTM 3.22","ref":"OB29","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["It only sets the N1 bugs to full GA; you advance the levers manually","Thrust automatically returns to maximum rated","The A/T disengages","The levers advance to the derate limit"],"a":0,"why":"Bank says TOGA returns thrust to maximum: wrong for the 737. Below 800 ft RA a second TO/GA push only resets the N1 bugs; above 800 ft RA TO/GA advances the levers.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-24","type":"mcq","q":"Which is allowed on a runway with slush?","src":"FCTM 3.21; FCOM 11.32","ref":"OB29","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Fixed derate (TO-1/TO-2)","Assumed temperature","Derate plus assumed temperature","Any reduced thrust if V1 is reduced"],"a":0,"why":"ATM is not allowed with standing water, slush, snow or ice. A derate is allowed on wet and contaminated runways.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-28","type":"recall","q":"Why is an assumed-temperature take-off not allowed on a contaminated runway, but a derate is?","src":"FCTM 3.21; Oral bank p.30; standard theory (VMCG/VMCA definitions)","ref":"OB30","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- Contamination needs a **lower V1**, but V1 may not be below VMCG.\n- **VMCG:** minimum ground speed at which, if the critical engine fails, directional control can be kept with aerodynamic controls alone, take-off thrust on the other engine. VMCA is the airborne equivalent (no more than 5° bank).\n- **ATM:** VMCG/VMCA stay at the full-rated value because the crew may advance to full thrust at any time. So ATM doesn't lower VMCG.\n- **Derate:** VMCG/VMCA are calculated at derated thrust, so VMCG really is lower and a lower V1 becomes legal.","keys":[{"k":"Lower V1 needed","m":["lower v1","reduced v1"]},{"k":"V1 not below VMCG","m":["vmcg"]},{"k":"ATM: VMCG at full thrust","m":["full rated","full thrust","doesnt lower","does not lower","not lower"]},{"k":"Derate: VMCG lower","m":["derated thrust","vmcg is lower","lower vmcg","vmcg lower"]},{"k":"VMCA 5° bank","m":["5 degree","5 deg","five degree","5°"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-29","type":"recall","q":"Engine failure during an ATM take-off versus a derate take-off: what do you do with thrust?","src":"FCTM 3.21, 3.45, 3.46","ref":"OB30","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **ATM:** no need to add thrust, performance already complies; you may advance the live engine to full rated thrust, at your discretion.\n- **Derate, or derate plus ATM:** do **not** go beyond the derate limit; it could cause loss of directional control, unless the captain judges terrain clearance cannot be assured.\n- Both engines may be advanced for windshear, or after V1 when more acceleration is needed.","keys":[{"k":"ATM: may advance to full rated","m":["full rated","may advance","full thrust"]},{"k":"Derate: don't exceed derate limit","m":["not exceed","dont exceed","do not go beyond","not go beyond","derate limit"]},{"k":"Loss of directional control","m":["directional control","loss of control"]},{"k":"Exception: terrain clearance","m":["terrain"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-chain-3","type":"chain","q":"You're planning a take-off from a slush-covered runway with performance to spare.","src":"FCTM 3.21, 3.45, 3.46; FCOM 11.32","ref":"OB30","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"Can you use assumed temperature?","a":"No. ATM isn't allowed with standing water, slush, snow or ice.","keys":[{"k":"No","m":["no","not"]}]},{"q":"Why not, when a derate is allowed?","a":"Contamination needs a lower V1, but V1 can't be below VMCG. ATM keeps VMCG at full-rated thrust; derate computes VMCG at derated thrust, so it's lower.","keys":[{"k":"VMCG","m":["vmcg"]},{"k":"Derate lowers VMCG","m":["lower","derated"]}]},{"q":"Engine fails after V1 on the derated take-off. Thrust?","a":"Don't go beyond the derate limit: risk of loss of directional control, unless terrain clearance can't be assured.","keys":[{"k":"Don't exceed derate","m":["not","dont","derate"]},{"k":"Directional control","m":["directional","control"]}]},{"q":"And if it had been an ATM take-off on a dry runway?","a":"Performance already complies; you may advance the live engine to full rated thrust at your discretion.","keys":[{"k":"May advance to full rated","m":["full rated","advance","full thrust"]}]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-30","type":"recall","q":"Explain the noise abatement departure procedures, NADP 1 and NADP 2.","src":"OM-A 19.2; Oral bank p.20, p.21; FCOM 11.32; FCTM 3.35, 3.36, 3.45","ref":"OB31","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"Safety takes precedence; if you can't fly it for safety reasons, tell ATC.\n- **NADP 1 (close-in areas):** take-off thrust, V2 + 10 to 20 kt; at or above 800 ft reduce power, keep take-off flaps and V2 + 10 to 20 kt; at 3000 ft accelerate and retract flaps on schedule.\n- **NADP 2 (distant areas):** at or above 800 ft accelerate towards VZF, reducing power with first flap retraction (or once clean); climb at VZF + 10 to 20 kt to 3000 ft, then en-route climb speed.\n- 737: thrust reduction height 800 to 9,999 ft, transition to **climb** thrust. Typical: thrust reduction about 1500 ft AFE, acceleration 1500 to 3000 ft AFE; minimum flap retraction 400 ft AFE. After an engine failure noise abatement no longer applies.","keys":[{"k":"NADP 1 close-in","m":["close in","close to the aerodrome","near the aerodrome","close"]},{"k":"NADP 2 distant","m":["distant","further away","far"]},{"k":"800 ft","m":["800"]},{"k":"3000 ft","m":["3000"]},{"k":"V2 + 10 to 20","m":["v2 plus 10","v2 + 10","v2 10 to 20","10 to 20"]},{"k":"Climb thrust (not MCT)","m":["climb thrust","clb"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["mct"],"why":"Bank says MCT at 3000 ft. Wrong: thrust goes to CLB at the thrust reduction height."}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-order-4","type":"order","q":"NADP 1: put the actions in order.","src":"Oral bank p.20","ref":"OB31","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Take-off thrust, climb at V2 + 10 to 20 kt","At or above 800 ft: reduce to climb power, keep take-off flaps and V2 + 10 to 20 kt","At 3000 ft: accelerate and retract flaps on schedule","Transition to en-route climb"],"why":"NADP 1 protects areas close to the aerodrome.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-order-5","type":"order","q":"NADP 2: put the actions in order.","src":"Oral bank p.21","ref":"OB31","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Take-off thrust, V2 + 10 to 20 kt","At or above 800 ft: accelerate towards VZF, reducing power with first flap retraction","Climb at VZF + 10 to 20 kt to 3000 ft","Transition to en-route climb speed"],"why":"NADP 2 protects areas further from the aerodrome.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-25","type":"mcq","q":"Which NADP protects noise-sensitive areas close to the aerodrome?","src":"Standard theory (ICAO NADP)","ref":"OB31","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["NADP 1","NADP 2","Both equally","Neither: it depends on terrain"],"a":0,"why":"NADP 1 close-in, NADP 2 distant. The bank says NADP 2 quickly takes the aircraft away and suits terrain: obstacle clearance comes from performance data, not the noise procedure.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-26","type":"mcq","q":"737: what does the thrust transition to at the thrust reduction height?","src":"FCOM 11.32","ref":"OB31","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["Climb (CLB) thrust","Maximum continuous thrust","Go-around thrust","Idle"],"a":0,"why":"Bank says MCT at 3000 ft: wrong. Thrust reduction height can be set 800 to 9,999 ft and the transition is to climb thrust.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-34","type":"num","q":"737 minimum flap retraction height? (ft AFE)","src":"FCTM 3.35","ref":"OB31","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":400,"unit":"ft","why":"Minimum flap retraction height 400 ft AFE.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-31","type":"recall","q":"What is a flat-rated engine?","src":"Oral bank p.24; FCOM 7.20; standard theory","ref":"OB32","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"a":"- Thrust at a given RPM rises as air gets colder and denser.\n- A flat-rated engine is limited to its rated thrust up to a flat-rating temperature, even though it could give more when cold. Above that temperature thrust falls as EGT becomes the limit.\n- Why: protects the engine from overboost, excessive internal pressures and over-temperature, and keeps thrust predictable.\n- 737 MAX: LEAP-1B with a full authority digital EEC (i.e. FADEC); in normal mode the EEC computes N1 ratings from sensed conditions and bleed demand. Ratings 1B25 or 1B27.","keys":[{"k":"Constant rated thrust up to a temperature","m":["flat rating temperature","flat rated temperature","up to a temperature","constant thrust","rated thrust"]},{"k":"Above it EGT limits","m":["egt"]},{"k":"Protect from overboost / over-temp","m":["overboost","over boost","over temperature","overtemp","pressure"]},{"k":"LEAP-1B EEC / FADEC","m":["eec","fadec","leap"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["fcu"],"why":"Bank says 'FCU (Boeing), FADEC (Airbus)'. The LEAP-1B has a full authority digital EEC, i.e. FADEC."}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-32","type":"recall","q":"What is driftdown, and why does the descent angle reduce as you go down?","src":"Oral bank p.24, p.25; FCOM 11.31; FCTM 4.12 to 4.14","ref":"OB33","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- After an engine failure in cruise, the remaining engine at MCT can't hold the level. You descend slowly at about best lift/drag speed: shallowest descent, most distance per foot lost.\n- Descending, the live engine's thrust increases (denser air) and weight falls; the thrust deficit shrinks, so the angle flattens until you hold level at the engine-out ceiling.\n- Net driftdown path must clear terrain all the way.\n- 737: ENG OUT on CRZ, disengage A/T, set CON (MCT), MCP speed ENG OUT SPD, MCP altitude MAX ALT, LVL CHG. Then engine-out LRC if range matters.","keys":[{"k":"Best L/D speed","m":["lift drag","l/d","ld speed","vmd","minimum drag"]},{"k":"MCT on live engine","m":["mct","maximum continuous","con thrust","con "]},{"k":"Thrust increases / weight decreases","m":["thrust increase","denser","weight","lighter"]},{"k":"Levels at engine-out ceiling","m":["ceiling","level off","levels off"]},{"k":"Net path clears terrain","m":["terrain","net"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-order-6","type":"order","q":"737 engine failure in cruise, driftdown: put the FMC/MCP actions in order.","src":"FCOM 11.31","ref":"OB33","asked":true,"prio":2,"items":["Select ENG OUT on the CRZ page","Disengage the A/T","Set CON (MCT) thrust","Set MCP speed to ENG OUT SPD","Set MCP altitude to MAX ALT","Select LVL CHG"],"why":"FCOM 11.31 driftdown sequence.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-33","type":"recall","q":"What are maximum range cruise and long range cruise?","src":"Oral bank p.7, p.8; FCTM 4.8, 4.11; FCOM 11.40","ref":"OB34","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **MRC:** the speed for maximum specific range (NM per kg) in still air; for a jet theoretically 1.32 VMD.\n- **LRC:** a faster schedule giving fuel mileage **1% less** than the maximum; about 4% faster than MRC.\n- Why LRC: the top of the specific range curve is flat, so you gain time for almost no fuel.\n- 737: CI 0 gives MRC. FMC applies no wind correction to LRC. Within 2000 ft of optimum, LRC ≈ M0.79; LRC also gives best buffet margin. Each 0.01 M above LRC costs 1 to 2% trip fuel.","keys":[{"k":"MRC = max specific range","m":["maximum specific range","max specific range","max range","maximum range"]},{"k":"LRC 1% less mileage","m":["1 percent","1%","one percent","99"]},{"k":"~4% faster","m":["4 percent","4%","four percent","faster"]},{"k":"Flat curve top","m":["flat"]},{"k":"CI 0 = MRC","m":["ci 0","cost index 0","cost index zero","ci zero"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-35","type":"num","q":"LRC gives fuel mileage how much less than MRC? (%)","src":"FCTM 4.8","ref":"OB34","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":1,"unit":"%","why":"LRC: 1% less than maximum fuel mileage.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-36","type":"num","q":"737: approximate LRC Mach within 2000 ft of optimum altitude?","src":"FCTM 4.11","ref":"OB34","prio":3,"ans":0.79,"unit":"M","tol":0.005,"why":"Within 2000 ft of optimum, LRC ≈ M0.79.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-34","type":"recall","q":"Why is 1.32 VMD the best range speed for a jet, and not VMD?","src":"Oral bank p.8, p.9; standard theory","ref":"OB35","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- Jet specific range = TAS / (SFC × drag). Jet SFC is roughly constant with speed, so maximum range means maximum TAS/drag.\n- At VMD drag is least, but the drag curve is flat there: going faster adds a lot of TAS for little extra drag, so TAS/drag keeps rising.\n- The best ratio is where a line from the origin is tangent to the drag curve: 3^¼ × VMD ≈ 1.32 VMD.\n- **Propeller:** SR = TAS / (SFC × power required), so best range is at VMD.\n- Higher altitude raises TAS for the same EAS and lowers SFC, which is why jets cruise high.","keys":[{"k":"SFC constant for jet","m":["sfc","constant"]},{"k":"Max TAS/drag","m":["tas drag","tas over drag","tas/drag","ratio"]},{"k":"Tangent from origin","m":["tangent","origin"]},{"k":"Prop best range at VMD","m":["propeller","prop"]},{"k":"Flat drag curve at VMD","m":["flat"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-37","type":"num","q":"Jet best range speed as a multiple of VMD?","src":"Oral bank p.7; standard theory","ref":"OB35","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":1.32,"unit":"x VMD","tol":0.01,"why":"3^(1/4) ≈ 1.32 VMD, where a line from the origin is tangent to the drag curve.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-35","type":"recall","q":"What is cost index? What do CI 0 and maximum CI give?","src":"Oral bank p.10; FCOM 11.31, 11.40; FCTM 4.8; OM-A 12.3, 16.4","ref":"OB36","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **CI = cost of time / cost of fuel.** Trades time-related costs against fuel to minimise total trip cost.\n- 737: valid entries 0 to 500. **0 = maximum range** speed; **500 = minimum time**. Entry required for VNAV. A low CI reflects high fuel cost and gives a lower cruise speed.\n- ECON speed varies with weight, altitude, CI and wind: headwind raises it, tailwind lowers it, but never below zero-wind MRC.\n- AIX: a higher CI is a listed cause of overburn; if fuel gets short, a lower CI (or CI 0) at optimum altitude is an option.","keys":[{"k":"Cost of time / cost of fuel","m":["time cost","cost of time","time related"]},{"k":"0 to 500","m":["500"]},{"k":"CI 0 = max range","m":["maximum range","max range","mrc"]},{"k":"Max CI = min time","m":["minimum time","min time","fastest"]},{"k":"Required for VNAV","m":["vnav"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-38","type":"num","q":"737 FMC: maximum valid cost index entry?","src":"FCOM 11.40","ref":"OB36","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":500,"why":"Valid entries 0 to 500; 500 = minimum time.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-36","type":"recall","q":"What is optimum altitude and why does it rise during the flight?","src":"Oral bank p.15, p.16, p.42; FCTM 4.7, 4.11","ref":"OB37","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- Classic: the pressure altitude giving maximum specific range for a given weight and speed.\n- Climbing raises TAS for the same EAS and lowers SFC, so range improves; above optimum, higher Mach brings compressibility (wave) drag, so range falls.\n- 737 FMC: OPT is from weight and cruise speed in **still air**. In ECON: minimum trip cost for the CI. With LRC or manual speed: minimum fuel burn. Ignores temperature deviation and winds; lower on short trips.\n- OPT rises as weight falls.\n- Off optimum (737-7/-9): 2000 ft above or below: 1 to 2% trip fuel; 4000 ft below: 2%; 8000 ft below: 7%.","keys":[{"k":"Max specific range for weight","m":["specific range","max range","maximum range"]},{"k":"Rises as weight falls","m":["weight","lighter"]},{"k":"Compressibility above optimum","m":["compressib","wave drag","mach"]},{"k":"Still air / ignores wind","m":["still air","wind"]},{"k":"2% / 7% penalty","m":["7 percent","7%","2 percent","2%"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-match-8","type":"match","q":"737-7/-9: cost of cruising off optimum altitude.","src":"FCTM 4.11","ref":"OB37","asked":true,"prio":2,"pairs":[["2000 ft above or below","1 to 2% trip fuel"],["4000 ft below","2%"],["8000 ft below","7%"]],"why":"FCTM 4.11 figures. The bank's '10% for no step climb' isn't the 737 figure.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-37","type":"recall","q":"What is a step climb, and how does it differ from a cruise climb?","src":"Oral bank p.16, p.17, p.42; FCTM 4.8, 4.9, 4.10, 4.11","ref":"OB38","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Cruise climb:** continuous slow climb keeping you at optimum as weight falls; rarely possible because ATC assigns fixed levels.\n- **Step climb:** climb in steps of available levels to stay near optimum, usually starting about 2000 ft above optimum and stepping again as optimum catches up.\n- 737: FMC does **not** compute the optimum step point; crew enters STEP TO, FMC gives ETA, distance and fuel saving or penalty.\n- A 4000 ft climb costs only about 45 to 90 kg; worth it only if you'll cruise about **20 min** or more at the higher level. More than 2000 ft above optimum increases burn.","keys":[{"k":"Cruise climb continuous","m":["continuous"]},{"k":"ATC fixed levels","m":["atc","fixed level"]},{"k":"Step climb in steps near optimum","m":["steps","step"]},{"k":"FMC doesn't compute step point; STEP TO","m":["step to","does not compute","doesnt compute"]},{"k":"20 min rule","m":["20 min","20 minutes","twenty min"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["10 percent","10%","200 nm","200nm"],"why":"Bank figures. For the 737 MAX the FCTM gives 2% for 4000 ft low and 7% for 8000 ft low, and uses the 20-minute rule."}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-27","type":"mcq","q":"737: a step climb is worthwhile only if you'll cruise at the higher level for at least about:","src":"FCTM 4.9","ref":"OB38","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["20 min","10 min","45 min","200 NM"],"a":0,"why":"FCTM 4.9: about 20 min. The bank's '200 NM from TOD' and 'no step climb costs 10%' aren't the 737 figures.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-28","type":"mcq","q":"Cost of cruising 8000 ft below optimum (737-7/-9)?","src":"FCTM 4.11","ref":"OB38","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["7% trip fuel","10% trip fuel","2% trip fuel","15% trip fuel"],"a":0,"why":"FCTM 4.11: 4000 ft low 2%, 8000 ft low 7%. The bank's 10% is not the 737 figure.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-38","type":"recall","q":"What is coffin corner, and how does the 737 protect you?","src":"FCOM L.10.2; FCTM 4.6, 4.7; standard theory","ref":"OB39","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- At high altitude the low-speed buffet (stall) speed rises in TAS and Mach, and the high-speed limit (MMO/Mach buffet) falls. They meet at the aerodynamic ceiling.\n- 737 FMC max altitude is the lowest of: certified altitude (41,000 ft), thrust-limited altitude, and buffet/manoeuvre-limited altitude, which needs at least **0.3 g (40° bank)** to buffet.\n- Near max altitude: at least 10 kt above the lower amber band, 10° bank or less. Below the band: reduce bank, increase thrust to max continuous, or descend.\n- Turbulence can briefly raise AoA to stick shaker.","keys":[{"k":"Low and high speed limits meet","m":["meet","converge","low speed","high speed"]},{"k":"41,000 ft certified","m":["41000","41 000","fl410"]},{"k":"0.3 g / 40° bank","m":["03 g","0.3 g","0.3g","40 degree","40 deg","40°"]},{"k":"10 kt above amber, 10° bank","m":["10 kt","10 knots","10 degree","10 deg","amber"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-39","type":"num","q":"737 FMC max altitude: minimum manoeuvre margin to buffet? (g)","src":"FCTM 4.6","ref":"OB39","prio":2,"ans":0.3,"unit":"g","why":"At least 0.3 g (40° bank) to buffet.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-39","type":"recall","q":"Climbing at constant CAS, what happens to TAS and Mach?","src":"FCTM 4.3; standard theory","ref":"OB40","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **TAS increases** because density falls.\n- **Mach increases even faster** below the tropopause: TAS rises while LSS falls with temperature.\n- Above the tropopause temperature is constant, so LSS is constant and Mach rises with TAS.\n- So climbs are at constant CAS to the **crossover altitude**, then constant Mach. In a constant-Mach climb below the tropopause, TAS and CAS both fall.\n- 737 ECON climb is constant speed/constant Mach; the Mach equals ECON cruise Mach for the entered level.","keys":[{"k":"TAS increases","m":["tas increase","tas rises","tas goes up"]},{"k":"Mach increases","m":["mach increase","mach rises","mach goes up"]},{"k":"LSS falls with temperature","m":["lss","speed of sound","temperature"]},{"k":"Crossover altitude","m":["crossover","cross over"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-mcq-29","type":"mcq","q":"Climbing at constant Mach below the tropopause, what happens to TAS and CAS?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB40","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Both decrease","Both increase","TAS increases, CAS decreases","TAS constant, CAS decreases"],"a":0,"why":"LSS falls with temperature, so at constant Mach TAS falls; CAS falls too.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-40","type":"recall","q":"What is critical Mach number?","src":"FCOM 9.20; standard theory","ref":"OB41","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **MCRIT:** the free-stream Mach at which local airflow first reaches Mach 1 somewhere on the aeroplane, usually over the upper wing.\n- Above MCRIT a shock wave forms: drag rises sharply (drag divergence Mach), then buffet, shock-induced separation ('shock stall') and Mach tuck.\n- Raised by wing sweep, thin aerofoils, supercritical sections.\n- 737: Mach trim works above M0.615; adjusts the elevators against the stabiliser to give speed stability at high Mach.","keys":[{"k":"Local flow first reaches Mach 1","m":["mach 1","sonic","local"]},{"k":"Shock wave / drag rise","m":["shock","drag"]},{"k":"Sweep / thin / supercritical","m":["sweep","thin","supercritical"]},{"k":"Mach trim above M0.615","m":["0615","0.615","mach trim"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t8-num-40","type":"num","q":"737: Mach trim operates above what Mach number?","src":"FCOM 9.20","ref":"OB41","prio":3,"ans":0.615,"unit":"M","why":"Mach trim works above M0.615.","topic":8},{"id":"t8-recall-41","type":"recall","q":"What is ATTCS? Does the 737 have it?","src":"FCTM 3.45; standard theory","ref":"OB42","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"- **Automatic Take-off Thrust Control System:** on some types it automatically raises thrust on the remaining engine when one fails during take-off, so normal take-offs can use lower thrust.\n- **The 737 has no ATTCS.** After an engine failure during an ATM take-off, the crew may advance the live engine to full rated thrust manually.","keys":[{"k":"Automatically raises thrust on live engine","m":["automatic","raises thrust","increases thrust"]},{"k":"737 has none","m":["no attcs","doesnt have","does not have","not fitted","no"]},{"k":"Manual advance to full rated","m":["manual","full rated"]}],"topic":8},{"id":"t9-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"Describe the Lambert conformal conic projection.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Charts, direction and great circles","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- Conformal.\n- Scale is correct on the two standard parallels, contracts between them and expands outside them.\n- A great circle is almost a straight line (slightly concave to the pole); a rhumb line is concave to the pole.\n- Convergency is constant: n = sin(parallel of origin).\n- Used for mid-latitude en-route charts (Jeppesen).","keys":[{"k":"Conformal","m":["conformal","orthomorphic"]},{"k":"Scale correct on two standard parallels","m":["standard parallel","two parallels"]},{"k":"GC almost straight","m":["great circle","nearly straight","almost straight"]},{"k":"Constant convergency n = sin origin","m":["sin","constant convergency","parallel of origin"]},{"k":"Mid-latitude en-route charts","m":["mid latitude","en route","jeppesen"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"Describe the Mercator projection, and why isn't it used for route charts?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Charts, direction and great circles","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- Rhumb lines are straight. Great circles curve concave to the pole, except meridians and the equator.\n- Scale expands with sec(latitude). Meridians are parallel, so no convergency.\n- Conformal.\n- **Not used for route charts** because great circles aren't straight and scale distortion is large away from the equator.","keys":[{"k":"Rhumb lines straight","m":["rhumb"]},{"k":"GC concave to pole","m":["concave","curve"]},{"k":"Scale sec latitude","m":["sec","secant"]},{"k":"No convergency / parallel meridians","m":["no convergency","parallel","zero convergency"]},{"k":"Distortion away from equator","m":["distortion","away from the equator","high latitude"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"Tell me all the types of chart projections. At the equator, which chart would you use?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR8","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Lambert conformal conic:** conformal, scale correct on two standard parallels, GC almost straight, constant convergency. Mid-latitude en-route.\n- **Mercator:** conformal, rhumb lines straight, scale grows with sec lat. Low latitudes.\n- **Transverse Mercator:** cylinder along a meridian; N-S strip. **Oblique Mercator:** along a chosen great circle.\n- **Polar stereographic:** conformal, GC almost straight, convergency = change of longitude. Polar.\n- **Gnomonic:** every GC straight, not conformal. GC planning.\n- **At the equator: normal Mercator.** It touches along the equator, so scale is correct there; it's conformal; near the equator GC and rhumb line are almost the same straight line.","keys":[{"k":"Normal Mercator at equator","m":["mercator"]},{"k":"Touches at equator, scale correct","m":["touch","tangent","scale is correct","scale correct"]},{"k":"Lambert mid latitudes","m":["lambert"]},{"k":"Polar stereographic for poles","m":["polar stereographic","stereographic"]},{"k":"Gnomonic: GC straight","m":["gnomonic"]},{"k":"Transverse Mercator","m":["transverse"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the chart projection to its main use.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR8","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Lambert conformal conic","Mid-latitude en-route charts"],["Normal Mercator","Plotting rhumb lines; low latitudes"],["Transverse Mercator","Topographic and N-S route charts"],["Oblique Mercator","Charts along a long great-circle route"],["Polar stereographic","Polar areas"],["Gnomonic","Planning great-circle tracks"]],"why":"Projection uses from the NR8 table.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-match-2","type":"match","q":"Match the projection to its convergency.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Charts, direction and great circles","asked":true,"prio":1,"pairs":[["Lambert","Constant: n = sin(parallel of origin)"],["Mercator","Zero (meridians parallel)"],["Polar stereographic","n = 1 (convergency = change of longitude)"]],"why":"Convergency on the common projections.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"On which projection is every great circle a straight line, but the chart isn't conformal?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR8","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Gnomonic","Polar stereographic","Lambert conformal conic","Transverse Mercator"],"a":0,"why":"Gnomonic: every great circle is straight; used for planning GC tracks; not conformal.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"On a Lambert chart, scale between the two standard parallels:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Charts, direction and great circles","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Contracts","Expands","Is exact everywhere","Expands with sec(latitude)"],"a":0,"why":"Correct on the standard parallels, contracts between, expands outside.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"On a Mercator chart, a great circle (other than a meridian or the equator) appears:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Charts, direction and great circles","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Curved, concave to the pole","Straight","Curved, convex to the pole","As a rhumb line"],"a":0,"why":"Rhumb lines are straight on Mercator; great circles curve concave to the pole.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"At the equator, which chart would you use?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR8","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Normal Mercator","Lambert conformal conic","Polar stereographic","Gnomonic"],"a":0,"why":"Normal Mercator touches along the equator: scale correct, conformal, GC and rhumb almost coincide.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-draw-1","type":"draw","q":"Sketch how a great circle and a rhumb line appear on a Lambert chart and on a Mercator chart.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Charts, direction and great circles","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Lambert:** two standard parallels marked; great circle almost straight (slightly concave to the pole); rhumb line curved, concave to the pole; meridians converge.\n- **Mercator:** meridians parallel; rhumb line straight; great circle curved, concave to the pole (bowing towards the pole).\n- In both, the great circle lies on the polar side of the rhumb line.","checklist":["Lambert: converging meridians, two standard parallels","Lambert: GC nearly straight","Lambert: rhumb concave to pole","Mercator: parallel meridians","Mercator: rhumb straight","Mercator: GC concave to pole","GC on polar side of rhumb line"],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"Explain convergency and conversion angle.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Charts, direction and great circles","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Convergency** = Δlong × sin(mean latitude): the angle between meridians.\n- **Conversion angle** = ½ × convergency: the angle between great circle and rhumb line.\n- Example: BOM to Goa runs nearly north-south, so GC and rhumb line almost coincide. BOM to Istanbul has a large east-west component, so the GC bows towards the pole.\n- Lambert: n = sin(parallel of origin); Mercator: zero; polar stereographic: n = 1.","keys":[{"k":"Δlong × sin mean lat","m":["sin","change of longitude","dlong","d long","difference in longitude"]},{"k":"Conversion angle half","m":["half","1/2","05"]},{"k":"GC vs rhumb line","m":["great circle","rhumb"]},{"k":"N-S route small, E-W large","m":["north south","east west"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-1","type":"num","q":"Convergency between two meridians 10° apart at a mean latitude of 30°? (°)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Charts, direction and great circles","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":5,"unit":"°","tol":0.1,"why":"Convergency = Δlong × sin(mean lat) = 10 × 0.5 = 5°.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-2","type":"num","q":"Convergency is 6°. What is the conversion angle? (°)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Charts, direction and great circles","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":3,"unit":"°","why":"Conversion angle = ½ × convergency = 3°.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"How do you go from true to magnetic to compass heading?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Charts, direction and great circles","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **True + variation (W +, E −) = magnetic.** 'Variation west, magnetic best; variation east, magnetic least.'\n- **Magnetic + deviation = compass.**\n- The FMC applies variation from a table in its database.","keys":[{"k":"Variation west add","m":["west plus","west add","w plus","west is best","west best"]},{"k":"Variation east subtract","m":["east minus","east subtract","e minus","east is least","east least"]},{"k":"Deviation gives compass","m":["deviation"]},{"k":"FMC variation table","m":["table","database"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-3","type":"num","q":"True heading 090°, variation 5°W. Magnetic heading? (°)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Charts, direction and great circles","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":95,"unit":"°","why":"West is best: 090 + 5 = 095°M.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"How would you navigate to the North Pole?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"NR49","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"**Grid navigation.**\n- Near the pole magnetic heading is unreliable (weak horizontal field, variation changing quickly) and meridians converge, so true heading changes very fast.\n- Use a polar stereographic chart with a grid aligned to the Greenwich meridian (grid north). Fly a constant grid heading, a straight line.\n- Grid heading = true heading ± convergency (or magnetic ± grivation, where grivation = variation + convergency).\n- Heading from IRS or gyro in true or grid mode, not the magnetic compass.","keys":[{"k":"Grid navigation","m":["grid"]},{"k":"Polar stereographic","m":["polar stereographic","stereographic"]},{"k":"Grid aligned to Greenwich","m":["greenwich"]},{"k":"Grivation = variation + convergency","m":["grivation"]},{"k":"IRS/gyro not magnetic compass","m":["irs","gyro","not the magnetic","not magnetic"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"What are the 737 and AIX limits for high-latitude and polar operations?","src":"FCTM 4.17; FCOM L, Flight Management, Navigation; OM-A 17.3.2","ref":"NR49","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- 737 (FCTM 4.17): LNAV as primary roll mode; HDG SEL and HOLD need TRUE heading reference. Don't use HDG SEL or ROLL CWS north of 89°30'N (or south of 89°30'S). North of 85° the RMI true heading may differ from the map. Loss of both GPS normally doesn't prevent continuing (ANP grows).\n- **FCOM limits:** maximum flight operating latitude 82°N and 82°S (ADIRU magnetic variation tables), except 73°N between 80°W and 170°W, and 60°S between 120°E and 160°E. No ADIRU alignment above 78°15'.\n- **AIX has no polar approval.**","keys":[{"k":"82° N/S max latitude","m":["82"]},{"k":"Exceptions 73°N / 60°S","m":["73","60 s","60s","60 degrees south"]},{"k":"No alignment above 78°15'","m":["78"]},{"k":"TRUE heading reference","m":["true heading","true reference","true"]},{"k":"AIX no polar approval","m":["no polar approval","not approved","no approval"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-4","type":"num","q":"737 FCOM: maximum flight operating latitude (general case)? (° N/S)","src":"FCOM L, Flight Management, Navigation","ref":"NR49","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":82,"unit":"°","why":"82°N and 82°S, set by the ADIRU magnetic variation tables, with exceptions (73°N between 80°W and 170°W; 60°S between 120°E and 160°E).","topic":9},{"id":"t9-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"Above what latitude must 737 ADIRU alignment not be attempted?","src":"FCOM L, Flight Management, Navigation","ref":"NR49","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["78°15'","82°00'","73°00'","85°00'"],"a":0,"why":"ADIRU alignment must not be attempted above 78°15' latitude.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"737: north of which latitude must you not use HDG SEL or ROLL CWS?","src":"FCTM 4.17","ref":"NR49","asked":true,"prio":3,"o":["89°30'N","85°N","82°N","78°15'N"],"a":0,"why":"Heading and track change too fast north of 89°30'N (or south of 89°30'S).","topic":9},{"id":"t9-chain-1","type":"chain","q":"You're asked to plan a flight that crosses the North Pole.","src":"FCTM 4.17; FCOM L; OM-A 17.3.2","ref":"NR49","asked":true,"prio":2,"steps":[{"q":"Why can't you use normal magnetic navigation?","a":"Weak horizontal field and rapidly changing variation make magnetic heading unreliable; meridians converge so true heading changes very fast.","keys":[{"k":"Magnetic unreliable","m":["magnetic","weak"]},{"k":"Meridians converge","m":["converge","true heading"]}]},{"q":"So how do you navigate?","a":"Grid navigation: polar stereographic chart, grid aligned to Greenwich, fly constant grid heading; heading from IRS in true/grid.","keys":[{"k":"Grid","m":["grid"]},{"k":"Polar stereographic","m":["stereographic"]}]},{"q":"Define grivation.","a":"Grivation = variation + convergency; grid heading = magnetic ± grivation.","keys":[{"k":"Variation + convergency","m":["variation","convergency"]}]},{"q":"Could you do it in our 737?","a":"No: AIX has no polar approval, and the FCOM limits flight to 82°N/S (with exceptions), no ADIRU alignment above 78°15'.","keys":[{"k":"No polar approval","m":["no","approval"]},{"k":"82°","m":["82"]}]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"Give me the nav rules of thumb you use.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Rules of thumb","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **1:60:** 1 NM off at 60 NM = 1°.\n- **3° glide path:** about 318 ft/NM (use 300).\n- **Rate of descent on a 3° path:** about 5 × ground speed.\n- **Descent distance:** about 3 NM per 1000 ft.\n- **Radar height:** height (ft) ≈ range (NM) × angle (°) × 100.\n- **DME overhead** at FL360 ≈ 6 NM.\n- **CP** = D × H / (O + H); **PNR** = E × H / (O + H).","keys":[{"k":"1 in 60","m":["1 in 60","1:60","one in sixty","160","1 60"]},{"k":"300 ft/NM","m":["300 ft","318","300 feet"]},{"k":"5 × GS","m":["5 times","five times","5 x","5x"]},{"k":"3 NM per 1000 ft","m":["3 nm per","3nm per","three miles","3 miles"]},{"k":"CP / PNR formulas","m":["o + h","o plus h","oh","critical point","pnr"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-5","type":"num","q":"A 3° glide path is about how many feet per NM? (exact rule-of-thumb figure)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Rules of thumb","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":318,"unit":"ft/NM","tol":3,"why":"About 318 ft/NM (use 300).","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-6","type":"num","q":"Rate of descent on a 3° path at 140 kt ground speed? (fpm)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Rules of thumb","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":700,"unit":"fpm","tol":20,"why":"About 5 × GS = 700 fpm.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-7","type":"num","q":"Radar: target at 20 NM, 1.5° elevation angle. Approximate height? (ft)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Rules of thumb","prio":3,"ans":3000,"unit":"ft","why":"Height ≈ range × angle × 100 = 20 × 1.5 × 100 = 3000 ft.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-8","type":"num","q":"Overhead a DME at FL360, what does the DME read (approximately)? (NM)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Rules of thumb","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":6,"unit":"NM","tol":0.2,"why":"Slant range: 36,000 / 6076 ≈ 6 NM.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-9","type":"num","q":"At what distance from the threshold should you be at 2400 ft on a 3° path? (NM)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Rules of thumb","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":7.5,"unit":"NM","tol":0.5,"why":"2400 / about 318 ft/NM ≈ 7.5 NM.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-10","type":"num","q":"1:60 rule: 3 NM off track at 60 NM. Track error? (°)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"Key facts: Rules of thumb","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":3,"unit":"°","why":"1 NM off at 60 NM = 1°, so 3°.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"You're 5 NM off track after 20 NM, with 60 NM to go. What heading change regains the destination?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S47","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Track error** = 5 × 60 / 20 = **15°**.\n- **Closing angle** = 5 × 60 / 60 = **5°**.\n- **Alter heading 20°** towards the track to reach the destination.\n- Or parallel the track first by turning 15°, then close by 5°.","keys":[{"k":"Track error 15°","m":["15"]},{"k":"Closing angle 5°","m":["5 degree","5 deg","5°","closing angle"]},{"k":"Total 20°","m":["20"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-11","type":"num","q":"5 NM off track after 20 NM flown. Track error? (°)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S47","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":15,"unit":"°","why":"5 × 60 / 20 = 15°.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-12","type":"num","q":"5 NM off track with 60 NM to go. Closing angle? (°)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S47","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":5,"unit":"°","why":"5 × 60 / 60 = 5°.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-13","type":"num","q":"5 NM off track after 20 NM flown, 60 NM to go. Total heading alteration to reach destination? (°)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S47","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":20,"unit":"°","why":"Track error 15° + closing angle 5° = 20°.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-draw-2","type":"draw","q":"Draw the 1:60 off-track problem: 5 NM off after 20 NM, 60 NM to go.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S47","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- Planned track A to B, 80 NM.\n- Actual position 20 NM along, 5 NM off to one side.\n- Track error angle at A: 15°.\n- Closing angle at B: 5°.\n- Heading change = 15 + 5 = 20° towards the track.","checklist":["Planned track drawn","Actual position 5 NM off at 20 NM","Track error 15° marked at start","Closing angle 5° marked at destination","Total correction 20° shown"],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"You're at FL350 with a 100 kt tailwind. How much extra descent distance? And what if you're 10 t below MLW?","src":"FCTM 4.19; standard theory (wind and weight effects)","ref":"S48","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **Still air:** about 3 NM per 1000 ft, so 35 × 3 ≈ 105 NM, plus about 10 NM to slow down. Boeing: about 3 NM per 1000 ft at ECON speed.\n- **Tailwind:** 2 NM per 10 kt, so about **20 NM more**. Cross-check: about 16 min of descent at 100 kt is about 25 NM.\n- **10 t lighter:** at the same descent speed you're further above best L/D speed, so you descend steeper. Descent distance shorter (TOD later), and it slows down more easily.","keys":[{"k":"~105 NM still air","m":["105"]},{"k":"+10 NM to slow","m":["10 nm","10nm","slow down","decelerat"]},{"k":"~20 NM extra for tailwind","m":["20 nm","20nm","20 miles","20 more","2 nm per 10","2nm per 10"]},{"k":"Lighter: steeper, shorter, later TOD","m":["steeper","shorter","later"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-14","type":"num","q":"Still-air descent from FL350 at about 3 NM per 1000 ft, excluding deceleration. Distance? (NM)","src":"FCTM 4.19","ref":"S48","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":105,"unit":"NM","tol":5,"why":"35 × 3 ≈ 105 NM; add about 10 NM to slow down.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-15","type":"num","q":"Descent from FL350 with a 100 kt tailwind: extra distance by the 2 NM per 10 kt rule? (NM)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S48","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":20,"unit":"NM","why":"2 NM per 10 kt × 10 = 20 NM more (cross-check: about 16 min at 100 kt ≈ 25 NM).","topic":9},{"id":"t9-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"Descending 10 t lighter at the same descent speed, the TOD will be:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S48","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["Later (shorter descent distance, steeper)","Earlier (longer descent distance)","Unchanged","Later only if there's a tailwind"],"a":0,"why":"Lighter at the same speed is further above best L/D speed, so it descends steeper; descent distance shorter, TOD later.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"With a 100 kt headwind outbound, where does the CP move?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S49","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **It moves into wind,** towards the destination when the headwind is outbound.\n- Distance to CP = D × H / (O + H), where O is ground speed onward and H ground speed home.\n- Example: D = 1000 NM, TAS 450 kt. O = 350, H = 550, so CP = 1000 × 550 / 900 = **611 NM**, against 500 NM in still air.","keys":[{"k":"Moves into wind","m":["into wind","into the wind","towards destination","toward destination","towards the destination"]},{"k":"D × H / (O + H)","m":["d x h","dh","d times h","o + h","o plus h"]},{"k":"611 NM example","m":["611"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-16","type":"num","q":"D = 1000 NM, TAS 450 kt, 100 kt headwind outbound. Distance to the CP? (NM)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S49","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":611,"unit":"NM","tol":1,"why":"O = 350, H = 550. CP = 1000 × 550 / 900 ≈ 611 NM (still air 500 NM).","topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"What are CP and PNR? Give the formulas and the factors that affect them.","src":"Oral bank p.108; OM-A 4.1.2.22; standard theory","ref":"OB45","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **CP (equal time point):** from it, the time to continue to destination equals the time to return (or reach the alternate). Distance to CP = D × H / (O + H).\n- **PNR (point of safe return):** the furthest point you can fly out and still return (or reach an alternate) with the safe endurance. Time to PNR = E × H / (O + H); distance = time × O.\n- **CP depends on wind and TAS, not fuel.** Still air: mid-point. Headwind outbound moves it into wind. Pure crosswind leaves it at the mid-point but times increase.\n- **PNR depends on endurance, fuel flow, TAS and wind.** Any wind, head or tail, brings PNR closer to base.\n- OM-A defines point of no return as the last geographic point from which the aircraft can reach both the destination and an available en-route alternate.","keys":[{"k":"CP = equal time","m":["equal time","same time"]},{"k":"CP = D × H / (O + H)","m":["d x h","dh","d times h","o + h","o plus h"]},{"k":"PNR = E × H / (O + H)","m":["e x h","eh","endurance"]},{"k":"CP not affected by fuel","m":["not fuel","independent of fuel","fuel doesnt","fuel does not","not on fuel"]},{"k":"Any wind brings PNR closer","m":["any wind","closer","reduces"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement about PNR and wind is correct?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB45","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Any wind, head or tail, brings the PNR closer to base","A tailwind outbound moves the PNR further out","Wind has no effect on PNR","Only crosswind affects PNR"],"a":0,"why":"Time lost into wind exceeds time gained downwind, so any wind reduces the PNR distance.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Which does NOT affect the position of the CP?","src":"Oral bank p.108","ref":"OB45","asked":true,"prio":1,"o":["Fuel on board","Wind","TAS","Total distance"],"a":0,"why":"CP depends on distance, TAS and wind, not fuel. PNR depends on fuel.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-17","type":"num","q":"Safe endurance 6 h, GS out 400 kt, GS home 500 kt. Time to PNR? (h)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB45","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":3.33,"unit":"h","tol":0.02,"why":"T = E × H / (O + H) = 6 × 500 / 900 ≈ 3.33 h (3 h 20 min).","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-18","type":"num","q":"Same case: safe endurance 6 h, GS out 400 kt, GS home 500 kt. Distance to PNR? (NM)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB45","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":1333,"unit":"NM","tol":5,"why":"Distance = time × O = 3.33 × 400 ≈ 1333 NM.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-chain-2","type":"chain","q":"You're crossing the Arabian Sea, 1000 NM leg, TAS 450 kt, forecast 100 kt headwind outbound.","src":"Oral bank p.108; standard theory","ref":"S49","asked":true,"prio":1,"steps":[{"q":"Where's the CP?","a":"D × H / (O + H) = 1000 × 550 / 900 ≈ 611 NM from departure, moved into wind from the 500 NM mid-point.","keys":[{"k":"611","m":["611"]},{"k":"Into wind","m":["into wind","towards destination","toward destination"]}]},{"q":"Does fuel on board change it?","a":"No, CP depends on distance, TAS and wind, not fuel.","keys":[{"k":"No","m":["no","not"]}]},{"q":"What's a PNR and what does it depend on?","a":"Furthest point from which you can return with safe endurance: T = E × H / (O + H). Depends on endurance, fuel flow, TAS and wind.","keys":[{"k":"Endurance","m":["endurance","fuel"]},{"k":"E × H / (O + H)","m":["e x h","eh","o + h","o plus h"]}]},{"q":"If the wind dropped to zero, would the PNR move?","a":"Yes, further from base: any wind, head or tail, brings the PNR closer to base.","keys":[{"k":"Further out","m":["further","farther","away"]}]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"FL310 and FL340, both at M0.78. Which has the higher ground speed?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S50","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **FL310.** TAS = Mach × LSS, and LSS depends only on temperature.\n- ISA FL310: -47°C, LSS ≈ 585 kt, TAS ≈ 457 kt.\n- ISA FL340: -53°C, LSS ≈ 577 kt, TAS ≈ 450 kt.\n- With the same wind, the lower (warmer) level is faster over the ground.\n- Above the tropopause (36,090 ft in ISA) temperature, and so TAS at a given Mach, is constant.","keys":[{"k":"FL310","m":["310"]},{"k":"TAS = Mach × LSS","m":["speed of sound","lss"]},{"k":"LSS depends on temperature","m":["temperature","warmer"]},{"k":"~457 vs ~450 kt","m":["457","450"]},{"k":"Tropopause 36,090 ft","m":["36090","tropopause"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-19","type":"num","q":"M0.78 at ISA FL310 (-47°C, LSS ≈ 585 kt). TAS? (kt)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S50","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":457,"unit":"kt","tol":2,"why":"0.78 × 585 ≈ 457 kt.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-20","type":"num","q":"M0.78 at ISA FL340 (-53°C, LSS ≈ 577 kt). TAS? (kt)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S50","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":450,"unit":"kt","tol":2,"why":"0.78 × 577 ≈ 450 kt.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-21","type":"num","q":"ISA temperature at FL340? (°C)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S50","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":-53,"unit":"°C","tol":0.5,"why":"15 - 2 × 34 = -53°C.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-22","type":"num","q":"ISA tropopause height? (ft)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"S50","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":36090,"unit":"ft","tol":10,"why":"36,090 ft; above it temperature, LSS and TAS at constant Mach stay constant.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"What is navigation?","src":"FCOM 11.31.4; standard theory","ref":"OB43","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"- The process of planning, recording and controlling the movement of an aircraft from one place to another: knowing where you are, where you want to go, and the heading, speed and time to get there.\n- **Methods:** pilotage (visual), dead reckoning, radio navigation, inertial, satellite, and area navigation combining them.\n- **On the MAX:** FMC area navigation from IRS updated by GPS and radio aids, with raw data VOR/DME, ILS and ADF as cross-check.","keys":[{"k":"Planning, recording, controlling","m":["planning","recording","controlling"]},{"k":"From one place to another","m":["one place to another","a to b","point to point"]},{"k":"Methods: DR, radio, inertial, satellite","m":["dead reckoning","inertial","satellite","radio"]},{"k":"MAX: FMC with IRS/GPS","m":["fmc","irs","gps"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"What is a sidereal day?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB44","asked":true,"prio":3,"a":"- **The time for one rotation of the earth relative to the fixed stars: about 23 h 56 min 4 s.**\n- **Solar day (24 h mean):** relative to the sun; about 4 min longer because the earth moves about 1° along its orbit each day, so it turns about 361°.\n- Why it matters: gyro apparent wander and IRS gyrocompassing use earth rate, about 15°/h (15.04°/h sidereal) × sin latitude.","keys":[{"k":"Rotation relative to fixed stars","m":["fixed stars","stars"]},{"k":"23 h 56 min","m":["23 h 56","23 hours 56","2356","23 56"]},{"k":"Solar 4 min longer / 361°","m":["4 min","4 minutes","361"]},{"k":"Earth rate 15°/h","m":["15 degrees per hour","15 deg","1504","15 per hour"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-23","type":"num","q":"Earth rate (sidereal) in degrees per hour?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB44","prio":3,"ans":15.04,"unit":"°/h","tol":0.01,"why":"About 15.04°/h sidereal; apparent wander = 15.04 × sin latitude.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"What is line-of-sight range? Optical versus radio horizon?","src":"Oral bank p.108; standard theory","ref":"OB46","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"- VHF and above travel by direct (space) wave, so range is limited by the horizon and the heights of both antennas.\n- **Radio LOS:** range (NM) ≈ 1.23 × (√h transmitter + √h receiver), heights in feet. The bank uses 1.25; both are rule-of-thumb constants, so say which you use.\n- Example: aircraft at 10,000 ft, station at sea level: 1.23 × 100 = 123 NM.\n- **Optical horizon** ≈ 1.06 × √h. Radio waves bend slightly more (standard atmosphere refraction), so the radio horizon is a little further.\n- Ducting under an inversion can give abnormal extra range.","keys":[{"k":"Direct / space wave","m":["direct wave","space wave","line of sight"]},{"k":"1.23 √h","m":["123","1.23","125","1.25"]},{"k":"Both antenna heights","m":["both","transmitter","receiver"]},{"k":"Optical 1.06","m":["106","1.06"]},{"k":"Radio horizon further (refraction)","m":["refraction","bend","further"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-24","type":"num","q":"VHF LOS range: aircraft at 10,000 ft, station at sea level, using 1.23√h? (NM)","src":"Oral bank p.108; standard theory","ref":"OB46","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"ans":123,"unit":"NM","tol":2,"why":"1.23 × √10,000 = 1.23 × 100 = 123 NM. With the bank's 1.25 you'd get 125 NM.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"VHF LOS range constant: the bank uses 1.25, the topic 10 key facts 1.23. What should you say?","src":"Oral bank p.108; standard theory","ref":"OB46","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"o":["Either; both are rule-of-thumb constants, so state which one you use","Only 1.25 is correct","Only 1.23 is correct","Use 1.06, the optical constant"],"a":0,"why":"Both are rule-of-thumb constants; state which you use. 1.06 is the optical (geometric) horizon.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"Where are Lambert charts used apart from aviation? How many cover India?","src":"Oral bank p.5, p.6, p.102, p.113; standard theory","ref":"OB47","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"a":"- **Properties:** conformal, scale correct on two standard parallels, near-straight great circles, constant convergency n = sin parallel of origin.\n- **Non-aviation:** topographic and atlas maps of mid-latitude regions with a large east-west extent, and mid-latitude meteorological charts.\n- **India:** the bank's notes contradict themselves (7 charts on one page; 8 charts with 7 and 8 covering most of India on another). The current Jeppesen India en-route chart index settles it.\n- Bank's scale note: scale constant across the chart to about ±1%, matching Lambert's small scale error near the standard parallels.","keys":[{"k":"Topographic / atlas","m":["topographic","atlas"]},{"k":"Meteorological charts","m":["met","weather","meteorolog"]},{"k":"Large east-west extent","m":["east west"]},{"k":"Jeppesen index settles India count","m":["jeppesen","index"]},{"k":"Scale ±1%","m":["1 percent","1%","one percent"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"Wind limits: headwind of at least 10 kt and crosswind limit 35 kt, with the wind 60° off the runway. What wind speeds are acceptable?","src":"Oral bank p.4; standard theory","ref":"OB48","asked":true,"prio":1,"a":"- Headwind = V cos 60° = 0.5 V ≥ 10, so **V ≥ 20 kt**.\n- Crosswind = V sin 60° = 0.866 V ≤ 35, so **V ≤ 40 kt** (40.4).\n- Acceptable wind: **20 to 40 kt**.","keys":[{"k":"cos 60 = 0.5","m":["cos 60","05","0.5","half"]},{"k":"V ≥ 20 kt","m":["20"]},{"k":"sin 60 = 0.866","m":["sin 60","0866","0.866"]},{"k":"V ≤ 40 kt","m":["40"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-25","type":"num","q":"Wind 60° off the runway; you need at least 10 kt headwind. Minimum wind speed? (kt)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB48","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":20,"unit":"kt","why":"V cos 60° ≥ 10, so V ≥ 20 kt.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-26","type":"num","q":"Wind 60° off the runway; crosswind limit 35 kt. Maximum wind speed? (kt)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB48","asked":true,"prio":1,"ans":40,"unit":"kt","tol":0.5,"why":"V sin 60° ≤ 35, so V ≤ 40.4, call it 40 kt.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"Tailwind limit 10 kt, runway QDM 047, variation 17°E, ATIS wind 210. What's the maximum wind speed?","src":"Doc 4444 11.4.3.2.3 Note; Oral bank p.4","ref":"OB48","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **The trap is the variation.** Wind passed by ATS units (ATIS, tower) is in degrees **magnetic**; METAR/TAF wind is true. QDM 047 is magnetic too, so no conversion is needed.\n- Tailwind direction is the reciprocal of 047, i.e. 227. Wind 210 is 17° off it.\n- Tailwind = V cos 17° = 0.956 V ≤ 10, so **V ≤ about 10.5 kt**.\n- If 210 had been a METAR (true) wind: 210T = 193M, 34° off, V ≤ about 12 kt.","keys":[{"k":"ATIS wind is magnetic","m":["magnetic"]},{"k":"No conversion needed","m":["no conversion","not convert","dont convert","do not convert","no need"]},{"k":"Reciprocal 227, 17° off","m":["227","17"]},{"k":"V ≤ ~10.5 kt","m":["105","10.5"]},{"k":"METAR wind true","m":["true","metar"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["193","12 kt","12 knots"],"why":"That treats the ATIS wind as true. ATIS/tower wind is magnetic, so no variation correction: answer about 10.5 kt."}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-27","type":"num","q":"Tailwind limit 10 kt, runway QDM 047, ATIS wind 210 (variation 17°E). Maximum wind speed? (kt)","src":"Doc 4444 11.4.3.2.3 Note","ref":"OB48","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"ans":10.5,"unit":"kt","tol":0.2,"why":"ATIS wind is magnetic, so no variation conversion. Tail direction 227, wind 17° off: V cos 17° ≤ 10, V ≤ about 10.5 kt.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-28","type":"num","q":"Same runway (QDM 047, variation 17°E, tailwind limit 10 kt) but the wind 210 is a METAR (true) wind. Maximum wind speed? (kt)","src":"Doc 4444 11.4.3.2.3 Note","ref":"OB48","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"ans":12,"unit":"kt","tol":0.2,"why":"210T = 193M, 34° off the tail direction 227. V cos 34° ≤ 10, V ≤ about 12 kt.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"Wind from ATIS or the tower is given in degrees:","src":"Doc 4444 11.4.3.2.3 Note","ref":"OB48","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"o":["Magnetic","True","Grid","Compass"],"a":0,"why":"ATS units pass wind in degrees magnetic; met office wind (METAR, TAF) is true.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"Magnetic heading 311°, drift 10° left, relative bearing of the NDB 270°. What is the magnetic bearing of the NDB from the aircraft?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB49","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"a":"- **QDM = MH + RB = 311 + 270 = 581 − 360 = 221°.**\n- Drift is a distractor: the ADF measures relative bearing from the aircraft's **heading** (fore-and-aft axis), not its track.\n- QDR (bearing of the aircraft from the NDB) = 041°.","keys":[{"k":"MH + RB","m":["mh + rb","mh plus rb","heading plus relative","heading + relative","add"]},{"k":"221°","m":["221"]},{"k":"Drift is a distractor","m":["drift","distractor","ignore"]},{"k":"QDR 041","m":["041","41"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["211","231"],"why":"Applying drift is wrong: relative bearing is measured from heading, not track."}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-29","type":"num","q":"MH 311°, drift 10° left, NDB relative bearing 270°. QDM? (°)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB49","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"ans":221,"unit":"°","why":"QDM = MH + RB = 311 + 270 - 360 = 221°. Drift is a distractor.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-num-30","type":"num","q":"MH 311°, NDB relative bearing 270°. QDR (bearing of the aircraft from the NDB)? (°)","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB49","asked":true,"prio":2,"ans":41,"unit":"°","why":"QDM 221°, so QDR = 221 - 180 = 041°.","topic":9},{"id":"t9-recall-21","type":"recall","q":"An aircraft flies a distance east, the same south, the same west, then the same north. Where does it end up?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB50","asked":true,"prio":2,"a":"- **Northern hemisphere: east of the start.** The east leg is at the higher latitude, where a given distance (departure) covers more degrees of longitude, because departure = Δlong × cos latitude. The west leg, nearer the equator, covers fewer degrees, so you don't get all the way back.\n- **Southern hemisphere (or starting on the equator): west of the start,** because flying south takes you to a higher latitude for the west leg.\n- The north leg returns you to the starting latitude in both cases.","keys":[{"k":"NH: east of start","m":["east of"]},{"k":"Departure = Δlong × cos lat","m":["cos","departure"]},{"k":"More longitude at higher latitude","m":["higher latitude","more degrees","more longitude"]},{"k":"SH: west of start","m":["west of","southern"]}],"topic":9},{"id":"t9-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"Starting in the northern hemisphere, fly X NM east, X south, X west, X north. You end up:","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB50","asked":true,"prio":2,"o":["East of the start","West of the start","Back at the start","North of the start"],"a":0,"why":"The east leg is at a higher latitude, covering more longitude than the west leg; departure = Δlong × cos lat.","topic":9},{"id":"tr-recall-1","type":"recall","q":"What is the alert height on the 737, and who sets it?","src":"OM-A 45.2.4.2; OM-A Ch 0; FCTM 5.17","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree","trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":5,"a":"- For the 737 the alert height is **200 ft**; OM-A 45.2.4.2 gives **200 ft for the B737** and 100 ft for the A320, set by the manufacturer.\n- The OM-A Ch 0 definition saying AH is \"typically 100 ft or less\" is generic; don't quote it for the 737.\n- Boeing: AFDS design gives an AH of **at least 200 ft HAT**, which **operators** may modify to a lower value.\n- So it is an **operator setting**, not something the crew changes in flight.","keys":[{"k":"200 ft for the 737","m":["200 ft","200ft","200 feet","200"]},{"k":"at least 200 ft HAT","m":["at least 200","hat","height above touchdown"]},{"k":"operator setting","m":["operator"]},{"k":"not changed by crew in flight","m":["not the crew","not crew","not changed","cannot change","can't change","cant change","not something"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["100 ft or less","100ft or less","100 feet or less"],"why":"That is the generic Ch 0 definition (and the A320 value); the 737 uses 200 ft."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-1","type":"mcq","q":"Alert height for the B737 per OM-A 45.2.4.2?","src":"OM-A 45.2.4.2; FCTM 5.17","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree","trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":5,"o":["200 ft","100 ft","50 ft","Typically 100 ft or less"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A gives 200 ft for the B737, 100 ft for the A320. Boeing: AFDS AH at least 200 ft HAT, operator may lower it."},{"id":"tr-recall-2","type":"recall","q":"What terrain clearance does a Grid MORA give?","src":"Jeppesen legend; OM-A Ch 0; NR3; NR39","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree; NR3; NR39","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":6,"a":"- Quote the **Jeppesen legend**: **1000 ft** clearance where the highest terrain in the grid is **5000 ft or lower**, and **2000 ft** where it is higher than 5000 ft.\n- The OM-A Ch 0 definition says \"minimum terrain clearance of **3000 ft**\"; that disagrees with Jeppesen, so don't quote it.\n- The oral bank's \"1000 ft over the whole grid\" is also wrong: it misses the 2000 ft case.","keys":[{"k":"1000 ft up to 5000 ft terrain","m":["1000 ft","1000ft","1000 feet","1000"]},{"k":"5000 ft threshold","m":["5000"]},{"k":"2000 ft above that","m":["2000 ft","2000ft","2000 feet","2000"]},{"k":"Jeppesen legend","m":["jeppesen","jepp"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["3000 ft","3000ft","3000 feet"],"why":"OM-A Ch 0 definition; Jeppesen standard is 1000/2000 ft."},{"m":["whole grid","entire grid","always 1000"],"why":"Bank p.112; 2000 ft applies where terrain exceeds 5000 ft."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-2","type":"mcq","q":"Grid MORA clearance where the highest terrain in the grid is 7000 ft?","src":"Jeppesen legend; NR3; NR39","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":6,"o":["2000 ft","1000 ft","3000 ft","1500 ft"],"a":0,"why":"Jeppesen: 1000 ft where highest terrain is 5000 ft or lower, 2000 ft above that. 3000 ft is the OM-A Ch 0 wording; 1000 ft everywhere is the bank's error."},{"id":"tr-recall-3","type":"recall","q":"Is the B737 CAT III fail-operational or fail-passive, and what minima apply?","src":"OM-A 7.3.6.2; OM-A 45.1.1; OM-A 45.2.4; FCOM 4.20; FCOM L.10.6, L.10.7","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree; NR27","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":5,"a":"- **Both exist in the fleet.** Fail-operational aircraft show **LAND 3, LAND 2 or NO AUTOLAND** and may use the fail-operational minima: **RVR 50 m, DH below 50 ft or no DH**.\n- **Fail-passive** aircraft have no such annunciations and are limited to **DH below 100 ft, RVR 175 m**.\n- Autoland limits for both: **flaps 30 or 40**, **both engines operating**, glideslope **2.5° to 3.25°**; FAA rules: headwind **25 kt**, crosswind **20 kt** (fail-passive) or **25 kt** (fail-operational), tailwind **15 kt**.","keys":[{"k":"both types in fleet","m":["both","fleet"]},{"k":"LAND 3 / LAND 2 annunciation","m":["land 3","land 2","land three"]},{"k":"fail-op RVR 50 m, DH below 50 ft","m":["50 m","50m","50 metres","50 meters"]},{"k":"fail-passive DH below 100 ft, RVR 175 m","m":["175 m","175m","175"]},{"k":"flaps 30 or 40, both engines","m":["flaps 30","flap 30","both engines"]}]},{"id":"tr-match-1","type":"match","q":"Match the autoland limit to its value (FAA rules, 737).","src":"FCOM L.10.6, L.10.7","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":5,"pairs":[["Headwind","25 kt"],["Crosswind, fail-passive","20 kt"],["Crosswind, fail-operational","25 kt"],["Tailwind","15 kt"],["Glideslope","2.5° to 3.25°"],["Flaps","30 or 40"]],"why":"FCOM autoland limits apply to both systems; only the crosswind differs."},{"id":"tr-mcq-3","type":"mcq","q":"A 737 with no LAND 3 / LAND 2 annunciations. Lowest CAT III minima it may use?","src":"FCOM 4.20; OM-A 7.3.6.2","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":5,"o":["DH below 100 ft, RVR 175 m","DH below 50 ft or no DH, RVR 50 m","DH 200 ft, RVR 550 m","DH 100 ft, RVR 300 m"],"a":0,"why":"No annunciations means fail-passive: DH below 100 ft, RVR 175 m. The 50 m / no DH minima need fail-operational."},{"id":"tr-recall-4","type":"recall","q":"Is ACAS II required to enter RVSM airspace?","src":"OM-A 41.2; OM-A 41.3.4","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree; S10","trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":2,"a":"- Give **both** sources:\n- The **CAR equipment list** includes **ACAS II** (OM-A 41.2).\n- The OM-A list of systems **required at RVSM entry** (41.3.4) does **not** include ACAS.\n- So: ACAS II is a CAR equipment requirement, but it is not on the RVSM-entry check list.","keys":[{"k":"CAR list includes ACAS II","m":["car","acas ii","acas 2","tcas"]},{"k":"OM-A 41.2","m":["41.2"]},{"k":"not on RVSM entry list","m":["not on","not in","does not","doesn't","doesnt","not included"]},{"k":"OM-A 41.3.4","m":["41.3.4"]}]},{"id":"tr-recall-5","type":"recall","q":"Over Delhi to Kolkata you need to deviate for weather. Do you use the weather-deviation table?","src":"OM-A 41.1.9.3; OM-A 17.3.7.2","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree; S7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":2,"a":"- The OM-A frames the weather-deviation table for **oceanic airspace** (41.1.9.3, 17.3.7.2).\n- Delhi to Kolkata is **continental and radar-covered**, so **get a clearance first**.\n- The table is the contingency for when you cannot get a clearance in oceanic airspace, not a substitute for a clearance under radar.","keys":[{"k":"table is for oceanic","m":["oceanic"]},{"k":"continental and radar","m":["continental","radar"]},{"k":"get a clearance first","m":["clearance"]}]},{"id":"tr-recall-6","type":"recall","q":"Under radar vectors, who is responsible for obstacle clearance?","src":"OM-A 6.1.2 note 2; OM-A 6.3","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree","trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":6,"a":"- The OM-A seems to say both: 6.1.2 note 2 says it rests with the **commander**; 6.3 says the **MVA** provides it when vectored.\n- Answer: **ATC provides it through the MVA** while vectoring.\n- But the **commander must refuse an unsafe altitude**.\n- And with a **communications failure** the commander must **climb to MSA**.","keys":[{"k":"ATC via MVA","m":["mva","minimum vectoring"]},{"k":"commander refuses unsafe altitude","m":["refuse","reject","decline"]},{"k":"comms failure: climb to MSA","m":["msa","minimum sector"]},{"k":"commander responsibility","m":["commander","captain","pic"]}]},{"id":"tr-recall-7","type":"recall","q":"What RFFS category must an EDTO en-route alternate have?","src":"OM-A 4.5.1 (10); OM-A 17.1.2.3","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree","trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":1,"a":"- For EDTO, quote OM-A 4.5.1 (10): **one category below the aeroplane's RFFS category**, but **never below Cat 4**.\n- The general rule in OM-A 17.1.2.3 is different: **Cat 4 minimum** with **more than 30 min notice**.\n- For EDTO questions, use the EDTO rule.","keys":[{"k":"one category below aeroplane","m":["one category below","one below","1 category below","one cat below"]},{"k":"never below Cat 4","m":["cat 4","category 4","cat four","category four"]},{"k":"30 min notice (general rule)","m":["30 min","30 minutes","30min"]}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-4","type":"mcq","q":"Under EDTO rules, minimum RFFS at an en-route alternate is:","src":"OM-A 4.5.1 (10); OM-A 17.1.2.3","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree","trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":1,"o":["One category below the aeroplane category, never below Cat 4","Same category as the aeroplane","Cat 4 with more than 30 min notice","Two categories below the aeroplane, never below Cat 3"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 4.5.1 (10). Cat 4 with 30 min notice is the general rule (17.1.2.3); quote the EDTO rule for EDTO."},{"id":"tr-recall-8","type":"recall","q":"At the critical point your fuel is below the critical fuel requirement. Must you divert?","src":"OM-A 4.6.3","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree; S3","trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":1,"a":"- **No, not automatically.** OM-A 4.6.3 says there is **no requirement to divert** if the **destination-alternate fuel is still protected**.\n- So check whether you still have the fuel to reach destination and then the alternate.\n- Know this wording exactly; examiners probe it in the S3 scenario.","keys":[{"k":"no requirement to divert","m":["no requirement","not required","need not","no need","don't have to","dont have to"]},{"k":"destination alternate fuel protected","m":["alternate"]},{"k":"OM-A 4.6.3","m":["4.6.3"]}]},{"id":"tr-match-2","type":"match","q":"These topics are not in the OM-A. Match each to its source.","src":"Reference Part 8","ref":"Flags: where sources disagree","trap":true,"prio":3,"topic":7,"pairs":[["GNSS spoofing and jamming","AIX Fly-Safe Issue 17"],["737 memory items and QRH values","QRH"],["PWS and TCAS logic","FCOM Chapter 15"],["Holding speeds","Doc 8168 Vol I"],["Radio aid standards","Annex 10 Vol I"]],"why":"The rest of the radio and nav theory is standard theory."},{"id":"tr-recall-9","type":"recall","q":"In RVSM airspace you cannot maintain level or must leave track without a clearance. What is the turn and offset?","src":"Doc 4444 15.2.2.3","ref":"NR7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":2,"a":"- Per your **Doc 4444 copy**: turn **at least 45°** to establish a **15 NM** offset.\n- **Later amendments** use **30°** and **5 NM**.\n- In the viva, quote your **Jeppesen ATC pages** for the current figures.\n- The bank's \"90° turn and 25 NM offset\" is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"45° turn (your Doc 4444)","m":["45 degree","45 deg","at least 45"]},{"k":"15 NM offset","m":["15 nm","15nm","15 nautical","15 miles"]},{"k":"later: 30° and 5 NM","m":["30 degree","30 deg","5 nm","5nm","5 nautical"]},{"k":"Jeppesen ATC pages","m":["jeppesen","jepp"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["90 degree","90 deg"],"why":"Bank p.67; Doc 4444 says at least 45° (later 30°)."},{"m":["25 nm","25nm","25 nautical","25 miles"],"why":"Bank p.67; Doc 4444 says 15 NM (later 5 NM)."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-5","type":"mcq","q":"RVSM in-flight contingency per your Doc 4444 copy (15.2.2.3)?","src":"Doc 4444 15.2.2.3","ref":"NR7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":2,"o":["Turn at least 45° to a 15 NM offset","Turn 90° to a 25 NM offset","Turn 30° to a 25 NM offset","Turn 90° to a 15 NM offset"],"a":0,"why":"Your Doc 4444 copy: 45°/15 NM; later amendments 30°/5 NM. The bank's 90°/25 NM is wrong."},{"id":"tr-recall-10","type":"recall","q":"Deviating for weather in oceanic airspace without a clearance: at what lateral distance from track do you start the level change?","src":"OM-A 17.3.7.2; OM-A 41.1.9.3","ref":"NR7; S7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":2,"a":"- **AIX uses 5 NM** from track (OM-A 17.3.7.2, 41.1.9.3).\n- **10 NM** is the older figure in your Doc 4444 copy.\n- The bank's \"beyond 10 NM\" follows the old figure; quote 5 NM.","keys":[{"k":"5 NM","m":["5 nm","5nm","5 nautical","five nm","5 miles"]},{"k":"AIX OM-A","m":["om-a","oma","aix","17.3.7.2","41.1.9.3"]},{"k":"10 NM is older","m":["older","old"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["beyond 10","10 nm","10nm","10 nautical"],"why":"Bank p.66 / old Doc 4444 figure; AIX uses 5 NM."}]},{"id":"tr-num-1","type":"num","q":"Weather deviation (AIX): lateral distance from track at which the level change starts?","src":"OM-A 17.3.7.2; OM-A 41.1.9.3","ref":"NR7; S7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":2,"ans":5,"unit":"NM","why":"OM-A 17.3.7.2, 41.1.9.3: 5 NM. 10 NM is the older Doc 4444 figure and the bank's answer."},{"id":"tr-recall-11","type":"recall","q":"Where may SLOP be used and how large are the offsets?","src":"Doc 4444 16.5.2; Doc 4444 16.5.3","ref":"OB2","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":2,"a":"- SLOP is **not only for the NAT**: any **oceanic or remote continental** airspace where the **AIP authorises** it.\n- Offsets are **right of track** only, in **tenths of a NM**, **maximum 2 NM**.\n- Where route spacing is **under 23 NM**, maximum **0.5 NM**.\n- The bank's \"always 0, 1, 2 miles\" is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"oceanic or remote continental","m":["oceanic","remote"]},{"k":"where AIP authorises","m":["aip","authoris","authoriz"]},{"k":"right of track","m":["right"]},{"k":"tenths of a NM, max 2 NM","m":["tenth","0.1","max 2","maximum 2","2 nm","2nm"]},{"k":"0.5 NM if spacing under 23 NM","m":["0.5","23 nm","23nm","half a"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["only applicable to the nat","only nat","only in the nat","only the nat"],"why":"Bank p.82; SLOP applies wherever the AIP authorises it."},{"m":["0 1 2","always 0"],"why":"Bank p.82; offsets are in tenths of a NM up to 2 NM."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-6","type":"mcq","q":"Maximum SLOP offset where route spacing is less than 23 NM?","src":"Doc 4444 16.5.2; Doc 4444 16.5.3","ref":"OB2","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":2,"o":["0.5 NM","2 NM","1 NM","0.2 NM"],"a":0,"why":"Doc 4444 16.5.3: max 2 NM normally, 0.5 NM where spacing is under 23 NM; right of track only."},{"id":"tr-recall-12","type":"recall","q":"Where is the approach ban point?","src":"OM-A 7.2.5 b","ref":"OB14","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":5,"a":"- The ban point is **1000 ft above the aerodrome** or the **FAF/FAP** (OM-A 7.2.5 b).\n- If the reported visibility/RVR is below minima, you may not continue past that point.\n- The **IAF** is where you **check before commencing** the approach; it is **not** the ban point (the bank's error).","keys":[{"k":"1000 ft above aerodrome","m":["1000 ft","1000ft","1000 feet","1000"]},{"k":"FAF / FAP","m":["faf","fap","final approach fix","final approach point"]},{"k":"IAF is check before commencing","m":["iaf","initial approach fix"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["ban point is the iaf","at the iaf","iaf is the ban"],"why":"Bank p.54; the IAF is where you check, the ban point is 1000 ft or FAF/FAP."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-7","type":"mcq","q":"The approach ban point (AIX) is:","src":"OM-A 7.2.5 b","ref":"OB14","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":5,"o":["1000 ft above the aerodrome or the FAF/FAP","The IAF","The MAPt","The outer marker only"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 7.2.5 b. The IAF is the check before commencing; the bank wrongly calls it the ban point."},{"id":"tr-recall-13","type":"recall","q":"Who sets the EDTO threshold and grants EDTO approval, and which aeroplanes does EDTO cover?","src":"OM-A 4.1.2.2; OM-A 4.1.2.23","ref":"Topic 1 definitions","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":1,"a":"- The **State of the Operator** (for AIX, the **DGCA**) **sets the threshold** time and **grants approval**.\n- EDTO covers aeroplanes with **two or more turbine engines**.\n- The bank is wrong twice: approval is not by the State of Registry for aircraft with more than two engines, and the threshold is not set by ICAO.","keys":[{"k":"State of the Operator","m":["state of the operator","state of operator"]},{"k":"DGCA","m":["dgca"]},{"k":"two or more turbine engines","m":["two or more","2 or more","twin and","more than one"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["state of registry"],"why":"Bank p.33; it is the State of the Operator."},{"m":["set by icao","icao sets"],"why":"Bank p.32; the State of the Operator sets the threshold."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-14","type":"recall","q":"Is there a DME arc speed? What speeds apply on a Cat C arc?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I Table II-5-1-2; Doc 8168 Vol I Table II-6-2-1","ref":"NR18","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":6,"a":"- **No separate DME arc speed.**\n- The arc is part of the initial approach: the **Cat C initial approach speed range is 160 to 240 kt**.\n- **230 kt** is the **holding speed limit up to 14,000 ft**, not an arc speed.\n- The bank's \"DME arc speed 230 KIAS\" mixes these up.","keys":[{"k":"no separate arc speed","m":["no separate","no specific","no arc speed","no dme arc speed","there is no"]},{"k":"Cat C initial 160 to 240 kt","m":["160","240"]},{"k":"230 kt is holding limit","m":["holding"]},{"k":"up to 14,000 ft","m":["14000","14 000"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["arc speed is 230","arc speed 230","230 kias on the arc"],"why":"Bank p.2 and p.3; 230 kt is the holding limit, not an arc speed."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-8","type":"mcq","q":"Cat C speed range for the initial approach segment (e.g. a DME arc)?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I Table II-5-1-2","ref":"NR18","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":6,"o":["160 to 240 kt","230 kt fixed","120 to 180 kt","210 to 250 kt"],"a":0,"why":"Doc 8168 Table II-5-1-2. There is no separate arc speed; 230 kt is the holding limit up to 14,000 ft."},{"id":"tr-recall-15","type":"recall","q":"What is the difference between ATC and FIS? Is FIS part of ATC?","src":"OM-A Ch 0 def 50; Doc 4444 9.1.4.1.3","ref":"OB21","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":7,"a":"- **No.** **FIS and ATC are separate air traffic services.**\n- ATC issues **clearances** and provides separation.\n- **FIS gives no clearances**: it provides information useful for the safe and efficient conduct of flight.\n- The bank's \"FIS is part of ATC\" is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"separate services","m":["separate","different service","not part"]},{"k":"ATC gives clearances","m":["clearance"]},{"k":"FIS gives information only","m":["information"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["fis is part of atc","part of atc"],"why":"Bank p.38; FIS and ATC are separate air traffic services."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-16","type":"recall","q":"Which airspace classes are used in India?","src":"NR11; AIP ENR 1.4; OM-A 17.48","ref":"OB22","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":7,"a":"- **C, D, E, F and G** are in use; **A and B** are **under consideration** (AIP ENR 1.4).\n- The OM-A even has a **Class C speed rule** (17.48).\n- The bank's \"D, E, F and G only (E = Q routes, F = non-radar)\" is wrong: it leaves out Class C.","keys":[{"k":"C, D, E, F, G in use","m":["c d e f","cdefg","c d e f g","c to g","class c"]},{"k":"A and B under consideration","m":["under consideration","a and b"]},{"k":"AIP ENR 1.4","m":["enr 1.4","aip"]},{"k":"Class C speed rule","m":["speed"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["d e f and g only","only d e f","d e f g only"],"why":"Bank p.1 and p.39; Class C is also used in India."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-9","type":"mcq","q":"Airspace classes in use in India?","src":"NR11; AIP ENR 1.4","ref":"OB22","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":7,"o":["C, D, E, F, G (A and B under consideration)","D, E, F, G only","A to G all in use","A, C, D, G"],"a":0,"why":"AIP ENR 1.4 (NR11). The bank omits Class C."},{"id":"tr-recall-17","type":"recall","q":"What is CANPA, and how does it relate to CDFA?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I II-5-1, 1.8.2.5; OM-A 7.2.7","ref":"NR4; NR33","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":6,"a":"- **CANPA is the older name for CDFA**: a continuous descent with **no level segment**.\n- The **decision is made at the DDA** (derived decision altitude).\n- The variant that levels off before the MAPt is what Doc 8168 calls **\"constant angle descent\"**.\n- The bank's \"CANPA allows level flight to the MAP and CDFA is more restrictive\" is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"CANPA = older name for CDFA","m":["older name","old name","same as cdfa","same thing"]},{"k":"no level segment","m":["no level","without level","no level off","no level-off"]},{"k":"decision at DDA","m":["dda","derived decision"]},{"k":"level-off = constant angle descent","m":["constant angle"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["level flight to the map","level to the map","cdfa is more restrictive"],"why":"Bank p.41; CANPA has no level segment."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-18","type":"recall","q":"Why is there a 50 ft increment between MDA and DDA?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I II-5-1, 1.8.2.3, 1.8.2.4; FCTM 5.48; OM-A 7.2.7","ref":"NR34","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":6,"a":"- It is the **operator's increment** so that during a **go-around** the aeroplane **never sinks below the MDA**.\n- **Boeing and AIX use 50 ft**: DDA = MDA + 50 ft.\n- The bank's story that \"the Jumbo jet took 50 ft to level out\" is not the reason.","keys":[{"k":"operator's increment","m":["operator"]},{"k":"go-around never below MDA","m":["below mda","below the mda","sink","height loss"]},{"k":"50 ft","m":["50 ft","50ft","50 feet"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["jumbo"],"why":"Bank p.43; it is an operator increment to avoid sinking below MDA in a go-around."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-19","type":"recall","q":"What is MEHT? Is it the same as the ILS reference point?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.1; Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.1.4; Annex 14 2.12 e)","ref":"OB61","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":11,"a":"- **No.** **MEHT** (minimum eye height over threshold) is a **PAPI/VASIS** term (Annex 14 2.12 e).\n- The **ILS reference datum (Point T)** is a point about **15 m (50 ft)** above the threshold on the glide path (Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.1, 3.1.5.1.4).\n- The bank's \"ILS reference point is the MEHT\" is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"MEHT is a PAPI/VASIS term","m":["papi","vasi"]},{"k":"ILS reference datum / Point T","m":["reference datum","point t","reference point"]},{"k":"15 m / 50 ft","m":["15 m","15m","15 metres","15 meters","50 ft","50ft","50 feet"]},{"k":"not the same","m":["not the same","different","separate"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["reference point is the meht","ils reference point is meht","same as meht"],"why":"Bank p.48; MEHT belongs to PAPI/VASIS."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-10","type":"mcq","q":"The ILS reference datum height over the threshold is:","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.1.4; Annex 14 2.12 e)","ref":"OB61","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":11,"o":["15 m (50 ft)","The MEHT of the PAPI","30 m (100 ft)","60 ft"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.1.4: 15 m (50 ft). MEHT is a PAPI/VASIS eye height; the bank confuses the two."},{"id":"tr-recall-20","type":"recall","q":"How is CPDLC used in oceanic airspace, and what are the FANS 1/A elements?","src":"OM-A 40.14.33; NR36; FCOM 11.34","ref":"OB3","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":2,"a":"- **FANS 1/A** = **CPDLC + ADS-C + RNP**, carried over **ACARS**.\n- The FANS **surveillance element is ADS-C** (contract reports to the ATS unit), not ADS-B.\n- GPS is a navigation sensor, not a FANS element.\n- The bank's \"CPDLC, ADS-B, GPS\" is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"CPDLC","m":["cpdlc"]},{"k":"ADS-C","m":["ads-c","ads c","adsc","contract"]},{"k":"RNP","m":["rnp"]},{"k":"over ACARS","m":["acars"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["ads-b","ads b","adsb"],"why":"Bank p.44 and p.45; the FANS surveillance element is ADS-C."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-21","type":"recall","q":"What was MNPS airspace, what is it called now, and could you fly the North Atlantic tracks?","src":"OM-A 42.0; OM-A 17.3.2 (2)","ref":"OB1","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":2,"a":"- MNPS airspace is now called **NAT HLA** (North Atlantic High Level Airspace).\n- An operator needs approval to fly it, and **AIX has no NAT HLA approval** (OM-A 42.0, 17.3.2 (2)).\n- So no, we could not plan on the tracks. The bank's use of the old MNPS name and its implication that we seek approval are out of date.","keys":[{"k":"NAT HLA","m":["hla","high level airspace"]},{"k":"AIX has no approval","m":["no approval","not approved","dont have approval","don't have approval","not authorised","not authorized"]},{"k":"OM-A 42.0","m":["42.0","om-a 42","oma 42"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["still mnps","called mnps"],"why":"Bank p.73 to p.79; now NAT HLA."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-22","type":"recall","q":"What corridor does an MEA protect, and what are AIX minimum IFR altitudes?","src":"OM-A Ch 0 def 16; OM-A 6.1.2","ref":"OB19","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":6,"a":"- For AIX the **MEA corridor is at least 5 NM each side** of track.\n- AIX own minimum altitudes: **2000 ft** in mountainous terrain / **1500 ft** otherwise, above the highest obstacle **within 10 NM**.\n- The bank's \"2000/1000 ft within 4 NM\" and \"MEA/MOCA protect 4 NM\" are **US FAA** figures; don't quote them.","keys":[{"k":"5 NM each side","m":["5 nm","5nm","5 nautical","five nm"]},{"k":"2000 / 1500 ft","m":["1500"]},{"k":"within 10 NM","m":["10 nm","10nm","10 nautical"]},{"k":"bank figures are FAA","m":["faa"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["4 nm","4nm","4 nautical"],"why":"Bank p.103 and p.112; US FAA figure. AIX: 5 NM corridor, 10 NM for minimum altitudes."},{"m":["2000 1000","1000 ft within"],"why":"Bank; FAA values. AIX uses 2000/1500 ft."}]},{"id":"tr-num-2","type":"num","q":"AIX minimum IFR altitude: radius around track within which obstacles are considered?","src":"OM-A 6.1.2","ref":"OB19","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":6,"ans":10,"unit":"NM","why":"OM-A 6.1.2: 2000/1500 ft above obstacles within 10 NM. 4 NM is the US FAA figure in the bank."},{"id":"tr-recall-23","type":"recall","q":"What are the CAT IIIB minima, and how does ICAO now classify CAT III?","src":"NR27; OM-A Ch 0","ref":"NR27","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":5,"a":"- **CAT IIIB**: **DH below 50 ft or no DH**, **RVR below 175 m but not below 50 m** (NR27).\n- **ICAO** now uses a **single CAT III**: **DH below 100 ft or no DH** (OM-A Ch 0).\n- The bank's \"lower than 50 ft to 15 ft\" is wrong: there is no 15 ft lower limit, and no DH is allowed.","keys":[{"k":"DH below 50 ft or no DH","m":["below 50","less than 50","no dh"]},{"k":"RVR below 175 m","m":["175"]},{"k":"not below 50 m","m":["50 m","50m","50 metres","50 meters"]},{"k":"ICAO single CAT III below 100 ft","m":["single","below 100","100 ft"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["15 ft","15ft","15 feet"],"why":"Bank p.70; there is no 15 ft limit, DH is below 50 ft or none."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-11","type":"mcq","q":"CAT IIIB minimum RVR?","src":"NR27","ref":"NR27","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":5,"o":["Below 175 m but not below 50 m","15 ft DH, 75 m RVR","Below 200 m but not below 75 m","Zero RVR"],"a":0,"why":"NR27: DH below 50 ft or no DH, RVR below 175 m but not below 50 m."},{"id":"tr-recall-24","type":"recall","q":"What holding and approach allowance goes into EDTO critical fuel?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.3 (2)","ref":"Topic 1 section 6","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":1,"a":"- **15 min holding at 1500 ft** over the **en-route alternate**,\n- then **an approach, a go-around and a second approach** (and landing) at that alternate.\n- The bank's \"15-minute hold at destination\" is wrong: critical fuel is about reaching the **en-route alternate**, not the destination.","keys":[{"k":"15 min hold","m":["15 min","15 minutes","15min"]},{"k":"1500 ft","m":["1500"]},{"k":"over the en-route alternate","m":["en-route alternate","enroute alternate","en route alternate","alternate"]},{"k":"approach, go-around, second approach","m":["go-around","go around","missed approach","second approach"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["at destination","over destination","over the destination"],"why":"Bank p.34; the hold is over the en-route alternate."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-12","type":"mcq","q":"EDTO critical fuel hold allowance (AIX)?","src":"OM-A 4.3.7.3 (2)","ref":"Topic 1 section 6","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":1,"o":["15 min at 1500 ft over the en-route alternate","15 min at destination","30 min at 1500 ft over destination","15 min at 3000 ft over the en-route alternate"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 4.3.7.3 (2): 15 min at 1500 ft over the en-route alternate, then approach, go-around and second approach."},{"id":"tr-recall-25","type":"recall","q":"What weather validity window applies when checking an EDTO en-route alternate is suitable?","src":"OM-A 4.3.3.2","ref":"NR38; Topic 1 key facts","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":1,"a":"- For AIX the window runs from the **earliest ETA to the latest ETA** at that alternate.\n- The OM-A gives **no 1 h buffer** either side (OM-A 4.3.3.2).\n- The bank's \"1 h before earliest ETA to 1 h after latest ETA\" is not the AIX rule.","keys":[{"k":"earliest ETA","m":["earliest"]},{"k":"latest ETA","m":["latest"]},{"k":"no 1 h buffer","m":["no buffer","no 1 h","no one hour","no 1 hour","without buffer","no hour"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["1 h before","1 hour before","one hour before"],"why":"Bank p.111; AIX has no 1 h buffer."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-26","type":"recall","q":"What is the longest EDTO approval? Is 240 min the maximum for the 777?","src":"NR23","ref":"NR23","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"topic":1,"a":"- **Approvals beyond 240 min exist**.\n- Up to about **330 to 370 min** for the **777, 787 and A350**.\n- So the bank's \"777 rated to 240 min\" and \"Air New Zealand's 310 min is the highest\" are out of date.\n- Note: this is flagged in NR23 as **not in your OM-A**.","keys":[{"k":"beyond 240 min","m":["beyond 240","more than 240","above 240","over 240"]},{"k":"330 to 370 min","m":["330","370"]},{"k":"777, 787, A350","m":["787","a350","777"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["310"],"why":"Bank p.32; approvals go to about 330 to 370 min."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-27","type":"recall","q":"What is restricted AOM at AIX?","src":"OM-A 7.2.2","ref":"OB11","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":5,"a":"- **Restricted AOM** adds **100 ft** to DA/MDA and **400 m** to the visibility/RVR.\n- It applies to **PICs until they have 100 h in command on type**.\n- The bank's version (non-scheduled operators; normal AOM after 200 h on type and 10 h in 90 days) is not the AIX rule.","keys":[{"k":"+100 ft","m":["100 ft","100ft","100 feet"]},{"k":"+400 m","m":["400 m","400m","400 metres","400 meters","400"]},{"k":"PICs","m":["pic","commander","captain"]},{"k":"until 100 h in command on type","m":["100 h","100 hours","100h","100 hrs"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["200 h","200 hours","200h"],"why":"Bank p.12; AIX restricted AOM ends at 100 h in command on type."},{"m":["non-scheduled","non scheduled","nonscheduled"],"why":"Bank p.12; at AIX it applies to new PICs."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-13","type":"mcq","q":"AIX restricted AOM increments and duration?","src":"OM-A 7.2.2","ref":"OB11","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":5,"o":["+100 ft, +400 m until 100 h in command on type","+200 ft, +400 m until 200 h on type","+100 ft, +200 m until 10 h in 90 days","+50 ft, +400 m until 100 h on type"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A 7.2.2. The bank's 200 h / 10 h in 90 days figures are not AIX's."},{"id":"tr-recall-28","type":"recall","q":"What aircraft equipment and approval does a low visibility take-off need at AIX?","src":"OM-A 45.1.3.1; OM-A 1.8 g (i)","ref":"OB12","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":5,"a":"- **Aircraft (AIX):** **wipers**, **window heat**, **anti-skid** and **reversers**, plus AFM/MEL items.\n- **Approval:** LVTO is a **DGCA OpsSpecs** authorisation.\n- The bank's list (FD, autopilot, RA, weather radar, two VHF COM/NAV) and \"State of Registry\" approval are not the AIX answer.","keys":[{"k":"wipers","m":["wiper"]},{"k":"window heat","m":["window heat"]},{"k":"anti-skid","m":["anti-skid","antiskid","anti skid"]},{"k":"reversers","m":["revers"]},{"k":"DGCA OpsSpecs","m":["opsspec","ops spec","operations specification","dgca"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["state of registry"],"why":"Bank p.35; LVTO is a DGCA OpsSpecs authorisation."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-29","type":"recall","q":"Classify approaches: where do LNAV, LNAV/VNAV and LPV fit?","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I Definitions (IAP); Doc 8168 Vol I Att A 3.3.4.5.1","ref":"OB16","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":6,"a":"- **Precision** approaches include ILS, MLS, GBAS **and SBAS CAT I**.\n- So an **LPV flown to CAT I minima is a precision approach**.\n- The bank lists precision as ILS, MLS and GBAS only and calls LPV an APV; that misses SBAS CAT I.","keys":[{"k":"SBAS CAT I is precision","m":["sbas"]},{"k":"LPV to CAT I = precision","m":["lpv"]},{"k":"ILS, MLS, GBAS","m":["ils","gbas","mls"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["lpv is apv","lpv is an apv","lpv as apv"],"why":"Bank p.26; an LPV to CAT I minima is precision (SBAS CAT I)."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-14","type":"mcq","q":"Per Doc 8168, an LPV approach with CAT I minima is classified as:","src":"Doc 8168 Vol I Definitions (IAP)","ref":"OB16","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":6,"o":["Precision approach (SBAS CAT I)","APV only","Non-precision approach","Visual approach"],"a":0,"why":"Doc 8168 Definitions (IAP); Att A 3.3.4.5.1: precision includes SBAS CAT I."},{"id":"tr-recall-30","type":"recall","q":"During an assumed-temperature take-off on the 737 you need full thrust. What happens if you press TO/GA?","src":"FCTM 3.22","ref":"OB29","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":8,"a":"- During the take-off roll the autothrottle is in **THR HLD**, so the **thrust levers must be advanced manually**.\n- **Below 800 ft RA**, a second TO/GA push only sets the **full-GA N1 bugs**; it does not move the levers.\n- The bank's \"the moment TOGA is pressed, thrust returns to the maximum rated value\" is wrong for the 737.","keys":[{"k":"THR HLD","m":["thr hld","thrust hold"]},{"k":"advance manually","m":["manual"]},{"k":"800 ft RA","m":["800"]},{"k":"only sets GA N1 bugs","m":["bug","n1"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["returns to maximum","automatically to max","thrust returns"],"why":"Bank p.29; in THR HLD the crew must advance the levers."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-31","type":"recall","q":"What is Q-feel on the 737, and what happens if it fails?","src":"FCOM 9.20; QRH 9.8","ref":"OB89","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":20,"a":"- The **elevator feel computer** uses **elevator pitot airspeed** and **stabiliser position**, powered by **hydraulic A or B**, to give column feel.\n- The downspring and bob weight are **not** the Q-feel unit.\n- With **FEEL DIFF PRESS**, column forces **can be significantly higher**, not lower (QRH 9.8).\n- The bank says failure \"will decrease the stick forces\"; that is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"elevator feel computer","m":["feel computer","feel unit"]},{"k":"pitot airspeed","m":["pitot","airspeed"]},{"k":"stabiliser position","m":["stabiliser","stabilizer","stab"]},{"k":"hydraulic A or B","m":["hydraulic","hyd"]},{"k":"FEEL DIFF PRESS: higher forces","m":["higher","heavier","increase"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["decrease","lighter","lower forces"],"why":"Bank p.17 to p.18; with FEEL DIFF PRESS forces can be significantly higher."},{"m":["bob weight","downspring"],"why":"Bank; these are not the Q-feel unit."}]},{"id":"tr-order-1","type":"order","q":"Put the take-off speeds in order from lowest to highest (VMCG at least equal to VEF etc.).","src":"Standard theory; FCTM 3.26","ref":"OB25","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":8,"items":["VMCG","VEF","V1","VR","VLOF","V2"],"why":"VMCG ≤ VEF < V1 ≤ VR < VLOF < V2, with VMU ≤ VLOF. The bank's \"Vmcg > Vef > V1 > Vr > Vmu > Vlof > V2\" reverses the inequalities."},{"id":"tr-recall-32","type":"recall","q":"What are the limits on V1?","src":"Standard theory; FCTM 3.26","ref":"OB25","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":8,"a":"- **VMCG ≤ VEF < V1 ≤ VR < VLOF < V2**, with **VMU ≤ VLOF**.\n- So V1 must be **at least VMCG** (via VEF) and **not more than VR**.\n- On the 737 the order called is **V1, VR, VLOF, V2** (FCTM 3.26).\n- The bank's chain \"Vmcg > Vef > V1 > Vr > Vmu > Vlof > V2\" has the inequalities the wrong way.","keys":[{"k":"V1 not below VMCG","m":["vmcg"]},{"k":"V1 not above VR","m":["vr","rotation"]},{"k":"VEF before V1","m":["vef"]},{"k":"VMU ≤ VLOF","m":["vmu"]}]},{"id":"tr-recall-33","type":"recall","q":"What do you do with a wet start (no light-up) on the 737 MAX?","src":"FCOM 7.20; QRH 7.1; FCOM L.10.8","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":20,"a":"- On the 737 MAX the **EEC cuts fuel and ignition 15 s** after start lever IDLE if **EGT doesn't rise**.\n- QRH **Aborted Engine Start**: start lever **CUTOFF**, then **motor for 60 s** to clear fuel.\n- A **15-minute cooling** period applies only after **three consecutive aborted starts** (FCOM L.10.8).\n- The bank's \"blowout cycle 2 min and cooling period 5 min\" is not the 737 procedure.","keys":[{"k":"EEC cuts fuel at 15 s","m":["15 s","15s","15 sec","15 seconds"]},{"k":"CUTOFF","m":["cutoff","cut off"]},{"k":"motor 60 s","m":["60 s","60s","60 sec","60 seconds","one minute","1 minute"]},{"k":"15 min cooling after 3 aborted starts","m":["three","3 consecutive","3 aborted"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["2 min","2 minutes","5 min","5 minutes"],"why":"Bank p.27; 737: motor 60 s; 15 min cooling only after three aborted starts."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-15","type":"mcq","q":"737 QRH Aborted Engine Start: after CUTOFF, how long do you motor the engine?","src":"QRH 7.1","ref":"OB85","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":20,"o":["60 s","2 min","15 s","5 min"],"a":0,"why":"QRH 7.1: motor 60 s. 2 min blowout / 5 min cooling is the bank's error."},{"id":"tr-recall-34","type":"recall","q":"How do you recover from windshear on the 737?","src":"QRH MAN.1.14","ref":"OB80","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":17,"a":"- In **automatic flight**: **push TO/GA** and **monitor**; the **autopilot may stay engaged**.\n- Pitch: **intermittent stick shaker or initial buffet** is the **upper pitch limit**, not a target.\n- **Don't exceed the PLI**.\n- The bank's \"disengage autopilot, open full throttle, climb till the stick shaker activates\" is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"push TO/GA","m":["toga","to/ga","to ga","take off go around"]},{"k":"A/P may stay engaged","m":["stay engaged","remain engaged","may stay","keep autopilot","autopilot engaged"]},{"k":"stick shaker is a limit, not a target","m":["limit","not a target"]},{"k":"PLI","m":["pli","pitch limit"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["climb till the stick shaker","until stick shaker","till stick shaker","climb to stick shaker"],"why":"Bank p.86; the shaker is the upper limit, not a target."},{"m":["disengage the autopilot","autopilot disengage","disconnect autopilot"],"why":"Bank p.86; in automatic flight push TO/GA, A/P may stay engaged."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-35","type":"recall","q":"In a noise abatement departure, when does thrust come back from take-off thrust, and what is it set to?","src":"FCOM 11.32","ref":"OB31","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":8,"a":"- Thrust goes from **take-off to climb thrust** at the **thrust reduction height**.\n- On the 737 that height can be set between **800 and 9,999 ft** (FCOM 11.32).\n- The bank's \"at 3000 ft in NADP 1, MCT to be set\" is wrong: it is climb thrust, not MCT.","keys":[{"k":"climb thrust","m":["climb thrust","clb"]},{"k":"thrust reduction height","m":["thrust reduction","reduction height"]},{"k":"800 to 9,999 ft","m":["800","9999"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["mct","maximum continuous"],"why":"Bank p.20; thrust is reduced to climb thrust."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-36","type":"recall","q":"NADP 1 vs NADP 2: which protects which areas? Is NADP 2 preferred for terrain?","src":"Oral bank p.20, p.21 diagrams","ref":"OB31","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":8,"a":"- **NADP 1** protects areas **close to the aerodrome**.\n- **NADP 2** protects areas **further away**.\n- **Obstacle clearance** comes from the **take-off performance data**, not the noise procedure, so neither is chosen \"for terrain\".\n- The bank's note that NADP 2 \"quickly takes the aircraft away\" and suits terrain is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"NADP 1 close-in","m":["close","near"]},{"k":"NADP 2 further away","m":["further","farther","distant"]},{"k":"obstacles from performance data","m":["performance"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["nadp 2 for terrain","preferred for terrain"],"why":"Bank p.21; obstacle clearance comes from take-off performance data."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-37","type":"recall","q":"Is the 737 MAX engine controlled by FADEC?","src":"FCOM 7.20","ref":"OB32","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":20,"a":"- **Yes.** The **LEAP-1B** on the 737 MAX has a **full authority digital EEC**, i.e. a **FADEC** (FCOM 7.20).\n- The bank's margin note \"FCU (Boeing), FADEC (Airbus)\" is wrong: FADEC is not an Airbus-only feature.","keys":[{"k":"Yes, FADEC","m":["fadec","full authority"]},{"k":"EEC","m":["eec","electronic engine control"]},{"k":"LEAP-1B","m":["leap"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["fcu"],"why":"Bank p.24; the LEAP-1B has a FADEC (EEC)."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-38","type":"recall","q":"Define V1.","src":"FCTM 3.28","ref":"OB24","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":8,"a":"- V1 is the **maximum speed** at which the **first action to stop** must be taken to stop **within the ASD**.\n- It is also the **minimum speed** from which, after an engine failure, you can **continue to the screen height within the TOD**.\n- The bank's \"maximum speed at which it is equally possible to complete or abort\" is not the definition.","keys":[{"k":"max speed for first action to stop","m":["first action","maximum speed"]},{"k":"stop within ASD","m":["asd","accelerate stop","accelerate-stop"]},{"k":"min speed to continue","m":["minimum speed","continue"]},{"k":"screen height within TOD","m":["tod","take-off distance","takeoff distance","screen"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["equally possible"],"why":"Bank p.19; V1 is max speed for first stop action and min speed to continue."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-39","type":"recall","q":"What is the screen height for the 737, and why do jets and propeller aircraft differ?","src":"FCTM 3.28; FCTM 3.31; Standard theory","ref":"OB28","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":8,"a":"- 737: **35 ft dry** and **15 ft wet** (FCTM 3.28, 3.31).\n- The **35/50 ft** split is by **performance class** (large vs small aeroplanes), **not wing sweep**.\n- The bank's \"35 ft because of the swept wing's lower CL\" is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"35 ft dry","m":["35 ft","35ft","35 feet"]},{"k":"15 ft wet","m":["15 ft","15ft","15 feet"]},{"k":"performance class","m":["performance class","class","large"]},{"k":"not wing sweep","m":["not sweep","not wing","not the wing","not because"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["swept wing","lower cl","sweep"],"why":"Bank p.29; the split is by performance class."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-16","type":"mcq","q":"737 take-off screen height on a wet runway?","src":"FCTM 3.28; FCTM 3.31","ref":"OB28","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":8,"o":["15 ft","35 ft","50 ft","25 ft"],"a":0,"why":"FCTM 3.28, 3.31: 35 ft dry, 15 ft wet."},{"id":"tr-recall-40","type":"recall","q":"What is the purpose of reduced (assumed temperature) thrust? Does it reduce noise?","src":"FCOM 11.32; FCTM 3.20","ref":"OB29","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":8,"a":"- Purpose: **lower EGT** and **longer engine life** (FCTM 3.20).\n- Boeing uses reduced thrust whenever **performance limits and noise abatement procedures permit** (FCOM 11.32).\n- So noise abatement can **limit** its use; the bank's claim that reduced thrust \"helps reduce noise\" gets this the wrong way.","keys":[{"k":"lower EGT","m":["egt"]},{"k":"longer engine life","m":["engine life","engine wear","life"]},{"k":"when performance and noise abatement permit","m":["permit","allow"]},{"k":"noise abatement","m":["noise"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["helps reduce noise","reduces noise","less noise"],"why":"Bank p.29; it is used when noise procedures permit; purpose is EGT and engine life."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-41","type":"recall","q":"What does flying below optimum altitude cost on the 737 MAX, and when is a step climb worth it?","src":"FCTM 4.11; FCTM 4.9","ref":"Where the oral bank is wrong (step climb)","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":8,"a":"- **4000 ft below optimum** costs about **2%** fuel; **8000 ft below** about **7%** (FCTM 4.11).\n- A step is justified only if you will spend about **20 min or more** at the higher level (FCTM 4.9).\n- The bank's \"constant altitude costs about 10% more\" and \"last step within 200 NM of TOD not worth it\" are not the 737 MAX figures.","keys":[{"k":"4000 ft below: 2%","m":["2 percent","2 per cent","two percent"]},{"k":"8000 ft below: 7%","m":["7 percent","7 per cent","seven percent"]},{"k":"20 min at higher level","m":["20 min","20 minutes","20min"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["10 percent","10 per cent"],"why":"Bank p.17; 737 MAX: 2% at 4000 ft below, 7% at 8000 ft below."},{"m":["200 nm","200nm"],"why":"Bank p.17; FCTM rule is about 20 min at the higher level."}]},{"id":"tr-num-3","type":"num","q":"737 MAX: fuel penalty for cruising 8000 ft below optimum altitude?","src":"FCTM 4.11","ref":"Where the oral bank is wrong (step climb)","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":8,"ans":7,"unit":"%","why":"FCTM 4.11: 2% at 4000 ft below, 7% at 8000 ft below. The bank's 10% is wrong."},{"id":"tr-recall-42","type":"recall","q":"Name some true differences between Airbus and Boeing (737) designs; which bank claims are wrong?","src":"FCOM 9.20; Standard theory","ref":"OB91","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"topic":20,"a":"- **Engines:** **both** mount engines on **pylons under the wing**; \"Boeing forward, Airbus under\" is wrong.\n- **Company:** EADS is now **Airbus SE**.\n- **Fly-by-wire:** the 737 MAX has **fly-by-wire spoilers** through the **SCE** (FCOM 9.20), so \"Boeing controls are not FBW\" is too absolute.","keys":[{"k":"both pylons under wing","m":["both","under the wing","pylon"]},{"k":"Airbus SE","m":["airbus se"]},{"k":"FBW spoilers","m":["spoiler"]},{"k":"SCE","m":["sce","spoiler control electronics"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["forward of the wing"],"why":"Bank p.18; both types mount engines on pylons under the wing."},{"m":["subsidiary of eads"],"why":"Bank p.18; EADS is now Airbus SE."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-43","type":"recall","q":"Why are jet wings swept? Distinguish tip stall pitch-up, deep stall and shock stall.","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB87","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"topic":20,"a":"- **Swept-wing tip stall**: tips stall first, the **CP moves forward**, giving **pitch-up**.\n- **Deep stall** is a **T-tail** problem: the tail is blanked by the stalled wing's **wake**.\n- **Shock stall** is **compressibility** separation behind a shock wave.\n- The bank calls swept-wing pitch-up \"deep stall\" and says sweep delays \"shock stall (deep stall)\"; those terms are mixed up.","keys":[{"k":"tip stall, CP forward, pitch-up","m":["tip stall","pitch-up","pitch up","cp forward","centre of pressure"]},{"k":"deep stall = T-tail wake","m":["t-tail","t tail","wake"]},{"k":"shock stall = compressibility","m":["compressib","shock"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["pitch up is deep stall","pitch-up is deep stall","shock stall deep stall"],"why":"Bank p.59; deep stall is a T-tail wake problem."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-44","type":"recall","q":"What are the advantages of a high bypass ratio? Does bypass air cool the turbines?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB83","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"topic":20,"a":"- **No.** **Turbines are cooled by compressor bleed air**, not the bypass stream.\n- The bank's \"promotes cooling of turbines\" as an advantage of high bypass is wrong.\n- The real advantages of high bypass ratio (standard theory) are better propulsive efficiency and lower noise; keep to those.","keys":[{"k":"cooled by compressor bleed","m":["bleed","compressor air"]},{"k":"not bypass air","m":["not bypass","not the bypass","not by bypass"]},{"k":"not the bypass stream","m":["bypass stream","not bypass air"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["cools the turbine","cooling of turbines","turbine cooling"],"why":"Bank p.13; turbines are cooled by compressor bleed air."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-45","type":"recall","q":"What are Rossby waves?","src":"Standard theory","ref":"OB98","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"topic":21,"a":"- **Very long waves** (thousands of km), large-scale **planetary waves** in the **upper westerlies**.\n- They **meander** between **troughs and ridges** and **steer surface lows and highs**.\n- **Temperature is not their defining feature**: the bank's \"very cold, high altitude waves\" is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"planetary / very long waves","m":["planetary","long wave","thousands of km","thousands"]},{"k":"upper westerlies","m":["westerl"]},{"k":"troughs and ridges","m":["trough","ridge"]},{"k":"steer surface systems","m":["steer"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["very cold"],"why":"Bank p.7; they are planetary waves in the upper westerlies, not defined by temperature."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-46","type":"recall","q":"How many satellites does RAIM need to detect a fault, and to exclude it?","src":"NR12; OM-A Ch 40","ref":"NR12","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":3,"a":"- **5 satellites**: RAIM can **detect** a faulty satellite only.\n- **6 satellites** are needed to **identify and exclude** it (**FDE**) so navigation can **continue**.\n- The bank's \"with 5, RAIM can detect the faulty satellite and give a more accurate fix from the best 4\" is wrong: that is exclusion, which needs 6.","keys":[{"k":"5 to detect","m":["5 sat","five sat","5 to detect","detect"]},{"k":"6 to exclude","m":["6 sat","six sat","6 to exclude","exclu"]},{"k":"FDE","m":["fde","fault detection and exclusion"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["best 4","best four"],"why":"Bank p.92; with 5 you only detect. Exclusion (FDE) needs 6."}]},{"id":"tr-num-4","type":"num","q":"Minimum satellites for RAIM fault detection AND exclusion (FDE)?","src":"NR12; OM-A Ch 40","ref":"NR12","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":3,"ans":6,"unit":"satellites","why":"5 detect only; 6 identify and exclude the faulty satellite."},{"id":"tr-recall-47","type":"recall","q":"How is GPS receiver clock bias removed? Name the main GPS errors.","src":"NR64; Annex 10 Vol I Att D; Topic 3 key facts","ref":"OB7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":3,"a":"- **Receiver clock bias** is the **fourth unknown** (with x, y, z), solved with a **fourth satellite**; the bank's \"three satellites at a time\" is wrong.\n- **Ionospheric delay** is the **largest natural GPS error**; the bank omits it.\n- **Selective availability** is **not** a current error: SA was **discontinued on 1 May 2000**.\n- Orbital planes are at **55°** inclination, not 56°.","keys":[{"k":"4th satellite / 4th unknown","m":["fourth","4th","four sat","4 sat"]},{"k":"ionospheric delay largest","m":["ionosph"]},{"k":"SA discontinued 2000","m":["2000","discontinued","switched off","turned off"]},{"k":"55° inclination","m":["55 degree","55 deg","fifty five"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["three sat","3 sat"],"why":"Bank p.92; clock bias is solved with a fourth satellite."},{"m":["56 degree","56 deg"],"why":"Bank p.93; inclination is 55°."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-17","type":"mcq","q":"When was GPS selective availability discontinued?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att D","ref":"OB7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":3,"o":["Midnight, 1 May 2000","It is still active","1 January 1995","1 May 2010"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 Vol I Att D, SA definition note. The bank still lists SA as a current error."},{"id":"tr-mcq-18","type":"mcq","q":"Inclination of the GPS orbital planes?","src":"NR64","ref":"OB7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":3,"o":["55°","56°","64.8°","45°"],"a":0,"why":"NR64: 55°. The bank's 56° is wrong."},{"id":"tr-mcq-19","type":"mcq","q":"Largest natural GPS error source?","src":"Topic 3 key facts","ref":"OB7","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":3,"o":["Ionospheric delay","Selective availability","Receiver clock bias","Satellite geometry only"],"a":0,"why":"Ionospheric delay is the largest natural error; the bank omits it. SA was switched off in 2000; clock bias is solved with a 4th satellite."},{"id":"tr-recall-48","type":"recall","q":"When does the 737 give UNABLE REQD NAV PERF-RNP, and what is ANP?","src":"FCOM 11.31","ref":"OB6","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":3,"a":"- **ANP** is the FMC's estimate of its actual position accuracy.\n- The **UNABLE REQD NAV PERF-RNP** alert comes when **ANP exceeds RNP**.\n- The bank's \"ANP < RNP [message on CDU] [inform ATC]\" has the inequality backwards.","keys":[{"k":"ANP exceeds RNP","m":["exceeds","greater","more than","anp > rnp","anp>rnp","above rnp"]},{"k":"UNABLE REQD NAV PERF","m":["unable","reqd nav perf"]},{"k":"actual navigation performance","m":["actual navigation","position accuracy","estimate"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["anp < rnp","anp less than rnp","anp<rnp"],"why":"Bank p.57; the alert comes when ANP exceeds RNP."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-49","type":"recall","q":"What equipment do RNAV 10, RNP 4, RNP 1 and RNP APCH need in your fleet?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"OB6","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":3,"a":"- **RNAV 10**: **two LRNS** (2 FMC, 2 CDU, 2 IRS); no transponder requirement stated.\n- **RNP 4 (30/30)**: **CPDLC plus ADS-C**.\n- **RNP 1**: **two GPS**. **RNP APCH** and RNP 4 need **GNSS**.\n- The bank's \"RNP 10 needs HF, CPDLC and ADS-B\" and \"DME/DME is the lowest sensor level\" are wrong for our fleet.","keys":[{"k":"RNAV 10: two LRNS","m":["lrns","long range","2 irs","two irs"]},{"k":"RNP 4: CPDLC + ADS-C","m":["ads-c","ads c","adsc"]},{"k":"RNP 1: two GPS","m":["two gps","2 gps","dual gps"]},{"k":"RNP APCH: GNSS","m":["gnss"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["ads-b","ads b","adsb"],"why":"Bank p.58; RNAV 10 needs two LRNS; CPDLC + ADS-C belongs to RNP 4."},{"m":["dme/dme","dme dme"],"why":"Bank p.57; RNP 1 needs two GPS in our fleet."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-20","type":"mcq","q":"RNAV 10 (\"RNP 10\") equipment requirement per the OM-A?","src":"OM-A Ch 40","ref":"OB6","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":3,"o":["Two LRNS (2 FMC, 2 CDU, 2 IRS)","HF, CPDLC and ADS-B","Two GPS and CPDLC","DME/DME and one IRS"],"a":0,"why":"OM-A Ch 40. CPDLC + ADS-C belongs to RNP 4 30/30; the bank's HF/CPDLC/ADS-B is wrong."},{"id":"tr-recall-50","type":"recall","q":"What does the 737 FMC do when outside radio and GPS coverage? Does it go to DR mode?","src":"FCOM 11.31.4; FCOM 11.20.6","ref":"OB5; OB9","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":3,"a":"- **No DR mode.** The FMC navigates on **IRS only**, applying a **learned IRS error correction** (FCOM 11.31.4).\n- The FMC position is **IRS updated by one source** at a time, **in priority order**; it does not average all sensors.\n- The 737 has **two ADIRUs**, not triple IRS.\n- The bank's \"shifts to DR\" and \"takes the aggregate of GPS, triple IRS and radio\" are wrong.","keys":[{"k":"IRS only","m":["irs only","irs"]},{"k":"learned error correction","m":["learned","correction"]},{"k":"one source in priority order","m":["priority"]},{"k":"two ADIRUs","m":["two adiru","2 adiru","adiru"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["dr mode","dead reckoning"],"why":"Bank p.56; the 737 FMC uses IRS with a learned error correction."},{"m":["aggregate","triple irs","three irs"],"why":"Bank p.55; two ADIRUs, updated by one source in priority order."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-51","type":"recall","q":"What is the PBN name for B-RNAV (\"RNP 5\"), as used on India's Q routes?","src":"OM-A 40.8.1","ref":"OB5","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":3,"a":"- The PBN name for B-RNAV / \"RNP 5\" is **RNAV 5** (OM-A 40.8.1).\n- The bank calls India's Q routes \"RNP 5 routes\"; under PBN they are RNAV 5.\n- The bank's route count (\"Q1 to Q14\") is dated; current list pending AIP India ENR 3.","keys":[{"k":"RNAV 5","m":["rnav 5","rnav5"]},{"k":"B-RNAV","m":["b-rnav","brnav","b rnav","basic rnav"]},{"k":"OM-A 40.8.1","m":["40.8.1"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["rnp 5 route","rnp5 route"],"why":"Bank p.56 and p.58; the PBN name is RNAV 5."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-52","type":"recall","q":"What is GAGAN, is it operational, and what receiver does the aircraft need for SBAS?","src":"Topic 3 key facts; Annex 10 Vol I 3.7.3.4.4.1","ref":"OB8","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":3,"a":"- **GAGAN** is India's **SBAS**; it is **operational**, with the **first LPV approach in India in April 2022**.\n- SBAS broadcasts on **1575.42 MHz**, the **GPS L1** carrier, so the **SBAS-capable GPS receiver** uses it; no separate \"GSAT receiver\".\n- The bank's \"planned implementation\" is out of date.","keys":[{"k":"SBAS","m":["sbas"]},{"k":"operational","m":["operational","in use","working"]},{"k":"first LPV April 2022","m":["2022"]},{"k":"1575.42 MHz / L1","m":["1575.42","l1"]},{"k":"GPS receiver, no separate","m":["gps receiver","same receiver","no separate"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["planned","not yet operational"],"why":"Bank p.47; GAGAN is operational (first LPV April 2022)."},{"m":["gsat receiver"],"why":"Bank p.50; SBAS uses GPS L1, received by the SBAS-capable GPS receiver."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-21","type":"mcq","q":"SBAS (GAGAN/WAAS) broadcast frequency?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.7.3.4.4.1","ref":"OB8","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":3,"o":["1575.42 MHz (GPS L1)","1227.60 MHz (GPS L2)","A separate GSAT band needing its own receiver","108 to 117.975 MHz VHF"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.7.3.4.4.1: SBAS uses the GPS L1 carrier."},{"id":"tr-recall-53","type":"recall","q":"Explain ACARS, CPDLC, FANS and ADS-C. What are the FANS 1/A components?","src":"NR36; FCOM 11.34","ref":"OB64","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":12,"a":"- **FANS 1/A** = **CPDLC + ADS-C + RNP**, carried **over ACARS** (NR36; FCOM 11.34).\n- The bank's \"CPDLC; ADS-B; GPS\" is wrong: the FANS surveillance element is **ADS-C**.\n- **AFN logon** is for **CPDLC/ADS-C**, not ADS-B.","keys":[{"k":"CPDLC","m":["cpdlc"]},{"k":"ADS-C","m":["ads-c","ads c","adsc"]},{"k":"RNP","m":["rnp"]},{"k":"over ACARS","m":["acars"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["ads-b","ads b","adsb"],"why":"Bank p.44; FANS uses ADS-C."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-22","type":"mcq","q":"FANS 1/A consists of:","src":"NR36; FCOM 11.34","ref":"OB64","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":12,"o":["CPDLC + ADS-C + RNP over ACARS","CPDLC + ADS-B + GPS","CPDLC + Mode S + HF","ADS-B + TCAS + GPS"],"a":0,"why":"NR36; FCOM 11.34. The bank's CPDLC/ADS-B/GPS is wrong."},{"id":"tr-recall-54","type":"recall","q":"Compare ADS-B and ADS-C: which needs a logon, and does the crew see ADS-C?","src":"FCOM 11.34; Standard theory","ref":"OB63","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":12,"a":"- The **AFN logon** is for **CPDLC/ADS-C**, not ADS-B.\n- **ADS-C** reports are **automatic**, but the crew **sees ADS status** and **can switch it off** (FCOM 11.34).\n- The bank's \"ADS-B involves a log-on and the pilot is unaware of ADS-C\" is wrong.\n- Also: the bank's \"NAS = North Atlantic airspace\" is wrong; **NAS is the US National Airspace System** (FAA text).","keys":[{"k":"AFN logon for CPDLC/ADS-C","m":["afn","logon","log on"]},{"k":"ADS-C automatic","m":["automatic"]},{"k":"crew sees status, can switch off","m":["switch","status","turn off"]},{"k":"NAS = US National Airspace System","m":["national airspace"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["north atlantic airspace"],"why":"Bank p.87; NAS is the US National Airspace System."},{"m":["unaware"],"why":"Bank p.44; the crew sees ADS status and can switch it off."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-55","type":"recall","q":"How sensitive is the glide slope? What is one dot?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.6.1; Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.6.2","ref":"OB59","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":11,"a":"- **CAT I** GS sensitivity: **0.0875 DDM** at an angular displacement of **0.07θ to 0.14θ** below/above the path.\n- **Recommended 0.12θ**, about **0.36°** on a 3° path.\n- The bank's \"1 dot deflection on GP is 0.14°\" is wrong: 0.14 is a fraction of θ, not degrees.","keys":[{"k":"0.0875 DDM","m":["0.0875"]},{"k":"0.07θ to 0.14θ","m":["0.07","0.14"]},{"k":"recommended 0.12θ","m":["0.12"]},{"k":"about 0.36° on 3°","m":["0.36"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["0.14 degree","0.14 deg"],"why":"Bank p.91; 0.14 is 0.14θ, recommended 0.12θ (about 0.36° on 3°)."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-23","type":"mcq","q":"CAT I glide path: recommended angular displacement for 0.0875 DDM on a 3° path?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.6.1; Annex 10 Vol I 3.1.5.6.2","ref":"OB59","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":11,"o":["0.12θ, about 0.36°","0.14°","0.5θ, 1.5°","0.07θ, about 0.07°"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 3.1.5.6.2: 0.07θ to 0.14θ, recommended 0.12θ (about 0.36° on 3°)."},{"id":"tr-recall-56","type":"recall","q":"Where should an ILS DME be sited, and where does it read zero?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 2.11.1; Annex 10 Vol I 7.1.6.3","ref":"OB62","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":11,"a":"- It **should be on the airport, near the runway**; not anywhere in the TMA.\n- If its **zero range is offset** (not at threshold/touchdown), the **offset must be published**.\n- Such a DME is **excluded from RNAV** use.\n- The bank's \"can be placed anywhere in the TMA\" is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"on airport near runway","m":["airport","near the runway","runway"]},{"k":"zero-range offset published","m":["offset","publish"]},{"k":"excluded from RNAV","m":["rnav","exclud"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["anywhere in the tma","anywhere"],"why":"Bank p.91; it should be on the airport near the runway."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-57","type":"recall","q":"What is NDB night effect, and does more beacon power reduce it?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 6.3.4 c)","ref":"OB53","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":10,"a":"- Night effect: at night **sky waves** return and interfere with the ground wave, causing bearing errors.\n- Night-effect distances **are independent of the power of the NDB** (Annex 10 Att C 6.3.4 c).\n- So the bank's \"use a beacon with more power\" does **not** reduce it.","keys":[{"k":"sky wave interference","m":["sky wave","skywave","ionosph"]},{"k":"independent of power","m":["independent","not depend","doesn't depend","doesnt depend"]},{"k":"more power does not help","m":["power"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["more power","higher power","increase power"],"why":"Bank p.98; night-effect distances are independent of NDB power."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-58","type":"recall","q":"What causes VOR scalloping and beam bending? Why is DVOR better?","src":"NR42; Annex 10 Vol I Att C 3.2.1; Topic 10 key facts","ref":"OB55; OB56","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":10,"a":"- **Beam bends** (and scalloping) come from **reflections** off **terrain, buildings and hangars** (site error / multipath), not refraction.\n- **DVOR** is better mainly because of its **large-aperture antenna**, which is more **resistant to multipath** (site error).\n- The bank's \"bending towards a denser medium\" and \"DVOR better because FM is less subject to interference\" are wrong.","keys":[{"k":"reflections","m":["reflect"]},{"k":"terrain, buildings, hangars","m":["terrain","building","hangar"]},{"k":"DVOR large-aperture antenna","m":["aperture","large antenna"]},{"k":"multipath / site error","m":["multipath","site error"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["denser","refraction"],"why":"Bank p.90; beam bends come from reflections."},{"m":["fm is less","because of fm"],"why":"Bank p.101; DVOR's advantage is its large-aperture antenna."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-24","type":"mcq","q":"Main reason a Doppler VOR is more accurate than a conventional VOR?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I Att C 3.2.1","ref":"OB56","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":10,"o":["Large-aperture antenna resists multipath (site error)","FM is less subject to interference","Higher transmitter power","It uses vertical polarisation"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 Att C 3.2.1. The bank's FM explanation is wrong."},{"id":"tr-recall-59","type":"recall","q":"What polarisation do VOR, LOC and GS use? When is vertical polarisation better?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.3.1; 3.1.3.2.2; 3.1.5.2.2","ref":"Where the oral bank is wrong (radio wave basics)","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":10,"a":"- **VOR, LOC and GS are horizontally polarised** (Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.3.1, 3.1.3.2.2, 3.1.5.2.2).\n- Vertical polarisation gives more range and less attenuation **only for surface waves**.\n- So the bank's blanket \"I prefer vertically polarized waves\" is wrong for VHF/UHF nav aids.","keys":[{"k":"horizontal","m":["horizontal"]},{"k":"VOR, LOC, GS","m":["vor","localiser","localizer","glide"]},{"k":"vertical only for surface waves","m":["surface wave","ground wave"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["vertically polarised","vertically polarized","vertical polarisation","vertical polarization"],"why":"Bank p.106; VOR, LOC and GS are horizontally polarised."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-25","type":"mcq","q":"Polarisation of VOR, localiser and glide path signals?","src":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.3.1","ref":"Where the oral bank is wrong (radio wave basics)","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":10,"o":["Horizontal","Vertical","Circular","Vertical for GS, horizontal for LOC"],"a":0,"why":"Annex 10 Vol I 3.3.3.1, 3.1.3.2.2, 3.1.5.2.2."},{"id":"tr-recall-60","type":"recall","q":"Describe the 737 EGPWS mode 6 bank angle alert envelope.","src":"FCOM 15.20.30","ref":"OB76","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":15,"a":"- **10°** bank from **5 to 30 ft** RA,\n- rising **linearly to 35° at 130 ft**,\n- then **35°, 40° or 45°** above 130 ft (FCOM 15.20.30).\n- The bank's \"150 ft RADALT gives 40°\" is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"10° at 5 to 30 ft","m":["10 degree","10 deg","30 ft","30ft"]},{"k":"35° at 130 ft","m":["130"]},{"k":"35, 40 or 45 above","m":["45 degree","45 deg","40 or 45"]},{"k":"linear","m":["linear"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["150 ft","150ft","150 feet"],"why":"Bank p.97; 10° from 5 to 30 ft rising to 35° at 130 ft."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-61","type":"recall","q":"What are the GPWS modes, what is GPWS, and what envelope heights should you quote?","src":"FCOM 15.20.14; NR1","ref":"OB75; NR53","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":15,"a":"- GPWS is a **computer** using **radio altitude**, **baro vertical speed**, **glideslope**, **gear and flaps** (plus a **terrain database** for EGPWS); it is **not a primary radar**.\n- The **FCOM lists the alerts but gives no envelope heights** (FCOM 15.20.14).\n- The bank's figures disagree with each other (mode 3: 700 vs 790 ft; mode 4B: 500 vs 200 ft; mode 5: 100 to 500 vs 50 to 1000 ft); quote them as **generic only**.","keys":[{"k":"computer, not radar","m":["computer","not a radar","not radar"]},{"k":"radio altitude","m":["radio alt","radio altitude","radalt"]},{"k":"glideslope, gear, flaps","m":["glideslope","glide slope","gear","flap"]},{"k":"terrain database","m":["database"]},{"k":"FCOM gives no heights; bank generic only","m":["generic","no envelope","no heights"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["primary radar"],"why":"Bank p.60 and p.96; GPWS is a computer, not a radar."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-62","type":"recall","q":"What can TCAS see, and what is the TRAFFIC, TRAFFIC aural?","src":"FCOM 15.20.37","ref":"Where the oral bank is wrong (TCAS TA and RA)","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":16,"a":"- **Any aircraft without an operating transponder is invisible** to TCAS; not only a Mode S one switched off (the bank's \"ghost plane\").\n- **Altitude is shown only for Mode S or Mode C** intruders.\n- **TRAFFIC, TRAFFIC is the TA aural**, not an RA command (the bank lists it among RAs).","keys":[{"k":"no transponder = invisible","m":["invisible","not seen","cannot see","can't see","not displayed"]},{"k":"altitude only Mode S or C","m":["mode c","mode s"]},{"k":"TRAFFIC TRAFFIC is TA","m":["ta aural","traffic advisory","advisory","is a ta","is the ta"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["ghost"],"why":"Bank p.52; any aircraft without an operating transponder is invisible."},{"m":["traffic traffic is an ra","ra command"],"why":"Bank p.52; TRAFFIC, TRAFFIC is the TA aural."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-26","type":"mcq","q":"\"TRAFFIC, TRAFFIC\" on the 737 is:","src":"FCOM 15.20.37","ref":"Where the oral bank is wrong (TCAS TA and RA)","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":16,"o":["The TA aural","An RA command","A clear-of-conflict call","A GPWS alert"],"a":0,"why":"FCOM 15.20.37: TRAFFIC, TRAFFIC is the TA aural; the bank lists it among RA commands."},{"id":"tr-recall-63","type":"recall","q":"How long does 737 IRS alignment take, and can NAV be restored if the ADIRU is lost in flight?","src":"FCOM 11.20.7; FCOM 11.20.12","ref":"OB71; Where the oral bank is wrong (INS/IRS modes)","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":14,"a":"- **737 alignment: 5 to 17 min** depending on **latitude** (FCOM 11.20.7); the bank's \"3 to 10 min\" is wrong.\n- On ADIRUs connected to the **MMRs**, **Align In Motion** restores **NAV using GPS** (FCOM 11.20.12).\n- So the bank's \"an INS switched off in flight can only be used for attitude\" is wrong for our aircraft.","keys":[{"k":"5 to 17 min","m":["17 min","17 minutes","5 to 17"]},{"k":"depends on latitude","m":["latitude"]},{"k":"Align In Motion","m":["align in motion","aim"]},{"k":"with GPS via MMR","m":["gps","mmr"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["3 to 10","10 min","10 minutes"],"why":"Bank p.60; 737 is 5 to 17 min."},{"m":["only attitude","only for attitude","attitude only"],"why":"Bank p.61; Align In Motion restores NAV with GPS."}]},{"id":"tr-mcq-27","type":"mcq","q":"737 IRS alignment time?","src":"FCOM 11.20.7","ref":"OB71","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":1,"topic":14,"o":["5 to 17 min depending on latitude","3 to 10 min","Always 10 min","1 to 3 min"],"a":0,"why":"FCOM 11.20.7. The bank's 3 to 10 min is wrong for the 737."},{"id":"tr-recall-64","type":"recall","q":"How many Lambert charts cover India?","src":"Oral bank p.102, p.113","ref":"OB47","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":3,"topic":9,"a":"- **Unresolved** in your sources: the bank says **7** on p.102 and **8** on p.113.\n- Pending the **Jeppesen India en-route chart index**.\n- In the viva, say the sources disagree rather than giving one number with confidence.","keys":[{"k":"unresolved / sources disagree","m":["unresolved","disagree","not sure","pending"]},{"k":"7 or 8","m":["seven","eight","7 or 8","7 lambert","8 lambert"]},{"k":"Jeppesen chart index","m":["jeppesen","index"]}]},{"id":"tr-recall-65","type":"recall","q":"Is the Mercator a perspective projection?","src":"Standard theory; Topic 9 key facts","ref":"NR8","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":9,"a":"- **No.** The Mercator is **mathematically constructed** (**non-perspective**) so that it is **conformal**.\n- Scale **expands as sec latitude**.\n- The bank's \"cylindrical perspective projection with the light source at the earth's centre\" is wrong.","keys":[{"k":"non-perspective / mathematical","m":["mathematical","non-perspective","non perspective","not perspective"]},{"k":"conformal","m":["conformal","orthomorphic"]},{"k":"scale as sec latitude","m":["sec lat","secant","sec"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["light source","earth's centre","earths centre","centre of the earth"],"why":"Bank p.102; the Mercator is mathematically constructed, not perspective."}]},{"id":"tr-recall-66","type":"recall","q":"When may you switch on the weather radar on the ground, and what modes does the 737 MAX radar have?","src":"OM-A 17.22; FCOM 11.10.30","ref":"NR45","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":12,"a":"- On the **apron: STBY**.\n- **ON** only with **tilt 5° or more up** and **at least 200 m from other aircraft** (OM-A 17.22).\n- 737 MAX modes: **WX, WX+T and MAP** (plus AUTO/MAN and GC) (FCOM 11.10.30).\n- The bank's \"full tilt up with caution\" and \"MAP to 60 NM, CONT mode\" are wrong.","keys":[{"k":"STBY on apron","m":["stby","standby"]},{"k":"tilt 5° up or more","m":["5 degree","5 deg","tilt up","5 up"]},{"k":"200 m from other aircraft","m":["200 m","200m","200 metres","200 meters"]},{"k":"WX, WX+T, MAP","m":["wx+t","wx t","turbulence","map"]}],"wrong":[{"m":["full tilt","cont"],"why":"Bank p.95; tilt 5° or more up, 200 m clear; modes WX, WX+T, MAP."}]},{"id":"tr-num-5","type":"num","q":"Minimum distance from other aircraft to switch the weather radar ON on the ground (AIX)?","src":"OM-A 17.22","ref":"NR45","asked":true,"trap":true,"prio":2,"topic":12,"ans":200,"unit":"m","why":"OM-A 17.22: ON only with tilt 5° or more up and at least 200 m from other aircraft; STBY on the apron."}];
